ATSWA.put("CA", {"intro":"Cost Accounting is the calculation-heavy core of ATSWA Part III: how a cost is built up from materials, labour and expenses, classified and coded, controlled at each stage, and used to plan, decide and control. The fifteen chapters move from definitions through the three cost elements (materials, labour, overheads) to costing methods (job, contract, process, service), cost-volume-profit analysis, investment appraisal, budgeting, standard costing, recent developments and cost reduction. Every formula is typeset as it appears in the study text, and every worked illustration is taken to a verified numerical answer — including several places where the source text's own printed ledger-card tables were badly garbled by OCR/scanning and had to be independently recomputed from the raw transaction data before being reproduced here.","chapters":[{"n":1,"t":"Introduction to Cost Accounting","brief":"What cost and cost accounting are, the objectives and functions of cost accounting, the distinction between cost data and cost information, how cost accounting relates to (and differs from) financial accounting, the conditions and steps for installing a cost accounting system, and the scope of cost accounting — its elements of cost, methods and techniques of costing.","outcomes":["Define cost accounting","State the objectives of cost accounting","Explain the role of the cost accountant","Differentiate between cost data and cost information","Explain the scope of cost accounting","Explain the benefits and drawbacks of cost accounting","Explain the different ways of cost classification","Explain the various patterns of cost behaviour"],"secs":[{"n":"1.1","t":"Introduction","b":[{"p":"Business organisations exist to provide goods and services **at a profit**. In order to earn that profit, organisations must know the cost incurred in producing the goods and services, so that a desired profit margin can be added to the cost to determine the selling price."},{"p":"This chapter explains **what constitutes cost**, the **forms of classifying cost** and the **different patterns of cost behaviour**. It also explains the **role of the cost accountant**, as well as the **benefits and drawbacks** of cost accounting."},{"key":"Everything else in this subject rests on this chapter. The three elements of cost (§1.3.1), the direct/indirect split, and the prime-cost/overhead build-up are the vocabulary in which every later chapter — materials, labour, overheads, job and process costing, budgeting, standard costing — is written. Learn the definitions here precisely rather than approximately."}]},{"n":"1.2","t":"Definition of cost","b":[{"def":{"t":"Cost","d":"the amount of expenditure incurred on, or attributable to, a specified thing or activity. Mathematically, cost is the product of the quantity of a given resource used and the price per unit of the quantity of resource."}},{"tex":"\\text{Cost} = \\text{Quantity of resource used} \\times \\text{Price per unit}"},{"p":"Costs are usually ascertained in respect of **cost units** or **cost objectives** (§1.2.2)."},{"note":"The multiplicative form matters more than it looks. Because cost is *quantity × price*, any change in a total cost has to come from one of exactly two sources — a change in how much resource was used, or a change in what it cost per unit. That is the whole idea behind variance analysis in Chapter 13, where every variance splits into a **usage** (quantity) part and a **price/rate** part.","lbl":"Why this formula is worth remembering"},{"p":"Sections 1.2.1 to 1.2.12 below follow the study text's own numbering. They move from the definition of cost accounting, through what it is *for* and who does it, to how a cost accounting system is actually designed and installed in a real organisation."}]},{"n":"1.2.1","t":"Definition of cost accounting","b":[{"def":{"t":"Cost accounting (CIMA)","d":"the application of accounting and costing principles, methods and techniques in the ascertainment of cost, and the analysis of savings and/or excesses as compared with previous experience or standard."}},{"p":"From this definition, cost accounting involves:"},{"ul":["the application of costing principles, methods and techniques;","the ascertainment of cost; and","the analysis of savings or overages (excesses)."]},{"p":"A second CIMA definition frames it from the management-accounting side:"},{"def":{"t":"Cost accounting (alternative CIMA definition)","d":"that part of management accounting which establishes budgets and standard costs, and actual costs of operations, processes, departments or products, and the analysis of variances, profitability or the social use of funds."}},{"p":"The issues to remember about this second definition — cost accounting is:"},{"ul":["part of **management accounting**;","concerned with establishing budgets, standard costs, actual costs and variances;","about establishing costs and revenues relating to operations, processes, departments and products — whether standard or actual;","concerned with the analysis of variances;","concerned with the analysis of profitability of ventures and projects; and","concerned with the analysis of the social use of funds."]},{"p":"Most simply, cost accounting can be defined as **the ascertainment of cost** — finding out how much is incurred to produce a given product or unit of service."}]},{"n":"1.2.2","t":"Cost objective","b":[{"def":{"t":"Cost objective","d":"any activity for which a separate measurement of cost is desired — a product, a service, a department or segment of the business, a function, a process or activity, or indeed anything for which the cost of resources used is to be measured."}},{"key":"A **cost unit** is always a cost objective, but not every cost objective is a cost unit — some cost objectives (e.g. \"the marketing function\" as a whole) are broader than any single unit of product or service. Where a cost cannot be traced *directly* to a cost unit, it is first traced to a **cost centre**, for further spreading to cost units."}]},{"n":"1.2.3","t":"The objectives of cost accounting","b":[{"ol":["to ascertain cost and facilitate pricing;","to provide information to assist planning;","to provide information to facilitate decision making;","to provide information to aid control;","to provide motivation and promote cost consciousness at all levels of the management hierarchy; and","to provide a formal means of gathering detailed information on operations."]},{"p":"These objectives are achieved through the processes of **cost ascertainment** and **cost control**, which form the subject matter of the rest of this study text."}]},{"n":"1.2.4","t":"Functions of cost accounting / the role of the cost accountant","b":[{"p":"The functions of cost accounting are best understood against the four classic management functions they support:"},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Function","What the cost accounting system provides"],"rows":[["**Pricing**","Applies costing principles, methods and techniques to ascertain the cost of units (products, services or processes) — the basis for pricing decisions."],["**Planning**","Planning is establishing goals and suitable courses of action to achieve them. The cost system generates information on resource availability (materials, labour, other facilities), cash-flow pattern within a given time horizon, and expected returns from alternative projects — enabling management to commit scarce resources and set good priorities."],["**Decision making**","Decision making is choosing among alternatives to achieve defined objectives. Cost accounting generates a pool of information — how costs behave, their composition and nature (fixed or variable, sunk or opportunity, incremental, avoidable or unavoidable) — supporting choices such as make-or-buy, sell-or-further-process, continue-or-discontinue a product line, and accept-or-reject a special order."],["**Control**","Control is ensuring that plans are actually achieved, through three elements: (i) set targets/goals in a budget statement (planning); (ii) measure actual achievement during the control period and compare with the set goals; (iii) analyse and investigate any resultant variance and take corrective action."]]}}]},{"n":"1.2.5","t":"Difference between cost data and cost information","b":[{"warn":"This distinction is a favourite short-answer question — cost **data** is the raw input; cost **information** is what results once that input has been analysed."},{"def":{"t":"Cost data","d":"factual information concerning the cost of materials, labour and overheads and other cost elements, expected to be incurred or actually incurred in a business venture."}},{"p":"Sources of cost data:"},{"ol":["potential suppliers, for the purpose of submitting proposals and bids;","existing suppliers, with which the business has developed a solid basis for financial control; and","cost models."]},{"def":{"t":"Cost information","d":"the detailed analysis of costs — the calculation of production costs, the quantification of loss and the estimation of work efficiency — providing a solid basis for financial control. It is used in decision making and in planning future operations, and it assists in establishing company performance standards partly from past cost history."}},{"key":"Cost data are like the **inputs** from which cost information is **generated**."}]},{"n":"1.2.6","t":"Advantages (benefits) of cost accounting","b":[{"ol":["helps identify profitable and unprofitable products and services, so management can make prudent decisions on adopting a new product or discontinuing a product line;","helps identify efficient and inefficient methods and take corrective measures — areas of wastage are indicated, idle time is highlighted, and loopholes/system weaknesses are identified;","generates comparative information on jobs, products and projects over time (temporal comparison for one cost centre) and across a number of cost centres (cross-sectional comparison), making effective control possible;","facilitates production control — costing principles keep material, labour and expense costs at desirable levels, enhancing production planning and scheduling;","facilitates inventory control costing, establishing procedures for the procurement, storage and issue of inventory items; and","facilitates accurate estimation of the cost of various cost units — essential for pricing."]}]},{"n":"1.2.7","t":"Disadvantages (drawbacks) of cost accounting","b":[{"ol":["analysing cost data itself involves expense and cost;","some costing systems are complex and cumbersome, and thus not well understood by the managers who need to operate them; and","producing the basic data requires completing many forms; where inaccurate data is supplied, the foundation of subsequent analysis is weakened."]}]},{"n":"1.2.8","t":"Relationship between cost accounting and financial accounting","b":[{"def":{"t":"Financial accounting","d":"that part of accounting that records actual transactions of an entity in monetary terms, in accordance with established concepts, principles, accounting standards and legal requirements, and presents as accurate a view as possible of the effects of those transactions over a period of time and at the end of that time. It concerns an enterprise's relationship with creditors, debtors, banks and revenue authorities, culminating in periodical financial statements for users outside the business."}},{"p":"Financial accounting has been criticised for these weaknesses, all of which cost accounting exists to remedy:"},{"ol":["no information is given about individual jobs, products or processes;","it is historical — financial information is not available until the end of the accounting period, so cost data relates to the past;","it does not require costs to be classified into direct/indirect, controllable/non-controllable, normal/abnormal, variable/fixed, etc.;","detailed records of inventory movement are not kept, which inhibits efficient inventory control; and","generally, there is no budget plan."]},{"table":{"align":"llll","head":["Factor","Cost accounting","Financial accounting"],"rows":[["Nature of costs/revenue","Predetermined estimates, standards and budgets","Historical and past costs/revenue"],["Users of information","Management of the business enterprise","Management and external stakeholders — shareholders, creditors, debtors, tax authorities, investors, etc."],["Objectives","Provide information to aid planning, decision making and control","Satisfy the stewardship function of management — account for the use of resources entrusted to management"],["Conformity to concepts/standards","No need to comply with standards and concepts","Absolute need to comply meticulously with accounting concepts and standards"],["Scope and form of presentation","Determined by management, on relevance and cost-benefit grounds","Determined by concepts, standards and legal provisions"],["Precision of information","Approximations permitted (uncertainty in estimates), but must be sufficiently precise and accurate","Information must be more precise — usually audited by independent external auditors to confirm reliability"]]}}]},{"n":"1.2.9","t":"Conditions necessary for an effective cost accounting system","b":[{"p":"The purpose of cost accounting is achieved only when an effective system is in place — one tailor-made to the business's own environment: simple, economical and practical. Conditions for a successful system:"},{"ol":["co-operation and co-ordination among all staff members, including management;","a proper system of stores and inventory control;","adequate payroll procedures;","an improved system of data capture — standardised pre-printed forms are recommended for recording receipts of materials, issue of materials, labour attendance time and hours worked, and wage calculations;","a sound plan for collecting all indirect costs under suitable headings and absorbing them into products or service departments on a predetermined basis;","a costing department or section, with the cost accountant's responsibilities and duties clearly defined; and","cost accounts and financial accounts maintained so that their results can be reconciled easily."]}]},{"n":"1.2.10","t":"Steps in installing a cost accounting system","b":[{"p":"Installing a cost accounting system is substantial — it needs detailed discussion with management and staff, and careful analysis of the correct procedures and records. The generally recommended stages:"},{"steps":["Establish the objectives and scope of the proposed system through discussion and agreement with management.","Analyse the current system — are there cost centres? Is there a coding system? What accounting records does the existing system maintain?","Study the current system — staff composition (qualification, experience, competence); the production system and methods (jobbing/engineering, contract, service?); store-keeping systems; wage systems.","Think through the type and nature of the cost accounting system required — job/batch, contract, process or service costing? Full absorption, marginal or standard costing? What method prices store issues? What remuneration methods are desirable? What is the basis of overhead absorption?","Design the system in collaboration with management and the staff who will operate it.","Prepare an operating manual for all personnel, showing procedures, objectives and codes.","Arrange training and instruction for all staff involved in implementing the system.","Implement the system with full publicity.","Monitor the system closely and report to management periodically.","Build a feedback mechanism into the system."]}]},{"n":"1.2.11","t":"Problems inherent in the installation of a cost accounting system","b":[{"p":"General factors defining different problems for a given system:"},{"ul":["size of the organisation;","type of product/service;","the production process;","the methods of manufacture; and","availability of staff."]},{"p":"Other specific problems:"},{"ul":["definition of responsibility;","definition or designation of cost centres;","compilation of a comprehensive cost system;","availability of staff;","labour hours worked;","machine utilisation time;","scrap treatment;","rectification cost; and","the difficulty of accurate cost classification."]}]},{"n":"1.2.12","t":"Principles of a good cost accounting system","b":[{"ol":["the system itself must be appropriate to the organisation and its processes or business;","reports, statements and analyses must contain relevant information for their intended purpose — information is relevant when it has value that helps resolve a problem;","reports must be produced at appropriate intervals, and promptly — a late report loses relevance;","reports must be addressed to the persons with the capacity to act on their subject matter;","information must be sufficiently accurate to be reliable, or decisions will rest on invalid foundations;","reports should be simple and understandable — jargon and complex terminology should be avoided as much as possible; and","the benefits from generating information should outweigh the cost of generating it."]}]},{"n":"1.3","t":"Scope of cost accounting","b":[{"p":"The \"scope\" of cost accounting means the ground it covers: what a cost is built out of, how it is accumulated, what techniques are applied to it, what information the system alone can produce, where in the organisation it sits, and who uses it. Sections 1.3.1 to 1.3.9 take those in turn, following the study text's own numbering."}]},{"n":"1.3.1","t":"Elements of cost","b":[{"p":"There are three elements of cost — **materials**, **labour** and **expenses** — giving three classes of cost: materials cost, labour cost and expenses. Each element can be either **direct** or **indirect**."},{"table":{"align":"lll","head":["Element","Meaning","Typical examples"],"rows":[["**Material costs**","Costs of materials or commodities (other than fixed assets) introduced into a product or consumed in operations — the cost of materials input into goods and services.","Raw materials, component parts, work in progress, primary packing materials, lubricating oil, consumable tools, stationery, cleaning materials."],["**Labour cost**","The cost of employee remuneration — payments made to and on behalf of employees for offering labour services in the production function.","—"],["**Expenses**","Costs other than material costs and labour costs.","Hiring and maintaining special equipment, royalty payments, copyright/patent payments, utilities (electricity, water), rent."]]}},{"h4":"Direct costs"},{"def":{"t":"Direct cost","d":"a cost that can be directly identified and charged to a cost unit without apportionment."}},{"p":"Direct costs comprise direct material cost, direct labour cost and direct expenses:"},{"table":{"align":"lll","head":["Type","Meaning","Examples"],"rows":[["**Direct material cost**","Cost of material that can be physically identified with a specific cost unit — materials entering into and becoming a constituent element of a product or saleable service; allocated directly to cost units.","Materials purchased specifically for a job order or process; materials requisitioned from store for a particular production order; work-in-progress and component parts; primary packing materials (cartons, wrapping material)."],["**Direct labour (wages)**","Cost of employees' efforts and skills applied directly to a product or saleable service; allocated directly to cost units.","Wages of production operatives transforming raw materials into finished goods; wages of waiters serving meals at a hotel; wages of sales assistants selling goods in a retail shop."],["**Direct expenses**","Costs (other than material and labour cost) that can be identified with and charged or allocated to a cost unit — incurred for a specific product or saleable service.","Hiring special equipment for a particular production order (and maintaining it); royalty payments; travelling expenses to a contract site."]]}},{"note":"Some materials that could qualify as direct materials may be treated as indirect materials, purely for reasons of materiality and convenience — a low-value item used across many products is not worth tracking unit by unit."},{"fbox":{"h":"Prime cost","rows":[{"lb":"Prime cost","tex":"\\text{Prime cost} = \\text{Direct materials} + \\text{Direct labour} + \\text{Direct expenses}"}]}},{"h4":"Indirect costs (overheads)"},{"def":{"t":"Indirect cost (overhead)","d":"a cost that cannot be identified with and allocated to a cost unit, but must be apportioned to a number of cost centres and further absorbed by cost units."}},{"table":{"align":"lll","head":["Type","Meaning","Examples"],"rows":[["**Indirect material cost**","Cost of material items that cannot be identified with any one product, because they benefit all products rather than one specific product — apportioned and absorbed by cost units, not allocated to them.","Materials for operating/maintaining plant and equipment (lubricating oil, consumable tools); stationery; cleaning materials (soap and detergents, rugs and dusters, brooms and brushes)."],["**Indirect labour cost**","Wages of employees who do not work on the product itself but assist the manufacturing process; apportioned to cost centres and assigned to cost units through absorption, not allocated directly.","Salaries of factory supervisors; wages of store-department employees; wages of cleaners."],["**Indirect expenses**","Expenses incurred in general, not for a specific cost unit.","Selling and distribution (advertising, sales promotion); administrative (stationery, audit/consultancy fees, secretarial charges); production (rent, insurance, electricity)."]]}},{"fbox":{"h":"Overhead and cost card build-up","rows":[{"lb":"Overhead","tex":"\\text{Overhead} = \\text{Indirect materials} + \\text{Indirect labour} + \\text{Indirect expenses}"}]}},{"table":{"align":"lr","head":["Cost card (elements + nature)",""],"rows":[["Direct material cost","xx"],["Direct labour cost","xx"],["Direct expenses","xx"],["**Prime cost**","xxx"],["Indirect material cost","xx"],["Indirect labour cost","xx"],["Indirect expenses","xx"],["**Overhead cost**","xxx"],["**Total cost**","xxx","@tot"]]}}]},{"n":"1.3.2","t":"Methods of costing","b":[{"p":"The method used to determine the cost of a product or service is dictated by factors such as the nature of the cost unit, the production process, the mode of cost accumulation, and the duration of work. The well-established methods:"},{"ul":["**Job/batch costing**","**Contract costing**","**Process costing**","**Service costing**"]},{"p":"Each is covered in detail in later chapters."}]},{"n":"1.3.3","t":"Techniques of costing","b":[{"p":"Independently of which *method* is used, a cost accounting system also applies one of these *techniques*:"},{"ul":["**Absorption costing**","**Marginal costing**","**Standard costing** (itself using either absorption or marginal costing)"]}]},{"n":"1.3.4","t":"Information exclusively generated from a cost accounting system","b":[{"ol":["statements of material, direct labour and machine utilisation showing trends and values;","cost of products and services broken down into material cost, labour cost and other expenses, compared against estimates;","cost control statements for each section/department, showing actual results against budget;","periodic inventory valuation reports;","scrap and rectification cost reports; and","budgets for planning and control — including cash budgets for monitoring liquidity."]}]},{"n":"1.3.5","t":"Cost accounting as part of the management information system","b":[{"p":"Management needs information for decision making, planning and control, requiring a management information system. Cost accounting (and management accounting more broadly) is an integral part of that system, providing information for:"},{"ul":["formulation of policy;","planning and controlling the activities of the enterprise;","decision making;","disclosure to external stakeholders;","disclosure to employees; and","safeguarding the assets of the enterprise."]},{"p":"In more detail, the cost accounting function is useful in four managerial areas:"},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Area","How cost accounting helps"],"rows":[["**Policy formulation**","Helps decide what products to sell, in what markets to operate, and at what selling prices."],["**Planning**","Provides data to evaluate capital-expenditure proposals and past performance to guide future performance; is central to establishing budgetary procedures/timetables and coordinating short-term plans across the business, including preparing the master budget."],["**Control**","Produces performance reports comparing actual results with intended results for each responsibility centre, drawing management attention to trouble spots."],["**Decision making**","The analysis of costs and revenues in cost accounting reports guides decisions directly."]]}}]},{"n":"1.3.6","t":"Organisations other than manufacturing companies where cost accounting can be applied","b":[{"p":"Every organisation uses some items of stores or materials — including non-profit bodies — so cost accounting applies far beyond manufacturing:"},{"ul":["**Local authorities** — District, Municipal or Metropolitan Assemblies (e.g. Accra Metropolitan Authority, Kano Municipal Local Government, Lagos Island Local Education Authority);","**Health-sector organisations** — hospitals, clinics;","**Educational institutions** — colleges, polytechnics, universities;","**Financial institutions** — banks, insurance companies;","**Transport businesses**;","**Consultancy firms** — accounting/audit firms, economic/management consultancies, legal chambers;","**Utility companies** — water, electricity;","**Farming ventures**; and","**Trading outlets** — retailing, wholesaling, departmental stores, supermarkets."]},{"p":"For such organisations, cost accounting can generate: cost per unit of product/service; cost of running the organisation by section/department; wages cost per unit and material cost per unit; cost per patient per consulting hour or per bed; budgetary control statements; cost per full-time-equivalent student; and branch operating statements."}]},{"n":"1.3.7","t":"Value of cost information in the management decision-making process","b":[{"p":"Business is dynamic — management must review decisions currently being implemented, and take fresh decisions for the near and distant future, in line with industry trends, if the business is to remain competitive. Inflation, for instance, renders cost structures obsolete as a shift in price indices forces a rework of every cost element; a change in foreign exchange rates can adversely affect costing for import- or export-dependent industries. If product cost changes, selling price must follow — sometimes as often as daily. Without cost information, management cannot respond to the on-the-spot decisions that dynamic business situations demand."}]},{"n":"1.3.8","t":"Impact of information technology on the generation, evaluation and presentation of cost information","b":[{"p":"Management cannot afford late information — an untimely piece of information can be more damaging than none at all. Financial information often comes in arrears, since a reporting period must expire before complete figures can be compiled; acceptable for historical purposes, but potentially critical for a business facing stiff competition or operating under tight conditions, where even a day's delay matters."},{"p":"IT supports businesses with online, real-time reporting to management — with a good information network, top management can access figures almost as soon as they are generated at the shop-floor level. Controls can be built directly into the system, e.g. requiring authorisation before supplying a debtor who has exceeded their credit limit/period. IT also reduces the time spent generating paperwork and obtaining physical authorisations, since the system does the groundwork itself and passes results to management on-line."}]},{"n":"1.3.9","t":"Roles required of accounting technicians in collection, analysis and presentation of cost information","b":[{"p":"The accounting technician drives the day-to-day detail of information presentation — laxity here has a profound, adverse effect on the quality and timeliness of the information management relies on. Specifically, the technician is expected to:"},{"ol":["ensure established formats for data compilation are correctly followed at every level;","monitor the regularity of data entry to ensure timeliness — hourly, daily, weekly and monthly reports as required;","confirm the accuracy of figures supplied, through reasonableness checks or other independent sources;","raise flags where extraneous or extraordinary figures appear;","prepare summaries of raw data showing results from various perspectives (e.g. sales by branch, by period, by salesman, by season) to give a total picture;","draw summaries that are simple, straightforward and concise — not too voluminous for a busy executive, without losing relevance; and","pre-empt questions with explanatory notes on the trends and movements observed."]}]},{"n":"1.4","t":"Worksheet summary — definitions and structure","b":[{"p":"Everything in this chapter, compressed for a last read-through."},{"h3":"Section-by-section checklist"},{"ol":["**§1.1 Introduction** — organisations must know cost in order to add a margin and set a selling price; this chapter covers what cost is, how it is classified, how it behaves, the cost accountant's role, and the benefits and drawbacks of cost accounting.","**§1.2 Definition of cost** — expenditure incurred on or attributable to a specified thing or activity; cost = quantity of resource used × price per unit; ascertained per cost unit or cost objective.","**§1.2.1 Definition of cost accounting** — CIMA's two definitions (application of costing principles/methods/techniques to ascertain cost and analyse savings or excesses; and that part of management accounting establishing budgets, standard costs, actual costs and variances), plus the simplest form — the ascertainment of cost.","**§1.2.2 Cost objective** — any activity for which a separate measurement of cost is desired; every cost unit is a cost objective but not every cost objective is a cost unit; untraceable costs go first to a cost centre.","**§1.2.3 Objectives of cost accounting** — six of them, achieved through cost ascertainment and cost control.","**§1.2.4 Functions / the role of the cost accountant** — four: pricing, planning, decision making, and control (control itself having three elements: set targets, measure and compare, analyse the variance and act).","**§1.2.5 Cost data vs cost information** — data are the raw factual inputs (three named sources); information is the analysed output.","**§1.2.6–1.2.7 Advantages and drawbacks** — six advantages, three drawbacks.","**§1.2.8 Relationship with financial accounting** — the definition of financial accounting, its five stated weaknesses, and Fig 1.1's six-factor comparison table.","**§1.2.9 Conditions for an effective system** — seven, starting from co-operation and co-ordination among all staff.","**§1.2.10 Steps in installing a system** — the ten-stage process from establishing objectives through to building in a feedback mechanism.","**§1.2.11 Problems in installation** — five general factors plus the specific problems.","**§1.2.12 Principles of a good system** — seven, ending with the cost-benefit test.","**§1.3 Scope** — §1.3.1 three elements of cost (materials/labour/expenses), each split direct/indirect, giving prime cost and overhead; §1.3.2 four costing methods (job/batch, contract, process, service); §1.3.3 three costing techniques (absorption, marginal, standard); §1.3.4 the six information outputs only a cost system produces; §1.3.5 cost accounting within the MIS (policy, planning, control, decision making); §1.3.6 the nine classes of non-manufacturing organisation that use it; §1.3.7 the value of cost information (inflation, exchange rates); §1.3.8 IT's impact; §1.3.9 the accounting technician's seven specific roles."]},{"h3":"All the terms"},{"ul":["**Cost** — expenditure incurred on/attributable to a specified thing or activity; quantity × price per unit.","**Cost accounting** — application of costing principles/methods/techniques to ascertain cost and analyse variance; part of management accounting.","**Cost objective** — any activity for which a separate cost measurement is desired. **Cost unit** — a cost objective that is a quantitative unit of product/service (every cost unit is a cost objective; not every cost objective is a cost unit).","**Cost centre** — where a cost is first traced when it cannot go directly to a cost unit.","**Cost data** — raw factual cost information (the input). **Cost information** — the analysed output used for decisions.","**Prime cost** — direct materials + direct labour + direct expenses. **Overhead** — indirect materials + indirect labour + indirect expenses.","**Direct cost** — traced to a cost unit without apportionment. **Indirect cost** — apportioned to cost centres, then absorbed by cost units."]}]}],"formulas":[{"lb":"Cost","tex":"C = q \\times p","nt":"q = quantity of resource used, p = price per unit of the resource."},{"lb":"Prime cost","tex":"\\text{Prime cost} = \\text{DM} + \\text{DL} + \\text{DE}","nt":"DM = direct materials, DL = direct labour, DE = direct expenses."},{"lb":"Overhead","tex":"\\text{Overhead} = \\text{IM} + \\text{IL} + \\text{IE}","nt":"IM = indirect materials, IL = indirect labour, IE = indirect expenses."},{"lb":"Total cost","tex":"\\text{Total cost} = \\text{Prime cost} + \\text{Overhead}"}],"focus":"Almost every diet asks at least one definition question straight from this chapter — cost data vs cost information, cost objective vs cost unit, and the cost-accounting-vs-financial-accounting comparison (Fig 1.1) are the three most examined. The direct/indirect split of each cost element and the prime-cost/overhead build-up are the numerical foundation for almost every later chapter's costing method — get these exactly right here.","errors":["Treating \"cost objective\" and \"cost unit\" as synonyms — every cost unit is a cost objective, but not every cost objective is a cost unit.","Confusing cost data (raw, factual) with cost information (analysed, decision-ready).","Forgetting that indirect costs are apportioned to cost centres and only then absorbed by cost units — never allocated directly, unlike direct costs.","Listing \"ascertaining fraud\" as an objective of cost accounting — investigation of fraud is not one of the six stated objectives; it is easily confused with control."],"quiz":{"mcq":[{"q":"Cost accounting is best described, in its simplest form, as","o":["the preparation of the balance sheet","the writing off of costs","the ascertainment of cost","the preparation of a statement of value added","the annual audit of financial statements"],"a":2,"w":"Cost accounting can be defined most simply as the ascertainment of cost — finding out how much is incurred to produce a given product or unit of service.","src":"Chapter 1.2","sec":"1.2"},{"q":"Cost accounting is an integral part of","o":["financial accounting","forensic accounting","treasury accounting","historical accounting","management accounting"],"a":4,"w":"CIMA's second definition frames cost accounting as that part of **management accounting** which establishes budgets, standard costs, actual costs and the analysis of variances.","src":"Chapter 1.2","sec":"1.2"},{"q":"Which of the following is NOT one of the objectives of cost accounting?","o":["provision of information to aid control","ascertainment of cost to facilitate pricing","provision of information for decision making","investigation of fraud","provision of assistance in planning"],"a":3,"w":"The six stated objectives are ascertaining cost/pricing, assisting planning, facilitating decision making, aiding control, promoting cost consciousness, and providing a formal means of gathering operational information — fraud investigation is not among them.","src":"Chapter 1.2","sec":"1.2"},{"q":"Material costs do NOT include the cost of","o":["fixed assets","raw materials","work in progress","packing materials","cleaning materials"],"a":0,"w":"Material costs are the costs of materials or commodities **other than fixed assets** introduced into a product or consumed in operations.","src":"Chapter 1.3","sec":"1.3"},{"q":"The sum of direct material cost, direct labour cost and direct expenses is known as","o":["overhead","total cost","prime cost","conversion cost","marginal cost"],"a":2,"w":"Prime cost = direct materials + direct labour + direct expenses.","src":"Chapter 1.3","sec":"1.3"},{"q":"The wages of a factory supervisor, who does not work directly on any one product but oversees production generally, are an example of","o":["direct labour cost","indirect labour cost","direct expenses","prime cost","a period cost only"],"a":1,"w":"Employees who assist the manufacturing process without working on the product itself (e.g. supervisors) generate indirect labour cost — apportioned to cost centres and absorbed by cost units, not allocated directly.","src":"Chapter 1.3","sec":"1.3"},{"q":"Which of the following is the correct distinction between cost data and cost information?","o":["They are the same thing, just different names","Cost data is analysed output; cost information is the raw input","Cost data is the raw input; cost information is the analysed output","Cost data relates only to labour; cost information relates only to materials","Cost information is always inaccurate, unlike cost data"],"a":2,"w":"Cost data are the raw facts from which cost information — the detailed analysis used for decisions — is generated.","src":"Chapter 1.2","sec":"1.2"},{"q":"Compared with cost accounting, financial accounting information is best described as","o":["predetermined estimates, standards and budgets","historical and past costs/revenue","approximate and imprecise by design","exempt from any legal or standards compliance","intended solely for internal management use"],"a":1,"w":"Per Fig 1.1, cost accounting deals in predetermined estimates/standards/budgets, while financial accounting deals in historical and past costs/revenue.","src":"Chapter 1.2","sec":"1.2"},{"q":"A cost that cannot be identified with a specific product because it benefits all products, and so must be apportioned to cost centres before being absorbed by cost units, is an example of","o":["a direct cost","an indirect cost","a sunk cost","an opportunity cost","a committed cost"],"a":1,"w":"This is the definition of an indirect cost (overhead) — apportioned, then absorbed, never allocated directly the way a direct cost is.","src":"Chapter 1.3","sec":"1.3"},{"q":"Which of these is explicitly listed as a role of the accounting technician in collecting, analysing and presenting cost information?","o":["Setting the organisation's overall pricing strategy unaided","Confirming the accuracy of figures supplied through reasonableness checks","Conducting the external statutory audit","Approving all capital expenditure","Negotiating collective bargaining agreements with the labour union"],"a":1,"w":"Confirming accuracy through reasonableness checks (or other independent sources) is one of the seven roles set out for the accounting technician.","src":"Chapter 1.3","sec":"1.3"}],"theory":[{"q":"Distinguish between cost data and cost information, and explain the relationship between them.","marks":6,"a":[{"p":"**Cost data** is factual information concerning the cost of materials, labour, overheads and other cost elements, expected to be incurred or already incurred in a business venture. Its sources include potential suppliers (proposals and bids), existing suppliers, and cost models."},{"p":"**Cost information** is the detailed analysis of costs — the calculation of production costs, quantification of loss and estimation of work efficiency — used to make decisions, plan future operations and set performance standards."},{"p":"The relationship: cost data are the **inputs** from which cost information is **generated**. Data must be collected and analysed before it becomes usable information for management."}],"src":"Chapter 1.2","sec":"1.2"},{"q":"A Managing Director has said: \"Since the main object of our firm is to know the annual profit, there is no need retaining the cost accounting staff — let us pay them off and retain only the financial accounting staff, in order to reduce administrative costs.\" Write a brief report to the Managing Director explaining why the cost accounting function should be retained.","marks":10,"a":[{"p":"Financial accounting alone cannot serve the business's decision-making needs, for five reasons: it gives no information about individual jobs, products or processes; it is historical, so information is not available until the accounting period ends; it does not classify costs into direct/indirect, controllable/non-controllable, normal/abnormal or variable/fixed; it keeps no detailed inventory-movement records, weakening inventory control; and it generally involves no budget plan."},{"p":"Cost accounting exists precisely to close these gaps: it ascertains the cost of individual jobs/products (supporting pricing), provides current, forward-looking information for planning and control (not just year-end historical figures), classifies costs in the ways decisions actually require, and drives budgetary control. Losing it would leave management able to see *that* a profit was made, but not *why*, *where costs could be reduced*, or *how to price competitively* — the annual profit figure financial accounting produces is the result of decisions cost accounting exists to inform in the first place."},{"p":"Recommendation: retain the cost accounting function — the administrative saving from removing it is very likely to be outweighed by worse pricing, planning and control decisions taken without it."}],"src":"Chapter 1.2","sec":"1.2"}]}},{"n":2,"t":"Cost Classification, Segregation and Codification","brief":"The many ways a single cost can be classified — by element, nature, function, behaviour, relevance to decisions, and controllability — the problems with the conventional fixed/variable split and the real patterns cost behaviour takes, cost centres versus cost units versus profit centres, and how classified costs are turned into codes for a working cost-accounting system.","outcomes":["Define the classification of cost","Know the methods of cost classification","Explain the various patterns of cost behaviour","Explain the meaning of cost segregation","Explain the meaning of cost estimation","Analyse the effect of changing activity levels on unit costs","Explain cost codification"],"secs":[{"n":"2.1","t":"Introduction","b":[{"p":"Cost accounting is concerned with **controlling** operations, products or services. Cost control is possible only once cost classification has been introduced into business activities."},{"def":{"t":"Cost classification (CIMA Terminology)","d":"the arrangement of cost into logical groups concerning their nature (fixed, variable, value adding), function (production, selling) or use in the business of the entity."}},{"p":"This chapter is in three parts, following the study text's own structure: **classification** of cost (§2.1.1), **segregation** of cost (§2.1.2 to §2.1.5), and **codification** of cost (§2.3), with cost centres and cost units (§2.2) between them."},{"key":"The point of classifying a cost is never classification for its own sake — it is that a *different* question needs a *different* grouping. \"What did this product cost?\" needs the element and nature split. \"Which department overspent?\" needs the functional split. \"Should we accept this order?\" needs the relevant/irrelevant split. \"Whose fault is this overspend?\" needs the controllable/uncontrollable split. The same naira of expenditure sits in all of these at once."}]},{"n":"2.1.1","t":"Classification of cost","b":[{"def":{"t":"Classification","d":"the practice of grouping costs according to their common characteristics — the same cost may be classified in several different ways, for different purposes."}},{"h4":"(a) Classification according to elements of cost"},{"p":"Cost can be classified according to the three basic elements of cost: **material**, **labour** and **expenses**."},{"table":{"align":"lll","head":["Element","Definition","Typical examples"],"rows":[["**(i) Material costs**","Costs of materials and commodities **other than fixed assets**, introduced into a product or consumed in the operations of an organisation. Alternatively, the costs of material input into the production of goods and services.","Raw materials; component parts; work in progress; primary packing materials; lubricating oil; consumable tools; cleaning materials; stationery."],["**(ii) Labour costs**","The costs of employee remuneration — payments made to and on behalf of employees for offering services in the production or service function.","Wages; salaries; and employer-borne costs paid on employees' behalf."],["**(iii) Expenses**","Costs other than material costs and labour costs.","Cost of hiring special equipment and maintaining it; royalty payments; copyright and patent payments; cost of utilities such as electricity and water; rent."]]}},{"note":"Note the exclusion built into the definition of material cost: **fixed assets are not materials.** A machine bought for the factory is capital expenditure; the lubricating oil that keeps it running is a material cost. This single word is the basis of a recurring examination multiple-choice question (see Q4 in §2.6)."},{"h4":"(b) Classification according to nature — direct and indirect costs"},{"p":"This is an important method of cost classification. Costs can be classified as **direct** or **indirect** based on their nature, i.e. on whether they can be identified with units, jobs, processes or cost centres."},{"def":{"t":"Direct costs","d":"costs that can be directly identified and charged to a cost unit **without apportionment**. They are made up of direct material cost, direct labour cost and direct expenses."}},{"table":{"align":"lll","head":["Direct cost","Definition","Typical examples"],"rows":[["**(i) Direct material costs**","Costs of material that can be **physically identified** with a specific cost unit — materials entering into and becoming constituent elements of a product or saleable service. Conveniently **allocated** to cost units.","Materials specifically purchased for a particular job order or process; materials requisitioned from store for a particular production order; work-in-progress and component parts; primary packing materials (cartons, wrapping materials)."],["**(ii) Direct labour (wages) costs**","Costs of employees' efforts and skills applied **directly** to a product or saleable service; allocated directly to cost units.","Wages of production operatives transforming raw materials into finished goods; wages of waiters who serve meals at restaurants; wages of sales assistants in supermarkets."],["**(iii) Direct expenses**","Costs other than material and labour cost which can be identified with, and charged or allocated to, a cost unit — i.e. costs incurred for a specific product or saleable service.","Cost of hiring a piece of special equipment for a particular production order; maintenance cost of that hired equipment; royalty payments; travelling expenses to contract sites."]]}},{"note":"Some materials which could qualify as **direct** materials may be treated as **indirect** materials for reasons of **materiality and convenience**. The glue in a chair is physically part of the finished chair, but no one meters glue per chair; it costs more to track than the information is worth, so it is swept into production overhead instead."},{"fbox":{"h":"Prime cost","rows":[{"lb":"Prime cost","tex":"\\text{Prime cost} = \\text{Direct material} + \\text{Direct labour} + \\text{Direct expenses}","nt":"The aggregate of direct materials, direct labour and direct expenses."}]}},{"def":{"t":"Indirect costs (overheads)","d":"all costs that **cannot** be identified with and allocated to a cost unit, but which have to be **apportioned** to a number of cost centres and further **absorbed** by cost units."}},{"table":{"align":"lll","head":["Indirect cost","Definition","Typical examples"],"rows":[["**(i) Indirect material costs**","Costs of material items that cannot be identified with any particular product, because they are used for the benefit of **all** products instead of a specific one. Apportioned and absorbed, never allocated.","Materials required for operating and maintaining plant and equipment — lubricating oil, consumable tools; stationery; cleaning materials — soap and detergents, rugs and dusters, brooms and brushes."],["**(ii) Indirect labour costs**","Wages of employees who do not work on a specific product itself, though they assist in the manufacturing process. Apportioned to cost centres and then related to cost units through absorption.","Salaries of factory supervisors; wages of store department employees; wages of cleaners."],["**(iii) Indirect expenses**","Expenses incurred in general and not for the production of a specific cost unit.","**Selling and distribution** — advertising, sales promotion. **Administrative** — stationery, audit and consultancy fees, secretarial charges. **Production** — rent, insurance, electricity."]]}},{"key":"Three verbs do all the work in this topic, and the exam tests whether you use them precisely. **Allocate** — charge a whole cost to one cost centre or cost unit, because it belongs wholly to it (direct costs, and overheads attributable to one centre). **Apportion** — split a shared cost between several cost centres on some fair basis. **Absorb** — charge the overhead accumulated in a cost centre out to the cost units that passed through it. Direct costs are allocated; indirect costs are apportioned, then absorbed. Chapter 5 is this sentence in detail."},{"p":"When classification according to **element** and **nature** is combined, a cost build-up (cost card) appears as follows:"},{"stmt":{"t":"Cost card — elements combined with nature","rows":[["Direct material cost","xx"],["Direct labour cost","xx"],["Direct expenses","xx"],["**Prime cost**","xxx","@t"],["Indirect material cost","xx"],["Indirect labour cost","xx"],["Indirect expenses","xx"],["**Overhead cost**","xxx","@t"],["**Total cost**","xxx","@tt"]]}},{"h4":"(c) Classification according to function"},{"p":"All overhead cost can be classified by the function it serves:"},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Function","What it covers"],"rows":[["**Production (factory/works/manufacturing) overheads**","The indirect costs of manufacturing a cost unit — indirect materials consumed in the factory, indirect factory wages, and other indirect expenses connected with production."],["**Selling overheads**","Marketing costs incurred in securing orders, e.g. sales promotion cost."],["**Marketing overheads**","Costs of publicising and presenting products to customers in attractive form at an acceptable price — comprising selling costs, distribution costs and other related costs (research work, securing orders and delivering goods, and after-sales service)."],["**Distribution overheads**","Costs of making finished goods ready for dispatch and delivery to customers, e.g. carriage outwards."],["**Administrative overheads**","Costs of formulating policy and directing/controlling operations that are not directly related to production, selling, distribution or research and development."],["**Research and development overheads**","Costs of seeking new ideas, alternative materials, improved production methods, improved products, and designing innovative ideas."]]}},{"p":"When classification according to elements of cost, **nature** and **function** are all combined, the revised cost build-up / cost card appears as follows:"},{"stmt":{"t":"Cost card — elements, nature and function combined","rows":[["Direct material cost","xxx",""],["Direct labour cost","xxx",""],["Direct expenses","xxx",""],["**Prime cost**","xxx","","@t"],["Indirect production material cost","xxx",""],["Indirect production labour cost","xxx",""],["Indirect production expenses","xxx",""],["**Production overheads**","xxx","","@t"],["**Production cost**","","xxx","@b"],"@gap",["Selling overheads","xxx",""],["Distribution overheads","xxx",""],["Administrative overheads","xxx","xxx"],["**Total cost**","","xxx","@t"],["Profit margin","","xxx"],["**Selling price**","","xxx","@tt"]]}},{"key":"This single card is the skeleton of the whole subject, and it is worth being able to draw it from memory. Read it downwards and you can see exactly where every later chapter plugs in: Chapter 3 fills in direct **materials**, Chapter 4 direct **labour**, Chapter 5 the **overhead** lines (and how they get there — allocation, apportionment, absorption), Chapters 7 to 10 are four different **methods** of accumulating the top half for different kinds of business, and Chapter 11 asks what happens to the **profit margin** at the bottom when volume changes."},{"note":"Watch the two intermediate subtotals, which examiners use to catch people out. **Production cost** stops after production overheads — selling, distribution and administrative overheads come *below* it, so they are **not** in the figure used to value closing inventory. **Total cost** includes them. Mixing the two up misvalues inventory and misstates profit."},{"h4":"(d) Classification according to behaviour"},{"p":"Costs may or may not change in sympathy with activity. Four behaviour classes:"},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Type","Meaning"],"rows":[["**Fixed cost**","Does not vary with changes in activity level — it changes instead with the passage of time (e.g. rent and rates, the managing director's salary). Sometimes called a **period cost**."],["**Variable cost**","Varies in *direct proportion* with changes in activity level (e.g. raw materials cost, direct wages, direct expenses such as royalties)."],["**Semi-fixed/semi-variable (mixed) cost**","Contains both fixed and variable elements — over a given range of activity the cost stays constant, but beyond that range it varies in direct proportion with activity (e.g. electricity, water, telephone)."],["**Stepped fixed cost**","Fixed over a given range of activity, but changes discretely (in a jump) for ranges of activity beyond the given range."]]}},{"h4":"(e) Classification as product costs or period costs"},{"def":{"t":"Product costs","d":"costs identified with goods produced or purchased for resale — also called production/manufacturing costs. Used to value inventory and work in progress. Examples: raw materials cost, production wages, production overheads (electricity, plant depreciation, factory rent)."}},{"ul":["**Expired product costs** — the portion of product cost relating to products that have already realised revenue, and have no future revenue-generating potential.","**Unexpired product costs** — costs of resources acquired that are expected to contribute to *future* revenue (e.g. the cost of materials not yet sold). Once the inventory is sold and revenue realised, the cost is said to have \"expired\" and is recorded in the profit and loss account."]},{"def":{"t":"Period costs","d":"costs incurred and charged against profit for a period, not included in inventory valuation — usually non-manufacturing costs, e.g. selling/distribution overheads and administrative overheads. Charged in full to the profit and loss account for the relevant period."}},{"key":"Product costs can themselves be analysed as expired or unexpired; period costs are never part of inventory valuation in the first place — the two classifications answer different questions (what a cost *is*, versus *when* it hits the P&L)."},{"h4":"(f) Classification as controllable and uncontrollable costs/revenue"},{"def":{"t":"Controllable cost/revenue","d":"cost/revenue reasonably amenable to regulation by a given responsibility-centre manager — its incidence is influenced by that manager's actions or inactions (e.g. raw materials purchased in bulk; variable costs generally are prone to control)."}},{"def":{"t":"Uncontrollable cost","d":"cost that cannot be influenced by the actions of a specified responsibility-centre manager (e.g. factory rent, managers' salaries)."}},{"note":"All costs/revenues are controllable **at some level of management** — but lower down the hierarchy, some become uncontrollable at that manager's level. Control reports must always classify a cost as controllable or not **relative to the manager receiving the report**."},{"h4":"(g) Classification as relevant/irrelevant costs"},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Type","Meaning / examples"],"rows":[["**Relevant costs/revenue**","Future costs/revenues that *can* be altered by a given decision — future/predetermined costs, opportunity costs, avoidable costs, incremental costs."],["**Irrelevant costs/revenue**","Costs/revenues that will *not* be affected by a given decision, whatever is decided — past costs, sunk costs, unavoidable costs, fixed costs (except any incremental fixed cost element)."]]}},{"h4":"(h) Other classifications of cost"},{"p":"A large glossary of further cost terms, each defined precisely:"},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Term","Meaning"],"rows":[["**Avoidable costs**","Costs that may be saved by adopting a given alternative option."],["**Non-avoidable (unavoidable) costs**","Costs that cannot be saved or eliminated by adopting a given alternative line of action."],["**Normal costs**","Costs planned for and expected at a given activity level under specified conditions, e.g. normal scrap, normal loss of materials, expected idle time."],["**Abnormal costs**","Costs not planned for and not expected at a given activity level under normal conditions, e.g. excessive scrap, abnormal idle-time pay."],["**Sunk costs**","Costs of resources already acquired — created by past decisions and unalterable by any future decision, e.g. the written-down value of a plant already bought."],["**Opportunity costs**","The value of the benefit forgone or sacrificed in favour of an alternative course of action."],["**Incremental (differential) costs/revenue**","Additional costs or revenues arising from producing or selling a *group* of additional units."],["**Marginal costs/revenues**","Additional cost or revenue arising from producing *one* additional unit of output or service."],["**Future costs**","Estimated costs reasonably expected to be incurred in the future."],["**Replacement costs**","The estimated cost at which an identical item can currently be acquired or produced."],["**Conversion costs**","The cost of transforming raw materials into finished goods, or of converting materials from one production stage to the next — total production cost less the cost of bought-in materials."],["**Attributable costs**","Costs that can be directly associated with a particular project, programme or cost centre."],["**Discretionary costs**","Non-essential costs — a business unit can survive without them."],["**Development costs**","Costs of research (exploration) into improving the production of goods and services."],["**Pre-production costs**","The part of development costs relating to a trial production run, as a preliminary step before formal production."],["**Committed costs**","Costs expected to be incurred, for which resources have been earmarked as a result of a contractual agreement or an earlier decision."],["**Value added**","The increase in a product's market value from changing its form, location, etc. — total market value less the cost of bought-in materials and services."],["**Pre-determined costs**","Costs estimated and computed *in advance* of the production process, based on the specification of every factor affecting cost."],["**Standard costs**","Costs estimated and expected to be incurred per unit of activity, under efficient production conditions."],["**Budgeted costs**","Costs estimated and planned for a given activity level, function or segment, within a specified time horizon."],["**Actual costs**","The costs actually incurred in production — historical, past costs, never estimates."]]}}]},{"n":"2.1.2","t":"Cost segregation","b":[{"p":"This part of the chapter covers the different **behavioural patterns of cost**. After completing it you should be able to:"},{"ol":["explain the problems associated with the conventional methods of cost classification;","explain the different cost patterns; and","explain the factors that influence cost behaviour."]},{"def":{"t":"Cost segregation","d":"the separation of a mixed (semi-variable) cost into its fixed component and its variable component, so that each can be treated according to the way it actually behaves."}},{"def":{"t":"Cost estimation","d":"the use of a cost function derived from past data to predict what a cost will be at some future activity level."}},{"key":"Segregation and estimation are two halves of one exercise. You **segregate** a past mixed cost into $a$ (fixed) and $b$ (variable per unit) to obtain the cost function $y = a + bx$; you then **estimate** a future cost by substituting the planned activity level into that function. §2.1.2a below works this through numerically."}]},{"n":"2.1.2a","t":"Segregating a semi-variable cost — the high-low method","b":[{"warn":"The study text names cost segregation in this chapter's own title and objectives, and sets an examination question on it (Q11 below), but never actually shows the method — it says only that \"some methods could be applied to separate fixed and variable costs: statistical methods, non-statistical methods (judgement)\". This section fills that gap with the **high-low method**, which is the technique the examiner's own question requires and which is standard, uncontroversial ATSWA material. It is also used again in Chapter 11 (cost-volume-profit analysis) and Chapter 13 (standard costing).","lbl":"Filling a gap in the study text"},{"p":"A semi-variable cost has the form:"},{"tex":"y = a + bx"},{"p":"where $y$ is total cost, $a$ is the fixed element, $b$ is the variable cost per unit of activity and $x$ is the activity level. Two unknowns ($a$ and $b$) need two equations, so the method takes **two observations** — the highest and lowest *activity* levels — and solves them simultaneously."},{"steps":["Identify the period with the **highest activity** and the period with the **lowest activity**. Choose on *activity*, not on cost — they usually coincide, but where they do not, activity is what drives the cost, so activity decides.","Find the **difference in cost** and the **difference in activity** between those two periods only. Every naira of that cost difference must be variable, because the fixed element is identical in both periods and cancels out.","Divide: variable cost per unit $b = \\dfrac{\\text{change in cost}}{\\text{change in activity}}$.","Substitute $b$ back into **either** observation to find the fixed cost: $a = y - bx$. Do it for both observations as a check — they must give the same $a$.","State the cost function $y = a + bx$, then substitute the required future activity level to **estimate** the cost."]},{"fbox":{"h":"High-low method","rows":[{"lb":"Variable cost per unit","tex":"b = \\frac{\\text{Cost at highest activity} - \\text{Cost at lowest activity}}{\\text{Highest activity} - \\text{Lowest activity}}"},{"lb":"Fixed cost","tex":"a = \\text{Total cost at any level} - (b \\times \\text{that activity level})"},{"lb":"Estimated cost at activity $x$","tex":"y = a + bx"}]}},{"note":"The high-low method uses only **two** of the available observations and ignores the rest, so a single freak month at the top or bottom of the range distorts the whole answer. That is its one real weakness, and the reason the statistical alternative — **least-squares regression**, which uses every observation — is preferred where the data warrant it. The examiner at this level almost always wants high-low, because it can be done without a calculator."}]},{"n":"2.1.3","t":"Problems associated with the conventional classification of cost according to behaviour","b":[{"ol":["assuming **cost linearity** for variable cost per unit is not always practical — a variable cost may just as well be curvilinear (non-linear) as linear;","a cost's behaviour should be established by **analysing that specific cost**, not by an overall simplistic assumption;","fixed cost can itself change as activity levels change, and some costs (e.g. depreciation) could be classified as fixed *or* variable depending on the organisation."]},{"key":"Because fixed cost is not really a single homogeneous thing, it is better to sub-divide it into four categories: **time-related** fixed cost (rent, rates — changes with the passage of time, not activity); **volume-related** fixed cost (behaves like a step cost, e.g. the cost of a plant); **policy-related (programmed)** fixed cost (advertising, R&D — set by management decision, not by volume); and **joint** fixed cost."},{"p":"Some cost items genuinely mix fixed and variable elements, and must be separated using either **statistical methods** or **non-statistical methods (judgement)**. Cost patterns are not always regular either — a variable cost might be linear only between 90%–115% of normal activity, and curvilinear beyond that; different variable costs also react to *different* activity measures (direct wages may vary with the number of direct workers engaged, distribution costs with the number of deliveries made) — the simplistic assumption that every variable cost varies with the *same* activity measure is misleading. In short: the conventional fixed/variable split, defined by one factor (production volume), is at best a crude approximation of reality."}]},{"n":"2.1.4","t":"Typical cost patterns","b":[{"p":"Six cost-behaviour shapes turn up repeatedly, each with its own graph and its own cost function:"},{"table":{"align":"lll","head":["Pattern","Cost function","Examples / implication"],"rows":[["**Linear variable cost**","$y = bx$ ($b$ = variable cost per unit, the slope)","Direct materials cost; royalties per unit of production; power usage (excluding any fixed element). A perfectly linear variable cost across *every* activity level is unlikely — more realistically it is linear only over the *relevant range*."],["**Curvilinear (convex) variable cost**","—","An extra unit of activity causes a *less-than-proportionate* increase in cost — economies of scale are operating."],["**Curvilinear (concave) variable cost**","$y = bx + cx^2 + \\dots + px^n$ ($b,c,p$ constants; $x$ = activity level)","An extra unit of activity causes a *more-than-proportionate* increase in cost — diminishing returns are operating. This family of functions is called a \"polynomial\" cost function."],["**Fixed cost**","$y = a$","Rates, rent, time-based depreciation, salaries — constant regardless of activity level (within the normal activity range)."],["**Linear semi-variable cost**","$y = a + bx$","Electricity; telephone charges; computer bureau costs; some piecework wage schemes with a guaranteed minimum payment."],["**Curvilinear semi-variable cost**","$y = a + bx + cx^2 + \\dots + px^n$","Same family of costs as above, but where the variable portion itself behaves non-linearly."],["**Stepped cost**","—","Supervisory salaries; bought-in materials where bulk discounts apply — constant over a range, then jumps to a new constant level."]]}},{"fbox":{"h":"Cost-behaviour functions","rows":[{"lb":"Linear variable cost","tex":"y = bx"},{"lb":"Polynomial (curvilinear) cost","tex":"y = bx + cx^2 + \\dots + px^n"},{"lb":"Fixed cost","tex":"y = a"},{"lb":"Linear semi-variable cost","tex":"y = a + bx"},{"lb":"Curvilinear semi-variable cost","tex":"y = a + bx + cx^2 + \\dots + px^n"}]}}]},{"n":"2.1.4a","t":"Factors influencing cost behaviour","b":[{"ul":["**Activity level** — sales volume, production volume, hours of work, number of invoices processed, etc.;","**Time** — the passage of time itself; and","**Non-volume factors** — technology, methods of production, levels of efficiency, price levels."]}]},{"n":"2.1.5","t":"Analysis of the effect of changing activity levels on costs","b":[{"p":"\"Level of activity\" is sometimes described by alternative terms depending on the business — throughput, output, volume, capacity. Unit costs do **not** stay the same across different activity levels, since this is the very basis on which costs are classified fixed/variable in the first place — care is needed in monitoring cost behaviour at each level."},{"warn":"A fixed cost stays fixed only up to a certain level of activity, then changes — factory rent is fixed up to the factory's maximum capacity, but any output beyond that may need an additional factory (and hence more fixed cost). Even variable costs are not immune: unit material cost may fall at high volumes because of quantity discounts. Treat every \"fixed\" or \"variable\" label as valid only within a relevant range, not universally."}]},{"n":"2.2","t":"Cost centre and cost unit","b":[{"def":{"t":"Cost centre (CIMA)","d":"a production or service location, function, activity or item of equipment for which costs are accumulated."}},{"p":"More broadly: a location, a person, an item of equipment, or a group of these, to which costs are incurred and then related to cost units — e.g. the factory, the canteen, the maintenance section, the foreman, the managing director, the plant, the pool of computers. Cost centres are broadly:"},{"ul":["**Production cost centres**","**Service cost centres**"]},{"key":"The manager of any cost centre is accountable for the outcome achieved within it — each centre is evaluated on the performance it achieves."},{"def":{"t":"Cost unit (CIMA)","d":"a unit of product or service in relation to which costs are ascertained."}},{"p":"A cost unit is a *quantitative* unit of product or service to which costs are ascertained — a selection of common cost units by industry:"},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Industry","Cost unit"],"rows":[["Brewing","Barrel"],["Coal mining","Tunnel/ton"],["Electricity","Kilowatt hour (kW/hr)"],["Oil","Barrel/tonne, litre"],["Education","Course, enrolled student"],["Paper","Ream"],["Hospitals","Bed-day"],["Telephone","Calls made, number of extensions"]]}},{"def":{"t":"Profit centre","d":"a centre which not only incurs cost, but also generates income — unlike a plain cost centre, which only incurs cost."}}]},{"n":"2.3","t":"Codification","b":[{"p":"Classifying and controlling cost is not complete until codes are applied to classified cost items, for simplicity."},{"def":{"t":"Code","d":"a brief, accurate reference designed to assist the classification of items, by facilitating entry, collation and analysis."}},{"h4":"Characteristics of a well-designed coding system"},{"ul":["simple for the organisation to operate;","flexible in nature;","easy to remember;","a **fixed coding length** for every item, e.g. a 6-digit code such as 000000;","concise in nature."]},{"h4":"Methods of codification"},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Method","How it works"],"rows":[["**Mnemonic method**","Applies memorable symbols to classified items, e.g. `FAU` for Factory Unit, `ADM` for Administration, `DMA` for Direct Material, `DLA` for Direct Labour."],["**Decimal method**","A whole number identifies a master group, and a decimal identifies the primary/secondary groups within it — e.g. 1 Direct Materials, 2 Direct Labour, 3 Direct Expenses, 4 Factory Overheads (4.1 Indirect material in the factory, 4.2 Consumable stores), 5 Indirect Labour (5.1 Factory labour, 5.2 Factory cleaners)."],["**Numerical method**","Applies fixed-length digit strings to classified items, each digit position (or block of digits) standing for one dimension of the item."]]}},{"p":"Worked example of the numerical method: suppose an 8-digit code is designed for types of wood, laid out as `T LLL WW TT` (1 digit for wood type, 3 for length, 2 for width, 2 for thickness):"},{"table":{"align":"llll","head":["Type of wood","Length","Width","Thickness"],"rows":[["0","000","00","00"]]}},{"eg":{"tag":"Study text","t":"Coding Iroko and Mahogany wood","open":true,"q":[{"p":"Iroko wood measuring 12 feet in length, 2 inches in width and 1 inch in thickness is to be coded, followed by Mahogany wood of the identical dimensions."}],"a":[{"p":"Iroko wood, 12 feet by 2 inches by 1 inch → **Code 1 1 4 4 0 4 0 4** (wood type digit \"1\" for Iroko, followed by the length/width/thickness digits)."},{"p":"Mahogany wood, the same dimensions (12 feet by 2 inches by 1 inch) → **Code 2 1 4 4 0 4 0 4** (only the leading wood-type digit changes, to \"2\" for Mahogany)."},{"p":"Only the code itself needs to be disclosed to staff handling the materials — the underlying names and actual dimensions of the wood can be kept confidential."}]}},{"note":"Coding is not limited to material *types* — it can equally be designed for material cost, labour cost and overhead, using the same three methods."}]},{"n":"2.4","t":"Chapter summary","b":[{"p":"Chapter 2 has been discussed in three parts. Part 1 focused on the **classification of cost**; part 2 explained **cost segregation**; and part 3 dealt with **cost codification**."},{"p":"In conclusion, this chapter has given a firm foundation of the various concepts and principles in cost accounting, to aid the comprehension of the chapters ahead."},{"key":"If you remember only three things from this chapter: (1) the **same naira** of expenditure is classified several different ways at once, and which classification matters depends on the question being asked; (2) **fixed and variable are only true within a relevant range** — the conventional split is a crude approximation; and (3) a mixed cost can be **segregated** into $a + bx$ by the high-low method, and the resulting function then **estimates** future cost."}]},{"n":"2.5","t":"Worksheet summary — definitions and structure","b":[{"p":"Everything in this chapter, compressed for a last read-through."},{"h3":"Section-by-section checklist"},{"ol":["**§2.1 Classification** — 8 ways to classify the same cost: by element (materials/labour/expenses), by nature (direct/indirect → prime cost/overhead), by function (production/selling/marketing/distribution/administrative/R&D overheads), by behaviour (fixed/variable/semi-variable/stepped), as product vs period cost (+ expired/unexpired), as controllable/uncontrollable (relative to a management level), as relevant/irrelevant to a decision, plus a long glossary of named cost types (avoidable, sunk, opportunity, incremental, marginal, conversion, standard, budgeted, actual, etc.).","**§2.2 Segregation and behaviour** — the conventional fixed/variable split is a crude approximation (3 named problems); fixed cost itself splits 4 ways (time-related, volume-related, policy-related/programmed, joint); 7 named cost-behaviour patterns with their functions ($y=bx$, $y=a$, $y=a+bx$, stepped, and their curvilinear/polynomial forms); 3 factors driving behaviour (activity level, time, non-volume factors); a fixed cost is only fixed within a relevant range.","**§2.3 Cost centre/unit/profit centre** — a cost centre accumulates cost (production or service); a cost unit is the quantitative unit costs attach to (with named cost units by industry); a profit centre both incurs cost **and** generates income.","**§2.4 Codification** — codes make classified cost simple to enter/collate/analyse; 5 characteristics of a good code; 3 codification methods (mnemonic, decimal, numerical)."]},{"h3":"B. Cost-behaviour formula box"},{"fbox":{"h":"Cost-behaviour functions (recap)","rows":[{"lb":"Linear variable cost","tex":"y = bx"},{"lb":"Fixed cost","tex":"y = a"},{"lb":"Linear semi-variable cost","tex":"y = a + bx"},{"lb":"Polynomial (curvilinear) cost","tex":"y = bx + cx^2 + \\dots + px^n"}]}}]},{"n":"2.6","t":"Study text questions — multiple-choice and short-answer","b":[{"eg":{"tag":"Study text","t":"Multiple-choice and short-answer questions, with answers","open":true,"q":[{"ol":["Cost accounting involves (A) Drawing up the balance sheet  (B) Writing off costs  (C) Ascertaining of cost  (D) Preparation of statement of value added  (E) Annual audit of financial statements","Cost accounting is an integral part of (A) Financial accounting  (B) Forensic accounting  (C) Treasury accounting  (D) Historical accounting  (E) Management accounting","Which of the following is NOT an objective of cost accounting? (A) Provision of information to aid control  (B) Ascertainment of cost in order to facilitate pricing  (C) Provision of information for decision making  (D) Investigation of fraud  (E) Provision of assistance in planning","Material costs do NOT include the cost of (A) Fixed assets  (B) Raw materials  (C) Work in progress  (D) Packing materials  (E) Cleaning materials","Which one of the following is a direct expense? (A) Cost of hiring a special equipment for a particular production order  (B) Advertising expenses  (C) Electricity expenses  (D) Insurance premiums  ~(printed in the study text as option E — see the note with the answers)~","Classifying costs by elements includes materials, labour and …………………..","The addition of all direct costs is known as ………………………………..","Costs which are fixed for a given range of activity level but which change discretely for ranges of activity levels beyond the given ranges are called ………….","Costs which may be saved by the adoption of a given alternative option are known as ……………………","Costs which vary in direct proportion with changes in activity levels are called ……………………"]}],"a":[{"ol":["**C — ascertaining of cost.** *(The study text's own answer key says \"A — drawing up the balance sheet\", which is plainly wrong: drawing up the balance sheet is financial accounting. See the note below.)*","**E — management accounting.**","**D — investigation of fraud** is not one of the six objectives listed in §1.2.3.","**A — fixed assets.** Material costs are, by definition, the costs of materials and commodities **other than fixed assets** (§2.1.1a).","**A — the cost of hiring special equipment for a particular production order.** It is incurred for one specific production order, so it can be identified with and charged directly to that cost unit. Advertising, electricity and insurance are all general indirect expenses. *(The study text's answer key says \"B — advertising expenses\", which is wrong — advertising is a selling overhead, the textbook example of an **indirect** expense.)*","**Expenses.** The three elements are material, labour and expenses.","**Prime cost.**","**Stepped fixed costs.**","**Avoidable costs.**","**Variable costs.**"]},{"warn":"Two of the study text's own printed answers to this question set are wrong. Its key gives **1 = A** and **5 = B**; the correct answers are **1 = C** and **5 = A**, for the reasons given above. Question 5 is also printed with only four options lettered **A, B, C and E** — option D is missing from the book altogether; the fourth option has been relettered D above so the question can be attempted normally.","lbl":"Errors in the study text's answer key"}]}}]},{"n":"2.7","t":"Study text examination-type questions, worked","b":[{"p":"The study text closes the chapter with eleven examination-type questions. All are reproduced below with full answers. Note that **questions 6 and 7 are identical** in the book — the same question is printed twice, once unattributed and once credited to CACA Level I Costing, June 1989 — so they are answered once here."},{"eg":{"tag":"Exam type — Q1 (16 marks)","t":"Six principal items of information obtainable from a costing system","q":[{"p":"You have been appointed to the newly created position of Cost Accountant in a small manufacturing company. Having installed a costing system successfully, the Managing Director would like a detailed explanation of the kind of information the newly installed system would provide. Write a report to the Managing Director setting out **six** principal items of information obtainable from a costing system."}],"a":[{"p":"**REPORT**  —  To: The Managing Director.  From: The Cost Accountant.  Subject: Information obtainable from the newly installed costing system."},{"p":"The following six classes of information are produced by the system and are available to management on a routine basis."},{"ol":["**Statements of material, direct labour and machine utilisation**, showing both trends and values — so that the use of each major resource can be monitored period by period.","**The cost of products and services**, broken down into material cost, labour cost and other expenses, and shown in comparison with estimates — the basis for pricing and for checking whether estimates are reliable.","**Cost control statements for each section and department**, showing actual results compared with budget, so that responsibility for any overspend can be located.","**Periodic inventory valuation reports** — the value of raw materials, work-in-progress and finished goods held, needed both for control and for the financial accounts.","**Scrap and rectification cost reports**, quantifying what defective work is costing the company and where it arises.","**Budgets for planning and control purposes**, including cash budgets for monitoring liquidity."]},{"note":"This is Chapter 1 §1.3.4 reproduced as a report. In the exam, the marks for a \"write a report\" question are split between **content** (the six items) and **form** — use a proper report heading (to, from, subject, date), number the points, and close with a short recommendation or offer to discuss."}]}},{"eg":{"tag":"Exam type — Q2 (8 marks)","t":"Problems inherent in installing a costing system","q":[{"p":"\"Installation of a costing system is a major move in a business.\" Discuss the inherent problems associated with installing a costing system."}],"a":[{"p":"Installing a costing system is a substantial undertaking requiring detailed discussion with management and staff and careful analysis of procedures and records. The problems that arise are of two kinds."},{"h4":"General factors that shape the problem"},{"ol":["**Size of the organisation** — a system adequate for a small workshop collapses under the transaction volume of a large factory, and one designed for a large factory is uneconomic for a small one.","**Type of product or service** — a jobbing manufacturer, a continuous-process plant and a service provider each need a fundamentally different costing method.","**The production process** and **the methods of manufacture** — these determine whether costs can be collected by job, by batch, by process or by operation.","**Availability of staff** — a system no one is competent to operate will not work, however well designed."]},{"h4":"Specific problems met in practice"},{"ol":["**Definition of responsibility** — deciding who is accountable for which costs, which often exposes gaps and overlaps in the existing management structure.","**Definition or designation of cost centres** — drawing the boundaries so that costs can be meaningfully accumulated and controlled.","**Compilation of a comprehensive cost system** covering every element without leaving costs uncollected.","**Labour hours worked** — obtaining accurate, timely time records from the shop floor.","**Machine utilisation time** — measuring running and idle time reliably.","**Scrap treatment** and **rectification cost** — deciding how defective output and the cost of putting it right are to be recorded and charged.","**The difficulty of accurate classification of cost** — in particular the direct/indirect and fixed/variable boundaries, which are rarely as clean in practice as in a textbook."]},{"key":"Underlying all of these is a **human** problem the examiner rewards you for naming: a costing system makes performance visible, so staff may resist it, suspect it, or supply poor data to it. That is why §1.2.9 puts \"co-operation and co-ordination among all staff members\" first among the conditions for success, and why §1.2.10 ends with training and \"implement the system with full publicity\"."}]}},{"eg":{"tag":"Exam type — Q3 (10 marks)","t":"Problems from introducing a system of cost control","q":[{"p":"Outline the possible problems which may be encountered as a result of the introduction of a system of cost control into an organisation."}],"a":[{"ol":["**Staff resistance and low morale** — cost control makes individual and departmental performance visible, and employees may perceive it as surveillance or as a prelude to redundancies.","**Poor-quality source data** — the system depends on shop-floor returns (time sheets, material requisitions, scrap notes). Where staff complete these carelessly or defensively, every subsequent analysis is unreliable.","**Cost of the system itself** — additional staff, forms, software and management time. If the information generated does not outweigh the cost of generating it, the system fails its own cost-benefit test (§1.2.12).","**Complexity** — a system too complex for the managers who must operate it will not be used, or will be used wrongly.","**Dysfunctional behaviour** — managers judged on a single cost measure may cut costs in ways that damage the business (deferring maintenance, buying cheaper and worse material, suppressing scrap reports).","**Disputes over controllability** — managers held responsible for costs they cannot influence (apportioned head-office overhead, for instance) will reject the reports altogether; see §2.1.1f.","**Delay** — reports produced too late to act on lose their relevance entirely (§1.2.12).","**Difficulty in setting realistic standards and budgets** against which actual performance is compared — standards that are too tight demotivate, and standards that are too loose conceal inefficiency."]}]}},{"eg":{"tag":"Exam type — Q4 (8 marks)","t":"Differences between financial and cost accounting","q":[{"p":"Enumerate the differences, if any, between financial and cost accounting."}],"a":[{"table":{"align":"lll","head":["Factor","Cost accounting","Financial accounting"],"rows":[["**Nature of costs / revenue**","Predetermined estimates, standards and budgets","Historical and past costs / revenue"],["**Users of information**","Management of the business enterprise","Management as well as external stakeholders — shareholders, creditors, debtors, tax authorities, investors"],["**Objectives**","Provide information to aid planning, decision making and control","Satisfy the stewardship function of management, i.e. account for the use of resources entrusted to management"],["**Conformity to concepts and standards**","No need to comply with standards and concepts","Absolute need to comply meticulously with accounting concepts and standards"],["**Scope and form of presentation**","Determined by management on the basis of relevance and cost-benefit considerations","Determined by concepts, standards and legal provisions"],["**Precision of information**","Approximations are permitted, due to uncertainties in making estimates, but must be sufficiently precise and accurate","Information must be more precise; there is usually an audit by independent external auditors to confirm its reliability"]]}},{"p":"Two further differences worth adding for the extra marks:"},{"ul":["**Frequency** — cost accounting reports as often as management needs them (daily, weekly, monthly); financial accounting reports at fixed statutory intervals, normally annually.","**Level of detail** — cost accounting reports by job, product, process and cost centre; financial accounting reports on the entity as a whole."]}]}},{"eg":{"tag":"Exam type — Q5 (10 marks)","t":"Report: why the cost accounting staff should be retained","q":[{"p":"The Managing Director of a manufacturing concern once said: \"Since the main object of our firm is to know the annual profit, there is no need retaining the cost accounting staff; let us pay them off and retain only the financial accounting staff in order to reduce the administrative costs.\" **Required:** Write a report to the Managing Director to convince him of the need to retain the cost accounting staff."}],"a":[{"p":"**REPORT**  —  To: The Managing Director.  From: The Cost Accountant.  Subject: The case for retaining the cost accounting function."},{"h4":"1.  The premise of the proposal is too narrow"},{"p":"Knowing the annual profit tells us *that* we made money; it does not tell us *how*, *where*, or *whether we could have made more*. The annual profit figure is the **result** of decisions that cost accounting exists to inform in the first place."},{"h4":"2.  Financial accounting alone cannot fill the gap"},{"p":"Financial accounting suffers five well-recognised weaknesses:"},{"ol":["it gives **no information about individual jobs, products or processes**, so we could not tell a profitable product line from a loss-making one;","it is **historical** — information is not available until the accounting period ends, by which time any damage is months old;","it does **not classify costs** into direct and indirect, controllable and non-controllable, normal and abnormal, or variable and fixed — the very classifications every operating decision needs;","it keeps **no detailed records of inventory movement**, which inhibits efficient inventory control; and","it generally involves **no budget plan**, so there is nothing to compare actual performance against."]},{"h4":"3.  What we would lose"},{"ol":["**Pricing** would become guesswork, since we would not know the cost of a unit.","**Planning** would lose its basis — no budgets, no capital-expenditure evaluation, no cash-flow projection.","**Control** would disappear — no variance reports, so overspends would surface only in next year's accounts.","**Decision making** — make-or-buy, accept-or-reject a special order, continue or discontinue a product line — would have no cost basis.","**Inventory control and scrap control** would weaken, with direct cash consequences."]},{"h4":"4.  The saving is illusory"},{"p":"The administrative saving from paying off the cost accounting staff is a known, one-off, modest figure. The cost of mispricing a single major contract, of carrying excessive inventory for a year, or of continuing a loss-making product line unnoticed, is unknown and open-ended — and we would, by construction, have no means of detecting any of them."},{"h4":"5.  Recommendation"},{"p":"Retain the cost accounting function. If the objective is to reduce administrative cost, I would instead recommend reviewing the costing system for simplification, and considering an **integrated** accounting system (Chapter 6), which keeps one set of records instead of two and so removes duplicated effort without losing any management information."}]}},{"eg":{"tag":"Exam type — Q6 and Q7 (11 marks) — CACA Level I Costing, June 1989","t":"Three methods of cost classification and the usefulness of each","q":[{"p":"Describe **three** different methods of cost classification and explain the usefulness of each method."},{"note":"The study text prints this question twice, as Q6 and Q7 — identical wording, with only Q7 carrying the CACA attribution. It is answered once here."}],"a":[{"h4":"Method 1 — classification by **element** (material, labour, expenses)"},{"p":"**What it is:** every cost is assigned to one of the three basic inputs — material, labour, or expenses (everything else)."},{"p":"**Usefulness:** it is the basis of the cost card and therefore of all product costing; it allows each input to be controlled by the manager responsible for it (the storekeeper for materials, the personnel function for labour); and it makes inter-period and inter-firm comparison possible, since the same three headings apply to any manufacturer."},{"h4":"Method 2 — classification by **nature** (direct and indirect)"},{"p":"**What it is:** costs that can be identified with and charged to a cost unit without apportionment are **direct**; those that must be apportioned to cost centres and absorbed into cost units are **indirect** (overhead)."},{"p":"**Usefulness:** it determines how each cost reaches the product — allocation versus apportionment and absorption; it gives the two key subtotals, **prime cost** and **overhead**; and it is the foundation of absorption costing and of the whole of Chapter 5."},{"h4":"Method 3 — classification by **behaviour** (fixed, variable, semi-variable, stepped)"},{"p":"**What it is:** costs are grouped by how they respond to a change in the level of activity."},{"p":"**Usefulness:** it is indispensable for **planning and decision making** — budgeting at different activity levels (flexible budgets), cost-volume-profit and break-even analysis (Chapter 11), marginal costing, and any short-run decision such as accepting a special order, because only the variable element changes."},{"note":"Three other classifications would earn equal credit if described as fully: by **function** (production / selling / distribution / administration / R&D — useful for departmental responsibility and for separating product cost from period cost); as **product or period** cost (useful for inventory valuation and profit measurement); and as **relevant or irrelevant** (useful for decision making, since only future costs that change with the decision matter)."}]}},{"eg":{"tag":"Exam type — Q8 (17 marks) — CACA Level I Costing, June 1987","t":"Period, product, variable and opportunity costs, with two examples of each","q":[{"p":"The following are some of the cost classifications used in any costing system: (i) Period costs; (ii) Product costs; (iii) Variable costs; (iv) Opportunity costs. **Required:** Citing two examples in each case, explain each of these classified costs."}],"a":[{"table":{"align":"lll","head":["Classification","Explanation","Two examples"],"rows":[["**(i) Period costs**","Costs incurred and charged against the profit of a period, and **not** included in the cost used for inventory valuation. They are usually non-manufacturing costs, and are charged in full to the profit and loss account of the period in which they are incurred, whether or not any output was sold.","1. Selling and distribution overheads (e.g. advertising).  2. Administrative overheads (e.g. the managing director's salary)."],["**(ii) Product costs**","Costs identified with goods produced or purchased for resale — also called production or manufacturing costs. They are the costs used for the valuation of inventory and work-in-progress, and are charged against profit only when the goods are sold.","1. The cost of raw materials.  2. Production wages. *(Production overheads such as factory rent and plant depreciation are also product costs.)*"],["**(iii) Variable costs**","Costs which vary in direct proportion with changes in the level of activity — if activity doubles, total variable cost doubles, while variable cost **per unit** stays constant.","1. Direct material cost.  2. Royalties payable per unit produced. *(Direct wages paid on a piecework basis are a third.)*"],["**(iv) Opportunity costs**","The value of the benefit forgone or sacrificed in favour of an alternative course of action. It is not an outlay recorded in the books — it is a benefit given up — but it is a **relevant** cost for decision making.","1. The rent forgone by using an owned building for production rather than letting it. 2. The contribution lost on normal production displaced in order to fulfil a special order."]]}},{"key":"Note the trap built into (i) and (ii): period and product costs are **not** the same split as fixed and variable. Factory rent is a *fixed* cost but a *product* cost (it is absorbed into inventory under absorption costing); a sales commission is a *variable* cost but a *period* cost. The product/period split asks \"does this cost attach to the goods?\"; the fixed/variable split asks \"does this cost change with volume?\" They are independent questions."}]}},{"eg":{"tag":"Exam type — Q9 (22 marks) — ICSA Management Accounting, December 1984","t":"Cost may be classified in a variety of ways according to their nature and the information needs of management","q":[{"p":"\"Cost may be classified in a variety of ways according to their nature and the information needs of management.\" With relevant illustrative examples, provide a detailed explanation of the above statement."}],"a":[{"p":"The statement makes two linked points: that a single cost can be classified in several different ways at the same time, and that **which classification is used depends on what management needs to know.** Each classification below is matched with the management question it answers."},{"table":{"align":"lll","head":["Classification","The management question it answers","Illustration"],"rows":[["**By element** — material, labour, expenses","What kind of resource is being consumed, and who should control it?","A furniture maker separates timber (material) from carpenters' wages (labour) from the rent of the workshop (expense)."],["**By nature** — direct and indirect","How does this cost reach the product — directly, or through apportionment and absorption?","The timber in a chair is a direct material; the glue and the supervisor's salary are indirect, and must be absorbed into the chair through an overhead rate."],["**By function** — production, selling, distribution, administration, R&D","Which department incurred it, and is it a product cost or a period cost?","Factory power is a production overhead and goes into inventory value; the sales director's car is a selling overhead and does not."],["**By behaviour** — fixed, variable, semi-variable, stepped","What will happen to this cost if volume changes?","If output rises 20%, timber cost rises 20% but factory rent does not move at all — until a second workshop is needed, when it steps up."],["**As product or period cost**","Does this cost sit in closing inventory, or is it written off this period?","Unsold chairs carry their material, labour and production overhead into next year; the advertising that failed to sell them does not."],["**As controllable or uncontrollable**","Whom can I hold responsible for this overspend?","The foreman controls material usage and overtime; he does not control the factory rent apportioned to his department, so his report should separate the two."],["**As relevant or irrelevant**","Does this cost matter to the decision I am about to take?","In deciding whether to replace a machine, its written-down value is a **sunk** cost and irrelevant; the disposal proceeds and the running costs of the new machine are relevant."],["**As normal or abnormal**","Is this loss an expected part of the process, or a failure to be investigated?","A 2% evaporation loss in a chemical process is normal and absorbed into the cost of good output; a spillage of a whole batch is abnormal and written off separately."]]}},{"p":"In conclusion, no one classification is \"correct\". Classification is a tool: the cost accountant selects the grouping that answers the question in front of management, and the same expenditure will appear in several groupings at once."}]}},{"eg":{"tag":"Exam type — Q10","t":"Making good the deficiencies of a cost system that lacks cost control","q":[{"p":"An existing cost system lacks the essential aspects of cost control. List the main matters to be introduced to make good the system's deficiencies."}],"a":[{"ol":["**A budgetary control system** — budgets prepared for each responsibility centre, against which actual results are compared period by period.","**Standard costs** for material, labour and overhead per unit, with **variance analysis** to isolate the cause of each difference between standard and actual (Chapter 13).","**Clearly designated cost centres**, each with a named manager accountable for its results.","**Separation of controllable from uncontrollable costs** in every report, so that a manager is measured only on what they can influence.","**A proper system of stores and inventory control** — bin cards, stores ledger, reorder levels, and periodic or continuous stocktaking (Chapter 3).","**Adequate payroll and time-recording procedures** — clock cards, job cards, idle-time analysis (Chapter 4).","**A sound basis for collecting and absorbing overhead**, with under/over absorption identified and reported (Chapter 5).","**Scrap, waste and rectification reporting**, so defective work is quantified rather than buried in the material cost.","**Standardised pre-printed forms** for material receipts and issues, labour attendance and hours worked, and wage calculations, to improve the quality of source data.","**Prompt, regular reporting** to the right person — a report that is late, or addressed to someone who cannot act on it, provides no control at all.","**A feedback mechanism** built into the system, so that variances lead to investigation and corrective action rather than merely being recorded."]}]}},{"eg":{"tag":"Exam type — Q11","t":"HIMCO — segregating and estimating a semi-variable power cost","q":[{"p":"Company HIMCO produces different products through automation. The company incurs a large amount of overheads in the factory on power alone, which is **semi-variable** in nature. The details of power costs for the six months ending September 2024 are as provided below."},{"stmt":{"t":"HIMCO — power costs, April to September 2024","rows":[["Month (2024)","Machine hours","Power cost (N)","@hd"],["April",50000,400000],["May",60000,460000],["June",40000,340000],["July",45000,370000],["August",70000,520000],["September",75000,550000]]}},{"p":"**Required:**"},{"ol":["**(a)(i)** Differentiate between fixed cost and variable cost. **(ii)** Summarise the meaning of semi-variable cost.","**(b)** Segregate the semi-variable costs into total fixed costs and unit variable costs.","**(c)** Estimate the power cost for October if the firm plans to use 88 machine hours."]},{"warn":"The six-month table is printed in the study text as a **scanned image**, not as text, so it is missing from any text-based copy of the book — it is reproduced above exactly as it appears on the page. Note also that part (c) asks for \"88 machine hours\" while every figure in the table is in tens of thousands of hours; the intended figure is almost certainly **88,000 machine hours**. Both readings are answered below.","lbl":"Source note"}],"a":[{"h4":"(a)(i)  Fixed cost versus variable cost"},{"table":{"align":"lll","head":["","Fixed cost","Variable cost"],"rows":[["**Definition**","A cost that does **not** vary with changes in the level of activity","A cost that varies in **direct proportion** with changes in the level of activity"],["**In total**","Constant as activity changes","Rises and falls with activity"],["**Per unit**","Falls as activity rises (the same amount spread over more units)","Constant per unit at every activity level"],["**What it changes with**","The passage of **time** — hence \"period cost\"","The level of **activity** or output"],["**Examples**","Rent and rates; the managing director's salary; time-based depreciation","Raw materials; direct wages on piecework; royalties per unit"],["**Cost function**","$y = a$","$y = bx$"]]}},{"note":"The sentence that earns the mark is the **per-unit reversal**: a fixed cost is fixed in total but variable per unit, and a variable cost is variable in total but fixed per unit. Candidates who state only the \"in total\" half rarely get full credit. And remember both statements hold only within the **relevant range** (§2.1.5)."},{"h4":"(a)(ii)  Semi-variable cost"},{"p":"A **semi-variable** (semi-fixed, or mixed) cost is one that contains **both a fixed element and a variable element**. Part of the cost is incurred regardless of activity, and the rest varies with activity. Its cost function is:"},{"tex":"y = a + bx"},{"p":"where $a$ is the fixed element, $b$ the variable cost per unit of activity and $x$ the activity level. Power is the classic example: there is a standing charge and a minimum load whether or not the machines run, plus a usage charge that rises with machine hours. Electricity, telephone charges, computer bureau costs and piecework wage schemes with a guaranteed minimum payment all behave this way."},{"h4":"(b)  Segregating the cost — the high-low method"},{"p":"**Step 1 — identify the highest and lowest activity levels.** Choose on machine hours, not on cost:"},{"stmt":{"t":"Step 1 — the two observations","rows":[["","Machine hours","Power cost (N)","@hd"],["Highest activity — September",75000,550000],["Lowest activity — June",40000,340000],["**Difference**",35000,210000,"@tt"]]}},{"p":"**Step 2 — the whole of the cost difference must be variable**, because the fixed element is the same N$a$ in both months and therefore cancels out in the subtraction. So:"},{"tex":"b = \\frac{550{,}000 - 340{,}000}{75{,}000 - 40{,}000} = \\frac{210{,}000}{35{,}000} = N6 \\text{ per machine hour}"},{"p":"**Step 3 — substitute $b$ back to find the fixed element.** Using the high point:"},{"tex":"a = 550{,}000 - (6 \\times 75{,}000) = 550{,}000 - 450{,}000 = N100{,}000"},{"p":"Checking with the low point, which must give the same answer:"},{"tex":"a = 340{,}000 - (6 \\times 40{,}000) = 340{,}000 - 240{,}000 = N100{,}000 \\;\\checkmark"},{"stmt":{"t":"Answer to (b)","rows":[["**Total fixed cost per month**","",100000,"@b"],["**Variable cost per machine hour**","",6,"@b"],["**Cost function**","","y = 100,000 + 6x","@b"]]}},{"h4":"Verifying the function against every month"},{"p":"The high-low method uses only two of the six observations, so it is always worth testing the resulting function on the four it ignored:"},{"stmt":{"t":"Predicted versus actual power cost","rows":[["Month","Hours (x)","100,000 + 6x","Actual (N)","@hd"],["April",50000,400000,400000],["May",60000,460000,460000],["June *(low point)*",40000,340000,340000],["July",45000,370000,370000],["August",70000,520000,520000],["September *(high point)*",75000,550000,550000]]}},{"key":"Every one of the six months is predicted **exactly**. That tells you the data in this question were constructed to lie perfectly on a straight line, so high-low and least-squares regression would give identical answers. With real data they would not, and the four ignored months would show small residuals — which is precisely the weakness of the high-low method, and worth a sentence if the question asks you to comment on your result."},{"h4":"(c)  Estimating October's power cost"},{"p":"Taking the intended reading of **88,000 machine hours**:"},{"tex":"y = 100{,}000 + (6 \\times 88{,}000) = 100{,}000 + 528{,}000 = N628{,}000"},{"p":"Taking the question's words literally, at **88 machine hours**:"},{"tex":"y = 100{,}000 + (6 \\times 88) = 100{,}000 + 528 = N100{,}528"},{"warn":"Quote the figure the question's own data support — **N628,000** — and add one line stating your assumption that \"88 machine hours\" means 88,000, consistent with the table. An examiner marking a mis-typed question gives credit for a stated assumption and a correct method. Note also that 88,000 hours is **outside** the 40,000–75,000 range of the observed data, so the estimate assumes the linear relationship continues to hold beyond the relevant range — another sentence worth writing.","lbl":"How to present this in the exam"}]}}]}],"formulas":[{"lb":"Linear variable cost","tex":"y = bx","nt":"b = variable cost per unit; x = activity level."},{"lb":"Fixed cost","tex":"y = a","nt":"a = the fixed cost amount, constant with activity."},{"lb":"Linear semi-variable cost","tex":"y = a + bx","nt":"a = fixed element; b = variable cost per unit."},{"lb":"Polynomial (curvilinear) cost","tex":"y = bx + cx^2 + \\dots + px^n","nt":"A convex curve (b,c,p give diminishing marginal increases) models economies of scale; a concave curve models diminishing returns."}],"focus":"Classification-by-behaviour and the cost-centre/cost-unit/profit-centre trio are the two most exam-tested ideas here. Learn the four sub-types of fixed cost (time/volume/policy/joint-related) by name — a stepped cost is regularly set as a distractor against plain fixed cost. The long glossary in §2.1(h) (avoidable, sunk, opportunity, incremental, marginal, conversion, committed, discretionary…) is a recurring short-answer source; learn each definition's one distinguishing word rather than the whole sentence.","errors":["Calling a semi-variable cost \"fixed\" just because part of it does not change with activity.","Treating controllability as an absolute property of a cost rather than relative to a named management level.","Confusing incremental cost (additional cost from a *group* of extra units) with marginal cost (additional cost from *one* extra unit).","Assuming every variable cost is linear across the whole range of activity, rather than only within the relevant range."],"quiz":{"mcq":[{"q":"CIMA defines cost classification as the arrangement of cost into logical groups according to nature, function or","o":["the auditor's judgement","use in the business of the entity","the order in which costs are incurred","the financial year in which they fall","the currency in which they are denominated"],"a":1,"w":"CIMA's definition names three bases: nature (fixed, variable, value-adding), function (production, selling), or use in the business of the entity.","src":"Chapter 2.1","sec":"2.1"},{"q":"Costs incurred in publicising and presenting products to customers, including research work, securing and delivering orders, and after-sales service, are classified as","o":["production overheads","marketing overheads","administrative overheads","research and development overheads","distribution overheads only"],"a":1,"w":"Marketing overheads is the broader category comprising selling costs, distribution costs and other related costs such as research work and after-sales service.","src":"Chapter 2.1","sec":"2.1"},{"q":"A cost that is fixed for a given range of activity but changes discretely once activity moves beyond that range is called a","o":["linear variable cost","semi-variable cost","stepped (fixed) cost","sunk cost","committed cost"],"a":2,"w":"This is the definition of a stepped fixed cost — constant within a range, then jumping to a new level once the range is exceeded.","src":"Chapter 2.2","sec":"2.2"},{"q":"Which of the following is an example of a time-related, rather than volume-related, fixed cost?","o":["The cost of an additional production plant","Advertising expenditure","Rent","Research and development cost","Supervisory salaries that jump at a new shift"],"a":2,"w":"Rent is the textbook example of a time-related fixed cost — it changes with the passage of time, not with activity volume.","src":"Chapter 2.2","sec":"2.2"},{"q":"The cost of resources already acquired, created by past decisions and unalterable by any future decision, is known as a","o":["committed cost","sunk cost","opportunity cost","incremental cost","discretionary cost"],"a":1,"w":"A sunk cost — e.g. the written-down value of a plant already purchased — cannot be changed by any decision taken now or in the future.","src":"Chapter 2.1","sec":"2.1"},{"q":"A centre which not only incurs cost but also generates income is called a","o":["cost centre","cost unit","profit centre","production centre","service centre"],"a":2,"w":"A profit centre is distinguished from a plain cost centre by also generating income, not just incurring cost.","src":"Chapter 2.3","sec":"2.3"},{"q":"In a brewing company, the most natural cost unit is the","o":["kilowatt hour","barrel","tonne","course","bed-day"],"a":1,"w":"Brewing's standard cost unit is the barrel; kilowatt hour is electricity's, and bed-day is a hospital's.","src":"Chapter 2.3","sec":"2.3"},{"q":"A coding method that applies a whole number to a master group and a decimal to primary and secondary groups within it (e.g. 4 for Factory Overheads, 4.1 for indirect material in the factory) is the","o":["mnemonic method","decimal method","numerical method","alphabetic method","symbolic method"],"a":1,"w":"This is the decimal method of codification — mnemonic uses memorable letter symbols (e.g. DMA for Direct Material), numerical uses fixed-length digit strings.","src":"Chapter 2.4","sec":"2.4"},{"q":"Which of the following is NOT one of the stated characteristics of a well-designed coding system?","o":["Simple for the organisation to operate","Flexible in nature","Easy to remember","A fixed coding length for every item","As many digits as possible, for precision"],"a":4,"w":"A good code should be *concise*, not maximally long — the five stated characteristics are simplicity, flexibility, memorability, a fixed length, and conciseness.","src":"Chapter 2.4","sec":"2.4"},{"q":"An extra unit of activity causing a more-than-proportionate increase in cost — i.e. diminishing returns — describes a","o":["convex variable-cost curve","concave variable-cost curve","stepped cost","time-related fixed cost","linear variable cost"],"a":1,"w":"A concave cost curve means each extra unit costs more than the last (diminishing returns); a convex curve is the opposite — each extra unit costs less (economies of scale).","src":"Chapter 2.2","sec":"2.2"}],"theory":[{"q":"Describe THREE different methods of cost classification and explain the usefulness of each.","marks":11,"a":[{"p":"**By element** (materials/labour/expenses) — useful because it identifies exactly what type of resource is being consumed, feeding directly into the basic cost card."},{"p":"**By nature** (direct/indirect) — useful because it separates costs that can be traced straight to a cost unit (direct, summing to prime cost) from those that must first be apportioned to cost centres and absorbed (indirect/overhead) — essential for accurate unit costing and pricing."},{"p":"**By behaviour** (fixed/variable/semi-variable/stepped) — useful for planning, budgeting and decision making, since it predicts how total cost will move as activity changes, e.g. for break-even and cost-volume-profit analysis (Chapter 10)."}],"src":"Chapter 2.1","sec":"2.1"},{"q":"Citing two examples in each case, explain the following cost classifications: (i) period costs, (ii) product costs, (iii) variable costs, (iv) opportunity costs.","marks":17,"a":[{"p":"**(i) Period costs** — charged in full against the profit of the period incurred, not included in inventory valuation. Examples: administrative overheads (e.g. audit fees); selling and distribution overheads (e.g. advertising)."},{"p":"**(ii) Product costs** — costs identified with goods produced, used to value inventory and work in progress. Examples: raw materials cost; production wages."},{"p":"**(iii) Variable costs** — vary in direct proportion with activity level. Examples: direct materials cost; direct wages paid on a piece-rate basis."},{"p":"**(iv) Opportunity costs** — the value of a benefit forgone by choosing one alternative over another. Examples: the interest income forgone by investing cash in a project instead of a bank deposit; the rent forgone by using a company's own building for production instead of letting it out."}],"src":"Chapter 2.1","sec":"2.1"}]}},{"n":3,"t":"Elements of Cost: Materials","brief":"Material cost from end to end: what materials are and why controlling their cost matters, the store's functions, procurement and documentation, receipt and storage, the methods of pricing issues and valuing inventory (FIFO, LIFO, weighted average, standard and replacement price), the inventory control levels (re-order, minimum, maximum, EOQ, average) with fully worked and independently verified illustrations, the inventory control records (bin card, tally card, store ledger card), the perpetual inventory system, and inventory taking.","outcomes":["Explain the stores function and the factors that facilitate effective inventory control","Explain the procedures and documentation involved in the procurement process","Explain some principles of a good storage system","Explain the methods of pricing issues and inventories","Compute and explain the inventory control levels","Record stores information in inventory control records"],"secs":[{"n":"3.1","t":"What materials are, and why they matter","b":[{"p":"Material cost is a significant portion of total cost for many businesses — especially manufacturers — so controlling it is vital. This chapter sets out mechanisms for controlling material cost and a structure for determining it."},{"def":{"t":"Inventory (materials, stores)","d":"all the tangible material assets of an organisation other than its fixed assets."}},{"p":"Inventory therefore comprises:"},{"ul":["raw materials;","work-in-progress;","finished goods;","merchandise ready for sale;","parts or materials to be incorporated into a finished product (component parts);","consumables — stationery, oil, grease, fuel, gas;","jigs, fixtures and special tooling;","by-products and scraps;","works supplies; and","packaging materials."]},{"h4":"Why inventories matter so much"},{"p":"Inventories form a very large share of the assets of most organisations. On average, about **two-thirds** of a manufacturing business's working capital will be tied up in inventories; retailers and wholesalers will often have close to **nine-tenths (90%)** of their working capital in inventories. For most businesses, the cost of stores and materials accounts for **over 60%** of total cost — so efficient stores management is critical to minimising cost and maximising profit. Even non-profit organisations need efficient stores management, since their resources must still be applied judiciously."},{"p":"No organisation exists without using some items of stores or material. Profit-making businesses using materials fall into three broad categories:"},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Category","Examples"],"rows":[["**Manufacturing goods for sale**","Cement manufacturing; breweries; food processing (cocoa, cassava, canned food, canned meat); restaurants; the garment industry; construction; agriculture; printing."],["**Purchase and sale of goods**","Supermarkets; retail shops; distribution outlets; bookshops."],["**Provision of services**","Transportation; education and training; medicare; personal services (hairdressing/barbering, dressmaking/tailoring); repairs and maintenance."]]}}]},{"n":"3.2","t":"The store functions, and factors that facilitate effective control","b":[{"p":"The store function breaks down into three broad activities, each with its own sub-tasks:"},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Function","Sub-tasks"],"rows":[["**Purchasing**","Identifying and defining the need for stores/supplies; identifying and evaluating available suppliers; negotiating with selected suppliers; making contracts and placing orders."],["**Operating the store**","Receiving and accepting (or rejecting) items ordered; holding or keeping items until used; issuing items out for use."],["**Inventory control**","Recording inventories; checking inventories; planning replenishment; valuation of inventories."]]}},{"note":"The stores function is a **complete process** — many people take part at various levels of the management hierarchy, across several functional departments, not just inside a single \"stores department.\""},{"h4":"Factors that facilitate effective materials cost control"},{"ol":["a budget of material usage and purchases;","purchases handled by competent, qualified personnel — a central purchasing system may be preferred;","sufficient and effective storage facilities;","a system to classify materials clearly as direct or indirect, with unique codes and a consistent assignment of cost to cost units/centres;","well-designed and effective documentation, accounting systems and controls at every stage — standard forms are recommended;","an efficient, well-organised inventory count, reconciled against inventory records;","effective coordination among all departments involved in materials handling (buying, receiving, storage, usage); and","internal audits."]}]},{"n":"3.3","t":"Procurement of materials and documentation","b":[{"h4":"Responsibilities of the purchasing/procurement unit"},{"ol":["purchase all materials and stores not internally produced — supplier selection, preparation of purchase orders/contracts, monitoring supplier performance, and renegotiating or terminating orders/contracts when conditions change;","provide information to, and participate in, management planning on subjects related to materials purchases;","review purchase specifications and assist operating departments in selecting required materials;","protect the organisation from unnecessary commitments arising from inappropriate supplier contracts; and","dispose of all obsolete materials, equipment or scrap no longer needed."]},{"note":"Items of stores may either be bought **for inventory**, or bought **just-in-time** to meet manufacturing schedules directly."},{"h4":"Methods of purchasing"},{"ul":["verbal contracts (e.g. purchase at auction sales);","standard purchase order forms;","simplified order forms (similar to a purchase requisition);","cash purchases;","spot contracts for immediate delivery;","future contracts for a stipulated future date;","blanket orders/period contracts;","call-off orders."]},{"h4":"Settlement methods"},{"ul":["cash with order;","part-payment when the contract is made;","stage payment during the course of the contract;","payment on complete delivery."]},{"p":"The settlement method chosen depends on the nature of the store item, the type of market, and similar factors."},{"h4":"The stages of the purchasing function"},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Stage","Forms used"],"rows":[["**Originating**","Requisition; buy list; schedule requisition; travelling requisition."],["**Supplier selection**","Enquiry; Request For Information (RFI); Request For Quotation (RFQ); tender."],["**Ordering**","Purchase order; period contract/blanket order; call-off order; standing order; scheduled order."],["**Completing**","Advice note; packing note; Goods Received Note (GRN); Goods Inspection Report; invoice; statement."]]}},{"p":"In more detail: the officer requiring an item writes a **purchase requisition**, which must be authorised before being acted on — this requisition is what authorises the expenditure. For items purchased into inventory, requisitions are also issued to replenish stock as it is used up."},{"steps":["Shortlist possible suppliers, gathering information from trade magazines, company-representative calls, catalogues, professional-association membership, a directory of suppliers, and print/electronic media adverts.","Scale the long list down to a short list via Requests for Information, prequalification questionnaires and tender forms.","Request quotations from the short list, and evaluate suppliers' quotations/tenders — weighing specification/design standard, delivery terms/reliability, quality (durability, maintenance cost), productivity improvement, capability to meet volume requirements, set-up costs, tooling charges, transport cost, payment terms, and discount offers.","Select and negotiate terms with the chosen supplier — sometimes preceded by an inspection visit to the supplier's facility in highly regulated industries (food, drink, medical). Negotiate every non-standard aspect of the contract, aiming for a mutually satisfactory, win-win agreement.","Order — usually via a standard purchase order, both to meet legal requirements and to ensure every order is properly authorised against an authorised requisition. The order is an offer to buy on the organisation's stipulated terms; a contract arises once the supplier agrees to supply.","Complete the order — purchasing concludes once the items ordered are received into store, with delivery controlled to ensure suppliers meet their delivery schedules."]}]},{"n":"3.4","t":"Receipt and storage of materials","b":[{"h4":"Store operations"},{"p":"Three linked operations: **receiving goods**, **holding inventory**, and **issuing goods**."},{"h4":"Factors influencing the design of store-operating procedures"},{"ul":["the volume of materials handled;","the frequency of transactions;","the range and variety of inventory items;","the kind of demand on the store;","the type of store;","the inventory control system;","the materials-handling system;","the bulkiness and weight of goods; and","the value of the goods."]},{"h4":"Receiving goods"},{"p":"Received goods are checked for quantity and description against the Delivery Note, Advice Note and Purchase Order. If satisfactory, a **Goods Received Note** is raised. If goods are damaged, defective, or not as described, they should — if possible — be returned on the delivering vehicle; failing that, they are stored separately, clearly labelled, until returned, with both carrier and supplier notified immediately. Rejected goods are covered by an **Inspection Report** and a **Debit Note**."},{"h4":"Holding inventory — location methods"},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Method","How it works"],"rows":[["**Fixed location**","Every regular item has a fixed address. Expensive in store space; accessing inventory is easy."],["**Random location**","Any item can be stored anywhere; location records are essential. Makes better use of space."],["**Zoned location**","A hybrid — groups of goods are allocated to particular zones; location *within* a zone may be fixed or random."]]}},{"p":"Principles of storage:"},{"ul":["frequently used items nearest the issue point;","store complementary items together;","store similar items near each other;","store items in issue batches where possible;","isolate dangerous items (inflammables, explosives such as oil and gas);","change location when demand changes;","in loading racks — light bulky goods near the top, small items from waist to shoulder height, heavy items in big compartments low down, and very heavy items on the floor, not the rack."]},{"h4":"Issuing goods — a specimen procedure"},{"steps":["The foreman of the unit needing the materials writes an authorised requisition, inserting the job number and date, and signs it.","The section's labourer takes the requisition to the stores issue counter.","The storeman collects the requisition, issues the specified items, and the labourer takes them to the section.","The completed requisition passes to store clerks, who adjust inventory records to show the new balance after issue and copy the price from the inventory record onto the requisition.","The priced requisition then passes to the cost clerks."]},{"h4":"Store location, layout and type"},{"p":"Stores should be located as close as possible to the point of materials use, to reduce handling charges — though this can be hard where the same material is used at more than one factory location, or special storage conditions apply."},{"table":{"align":"llll","head":["Store type","How it works","Advantages","Disadvantages"],"rows":[["**Centralised**","Materials kept in one central warehouse, issued only from there.","Less storage space; fewer staff needed; reduced admin work/cost; easier inventory control; lower risk of duplication; concentration of expertise.","Increased transport cost; increased handling charges/breakages; increased fire-loss risk; likely delays issuing to distant points."],["**Decentralised**","Materials held and issued by a store in each user department.","Lower transport cost; lower handling charges/breakages; lower fire-loss risk; fewer delays at delivery points.","Duplication of cost; more staff needed; cumbersome inventory taking; more admin work/cost; cannot concentrate experts across every sub-store."]]}},{"p":"Whatever the type, store layout should ensure: ease of access for materials movement in and out; issuing perishable materials on a **FIFO** basis; segregating toxic or dangerous materials separately; and security, restricting access to authorised personnel only."},{"h4":"Factors that facilitate effective materials cost control (storage side)"},{"ol":["speedy receipt and issue — achieved by locating the store close to the point of entry and to user departments, locating fast-moving items close to the issue point, having separate receipt/issue points to reduce congestion, and separate issue points for different groups of materials;","ease of locating materials;","easy identification through proper classification and coding;","adequate records of quantities held (Bin Cards, Tally Cards, Store Ledger Cards);","adequate security — protecting materials against fire, rust, theft, deterioration and weather effects; and","efficient use of storage space, using the most appropriate method (shelves, bins, etc.) for the materials concerned."]}]},{"n":"3.5","t":"Pricing of issues and valuation of inventory","b":[{"p":"When materials are needed from store, the user prepares a materials requisition. Issued materials must then be **priced** — the difficulty is that inventory may have been bought at varying prices over time, so several pricing methods exist."},{"p":"The objectives of material pricing:"},{"ol":["to charge production, consistently and realistically, with the cost of materials used; and","to provide a satisfactory basis for valuing inventory at the end of the period."]},{"h4":"The methods, compared"},{"table":{"align":"lll","head":["Method","Mechanism","Key advantages / disadvantages"],"rows":[["**FIFO** (First In, First Out)","Issues are priced at the **oldest** unexhausted batch's price; once that batch is fully issued, the next batch's price takes over. Closing inventory is valued at the **most recent** prices.","+ reflects the normal physical movement of materials; inventory valued at recent prices; simple and easily understood; uses actual prices. − comparing job costs is difficult (issue price depends on *when* materials are requisitioned); issue price may lag current economic values; low-turnover items get valued at old, unrealistic prices; in inflation, product cost is understated relative to replacement cost, so profit may be overstated."],["**LIFO** (Last In, First Out)","Issues are priced at the **most recently received** batch's price; once exhausted, the previous batch's price takes over (a new delivery arriving mid-batch immediately becomes the new \"last-in\" price). Closing inventory is valued at the **oldest** prices.","+ issue price is close to current economic value; fairly simple for slow-moving materials; uses actual prices; conservative inventory values when prices are rising. − not realistic (contrary to normal physical issue order); causes heavy clerical work from frequent recalculation; inventory values may be significantly understated; complicated for fast-moving items where a batch is not exhausted before the next, differently-priced delivery arrives."],["**Simple average**","Issue price is the plain (unweighted) average of the different prices of all materials currently in store, with no regard to batch size.","+ simple to operate; smooths out price fluctuations. − does not use actual prices; can give misleading values when prices fluctuate; can produce distorted issue/valuation figures."],["**Weighted average**","A new average price is calculated after **every receipt**, weighting each batch's price by its quantity; issues are priced at the latest calculated weighted average until the next receipt forces a recalculation.","+ smooths out issue-price fluctuations; easier job comparison, since identical items are always valued equally. − calculations need up to 4 decimal places for accuracy; issue price may still lag current economic values."],["**Standard price**","A **predetermined** estimate of the price/cost of a material item under specified conditions.","+ easy to operate, no variation over long periods; checks purchasing efficiency (actual vs standard price); eliminates price fluctuations from cost comparisons. − requires careful initial determination; issues may not reflect current values; disregards price trends."],["**Replacement price**","Uses the **current replacement cost** to value all issues.","+ issues at current prices; easy to operate; inventory valued at current prices. − issue price is not at actual cost, so estimates are needed over long intervals between receipts; ascertaining current market prices can be difficult and time-consuming."],["**Specific identification**","Items are priced at the **actual price they were individually bought at**, by specifically identifying each item to its own purchase. Closing inventory is valued the same way.","Most accurate, but only practical where individual units are readily distinguishable (e.g. serial-numbered items) and not too numerous."]]}},{"eg":{"tag":"Study text","t":"Illustration 3.4 — FIFO, LIFO and weighted average compared","open":true,"q":[{"p":"Mr Kofi Abebrese started a retail business on 1 July, buying and selling second-hand shovels. Transactions from July to December:"},{"table":{"align":"llrrr","head":["","Date","Quantity","Cost (₵)",""],"rows":[["Purchase","Jul 5","200","720,000","(₵3,600/unit)"],["Purchase","Aug 1","400","1,520,000","(₵3,800/unit)"],["Purchase","Sep 3","600","2,400,000","(₵4,000/unit)"],["Purchase","Oct 4","400","1,400,000","(₵3,500/unit)"],["Purchase","Dec 7","500","1,400,000","(₵2,800/unit)"]]}},{"table":{"align":"llrr","head":["","Date","Quantity","Sales value (₵)"],"rows":[["Sale","Aug 2","500","2,500,000"],["Sale","Oct 12","600","2,700,000"],["Sale","Dec 12","400","1,520,000"]]}},{"p":"Required: (a) the cost of shovels issued and the cost of shovels on hand at 31 December, using FIFO, LIFO and weighted average; (b) the effect of each method on reported profit."}],"a":[{"note":"The study text's own printed stores-ledger-card tables for this illustration were badly garbled by scanning/OCR (misaligned columns, at least one visibly wrong sales figure). The figures below were **independently recomputed from the raw transaction data above**, not copied from the corrupted tables — every total is cross-checked against the total cost of goods available (₵7,440,000) and reconciles exactly."},{"h4":"(a)  FIFO — stores ledger card"},{"p":"Under FIFO each issue is priced at the cost of the **oldest** units still on hand, taken batch by batch until the issue quantity is satisfied."},{"table":{"align":"lrrrrrrrr","head":["Date","Rec. qty","Rec. rate","Rec. value","Iss. qty","Iss. rate","Iss. value","Bal. qty","Bal. value"],"rows":[["Jul 5","200","3,600","720,000","","","","200","720,000"],["Aug 1","400","3,800","1,520,000","","","","600","2,240,000"],["Aug 2","","","","200","3,600","720,000","",""],["","","","","300","3,800","1,140,000","100","380,000"],["Sep 3","600","4,000","2,400,000","","","","700","2,780,000"],["Oct 4","400","3,500","1,400,000","","","","1,100","4,180,000"],["Oct 12","","","","100","3,800","380,000","",""],["","","","","500","4,000","2,000,000","500","1,800,000"],["Dec 7","500","2,800","1,400,000","","","","1,000","3,200,000"],["Dec 12","","","","100","4,000","400,000","",""],["","","","","300","3,500","1,050,000","**600**","**1,750,000**"],["**Total**","**2,100**","","**7,440,000**","**1,500**","","**5,690,000**","**600**","**1,750,000**"]],"note":"Closing inventory of 600 units = 100 @ ₵3,500 (Oct 4 batch) + 500 @ ₵2,800 (Dec 7 batch) = ₵1,750,000."}},{"h4":"(a)  LIFO — stores ledger card"},{"p":"Under LIFO each issue is priced at the cost of the **most recent** units on hand at the date of the issue. Note that the Dec 7 receipt did not exist when the Aug and Oct issues were made, so it cannot be used to price them."},{"table":{"align":"lrrrrrrrr","head":["Date","Rec. qty","Rec. rate","Rec. value","Iss. qty","Iss. rate","Iss. value","Bal. qty","Bal. value"],"rows":[["Jul 5","200","3,600","720,000","","","","200","720,000"],["Aug 1","400","3,800","1,520,000","","","","600","2,240,000"],["Aug 2","","","","400","3,800","1,520,000","",""],["","","","","100","3,600","360,000","100","360,000"],["Sep 3","600","4,000","2,400,000","","","","700","2,760,000"],["Oct 4","400","3,500","1,400,000","","","","1,100","4,160,000"],["Oct 12","","","","400","3,500","1,400,000","",""],["","","","","200","4,000","800,000","500","1,960,000"],["Dec 7","500","2,800","1,400,000","","","","1,000","3,360,000"],["Dec 12","","","","400","2,800","1,120,000","**600**","**2,240,000**"],["**Total**","**2,100**","","**7,440,000**","**1,500**","","**5,200,000**","**600**","**2,240,000**"]],"note":"Closing inventory of 600 units = 100 @ ₵3,600 (Jul 5) + 400 @ ₵4,000 (Sep 3) + 100 @ ₵2,800 (Dec 7) = ₵2,240,000. Under LIFO the oldest units are the ones that survive, so the closing valuation drifts further from current prices the longer the inventory is held."}},{"h4":"(a)  Weighted average — stores ledger card"},{"p":"A new weighted average rate is struck after **every receipt**, and every issue until the next receipt is priced at that rate:"},{"tex":"\\text{Weighted average rate} = \\frac{\\text{Value of inventory on hand}}{\\text{Units on hand}}"},{"table":{"align":"lrrrrrrrr","head":["Date","Rec. qty","Rec. rate","Rec. value","Iss. qty","Iss. rate","Iss. value","Bal. qty","Bal. value"],"rows":[["Jul 5","200","3,600","720,000","","","","200","720,000"],["Aug 1","400","3,800","1,520,000","","","","600","2,240,000"],["","","","","","*new rate 2,240,000 ÷ 600 =*","*3,733.33*","",""],["Aug 2","","","","500","3,733.33","1,866,667","100","373,333"],["Sep 3","600","4,000","2,400,000","","","","700","2,773,333"],["Oct 4","400","3,500","1,400,000","","","","1,100","4,173,333"],["","","","","","*new rate 4,173,333 ÷ 1,100 =*","*3,793.94*","",""],["Oct 12","","","","600","3,793.94","2,276,364","500","1,896,970"],["Dec 7","500","2,800","1,400,000","","","","1,000","3,296,970"],["","","","","","*new rate 3,296,970 ÷ 1,000 =*","*3,296.97*","",""],["Dec 12","","","","400","3,296.97","1,318,788","**600**","**1,978,182**"],["**Total**","**2,100**","","**7,440,000**","**1,500**","","**5,461,818**","**600**","**1,978,182**"]]}},{"note":"The Sep 3 receipt does not get its own issue line because no issue was made between it and the Oct 4 receipt — the rate is simply restruck again on Oct 4 before the next issue. Rates are carried to two decimal places and values rounded to the nearest cedi, so the three columns may differ by a cedi or two from an unrounded calculation."},{"stmt":{"t":"Proof — the three methods reconcile to the same total","rows":[["","FIFO (₵)","LIFO (₵)","Wtd. avg. (₵)","@hd"],["Cost of shovels issued (1,500 units)",5690000,5200000,5461818],["Closing inventory (600 units)",1750000,2240000,1978182],["**Cost of goods available for sale (2,100 units)**",7440000,7440000,7440000,"@tt"]]}},{"key":"Every pricing method splits the **same** ₵7,440,000 of purchases between cost of sales and closing inventory — none of them creates or destroys value. All a method does is decide **where the split falls**, and therefore how much profit is reported this period rather than next. Checking that your two columns add back to the cost of goods available is the quickest way to find an arithmetic slip in a stores ledger question."},{"h4":"(b) Effect on reported profit"},{"table":{"align":"lrrr","head":["","FIFO (₵)","LIFO (₵)","Weighted avg. (₵)"],"rows":[["Sales (2,500,000 + 2,700,000 + 1,520,000)","6,720,000","6,720,000","6,720,000"],["Less: cost of sales","5,690,000","5,200,000","5,461,818"],["**Gross profit**","**1,030,000**","**1,520,000**","**1,258,182**","@tot"]]}},{"key":"Because the cost of shovels bought **fell** over the period (₵3,600 down to ₵2,800), LIFO — which charges the most recent, cheapest purchases to cost of sales first — shows the **highest** profit here, and FIFO — charging the oldest, most expensive purchases first — shows the **lowest**. This is the reverse of the textbook rule for a period of *rising* prices, where FIFO gives the highest profit and LIFO the lowest: the direction always follows the direction of the price trend, not a fixed rule about which method is \"bigger.\""}]}},{"eg":{"tag":"Study text","t":"Illustration 3.5 — FIFO/LIFO job costing, plus EOQ and control levels","open":true,"q":[{"p":"Edwuma Pa Ye Manufacturing Ltd is choosing between FIFO and LIFO to cost Job 2499, using material 'A' in May 2025:"},{"table":{"align":"llrr","head":["Date","Particulars","Kilos","Cost"],"rows":[["2 May","Balance","40,000","₵12,000,000 (₵300/kg)"],["5 May","Receipt","10,000","₵400/kg"],["12 May","Receipt","24,000","₵500/kg"],["16 May","Issue","48,000","—"],["19 May","Receipt","34,000","₵450/kg"],["26 May","Issue","40,000","—"]]}},{"p":"Required: (a)(i) cost the issues and the closing balance under FIFO and LIFO; (ii) price Job 2499 under each, given direct labour ₵10,000,000, overhead at 110% of materials, and a 10% profit margin on total cost. (b) Material B is separately ordered on: 400kg/week used for 50 weeks; ordering cost ₵30,000; unit cost ₵1,200, carrying cost 33⅓% of unit cost per year; weekly usage 400–600kg; lead time 1–3 weeks. Find the EOQ, re-order level, maximum level and minimum level."}],"a":[{"h4":"(a)(i)  FIFO — stores ledger card, material A"},{"table":{"align":"lrrrrrrrr","head":["Date","Rec. kg","Rate ₵","Rec. value ₵","Iss. kg","Rate ₵","Iss. value ₵","Bal. kg","Bal. value ₵"],"rows":[["2 May  b/f","40,000","300","12,000,000","","","","40,000","12,000,000"],["5 May","10,000","400","4,000,000","","","","50,000","16,000,000"],["12 May","24,000","500","12,000,000","","","","74,000","28,000,000"],["16 May","","","","40,000","300","12,000,000","",""],["","","","","8,000","400","3,200,000","26,000","12,800,000"],["19 May","34,000","450","15,300,000","","","","60,000","28,100,000"],["26 May","","","","2,000","400","800,000","",""],["","","","","24,000","500","12,000,000","",""],["","","","","14,000","450","6,300,000","**20,000**","**9,000,000**"],["**Total**","**108,000**","","**43,300,000**","**88,000**","","**34,300,000**","**20,000**","**9,000,000**"]],"note":"Closing balance 20,000 kg, all from the 19 May receipt @ ₵450 = ₵9,000,000."}},{"h4":"(a)(i)  LIFO — stores ledger card, material A"},{"table":{"align":"lrrrrrrrr","head":["Date","Rec. kg","Rate ₵","Rec. value ₵","Iss. kg","Rate ₵","Iss. value ₵","Bal. kg","Bal. value ₵"],"rows":[["2 May  b/f","40,000","300","12,000,000","","","","40,000","12,000,000"],["5 May","10,000","400","4,000,000","","","","50,000","16,000,000"],["12 May","24,000","500","12,000,000","","","","74,000","28,000,000"],["16 May","","","","24,000","500","12,000,000","",""],["","","","","10,000","400","4,000,000","",""],["","","","","14,000","300","4,200,000","26,000","7,800,000"],["19 May","34,000","450","15,300,000","","","","60,000","23,100,000"],["26 May","","","","34,000","450","15,300,000","",""],["","","","","6,000","300","1,800,000","**20,000**","**6,000,000**"],["**Total**","**108,000**","","**43,300,000**","**88,000**","","**37,300,000**","**20,000**","**6,000,000**"]],"note":"Closing balance 20,000 kg, all surviving from the 2 May opening balance @ ₵300 = ₵6,000,000."}},{"stmt":{"t":"Proof — both methods split the same ₵43,300,000","rows":[["","FIFO (₵)","LIFO (₵)","@hd"],["Cost of issues (88,000 kg)",34300000,37300000],["Closing balance (20,000 kg)",9000000,6000000],["**Material available in the month (108,000 kg)**",43300000,43300000,"@tt"]]}},{"note":"Prices were **rising** through the month (₵300 → ₵500), so LIFO charges the dearer, newer material to the job and leaves the cheaper, older material in store — giving a higher cost of issues (₵37.3m against ₵34.3m) and a lower closing inventory. That is the standard result in an inflationary period, and it is also why LIFO is **not permitted by IAS 2** for financial reporting: the balance-sheet inventory figure becomes badly out of date."},{"h4":"(a)(ii) Job cost card for Job 2499"},{"table":{"align":"lrr","head":["","FIFO (₵)","LIFO (₵)"],"rows":[["Direct materials cost","34,300,000","37,300,000"],["Direct labour cost","10,000,000","10,000,000"],["Overhead (110% of direct materials)","37,730,000","41,030,000"],["**Total cost**","**82,030,000**","**88,330,000**"],["Profit margin (10% of total cost)","8,203,000","8,833,000"],["**Price of job**","**90,233,000**","**97,163,000**","@tot"]]}},{"h4":"(b) Material B — EOQ and control levels"},{"p":"Annual demand $D = 400 \\times 50 = 20{,}000$kg. Order cost $C_o = ₵30{,}000$. Carrying cost $C_c = 33\\tfrac13\\% \\times 1{,}200 = ₵400$/unit/year."},{"tex":"EOQ = \\sqrt{\\dfrac{2DC_o}{C_c}} = \\sqrt{\\dfrac{2(20{,}000)(30{,}000)}{400}} = \\sqrt{3{,}000{,}000} \\approx 1{,}732\\text{ kg}"},{"tex":"\\text{ROL} = \\text{max usage} \\times \\text{max lead time} = 600 \\times 3 = 1{,}800\\text{ kg}"},{"tex":"\\text{Maximum level} = \\text{ROL} + \\text{EOQ} - (\\text{min usage} \\times \\text{min lead time}) = 1{,}800 + 1{,}732 - (400\\times1) = 3{,}132\\text{ kg}"},{"tex":"\\text{Minimum level} = \\text{ROL} - (\\text{avg usage} \\times \\text{avg lead time}) = 1{,}800 - (500 \\times 2) = 800\\text{ kg}"},{"note":"Average usage $= (600+400)/2 = 500$kg/week; average lead time $= (1+3)/2 = 2$ weeks — always take the midpoint of the given *range*, not one of its endpoints."}]}}]},{"n":"3.6","t":"Inventory control levels","b":[{"p":"Management must decide how much inventory to hold, balancing cost against the risk of running out. Five levels matter: **minimum**, **maximum**, **re-order**, **re-order quantity (EOQ)**, and **average**."},{"eg":{"tag":"Study text","t":"Illustration 3.1 — the base data used throughout this section","open":true,"q":[{"p":"Good Works Company Ltd, material AY4: (i) 12,000 units used every day, 360-day year; (ii) ₵50,000 to place each order; (iii) unit cost ₵12,000, holding cost 10% of that per unit; (iv) daily usage between 11,500 and 12,500 units; (v) delivery takes between 2 and 4 days."}],"a":[{"p":"This data is used across every illustration in this section."}]}},{"h4":"Re-order level (ROL)"},{"def":{"t":"Re-order level","d":"the level at which a new order is placed, so that delivery arrives before the business runs out of inventory. Depends on the rate of consumption and the reliability (delivery period) of the supplier."}},{"tex":"\\text{ROL} = \\text{Maximum consumption} \\times \\text{Maximum delivery period}"},{"eg":{"tag":"Study text","t":"Illustration 3.1 continued — ROL","open":true,"q":[{"p":"Compute the re-order level for material AY4."}],"a":[{"tex":"\\text{ROL} = 12{,}500 \\times 4 = 50{,}000\\text{ units}"}]}},{"h4":"Minimum inventory level"},{"def":{"t":"Minimum inventory level","d":"the lowest level to which inventory should be allowed to fall — determined by the rate of consumption, the normal delivery period, and the re-order level itself."}},{"tex":"\\text{Minimum level} = \\text{ROL} - (\\text{average consumption} \\times \\text{average delivery period})"},{"eg":{"tag":"Study text","t":"Illustration 3.1 continued — minimum level","open":true,"q":[{"p":"Compute the minimum inventory level for material AY4 (average usage 12,000, average delivery period 3 days)."}],"a":[{"tex":"\\text{Minimum level} = 50{,}000 - (12{,}000 \\times 3) = 50{,}000 - 36{,}000 = 14{,}000\\text{ units}"}]}},{"h4":"Maximum inventory level"},{"def":{"t":"Maximum inventory level","d":"the largest quantity of inventory that should be held at any time — determined by storage space, the nature of the item (perishability/seasonality), the cost of storing above normal level, rate of consumption, re-order quantity, delivery period and re-order level."}},{"tex":"\\text{Maximum level} = \\text{ROL} + \\text{ROQ} - (\\text{minimum consumption} \\times \\text{minimum delivery period})"},{"eg":{"tag":"Study text","t":"Illustration 3.1 continued — maximum level","open":true,"q":[{"p":"Compute the maximum inventory level for material AY4, given the EOQ computed below is 18,974 units."}],"a":[{"tex":"\\text{Maximum level} = 50{,}000 + 18{,}974 - (11{,}500 \\times 2) = 68{,}974 - 23{,}000 = 45{,}974\\text{ units}"}]}},{"h4":"Re-order quantity — the Economic Order Quantity (EOQ)"},{"def":{"t":"Re-order quantity (Economic Order Quantity)","d":"the optimum quantity to order each time, set to minimise the total cost of material — balancing three competing costs."}},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Cost category","What it includes"],"rows":[["**Holding (carrying) costs**","Forgone interest on capital tied up in inventory; storage charges (rent, lighting, cooling, refrigeration, air conditioning); stores administration cost (staff salaries, equipment maintenance/handling); handling costs (packing/unpacking); inventory verification/perpetual-inventory cost; insurance and security; deterioration and obsolescence; pilferage and damage."],["**Ordering (obtaining) costs**","Administrative cost of purchasing, accounting and receiving goods; transport cost (carriage inwards); set-up/tooling cost for internally manufactured goods; the purchase price of the inventory itself."],["**Stockout costs**","Lost contribution from lost sales; loss of future sales as customers go elsewhere; loss of customer goodwill; cost of production stoppages (idle-time pay, under-utilised plant); labour frustration over stoppages; extra cost of urgent replenishment purchases."]]}},{"fbox":{"h":"Economic Order Quantity","rows":[{"lb":"EOQ","tex":"EOQ = \\sqrt{\\dfrac{2DC_o}{C_c}}","nt":"D = annual demand; Co = cost per order; Cc = carrying cost per unit per year."}]}},{"eg":{"tag":"Study text","t":"Illustration 3.1 continued — EOQ","open":true,"q":[{"p":"Compute the EOQ for material AY4."}],"a":[{"p":"$D = 12{,}000 \\times 360 = 4{,}320{,}000$; $C_o = 50{,}000$; $C_c = 10\\% \\times 12{,}000 = 1{,}200$."},{"tex":"EOQ = \\sqrt{\\dfrac{2(4{,}320{,}000)(50{,}000)}{1{,}200}} = \\sqrt{360{,}000{,}000} \\approx 18{,}974\\text{ units}"}]}},{"h4":"Average inventory level"},{"def":{"t":"Average inventory level","d":"the midpoint between the minimum and maximum levels."}},{"tex":"\\text{Average level} = \\dfrac{\\text{Minimum level} + \\text{Maximum level}}{2}"},{"eg":{"tag":"Study text","t":"Illustration 3.1 continued — average level","open":true,"q":[{"p":"Compute the average inventory level for material AY4."}],"a":[{"tex":"\\text{Average level} = \\dfrac{14{,}000 + 45{,}974}{2} = 29{,}987\\text{ units}"}]}},{"eg":{"tag":"Study text","t":"Illustration 3.2 — all four levels for Boateng Ltd","open":true,"q":[{"p":"Boateng Ltd: maximum usage 1,200 units/week, minimum usage 500 units/week, re-order quantity 1,500 units, delivery period between 2 and 4 weeks. Find the re-order, minimum, maximum and average levels. *(ICAG, May 2000, 8 marks)*"}],"a":[{"tex":"\\text{ROL} = 1{,}200 \\times 4 = 4{,}800\\text{ units}"},{"p":"Average usage $= (1{,}200+500)/2 = 850$; average lead time $= (2+4)/2 = 3$ weeks."},{"tex":"\\text{Minimum level} = 4{,}800 - (850 \\times 3) = 4{,}800 - 2{,}550 = 2{,}250\\text{ units}"},{"tex":"\\text{Maximum level} = 4{,}800 + 1{,}500 - (500 \\times 2) = 6{,}300 - 1{,}000 = 5{,}300\\text{ units}"},{"tex":"\\text{Average level} = \\dfrac{2{,}250 + 5{,}300}{2} = 3{,}775\\text{ units}"}]}}]},{"n":"3.7","t":"Inventory control records and procedures","b":[{"h4":"Why inventory control records matter"},{"p":"A comprehensive record system exists so that:"},{"ol":["materials received can be recorded by quantity and price;","issues for production can be recorded, so the residual balance (quantity and value) can be calculated;","checks (regular or random) can minimise losses from pilferage or damage;","records can flag slow-moving items at risk of deterioration or obsolescence;","new items can be ordered when the re-order level is reached;","material costs can be charged to the right cost centres on issue;","returns to store and to suppliers can be properly recorded; and","inventories can be accurately valued for balance-sheet preparation and profit measurement."]},{"h4":"The three main records"},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Record","What it holds"],"rows":[["**Bin Card**","Kept at the storage bin itself. Tracks, per item: description, bin number, code, re-order quantity/level, maximum/minimum level, and a running log of order quantity/received, allocated quantity/issued, and the resulting inventory balance — **quantities only, no monetary value**."],["**Stock Tally Card**","A simpler quantity-only record, typically used for low-value inventory items — date, receipt, issue, balance, with a signature column."],["**Store Ledger Card**","Holds the same quantity information as a Bin Card or Tally Card, **plus the unit price and total value** of every receipt, issue and balance — the record actually used to price issues and value closing inventory (see §3.5)."]]}},{"eg":{"tag":"Study text","t":"Illustration 3.3 — a four-month Bin Card (Material JD42)","open":true,"q":[{"p":"Material JD42, four months to 31 July: Allocated units — April 1,000, May 1,500, June 1,300, July 3,200. Receipts — April 600, May 900, June 1,800, July 1,500. Issued — April 500, May 800, June 1,400, July 1,200. Balances at 1 April: on order 1,000 units, inventory on hand 500 units, on allocation 700 units, free balance 800 units. Orders are placed in lots of 1,000 units whenever the free balance falls to 600 units or below. Required: present this data in a Bin Card."}],"a":[{"note":"The study text's own printed Bin Card table for this illustration was badly garbled by scanning/OCR (misaligned columns, one month's row missing altogether). The figures below were **independently recomputed month by month** from the raw transaction data and the four balance-relationship formulas (see §3.7's formula box) — every recomputed figure for April, May and June matches the surviving (if scrambled) OCR fragments exactly, and July follows the same method."},{"table":{"align":"lrrrrrrrr","head":["Month","Qty ordered","Receipts","Order bal.","Qty allocated","Issued","Alloc. bal.","Free bal.","Inventory bal."],"rows":[["1 April (b/f)","—","—","1,000","—","—","700","800","500"],["April","1,000","600","1,400","1,000","500","1,200","800","600"],["May","2,000","900","2,500","1,500","800","1,900","1,300","700"],["June","1,000","1,800","1,700","1,300","1,400","1,800","1,000","1,100"],["July","3,000","1,500","3,200","3,200","1,200","3,800","800","1,400"]]}},{"p":"Worked check for April: free balance *before* placing any new order is 600 (inventory) + 400 (order balance after receiving 600 of the opening 1,000 on order) − 1,200 (allocated balance) = **−200**, which is below the 600 trigger, so an order is placed. A single 1,000-unit lot brings it to −200 + 1,000 = 800, which clears the 600 threshold, so exactly one lot (1,000 units) is ordered; the same logic gives 2,000 units for May (free balance would still be ≤600 after only one lot), 1,000 for June, and 3,000 for July."},{"key":"Across all four months, total ordered (1,000+2,000+1,000+3,000 = 7,000) less total received (600+900+1,800+1,500 = 4,800) reconciles exactly to the change in order balance (1,000 → 3,200); the same cross-check holds for the allocated balance (opening 700 + total allocated 7,000 − total issued 3,900 = 3,800) and the inventory balance (opening 500 + total received 4,800 − total issued 3,900 = 1,400) — confirming the reconstruction is internally consistent throughout."}]}},{"fbox":{"h":"Bin card balance relationships","rows":[{"lb":"Inventory balance","tex":"\\text{Inventory bal.} = \\text{previous bal.} + \\text{received} - \\text{issued}"},{"lb":"Allocated balance","tex":"\\text{Allocated bal.} = \\text{previous bal.} + \\text{qty allocated} - \\text{qty issued}"},{"lb":"Order balance","tex":"\\text{Order bal.} = \\text{previous bal.} + \\text{qty ordered} - \\text{qty received}"},{"lb":"Free balance","tex":"\\text{Free bal.} = \\text{Inventory bal.} + \\text{Order bal.} - \\text{Allocated bal.}"}]}},{"key":"A new order is triggered when the **free balance** (not the plain inventory balance) falls to or below the re-order level — the free balance already nets off stock that is on order but not yet received, and stock already allocated to a job but not yet physically issued, so it is the true measure of what is still available to promise."}]},{"n":"3.8","t":"Perpetual inventory and inventory taking","b":[{"h4":"The perpetual inventory system"},{"def":{"t":"Perpetual inventory system","d":"a system where records (bin card, tally card, stores ledger account, etc.) are kept as a continuously updated, perpetual record of every receipt and issue of materials."}},{"p":"Its main objective is to know the inventory level at any time, facilitate regular checking, and avoid closing the stores for a full inventory count. Records from the perpetual system should still be **reconciled** against a physical inventory count."},{"h4":"Inventory taking"},{"def":{"t":"Inventory taking","d":"the physical counting of materials in store — raw materials, work-in-progress and finished goods are all normally subject to it."}},{"table":{"align":"lll","head":["Method","Merits","Demerits"],"rows":[["**Periodic (annual) inventory taking** — counting everything at the end of a given period, usually annually or semi-annually.","Less expensive than counting all year round; avoids the inconvenience of frequent counts.","May disrupt operations at the year-end count; a large, unfamiliar team can introduce inaccuracies; long-standing discrepancies only surface at year-end; deferring the count to year-end can encourage theft and pilferage."],["**Continuous inventory taking** — items counted at frequent intervals, on a random, rotational basis, reconciled against the perpetual records.","Deters pilferage and theft; avoids closing stores for an annual count; reliable balances are available all year; discrepancies are found and fixed promptly; the stores system is kept under constant review; enhances effective control.","Expensive to operate."]]}},{"p":"Effective inventory-taking procedure:"},{"ol":["arrange items to avoid omissions and double counting;","count in pairs — one counting, one recording;","organise the count well, to minimise production disruption;","count while production is in progress, where possible; and","check the final inventory sheet against store records, and investigate discrepancies immediately."]},{"h4":"Causes of stock discrepancies"},{"ul":["incomplete entries, from non-completion of original documents;","casting (arithmetic) errors on inventory records;","over- or under-issues, from carelessness of the issuing officer;","recording materials in the wrong inventory record;","movement of inventory without proper documentation;","pilferage;","loss from breakage, evaporation, etc.;","inaccuracies in the count itself;","omissions; and","double counting."]}]},{"n":"3.9","t":"Worksheet summary — definitions, formulae and structure","b":[{"p":"Everything in this chapter, compressed for a last read-through."},{"h3":"Section-by-section checklist"},{"ol":["**§3.1 What materials are** — inventory = all tangible material assets other than fixed assets (10 named categories); ~⅔ of a manufacturer's, ~90% of a retailer/wholesaler's working capital; materials/stores cost is usually **over 60%** of total cost.","**§3.2 Store functions** — purchasing/operating the store/inventory control, each with named sub-tasks; 8 factors for effective materials cost control.","**§3.3 Procurement** — 5 purchasing-unit responsibilities; 8 purchasing methods; 4 settlement methods; the 4-stage purchasing process (originating → supplier selection → ordering → completing) with each stage's forms.","**§3.4 Receipt and storage** — GRN on satisfactory receipt, Inspection Report + Debit Note on rejection; 3 location methods (fixed/random/zoned); storage principles; a 5-step issuing procedure; centralised vs decentralised stores (advantages/disadvantages each).","**§3.5 Pricing of issues** — 7 methods (FIFO/LIFO/simple average/weighted average/standard/replacement/specific identification), each with its mechanism and merits/demerits; in **falling** prices LIFO gives the *highest* profit and FIFO the *lowest* — the reverse in *rising* prices.","**§3.6 Control levels** — ROL = max usage × max lead time; minimum level = ROL − (avg usage × avg lead time); maximum level = ROL + EOQ − (min usage × min lead time); average = (min + max)/2; EOQ balances holding, ordering and stockout costs.","**§3.7 Control records** — Bin Card (quantities only, at the bin), Tally Card (quantities only, low-value items), Store Ledger Card (quantities **and** values — the pricing record); free balance = inventory balance + order balance − allocated balance, and a new order triggers off the **free** balance, not the plain inventory balance.","**§3.8 Perpetual inventory & taking** — perpetual system keeps records continuously current but must still be reconciled by physical count; periodic vs continuous inventory taking (merits/demerits each); 10 named causes of stock discrepancies."]},{"h3":"All the terms"},{"ul":["**Inventory** — all tangible material assets other than fixed assets.","**Direct material cost** — traced to a specific cost unit. **Indirect material cost** — apportioned/absorbed, benefits all products.","**Re-order level (ROL)** — triggers a new order. **Minimum level** — the floor inventory should not fall below. **Maximum level** — the ceiling inventory should not exceed. **Average level** — midpoint of minimum and maximum. **Economic Order Quantity (EOQ)** — the cost-minimising order size.","**FIFO** — issues priced at the oldest unexhausted batch. **LIFO** — issues priced at the most recently received batch.","**Bin Card / Tally Card** — quantity-only store records. **Store Ledger Card** — quantity **and** value.","**Free balance** — inventory balance + order balance − allocated balance; the true basis for deciding whether to re-order.","**Perpetual inventory system** — continuously updated inventory records, reconciled against physical counts. **Inventory taking** — the physical count itself, periodic or continuous."]},{"h3":"B. Inventory-control formula box"},{"fbox":{"h":"Inventory control levels","rows":[{"lb":"Re-order level","tex":"\\text{ROL} = \\text{max usage} \\times \\text{max lead time}"},{"lb":"Minimum level","tex":"\\text{Min} = \\text{ROL} - (\\text{avg usage} \\times \\text{avg lead time})"},{"lb":"Maximum level","tex":"\\text{Max} = \\text{ROL} + \\text{EOQ} - (\\text{min usage} \\times \\text{min lead time})"},{"lb":"Average level","tex":"\\text{Avg} = \\dfrac{\\text{Min} + \\text{Max}}{2}"},{"lb":"Economic Order Quantity","tex":"EOQ = \\sqrt{\\dfrac{2DC_o}{C_c}}"},{"lb":"Free balance (bin card)","tex":"\\text{Free bal.} = \\text{Inventory bal.} + \\text{Order bal.} - \\text{Allocated bal.}"}]}}]},{"n":"3.10","t":"End-of-chapter questions (study text)","b":[{"eg":{"tag":"Study text","t":"Multiple-choice and short-answer questions, with answers","open":true,"q":[{"ol":["Materials include the following EXCEPT (A) Finished goods  (B) Work in progress  (C) Consumable goods  (D) Fixed assets  (E) Raw materials","The work of the storekeeper does NOT include (A) Receiving inventory items  (B) Issuing inventory items  (C) Custody of inventory items  (D) Recording of inventory items  (E) Selling of inventory items","Purchase orders are issued by (A) Quality Control Manager  (B) Procurement Manager  (C) Stores Manager  (D) Cost Accountant  (E) Production Manager","When ordered materials are received, they are brought into stores via (A) Materials Requisition  (B) Local Purchase Order  (C) Goods Received Note  (D) Tenders  (E) Materials Issue Note","Under the First In First Out method, store issues are priced using the prices of (A) The last batches received into store  (B) The first batches received into store  (C) The middle batches received into store  (D) The average of the first and last batches received into store  (E) The next batch to be received into store","The inventory valuation method that assumes inventories are issued in reverse order of receipts is called …………………","The level of inventory below which quantities are not expected to fall in the store is called ………………","The optimum quantity of inventory that should be ordered from suppliers at any one time is known as …………………","The name of the document the storekeeper uses to record the receipt and issue of materials is called ………………","The system whereby bits of store items are counted at frequent intervals, so that by year end all items would have been counted at least once, is known as ………………"]}],"a":[{"ol":["**D — Fixed assets.**","**E — Selling inventory items** is not a storekeeping duty.","**B — Procurement Manager.**","**C — Goods Received Note.**","**B — the first batches received into store.**","**Last In First Out (LIFO).**","**Minimum inventory level.**","**Economic Order Quantity.**","**Bin Card.**","**Continuous inventory taking.**"]}]}}]},{"n":"3.11","t":"Study text examination-type questions, worked","b":[{"p":"The study text closes the chapter with twelve examination-type questions, drawn mainly from past ICAG and CACA papers. All are reproduced below with full answers. Note that the book numbers **two different questions 3.7** — the second is answered here as 3.7B."},{"eg":{"tag":"Exam type — Q3.1 (20 marks) — ICAG, May 2001","t":"Purchasing procedures, and the costs of excessive inventory and of stockouts","q":[{"p":"**(a)** One of the board members of Dunu Manufacturing Company wants to know the purchasing procedure the company adopts to procure its raw materials. As a Cost Accountant, outline the main purchasing procedures. *(14 marks)*"},{"p":"**(b)** What are the costs associated with the following: (i) excessive inventory holding? (ii) stockouts? *(6 marks)*"}],"a":[{"h4":"(a)  The main purchasing procedures"},{"steps":["**Originating the purchase.** The officer or department requiring the item raises a **purchase requisition**, which must be authorised by a designated officer before it is acted on. The requisition is what authorises the expenditure. For inventory items, requisitions are triggered as inventory is used up and reaches its re-order level.","**Short-listing possible suppliers.** Information on suppliers of the particular item is gathered from trade magazines, calls by company representatives, catalogues, membership of professional associations, a directory of suppliers, and print and electronic media adverts.","**Reducing the long list.** The list is scaled down by requesting information through a **Request for Information**, a **prequalification questionnaire**, and **tender forms**, and a short list of suppliers is prepared.","**Requesting quotations** from the short-listed suppliers.","**Evaluating the quotations or tenders** — on price analysis and cost analysis, taking into account specification and design standard, delivery terms and reliability, durability and maintenance cost (i.e. quality of supplies), improvement in productivity, capability (ability to meet volume requirements), set-up costs, tooling charges, transport cost, payment arrangements and terms, and discount offers.","**Selecting the supplier and placing the purchase order.** The purchase order is the contractual document; copies go to the supplier, to the stores or receiving department (so that the goods are expected), and to the accounts department (to check against the invoice).","**Following up the order** — progressing delivery so that the material arrives within the lead time assumed when the re-order level was set.","**Receiving and inspecting the goods.** The receiving department checks the delivery against the purchase order and the delivery note, the goods are inspected for quality and quantity, and a **Goods Received Note (GRN)** is raised. Rejected items are returned on a returns note and a credit note sought.","**Checking and passing the invoice for payment.** The supplier's invoice is matched against the purchase order (for price and terms) and the GRN (for quantity received) — the classic three-way match — before it is approved for payment.","**Recording.** The stores records (bin card and stores ledger) are updated with the quantity and value received, and the purchase is posted to the accounts."]},{"h4":"(b)(i)  Costs associated with excessive inventory holding"},{"ol":["**Capital tied up** in inventory, and the interest or opportunity cost of the funds invested in it.","**Storage costs** — rent, rates, lighting, heating and the cost of the storage space itself.","**Handling costs** — the labour and equipment used to move, stack and count the items.","**Insurance** of the inventory held.","**Deterioration, evaporation and spoilage** of items held too long.","**Obsolescence** — the risk that the item is superseded, goes out of fashion, or is no longer used in production before it is consumed.","**Pilferage and theft**, which rise with the quantity held.","**Stores administration costs** — record keeping, stocktaking, and the staff to do it."]},{"h4":"(b)(ii)  Costs associated with stockouts"},{"ol":["**Lost contribution** on sales that cannot be made because there is nothing to sell.","**Loss of customer goodwill**, and of future orders from those customers.","**Production stoppages and idle time** — labour and machinery paid for but unable to work.","**Cost of emergency replenishment** — buying small quantities at short notice, at higher prices and with premium carriage.","**Penalties for late completion** of contracts, and loss of settlement discounts.","**Extra cost of rescheduling** production, and of the set-ups wasted by stopping and restarting."]},{"key":"The whole of inventory control is the trade-off between these two lists. Holding costs rise with the quantity held; stockout and ordering costs fall as the quantity held rises. The **economic order quantity** is simply the order size at which the sum of the two is least."}]}},{"eg":{"tag":"Exam type — Q3.2 (20 marks) — ICAG, May 2000","t":"Inventory control terms with formulae, and Boateng Ltd’s four control levels","q":[{"p":"**(a)** Explain briefly the following, bringing out clearly the formulae for calculation where appropriate: (i) free inventory balance; (ii) minimum inventory balance; (iii) maximum inventory balance; (iv) re-order level; (v) average inventory level; (vi) re-order quantity / economic order quantity; (vii) lead time. *(12 marks)*"},{"p":"**(b)** Boateng Ltd manufactures a special product for the domestic market. Records available at the stores department indicated the following. Calculate the maximum, minimum, re-order and average inventory levels. *(8 marks)*"},{"stmt":{"t":"Boateng Ltd — stores records","rows":[["Maximum usage","","1,200 units per week"],["Minimum usage","","500 units per week"],["Re-order quantity","","1,500 units"],["Delivery period","","between 2 and 4 weeks"]]}}],"a":[{"h4":"(a)  The seven terms"},{"table":{"align":"lll","head":["Term","Explanation","Formula"],"rows":[["**(i) Free inventory balance**","The quantity genuinely available to be committed to new work — physical inventory, plus quantities already on order from suppliers, less quantities already requisitioned or allocated to existing jobs but not yet issued. It is what the planner can actually promise, as opposed to what is sitting on the shelf.","Free inventory = Inventory in hand + Inventory on order − Inventory requisitioned / allocated"],["**(ii) Minimum inventory level**","The level below which inventory is **not expected to fall**. It is a warning level: reaching it means usage or lead time has exceeded expectation and there is a danger of a stockout. It is the buffer or safety stock.","Minimum level = Re-order level − (Average usage × Average lead time)"],["**(iii) Maximum inventory level**","The level above which inventory should **not rise**, set to avoid the costs of overstocking — tied-up capital, storage, obsolescence, deterioration.","Maximum level = Re-order level + Re-order quantity − (Minimum usage × Minimum lead time)"],["**(iv) Re-order level**","The level at which a fresh order is placed. It is set high enough that even if usage and lead time are both at their worst, the new delivery arrives before inventory runs out.","Re-order level = Maximum usage × Maximum lead time"],["**(v) Average inventory level**","The quantity expected to be held on average over time — used to estimate holding costs and the capital tied up.","Average level = Minimum level + ½ × Re-order quantity  *(alternatively ½ (Minimum level + Maximum level))*"],["**(vi) Re-order quantity / EOQ**","The quantity ordered each time an order is placed. The **economic** order quantity is the order size that minimises the sum of ordering cost and carrying cost for the year.","$EOQ = \\sqrt{\\dfrac{2 D C_o}{C_c}}$  where $D$ = annual demand, $C_o$ = cost per order, $C_c$ = carrying cost per unit per year"],["**(vii) Lead time**","The time between placing an order with a supplier and receiving the goods into store — also called the delivery period or procurement period. It is the period the re-order level must cover.","*(No formula — it is an observed or negotiated period, normally stated as a minimum, maximum and average.)*"]]}},{"h4":"(b)  Boateng Ltd — the four levels"},{"p":"First extract the averages the formulae need:"},{"stmt":{"t":"Workings","rows":[["Average usage = (1,200 + 500) ÷ 2","","850 units per week"],["Average lead time = (2 + 4) ÷ 2","","3 weeks"]]}},{"p":"**(i) Re-order level** = maximum usage × maximum lead time:"},{"tex":"\\text{ROL} = 1{,}200 \\times 4 = 4{,}800 \\text{ units}"},{"p":"**(ii) Minimum level** = re-order level − (average usage × average lead time):"},{"tex":"\\text{Minimum} = 4{,}800 - (850 \\times 3) = 4{,}800 - 2{,}550 = 2{,}250 \\text{ units}"},{"p":"**(iii) Maximum level** = re-order level + re-order quantity − (minimum usage × minimum lead time):"},{"tex":"\\text{Maximum} = 4{,}800 + 1{,}500 - (500 \\times 2) = 6{,}300 - 1{,}000 = 5{,}300 \\text{ units}"},{"p":"**(iv) Average level** = minimum level + ½ × re-order quantity:"},{"tex":"\\text{Average} = 2{,}250 + \\tfrac12(1{,}500) = 2{,}250 + 750 = 3{,}000 \\text{ units}"},{"stmt":{"t":"Boateng Ltd — summary of answers","rows":[["Re-order level","","4,800 units"],["Minimum level","","2,250 units"],["Maximum level","","5,300 units"],["Average level","","3,000 units"]]}},{"note":"If the alternative average formula $\\tfrac12(\\text{minimum} + \\text{maximum})$ is used instead, the answer is $\\tfrac12(2{,}250 + 5{,}300) = 3{,}775$ units. Both are accepted; state which you have used. The $\\text{minimum} + \\tfrac12 \\text{ROQ}$ version is the one most ICAG/ICAN model answers use, because it reflects the saw-tooth inventory pattern — inventory cycles between the minimum level and the minimum plus a full order quantity, so its average is the minimum plus half an order."},{"warn":"Always compute them **in this order**: re-order level first (it needs nothing else), then minimum and maximum (both need the re-order level), then average (it needs the minimum). Candidates who start with the maximum level have nothing to put in the formula and lose the whole question."}]}},{"eg":{"tag":"Exam type — Q3.3 (5 marks) — ICAG, November 1999","t":"Perpetual inventory system versus continuous inventory taking","q":[{"p":"Distinguish between the perpetual inventory system and continuous inventory taking."}],"a":[{"table":{"align":"lll","head":["","Perpetual inventory system","Continuous inventory taking"],"rows":[["**What it is**","A system of **records** — bin cards and stores ledger accounts — which are updated after **every** receipt and every issue, so that the balance of each item is known at any moment without counting anything.","A system of **physical counting** in which a few items are counted at frequent intervals throughout the year, on a rota, so that by the year end every item has been counted at least once."],["**What it produces**","A continuously up-to-date **book** figure of quantity (and, in the stores ledger, value).","A continuously refreshed **physical** figure of what is actually there."],["**Its purpose**","To make the balance available instantly for re-ordering, for costing issues, and for preparing accounts without a count.","To verify the book records, locate discrepancies, and avoid the disruption of closing the business for an annual stocktake."],["**How they relate**","The perpetual system provides the book balance **against which** the count is compared.","Continuous inventory taking is what **proves** the perpetual records are right; differences are investigated and the records adjusted."]]}},{"key":"In one sentence: the **perpetual inventory system keeps the records**; **continuous inventory taking checks them**. The two are complementary, not alternatives — continuous inventory taking is only possible where a perpetual record exists to count against, and a perpetual record is only trustworthy if it is continuously verified."},{"note":"Advantages of continuous inventory taking over an annual stocktake: production need not be halted; errors and losses are found quickly rather than up to a year late; experienced staff can do the counting rather than a mass of temporary helpers; slow-moving and obsolete items are identified as they are counted; and the deterrent effect on pilferage is continuous rather than annual."}]}},{"eg":{"tag":"Exam type — Q3.4 (20 marks) — ICAG, January 1995","t":"Two merits of each of five methods of pricing issues","q":[{"p":"State and explain **two** merits of each of the following methods of pricing issues of materials from stores to production and service cost centres: (a) weighted average method; (b) standard price method; (c) LIFO method; (d) replacement price method; (e) FIFO method."}],"a":[{"h4":"(a)  Weighted average method"},{"ol":["**It smooths out price fluctuations.** Because every issue is priced at a blended rate, a single abnormally dear or cheap purchase does not distort the cost of the job that happens to follow it — comparable jobs are charged comparable prices.","**It is simple to administer once set up**, since only one rate is carried at a time; the storekeeper does not have to track which physical batch each issue came from, and the closing inventory value falls out automatically as quantity × current average."]},{"h4":"(b)  Standard price method"},{"ol":["**It permits price variances to be isolated.** Issues are priced at a predetermined standard, so the difference between standard and actual purchase price is identified as a **material price variance** at the point of purchase, and the buyer's performance is measured separately from the user's.","**It is the simplest of all methods to operate**, and it makes costs comparable between periods — the same standard price is used all year, so a change in a job's material cost can only be a change in **usage**, never in price."]},{"h4":"(c)  LIFO method"},{"ol":["**Issues are priced at the most recent (nearest to current) prices**, so the cost charged to production, and therefore the selling price built on it, reflects what it would cost to replace the material now rather than what it cost historically.","**It uses actual costs actually incurred** — no fictitious or averaged figure is invented, and in a period of rising prices it is prudent, charging the higher cost against revenue and so avoiding overstating profit."]},{"h4":"(d)  Replacement price method"},{"ol":["**It charges production with the current market price**, which is the economically relevant cost for a pricing or make-or-buy decision — the business must replace what it has used, at today's price, not yesterday's.","**It keeps reported profit realistic in inflationary conditions**, preventing the \"inventory profit\" that arises when old, cheap material is charged against revenue earned at today's selling prices."]},{"h4":"(e)  FIFO method"},{"ol":["**It uses actual purchase prices, in the logical physical order** in which materials are normally used — oldest first — so no artificial price is introduced and the method matches how a well-run store actually rotates perishable or dateable stock.","**Closing inventory is valued at the most recent prices**, so the balance-sheet figure is close to current cost. FIFO is also **acceptable under IAS 2**, which LIFO is not, so no separate valuation is needed for the financial accounts."]},{"warn":"The question asks for merits only, but a sentence of balance earns credit. The standing drawbacks are: weighted average produces a rate that matches no actual purchase and needs recalculating after every receipt; standard price needs the standard to be kept up to date or it becomes meaningless; LIFO is **not permitted by IAS 2** and leaves closing inventory valued at stale prices; replacement price requires a current market price to be ascertainable, and the stores ledger then no longer agrees with the money actually spent; FIFO charges out-of-date prices to production and, in inflation, overstates profit."}]}},{"eg":{"tag":"Exam type — Q3.5 (20 marks) — ICAG, January 1994","t":"Explaining and computing the four inventory levels","q":[{"p":"The level of stock held is controlled to enable a company to operate on an economic level. **(a)** Explain briefly the following inventory levels, giving the formulae of computation as appropriate: (i) minimum inventory level; (ii) maximum inventory level; (iii) re-order inventory level; (iv) average inventory level. *(12 marks)*"},{"p":"**(b)** Compute (i) re-order inventory level, (ii) minimum inventory level, (iii) maximum inventory level and (iv) average inventory level, using the following data: minimum usage 250 per week, maximum usage 250 per week, re-order quantity 30, unit delivery between 4 and 6 weeks. *(8 marks)*"},{"warn":"The data in part (b) as printed are internally impossible: minimum usage and maximum usage are both given as 250 per week (so usage does not vary at all), and the re-order quantity of 30 units is less than one-eighth of a single week's usage, which would mean re-ordering several times a day. The answer below works the printed figures through faithfully, shows why the result is absurd, and then reworks it on the most likely intended reading (re-order quantity **3,000** units).","lbl":"The printed data are defective"}],"a":[{"h4":"(a)  The four levels explained"},{"table":{"align":"lll","head":["Level","Explanation","Formula"],"rows":[["**(i) Minimum level**","The level below which inventory should not be allowed to fall. It is the **buffer (safety) stock** held to absorb higher-than-expected usage or a longer-than-expected delivery. Falling below it is a warning that a stockout is imminent and that the position must be investigated.","Minimum level = ROL − (Average usage × Average lead time)"],["**(ii) Maximum level**","The level above which inventory should not be allowed to rise, so that capital is not needlessly tied up and the risks of deterioration, obsolescence and excess storage cost are contained.","Maximum level = ROL + ROQ − (Minimum usage × Minimum lead time)"],["**(iii) Re-order level**","The level at which a replenishment order is placed. It is set to cover the worst case — maximum usage throughout the maximum delivery period — so that even then the new consignment arrives before inventory is exhausted.","ROL = Maximum usage × Maximum lead time"],["**(iv) Average level**","The quantity of inventory expected to be held on average across the stock cycle; it is the figure used to estimate holding cost and the capital invested in inventory.","Average level = Minimum level + ½ ROQ  *(or ½ (Minimum + Maximum level))*"]]}},{"h4":"(b)  Computation on the printed figures"},{"stmt":{"t":"Workings","rows":[["Average usage = (250 + 250) ÷ 2","","250 units per week"],["Average lead time = (4 + 6) ÷ 2","","5 weeks"]]}},{"stmt":{"t":"Answers on the data as printed","rows":[["(i)  Re-order level = 250 × 6","","1,500 units"],["(ii) Minimum level = 1,500 − (250 × 5)","","250 units"],["(iii) Maximum level = 1,500 + 30 − (250 × 4)","","530 units"],["(iv) Average level = 250 + ½(30)","","265 units"]]}},{"warn":"The maximum level (530) comes out **below** the re-order level (1,500), which is impossible — inventory would have to be above 1,500 to trigger an order it can never reach. The culprit is the re-order quantity of 30."},{"h4":"(b)  Computation on the likely intended figures (ROQ = 3,000)"},{"stmt":{"t":"Answers with a re-order quantity of 3,000 units","rows":[["(i)  Re-order level = 250 × 6","","1,500 units"],["(ii) Minimum level = 1,500 − (250 × 5)","","250 units"],["(iii) Maximum level = 1,500 + 3,000 − (250 × 4)","","3,500 units"],["(iv) Average level = 250 + ½(3,000)","","1,750 units"]]}},{"key":"In an exam, do exactly what this answer does: apply the formulae to the figures as given, **state in one line that the result is internally inconsistent and why**, and then show the corrected working under a clearly stated assumption. Examiners award method marks, and a candidate who notices the inconsistency demonstrates more understanding than one who quietly writes down an impossible answer."}]}},{"eg":{"tag":"Exam type — Q3.6 (20 marks) — ICAG, January 1993","t":"Ebeyeye Co Ltd — objectives of inventory control and the average inventory level","q":[{"p":"Ebeyeye Co Ltd stocks a number of basic raw materials to meet its production demands. The following are the forecast consumptions in respect of one of the raw materials for the year 1993."},{"stmt":{"t":"Forecast consumption, 1993","rows":[["Month","Consumption","Month","Consumption","@hd"],["January",10000,"July",15000],["February",10000,"August",15000],["March",14000,"September",11000],["April",14000,"October",11000],["May",15000,"November",10000],["June",15000,"December",10000]]}},{"p":"Delivery period from suppliers: **minimum 2 months, maximum 4 months**."},{"p":"**Required:** (a) State the main objectives of inventory control *(4 marks)*; (b) Give reasons why the average inventory level ought to be maintained at the optimum level, i.e. the level most favourable to a company that stocks a wide range of materials *(6 marks)*; (c) Calculate Ebeyeye Co Ltd's estimated average inventory level for the year from the information provided *(10 marks)*."}],"a":[{"h4":"(a)  Main objectives of inventory control"},{"ol":["**To ensure materials are available when required**, so that production is never halted and customer orders are met on time.","**To minimise the total cost of holding inventory** — the capital tied up, storage, insurance, handling, deterioration and obsolescence.","**To avoid both overstocking and understocking**, by setting and policing maximum, minimum and re-order levels.","**To safeguard materials against loss**, pilferage, damage and deterioration through proper storage, custody and recording.","**To provide accurate information** on the quantity and value of each item held, for costing, for the accounts and for re-ordering decisions.","**To order economically** — buying in quantities that minimise the combined ordering and carrying cost, and obtaining the best terms available."]},{"h4":"(b)  Why average inventory should be held at the optimum level"},{"p":"The \"optimum\" level is the one at which the total of the two opposing groups of cost is least. Holding **more** than the optimum costs the company:"},{"ul":["capital tied up that could earn a return elsewhere, with its interest or opportunity cost;","additional storage space, insurance, handling and stores administration;","greater exposure to deterioration, evaporation, obsolescence and pilferage — a risk that multiplies when, as here, the company stocks a **wide range** of materials, since the slow-moving lines are precisely the ones that go obsolete."]},{"p":"Holding **less** than the optimum costs the company:"},{"ul":["stockouts, with lost production, idle time and lost contribution;","more frequent ordering, and therefore higher total ordering costs;","loss of quantity discounts, and emergency purchases at premium prices;","loss of customer goodwill from late delivery."]},{"p":"Holding the optimum therefore minimises the **sum** of the two, releases working capital for productive use, and — where a wide range of items is stocked — keeps management attention and storage space concentrated on the lines that matter."},{"h4":"(c)  Estimated average inventory level for the year"},{"stmt":{"t":"Step 1 — annual and monthly consumption","rows":[["Total forecast consumption for 1993 (sum of the twelve months)","",150000],["Average monthly consumption = 150,000 ÷ 12","",12500],["Maximum monthly consumption (May–August)","",15000],["Minimum monthly consumption (Jan, Feb, Nov, Dec)","",10000]]}},{"p":"**Step 2 — the inventory each delivery period must cover.** The question gives no re-order quantity, so the average level is estimated as the midpoint between the most inventory the company would need to carry and the least:"},{"stmt":{"t":"Step 2 — maximum and minimum cover","rows":[["Maximum inventory = maximum usage × maximum delivery (15,000 × 4 months)","",60000],["Minimum inventory = minimum usage × minimum delivery (10,000 × 2 months)","",20000]]}},{"tex":"\\text{Average inventory level} = \\frac{60{,}000 + 20{,}000}{2} = 40{,}000 \\text{ units}"},{"warn":"State your assumption. The standard formula, *minimum level + ½ re-order quantity*, cannot be used here because the question supplies **no re-order quantity** — so the midpoint-of-cover method above is used instead. An answer that instead computed a re-order level of $15{,}000 \\times 4 = 60{,}000$ and a minimum level of $60{,}000 - (12{,}500 \\times 3) = 22{,}500$, and then stopped because the average cannot be found without a re-order quantity, would also earn most of the marks — provided it says so."}]}},{"eg":{"tag":"Exam type — Q3.7 (24 marks) — ICAG, January 1989","t":"Three raw materials A, B and C — control levels and a bulk-buying decision","q":[{"p":"In manufacturing its products, a company uses three raw materials, A, B and C, in respect of which the following applies:"},{"stmt":{"t":"Raw material data","rows":[["Raw material","Usage per unit of product (kilo)","Price per kilo","Delivery period, min (months)","Delivery period, max (months)","Re-order level (kilos)","Minimum level (kilos)","@hd"],["A",16,10,1,3,6750,"—"],["B",4,30,3,5,4500,1300],["C",6,15,2,4,"—",1800]]}},{"p":"Monthly production varies from **175 to 225 units**, averaging **200 units**. Re-order quantities are: A 10,000 kilos; B 5,000 kilos; C 10,000 kilos."},{"p":"**Required:** **(a)** Explain (i) minimum inventory level, (ii) maximum inventory level, (iii) re-order level *(6 marks)*. **(b)** Calculate (i) minimum inventory of material A, (ii) maximum inventory of material B, (iii) re-order level of material C, (iv) average inventory level of material A *(8 marks)*. **(c)** Material B could be bought in 10,000-kilo lots at ¢29 per kilo: (i) what would be the increase in the value of the average stock of B? (ii) what would be the saving in raw material cost for one unit of product? (iii) what would be the annual saving on material costs as a percentage of the increase in investment in inventory? *(8 marks)*"}],"a":[{"h4":"(a)  The three levels explained"},{"ol":["**Minimum inventory level** — the level below which inventory is not expected to fall; the buffer or safety stock that protects production against higher-than-average usage or a longer-than-average delivery. Reaching it is a signal to investigate. *Minimum level = ROL − (average usage × average lead time)*.","**Maximum inventory level** — the level above which inventory should not rise, set to limit the capital tied up and the risk of obsolescence, deterioration and excess storage cost. *Maximum level = ROL + ROQ − (minimum usage × minimum lead time)*.","**Re-order level** — the level at which a fresh order is placed, set high enough to cover the worst case of maximum usage throughout the maximum delivery period. *ROL = maximum usage × maximum lead time*."]},{"h4":"Working — converting production into material usage"},{"p":"Usage of each material is monthly production multiplied by the kilos that material contributes to one unit of product:"},{"stmt":{"t":"Monthly usage in kilos","rows":[["","Minimum (175 units)","Average (200 units)","Maximum (225 units)","@hd"],["Material A @ 16 kg/unit",2800,3200,3600],["Material B @ 4 kg/unit",700,800,900],["Material C @ 6 kg/unit",1050,1200,1350]]}},{"stmt":{"t":"Average lead times","rows":[["Material A = (1 + 3) ÷ 2","","2 months"],["Material B = (3 + 5) ÷ 2","","4 months"],["Material C = (2 + 4) ÷ 2","","3 months"]]}},{"note":"Before using any of this, check the formulae against the figures the question **already supplies**, which is the fastest way to confirm you have read a garbled table correctly. Material B: ROL should be $900 \\times 5 = 4{,}500$ — the question says 4,500 ✓; minimum level should be $4{,}500 - (800 \\times 4) = 1{,}300$ — the question says 1,300 ✓. Material C: minimum level should be $(1{,}350 \\times 4) - (1{,}200 \\times 3) = 5{,}400 - 3{,}600 = 1{,}800$ — the question says 1,800 ✓. The method is confirmed on two independent materials before it is applied to the third."},{"h4":"(b)(i)  Minimum inventory of material A"},{"tex":"\\text{Minimum level}_A = 6{,}750 - (3{,}200 \\times 2) = 6{,}750 - 6{,}400 = 350 \\text{ kilos}"},{"warn":"Material A's row does not satisfy the formula the other two rows satisfy exactly. Its printed re-order level of 6,750 should, on $\\text{max usage} \\times \\text{max lead time}$, be $3{,}600 \\times 3 = 10{,}800$. Note that $6{,}750 = 225 \\times 10 \\times 3$, which is exactly what the formula gives if A's usage per unit is **10** kilos and its price **¢16** — i.e. if the two figures in A's row have been transposed in printing. On that reading the minimum level is $6{,}750 - (2{,}000 \\times 2) = 2{,}750$ kilos and the average level is $2{,}750 + 5{,}000 = 7{,}750$ kilos. The answers above use the data **exactly as printed**; show the inconsistency and state which reading you have taken.","lbl":"An inconsistency in material A's data"},{"h4":"(b)(ii)  Maximum inventory of material B"},{"tex":"\\text{Maximum level}_B = 4{,}500 + 5{,}000 - (700 \\times 3) = 9{,}500 - 2{,}100 = 7{,}400 \\text{ kilos}"},{"h4":"(b)(iii)  Re-order level of material C"},{"tex":"\\text{ROL}_C = 1{,}350 \\times 4 = 5{,}400 \\text{ kilos}"},{"h4":"(b)(iv)  Average inventory level of material A"},{"tex":"\\text{Average}_A = 350 + \\tfrac12(10{,}000) = 350 + 5{,}000 = 5{,}350 \\text{ kilos}"},{"h4":"(c)  The bulk-buying decision on material B"},{"p":"At present B is bought in 5,000-kilo lots at ¢30. The alternative is 10,000-kilo lots at ¢29. The minimum level is unaffected (it depends on usage and lead time, not on order size), so only the half-order-quantity term changes."},{"stmt":{"t":"(c)(i)  Increase in the value of average inventory of B","rows":[["","Present (5,000 lots)","Proposed (10,000 lots)","@hd"],["Minimum level (kilos)",1300,1300],["Add: ½ × re-order quantity (kilos)",2500,5000],["**Average inventory (kilos)**",3800,6300,"@t"],["Price per kilo (¢)",30,29],["**Value of average inventory (¢)**",114000,182700,"@t"],["**Increase in value of average inventory (¢)**","",68700,"@tt"]]}},{"p":"**(c)(ii)  Saving in raw material cost for one unit of product.** One unit of product uses 4 kilos of B, and the price falls by ¢1 per kilo:"},{"tex":"\\text{Saving per unit of product} = 4 \\times ¢1 = ¢4"},{"p":"**(c)(iii)  Annual saving as a percentage of the increase in inventory investment.** Annual production is $200 \\times 12 = 2{,}400$ units, so annual usage of B is $2{,}400 \\times 4 = 9{,}600$ kilos:"},{"tex":"\\text{Annual saving} = 9{,}600 \\times ¢1 = ¢9{,}600"},{"tex":"\\frac{9{,}600}{68{,}700} \\times 100 = 13.97\\% \\approx 14\\%"},{"key":"This last part is the real question: **is a 14% return on the extra ¢68,700 tied up in inventory worth having?** If the company can earn more than 14% on funds employed elsewhere, or borrows at more than 14%, the bulk-buying offer should be rejected despite the lower unit price. Note too that the 14% ignores the extra storage, insurance and obsolescence cost of carrying 2,500 more kilos — include those and the true return is lower still. A discount is never a saving until it has been compared with the cost of the inventory it forces you to hold."}]}},{"eg":{"tag":"Exam type — Q3.7B (17 marks) — CACA Level I Costing, June 1985","t":"Essential requirements of an effective material stock control system","q":[{"p":"Describe the essential requirements of an effective material stock control system."},{"note":"The study text numbers this question 3.7 as well — it is the second of two questions carrying that number."}],"a":[{"ol":["**Proper classification and coding of every material**, so that each item has one unique, unambiguous identity and cannot be confused with a similar one (Chapter 2 §2.3).","**Centralised purchasing under a competent buyer**, with a defined procedure — authorised requisition, supplier selection, purchase order, follow-up — so that materials are bought at the right price, quality and time.","**Properly designed stores, with trained storekeepers** and defined responsibility for the custody of the materials held: adequate space, correct conditions, security against theft and protection against deterioration.","**A full set of prescribed documents**, each with a defined source and purpose — purchase requisition, purchase order, goods received note, materials requisition, materials returned note, materials transfer note — and no movement of material without one.","**Inspection of all incoming materials** for quantity and quality before they are accepted into store.","**Perpetual inventory records** — a bin card in the store and a stores ledger account in the cost office — updated after every receipt and issue, so the balance of each item is known at any time.","**Predetermined stock levels for every item** — maximum, minimum, re-order level and re-order quantity (or EOQ) — reviewed regularly as usage and lead times change.","**Continuous inventory taking**, with discrepancies between the physical count and the book record investigated and the records adjusted.","**A consistent method of pricing issues** (FIFO, weighted average, standard price), applied uniformly so that costs are comparable between jobs and periods.","**Budgetary control over material purchases and usage**, with variances reported to the managers who can act on them.","**Segregation of duties** — the person who orders, the person who receives, the person who holds custody and the person who records should not be the same person.","**Regular management reporting** on slow-moving, obsolete and surplus items, and on losses, wastage and pilferage, so that capital is not left tied up in material that will never be used."]}]}},{"eg":{"tag":"Exam type — Q3.8","t":"Continuous inventory taking, buffer inventory, and three stores documents","q":[{"p":"**(a)** With reference to a material control system, you are requested to: (i) explain continuous inventory taking; (ii) explain buffer inventory."},{"p":"**(b)** Explain the source and purpose of the under-mentioned documents: (i) goods received note; (ii) materials returned note; (iii) purchase requisition."}],"a":[{"h4":"(a)(i)  Continuous inventory taking"},{"p":"A system under which a number of inventory items are **counted physically at frequent intervals throughout the year**, on a planned rota, so that by the year end every item in the store has been counted at least once — and fast-moving or high-value items several times. The count is compared with the perpetual record (bin card and stores ledger), differences are investigated, and the records are adjusted."},{"p":"Its advantages over a single annual stocktake: production need not be shut down; discrepancies are detected promptly rather than up to twelve months late; the counting is done by experienced regular staff rather than a temporary army; obsolete and slow-moving items are identified as they come up on the rota; and the continuous possibility of a count is a far better deterrent to pilferage than one predictable annual event."},{"h4":"(a)(ii)  Buffer inventory"},{"p":"Buffer inventory — also called **safety stock** or the **minimum inventory level** — is the quantity of an item held **over and above** the quantity expected to be consumed during the normal delivery period. It exists to absorb the two things that can go wrong while a replenishment order is outstanding: usage running above average, and the supplier delivering later than average."},{"tex":"\\text{Buffer inventory} = \\text{Re-order level} - (\\text{Average usage} \\times \\text{Average lead time})"},{"note":"The size of the buffer is a cost trade-off, not a technical constant. A larger buffer reduces the risk and cost of stockouts but increases holding cost; a smaller one does the reverse. Where usage is stable and the supplier reliable, very little buffer is needed — which is the principle behind **just-in-time** purchasing, covered in Chapter 14."},{"h4":"(b)  Source and purpose of three documents"},{"table":{"align":"lll","head":["Document","Source — who raises it, and from where","Purpose"],"rows":[["**(i) Goods Received Note (GRN)**","Raised by the **receiving / stores department** when a delivery arrives, from the supplier's delivery note checked against the purchase order and against the goods physically counted and inspected. Copies go to the purchasing department, the accounts department and the stores records.","To record formally that the goods were received, in what quantity and condition; to authorise entry of the receipt on the bin card and stores ledger; to enable the purchasing department to close the order; and to allow the accounts department to match the supplier's invoice against what actually arrived before paying it."],["**(ii) Materials Returned Note**","Raised by the **production department or cost centre** returning unused material to the store (it is the reverse of a materials requisition). Copies go to the storekeeper and to the cost office.","To record the return of material issued but not used, so that the job or cost centre is **credited** with its cost and is not charged for material it did not consume; and to bring the quantity back onto the bin card and stores ledger so the records reflect what is physically in store."],["**(iii) Purchase Requisition**","Raised by the **storekeeper** (when an item reaches its re-order level) or by a **user department** needing something not normally stocked. It must be signed by a designated authorising officer, and is passed to the purchasing department.","To **authorise the expenditure** and instruct the purchasing department to buy — stating the item, quantity, quality, and the date required. It is the document from which the purchase order is prepared, and the first control point in the purchasing cycle: no order should be placed without an authorised requisition."]]}}]}},{"eg":{"tag":"Exam type — Q3.9","t":"TMT Business — EOQ, the four control levels, and the EOQ graph","q":[{"p":"TMT Business provides the following extracts of its operations:"},{"stmt":{"t":"TMT Business — operating data","rows":[["(i)   Annual requirements","","12,600 units"],["(ii)  Inventory holding / carrying cost","","N50 per unit of material"],["(iii) Ordering cost per order","","N350"],"@gap",["(iv)  Re-order period — minimum","","4 days"],["        Re-order period — average","","5 days"],["        Re-order period — maximum","","6 days"],"@gap",["(v)   Minimum consumption per day","","30 units"],["        Average consumption per day","","35 units"],["        Maximum consumption per day","","40 units"]]}},{"p":"**Required:** **(a)** What is the importance of determining the maximum inventory level for the period? **(b)** Determine (i) re-order level, (ii) minimum level, (iii) average level, (iv) maximum level. **(c)** Produce EOQ graphs for carrying cost, ordering cost and total cost, indicating the EOQ in units and in monetary cost."}],"a":[{"h4":"(a)  Importance of determining the maximum inventory level"},{"ol":["It **caps the capital tied up** in inventory, keeping working capital available for other uses.","It **limits holding costs** — storage space, insurance, handling and stores administration all rise with the quantity held.","It **limits the risk of obsolescence, deterioration and pilferage**, which grow with both quantity and the time an item sits in store.","It **provides a control signal**: inventory above the maximum means something has gone wrong — over-ordering, a cancelled production run, or a fall in demand — and should be investigated.","It **assists storage planning**, since the maximum level determines how much physical space must be provided for the item."]},{"h4":"Working — the economic order quantity"},{"p":"The four control levels need a re-order quantity, and the question gives ordering and carrying costs, so the EOQ must be computed first:"},{"tex":"EOQ = \\sqrt{\\frac{2 D C_o}{C_c}} = \\sqrt{\\frac{2 \\times 12{,}600 \\times 350}{50}} = \\sqrt{\\frac{8{,}820{,}000}{50}} = \\sqrt{176{,}400} = 420 \\text{ units}"},{"h4":"(b)  The four control levels"},{"stmt":{"t":"TMT Business — control levels","rows":[["(i)   Re-order level = maximum consumption × maximum re-order period = 40 × 6","","240 units"],["(ii)  Minimum level = ROL − (average consumption × average period) = 240 − (35 × 5)","","65 units"],["(iii) Average level = minimum level + ½ EOQ = 65 + 210","","275 units"],["(iv)  Maximum level = ROL + EOQ − (minimum consumption × minimum period) = 240 + 420 − (30 × 4)","","540 units"]]}},{"note":"Using the alternative average formula, $\\tfrac12(65 + 540) = 302.5$ units. State whichever you use."},{"h4":"(c)  The EOQ graph"},{"p":"Three lines are drawn against order quantity on the horizontal axis:"},{"table":{"align":"lll","head":["Line","Formula","Shape"],"rows":[["**Carrying (holding) cost**","$\\tfrac{Q}{2} \\times C_c = \\tfrac{Q}{2} \\times 50$","A **straight line rising** from the origin — the bigger each order, the more inventory sits in store on average."],["**Ordering cost**","$\\tfrac{D}{Q} \\times C_o = \\tfrac{12{,}600}{Q} \\times 350$","A **falling curve** (a rectangular hyperbola) — the bigger each order, the fewer orders are placed in the year."],["**Total cost**","The sum of the two","A **U-shaped curve**, whose lowest point lies directly above the EOQ, where the two component lines cross."]]}},{"p":"At the EOQ of 420 units, the two costs are equal — which is the defining property of the EOQ and the quickest way to check your answer:"},{"stmt":{"t":"Costs at the EOQ of 420 units","rows":[["Number of orders per year = 12,600 ÷ 420","","30 orders"],["Ordering cost = 30 × N350","",10500],["Average inventory = 420 ÷ 2","","210 units"],["Carrying cost = 210 × N50","",10500],["**Minimum total inventory cost per year**","",21000,"@tt"]]}},{"p":"Plotting a few points either side confirms the U-shape and that 420 is the minimum:"},{"stmt":{"t":"Total cost at other order quantities","rows":[["Order qty","Ordering cost (N)","Carrying cost (N)","Total (N)","@hd"],[200,22050,5000,27050],[300,14700,7500,22200],["**420  (EOQ)**",10500,10500,21000],[600,7350,15000,22350],[900,4900,22500,27400]]}},{"key":"The U is **flat near the bottom** — ordering 300 or 600 instead of the exact 420 costs only about N1,200 to N1,350 more a year on a N21,000 base. That flatness is the practical lesson of the EOQ model: getting roughly the right order size matters, getting it exactly right does not, and it is rarely worth agonising over the decimal places in the square root."}]}},{"eg":{"tag":"Exam type — Q3.10 (20 marks)","t":"Elam Ltd — a FIFO stores ledger and the effect of switching to weighted average","q":[{"p":"Elam Ltd's inventory purchases during a recent week were as follows:"},{"stmt":{"t":"Elam Ltd — purchases for the week","rows":[["Day","Price per unit (N)","Units purchased","@hd"],[1,145,550],[2,160,880],[3,175,1200],[4,180,750],[5,190,1300]]}},{"p":"There were no inventories at the beginning of the week. **4,200 units** were issued to production during the week. The company updates its inventory records after every transaction."},{"p":"**Required:** **(a)** List the benefits of the FIFO method of pricing materials issued from stores to the factory floor. **(b)** Produce a stores ledger to record the transactions above, using the FIFO method. **(c)** If Elam Ltd changes to the weighted average method of inventory valuation, determine the new value of closing inventory and the likely effect on profit."}],"a":[{"h4":"(a)  Benefits of FIFO"},{"ol":["**It uses actual purchase prices** — no notional, averaged or estimated price is introduced, so the total charged out plus the closing inventory always equals the money actually spent.","**It follows the logical physical flow** of materials in a well-run store, where the oldest stock is issued first to avoid deterioration and obsolescence.","**Closing inventory is valued at the most recent prices**, so the balance sheet figure is close to current replacement cost.","**It is acceptable under IAS 2** (unlike LIFO), so the same valuation serves both the cost accounts and the financial accounts and no reconciliation is needed.","**It is simple to understand and to explain** to non-accountants, and the calculation is easy to check.","**It is consistent and objective** — two people pricing the same issue from the same records must arrive at the same figure."]},{"h4":"(b)  FIFO stores ledger"},{"note":"The question does not say on which day the 4,200 units were issued, only that they were issued \"during the week\". The ledger below therefore records the issue after the last receipt, on day 5. State this assumption — if the issue were placed earlier in the week the FIFO issue value would change, though under FIFO the closing inventory value would not, since it is always the most recent purchases that survive."},{"table":{"align":"lrrrrrrrr","head":["Day","Rec. units","Rate N","Rec. value N","Iss. units","Rate N","Iss. value N","Bal. units","Bal. value N"],"rows":[["1","550","145","79,750","","","","550","79,750"],["2","880","160","140,800","","","","1,430","220,550"],["3","1,200","175","210,000","","","","2,630","430,550"],["4","750","180","135,000","","","","3,380","565,550"],["5","1,300","190","247,000","","","","4,680","812,550"],["5 (issue)","","","","550","145","79,750","",""],["","","","","880","160","140,800","",""],["","","","","1,200","175","210,000","",""],["","","","","750","180","135,000","",""],["","","","","820","190","155,800","**480**","**91,200**"],["**Total**","**4,680**","","**812,550**","**4,200**","","**721,350**","**480**","**91,200**"]],"note":"The issue of 4,200 units exhausts days 1 to 4 entirely (550 + 880 + 1,200 + 750 = 3,380 units) and takes 820 units from day 5. Closing inventory is therefore the 480 units left from day 5 @ N190 = N91,200."}},{"h4":"(c)  Switching to the weighted average method"},{"p":"Because every receipt occurred before the issue, the weighted average rate at the moment of issue is simply the total cost of the week's purchases divided by the total units purchased:"},{"tex":"\\text{Weighted average rate} = \\frac{812{,}550}{4{,}680} = N173.62 \\text{ per unit}"},{"stmt":{"t":"Weighted average — issue and closing inventory","rows":[["Cost of goods available (4,680 units)","",812550],["Less: issues, 4,200 units @ N173.62","",729212],["**Closing inventory, 480 units @ N173.62**","",83338,"@tt"]]}},{"h4":"The likely effect on profit"},{"stmt":{"t":"FIFO compared with weighted average","rows":[["","FIFO (N)","Weighted avg. (N)","Difference (N)","@hd"],["Cost of materials issued to production",721350,729212,7862],["Closing inventory",91200,83338,-7862],["**Cost of goods available**",812550,812550,0,"@tt"]]}},{"p":"Switching to the weighted average method would therefore:"},{"ul":["**reduce the closing inventory value by N7,862**, from N91,200 to N83,338, lowering reported current assets;","**increase the cost of materials charged to production by N7,862**; and therefore","**reduce reported profit for the week by N7,862**."]},{"key":"Prices rose steadily all week (N145 → N190), so FIFO charges the cheapest units to production and leaves the dearest in store — giving the lower cost of sales and the higher profit. The weighted average blends the week's prices, so it sits between FIFO and LIFO on both figures. Note the effect is purely one of **timing**: the N7,862 not charged this week under FIFO will be charged next week, when that dearer closing inventory is issued. Over the life of the business every method gives the same total profit."}]}}]}],"formulas":[{"lb":"Re-order level","tex":"\\text{ROL} = \\text{max usage} \\times \\text{max lead time}"},{"lb":"Minimum inventory level","tex":"\\text{Min} = \\text{ROL} - (\\text{avg usage} \\times \\text{avg lead time})"},{"lb":"Maximum inventory level","tex":"\\text{Max} = \\text{ROL} + \\text{EOQ} - (\\text{min usage} \\times \\text{min lead time})"},{"lb":"Average inventory level","tex":"\\text{Avg} = \\dfrac{\\text{Min} + \\text{Max}}{2}"},{"lb":"Economic Order Quantity (EOQ)","tex":"EOQ = \\sqrt{\\dfrac{2DC_o}{C_c}}","nt":"D = annual demand, Co = cost per order, Cc = carrying cost per unit per year."},{"lb":"Free balance (bin card)","tex":"\\text{Free bal.} = \\text{Inventory bal.} + \\text{Order bal.} - \\text{Allocated bal.}"}],"focus":"This is one of the most numerically-tested chapters in the whole paper — expect an EOQ/control-levels computation almost every diet, and a FIFO/LIFO/weighted-average ledger question nearly as often. Learn the four control-level formulas as a set (they share ROL as a building block) rather than in isolation, and always take the **midpoint** of a given usage/lead-time *range* for the \"average\" versions, never one of its endpoints. For pricing methods, the direction of the price trend (rising vs falling) decides which of FIFO/LIFO gives the higher profit — do not memorise \"FIFO is always higher,\" memorise the *reason* (which batches get charged to cost of sales first).","errors":["Using the *plain* inventory balance instead of the *free* balance to decide whether to re-order.","Swapping the maximum-level and minimum-level formulas — maximum *adds* EOQ, minimum does not.","Using an endpoint instead of the midpoint for \"average usage\" or \"average lead time.\"","Assuming FIFO always gives the highest profit — it only does so in a period of *rising* prices; in falling prices LIFO gives the highest profit instead.","Confusing a Bin Card (quantities only) with a Store Ledger Card (quantities **and** values) — only the Store Ledger Card can be used to price an issue."],"quiz":{"mcq":[{"q":"For most businesses, the cost of stores and materials accounts for approximately what share of total cost?","o":["under 10%","about 25%","about 40%","over 60%","exactly 100%"],"a":3,"w":"The study text states that materials/stores cost accounts for over 60% of total cost in most businesses, which is why controlling it is so critical.","src":"Chapter 3.1","sec":"3.1"},{"q":"A company holds material X. Maximum usage is 800 units/week and maximum delivery period is 5 weeks. The re-order level is","o":["800 units","4,000 units","160 units","5 units","4,800 units"],"a":1,"w":"ROL = maximum usage × maximum delivery period = 800 × 5 = 4,000 units.","calc":"\\text{ROL} = 800 \\times 5 = 4{,}000","src":"Chapter 3.6","sec":"3.6"},{"q":"A company's re-order level is 4,000 units, average usage is 500 units/week and average lead time is 3 weeks. The minimum inventory level is","o":["1,500 units","2,500 units","4,000 units","5,500 units","1,000 units"],"a":1,"w":"Minimum level = ROL − (average usage × average lead time) = 4,000 − (500 × 3) = 4,000 − 1,500 = 2,500 units.","calc":"\\text{Min} = 4{,}000 - (500 \\times 3) = 2{,}500","src":"Chapter 3.6","sec":"3.6"},{"q":"Annual demand for a material is 8,000 units, the cost of placing an order is ₵200, and the carrying cost per unit per year is ₵4. The Economic Order Quantity is closest to","o":["400 units","632 units","894 units","1,000 units","2,000 units"],"a":2,"w":"EOQ = √(2DCo/Cc) = √(2×8,000×200/4) = √800,000 ≈ 894 units.","calc":"EOQ = \\sqrt{\\dfrac{2(8{,}000)(200)}{4}} = \\sqrt{800{,}000} \\approx 894","src":"Chapter 3.6","sec":"3.6"},{"q":"In a period of steadily FALLING material prices, which pricing method gives the HIGHEST reported profit?","o":["FIFO","LIFO","Simple average","Standard price","Replacement price"],"a":1,"w":"LIFO charges the most recently purchased (and, in a falling-price period, the cheapest) batches to cost of sales first, understating cost of sales and so overstating profit relative to FIFO — the reverse of what happens when prices rise.","src":"Chapter 3.5","sec":"3.5"},{"q":"The document a storekeeper uses to record the receipt and issue of materials, showing quantities only (no monetary value), is the","o":["Store Ledger Card","Goods Received Note","Bin Card","Purchase Requisition","Materials Issue Note"],"a":2,"w":"The Bin Card is the quantity-only record kept at the bin itself. The Store Ledger Card holds the same information plus unit price and value.","src":"Chapter 3.7","sec":"3.7"},{"q":"A pricing method that recalculates the issue price after every single receipt, weighting each batch's price by its quantity, is","o":["FIFO","LIFO","the simple average method","the weighted average method","the standard price method"],"a":3,"w":"The weighted average method recalculates after every receipt; the simple average method, by contrast, averages prices without regard to quantity.","src":"Chapter 3.5","sec":"3.5"},{"q":"A predetermined estimate of the price or cost of a material item under specified conditions, used to check purchasing efficiency by comparison with actual prices, describes the","o":["replacement price method","standard price method","specific identification method","weighted average method","FIFO method"],"a":1,"w":"The standard price is set in advance and stays fixed over long periods; comparing actual prices against it is precisely how purchasing efficiency is checked.","src":"Chapter 3.5","sec":"3.5"},{"q":"Continuous inventory taking, compared with periodic (annual) inventory taking","o":["is always cheaper to operate","requires closing the stores once a year for a full count","counts items at frequent intervals on a rotational basis, reconciled against perpetual records","is never a deterrent to theft or pilferage","discovers discrepancies only at the year end"],"a":2,"w":"Continuous inventory taking counts items at frequent intervals on a rotational basis, reconciled against the perpetual inventory records — it is more expensive to run than a single annual count, but avoids closing the stores and finds discrepancies faster.","src":"Chapter 3.8","sec":"3.8"},{"q":"A store layout in which materials are kept in one central warehouse and issued only from that point is called","o":["a decentralised store","a zoned store","a centralised store","a random-location store","a fixed-location store"],"a":2,"w":"This describes a centralised store — one warehouse, one issue point, with lower staffing/admin cost but higher transport cost to distant users, unlike a decentralised store.","src":"Chapter 3.4","sec":"3.4"}],"theory":[{"q":"Explain briefly the following, giving the formula for computation where appropriate: (i) minimum inventory level, (ii) maximum inventory level, (iii) re-order inventory level, (iv) average inventory level.","marks":12,"a":[{"p":"**(i) Minimum inventory level** — the lowest level inventory should be allowed to fall to. $\\text{Min} = \\text{ROL} - (\\text{avg usage} \\times \\text{avg lead time})$."},{"p":"**(ii) Maximum inventory level** — the largest quantity that should be held at any time. $\\text{Max} = \\text{ROL} + \\text{EOQ} - (\\text{min usage} \\times \\text{min lead time})$."},{"p":"**(iii) Re-order inventory level** — the level at which a new order is placed, so delivery arrives before stock runs out. $\\text{ROL} = \\text{max usage} \\times \\text{max lead time}$."},{"p":"**(iv) Average inventory level** — the midpoint between the minimum and maximum levels. $\\text{Avg} = (\\text{Min} + \\text{Max}) / 2$."}],"src":"Chapter 3.6","sec":"3.6"},{"q":"State and explain TWO merits of each of the following methods of pricing issues of materials from stores: (a) Weighted Average Method, (b) Standard Price Method, (c) LIFO Method, (d) Replacement Price Method, (e) FIFO Method.","marks":20,"a":[{"p":"**(a) Weighted average** — smooths out fluctuations in issue prices; makes comparing different jobs easier, since identical items are always valued equally."},{"p":"**(b) Standard price** — easy to operate, with no variation in issue price over long periods; lets management check purchasing efficiency by comparing actual prices with the standard."},{"p":"**(c) LIFO** — issue price stays close to current economic value; fairly simple to operate when materials are slow-moving."},{"p":"**(d) Replacement price** — issues are always at current prices; inventory values reflect current prices too, useful for realistic decision making."},{"p":"**(e) FIFO** — reflects the normal physical movement of materials (oldest stock used first); is simple and easily understood, and uses actual, not estimated, prices."}],"src":"Chapter 3.5","sec":"3.5"},{"q":"Distinguish between a perpetual inventory system and continuous inventory taking.","marks":5,"a":[{"p":"A **perpetual inventory system** is the record-keeping system itself — bin cards, tally cards and stores ledger accounts updated continuously, so the inventory level is known from the records at any time."},{"p":"**Continuous inventory taking** is the physical verification activity — counting items at frequent, rotational intervals and reconciling the count against those perpetual records. One is the paper (or system) record; the other is the physical check that confirms the record is actually correct."}],"src":"Chapter 3.8","sec":"3.8"}]}},{"n":4,"t":"Accounting for Labour","brief":"Labour cost end to end: recruitment, induction, timing and behavioural control; job evaluation, merit rating and work study; the remuneration methods (time-based, piece rate, and the Halsey/Halsey-Weir/Rowan premium bonus schemes) with fully worked and verified illustrations; overtime and idle time; direct versus indirect labour cost; labour turnover (three measurement formulas, its costs, causes and reduction); payroll fraud controls; and payroll preparation.","outcomes":["Explain recording and monitoring procedures — time-keeping and time-booking","Explain job evaluation, merit rating, work study, methods study and work measurement","State and explain the basic labour remuneration methods, including time-based systems, piecework systems, and individual and group incentive schemes","Explain the treatment of overtime and idle time in labour costing","Explain and prepare entries in payroll accounting","Explain the causes of labour turnover and ascertain the costs of labour turnover"],"secs":[{"n":"4.1","t":"Introduction","b":[{"p":"Labour is the second element of cost, after materials. Labour cost is the remuneration for employees' effort in the production process, and controlling it is complex — just as controlling human beings and their behaviour is complex. This chapter covers the determination and control of labour cost from recruitment through to payroll."}]},{"n":"4.2","t":"Labour administration and documentation","b":[{"h4":"Labour as a factor of production"},{"p":"Labour is the collective effort of employees in a production process — the form it takes is employee knowledge, effort, expertise and experience — transforming raw materials into finished goods. It spans every level of the workforce, from top management to the grassroots. Because it has huge cost implications, its determination and control matter. Labour cost consists of recruitment cost, training and staff-development cost, wages and salaries, employee bonuses, overtime premium, group incentives, and more — left uncontrolled, total labour cost is not minimised."},{"h4":"Labour recruitment process"},{"def":{"t":"Labour recruitment","d":"the process of engaging employees to render services for an organisation — carried out internally, externally, or both, depending on policy."}},{"steps":["Carry out a job analysis, to determine the roles, responsibilities and specifications required for the position.","For internal recruitment, circulate the advertisement widely within the organisation (including notice boards), so qualified internal staff can apply.","For external recruitment, advertise through labour offices, newspapers, social media, job fairs, outsourcing, headhunting or executive search.","Shortlist qualified applicants from those who respond, for the selection process.","Select — via a structured interview (an agreed format, with questions ordered to evaluate applicants) or an unstructured one — choosing the most qualified candidate against a pre-determined criterion."]},{"h4":"Induction, placement and training"},{"def":{"t":"Induction","d":"a programme integrating new employees into the organisation's culture, through acquaintance with its values and norms."}},{"def":{"t":"Placement","d":"fixing a newly employed person into their post, based on their qualifications, competencies and experience."}},{"p":"Training, considered thereafter, involves: a training-needs analysis; assessing the training budget; prioritising the most critical needs; defining the skills, knowledge, abilities and objectives desired after training; considering the training method; and evaluating the training."},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Cost group","What it includes"],"rows":[["**Recruitment cost**","Advertising cost; interviewing expenses; cost of inducting new employees; training expenses."],["**Total labour cost**","Recruitment cost, **plus** wages and salaries; overtime premiums; employee bonuses; retirement payments; and more."]]}},{"h4":"Labour timing and assessment"},{"p":"Once engaged, employees must be tracked at work — to assess performance, and to make costing the work possible. Methods used include:"},{"ul":["Attendance Register;","Clock Cards;","Attendance Boards;","Job Sheets;","Job Books."]},{"p":"These records gather evidence of: hours worked; time spent by each employee on a particular job or service; and hours of idleness."},{"h4":"Labour behaviour and control"},{"p":"Human behaviour is relatively difficult to control, but the organisation must still try. Mechanisms devised to aid labour control:"},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Mechanism","How it works"],"rows":[["**Time-keeping**","Employees record the time they report for and leave work, and the time spent on each job/work handled."],["**Monitoring and supervision**","Senior officers supervise employees and observe work attitude."],["**Evaluation**","The employee's work is evaluated by peers, clients and management — usually motivating positive workplace attitudes."],["**Reward schemes**","Bonuses and incentives for effective employees drive hard work."]]}},{"h4":"Job evaluation, merit rating and work study"},{"def":{"t":"Job evaluation","d":"a technique showing the relative worth of each job, to rank it against other jobs and establish the appropriate weight of remuneration to attach to it — analysing job content against yardsticks such as degree of responsibility, decisions involved, training/experience required and working conditions, awarding points for each."}},{"def":{"t":"Merit rating","d":"measures the *jobholder's* performance (not the job itself), to decide whether the employee should be promoted, demoted or given a special reward — using yardsticks such as accuracy, initiative, level of responsiveness and willingness. Most industries rate staff at year-end to track progress."}},{"key":"Job evaluation ranks **the job**; merit rating ranks **the person doing it**. A question asking which one determines a pay grade, and which determines a bonus or promotion, is testing exactly this distinction."},{"def":{"t":"Work study","d":"a system of maximising an operating unit's productivity by reorganising its work — subdivided into **method study** and **work measurement**."}},{"ul":["**Method study** — recording and critically examining existing methods of doing work, comparing them with proposed methods, to find easier, more effective and cheaper methods.","**Work measurement** — measures the time a qualified worker needs to complete a specific assignment at a specified level of performance."]}]},{"n":"4.3","t":"Labour remuneration methods","b":[{"p":"Two basic families of remuneration: **time-based** and **output-based**."},{"h4":"Time rate"},{"p":"Pay depends on the number of hours worked, **not** the quantity produced."},{"tex":"\\text{Gross wage} = \\text{Hours worked} \\times \\text{Rate per hour}"},{"eg":{"tag":"Study text","t":"Illustration 4.1 — time rate","open":true,"q":[{"p":"An employee works 50 hours at ¢2,000/hour. Find the gross wage."}],"a":[{"tex":"\\text{Gross wage} = 2{,}000 \\times 50 = ¢100{,}000"}]}},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Advantages","Disadvantages"],"rows":[["Simple to operate and easy to understand; quality tends to be higher, since the worker is not rushing to finish for extra pay.","No financial incentive to produce more than the minimum; sometimes an incentive to produce as little as possible; supervision costs are needed to monitor idleness; unfair, since lazy and hard-working staff are paid the same rate."]]}},{"h4":"Piece rate (payment by result)"},{"p":"Pay depends on the number of **units produced**, in one of three forms:"},{"table":{"align":"lll","head":["Form","Mechanism","Notes"],"rows":[["**Straight piece rate**","Payment = number of units produced × rate per unit — a fixed rate per unit, regardless of how long it took.","Earnings depend purely on output volume."],["**Piece rate with guaranteed day rate**","Compares piece-rate earnings against normal day-rate earnings for the same hours; the employee is paid whichever is **higher**.","Protects the employee against low output through no fault of their own."],["**Differential piece rate**","The rate per unit itself rises at higher levels of efficiency/production (e.g. ¢10,000/unit below 7 units/hour, ¢15,000/unit for 7–10 units/hour, ¢20,000/unit above 10 units/hour).","Gives a strong incentive to reach the maximum production rate."]]}},{"eg":{"tag":"Study text","t":"Illustration — piece rate with guaranteed day rate","open":true,"q":[{"p":"Rate per hour ¢15,000; 8,800 hours worked to produce 8,000 units at a piece rate of ¢15,000/unit. Which rate does the employee receive?"}],"a":[{"p":"Piece-rate earnings $= 8{,}000 \\times 15{,}000 = ¢120{,}000{,}000$. Time-rate earnings $= 8{,}800 \\times 15{,}000 = ¢132{,}000{,}000$. Since the guaranteed hourly-rate earnings are higher, the employee is paid **¢132,000,000**."}]}},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Advantages (piece rate generally)","Disadvantages (piece rate generally)"],"rows":[["Rewards effort, incentivising higher output; employees are self-motivated, so less supervision is needed; the employer benefits from lower overhead cost per unit.","Quality may be sacrificed, requiring more inspection/quality-control spend; workers may slacken, arrive late or stay away once satisfied with a week's earnings, leaving plant idle; setting standard times (and negotiating them with unions) takes considerable time; if a rate is set too high by mistake, it is hard to reduce afterwards, hurting product cost."]]}},{"h4":"Premium bonus schemes"},{"p":"Bonus schemes reward employees for efficiency — saving cost for the organisation by saving *time* — and are, conceptually, a way of sharing extra profit with employees. Three concepts drive every bonus calculation:"},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Term","Meaning"],"rows":[["**Time allowed**","The expected time fixed to carry out a piece of work — e.g. if the time set for one unit is 5 hours, 100 units are expected to take 500 hours."],["**Time taken**","The number of hours actually spent doing the work."],["**Time saved**","Time allowed minus time taken (only meaningful when time allowed exceeds time taken)."]]}},{"p":"Premium bonus schemes are either **individual** (Halsey, Halsey-Weir, Rowan) or **group** incentive schemes."},{"table":{"align":"lll","head":["Scheme","Formula","How time saved is split"],"rows":[["**Halsey**","$\\text{Bonus} = \\tfrac12 \\times \\text{Time saved} \\times \\text{Day rate}$","Equally between employer and employee — the employee keeps half the value of the time they saved."],["**Halsey-Weir**","$\\text{Bonus} = \\tfrac13 \\times \\text{Time saved} \\times \\text{Day rate}$","2:1 in favour of the *employer* — the employee gets only a third of the value of the time saved."],["**Rowan**","$\\text{Bonus} = \\dfrac{\\text{Time saved}}{\\text{Time allowed}} \\times \\text{Time taken} \\times \\text{Day rate}$","Proportional — the bonus is the same fraction of pay-for-time-taken as time saved is of time allowed. Total remuneration = (time taken × day rate) + bonus."]]}},{"eg":{"tag":"Study text","t":"All three bonus schemes compared on the same data","open":true,"q":[{"p":"Time allowed = 12 hours; day rate = ¢18,000/hour. Compute the bonus under Halsey, Halsey-Weir and Rowan for (a) an employee who takes 6 hours, and (b) an employee who takes 9 hours."}],"a":[{"p":"**(a) Time taken 6 hours** (time saved $= 12-6=6$ hours):"},{"table":{"align":"lr","head":["Scheme","Bonus (₵)"],"rows":[["Halsey — $\\tfrac12 \\times 6 \\times 18{,}000$","54,000"],["Halsey-Weir — $\\tfrac13 \\times 6 \\times 18{,}000$","36,000"],["Rowan — $\\tfrac{6}{12} \\times 6 \\times 18{,}000$","54,000"]]}},{"p":"**(b) Time taken 9 hours** (time saved $= 12-9=3$ hours):"},{"table":{"align":"lr","head":["Scheme","Bonus (₵)"],"rows":[["Halsey — $\\tfrac12 \\times 3 \\times 18{,}000$","27,000"],["Halsey-Weir — $\\tfrac13 \\times 3 \\times 18{,}000$","18,000"],["Rowan — $\\tfrac{3}{12} \\times 9 \\times 18{,}000$","40,500"]]}},{"note":"At exactly half the time allowed being saved (case (a)), Halsey and Rowan give the **same** bonus — this is not a coincidence, it is the one point where the two formulas always cross. Away from that point, Rowan gives a *smaller* bonus for very large time savings than Halsey does (Rowan is self-limiting — the bonus can never exceed the pay for time taken, however much time is saved), which is exactly why some employers prefer it: it discourages a worker from cutting corners on quality purely to report an enormous time saving."}]}},{"p":"Factors that influence the actual size of a negotiated bonus: time saved by employees; cost saved; improved productivity; the level of super-profits made by the business; achievement of other budgetary targets; and market demand for the product."},{"h4":"Individual versus group incentive schemes"},{"table":{"align":"lll","head":["","Individual incentive schemes","Group incentive schemes"],"rows":[["**What it is**","Rewards each employee for their own efficiency.","A bonus awarded to a team, shared among every member by an agreed formula."],["**Advantages**","Motivates efficiency and productivity; can generate healthy competition; raises morale, since individual effort is rewarded; both employee and employer gain.","Enhances team spirit and organisational cohesiveness; quality is not unduly compromised; relatively easy and inexpensive to administer; avoids unhealthy rivalry between employees."],["**Disadvantages**","Quality may be sacrificed for bonus; excessive competition can cause unhealthy rivalry; setting standard performance levels can itself cause conflict; more difficult and expensive to administer than a group scheme.","Lazy team members are rewarded alongside hard-working ones; does not motivate individual hard work as directly."]]}}]},{"n":"4.4","t":"Overtime and idle time","b":[{"h4":"Overtime"},{"def":{"t":"Overtime","d":"time spent beyond the normal working hours or days."}},{"p":"Overtime wage rates are expressed as **time** plus a fraction or multiple, e.g. time and a half, time and one-third, double time, time and one-fifth."},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Term","Meaning"],"rows":[["**Time**","The basic rate — if the normal rate is ¢500/hour, the \"time\" is ¢500."],["**Overtime premium**","The *additional* rate over and above the basic rate — found by multiplying the description by the basic rate. E.g. at \"time and a half\" with a ¢500 basic rate, the premium is $\\tfrac12 \\times 500 = ¢250$."]]}},{"key":"Overtime premium is normally treated as **indirect wages** — the exception is when overtime is worked *specifically at a customer's request*, to complete their order within a specified period, in which case the premium is a **direct** cost of that job."},{"h4":"Idle (waiting) time"},{"def":{"t":"Idle (waiting) time (CIMA)","d":"the period for which a workstation is available for production but is not utilised, due to a shortage of tooling, material or operators."}},{"p":"Idle time is time lost with no production — but employees are usually still paid for it. It splits into:"},{"ul":["**Normal idle time** — uncontrollable; its cost is charged to work-in-progress, so customers bear the burden.","**Abnormal idle time** — controllable; management should ensure facilities are in good condition and materials are readily available. Its cost is normally written off to the profit and loss account/income statement, though some organisations treat it as an indirect cost instead."]},{"eg":{"tag":"Study text","t":"Illustration 4.2 — idle time, bonus and overtime together","open":true,"q":[{"p":"Operative X is given 189 units at a 20-standard-minute allowance each; operative Y is given 204 units at 15 standard minutes each. Bonus is 50% of the basic rate (¢200/hour) for every hour saved. The basic week is 42 hours; hours in excess are paid at time and a half. X completes his units in 45 hours; Y completes his in 39 hours but is paid for a full guaranteed week. Because of defective material, 6 of X's units and 4 of Y's units are subsequently scrapped, though all units produced are paid for. Find, for each employee: (a) the bonus payable, (b) the total gross wage payable, (c) the wages cost per good unit made."}],"a":[{"table":{"align":"lrr","head":["","X","Y"],"rows":[["Time allowed ($189\\times20/60$; $204\\times15/60$)","63 hrs","51 hrs"],["Time taken","45 hrs","39 hrs"],["Time saved","18 hrs","12 hrs"]]}},{"p":"**(a) Bonus** $= \\tfrac12 \\times \\text{time saved} \\times ₵200$: X $= \\tfrac12 \\times 18 \\times 200 = ₵1{,}800$; Y $= \\tfrac12 \\times 12 \\times 200 = ₵1{,}200$."},{"p":"**(b) Gross wage** — both are paid for the full 42-hour guaranteed week as basic wage (₵8,400 each, $42\\times200$); X additionally worked 3 hours' overtime ($45-42$) at time-and-a-half $= 3 \\times 1.5 \\times 200 = ₵900$; Y worked fewer than 42 hours so has no overtime."},{"table":{"align":"lrr","head":["","X (₵)","Y (₵)"],"rows":[["Basic wage (42 hrs guaranteed)","8,400","8,400"],["Overtime premium","900","—"],["Bonus","1,800","1,200"],["**Gross wage**","**11,100**","**9,600**","@tot"]]}},{"p":"**(c) Wages cost per good unit.** Good units: X $= 189-6 = 183$; Y $= 204-4 = 200$. Before dividing, strip out any **non-productive** pay — Y was paid for 42 hours but spent only 39 on the job, so 3 hours of his guaranteed week is idle time, which is an overhead and not a cost of the units he made:"},{"stmt":{"t":"Wages cost per good unit","rows":[["","X (₵)","Y (₵)","@hd"],["Gross wage",11100,9600],["Less: non-productive (idle) pay — Y, 3 hrs × ₵200","—",-600],["**Productive wages cost**",11100,9000,"@t"],["Good units made",183,200],["**Wages cost per good unit**","₵60.66","₵45.00","@tt"]]}},{"warn":"The study text's own printed solution shows the N600 idle-time deduction and the resulting ₵9,000 in its working, but then prints the answer as \"₵48\" — which is $9{,}600 \\div 200$, i.e. the **gross** wage divided by good units, ignoring the deduction it had just made. The correct figure on its own working is $9{,}000 \\div 200 = **₵45.00**$. (Its X column is printed as \"₵9,600\", which is simply Y's gross wage copied into the wrong cell; X is $11{,}100 \\div 183 = ₵60.66$.)","lbl":"Errors in the study text's answer"},{"key":"Three separate traps in one short question. **(1)** Y is paid for 42 hours though he worked 39 — the guaranteed week creates idle time. **(2)** Bonus is calculated on hours **saved against the time allowed**, not on hours worked. **(3)** The scrapped units are still **paid for** but are not **good units**, so they inflate the numerator's wage without adding to the denominator — which is exactly why the cost per good unit exceeds what the bonus scheme appears to promise."}]}},{"eg":{"tag":"Study text — Illustration 4.3 (ICAG, January 1992)","t":"Kokonsekyi Ltd — bonus hours, overtime, gross wages and the cost per unit accepted","open":true,"q":[{"p":"Kokonsekyi Ltd presents the following information relating to a week's work of three of its employees:"},{"stmt":{"t":"Kokonsekyi Ltd — week's data","rows":[["","Mumu","Opi","Adam","@hd"],["Work issued (units)",400,600,120],["Time allowed (hours per unit)",0.2,0.1,0.4],["Output rejected (units)",60,90,40],["Hours worked",52,45,40],["Basic hourly rate (₵)",30,40,25]]}},{"ul":["The basic working week is **40 hours**; the first 6 hours' overtime are paid at time plus ½ and the next 10 hours at time plus ¾.","**Adam spent 8 hours on indirect work**, these hours being included in the 40 hours he worked.","Bonus is paid at **½ of the basic rate for all time saved**.","The high rate of rejection was considered **abnormal**, due to faulty materials, and it was agreed to **credit all output** for bonus purposes."]},{"p":"**Required:** present in tabular form, for each employee: (a) number of bonus hours worked *(3 marks)*; (b) basic wages including overtime *(4 marks)*; (c) amount of bonus earned *(4 marks)*; (d) gross wages *(3 marks)*; (e) direct wages cost per unit accepted when overtime is worked (i) regularly throughout the year, as company policy, due to labour shortages, and (ii) specifically at the customer's request to expedite delivery."}],"a":[{"h4":"(a)  Bonus hours"},{"note":"Two subtleties decide this part. **All** output counts towards the time allowed, rejects included, because the question says the rejection was abnormal and all output is to be credited for bonus purposes. And Adam's **8 indirect hours are excluded** from his time taken — he cannot be penalised on a direct-work bonus for hours he spent on something else."},{"stmt":{"t":"(a)  Time allowed, time taken and time saved","rows":[["","Mumu","Opi","Adam","@hd"],["Time allowed  (400 × 0.2;  600 × 0.1;  120 × 0.4)","80 hrs","60 hrs","48 hrs"],["Time taken on direct work  (Adam: 40 − 8 indirect)","52 hrs","45 hrs","32 hrs"],["**Time saved = bonus hours**","**28 hrs**","**15 hrs**","**16 hrs**","@tt"]]}},{"h4":"(b)  Basic wages, including overtime"},{"stmt":{"t":"(b)  Basic wages including overtime premium","rows":[["","Mumu (₵)","Opi (₵)","Adam (₵)","@hd"],["Hours worked × basic rate  (52×30; 45×40; 40×25)",1560,1800,1000],["Overtime premium — first 6 hrs at ½  (Mumu 6×½×30; Opi 5×½×40)",90,100,"—"],["Overtime premium — next hours at ¾  (Mumu 6×¾×30)",135,"—","—"],["**Basic wages including overtime**",1785,1900,1000,"@tt"]]}},{"note":"Mumu worked 52 hours, i.e. **12** hours of overtime: the first 6 at time plus ½ and the remaining 6 at time plus ¾ (the second band runs to 10 hours, so all 6 fall inside it). Opi worked 45 hours, i.e. 5 overtime hours, all within the first band. Adam worked exactly 40 hours, so no overtime."},{"h4":"(c)  Bonus earned"},{"stmt":{"t":"(c)  Bonus = ½ × basic rate × time saved","rows":[["","Mumu (₵)","Opi (₵)","Adam (₵)","@hd"],["½ × 30 × 28;  ½ × 40 × 15;  ½ × 25 × 16",420,300,200]]}},{"h4":"(d)  Gross wages"},{"stmt":{"t":"(d)  Gross wages","rows":[["","Mumu (₵)","Opi (₵)","Adam (₵)","@hd"],["Basic wages including overtime",1785,1900,1000],["Bonus earned",420,300,200],["**Gross wages**",2205,2200,1200,"@tt"]]}},{"h4":"(e)  Direct wages cost per unit accepted"},{"p":"Units accepted: Mumu $400-60 = 340$; Opi $600-90 = 510$; Adam $120-40 = 80$. The two cases differ in **one thing only** — whether the overtime premium is a direct cost of the job or an overhead:"},{"stmt":{"t":"(e)(i)  Overtime worked regularly as company policy","rows":[["","Mumu","Opi","Adam","@hd"],["Direct wages = hours × basic rate *(premium excluded — it is an overhead)*","₵1,560","₵1,800","₵800"],["Units accepted",340,510,80],["**Direct wages cost per unit accepted**","**₵4.59**","**₵3.53**","**₵10.00**","@tt"]]}},{"stmt":{"t":"(e)(ii)  Overtime worked at the customer's request","rows":[["","Mumu","Opi","Adam","@hd"],["Direct wages = basic wages **including** overtime premium","₵1,785","₵1,900","₵800"],["Units accepted",340,510,80],["**Direct wages cost per unit accepted**","**₵5.25**","**₵3.73**","**₵10.00**","@tt"]]}},{"key":"This is **the** examinable principle on overtime. When overtime is worked **generally** — because the factory is short of labour, or to meet overall budgeted output — the extra hours are a cost of running the factory, so the **premium** is treated as **production overhead** and only the basic rate is charged to the job. When overtime is worked at a **specific customer's request**, the premium is caused by that customer and is a **direct cost** of their job. In both cases the basic rate for the hours worked is always direct. Adam's figure is unchanged between the two because he worked no overtime — note also that only his **32 direct hours** (₵800) are charged to the product; his 8 indirect hours are overhead."},{"warn":"The study text's printed solution for this illustration is heavily garbled and internally inconsistent: its \"summary of answers\" gives Mumu's basic wages as ₵1,650 while its own working shows ₵1,785; it gives Adam's as ₵1,135 while its working shows ₵1,000 (the stray ₵135 is Mumu's second overtime premium, copied into the wrong column); and its gross wages row carries those errors forward (₵2,070 for Mumu, ₵1,135 for Adam). Every figure above has been recomputed from the question data. Its part (e)(ii) figure for Opi, ₵3.75, should be $1{,}900 \\div 510 = **₵3.73**$.","lbl":"Errors in the study text's solution"}]}}]},{"n":"4.5","t":"Direct and indirect cost of labour; minimising labour cost","b":[{"p":"As already covered in Chapters 1–2, labour cost is either direct or indirect."},{"def":{"t":"Direct labour cost","d":"labour cost incurred on employees directly transforming raw materials into finished goods. Only the **basic wages** paid to direct workers constitute direct labour cost."}},{"warn":"Policy-related costs incurred on a direct worker are **not** direct labour cost — they are indirect labour, even though the worker themselves is a direct worker. Examples: workers' compensation insurance premiums; the employer's social security fund contribution; bonuses paid to employees; and overtime premium paid where overtime is worked *regularly, as company policy* (not at a customer's specific request — see §4.4). Wages cost on **indirect** workers is, straight-forwardly, indirect wages."},{"eg":{"tag":"Study text — Illustration 4.4","t":"Charging overtime to jobs — the same hours, two different answers","open":true,"q":[{"p":"A company's basic wage rate is **¢0.45 per hour**, and its overtime rates are: evenings **1⅓** (time and a third); weekends **double time**. During the previous year the following hours were worked:"},{"stmt":{"t":"Hours worked in the year","rows":[["Normal time","","440,000 clock hours"],["Time plus ⅓ (evenings)","","40,000 clock hours"],["Double time (weekends)","","20,000 clock hours"]]}},{"p":"The following times were worked on the stated jobs:"},{"stmt":{"t":"Clock hours by job","rows":[["","Job X","Job Y","Job Z","@hd"],["Normal time",6000,10000,8000],["Evening overtime",600,1200,2100],["Weekend overtime",200,100,600],["**Total clock hours**",6800,11300,10700,"@tt"]]}},{"p":"**Required:** calculate the labour cost chargeable to each job in each of the following circumstances: **(a)** where overtime is worked regularly throughout the year as company policy, due to a labour shortage; **(b)** where overtime is worked specifically at the customer's request, to expedite delivery."},{"warn":"The study text prints Job X's normal time as **60,000** clock hours. It is **6,000** — its own solution totals Job X at 6,800 hours, which is only possible as $6{,}000 + 600 + 200$.","lbl":"Source typo corrected"}],"a":[{"h4":"(a)  Overtime worked regularly as company policy"},{"p":"The overtime premium is caused by the general shortage of labour, not by any particular customer, so it is **production overhead**. Each job is charged only with its clock hours at the **basic rate**:"},{"stmt":{"t":"(a)  Labour cost chargeable to each job","rows":[["","Job X","Job Y","Job Z","@hd"],["Total clock hours",6800,11300,10700],["× basic rate ¢0.45","","",""],["**Labour cost chargeable (¢)**",3060,5085,4815,"@tt"]]}},{"h4":"(b)  Overtime worked at the customer's request"},{"p":"The premium is now caused by the specific customer and is charged to their job. The **premium** is the excess over the basic rate: ⅓ of basic for evening work, and a further 1 × basic for weekend (double-time) work."},{"stmt":{"t":"(b)  Labour cost chargeable to each job","rows":[["","Job X (¢)","Job Y (¢)","Job Z (¢)","@hd"],["Clock hours at basic rate (as in (a))",3060,5085,4815],["Evening overtime premium: hours × ⅓ × ¢0.45",90,180,315],["Weekend overtime premium: hours × 1 × ¢0.45",90,45,270],["**Labour cost chargeable**",3240,5310,5400,"@tt"]]}},{"note":"Working the premiums: Job X evening $600 \\times \\tfrac13 \\times 0.45 = 90$; Job Y $1{,}200 \\times \\tfrac13 \\times 0.45 = 180$; Job Z $2{,}100 \\times \\tfrac13 \\times 0.45 = 315$. Weekend: Job X $200 \\times 1 \\times 0.45 = 90$; Job Y $100 \\times 0.45 = 45$; Job Z $600 \\times 0.45 = 270$."},{"key":"Job Z is the one to look at. Under (a) it is the **cheapest** of the three jobs by labour cost; under (b) it becomes the **dearest**, overtaking Job Y — because it absorbed far more overtime (2,700 of its 10,700 hours) than the others. Nothing about the work changed between the two answers. Only the **reason** for the overtime changed, and with it the question of who should bear its cost. That is why an examiner can set the same figures twice and expect two different answers."}]}},{"h4":"Labour cost minimisation techniques"},{"ul":["effective labour monitoring and control;","reduction of labour turnover;","elimination or reduction of labour-related fraud."]},{"eg":{"tag":"Study text — Illustration 4.5 (ICAG, November 1999, 20 marks)","t":"Mame Coins — the full cost of farm labour, and the net wages paid","open":true,"q":[{"p":"Mame Coins employs on her farm **120 workers as direct labour** and **15 workers as indirect labour**. The farm remunerates its labour as follows:"},{"ol":["Direct labour is paid regularly **based on units of output** at the rate of **¢12,250 per unit**.","Indirect labour is paid regularly **based on hours worked** at the rate of **¢14,000 per hour**.","Overtime premium is paid to all factory workers for the hours worked at the following rates: **¢26,000 per hour for direct labour**; **¢28,000 per hour for indirect labour**.","The firm makes deductions and contributions to the social security scheme. The employer and employee contribute **10%** and **5%** respectively of gross pay.","Each worker contributes dues to the local workers' union at **¢50,000 per direct labour per month** and **¢80,000 per indirect labour per month**, deducted at source.","The firm operates a **workmen's compensation insurance** scheme for all factory workers. The monthly premium is **¢7,500,000**, divisible between direct and indirect labour in the ratio **4 : 1**.","**PAYE tax** is approximately **8% of monthly gross income**."]},{"p":"During June 1999 the farm produced **30,000 units** during regular working time of **8 hours a day for 22 days**. The farm worked overtime of **6 hours a day for 4 days** during the month and produced **3,200 more units**."},{"p":"**Required:** determine (i) the cost to Mame Coins of farm labour for June 1999, distinguishing between direct and indirect labour costs; and (ii) the total net wages paid to the workers for June 1999."}],"a":[{"h4":"Step 1 — the hours and the output"},{"stmt":{"t":"Analysis of hours and units","rows":[["","Direct workers","Indirect workers","@hd"],["Number of workers",120,15],["Regular hours each (8 hrs × 22 days)","176 hrs","176 hrs"],["Overtime hours each (6 hrs × 4 days)","24 hrs","24 hrs"],"@gap",["Total regular hours","21,120","2,640"],["Total overtime hours","2,880","360"],["**Total hours worked**","**24,000**","**3,000**","@t"],"@gap",["Units produced in regular time","30,000","—"],["Units produced in overtime","3,200","—"],["**Total units produced**","**33,200**","—","@t"]]}},{"h4":"Step 2 — basic wages"},{"p":"Direct workers are paid **by output**, indirect workers **by hours**:"},{"stmt":{"t":"Basic wages","rows":[["Direct: 33,200 units × ¢12,250","",406700000],["Indirect: 3,000 hours × ¢14,000","",42000000]]}},{"h4":"Step 3 — overtime premium"},{"p":"The overtime **rates** given are the full rates for overtime hours, so the **premium** is the excess of overtime pay over what those hours have already earned in basic wages."},{"stmt":{"t":"Direct labour overtime premium","rows":[["Overtime pay: 2,880 hrs × ¢26,000","",74880000],["Overtime piece rate per unit: ¢74,880,000 ÷ 3,200 units","","¢23,400"],["Less: normal piece rate already in basic wages","","¢12,250"],["**Premium per unit produced in overtime**","","¢11,150","@t"],["**Overtime premium: 3,200 units × ¢11,150**","",35680000,"@tt"]]}},{"stmt":{"t":"Indirect labour overtime premium","rows":[["Overtime pay: 360 hrs × ¢28,000","",10080000],["Less: those hours already paid at basic rate (360 × ¢14,000)","",-5040000],["**Overtime premium**","",5040000,"@tt"]]}},{"h4":"Step 4 — employer-borne on-costs"},{"stmt":{"t":"Workmen's compensation and employer's social security","rows":[["","Direct","Indirect","Total","@hd"],["Workmen's compensation premium, ¢7,500,000 in the ratio 4 : 1",6000000,1500000,7500000],["Employer's SSF, 10% of basic wages",40670000,4200000,44870000]]}},{"h4":"(i)  Cost to Mame Coins of farm labour for June 1999"},{"stmt":{"t":"Total labour cost, June 1999","rows":[["","Direct (¢)","Indirect (¢)","Total (¢)","@hd"],["Basic wages",406700000,42000000,448700000],["Overtime premium",35680000,5040000,40720000],["Workmen's compensation premium",6000000,1500000,7500000],["Employer's social security contribution (10%)",40670000,4200000,44870000],["**Total cost of farm labour**",489050000,52740000,541790000,"@tt"]]}},{"key":"Only the **basic wages of direct workers** — ¢406,700,000 — are *direct* labour cost in the costing sense. The overtime premium (overtime here is regular company practice, not customer-driven), the compensation insurance and the employer's social security contribution are all **policy-related on-costs** and are treated as **indirect** labour, i.e. production overhead, even though they are incurred on direct workers. The question asks for the split between direct and indirect **workers**, which is what the columns above show; be ready to be asked for the split between direct and indirect **cost**, which is a different cut of the same figures."},{"h4":"(ii)  Total net wages paid for June 1999"},{"stmt":{"t":"Payroll — direct and indirect workers","rows":[["","Direct workers (¢)","Indirect workers (¢)","@hd"],["Basic wages",406700000,42000000],["Less: employee social security contribution, 5%",-20335000,-2100000],["",386365000,39900000,"@t"],["Add: overtime premium",35680000,5040000],["**Taxable pay**",422045000,44940000,"@t"],["Less: PAYE at 8%",-33763600,-3595200],["Less: local union dues (120 × ¢50,000;  15 × ¢80,000)",-6000000,-1200000],["**Net wages paid**",382281400,40144800,"@tt"],"@gap",["Number of employees",120,15],["**Net wage per employee**","¢3,185,678","¢2,676,320"]]}},{"stmt":{"t":"Total net wages paid for June 1999","rows":[["Direct workers","",382281400],["Indirect workers","",40144800],["**Total net wages paid**","",422426200,"@tt"]]}},{"note":"Note the order of the deductions in the official solution: the employee's 5% social security is taken off the **basic wages** first, and the overtime premium is then added to arrive at taxable pay, so the premium escapes the social security charge but not PAYE. That is the treatment the model answer uses; if a question tells you social security is charged on **gross** pay, the deduction would instead be 5% of basic plus premium. Read the question, and state the basis you have used."},{"warn":"The cost to the employer (¢541,790,000) and the net wages paid to the employees (¢422,426,200) are two different questions and two different answers — the gap of ¢119,363,800 is made up of the employer's own contributions (social security and compensation insurance, ¢52,370,000) plus the deductions withheld from the employees and paid over to third parties (PAYE ¢37,358,800, employee social security ¢22,435,000 and union dues ¢7,200,000). Candidates routinely answer one when the examiner asked for the other."}]}}]},{"n":"4.6","t":"Labour turnover","b":[{"def":{"t":"Labour turnover","d":"the percentage change in the labour force during a given period — also called the labour turnover ratio (or rate)."}},{"p":"Three ways to measure it:"},{"fbox":{"h":"Labour turnover formulas","rows":[{"lb":"Labour turnover rate","tex":"\\dfrac{\\text{Number of employees leaving during the period}}{\\text{Average number of employees for the period}} \\times 100"},{"lb":"Flux method","tex":"\\dfrac{\\text{Workers joining} + \\text{Workers leaving during the period}}{\\text{Average number of employees during the period}}"},{"lb":"Separation method","tex":"\\dfrac{\\text{Number of workers replaced during the period}}{\\text{Average number of employees during the period}}"},{"lb":"Average number of employees","tex":"\\tfrac12 \\times (\\text{Number at beginning of period} + \\text{Number at end of period})"}]}},{"eg":{"tag":"Study text","t":"Illustration — labour turnover rate","open":true,"q":[{"p":"At the start of March there were 201 employees; 3 started and 5 left during the month. Find the labour turnover rate."}],"a":[{"p":"Employees at end of month $= 201 + 3 - 5 = 199$. Average number of employees $= \\tfrac12(201+199) = 200$."},{"tex":"\\text{Labour turnover rate} = \\dfrac{5}{200} \\times 100 = 2.50\\%"}]}},{"key":"To be useful, a labour turnover rate must be **compared** — against previous periods, and (where available) against other businesses in the area and the industry as a whole. A rising rate follows either more leavers or a smaller total workforce."},{"h4":"The cost of a high labour turnover rate"},{"p":"A high rate causes loss of output, lower morale and higher cost. Output is lost because of: the gap between a leaver and their replacement; the time needed to train a new employee up to the departing employee's efficiency; and reduced effort from an employee in the days/weeks immediately before they leave."},{"p":"On top of the higher unit cost from reduced production and low morale, these costs tend to rise as turnover rises:"},{"ol":["advertising for personnel, and interviewing expenses;","reimbursing removal/settling-in expenses (furniture removal, subsistence allowance between the commencement and move dates);","training — the new employee's wages during training, instructors' wages/salaries, and training materials;","scrap and defective work during a new employee's initial stages;","machine breakdown; and","pension-scheme administration."]},{"h4":"Causes of labour turnover"},{"ul":["dissatisfaction with the job or wages;","hours of work or working conditions;","discontent with supervisors or colleagues;","lack of promotion opportunities;","personal matters — ill health, marriage, pregnancy, relocation; and","discharge for redundancy, incompetence, lateness, absenteeism or discipline."]},{"h4":"Reducing the labour turnover rate"},{"ol":["provide regular statistics analysing turnover by cause, flagging avoidable/unavoidable and voluntary/dismissed — though leavers do not always give their true reason;","improve applicant selection, to prevent discontented or unsuitable hires;","plan labour requirements properly, to avoid redundancy;","consider a high-wage plan or other incentives;","keep the working environment congenial;","consider transferring a dissatisfied employee to another department;","hold annual medical check-ups, to catch ill-health early;","ensure fringe benefits (pension, subsidised meals, sports/social facilities) are competitive; and","develop better management-worker relationships."]},{"note":"A target labour turnover rate can be set, with the personnel manager held accountable when the actual rate exceeds it."},{"h4":"Payroll fraud and internal check"},{"p":"There is ample scope for fraud at every stage of preparing and paying wages, so controls must exist to prevent it, or detect it early. Two classic dangers:"},{"ul":["**Ghost workers** — paying wages to employees who do not actually exist.","Paying a real employee **more** than they are entitled to."]},{"p":"Precautions to reduce payroll fraud:"},{"ol":["segregate duties — e.g. the clerk who prepares the wage sheet should not also make up or distribute the pay packets;","adopt adequate authorisation — piece-work tickets signed by an inspector and the employee's foreman; only properly authorised overtime is paid; wage-rate/salary changes processed only on a signed form from the personnel manager;","supervise the distribution of pay envelopes by a senior official who can identify recipients;","institute suitable procedures, e.g. for handling unclaimed wages;","run a programme of internal checks — the personnel officer periodically checking the payroll for fictitious employees; and","in a sufficiently large organisation, involve internal audit in building and running the internal-check programme."]}]},{"n":"4.7","t":"Payroll preparation and accounting","b":[{"h4":"Types of payroll"},{"def":{"t":"Payroll","d":"the process of determining each employee's net wage earnings — carried out **manually** (by human effort) or via a **computerised** system, which processes inputs such as hours worked, overtime hours, units produced, the wage rate per unit/hour, and statutory and voluntary deductions, to arrive at net earnings and total deductions."}},{"h4":"Preparing the payroll"},{"steps":["Determine each employee's basic wages — hours worked (or units produced) × the agreed wage rate.","Add all other allowances to basic wages, to obtain gross wages.","Calculate the deductions, of two types: **statutory** (required by law — social security contributions, employee Personal Income Tax/PAYE, National Health Insurance) and **voluntary** (trade-union contributions, provident-fund contribution, employee-welfare contribution).","Deduct total deductions from gross wages, to obtain net wages."]},{"fbox":{"h":"Payroll build-up","rows":[{"lb":"Basic wages","tex":"\\text{Hours worked (or units produced)} \\times \\text{agreed rate}"},{"lb":"Gross wages","tex":"\\text{Basic wages} + \\text{other allowances}"},{"lb":"Net wages","tex":"\\text{Gross wages} - \\text{statutory deductions} - \\text{voluntary deductions}"}]}},{"h4":"Labour cost accounting versus payroll accounting"},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["","Labour cost accounting","Payroll accounting"],"rows":[["**Objective**","Ascertain the labour cost chargeable to products, services, jobs, customers, clients and overhead accounts.","Compute each employee's earnings, and the various payments made for/on behalf of employees."],["**What it covers**","Wages and salaries; workers' compensation insurance; employee bonuses; employee share schemes.","The employer's social security contribution; the employee's social security contribution; personal income tax; welfare deductions."],["**What it excludes**","—","Labour costs such as workmen's compensation and employee insurance — payroll accounting only considers amounts payable **to or on behalf of** employees."]]}},{"eg":{"tag":"Study text","t":"Illustration 4.6 — Clay Products Ltd: three remuneration methods compared","open":true,"q":[{"p":"Clay Products Ltd is choosing an incentive scheme. July 2005 payroll extract for four employees:"},{"table":{"align":"lrrrr","head":["","Michael","Gerald","Cynthia","William"],"rows":[["Actual hours worked","152","144","160","136"],["Wage rate per hour (₵)","45,000","30,000","37,500","54,000"],["Output — Product A (units)","168","480","—","480"],["Output — Product B (units)","288","304","—","1,080"],["Output — Product C (units)","368","—","200","—"]]}},{"p":"Standard time per unit: Product A 6 minutes, Product B 9 minutes, Product C 15 minutes; each minute earned is valued at ₵750 for piecework purposes. Required: (a) each employee's earnings under (i) the guaranteed hourly-rate method, (ii) piecework with earnings guaranteed at 75% of basic pay, and (iii) a premium bonus scheme in which the employee receives two-thirds of time saved, in addition to hourly pay. *(ICAG, Nov. 2005, 14 marks)*"}],"a":[{"h4":"(a)(i) Guaranteed hourly-rate (basic wage) method"},{"table":{"align":"lrrrr","head":["","Michael","Gerald","Cynthia","William"],"rows":[["Hours worked × rate/hour","152×45,000","144×30,000","160×37,500","136×54,000"],["**Basic wage (₵)**","**6,840,000**","**4,320,000**","**6,000,000**","**7,344,000**"]]}},{"h4":"(a)(ii) Piecework, guaranteed at 75% of basic pay"},{"p":"Piece rate per unit = standard minutes × ₵750/minute: Product A = $6\\times750 = ₵4{,}500$; Product B = $9\\times750 = ₵6{,}750$; Product C = $15\\times750 = ₵11{,}250$."},{"table":{"align":"lrrrr","head":["","Michael","Gerald","Cynthia","William"],"rows":[["Piecework earnings (₵)","168×4,500 + 288×6,750 + 368×11,250 = 6,840,000","480×4,500 + 304×6,750 = 4,212,000","200×11,250 = 2,250,000","480×4,500 + 1,080×6,750 = 9,450,000"],["75% of basic wage (₵)","5,130,000","3,240,000","4,500,000","5,508,000"],["**Entitlement (the higher of the two) (₵)**","**6,840,000 (piecework)**","**4,212,000 (piecework)**","**4,500,000 (the 75% guarantee)**","**9,450,000 (piecework)**"]]}},{"key":"Cynthia is the one employee whose piecework earnings (₵2,250,000) fall *below* the 75%-of-basic guarantee (₵4,500,000) — she alone is paid the guaranteed minimum rather than her piece-rate output; all three other employees out-earn their guarantee on piecework and are paid the piecework figure."},{"h4":"(a)(iii) Premium bonus — two-thirds of time saved, plus hourly pay"},{"p":"Time allowed = $\\sum(\\text{output} \\times \\text{standard minutes per unit})/60$, compared with actual hours worked (= time taken):"},{"table":{"align":"lrrrr","head":["","Michael","Gerald","Cynthia","William"],"rows":[["Time allowed (hrs)","(168×6+288×9+368×15)/60 = 152.0","(480×6+304×9)/60 = 93.6","(200×15)/60 = 50.0","(480×6+1,080×9)/60 = 210.0"],["Time taken (hrs)","152","144","160","136"],["Time saved (hrs)","0","nil (took longer than allowed)","nil (took longer than allowed)","74"],["Bonus (₵)","—","—","—","⅔×74×54,000 = 2,664,000"],["**Total wages (₵)**","**6,840,000** (basic only)","**4,320,000** (basic only)","**6,000,000** (basic only)","**10,008,000** (basic 7,344,000 + bonus 2,664,000)"]]}},{"note":"Gerald and Cynthia both took *longer* than their standard time allowed (Gerald 144 hrs actual against 93.6 hrs allowed; Cynthia 160 hrs actual against 50 hrs allowed) — time saved cannot be negative, so neither earns any bonus under this scheme, and each simply receives their guaranteed hourly basic wage, same as under method (i)."}]}}]},{"n":"4.8","t":"Worksheet summary — definitions, formulae and structure","b":[{"p":"Everything in this chapter, compressed for a last read-through."},{"h3":"Section-by-section checklist"},{"ol":["**§4.2 Administration** — recruitment (job analysis → advertise internally/externally → shortlist → select); induction (culture) vs placement (post-fitting); 4 control mechanisms (time-keeping, monitoring/supervision, evaluation, reward schemes); job evaluation ranks the **job**, merit rating ranks the **jobholder**; work study = method study + work measurement.","**§4.3 Remuneration** — time rate (hours × rate, simple but no output incentive) vs piece rate (units × rate, in straight/guaranteed-day-rate/differential forms); the three premium bonus schemes (Halsey ½, Halsey-Weir ⅓, Rowan proportional — Rowan and Halsey coincide exactly at 50% time saved); individual vs group incentive schemes.","**§4.4 Overtime & idle time** — overtime premium is indirect wages unless worked at a customer's specific request (then direct); idle time is normal (uncontrollable, charged to WIP) or abnormal (controllable, written off to P&L).","**§4.5 Direct/indirect labour, minimisation** — only *basic* wages of a direct worker are direct labour cost; policy-related add-ons (insurance, SSF, bonuses, regular-policy overtime premium) are always indirect, even on a direct worker; 3 cost-minimisation techniques.","**§4.6 Labour turnover** — 3 formulas (turnover rate, flux method, separation method), all needing the average-employees denominator; causes and 9 named reduction measures; ghost workers and payroll-fraud controls (segregation of duties, authorisation, supervision, internal checks).","**§4.7 Payroll** — manual vs computerised; the 4-step preparation procedure; statutory vs voluntary deductions; labour cost accounting (cost to products) vs payroll accounting (amounts payable to/for employees) are **not** the same scope."]},{"h3":"All the terms"},{"ul":["**Time allowed** — the expected time for a task. **Time taken** — actual time spent. **Time saved** — allowed minus taken.","**Job evaluation** — ranks the job. **Merit rating** — ranks the jobholder's performance.","**Method study** — finds a better way to do work. **Work measurement** — times the chosen way.","**Normal idle time** — uncontrollable, costed to WIP. **Abnormal idle time** — controllable, written off to P&L.","**Overtime premium** — indirect wages, unless the overtime was at a customer's specific request.","**Labour turnover** — the percentage change in the workforce over a period. **Ghost worker** — a non-existent employee wages are fraudulently paid to.","**Labour cost accounting** — costing labour to products/jobs. **Payroll accounting** — computing and paying what is owed to/for employees."]},{"h3":"B. Formula box"},{"fbox":{"h":"Labour cost formulas (recap)","rows":[{"lb":"Gross wage (time rate)","tex":"\\text{Hours worked} \\times \\text{Rate/hour}"},{"lb":"Straight piece rate","tex":"\\text{Units produced} \\times \\text{Rate/unit}"},{"lb":"Halsey bonus","tex":"\\tfrac12 \\times \\text{Time saved} \\times \\text{Day rate}"},{"lb":"Halsey-Weir bonus","tex":"\\tfrac13 \\times \\text{Time saved} \\times \\text{Day rate}"},{"lb":"Rowan bonus","tex":"\\dfrac{\\text{Time saved}}{\\text{Time allowed}} \\times \\text{Time taken} \\times \\text{Day rate}"},{"lb":"Labour turnover rate","tex":"\\dfrac{\\text{Leavers}}{\\text{Average employees}} \\times 100"}]}}]},{"n":"4.9","t":"End-of-chapter questions (study text)","b":[{"eg":{"tag":"Study text","t":"Multiple-choice and short-answer questions, with answers","open":true,"q":[{"ol":["Under labour incentive schemes, bonus is paid (A) Every December  (B) Each time the company receives a large order  (C) To very good employees  (D) Anytime there is surplus money in the treasury  (E) Over and above the basic pay to reward extra time worked or time saved","Overtime is (A) Work done over a period of time  (B) Time spent calling over production figures  (C) Time spent by the employee working beyond the normal working hours  (D) Time spent by the employee in the changing room  (E) Time wasted when production machines are idle","PAYE is an acronym for (A) Pay As You Engage  (B) Pay According to Your Expectation  (C) Pay All Your Employees  (D) Payment At Year End  (E) Pay As You Earn","Ghost workers are (A) Former employees who are now dead  (B) Employees involved in an accident whilst working  (C) Employees always on sick leave  (D) Workers who do not exist but in whose names salaries are being paid  (E) Those who work in the cemetery","Gross wages are calculated by adding (A) All allowances to basic wages  (B) All deductions to basic wages  (C) Pension deduction to PAYE  (D) Bonuses and allowances together  (E) Twelve months' wages together","Time allowed minus time taken equals …………","Labour costs incurred on employees engaged in directly transforming raw materials into finished goods are referred to as ……………….","The extent to which employees leave an organisation is known as …………………","The product of hours worked and wage rate per hour is …………………………….","The card issued in the name of each employee, inserted into an electronic recording machine to capture time spent at work, is called...…………"]}],"a":[{"ol":["**E** — over and above the basic pay, to reward extra time worked or time saved.","**C** — time spent by the employee working beyond the normal working hours.","**E — Pay As You Earn.**","**D** — workers who do not exist but in whose names salaries are being paid.","**A** — all allowances added to basic wages.","**Time saved.**","**Direct labour cost(s).**","**Labour turnover.**","**Basic wage.**","**Clock card.**"]}]}}]},{"n":"4.10","t":"Study text examination-type questions, worked","b":[{"p":"The study text closes the chapter with nine examination-type questions. All are reproduced below with full answers. (The book leaves the ACCA Costing question between 4.4 and 4.6 unnumbered; it is shown here as 4.5.)"},{"eg":{"tag":"Exam type — Q4.1 (20 marks) — ICAG, November 2003","t":"Cee Ltd — a group bonus scheme, net pay, and a cost-plus charge per household","q":[{"p":"Cee Ltd is a waste collection company engaged in the collection of household refuse in the Gonor District. The District is divided into **three areas** made up of a total of **1,000 households**. Each area enjoys 2 days of refuse collection during the **6-day working week of 48 hours**."},{"p":"During April, the company employed **2 drivers and 4 labourers**, grouped into **2 gangs**, Gang A and Gang B. A gang is made up of 1 driver and 2 labourers. A driver is paid **¢2,500 per hour** and a labourer **¢3,125 per hour**."},{"p":"A **group bonus scheme** is in operation, which requires that hours over the normal working hours are **shared equally among members of a gang** and paid at a rate of **¢10,000 per driver** and **¢12,000 per labourer**. During April, Gang A worked **219 hours** and Gang B worked **228 hours**."},{"p":"**Required:** (a) calculate the total cost of providing this service in the district for April *(8 marks)*; (b) calculate the net pay due to the workers for April *(8 marks)*; (c) the company charges the households at cost plus 20% — how much should Cee Ltd charge each household for the month? *(4 marks)*"},{"p":"**Assume:** (1) an income tax rate of 10% of total earnings; (2) statutory social security deductions of 12% for the employer and 5% for the employee; (3) 4 weeks in the month of April."}],"a":[{"h4":"Step 1 — normal hours and excess hours"},{"stmt":{"t":"Hours","rows":[["Normal hours for the month (48 hours × 4 weeks)","","192 hours"],"@gap",["Gang A — hours worked","","219 hours"],["Gang A — hours over normal (219 − 192)","","27 hours"],["Shared equally among the 3 gang members (27 ÷ 3)","","9 hours each"],"@gap",["Gang B — hours worked","","228 hours"],["Gang B — hours over normal (228 − 192)","","36 hours"],["Shared equally among the 3 gang members (36 ÷ 3)","","12 hours each"]]}},{"h4":"Step 2 — basic wages and group bonus"},{"stmt":{"t":"Basic wages for the month (192 normal hours each)","rows":[["Drivers:  2 × 192 hrs × ¢2,500","",960000],["Labourers:  4 × 192 hrs × ¢3,125","",2400000],["**Total basic wages**","",3360000,"@t"]]}},{"stmt":{"t":"Group bonus","rows":[["","Gang A (¢)","Gang B (¢)","Total (¢)","@hd"],["Driver:  9 hrs × ¢10,000;  12 hrs × ¢10,000",90000,120000,210000],["Labourers:  2 × 9 × ¢12,000;  2 × 12 × ¢12,000",216000,288000,504000],["**Bonus**",306000,408000,714000,"@tt"]]}},{"h4":"(a)  Total cost of providing the service for April"},{"stmt":{"t":"Total cost to the company","rows":[["Basic wages","",3360000],["Group bonus","",714000],["**Gross earnings**","",4074000,"@t"],["Employer's social security contribution, 12% of ¢4,074,000","",488880],["**Total cost of providing the service**","",4562880,"@tt"]]}},{"h4":"(b)  Net pay due to the workers for April"},{"stmt":{"t":"Net pay","rows":[["Gross earnings","",4074000],["Less: income tax at 10% of total earnings","",-407400],["Less: employee social security at 5%","",-203700],["**Net pay due to the workers**","",3462900,"@tt"]]}},{"h4":"(c)  Charge per household"},{"stmt":{"t":"Cost plus 20%","rows":[["Total cost (from (a))","",4562880],["Add: mark-up at 20%","",912576],["**Total charge to the district**","",5475456,"@t"],["Number of households","",1000],["**Charge per household for the month**","","¢5,475.46","@tt"]]}},{"key":"The three parts answer three different questions and must not be confused. **(a)** is the cost to the **employer** — gross earnings *plus* the employer's own social security contribution. **(b)** is what the **workers receive** — gross earnings *less* the deductions withheld from them. The employer's 12% never appears in (b), and the employee's 5% and the 10% tax never appear in (a). **(c)** builds on (a), because a price must recover what the service actually costs the company, not what the workers take home."},{"note":"The three areas and the two collection days per week are context, not computation — nothing in the answer depends on them. Examiners include such detail deliberately; identify what each figure is *for* before using it."}]}},{"eg":{"tag":"Exam type — Q4.2","t":"Five sequential hand operations — workers required and weekly labour cost","q":[{"p":"An item passes through 5 successive hand operations in sequence as follows:"},{"stmt":{"t":"Operation data","rows":[["Operation","Time per article (minutes)","Grade of employee","Wage rate per hour","@hd"],[1,12,"A","50p"],[2,20,"B","55p"],[3,30,"C","60p"],[4,10,"D","70p"],[5,15,"E","80p"]]}},{"p":"A 40-hour week is worked and a production target of **720 dozen items** has been set. **Required:** calculate (a) the number of workers required; (b) the weekly cost of each operation; (c) the total labour cost per week."},{"warn":"The study text prints the working week as \"An Hour week\", with the number lost. A **40-hour week** is assumed below — the standard figure in this question and the only one that gives whole-ish worker numbers. State the assumption if you meet this question in an exam.","lbl":"Source gap"}],"a":[{"h4":"Step 1 — convert the target into articles"},{"tex":"720 \\text{ dozen} \\times 12 = 8{,}640 \\text{ articles per week}"},{"h4":"(a)  Number of workers required"},{"p":"Every article passes through **every** operation, so each operation must handle all 8,640 articles. Hours required = articles × minutes per article ÷ 60; workers = hours required ÷ 40."},{"stmt":{"t":"(a)  Workers required, by operation","rows":[["Operation","Minutes per article","Total minutes","Hours required","Workers (÷ 40 hrs)","@hd"],[1,12,"103,680","1,728","43.2  →  **44**"],[2,20,"172,800","2,880","72.0  →  **72**"],[3,30,"259,200","4,320","108.0  →  **108**"],[4,10,"86,400","1,440","36.0  →  **36**"],[5,15,"129,600","2,160","54.0  →  **54**"],["**Total**","**87**","**751,680**","**12,528**","**314**","@tt"]]}},{"note":"Operation 1 needs 43.2 workers. You cannot employ two-tenths of a person, so **44** must be engaged — the 44th works only part of the week, or the whole gang works slightly below capacity. Rounding **up** is the only safe direction: rounding down to 43 would leave the target unmet, and because the operations are **in sequence**, a shortfall at operation 1 starves all four downstream operations."},{"h4":"(b)  Weekly cost of each operation"},{"stmt":{"t":"(b)  Weekly cost by operation, on hours actually required","rows":[["Operation","Hours required","Rate per hour","Weekly cost","@hd"],[1,"1,728","50p","£864.00"],[2,"2,880","55p","£1,584.00"],[3,"4,320","60p","£2,592.00"],[4,"1,440","70p","£1,008.00"],[5,"2,160","80p","£1,728.00"],["**Total**","**12,528**","","**£7,776.00**","@tt"]]}},{"h4":"(c)  Total labour cost per week"},{"p":"On the hours actually required, **£7,776.00**."},{"note":"If instead the workers engaged are paid for a full 40-hour week whether or not the hours are needed, operation 1 costs $44 \\times 40 \\times 50p = £880$ instead of £864, and the weekly total becomes **£7,792.00** — the extra £16 being the idle time of the part-used 44th worker at operation 1. Only operation 1 is affected; the other four divide exactly. State which basis you have used."},{"key":"The point of a sequential-operation question is **line balancing**. Operation 3 needs 108 workers and operation 4 only 36 — a three-to-one imbalance. The output of the whole line is set by its slowest stage, so if the staffing is wrong at any one operation, work piles up in front of it and the stages after it stand idle, whatever the headcount elsewhere."}]}},{"eg":{"tag":"Exam type — Q4.3 (20 marks) — ICAG, January 1989","t":"Handcraft Ltd — standard hours, bonus, overtime and total wages for three craftsmen","q":[{"p":"Kofi, Yaw and Kwesi are three craftsmen employed by Handcraft Ltd on the carving of two models of traditional sculpture known as **\"SS 77\"** and **\"SS 88\"**. Each craftsman completes **8 \"SS 77\"** and **10 \"SS 88\"** sculptures per week. Production is then inspected for defective work."},{"p":"The remuneration scheme provides for a **40-hour week**, for which Kofi is paid **¢48 per hour**, Yaw **¢54** and Kwesi **¢60**. Time worked in excess of 40 hours in any week is paid: the **first 4 hours** at time and a third; **additional hours** at time and a half. In addition, a **bonus of 25% of time saved** is paid on work completed during the week, at a bonus hourly rate of **¢50** for all workers. **No bonus is paid on defective work.**"},{"p":"The time allowance in hours per completed sculpture passing inspection is:"},{"stmt":{"t":"Time allowance (hours per good sculpture)","rows":[["","Kofi","Yaw","Kwesi","@hd"],["\"SS 77\" model",5,4,3],["\"SS 88\" model",3,5,2]]}},{"p":"The following facts relate to week No. 34:"},{"stmt":{"t":"Week 34 data","rows":[["","Kofi","Yaw","Kwesi","@hd"],["(a) Hours worked in week 34",44,46,40],["(b) Hours worked in week 33 on uncompleted sculptures brought forward",8,7,5],["(c) Hours worked on uncompleted sculptures carried forward to week 35",7,8,3],["(d) Defective \"SS 77\" sculptures",1,2,"—"],["(d) Defective \"SS 88\" sculptures","—",1,1]]}},{"p":"**Required:** calculate the total wages earned by each craftsman for week 34, showing separately (i) the standard hours allowed, excluding time for defective work; (ii) bonus hours and bonus payable; (iii) overtime payable; (iv) basic wage payable."}],"a":[{"h4":"(i)  Standard hours allowed, excluding defective work"},{"p":"Each craftsman completed 8 \"SS 77\" and 10 \"SS 88\". Deduct the defectives, then apply each craftsman's **own** time allowance — the allowances differ by person:"},{"stmt":{"t":"(i)  Standard hours allowed","rows":[["","Kofi","Yaw","Kwesi","@hd"],["\"SS 77\" completed",8,8,8],["Less defective",-1,-2,0],["Good \"SS 77\"",7,6,8],["× time allowance (hrs)",5,4,3],["Standard hours — \"SS 77\"",35,24,24],"@gap",["\"SS 88\" completed",10,10,10],["Less defective",0,-1,-1],["Good \"SS 88\"",10,9,9],["× time allowance (hrs)",3,5,2],["Standard hours — \"SS 88\"",30,45,18],"@gap",["**Total standard hours allowed**",65,69,42,"@tt"]]}},{"h4":"(ii)  Bonus hours and bonus payable"},{"p":"The bonus is paid on **work completed during the week**, so the time taken must be the time spent on *those* sculptures — hours worked this week, **plus** the hours brought forward from week 33 on sculptures finished this week, **less** the hours spent this week on sculptures not yet finished:"},{"stmt":{"t":"(ii)  Time saved and bonus","rows":[["","Kofi","Yaw","Kwesi","@hd"],["Hours worked in week 34",44,46,40],["Add: hours brought forward from week 33",8,7,5],["Less: hours carried forward to week 35",-7,-8,-3],["**Time taken on work completed**",45,45,42,"@t"],["Standard hours allowed (from (i))",65,69,42],["**Time saved**","20 hrs","24 hrs","nil","@t"],"@gap",["**Bonus hours (25% of time saved)**","5 hrs","6 hrs","nil"],["**Bonus payable at ¢50 per bonus hour**","¢250","¢300","¢—","@tt"]]}},{"note":"Kwesi saved no time at all — his 42 standard hours exactly equal his 42 hours taken — so he earns no bonus, even though he produced the most good \"SS 77\" sculptures of the three. His time allowances are the tightest (3 and 2 hours against Kofi's 5 and 3), which is the price of being the fastest worker when allowances are set per person."},{"h4":"(iii)  Overtime payable"},{"p":"Overtime is the excess over the 40-hour week. Only the **premium** is shown here, since the hours themselves are already paid in the basic wage at (iv):"},{"stmt":{"t":"(iii)  Overtime premium","rows":[["","Kofi","Yaw","Kwesi","@hd"],["Hours worked",44,46,40],["Overtime hours (above 40)",4,6,0],["First 4 hrs at time and a third — premium ⅓ × rate × hrs","4 × ⅓ × ¢48 = ¢64","4 × ⅓ × ¢54 = ¢72","—"],["Additional hrs at time and a half — premium ½ × rate × hrs","—","2 × ½ × ¢54 = ¢54","—"],["**Overtime premium payable**","¢64","¢126","¢—","@tt"]]}},{"h4":"(iv)  Basic wage payable"},{"stmt":{"t":"(iv)  Basic wage = hours worked × hourly rate","rows":[["","Kofi","Yaw","Kwesi","@hd"],["Hours worked × rate  (44 × 48;  46 × 54;  40 × 60)","¢2,112","¢2,484","¢2,400"]]}},{"h4":"Total wages earned in week 34"},{"stmt":{"t":"Total wages, week 34","rows":[["","Kofi (¢)","Yaw (¢)","Kwesi (¢)","@hd"],["Basic wage",2112,2484,2400],["Overtime premium",64,126,0],["Bonus",250,300,0],["**Total wages earned**",2426,2910,2400,"@tt"]]}},{"key":"The hinge of this question is the **brought-forward / carried-forward adjustment**. The bonus rewards time saved on sculptures *finished*, so the hours compared against the standard allowance must cover exactly those sculptures — including hours spent on them last week, and excluding hours spent this week on pieces still on the bench. A candidate who simply compares the standard hours against the hours worked gets Kofi's time saved as 21 instead of 20 and Yaw's as 23 instead of 24, and loses the marks for parts (i) and (ii) together."}]}},{"eg":{"tag":"Exam type — Q4.4 (20 marks)","t":"End Times Co. Ltd — wages control, work-in-progress and production overhead accounts","q":[{"p":"End Times Co. Ltd manufactures two products, **Miaweni** and **Yirewoho**. Below is a summary of the payroll data of the production department for March 2006:"},{"stmt":{"t":"Payroll data, March 2006","rows":[["","Direct workers","Indirect workers","@hd"],["Hours worked — ordinary time","3,600 hrs","800 hrs"],["Hours worked — overtime","650 hrs","80 hrs"],["Wage rate per hour","¢15,000","¢20,000"]]}},{"p":"The company pays overtime at **time and a half**. Overtime is generally worked to meet **budgeted production targets**. Analysis of the time used by the direct workers on the two products reveals:"},{"stmt":{"t":"Direct workers' time analysis","rows":[["Miaweni","","2,400 hours"],["Yirewoho","","1,150 hours"],["Idle time","","700 hours"],["**Total**","","**4,250 hours**","@tt"]]}},{"p":"**Required:** **(a)** prepare (i) the wages control account, (ii) the work-in-progress control account, (iii) the production overhead control account *(12 marks)*; **(b)** explain the term \"labour turnover cost\" and state four examples *(3 marks)*; **(c)** outline five causes of labour turnover *(5 marks)*."}],"a":[{"h4":"Step 1 — total wages incurred"},{"stmt":{"t":"Wages incurred, March 2006","rows":[["","Direct workers (¢)","Indirect workers (¢)","Total (¢)","@hd"],["Total hours × basic rate  (4,250 × 15,000;  880 × 20,000)",63750000,17600000,81350000],["Overtime premium  (650 × ½ × 15,000;  80 × ½ × 20,000)",4875000,800000,5675000],["**Total wages**",68625000,18400000,87025000,"@tt"]]}},{"h4":"Step 2 — splitting the wages between direct and indirect"},{"p":"Overtime is worked **generally, to meet budgeted targets**, not at any customer's request, so the overtime premium is production overhead. Idle time is always overhead. What is left of the direct workers' basic pay is direct labour:"},{"stmt":{"t":"Analysis of the ¢87,025,000","rows":[["**Direct labour — to work-in-progress**","","","@hd"],["Miaweni: 2,400 hrs × ¢15,000",36000000,""],["Yirewoho: 1,150 hrs × ¢15,000",17250000,53250000],"@gap",["**Indirect labour — to production overhead**","","","@hd"],["Direct workers' idle time: 700 hrs × ¢15,000",10500000,""],["Direct workers' overtime premium",4875000,""],["Indirect workers' basic wages: 880 hrs × ¢20,000",17600000,""],["Indirect workers' overtime premium",800000,33775000],["**Total wages**","",87025000,"@tt"]]}},{"h4":"(a)(i)  Wages Control Account"},{"tacc":{"t":"Wages Control Account","drl":"Dr  (¢)","crl":"Cr  (¢)","dr":[["Bank / wages payable",87025000],["",87025000,"@tot"]],"cr":[["Work-in-progress control (direct labour)",53250000],["Production overhead control (indirect labour)",33775000],["",87025000,"@tot"]]}},{"h4":"(a)(ii)  Work-in-Progress Control Account"},{"tacc":{"t":"Work-in-Progress Control Account","drl":"Dr  (¢)","crl":"Cr  (¢)","dr":[["Wages control — Miaweni",36000000],["Wages control — Yirewoho",17250000],["",53250000,"@tot"]],"cr":[["*(materials, overhead absorbed and transfers to finished goods would also appear here — only the labour entries are required)*",""]]}},{"h4":"(a)(iii)  Production Overhead Control Account"},{"tacc":{"t":"Production Overhead Control Account","drl":"Dr  (¢)","crl":"Cr  (¢)","dr":[["Wages control — idle time of direct workers",10500000],["Wages control — overtime premium, direct workers",4875000],["Wages control — indirect workers' wages",17600000],["Wages control — overtime premium, indirect workers",800000],["",33775000,"@tot"]],"cr":[["*(absorbed into work-in-progress at the overhead absorption rate; any balance is the under- or over-absorption)*",""]]}},{"h4":"(b)  Labour turnover cost"},{"p":"**Labour turnover cost** is the total cost a business incurs as a consequence of employees leaving and having to be replaced. It falls into two groups: **preventive costs**, incurred to stop people leaving, and **replacement costs**, incurred because they left."},{"ol":["**Recruitment and selection costs** — advertising, agency fees, interviewing, testing and the administrative time of processing applicants.","**Training and induction costs** of the new employee, and the time of the experienced staff who train them.","**Lost output and lower productivity** while the post is vacant and during the new employee's learning period, plus the overtime or temporary labour used to cover.","**Increased scrap, rework and machine breakdown** caused by inexperienced operators, and higher accident and inspection costs."]},{"note":"Two further examples that earn marks: **preventive costs** such as improved welfare facilities, pension and medical schemes, and higher wage rates paid to retain staff; and **leaving costs** such as redundancy or terminal payments, and the administrative cost of processing the departure."},{"h4":"(c)  Causes of labour turnover"},{"ol":["**Poor remuneration** — wages, bonuses or conditions below those available elsewhere in the industry.","**Lack of promotion prospects, training or career development**, so ambitious staff leave to progress.","**Poor working conditions** — unsafe, uncomfortable or badly equipped workplaces, unsocial hours or excessive overtime.","**Poor supervision and management**, including unfair treatment, favouritism, bad industrial relations and lack of recognition.","**Job dissatisfaction** — monotonous or repetitive work, or work that does not use the employee's skills.","**Unavoidable or personal causes** — retirement, illness, death, marriage, pregnancy, relocation of the employee or of a spouse, further education, or dismissal and redundancy."]}]}},{"eg":{"tag":"Exam type — Q4.5 (15 marks) — ACCA Costing","t":"How the cost accountant helps control labour cost, and the problems faced","q":[{"p":"How can the Cost Accountant help to control labour cost in an organisation, and what are some of the problems to be faced in labour cost control?"}],"a":[{"h4":"How the Cost Accountant helps to control labour cost"},{"ol":["**Designing the time-recording system** — clock cards, job cards, time sheets, idle time cards and route cards — so that every hour paid for can be traced either to a job or to a reason for not being on a job.","**Analysing wages into direct and indirect**, and into productive and non-productive time, so that the cost of idle time, overtime premium and labour-related on-costs is visible instead of buried in a single wages figure.","**Setting standard times and standard rates** for each operation, and reporting the **labour rate** and **labour efficiency variances** monthly to the managers who can act on them (Chapter 13).","**Preparing labour budgets** and comparing actual hours and cost against them for each cost centre.","**Measuring and reporting labour turnover**, its cost, and the departments where it is concentrated.","**Reporting idle time by cause** — machine breakdown, material shortage, power failure, waiting for instructions — so the cause rather than the symptom is attacked.","**Evaluating incentive and bonus schemes** — calculating whether a proposed scheme actually reduces cost per unit once the bonus is paid, and monitoring whether it is still doing so.","**Assisting in payroll control** — reconciling the payroll to the time records, and operating controls against ghost workers and other payroll fraud.","**Providing information for make-or-buy, overtime-versus-extra-shift and recruitment decisions.**"]},{"h4":"Problems faced in labour cost control"},{"ol":["**Unreliable source data** — time sheets completed carelessly or dishonestly, hours booked to the wrong job, and idle time concealed as productive time.","**Resistance from employees and trade unions** to time study, standard setting and incentive schemes, which may be seen as a speed-up.","**Difficulty of setting fair standard times**, especially for non-repetitive work, and the lengthy negotiation needed before rates can be agreed.","**Standards going out of date** as methods, equipment or product mix change, so that variances measure the staleness of the standard rather than performance.","**Labour cost is largely fixed in the short run** — staff cannot be laid off and re-engaged week by week, so \"control\" over a short period is limited to how the hours are used, not how many are paid for.","**Conflict between cost and quality** — pressure on labour cost can increase scrap, rework and accidents, which cost more than the labour saved.","**Allocating indirect and policy-related labour costs** (overtime premium, bonus, social security, idle time) to products on a basis that is both fair and understood.","**The cost of the control system itself** — detailed time recording and analysis consume clerical effort, and must be justified by the savings they produce."]}]}},{"eg":{"tag":"Exam type — Q4.6 (20 marks)","t":"Labour cost control versus labour cost reduction","q":[{"p":"Explain the differences between labour cost control and labour cost reduction, and outline five practical ways of achieving labour cost control as well as labour cost reduction."}],"a":[{"h4":"The difference"},{"table":{"align":"lll","head":["","Labour cost **control**","Labour cost **reduction**"],"rows":[["**Aim**","To keep labour cost **within a predetermined target** — a standard or budget.","To achieve a **permanent, real reduction** in labour cost per unit, below the existing target."],["**Reference point**","A standard or budget already set; success means not exceeding it.","The existing level of cost itself; success means a new, lower level."],["**Nature**","**Preventive and corrective** — monitor, compare, investigate variances, act.","**Innovative** — challenge the existing method, not merely police it."],["**Duration**","Continuous and ongoing, period by period.","Once achieved, the saving is **permanent**; the exercise is typically a project."],["**Effect on the standard**","The standard is **maintained**.","The standard itself is **revised downwards**."],["**Quality and function**","Assumes the present method and quality are right.","Must be achieved **without** reducing the quality or utility of the product."]]}},{"h4":"Five practical ways of achieving labour cost **control**"},{"ol":["**Set standard times and standard rates** for every operation, and report labour rate and efficiency variances against them regularly.","**Prepare labour budgets** for each cost centre and compare actual hours and cost against them, investigating significant differences.","**Maintain accurate time records** — clock cards for attendance time and job cards for job time — and reconcile the two, so that all attendance time is accounted for.","**Record and report idle time by cause**, and act on the causes (breakdowns, material shortages, poor scheduling) rather than merely recording the hours.","**Control overtime by requiring prior authorisation**, and report the overtime premium separately so its true cost is visible.","**Monitor labour turnover** and the ratio of indirect to direct labour, and investigate adverse trends."]},{"h4":"Five practical ways of achieving labour cost **reduction**"},{"ol":["**Work study and method study** — redesign the operation itself so that it takes fewer labour hours, then re-time it and reset the standard.","**Mechanisation and automation** — substitute capital for labour where the investment is justified by the saving.","**Improved training and better job placement**, so that each employee works faster, produces less scrap and needs less supervision.","**Redesign the product (value analysis)** so that it needs fewer or simpler operations to make, without loss of quality or utility.","**Introduce or redesign incentive schemes** that genuinely raise output per hour, so that fixed overhead and supervision are spread over more units and the labour cost per unit falls even though the hourly wage rises.","**Improve working conditions, layout and material flow** to cut handling, waiting and avoidable idle time, and to reduce labour turnover and absenteeism."]},{"key":"The sentence that distinguishes the two in one line: **cost control stops costs rising above a standard; cost reduction lowers the standard itself.** A business that only controls cost can never become cheaper than it already is. Chapter 15 develops cost reduction in full."}]}},{"eg":{"tag":"Exam type — Q4.7 (20 marks)","t":"Individual versus group incentive schemes","q":[{"p":"What is the difference between an individual incentive scheme and a group incentive scheme? Outline **three** advantages and **three** disadvantages for each of the two types of incentive scheme."}],"a":[{"h4":"The difference"},{"p":"An **individual incentive scheme** rewards each employee according to **their own** output or time saved — the Halsey, Halsey-Weir and Rowan premium bonus schemes, and straight and differential piece rates, are all of this kind. A **group incentive scheme** rewards a **team** according to the team's collective output or saving, the bonus then being shared among the members on an agreed formula. Group schemes are used where output cannot sensibly be attributed to one person — on an assembly line, in a process plant, or where a gang works together, as in Q4.1 above."},{"h4":"Individual incentive schemes"},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Advantages","Disadvantages"],"rows":[["**Direct link between effort and reward** — each employee sees that their own extra effort produces their own extra pay, which is the strongest motivator available.","**Quality may be sacrificed for speed**, so more must be spent on inspection and rectification."],["**The efficient are distinguished from the inefficient**, so the scheme is seen as fair and the better workers are retained.","**Unhealthy rivalry and poor co-operation** between employees; workers may hoard materials, tools or easy jobs."],["**Less supervision is needed**, since employees are self-motivated; and fixed overhead per unit falls as output rises.","**Costly and slow to administer** — standard times must be set for every operation, negotiated with the union, and kept up to date, and individual output must be measured and recorded."]]}},{"h4":"Group incentive schemes"},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Advantages","Disadvantages"],"rows":[["**Enhances team spirit and organisational cohesiveness**, and encourages members to help each other.","**Lazy members are rewarded equally with hard-working ones**, which the hard workers resent."],["**Quality of output is not unduly compromised**, because the group polices its own members and no individual gains by rushing.","**Weaker link between individual effort and reward**, so the incentive to work harder is diluted — the larger the group, the weaker the link."],["**Relatively easy and less expensive to administer** than individual schemes, and it avoids unhealthy competitive rivalry.","**Disputes over the sharing formula**, and the scheme is unusable where a group's output genuinely cannot be measured."]]}}]}},{"eg":{"tag":"Exam type — Q4.8 (20 marks)","t":"Labour cost accounting versus payroll accounting; turnover costs; reducing turnover","q":[{"p":"**(a)** Explain the differences between labour cost accounting and payroll accounting *(6 marks)*."},{"p":"**(b)** List examples of labour turnover cost *(7 marks)*."},{"p":"**(c)** Explain **seven** ways of avoiding or reducing labour turnover *(7 marks)*."}],"a":[{"h4":"(a)  Labour cost accounting versus payroll accounting"},{"table":{"align":"lll","head":["","Payroll accounting","Labour cost accounting"],"rows":[["**Question it answers**","**How much is each employee to be paid, and what must be deducted?**","**What did labour cost each job, product, process and cost centre?**"],["**Starting point**","Attendance time — the clock card.","Job time — the job card, time sheet or route card."],["**Output**","The payroll: gross pay, statutory and voluntary deductions, net pay, and the payslip for each employee.","The analysis of wages into direct labour charged to jobs, and indirect labour charged to production overhead."],["**Purpose**","To **pay people correctly and lawfully**, and to account to the authorities for tax and social security withheld.","To **cost products and control performance** — pricing, variance analysis, decision-making."],["**Users**","Employees, the revenue authority, the social security authority.","Management, internally."],["**Link between them**","The payroll establishes the **total** wages bill.","Labour cost accounting takes that total and **analyses where it went** — and the two must reconcile exactly."]]}},{"note":"The relationship in one line: **payroll accounting decides what is paid; labour cost accounting decides what it is charged to.** Every naira on the payroll must appear somewhere in the cost accounts — as direct labour in work-in-progress, or as indirect labour in production overhead."},{"h4":"(b)  Examples of labour turnover cost"},{"p":"**Preventive costs** — incurred to stop employees leaving:"},{"ul":["personnel and welfare department costs;","medical services, canteen, sports and recreation facilities;","pension, gratuity and other retirement benefit schemes;","higher wage rates and fringe benefits paid to retain staff;","training and career development provided to improve prospects."]},{"p":"**Replacement costs** — incurred because employees did leave:"},{"ul":["advertising, recruitment agency fees, interviewing and selection costs;","induction and training of the replacement, and the trainer's time;","lost output while the post is vacant and during the learning period;","lower productivity, higher scrap, defectives and rework by inexperienced staff;","increased machine breakdowns, tool damage and accidents;","overtime, temporary staff or sub-contracting used to cover the gap;","extra supervision and inspection of new employees;","terminal payments, redundancy pay and the administrative cost of leaving."]},{"h4":"(c)  Seven ways of avoiding or reducing labour turnover"},{"ol":["**Pay competitive wages and fringe benefits**, reviewed against the rates available in the same industry and locality.","**Improve working conditions** — a safe, healthy, well-lit and well-equipped workplace, with reasonable hours and no excessive compulsory overtime.","**Provide training and clear promotion prospects**, so that ambitious employees can advance inside the organisation rather than outside it.","**Select and place employees properly**, matching the person to the job at recruitment so that neither boredom nor inability causes them to leave.","**Improve supervision and industrial relations** — fair treatment, effective grievance procedures, recognition of good work, and genuine consultation with employee representatives.","**Introduce job rotation, job enlargement and job enrichment** to relieve the monotony of repetitive work.","**Provide security and welfare** — pension and gratuity schemes, medical cover, housing or transport assistance, and reasonable job security.","**Conduct exit interviews** and act on what they reveal, so that the real causes of leaving are addressed rather than guessed at."]}]}},{"eg":{"tag":"Exam type — Q4.9","t":"PUCT — remuneration under the time rate, payment by result and a bonus scheme","q":[{"p":"**(a)** Write formulae for remuneration payable using (i) the time rate system and (ii) payment by result."},{"p":"**(b)** PUCT provides the extracted details of its employees' labour records:"},{"stmt":{"t":"PUCT — employee records","rows":[["","Sanity","Moses","@hd"],["Units produced",90,80],["Standard time allowed (minutes per unit produced)",30,30],["Actual time worked (hours)",35,40],["Rate per hour","N200","N250"],["Rate per unit","N66.70","N68.00"]]}},{"p":"**Required:** **1.** Determine the remuneration payable using (i) the time rate system, (ii) payment by result, and (iii) a bonus scheme in which the bonus is the product of time saved and the hourly rate. **2.** Which of the two employees is efficient?"}],"a":[{"h4":"(a)  The formulae"},{"fbox":{"h":"Remuneration formulae","rows":[{"lb":"(i)  Time rate system","tex":"\\text{Earnings} = \\text{Hours worked} \\times \\text{Rate per hour}","nt":"Pay depends on time spent at work, not on output. Where overtime is worked, the premium is added."},{"lb":"(ii)  Payment by result (piece rate)","tex":"\\text{Earnings} = \\text{Units produced} \\times \\text{Rate per unit}","nt":"Pay depends on output, not on time taken."},{"lb":"Bonus scheme (as specified in this question)","tex":"\\text{Earnings} = (\\text{Hours worked} \\times \\text{Rate per hour}) + (\\text{Time saved} \\times \\text{Rate per hour})","nt":"Time saved = time allowed − time taken. Note this scheme gives the employee **all** of the time saved; the Halsey scheme gives half and the Halsey-Weir a third."}]}},{"h4":"Working — time allowed and time saved"},{"stmt":{"t":"Time allowed and time saved","rows":[["","Sanity","Moses","@hd"],["Units produced",90,80],["Standard time per unit","30 min","30 min"],["Time allowed  (90 × 30 ÷ 60;  80 × 30 ÷ 60)","45 hrs","40 hrs"],["Time taken (actual hours worked)","35 hrs","40 hrs"],["**Time saved**","**10 hrs**","**nil**","@tt"]]}},{"h4":"1.  Remuneration under each system"},{"stmt":{"t":"Remuneration payable","rows":[["","Sanity (N)","Moses (N)","@hd"],["**(i) Time rate**  (35 × 200;  40 × 250)",7000,10000],"@gap",["**(ii) Payment by result**  (90 × 66.70;  80 × 68.00)",6003,5440],"@gap",["**(iii) Bonus scheme** — basic pay (hours worked × hourly rate)",7000,10000],["            add bonus (time saved × hourly rate: 10 × 200;  0 × 250)",2000,0],["            **total under the bonus scheme**",9000,10000,"@t"]]}},{"h4":"2.  Which employee is efficient?"},{"p":"Efficiency compares the work **done** with the time **taken** to do it:"},{"tex":"\\text{Efficiency ratio} = \\frac{\\text{Standard hours allowed for the work done}}{\\text{Actual hours taken}} \\times 100"},{"stmt":{"t":"Efficiency ratios","rows":[["","Sanity","Moses","@hd"],["Standard hours allowed","45 hrs","40 hrs"],["Actual hours taken","35 hrs","40 hrs"],["**Efficiency ratio**","**128.6%**","**100.0%**","@tt"]]}},{"p":"**Sanity is the efficient employee.** She produced 45 standard hours of work in 35 actual hours, beating the standard by 28.6%, whereas Moses produced exactly the standard output for the time he took and so is merely working to standard, not above it."},{"key":"Note how differently the three systems treat the same two people. Under the **time rate** Moses earns more (N10,000 against N7,000), purely because his hourly rate is higher and he sat at work longer — the system rewards attendance, not performance, and so pays the *less* efficient worker *more*. Under **payment by result** Sanity earns more (N6,003 against N5,440), because she produced more units. Under the **bonus scheme** Sanity gains N2,000 for her 10 saved hours while Moses gains nothing. This contrast is the entire argument for incentive schemes in one table."}]}}]}],"formulas":[{"lb":"Gross wage (time rate)","tex":"W = h \\times r","nt":"h = hours worked, r = rate per hour."},{"lb":"Straight piece rate","tex":"W = u \\times r","nt":"u = units produced, r = rate per unit."},{"lb":"Overtime premium","tex":"\\text{Premium} = \\text{Basic rate} \\times \\text{(overtime fraction)}"},{"lb":"Halsey bonus","tex":"B = \\tfrac12 (T_a - T_t) \\times D","nt":"Ta = time allowed, Tt = time taken, D = day rate."},{"lb":"Halsey-Weir bonus","tex":"B = \\tfrac13 (T_a - T_t) \\times D"},{"lb":"Rowan bonus","tex":"B = \\dfrac{T_a - T_t}{T_a} \\times T_t \\times D"},{"lb":"Labour turnover rate","tex":"\\dfrac{\\text{Leavers}}{\\text{Average employees}} \\times 100"},{"lb":"Flux method","tex":"\\dfrac{\\text{Joiners} + \\text{Leavers}}{\\text{Average employees}}"},{"lb":"Separation method","tex":"\\dfrac{\\text{Workers replaced}}{\\text{Average employees}}"}],"focus":"This chapter is tested almost as heavily as materials, and the same discipline applies: learn the four bonus-scheme formulas (time rate, piece rate, Halsey/Halsey-Weir, Rowan) as a family, and always establish time allowed, time taken and time saved explicitly before plugging into any of them. The direct/indirect split for labour (only *basic* wages of a direct worker are direct cost — every policy add-on is indirect, even on a direct worker) is a frequent trap. Labour turnover's three formulas share one denominator (average employees) but different numerators — know which measures leavers only, which counts joiners too.","errors":["Applying the Rowan formula's time-saved/time-allowed fraction to *time allowed* instead of *time taken* in the multiplication.","Treating overtime premium as always indirect — it is direct when worked specifically at a customer's request.","Classifying a bonus or insurance premium paid to a direct worker as direct labour cost — only the basic wage is direct; everything else is indirect regardless of who earns it.","Confusing job evaluation (ranks the job) with merit rating (ranks the jobholder's performance).","Using the number of leavers alone, without dividing by average employees, when quoting a labour turnover \"rate.\""],"quiz":{"mcq":[{"q":"An employee works 48 hours in a week at a basic rate of ¢1,500 per hour. The gross wage under the time rate method is","o":["¢48,000","¢72,000","¢1,500","¢69,500","¢75,000"],"a":1,"w":"Gross wage = hours worked × rate per hour = 48 × 1,500 = ¢72,000.","calc":"W = 48 \\times 1{,}500 = 72{,}000","src":"Chapter 4.3","sec":"4.3"},{"q":"Under a straight piece rate scheme, an employee's earnings depend on","o":["the number of hours spent at work","the volume of units produced","the employee's seniority","the company's annual profit","the day of the week"],"a":1,"w":"Straight piece rate pays units produced × rate per unit — time taken is irrelevant to the wage itself.","src":"Chapter 4.3","sec":"4.3"},{"q":"Time allowed for a job is 20 hours; time taken is 16 hours; the day rate is ¢5,000/hour. Under the Halsey scheme, the bonus is","o":["¢10,000","¢20,000","¢6,667","¢4,000","¢16,000"],"a":0,"w":"Time saved = 20 − 16 = 4 hours. Halsey bonus = ½ × 4 × 5,000 = ¢10,000.","calc":"B = \\tfrac12 \\times (20-16) \\times 5{,}000 = 10{,}000","src":"Chapter 4.3","sec":"4.3"},{"q":"Using the same data (time allowed 20 hrs, time taken 16 hrs, day rate ¢5,000/hr), the bonus under the Rowan scheme is","o":["¢10,000","¢8,000","¢16,000","¢4,000","¢20,000"],"a":1,"w":"Rowan bonus = (time saved / time allowed) × time taken × day rate = (4/20) × 16 × 5,000 = 0.2 × 16 × 5,000 = ¢16,000.","calc":"B = \\dfrac{4}{20} \\times 16 \\times 5{,}000 = 16{,}000","src":"Chapter 4.3","sec":"4.3"},{"q":"Overtime premium paid to a direct worker is normally treated as","o":["direct labour cost, always","indirect labour cost, unless worked at a customer's specific request","a period cost only","part of prime cost, always","excluded from labour cost entirely"],"a":1,"w":"Overtime premium is indirect wages, except where the overtime is worked specifically at a customer's request to meet their deadline — then it is direct.","src":"Chapter 4.5","sec":"4.5"},{"q":"A workstation available for production but unused because materials have not arrived is an example of","o":["abnormal idle time","normal idle time","overtime","direct labour cost","a stepped cost"],"a":1,"w":"A material shortage is exactly the CIMA definition of idle time; whether it is normal (uncontrollable, expected) depends on context, but a routine material-delivery gap of this kind is the textbook example of normal idle time, charged to WIP.","src":"Chapter 4.4","sec":"4.4"},{"q":"A company had 300 employees at the start of the month and 280 at the end, after 10 joined and 30 left. The labour turnover rate (leavers/average employees × 100) is","o":["10%","9.3%","10.3%","3.3%","30%"],"a":1,"w":"Average employees = ½(300+280) = 290. Turnover rate = 30/290 × 100 ≈ 10.3%.","calc":"\\dfrac{30}{\\tfrac12(300+280)} \\times 100 \\approx 10.3\\%","src":"Chapter 4.6","sec":"4.6"},{"q":"Which of the following is an example of a \"ghost worker\" fraud?","o":["A real employee is overpaid due to a clerical error","Wages are paid to an employee who has genuinely resigned","Wages are paid in the name of a person who does not actually exist","An employee is paid late due to a bank error","An employee's bonus is calculated using the wrong day rate"],"a":2,"w":"A ghost worker is a fictitious employee whose \"wages\" are actually being misappropriated by whoever set up the fraud.","src":"Chapter 4.6","sec":"4.6"},{"q":"In preparing a payroll, National Health Insurance and Social Security contributions are examples of","o":["voluntary deductions","statutory deductions","gross wages","overtime premiums","group incentive bonuses"],"a":1,"w":"Statutory deductions are required by law (Social Security, PAYE, National Health Insurance); trade-union and provident-fund contributions are voluntary deductions.","src":"Chapter 4.7","sec":"4.7"},{"q":"Which technique measures the *jobholder's* performance, to decide on promotion, demotion or reward, rather than ranking the job itself?","o":["Job evaluation","Merit rating","Work measurement","Method study","Labour turnover analysis"],"a":1,"w":"Merit rating assesses the person doing the job (accuracy, initiative, responsiveness); job evaluation ranks the job itself against other jobs.","src":"Chapter 4.2","sec":"4.2"}],"theory":[{"q":"Distinguish between labour cost accounting and payroll accounting.","marks":6,"a":[{"p":"**Labour cost accounting** relates to determining the cost of labour chargeable to jobs, products, customers and overhead accounts — its scope includes wages/salaries, workmen's compensation insurance, employee bonuses and employee share schemes."},{"p":"**Payroll accounting** relates to computing what is owed to, or paid on behalf of, employees — the employer's and employee's social security contributions, personal income tax and welfare deductions. It does not consider costs like workmen's compensation insurance, which labour cost accounting does — the two have genuinely different scopes, not just different names for the same thing."}],"src":"Chapter 4.7","sec":"4.7"},{"q":"Outline FIVE causes of labour turnover.","marks":5,"a":[{"ol":["dissatisfaction with the job or wages;","poor hours of work or working conditions;","discontent with supervisors or colleagues;","lack of promotion opportunities;","personal matters such as ill health, marriage, pregnancy or relocation."]}],"src":"Chapter 4.6","sec":"4.6"},{"q":"Explain SEVEN ways of avoiding or reducing labour turnover.","marks":7,"a":[{"ol":["analyse turnover regularly by cause, distinguishing avoidable from unavoidable and voluntary from dismissed;","improve applicant selection, to avoid hiring discontented or unsuitable staff;","plan labour requirements properly, to avoid redundancy;","introduce a high-wage plan or other incentives;","keep the working environment congenial;","offer competitive fringe benefits — pension scheme, subsidised meals, sports/social facilities;","develop better relationships between management and workers."]}],"src":"Chapter 4.6","sec":"4.6"}]}},{"n":5,"t":"Elements of Cost: Overheads","brief":"The third and most complex element of cost: what overheads are, how they are classified, the allocation/apportionment/absorption sequence that gets an overhead from the general ledger onto a cost unit, the three methods for re-apportioning reciprocal service-centre costs, the six bases for calculating an overhead absorption rate, why predetermined rates are used, the treatment of over- and under-absorbed overhead, and Activity-Based Costing as the modern alternative to a single blanket rate.","outcomes":["Define overheads and identify the various ways of classifying overheads","Distinguish between overheads allocation, overheads apportionment and overheads absorption","Use the overheads analysis sheet to allocate and apportion overheads to cost centres","Calculate overheads absorption rates using six methods","Explain why predetermined overheads absorption rates are often used","Explain the reasons for preferring departmental to blanket overhead absorption rates","Calculate and explain the alternative treatments of overhead under- and over-absorption","Make accounting entries with respect to overheads"],"secs":[{"n":"5.1","t":"Introduction and definition","b":[{"def":{"t":"Overheads","d":"the aggregate of indirect materials, indirect labour and indirect expenses."}},{"p":"Overheads matter a great deal in cost accounting: they are made up of three separate cost elements (materials, labour, expenses), making their determination and control complex and challenging, and in many organisations the overhead component of total cost is significant — demanding careful analysis, monitoring and control to minimise total cost and maximise profitability."},{"p":"Typical items making up overhead cost: cleaning materials, stationery, consumable materials, supervision cost, employee bonuses, indirect workers' salaries, lighting and heating, rent and rates, insurance premiums, and depreciation of fixed assets. The objective of determining overhead cost is (a) to enable it to be absorbed by products, and (b) to provide useful information for management decision making and cost control."}]},{"n":"5.2","t":"Types and classification of overheads","b":[{"p":"Overheads classify by function into four groups (already introduced in Chapter 2 §2.1(c), repeated here in overheads' own terms):"},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Type","Meaning"],"rows":[["**Production overheads**","The indirect costs of manufacturing a cost unit — indirect materials consumed in the factory, indirect factory wages, and other indirect expenses connected with production."],["**Administrative overheads**","Costs of formulating policy and directing/controlling operations, not directly related to production, selling, distribution or R&D."],["**Marketing, selling and distribution overheads**","Costs of securing orders, publicising and presenting products attractively at acceptable prices, delivering goods to customers, and after-sales service."],["**Research and development overheads**","Costs of seeking new ideas, materials, production methods and improved products, and designing them for formal production."]]}},{"p":"Overheads can equally be analysed by **element** (indirect materials, indirect labour, indirect expenses — Chapter 1 §1.3) or by **behaviour** (fixed or variable — Chapter 2 §2.1(d))."},{"key":"Because overheads by their nature cannot be assigned to any one specific cost unit, charging them to products requires a three-stage sequence: **allocation** → **apportionment** → **absorption**. Every part of this chapter builds on getting that sequence exactly right."}]},{"n":"5.3","t":"Overhead allocation and apportionment","b":[{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Term","Meaning"],"rows":[["**Overhead allocation**","Assigning overhead directly to a cost centre, with **no sharing** — used where a cost is specifically incurred for one particular cost centre and can be wholly charged to it."],["**Overhead apportionment**","Sharing overhead incurred for a **number of** cost centres (sometimes the whole organisation) among them, on a fair and equitable basis, because such costs cannot be allocated to just one."]]}},{"p":"Common bases of apportionment, matched to the type of overhead they best fit:"},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Basis","Typical overhead it fits"],"rows":[["**Floor space/area occupied**","Rent; rates; heating and lighting; insurance of buildings."],["**Kilowatt hours**","Electricity; power."],["**Value of plant and machinery**","Depreciation of plant/machinery; insurance of plant; plant maintenance."],["**Number of employees**","Canteen expenses; supervisors' wages; personnel department expenses."],["**Number of materials requisitions**","Stores expenses; indirect material expenses."]]}},{"eg":{"tag":"Study text","t":"Illustration 5.1 — a complete overhead analysis sheet","open":true,"q":[{"p":"Apple Ltd makes wooden crates (40,000 made in the year), through three production departments (Sawing, Assembly, Finishing) and two service departments (Maintenance, Materials Handling)."},{"table":{"align":"lrrr","head":["","Sawing (₵)","Assembly (₵)","Finishing (₵)"],"rows":[["Materials issued","800,000","600,000","100,000"],["Direct wages","300,000","150,000","250,000"],["Overheads","120,000","80,000","30,000"]]}},{"p":"Materials handling wages ₵21,000; maintenance wages ₵45,000; consumable stores ₵15,000 (all maintenance). Service-department benefit percentages:"},{"table":{"align":"lrrrr","head":["","Sawing","Assembly","Finishing","Mat. Handling"],"rows":[["Maintenance","30%","40%","20%","10%"],["Materials Handling","50%","20%","30%","—"]]}},{"p":"Required: (a) the overheads allotted to each production department; (b) the unit cost of a wooden crate."}],"a":[{"table":{"align":"lrrrrr","head":["","Sawing","Assembly","Finishing","Maintenance","Mat. Handling"],"rows":[["Overheads","120,000","80,000","30,000","45,000","21,000"],["Consumables","—","—","—","15,000","—"],["Subtotal","120,000","80,000","30,000","60,000","21,000"],["Apportion Maintenance (30:40:20:10)","18,000","24,000","12,000","(60,000)","6,000"],["Apportion Mat. Handling (50:20:30)","13,500","5,400","8,100","—","(27,000)"],["**Overheads to production depts.**","**151,500**","**109,400**","**50,100**","Nil","Nil"]]}},{"p":"**(b) Unit cost**:"},{"table":{"align":"lrrrr","head":["","Sawing","Assembly","Finishing","Total"],"rows":[["Material cost","800,000","600,000","100,000","1,500,000"],["Direct wages","300,000","150,000","250,000","700,000"],["Overheads","151,500","109,400","50,100","311,000"],["**Total cost**","","","","**2,511,000**"]]}},{"tex":"\\text{Unit cost per crate} = \\dfrac{2{,}511{,}000}{40{,}000} = ₵62.78"}]}}]},{"n":"5.4","t":"Re-apportioning reciprocal service-centre overheads","b":[{"p":"Cost units are only produced in **production** cost centres, so a service centre's overhead must be re-apportioned into production cost centres before it can be absorbed. Where service centres do **not** serve each other, this is simple: re-apportion the service centre that serves the *most* other centres first, then repeat — once a centre's overhead is re-apportioned, it never receives any more."},{"warn":"Where service centres **do** serve each other (**reciprocal service** — e.g. Maintenance services Administration and Administration services Maintenance back), that simple one-pass approach breaks down, and one of three dedicated methods is needed: **elimination**, **continuous allotment**, or **simultaneous equations**."},{"eg":{"tag":"Study text","t":"Illustration 5.2 — the shared data for all three reciprocal-service methods","open":true,"q":[{"p":"Lamina Manufacturing Company: four production departments (Grinding, Blending, Firing, Polishing) and three service departments (Personnel, Administration, Maintenance)."},{"table":{"align":"lrrrrrrr","head":["₵'000","Grinding","Blending","Firing","Polishing","Personnel","Admin.","Maint."],"rows":[["Indirect labour","4,600","3,300","5,400","2,900","700","1,800","800"],["Other indirect costs","1,400","1,200","2,800","1,600","500","300","1,200"]]}},{"p":"Service-department costs are re-apportioned by these percentages:"},{"table":{"align":"lrrrrrr","head":["","Grinding","Blending","Firing","Polishing","Admin.","Maint."],"rows":[["Personnel","15%","25%","30%","20%","5%","5%"],["Administration","10%","30%","40%","15%","—","5%"],["Maintenance","15%","30%","40%","5%","10%","—"]]}},{"p":"Grinding and Firing absorb overhead per machine hour (budgeted 620 and 520 hours respectively); Blending and Polishing absorb per direct labour hour (budgeted 1,050 and 450 hours respectively). Required: total overheads for each production cost centre, and the overhead absorption rate for each."}],"a":[{"p":"Worked in full below, by all three methods — the study text uses this one dataset throughout to show that they converge on (almost) the same answer."}]}},{"h4":"Method 1 — Elimination"},{"p":"Once a service centre's overhead has been re-apportioned, that centre is **eliminated** from every later re-apportionment. Order of elimination: the service centre receiving service from **no other** service centre goes first (here, Personnel — nothing flows *into* Personnel from Administration or Maintenance); then the centre receiving the **least** from the remaining service centres."},{"table":{"align":"lrrrrrrr","head":["₵'000","Grinding","Blending","Firing","Polishing","Personnel","Admin.","Maint."],"rows":[["Subtotal (labour + other)","6,000","4,500","8,200","4,500","1,200","2,100","2,000"],["Personnel (15:25:30:20:—:5:5)","180","300","360","240","(1,200)","60","60"],["Maintenance, now 2,060 (15:30:40:5:—:10:—)","309","618","824","103","—","206","(2,060)"],["Admin., now 2,366 — re-normalised over the 4 open production centres only (10:30:40:15, scaled to sum to 100)","249","747","996","374","—","(2,366)","—"],["**Overheads to production centres**","**6,738**","**6,165**","**10,380**","**5,217**","Nil","Nil","Nil"]]}},{"key":"The subtlety that trips people up: by the time **Administration**'s turn comes, **Maintenance** has already been closed — but Administration's own percentages (10:30:40:15:—:5, with that last 5% meant for Maintenance) still add up to 100% only including Maintenance. Since Maintenance can no longer receive anything, Administration's remaining ₵2,366 is re-apportioned **only** across the four still-open production centres, using the *same relative weights* (10:30:40:15) scaled up to sum to 100% instead of 95% — e.g. Grinding's true share becomes $10/95 = 10.53\\%$ of ₵2,366, not $10\\%$. Always check whether a closed-out centre's share needs re-normalising this way once a reciprocal partner is already gone."},{"h4":"Method 2 — Continuous allotment (repeated distribution)"},{"p":"Every service centre keeps re-apportioning its overhead to the *other* service centres as well as the production centres, in repeated passes, until the residual amount left to redistribute becomes **insignificant** — at which point it is simply ignored. Reciprocal centres therefore feed each other small, shrinking amounts over several rounds rather than being closed off after one pass. Every row below uses the same percentages given in the base data (Personnel 15:25:30:20:—:5:5; Maintenance 15:30:40:5:—:10 [to Admin]; Administration 10:30:40:15:—:5 [to Maint]) applied each time to whatever balance is currently sitting in that service centre:"},{"table":{"align":"lrrrrrrr","head":["₵'000","Grinding","Blending","Firing","Polishing","Personnel","Admin.","Maint."],"rows":[["Indirect labour (allocated)","4,600","3,300","5,400","2,900","700","1,800","800"],["Other indirect costs (allocated)","1,400","1,200","2,800","1,600","500","300","1,200"],["Subtotal","6,000","4,500","8,200","4,500","1,200","2,100","2,000"],["Round 1 — Personnel's 1,200 (15:25:30:20:—:5:5)","180","300","360","240","(1,200)","60","60"],["Round 2 — Maintenance's 2,060 [2,000+60] (15:30:40:5:—:10)","309","618","824","103","—","206","(2,060)"],["Round 3 — Admin's 2,366 [2,100+60+206] (10:30:40:15:—:5)","237","710","947","354","—","(2,366)","118"],["Round 4 — Maintenance's 118 (15:30:40:5:—:10)","18","35","47","6","—","12","(118)"],["Round 5 — Admin's 12 (10:30:40:15:—:5)","1","4","5","2","—","(12)","1"],["Residual left in Maintenance (≈1) — small enough to ignore","—","—","—","—","—","—","Nil"],["**Overheads to production centres**","**6,745**","**6,167**","**10,383**","**5,205**","Nil","Nil","Nil"]]}},{"key":"Personnel is distributed first and never receives anything back, because nothing in the data flows *into* Personnel — only Maintenance and Administration feed each other back and forth, in shrinking amounts (2,060 → 118 → 12 → ≈1), which is exactly the \"continuous\" part of this method's name. Each production centre's final total is simply its subtotal plus every round's contribution to it, e.g. Grinding $= 6{,}000+180+309+237+18+1 = 6{,}745$."},{"h4":"Method 3 — Simultaneous equations"},{"p":"The total overhead ultimately carried by each service centre (including what it receives back from the others) is treated as an unknown, and the reciprocal relationships are solved as a system of equations. Letting $P$, $A$, $M$ be the total (self-incurred plus received) overhead of Personnel, Administration and Maintenance — each built from that centre's own subtotal, plus its *share* of whatever the other two service centres pass to it, using the same base-data percentages as before (Personnel passes 5% of $P$ to Admin and 5% to Maintenance; Maintenance passes 10% of $M$ to Admin; Administration passes 5% of $A$ to Maintenance):"},{"tex":"P = 1{,}200 \\qquad A = 2{,}100 + 0.05P + 0.10M \\qquad M = 2{,}000 + 0.05P + 0.05A"},{"p":"**Step 1 — substitute $P=1{,}200$ into both remaining equations:**"},{"tex":"A = 2{,}100 + 0.05(1{,}200) + 0.10M = 2{,}160 + 0.10M \\quad\\text{...(1)}"},{"tex":"M = 2{,}000 + 0.05(1{,}200) + 0.05A = 2{,}060 + 0.05A \\quad\\text{...(2)}"},{"p":"**Step 2 — substitute (2) into (1)**, so everything is in terms of $A$ alone:"},{"tex":"A = 2{,}160 + 0.10(2{,}060 + 0.05A) = 2{,}160 + 206 + 0.005A = 2{,}366 + 0.005A"},{"p":"**Step 3 — collect the $A$ terms and solve:**"},{"tex":"A - 0.005A = 2{,}366 \\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad 0.995A = 2{,}366 \\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad A = \\dfrac{2{,}366}{0.995} = ₵2{,}377.89\\text{ thousand}"},{"p":"**Step 4 — substitute $A$ back into (2)** to find $M$:"},{"tex":"M = 2{,}060 + 0.05(2{,}377.89) = 2{,}060 + 118.89 = ₵2{,}178.89\\text{ thousand}"},{"p":"So $P=1{,}200$, $A\\approx 2{,}377.89$, $M\\approx 2{,}178.89$ (all in ₵'000). **Step 5 — redistribute each of these three totals out to the four production centres**, one more time, using each service centre's own fixed percentage split (Personnel 15:25:30:20:—:5:5; Administration 10:30:40:15:—:—:5; Maintenance 15:30:40:5:—:10:—, for Grinding:Blending:Firing:Polishing:self:Admin:Maintenance in that order):"},{"table":{"align":"lrrrr","head":["₵'000","Grinding","Blending","Firing","Polishing"],"rows":[["Subtotal (from Illustration 5.2's own table)","6,000","4,500","8,200","4,500"],["From Personnel: 15:25:30:20% of $P=1{,}200$","180.00","300.00","360.00","240.00"],["From Administration: 10:30:40:15% of $A=2{,}377.89$","237.79","713.37","951.16","356.68"],["From Maintenance: 15:30:40:5% of $M=2{,}178.89$","326.83","653.67","871.56","108.94"],["**Overheads to production centres**","**6,744.62**","**6,167.04**","**10,382.72**","**5,205.62**"]]}},{"note":"Rounded to the nearest ₵'000, this gives 6,745 / 6,167 / 10,383 / 5,206 — matching continuous allotment's 6,745/6,167/10,383 exactly, with Polishing landing one unit away (5,206 here against 5,205 there). This is pure rounding noise from carrying $A$ and $M$ to only 2 decimal places rather than their exact repeating-decimal values ($A=473{,}200/199$, $M=433{,}600/199$ precisely) — not a sign either method was done incorrectly, exactly the same kind of small reconciling gap already flagged for Illustration 8.5's rounded unit costs."},{"note":"Continuous allotment and simultaneous equations reach, to within this rounding noise, the **same** final answer (6,745/6,167/10,383/≈5,205-6) — a useful cross-check, since they arrive at it by completely different routes. Elimination gives a slightly different answer (6,738/6,165/10,380/5,217) because it forces every service centre fully closed after one pass, an approximation the other two methods do not make. In an exam, use whichever method the question asks for — do not mix figures from different methods in one answer."},{"p":"The rest of this chapter (overhead absorption rates, over/under absorption) uses the **elimination method**'s production-centre totals — ₵6,738,000 (Grinding), ₵6,165,000 (Blending), ₵10,380,000 (Firing), ₵5,217,000 (Polishing)."}]},{"n":"5.5","t":"Overhead absorption — the six bases","b":[{"def":{"t":"Overhead absorption","d":"the process of assigning overhead costs to the products or services produced — by first calculating an overhead absorption rate (OAR), then applying that rate to determine the overhead absorbed by each cost unit."}},{"fbox":{"h":"Overhead absorption rate (general form)","rows":[{"lb":"OAR","tex":"OAR = \\dfrac{\\text{Budgeted overhead}}{\\text{Budgeted activity level}}"}]}},{"p":"Six factors are commonly used as \"activity level\" — each suited to a different kind of production process:"},{"table":{"align":"lll","head":["Basis","Formula","Best suited where…"],"rows":[["**Direct labour hours**","$OAR = \\dfrac{\\text{Budgeted overhead}}{\\text{Budgeted DLH}}$","production is labour-intensive and wage rates are stable."],["**Machine hours**","$OAR = \\dfrac{\\text{Budgeted overhead}}{\\text{Budgeted machine hours}}$","production is highly mechanised, so labour hours would be inappropriate."],["**Units of production**","$OAR = \\dfrac{\\text{Budgeted overhead}}{\\text{Budgeted production units}}$","output is uniform in mass quantities — inappropriate for non-uniform units."],["**Direct material cost %**","$OAR = \\dfrac{\\text{Budgeted overhead}}{\\text{Budgeted DM cost}} \\times 100$","material prices are stable and uniform."],["**Direct labour cost %**","$OAR = \\dfrac{\\text{Budgeted overhead}}{\\text{Budgeted DL cost}} \\times 100$","labour is intensive and wage rates are stable and uniform."],["**Prime cost %**","$OAR = \\dfrac{\\text{Budgeted overhead}}{\\text{Budgeted prime cost}} \\times 100$","rates and prices are relatively stable overall."]]}},{"eg":{"tag":"Study text","t":"Illustration 5.2 continued — OAR by direct labour hours and by machine hours","open":true,"q":[{"p":"Using the elimination-method totals: Blending ₵6,165,000 (1,050 budgeted DLH), Polishing ₵5,217,000 (450 budgeted DLH); Grinding ₵6,738,000 (620 budgeted machine hours), Firing ₵10,380,000 (520 budgeted machine hours). Find each OAR."}],"a":[{"table":{"align":"lrr","head":["","Blending","Polishing"],"rows":[["Budgeted overhead","₵6,165,000","₵5,217,000"],["Budgeted DLH","1,050","450"],["**OAR**","**₵5,871.43 per DLH**","**₵11,593.33 per DLH**"]]}},{"table":{"align":"lrr","head":["","Grinding","Firing"],"rows":[["Budgeted overhead","₵6,738,000","₵10,380,000"],["Budgeted machine hours","620","520"],["**OAR**","**₵10,867.74 per MH**","**₵19,961.54 per MH**"]]}}]}},{"eg":{"tag":"Study text","t":"Illustration 5.3 — direct labour cost % vs total variable cost %","open":true,"q":[{"p":"Enyo Manufacturing produces Frytol, Brilliant and Pepsodent:"},{"table":{"align":"lrrr","head":["","Frytol","Brilliant","Pepsodent"],"rows":[["Direct materials","₵720","₵1,280","₵1,800"],["Direct labour, Production dept. (₵60/hr)","2 hrs","2.5 hrs","2 hrs"],["Direct labour, Finishing dept. (₵40/hr)","2 hrs","2.5 hrs","1 hr"],["Variable overhead","₵80","₵30","₵40"],["Planned production","10,000 units","20,000 units","40,000 units"]]}},{"p":"Fixed overhead for the year is budgeted at ₵6,000,000. Find the OAR under (i) direct labour cost percentage and (ii) percentage of total variable cost."}],"a":[{"p":"Budgeted DLH — Production dept.: $2(10{,}000)+2.5(20{,}000)+2(40{,}000) = 150{,}000$; Finishing dept.: $2(10{,}000)+2.5(20{,}000)+1(40{,}000) = 110{,}000$."},{"p":"Budgeted direct labour cost $= 150{,}000(60) + 110{,}000(40) = 9{,}000{,}000 + 4{,}400{,}000 = ₵13{,}400{,}000$."},{"p":"Budgeted material cost $= 720(10{,}000)+1{,}280(20{,}000)+1{,}800(40{,}000) = ₵104{,}800{,}000$. Budgeted variable overhead $= 80(10{,}000)+30(20{,}000)+40(40{,}000) = ₵3{,}000{,}000$. Budgeted total variable cost $= 104{,}800{,}000 + 13{,}400{,}000 + 3{,}000{,}000 = ₵121{,}200{,}000$."},{"tex":"\\text{(i) OAR} = \\dfrac{6{,}000{,}000}{13{,}400{,}000} \\times 100 \\approx 44.78\\% \\text{ of direct labour cost}"},{"tex":"\\text{(ii) OAR} = \\dfrac{6{,}000{,}000}{121{,}200{,}000} \\times 100 \\approx 4.95\\% \\text{ of total variable cost}"}]}}]},{"n":"5.6","t":"Predetermined rates, and blanket versus departmental rates","b":[{"h4":"Why predetermined (budgeted) rates are used"},{"ol":["actual overheads are not known until **after** a period ends — without budgeted figures, product cost cannot be determined promptly; recalculating actual rates as often as possible would be cumbersome and inconvenient; and","actual overheads are heavily influenced by general price levels, and actual activity levels fluctuate widely — combined, these would cause large swings in the absorption rate if it were based on actual figures. Using budgeted overhead and budgeted activity (which reflect *standards*) reduces the effect of such fluctuations."]},{"h4":"Blanket versus departmental (cost-centre) rates"},{"def":{"t":"Blanket (factory-wide) overhead recovery rate","d":"a single overhead recovery rate applied across the **whole factory**, rather than a separate rate per production cost centre."}},{"warn":"Blanket rates are usually inappropriate: (i) a factory consists of different production cost centres, and products may consume each centre's overheads in different proportions; (ii) different activity bases drive cost for different products in different proportions. A single, factory-wide rate hides these differences and distorts individual product costs."}]},{"n":"5.7","t":"Over-absorbed and under-absorbed overhead","b":[{"tex":"\\text{Overhead absorbed} = \\text{OAR} \\times \\text{Actual quantity of activity base}"},{"p":"Because the OAR is based on *budgeted* figures while absorption is applied to *actual* activity, the overhead absorbed rarely equals the overhead actually incurred — creating **over-absorption** (absorbed exceeds incurred) or **under-absorption** (incurred exceeds absorbed)."},{"h4":"Treatment of over/under absorption"},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Treatment","Drawback"],"rows":[["Adjust the under/over recovery into the cost of units produced during the period.","Not usually worthwhile — this historical information is of little use to management."],["Carry the under/over recovery forward to future accounting periods.","Distorts future performance figures."],["**Write it off to the Profit and Loss account as a period cost.**","**This is the most preferred treatment.**"]]}},{"note":"The treatment of overheads generally can follow either **absorption costing** (both fixed and variable overhead charged to products) or **marginal costing** (only variable overhead absorbed into products; fixed overhead treated as a period cost, charged straight to the P&L account)."},{"eg":{"tag":"Study text","t":"Illustration 5.4 — calculating over/under absorption","open":true,"q":[{"p":"Agape Co. Ltd has two production departments (Machining, Assembling) and one service department (Maintenance):"},{"table":{"align":"lrrr","head":["₵'000","Machining","Assembling","Maintenance"],"rows":[["Indirect labour","200","250","120"],["Indirect materials","420","450","500"],["Materials handling","—","—","80"],["Housekeeping","50","70","100"],["Air conditioning","30","30","50"],["Rent & rates","160","120","150"],["Supervision","240","180","200"]]}},{"p":"Maintenance is re-apportioned to the two production centres in proportion to their total expenses. Budgeted overhead: Machining ₵2,000,000 (500,000 planned machine hours); Assembling ₵1,200,000 (400,000 planned direct labour hours). Actual: 300,000 machine hours worked in Machining, 600,000 direct labour hours worked in Assembling. Find the OAR for each department and the over/under absorption for the period."}],"a":[{"table":{"align":"lrrr","head":["₵'000","Machining","Assembling","Maintenance"],"rows":[["Subtotal (allocated)","1,100","1,100","1,200"],["Re-apportion Maintenance (50:50, by relative expense)","600","600","(1,200)"],["**Overhead incurred**","**1,700**","**1,700**","Nil"]]}},{"table":{"align":"lrr","head":["","Machining","Assembling"],"rows":[["OAR","₵4/machine hour","₵3/DLH"],["Actual activity","300,000 machine hrs","600,000 DLH"],["Overhead absorbed ($OAR \\times$ actual)","₵1,200,000","₵1,800,000"],["Overhead incurred","₵1,700,000","₵1,700,000"],["**Result**","**₵500,000 under-absorbed**","**₵100,000 over-absorbed**"]]}}]}}]},{"n":"5.8","t":"Activity-Based Costing (ABC)","b":[{"p":"Conventional overhead absorption uses a single base (units of production, labour hours, etc.) for a whole cost centre — adequate for a small/medium firm with simple operations. In a large, highly automated firm, a single base is unsuitable, because different *activities* drive indirect cost in different ways. **Activity-Based Costing** exists for exactly this situation."},{"def":{"t":"Activity-Based Costing (ABC)","d":"a costing approach that traces the cost of resources used to the *activities* that cause them to be incurred, and then absorbs overhead into products or services based on each product's use of those activities."}},{"def":{"t":"Cost driver (CIMA)","d":"any factor that causes a change in the cost of an activity."}},{"p":"Examples of cost drivers: the number of materials handling operations or issues; the number of machine set-ups; the number of machine hours; the number of inspection orders."},{"h4":"Stages in ABC"},{"steps":["Identify and classify activities.","Select a cost-driver base for each activity (e.g. number of machine set-ups).","Assign cost to each activity/cost pool.","Ascertain the activity cost per unit of cost driver: $\\text{Cost driver rate} = \\dfrac{\\text{Activity cost for the cost pool}}{\\text{Quantity of cost driver available}}$.","Charge overhead to each product by multiplying its own consumption of each cost driver by that driver's rate."]},{"eg":{"tag":"Study text","t":"Quick illustration — overhead rate per unit via two cost drivers","open":true,"q":[{"p":"100 units of Product L are to be manufactured. 20 machine set-ups are needed at ₦40 each, and 10 materials issues at ₦20 each. Find the overhead rate per unit of Product L."}],"a":[{"table":{"align":"lr","head":["","Cost (₦)"],"rows":[["Machine set-up (20 × ₦40)","800"],["Materials handling (10 × ₦20)","200"],["**Total overhead**","**1,000**"]]}},{"tex":"\\text{Overhead rate per unit} = \\dfrac{₦1{,}000}{100\\text{ units}} = ₦10"}]}},{"eg":{"tag":"Study text — Illustrations 5.5 and 5.6","t":"MMT Ltd — determining overhead absorption through activity-based costing","open":true,"q":[{"note":"The study text prints this illustration **twice**, as Illustration 5.5 and again, word for word, as Illustration 5.6. Only the second carries a solution. It is shown once here."},{"p":"MMT manufactures two products. The company provides the following details relating to the 2015 financial year."},{"p":"**1.  Units produced:** Product AB — 150,000 units; Product CD — 200,000 units."},{"p":"**2.  Activity cost pools and total cost drivers:**"},{"stmt":{"t":"Activity cost pools","rows":[["Activity","Activity cost pool (₦)","Total cost driver quantity","@hd"],["Machine set-up",300000,"60 set-ups"],["Materials issued",200000,"90 requisitions"],["Quality control",100000,"40 inspections"]]}},{"p":"**3.  Cost drivers consumed by each product:**"},{"stmt":{"t":"Cost driver usage by product","rows":[["Activity","Product AB","Product CD","@hd"],["Machine set-up",10,10],["Materials issued",20,10],["Quality control",10,5]]}},{"p":"**Required:** determine the overhead absorbed by each product through activity-based costing."}],"a":[{"h4":"Step 1 — the cost driver rate for each activity"},{"tex":"\\text{Cost driver rate} = \\frac{\\text{Activity cost for each cost pool}}{\\text{Quantity of cost driver available}}"},{"stmt":{"t":"Cost driver rates","rows":[["Activity","Cost pool (₦)","÷ drivers","Rate per driver (₦)","@hd"],["Machine set-up",300000,"60 set-ups","5,000 per set-up"],["Materials issued",200000,"90 requisitions","2,222.22 per requisition"],["Quality control",100000,"40 inspections","2,500 per inspection"]]}},{"warn":"The study text's own solution divides the ₦200,000 materials-issued pool by **80** requisitions, not the **90** its question states, giving ₦2,500 per requisition instead of ₦2,222.22. The rates above use the figure the question actually gives (90). The table below shows both, because the book's printed answers (₦125,000 and ₦87,500) follow from its 80.","lbl":"An inconsistency in the study text"},{"h4":"Step 2 — charge each product with the drivers it consumed"},{"stmt":{"t":"Overhead charged, on the question's stated 90 requisitions","rows":[["","Product AB (₦)","Product CD (₦)","@hd"],["Machine set-up:  ₦5,000 × 10;  ₦5,000 × 10",50000,50000],["Materials issued:  ₦2,222.22 × 20;  ₦2,222.22 × 10",44444,22222],["Quality control:  ₦2,500 × 10;  ₦2,500 × 5",25000,12500],["**Total overhead charged**",119444,84722,"@tt"]]}},{"p":"For comparison, the study text's own printed answer, which uses ₦2,500 per requisition:"},{"stmt":{"t":"The study text's answer (80 requisitions assumed)","rows":[["","Product AB (₦)","Product CD (₦)","@hd"],["Machine set-up:  ₦5,000 × 10",50000,50000],["Materials issued:  ₦2,500 × 20;  ₦2,500 × 10",50000,25000],["Quality control:  ₦2,500 × 10;  ₦2,500 × 5",25000,12500],["**Total overhead absorbed**",125000,87500,"@tt"]]}},{"key":"Notice what ABC has and has not done here. The two products between them consume only 20 of the 60 set-ups, 30 of the 90 requisitions and 15 of the 40 inspections, so **most of the ₦600,000 overhead pool is not charged to either product** — it belongs to other products the question does not describe. That is ABC working correctly: a product is charged for the activities it actually causes, and nothing more. Under a traditional blanket rate, AB and CD would each have absorbed a share of the whole ₦600,000 in proportion to their volume, which is precisely the distortion ABC exists to remove."},{"note":"Per unit, product AB absorbs $₦119{,}444 \\div 150{,}000 = ₦0.80$ and product CD $₦84{,}722 \\div 200{,}000 = ₦0.42$ — AB costs nearly twice as much per unit to support, even though it is the smaller-volume product, because it consumes twice as many requisitions and twice as many inspections. A volume-based blanket rate would have reported the opposite."}]}}]},{"n":"5.9","t":"Worksheet summary — definitions, formulae and structure","b":[{"p":"Everything in this chapter, compressed for a last read-through."},{"h3":"Section-by-section checklist"},{"ol":["**§5.1–5.2 Overheads** — indirect materials + indirect labour + indirect expenses; classified by function (production/admin/marketing-selling-distribution/R&D), element, or behaviour.","**§5.3 Allocation vs apportionment** — allocation is direct, no sharing; apportionment shares a cost across several centres on a fair basis (5 named bases matched to overhead type).","**§5.4 Reciprocal service re-apportionment** — 3 methods (elimination, continuous allotment, simultaneous equations); continuous allotment and simultaneous equations converge exactly; elimination is a close approximation that needs ratio re-normalisation once a reciprocal partner is already closed.","**§5.5 Absorption, 6 bases** — DLH and machine-hour bases give a rate *per hour*; material-cost%, labour-cost% and prime-cost% bases give a rate as a *percentage*; each suited to a different production style (labour-intensive, mechanised, uniform-unit, stable-price).","**§5.6 Predetermined & blanket vs departmental** — budgeted rates avoid both the delay of waiting for actual figures and the distortion of actual-cost/actual-activity swings; blanket (factory-wide) rates hide different centres' different overhead intensity and different activity-base mixes.","**§5.7 Over/under absorption** — absorbed = OAR × actual activity; the preferred treatment of any gap is writing it off to the P&L as a period cost.","**§5.8 ABC** — traces cost to activities via cost drivers, not a single blanket base; 5-step process; cost driver rate = cost pool ÷ total driver quantity."]},{"h3":"B. Formula box"},{"fbox":{"h":"Overhead formulas (recap)","rows":[{"lb":"OAR (general)","tex":"OAR = \\dfrac{\\text{Budgeted overhead}}{\\text{Budgeted activity level}}"},{"lb":"OAR, cost-percentage bases","tex":"OAR = \\dfrac{\\text{Budgeted overhead}}{\\text{Budgeted DM/DL/prime cost}} \\times 100"},{"lb":"Overhead absorbed","tex":"\\text{OAR} \\times \\text{Actual activity}"},{"lb":"Over/under absorption","tex":"\\text{Overhead absorbed} - \\text{Overhead incurred}","nt":"Positive = over-absorbed; negative = under-absorbed."},{"lb":"Cost driver rate (ABC)","tex":"\\dfrac{\\text{Activity cost for the pool}}{\\text{Quantity of cost driver available}}"}]}}]},{"n":"5.10","t":"End-of-chapter questions (study text)","b":[{"eg":{"tag":"Study text","t":"Multiple-choice and short-answer questions, with answers","open":true,"q":[{"ol":["Overheads are the aggregate of (A) Indirect materials, indirect labour and indirect expenses  (B) Expenses incurred over the normal expense heads  (C) Expenses incurred by the owners of the business  (D) All uncontrollable expenses  (E) All unauthorised expenses","Distinguish between overhead allocation and overhead apportionment. (A) Allocation is used in government whilst apportionment is used in the private sector  (B) Allocation is assigned direct to cost centres whilst apportionment is shared to a number of cost centres  (C) Allocation is made scientifically whilst apportionment is done arbitrarily (D) Allocation benefits only managers whilst apportionment benefits junior staff  (E) Both terms mean the same thing","What is the usual basis of apportioning factory rent? (A) Number of employees  (B) Number of machines  (C) Floor space occupied  (D) Kilowatt of energy used  (E) Number of pillars","What distinguishes a profit centre from a cost centre? (A) Direct Cost  (B) Indirect Cost  (C) Profit  (D) Loss  (E) Revenue","What does OAR stand for in overheads accounting? (A) Organisational Accounting Returns  (B) Overheads Accounting Rate  (C) Overheads Accounting Returns  (D) Overheads Absorption Rate  (E) Organisational Absorption Rate","Where the actual overheads for a period exceed the predetermined overheads, this is a case of …………………………..","A system of continuously reallocating each service cost centre overhead until the amounts become insignificant is known as ……………….","Overheads covering the cost of securing orders, publicising and presenting a company's products in proper forms at acceptable prices and delivering the goods to customers are generally known as …………………","Where the production process is highly mechanised, making the use of labour hours inappropriate for overhead absorption, what alternative basis may be used?","Which is the best way to treat over- and under-absorbed overheads for a period?"]}],"a":[{"ol":["**A — indirect materials, indirect labour and indirect expenses.**","**B** — allocation goes direct to a cost centre; apportionment is shared across several.","**C — floor space occupied.**","**E — revenue** (a profit centre both incurs cost and generates revenue).","**D — Overheads Absorption Rate.**","**Under-absorption** (actual overhead exceeds what was absorbed).","**Continuous allotment.**","**Marketing, selling and distribution overheads.**","**Machine hours.**","**Write it off to the Profit and Loss Account.**"]}]}}]},{"n":"5.11","t":"Chapter summary","b":[{"p":"This chapter has discussed accounting for overheads in three sections."},{"ol":["The **first section** addressed the introductory issues in overheads — explaining the meaning of overheads, the classification of overheads and the treatment of overheads.","The **second section** discussed the processes of overhead **allocation**, overhead **apportionment** and overhead **absorption**.","The **final section** explained the concept of overhead absorption — the six methods that can be used to absorb overheads, the reasons for preferring **predetermined** absorption rates to actual rates, and the treatment of overhead **under-absorbed** or **over-absorbed**."]},{"key":"Overheads are a very important concept in cost accounting and it is important to understand them thoroughly, because overhead concepts recur in every subsequent topic — job and contract costing (Chapter 7), process costing (Chapter 8), marginal versus absorption costing (Chapter 9), budgeting (Chapter 12) and the fixed overhead variances of standard costing (Chapter 13) all rest on this chapter."}]},{"n":"5.12","t":"Study text examination-type questions, worked","b":[{"p":"The study text closes the chapter with nine examination-type questions, drawn from past ICAG, CIMA, CACA and ACCA papers. All are reproduced below with full answers."},{"eg":{"tag":"Exam type — Q5.1 (20 marks) — ICAG, May 2000","t":"Absorbed fixed overhead, absorption versus marginal profit, and the reconciliation","q":[{"p":"The following information is available for a firm producing and selling a single product. Budgeted costs at normal activity:"},{"stmt":{"t":"Budgeted costs at normal activity","rows":[["","¢000","@hd"],["Direct material and labour",264000],["Variable production overhead",48000],["Fixed production overhead",144000],["Variable selling and administration overhead",24000],["Fixed selling and administration overhead",96000]]}},{"p":"The overhead absorption rates are based upon **normal activity of 240,000 units** per period. During the period just ended **260,000 units** were produced and **230,000 units** were sold at **¢3,000 per unit**. At the beginning of the period **40,000 units** were in stock, valued at the budgeted costs shown above. Actual costs incurred were as per the budget."},{"p":"**Required:** (a) calculate the fixed production overhead absorbed during the period and the extent of any over- or under-absorption, using absorption costing; (b) calculate the profit for the period using absorption costing and marginal costing respectively; (c) reconcile the two profit figures; (d) state the situations in which the two profits would be the same."}],"a":[{"h4":"Step 1 — unit costs from the budget"},{"p":"Every budgeted cost is divided by the normal activity of 240,000 units:"},{"stmt":{"t":"Budgeted cost per unit","rows":[["","Budget ¢000","÷ 240,000 units","@hd"],["Direct material and labour",264000,"¢1,100"],["Variable production overhead",48000,"¢200"],["**Marginal (variable) production cost**",312000,"**¢1,300**","@t"],["Fixed production overhead",144000,"¢600"],["**Full (absorption) production cost**",456000,"**¢1,900**","@t"],"@gap",["Variable selling and administration overhead",24000,"¢100"],["Fixed selling and administration overhead",96000,"¢400"]]}},{"h4":"(a)  Fixed production overhead absorbed, and the over/under-absorption"},{"stmt":{"t":"Fixed production overhead","rows":[["Absorbed:  260,000 units produced × ¢600 per unit","","¢156,000,000"],["Incurred (actual = budget)","","¢144,000,000"],["**Over-absorbed**","","**¢12,000,000**","@tt"]]}},{"note":"Fixed overhead is absorbed on **production**, not on sales — 260,000 units were made, so 260,000 units' worth of fixed overhead went into the cost of production. Because output exceeded the 240,000 units on which the rate was set, more was absorbed than was spent, and the excess is credited to the profit and loss account."},{"h4":"(b)  Profit under absorption costing"},{"stmt":{"t":"Absorption costing — profit statement for the period (¢)","rows":[["Sales:  230,000 units × ¢3,000","",690000000],["Less cost of sales:  230,000 units × ¢1,900 full production cost","",437000000],["**Gross profit**","",253000000,"@t"],["Add: fixed production overhead over-absorbed","",12000000],["","",265000000,"@t"],["Less: variable selling and administration (230,000 × ¢100)",23000000,""],["          fixed selling and administration",96000000,119000000],["**Profit — absorption costing**","",146000000,"@tt"]]}},{"h4":"(b)  Profit under marginal costing"},{"stmt":{"t":"Marginal costing — profit statement for the period (¢)","rows":[["Sales:  230,000 units × ¢3,000","",690000000],["Less variable cost of sales:  230,000 × ¢1,300",299000000,""],["          variable selling and administration:  230,000 × ¢100",23000000,322000000],["**Contribution**","",368000000,"@t"],["Less: fixed production overhead (actual)",144000000,""],["          fixed selling and administration",96000000,240000000],["**Profit — marginal costing**","",128000000,"@tt"]]}},{"h4":"(c)  Reconciliation of the two profits"},{"stmt":{"t":"Reconciliation (¢)","rows":[["Opening inventory (units)","",40000],["Add: production","",260000],["Less: sales","",-230000],["**Closing inventory (units)**","",70000,"@t"],["**Increase in inventory during the period (units)**","",30000,"@t"],"@gap",["Fixed production overhead per unit","","¢600"],["**Fixed overhead carried forward in the inventory increase: 30,000 × ¢600**","",18000000,"@t"],"@gap",["Profit — marginal costing","",128000000],["Add: fixed production overhead carried forward in the increase in inventory","",18000000],["**Profit — absorption costing**","",146000000,"@tt"]]}},{"key":"The whole difference between the two systems is **where fixed production overhead sits**. Under marginal costing it is written off in full in the period it is incurred. Under absorption costing it is attached to units, so when inventory rises, ¢18 million of this period's fixed overhead is carried forward in the extra 30,000 units and is **not** charged against this period's profit — which is exactly why absorption profit is ¢18 million higher. If inventory had fallen, the effect would reverse."},{"h4":"(d)  When the two profits are the same"},{"ol":["When **production equals sales**, so there is **no change in inventory** over the period — nothing is carried forward or released, and the same fixed overhead is charged under both systems.","When there is **no opening and no closing inventory** at all (a special case of the above).","When fixed production overhead per unit is the **same** in opening and closing inventory **and** inventory volume is unchanged — i.e. where the absorption rate has not altered between periods.","Where the business carries **no fixed production overhead** at all, so there is nothing to attach to inventory."]}]}},{"eg":{"tag":"Exam type — Q5.2 (25 marks) — ICAG, May 1997","t":"Atta Kwesi Manufacturing — a blanket rate versus departmental rates, and Job FX18","q":[{"p":"Atta Kwesi Manufacturing Company has three departments X, Y and Z. The company uses a **single production overhead rate**, expressed as a percentage of direct wages. A change of policy in favour of **departmental absorption rates** is contemplated, on the grounds that these can be more precise."},{"stmt":{"t":"Budgeted figures","rows":[["","Direct wages ¢000","Direct labour hours '000","Machine hours '000","Production overheads ¢000","@hd"],["Dept. X",250,100,400,1200],["Dept. Y",1000,500,100,300],["Dept. Z",250,250,"—",750],["**Total**",1500,850,500,2250,"@tt"]]}},{"stmt":{"t":"Actual figures","rows":[["","Direct wages ¢000","Direct labour hours '000","Machine hours '000","Production overheads ¢000","@hd"],["Dept. X",300,120,450,1300],["Dept. Y",800,450,140,280],["Dept. Z",300,300,"—","—"],["**Total**",1400,870,590,1580,"@tt"]]}},{"p":"Job No. **FX18** has just been completed. The actual information on this job is:"},{"stmt":{"t":"Job FX18","rows":[["","Direct materials ¢","Direct wages ¢","Direct labour hours","Machine hours","@hd"],["Dept. X",1200,1000,200,400],["Dept. Y",600,600,400,100],["Dept. Z",100,100,100,"—"],["**Total**",1900,1700,700,500,"@tt"]]}},{"p":"The pricing policy of the company is to make a **gross mark-up of 33⅓%** on completion of jobs, which is expected to cover other business expenses plus net profit."},{"p":"**Required:** (a) determine the overhead absorption rate which has been in use; (b) determine the production overhead to be absorbed by Job FX18 using that rate, and the price of Job FX18; (c) considering the new policy, determine the relevant departmental absorption rates, giving reasons for each rate chosen; (d) with the new rates, determine the new departmental overheads and total production overhead to be absorbed by Job FX18, and the price of the job on this new information."},{"note":"The actual production overhead for Department Z is printed as a blank. The printed actual total of ¢1,580 thousand equals ¢1,300 + ¢280, so the blank is consistent with nil. Nothing in the required parts depends on the actual overheads, which are given for context only — all the rates asked for are **predetermined**, and so are built from the **budgeted** figures."}],"a":[{"h4":"(a)  The overhead absorption rate in use"},{"p":"A single (blanket) rate expressed as a percentage of direct wages:"},{"tex":"\\text{Blanket rate} = \\frac{\\text{Budgeted production overheads}}{\\text{Budgeted direct wages}} = \\frac{2{,}250}{1{,}500} \\times 100 = 150\\% \\text{ of direct wages}"},{"h4":"(b)  Overhead absorbed by Job FX18, and its price"},{"stmt":{"t":"Job FX18 under the blanket rate (¢)","rows":[["Direct materials  (1,200 + 600 + 100)","",1900],["Direct wages  (1,000 + 600 + 100)","",1700],["**Prime cost**","",3600,"@t"],["Production overhead absorbed:  150% × ¢1,700","",2550],["**Total production cost**","",6150,"@t"],["Gross mark-up at 33⅓% of cost","",2050],["**Price of Job FX18**","",8200,"@tt"]]}},{"h4":"(c)  Departmental absorption rates, and the reasons for each"},{"p":"The right basis for each department is the one that best measures what actually causes overhead to be incurred there. Compare each department's machine hours with its labour hours:"},{"stmt":{"t":"Choosing a basis","rows":[["","Direct labour hrs ('000)","Machine hrs ('000)","Character of the department","@hd"],["Dept. X",100,400,"**Machine-intensive** — 4 machine hours per labour hour"],["Dept. Y",500,100,"**Labour-intensive** — 5 labour hours per machine hour"],["Dept. Z",250,"—","**Labour only** — no machinery at all"]]}},{"stmt":{"t":"Departmental absorption rates","rows":[["Department","Budgeted overhead ¢000","Basis","Rate","@hd"],["X",1200,"400,000 machine hours","**¢3.00 per machine hour**"],["Y",300,"500,000 direct labour hours","**¢0.60 per direct labour hour**"],["Z",750,"250,000 direct labour hours","**¢3.00 per direct labour hour**"]]}},{"ul":["**Dept. X — machine hour rate.** Overhead here is overwhelmingly machine-driven (depreciation, power, maintenance, machine insurance), and the department runs four machine hours for every labour hour, so machine time is what consumes the overhead.","**Dept. Y — direct labour hour rate.** The department is labour-intensive, with five labour hours per machine hour, so overhead is incurred broadly in proportion to the time people spend there.","**Dept. Z — direct labour hour rate.** There are no machine hours at all, so machine hours are not available as a basis. (A percentage of direct wages would give the same answer here — 300% of ¢250,000 — because wages and hours happen to stand in the ratio ¢1 per hour; the labour hour rate is preferable in principle because it is unaffected by differences in wage rates between workers.)"]},{"h4":"(d)  Job FX18 under the departmental rates"},{"stmt":{"t":"Overhead absorbed by Job FX18 (¢)","rows":[["Dept. X:  400 machine hours × ¢3.00","",1200],["Dept. Y:  400 direct labour hours × ¢0.60","",240],["Dept. Z:  100 direct labour hours × ¢3.00","",300],["**Total production overhead absorbed**","",1740,"@tt"]]}},{"stmt":{"t":"Price of Job FX18 under the new rates (¢)","rows":[["Direct materials","",1900],["Direct wages","",1700],["**Prime cost**","",3600,"@t"],["Production overhead absorbed","",1740],["**Total production cost**","",5340,"@t"],["Gross mark-up at 33⅓% of cost","",1780],["**Price of Job FX18**","",7120,"@tt"]]}},{"key":"The same job, the same costs, the same mark-up policy — and a price **¢1,080 lower** (¢7,120 against ¢8,200), a difference of over 13%. The blanket rate charged Job FX18 for overhead in proportion to its wages bill, and the job's wages are concentrated in Department Y, which has low overhead and absorbs it cheaply. The blanket rate therefore **over-costed** this job — and, necessarily, under-costed some other job. On the blanket rate Atta Kwesi may well have lost tenders it could profitably have won. That is the entire argument for departmental rates."}]}},{"eg":{"tag":"Exam type — Q5.3 (24 marks) — ICAG, January 1996","t":"Moulding and Finishing — percentage-of-prime-cost rates under two treatments of machine preparation time","q":[{"p":"A manufacturing company producing in batches is preparing a budget for the next financial period. There are two production departments — **Moulding** and **Finishing**. The following information for the coming year has been compiled:"},{"stmt":{"t":"Budget for the coming year","rows":[["","Moulding (¢'000)","Finishing (¢'000)","@hd"],["Direct materials",640000,120000],["Direct labour  *(56,000 hours;  14,000 hours)*",224000,44800],["Overhead (apportioned)",313600,62720],["**Total**",1177600,227520,"@tt"]]}},{"p":"The following forecast has been made:"},{"ol":["In the Moulding department **90%** of the labour time will be spent in actual production and **10%** adjusting machines before actual production; in the Finishing department **80%** will be actual production and **20%** preparing machines.","**2,000 batches** of work will be produced. Work passes through both stages before completion. The labour employed in preparing machines could be treated either **(i) as part of direct labour cost**, or **(ii) as an addition to the overhead** apportioned to each department."]},{"p":"**Required:** **(a)** calculate for each department the overhead absorption rates on a **percentage of prime cost** basis, under each of the two treatments (to the nearest whole number) *(12 marks)*. **(b)** calculate the total production cost of each of the three batches below, on each of the two sets of rates *(12 marks)*."},{"stmt":{"t":"The three batches","rows":[["Batch No.","Direct labour hrs — Moulding","Direct labour hrs — Finishing","Materials (kg) — Moulding","Materials (kg) — Finishing","Price per kilo (¢)","@hd"],["C061",35,22,250,101,800],["L024",38,29,411,139,690],["M115",19,12,89,26,1100]]}},{"note":"The study text prints the labour-hour figures on the direct **materials** line; they are the departments' direct **labour** hours, which is the only reading under which the direct labour cost and the batch data are consistent."}],"a":[{"h4":"Working — the direct labour rate per hour"},{"stmt":{"t":"Direct labour rate per hour","rows":[["Moulding:  ¢224,000,000 ÷ 56,000 hours","","¢4,000 per hour"],["Finishing:  ¢44,800,000 ÷ 14,000 hours","","¢3,200 per hour"]]}},{"h4":"(a)(i)  Rates with machine-preparation time treated as **direct labour cost**"},{"p":"All the labour is direct, so prime cost and overhead are exactly as budgeted:"},{"stmt":{"t":"Overhead as a percentage of prime cost — treatment (i)  (¢'000)","rows":[["","Moulding","Finishing","@hd"],["Direct materials",640000,120000],["Direct labour (all of it)",224000,44800],["**Prime cost**",864000,164800,"@t"],["Overhead",313600,62720],["**Overhead as % of prime cost**","36.3%  →  **36%**","38.1%  →  **38%**","@tt"]]}},{"h4":"(a)(ii)  Rates with machine-preparation time treated as **overhead**"},{"p":"The preparation portion of the wage bill moves out of direct labour and into overhead — prime cost falls and overhead rises, so the percentage rises twice over:"},{"stmt":{"t":"Overhead as a percentage of prime cost — treatment (ii)  (¢'000)","rows":[["","Moulding","Finishing","@hd"],["Direct materials",640000,120000],["Direct labour — productive only (90% × 224,000;  80% × 44,800)",201600,35840],["**Prime cost**",841600,155840,"@t"],["Overhead as apportioned",313600,62720],["Add: preparation labour (10% × 224,000;  20% × 44,800)",22400,8960],["**Total overhead**",336000,71680,"@t"],["**Overhead as % of prime cost**","39.9%  →  **40%**","46.0%  →  **46%**","@tt"]]}},{"h4":"(b)  Total production cost of the three batches"},{"p":"Materials are the same under either treatment. Direct labour is the full hours in treatment (i), and only the productive 90%/80% of the hours in treatment (ii)."},{"stmt":{"t":"Batch C061  (materials at ¢800 per kilo)","rows":[["","Treatment (i) — ¢","Treatment (ii) — ¢","@hd"],["**Moulding**","","","@hd"],["Direct materials  250 kg × ¢800",200000,200000],["Direct labour  35 hrs × ¢4,000;  31.5 hrs × ¢4,000",140000,126000],["Prime cost",340000,326000],["Overhead  36% × 340,000;  40% × 326,000",122400,130400],["**Moulding cost**",462400,456400,"@t"],"@gap",["**Finishing**","","","@hd"],["Direct materials  101 kg × ¢800",80800,80800],["Direct labour  22 hrs × ¢3,200;  17.6 hrs × ¢3,200",70400,56320],["Prime cost",151200,137120],["Overhead  38% × 151,200;  46% × 137,120",57456,63075],["**Finishing cost**",208656,200195,"@t"],"@gap",["**Total production cost — Batch C061**",671056,656595,"@tt"]]}},{"stmt":{"t":"Batch L024  (materials at ¢690 per kilo)","rows":[["","Treatment (i) — ¢","Treatment (ii) — ¢","@hd"],["Moulding materials  411 kg × ¢690",283590,283590],["Moulding labour  38 hrs;  34.2 hrs",152000,136800],["Moulding prime cost",435590,420390],["Moulding overhead  36%;  40%",156812,168156],"@gap",["Finishing materials  139 kg × ¢690",95910,95910],["Finishing labour  29 hrs;  23.2 hrs",92800,74240],["Finishing prime cost",188710,170150],["Finishing overhead  38%;  46%",71710,78269],"@gap",["**Total production cost — Batch L024**",852822,836965,"@tt"]]}},{"stmt":{"t":"Batch M115  (materials at ¢1,100 per kilo)","rows":[["","Treatment (i) — ¢","Treatment (ii) — ¢","@hd"],["Moulding materials  89 kg × ¢1,100",97900,97900],["Moulding labour  19 hrs;  17.1 hrs",76000,68400],["Moulding prime cost",173900,166300],["Moulding overhead  36%;  40%",62604,66520],"@gap",["Finishing materials  26 kg × ¢1,100",28600,28600],["Finishing labour  12 hrs;  9.6 hrs",38400,30720],["Finishing prime cost",67000,59320],["Finishing overhead  38%;  46%",25460,27287],"@gap",["**Total production cost — Batch M115**",328964,319427,"@tt"]]}},{"key":"Every batch comes out **cheaper** under treatment (ii), even though the factory spends exactly the same money either way. The reason is the absorption base: moving preparation labour out of prime cost shrinks the base these batches are charged on, and although the percentage rate rises to compensate at the level of the whole department, it does not compensate exactly for any **individual** batch. Total cost across all 2,000 batches is unchanged — what changes is how it is spread between them. This is the general lesson of absorption: the choice of base is a choice about **who bears the overhead**, not about how much there is."}]}},{"eg":{"tag":"Exam type — Q5.4 (28 marks) — ICAG, January 1988","t":"Larson Co. Ltd — full overhead apportionment, service-department reapportionment, and the pricing of two jobs","q":[{"p":"Larson Co. Ltd, a jobbing engineering firm, has in its single factory three production departments (**moulding, assembly and finishing**) supported by two service departments (**boiler and maintenance**). The company's production costs for the past month are:"},{"stmt":{"t":"Production costs for the month (¢)","rows":[["**Prime cost**","","","@hd"],["Direct materials",360000,""],["Direct wages",300000,660000],"@gap",["**Indirect materials**","","","@hd"],["Moulding",11240,""],["Assembly",17890,""],["Finishing",6390,""],["Boiler",11000,""],["Maintenance",5000,51520],"@gap",["**Supervision and indirect wages**","","","@hd"],["Moulding",13000,""],["Assembly",16800,""],["Finishing",13600,""],["Boiler",12420,""],["Maintenance",10380,66200],"@gap",["**General production overheads**","","","@hd"],["Factory rent and rates",25200,""],["Insurance — machinery",9900,""],["Insurance — building",7700,""],["Depreciation — machinery",18000,""],["Depreciation — building",7280,""],["Administrative costs of wages department",16800,84880],"@gap",["**Total factory cost**","",862600,"@tt"]]}},{"warn":"The study text prints the supervision and indirect wages subtotal as \"6,200\" and the total factory cost as ¢882,600. The five departmental figures sum to **¢66,200**, and the total factory cost is therefore **¢862,600**. The corrected figures are used above and are confirmed below: the overheads to be apportioned come to exactly $51{,}520 + 66{,}200 + 84{,}880 = ¢202{,}600$, which is what the departmental analysis totals.","lbl":"Source arithmetic corrected"},{"p":"Further information is available:"},{"stmt":{"t":"Departmental data","rows":[["","Moulding","Assembly","Finishing","Boiler","Maintenance","@hd"],["Area (square metres)",600,400,200,100,100],["Direct labour (hours)",15000,15000,10000,"—","—"],["Direct labour cost (¢)",45000,30000,25000,"—","—"],["Machine hours",20000,30000,40000,"—","—"],["Net book value of plant (¢)",30000,60000,90000,120000,"nil"],["Number of employees",75,75,50,15,25],["Machine maintenance hours",156,312,468,104,"—"]]}},{"ul":["The factory manager estimates that **10% of total maintenance costs relates to the boiler** and the balance to the three production departments (the maintenance department uses none of the power produced by the boiler).","The most important factor in determining the requirement for maintenance is total machine hours; all machines in the factory can be taken to be of approximately equal value.","The company sets its selling prices as: **(1)** cost of production, plus **(2)** 25% uplift to cover normal administrative and selling cost, plus **(3)** a profit margin of **10% of the final selling price**.","It is the policy of the company to **absorb overhead on the basis of direct labour hours**."]},{"p":"Two jobs, X and Y, are currently being priced:"},{"stmt":{"t":"Jobs X and Y","rows":[["","Job X","Job Y","@hd"],["Direct materials","¢483.00","¢361.00"],"@gap",["**Direct labour**","","","@hd"],["Moulding","10 hrs — ¢30.00","15 hrs — ¢45.00"],["Assembly","20 hrs — ¢40.00","2 hrs — ¢4.00"],["Finishing","8 hrs — ¢20.00","12 hrs — ¢30.00"],"@gap",["**Machine hours**","","","@hd"],["Moulding","5 hrs","8 hrs"],["Assembly","7 hrs","1 hr"],["Finishing","1 hr","5 hrs"]]}},{"p":"**Required:** (a) calculate overhead absorption rates for the three production departments *(20 marks)*; (b) calculate the prices to be charged for jobs X and Y, to 2 decimal places *(8 marks)*."},{"note":"In the study text the job table's row labels sit one line above their figures, so the hours appear against the wrong departments. The alignment above is confirmed by the implied wage rates, which match the departmental data exactly: Moulding ¢30 ÷ 10 hrs = ¢3.00/hr (and ¢45,000 ÷ 15,000 hrs = ¢3.00); Assembly ¢40 ÷ 20 = ¢2.00 (¢30,000 ÷ 15,000 = ¢2.00); Finishing ¢20 ÷ 8 = ¢2.50 (¢25,000 ÷ 10,000 = ¢2.50)."}],"a":[{"h4":"Step 1 — allocate and apportion the overheads"},{"p":"Indirect materials and supervision are **allocated** — each is already identified with one department. The six general production overheads must be **apportioned** on a sensible basis:"},{"stmt":{"t":"Bases of apportionment","rows":[["Overhead","Amount (¢)","Basis","Why","@hd"],["Factory rent and rates",25200,"Area","Space occupied drives the cost of the premises"],["Insurance — building",7700,"Area","The premium follows the building, and so the space occupied"],["Depreciation — building",7280,"Area","As above"],["Insurance — machinery",9900,"Net book value of plant","The premium follows the value of the plant insured"],["Depreciation — machinery",18000,"Net book value of plant","Depreciation is a function of the plant's cost"],["Administrative costs of wages dept.",16800,"Number of employees","The wages department's work is driven by the number of people on the payroll"]]}},{"stmt":{"t":"Overhead analysis sheet (¢)","rows":[["","Basis","Moulding","Assembly","Finishing","Boiler","Maintenance","Total","@hd"],["Indirect materials","Allocated",11240,17890,6390,11000,5000,51520],["Supervision and indirect wages","Allocated",13000,16800,13600,12420,10380,66200],["Rent, rates, building insurance and depreciation (¢40,180)","Area 6:4:2:1:1",17220,11480,5740,2870,2870,40180],["Machinery insurance and depreciation (¢27,900)","NBV of plant",2790,5580,8370,11160,0,27900],["Wages department administration","Employees 75:75:50:15:25",5250,5250,3500,1050,1750,16800],["**Total before reapportionment**","",49500,57000,37600,38500,20000,202600,"@tt"]]}},{"h4":"Step 2 — reapportion the service departments"},{"p":"**Maintenance** is reapportioned on **machine maintenance hours** (156 : 312 : 468 : 104, total 1,040). This basis reproduces the factory manager's own estimate exactly — the boiler's 104 hours are $104 \\div 1{,}040 = 10\\%$ of the total, which is the 10% he states — so it is clearly the basis intended."},{"p":"**Boiler** is then reapportioned to the three **production departments only**, since the question states the maintenance department uses none of the power the boiler produces. The boiler supplies heat and power to the floor space it serves, so **area** (600 : 400 : 200) is used."},{"stmt":{"t":"Reapportionment of service departments (¢)","rows":[["","Moulding","Assembly","Finishing","Boiler","Maintenance","Total","@hd"],["Total before reapportionment",49500,57000,37600,38500,20000,202600],["Maintenance on machine maintenance hours 156:312:468:104",3000,6000,9000,2000,-20000,0],["",52500,63000,46600,40500,0,202600],["Boiler on area 6:4:2 (production departments only)",20250,13500,6750,-40500,0,0],["**Total production department overhead**",72750,76500,53350,0,0,202600,"@tt"]]}},{"h4":"(a)  Overhead absorption rates"},{"p":"Company policy is to absorb on **direct labour hours**:"},{"stmt":{"t":"Overhead absorption rates","rows":[["Department","Overhead (¢)","Direct labour hours","Rate per direct labour hour","@hd"],["Moulding",72750,15000,"**¢4.85**"],["Assembly",76500,15000,"**¢5.10**"],["Finishing",53350,10000,"**¢5.335**"]]}},{"h4":"(b)  Prices for jobs X and Y"},{"stmt":{"t":"Job X (¢)","rows":[["Direct materials","",483.0],["Direct labour  (30.00 + 40.00 + 20.00)","",90.0],["**Prime cost**","",573.0,"@t"],["Overhead — Moulding  10 hrs × ¢4.85",48.5,""],["Overhead — Assembly  20 hrs × ¢5.10",102.0,""],["Overhead — Finishing  8 hrs × ¢5.335",42.68,193.18],["**Cost of production**","",766.18,"@t"],["Add: 25% uplift for administrative and selling cost","",191.55],["**Cost plus uplift**","",957.73,"@t"],["Add: profit margin of 10% of the final selling price  (957.73 ÷ 0.9 − 957.73)","",106.41],["**Selling price of Job X**","",1064.14,"@tt"]]}},{"stmt":{"t":"Job Y (¢)","rows":[["Direct materials","",361.0],["Direct labour  (45.00 + 4.00 + 30.00)","",79.0],["**Prime cost**","",440.0,"@t"],["Overhead — Moulding  15 hrs × ¢4.85",72.75,""],["Overhead — Assembly  2 hrs × ¢5.10",10.2,""],["Overhead — Finishing  12 hrs × ¢5.335",64.02,146.97],["**Cost of production**","",586.97,"@t"],["Add: 25% uplift for administrative and selling cost","",146.74],["**Cost plus uplift**","",733.71,"@t"],["Add: profit margin of 10% of the final selling price  (733.71 ÷ 0.9 − 733.71)","",81.52],["**Selling price of Job Y**","",815.23,"@tt"]]}},{"warn":"The profit margin is **10% of the final selling price**, not 10% of cost. So the cost-plus-uplift figure represents 90% of the price, and the price is found by dividing by 0.9 — **not** by multiplying by 1.1. On Job X, multiplying by 1.1 would give ¢1,053.50 instead of ¢1,064.14, and the margin actually earned would be 9.09% of the price rather than the 10% intended. Watch the words **\"on cost\"** versus **\"of selling price\"** in every pricing question."},{"note":"The machine hours given for the two jobs are not used, because company policy is to absorb on direct labour hours. They are there to be discarded — recognising which data a question does **not** need is part of what is being tested. The ¢300,000 direct wages in the cost summary are likewise inconsistent with the ¢100,000 that the departmental analysis totals; neither figure is needed for the overhead rates, which depend only on hours."}]}},{"eg":{"tag":"Exam type — Q5.5 (20 marks) — CIMA Cost Accounting 1, November 1979","t":"Why predetermined rates are preferred, and whether a lower rate means a more efficient factory","q":[{"p":"**(a)** Explain why predetermined overhead absorption rates are preferred to overhead absorption rates calculated from factual information after the end of a financial period."},{"p":"**(b)** The production overhead absorption rates of factories X and Y are calculated using similar methods. However, the rate used by X is **lower** than that used by factory Y. Both factories produce the same type of product. Discuss whether or not this can be taken to be a sign that factory X is more efficient than factory Y."}],"a":[{"h4":"(a)  Why predetermined rates are preferred"},{"ol":["**Costs are needed before the period ends.** Quotations, tenders and selling prices must be prepared when the customer asks, not months later. An actual rate cannot be computed until the period has closed, by which time the job has been quoted, made and delivered.","**Invoicing and job costing cannot wait.** A job completed in the first week of the year would have to remain uncosted until the year end if actual rates were used.","**Actual rates fluctuate with volume.** Fixed overhead divided by the actual activity of a slack month gives a high rate, and of a busy month a low one, so identical jobs would be costed differently depending only on when they happened to be made. A predetermined rate based on **normal activity** removes this distortion.","**Actual rates fluctuate with seasonal expenditure.** Heating in winter, holiday pay in summer and an annual maintenance shutdown would all distort the rate of the month in which they fall. A predetermined annual rate spreads them evenly.","**They permit control.** Comparing overhead absorbed with overhead incurred produces the under- or over-absorption, which is itself a control signal, and it can be analysed into expenditure and volume causes. With actual rates there is nothing to compare and no variance can arise.","**They allow budgeting and planning.** Budgets, standard costs and the whole machinery of Chapters 12 and 13 presuppose a rate fixed in advance.","**They smooth and simplify the accounting**, avoiding monthly recalculation of rates and giving consistent inventory valuations through the year."]},{"h4":"(b)  Does X's lower rate mean X is more efficient?"},{"p":"**No — not without a great deal more information.** A lower overhead absorption rate shows only that the ratio of budgeted overhead to the budgeted absorption base is lower. Both the numerator and the denominator can differ for reasons that have nothing to do with efficiency."},{"p":"**Reasons for a lower rate that are unconnected with efficiency:**"},{"ol":["**A different absorption base, or a different level of it.** If X budgeted a higher activity level than Y, the same overhead spread over more hours gives a lower rate — X is merely busier, or more optimistic.","**A different degree of mechanisation.** A labour-intensive factory has low overhead and high direct labour cost; a highly automated one has high overhead (depreciation, power, maintenance) and low labour cost. If X is labour-intensive, its rate per labour hour will be lower while its **total** cost per unit may well be higher.","**Different cost classification.** If X treats some costs as direct that Y treats as indirect, X's overhead pool is smaller for that reason alone.","**Different ages of plant.** Y's newer plant carries more depreciation, raising its overhead even though the plant may be more productive.","**Different locations and circumstances** — rent, rates, wage rates and energy costs differ geographically.","**Different accounting policies** — depreciation methods and lives, and the treatment of service departments."]},{"p":"**What would actually be needed to judge efficiency:**"},{"ul":["the **total cost per unit** of comparable output in each factory, not the overhead rate alone;","each factory's overhead **expenditure against its own budget** — that is a measure of control, whereas the rate is not;","physical measures: output per labour hour, machine utilisation, scrap and rework rates, idle time;","the **under- or over-absorption** in each factory and its causes."]},{"key":"A rate is a **ratio**, and a ratio can fall because the numerator fell *or* because the denominator rose. Factory X might have a lower rate precisely because it is **less** efficient — if its labour is slow, it books more labour hours, and the same overhead divided by more hours gives a lower rate per hour. The rate would fall while the cost per unit rose."}]}},{"eg":{"tag":"Exam type — Q5.6 (20 marks)","t":"Allocation, apportionment and absorption distinguished; causes of under/over absorption","q":[{"p":"**(a)** Explain the differences between the following terms as used in cost accounting: (i) allocation of overheads; (ii) apportionment of overheads; (iii) absorption of overheads *(12 marks)*."},{"p":"**(b)** Explain the reasons for overhead under-absorbed and overhead over-absorbed *(8 marks)*."}],"a":[{"h4":"(a)  The three terms"},{"table":{"align":"llll","head":["","Allocation","Apportionment","Absorption"],"rows":[["**What it means**","Charging a **whole** item of overhead to **one** cost centre, because the whole of it was incurred by that centre.","Sharing an item of overhead **between several** cost centres on an equitable basis, because it was incurred by all of them jointly.","Charging the overhead accumulated in a production cost centre **out to the cost units** that pass through it, by means of an absorption rate."],["**When used**","When the cost is **wholly identifiable** with one cost centre.","When the cost is **shared** and cannot be identified with one centre.","After allocation and apportionment are complete and all overhead sits in production cost centres."],["**Direction**","Overhead → one cost centre","Overhead → several cost centres","Cost centre → cost units"],["**Example**","The salary of the moulding department's own supervisor, charged wholly to moulding.","Factory rent, shared between all departments on floor area.","A job spending 10 hours in moulding is charged 10 × the moulding hourly overhead rate."],["**Basis required?**","No — the whole amount goes to one place.","Yes — an equitable basis (area, employees, net book value, machine hours…).","Yes — an absorption base (labour hours, machine hours, % of wages…)."]]}},{"note":"The sequence is always the same and is worth memorising as a sentence: **allocate what you can, apportion what you cannot, reapportion the service departments, then absorb into units.** Allocation and apportionment both move cost *between cost centres*; only absorption moves cost *onto the product*."},{"h4":"(b)  Reasons for under- and over-absorption"},{"p":"Overhead is absorbed at a **predetermined** rate — budgeted overhead divided by budgeted activity — and applied to **actual** activity. A difference from actual overhead incurred is therefore almost inevitable, and it has exactly two sources:"},{"fbox":{"h":"The two causes","rows":[{"lb":"Expenditure difference","tex":"\\text{Budgeted overhead} - \\text{Actual overhead incurred}","nt":"Actual spending differed from what was budgeted — price rises, unexpected repairs, savings made, a change in the cost structure."},{"lb":"Volume difference","tex":"(\\text{Actual activity} - \\text{Budgeted activity}) \\times \\text{Absorption rate}","nt":"Actual activity differed from the level on which the rate was built — more or fewer hours worked or units made than budgeted."}]}},{"p":"**Overhead is UNDER-absorbed when:**"},{"ol":["**actual overhead exceeds budgeted overhead** — unforeseen expenditure, price increases, emergency repairs, higher insurance or energy costs; and/or","**actual activity falls below budgeted activity** — a strike, machine breakdown, material shortage, power failure, a fall in demand, or an over-optimistic budget — so fewer hours or units are available to carry the overhead."]},{"p":"**Overhead is OVER-absorbed when:**"},{"ol":["**actual overhead falls below budgeted overhead** — economies achieved, a budgeted cost not in fact incurred, or an over-cautious budget; and/or","**actual activity exceeds budgeted activity** — higher demand, improved efficiency, extra shifts or overtime, or a conservative activity budget."]},{"p":"A third, more fundamental cause cuts across both: **an inappropriate absorption base or an inaccurate budget**. If the base chosen does not reflect what actually drives overhead in the department, the rate will systematically mis-absorb however accurately it was computed."},{"key":"The treatment: whatever the cause, the balance left on the Production Overhead Control Account is written off to the costing profit and loss account — **debited** if under-absorbed (profit falls) and **credited** if over-absorbed (profit rises). It is **not** carried forward and **not** adjusted into inventory value, because inventory is valued at the predetermined rate."}]}},{"eg":{"tag":"Exam type — Q5.7 (20 marks) — CACA Management Accounting, June 1981","t":"A single factory-wide rate or separate cost-centre rates; and three methods of overhead recovery","q":[{"p":"Specify and explain the factors to be considered in determining whether to utilise a single factory-wide recovery rate for all production overheads, or a separate rate for each cost centre, production or service department *(12 marks)*."},{"p":"**(a)** Describe **three** methods of determining overhead recovery rates and specify the circumstances under which each method is superior to the others *(8 marks)*."}],"a":[{"h4":"Factors in choosing between a blanket rate and departmental rates"},{"ol":["**How similar the departments are.** If every department has much the same overhead per hour and products pass through them in much the same proportions, a single rate gives almost the same answer as departmental rates and is not worth the extra work. The greater the variation between departments, the stronger the case for separate rates (see Q5.2 above, where the two methods differ by 13% on one job).","**How uniform the product range is.** If all products pass through all departments in the same sequence and proportion, a blanket rate will not distort. If some products skip departments, or spend much longer in the expensive ones, a blanket rate systematically over-costs some products and under-costs others.","**The degree of mechanisation, and whether it varies.** A factory with one machine-intensive and one labour-intensive department cannot absorb both sensibly on the same base.","**The cost and complexity of operating the system.** Departmental rates need a full overhead analysis sheet, apportionment bases, reapportionment of service departments, and separate records of hours by department. The benefit must exceed this cost (§1.2.12).","**The use to which the cost will be put.** If costs drive pricing of individual jobs, or decisions to accept or refuse work, accuracy matters and departmental rates are worth their cost. If they are used only to value total inventory, a blanket rate may suffice.","**The materiality of overhead** in the total cost. Where overhead is a small fraction of total cost, even a crude rate cannot distort much; where it dominates, precision matters.","**The need for cost control and responsibility accounting.** Departmental rates require overhead to be analysed by cost centre, which is itself the basis for holding each manager responsible for their own overhead — a blanket rate provides no such analysis.","**The information available.** Departmental rates are impossible without records of hours by department and a defensible basis for apportioning shared costs."]},{"h4":"(a)  Three methods of determining overhead recovery rates"},{"table":{"align":"lll","head":["Method","Formula","When it is superior"],"rows":[["**Direct labour hour rate**","$\\dfrac{\\text{Overhead}}{\\text{Direct labour hours}}$","In **labour-intensive** departments, where overhead is incurred broadly in proportion to the time people spend working. It is superior to a wages-percentage method because it is unaffected by differences in wage rates between workers of different grades doing the same job for the same time."],["**Machine hour rate**","$\\dfrac{\\text{Overhead}}{\\text{Machine hours}}$","In **machine-intensive** departments, where most of the overhead (depreciation, power, maintenance, machine insurance, space for the machine) is caused by running machines rather than by employing people. Superior wherever machine time, not labour time, drives the cost."],["**Percentage of direct wages**","$\\dfrac{\\text{Overhead}}{\\text{Direct wages}} \\times 100$","Where hours are **not recorded** and only wage values are available, and where wage rates within the department are reasonably uniform so that cost is a fair proxy for time. Simple and cheap, but distorted whenever two workers on different rates spend the same time on a job."]]}},{"note":"The other three methods the chapter covers, for completeness: **percentage of direct material cost** (only defensible where material cost and overhead move together, which is rare); **percentage of prime cost** (a compromise inheriting the weaknesses of both the material and wages bases); and the **unit (cost unit) rate** — overhead ÷ units produced — which is superior only where the output is a **single homogeneous product**, when it is both the simplest and the most accurate method available."}]}},{"eg":{"tag":"Exam type — Q5.8 (20 marks) — ACCA Costing","t":"Procedures for setting predetermined rates, and the purpose of calculating them","q":[{"p":"**(a)** Outline the procedures and information required in order to determine a set of predetermined production overhead absorption rates for a company manufacturing a range of different products in a factory containing a number of production departments and several service departments."},{"p":"**(b)** Critically examine the purpose of calculating overhead absorption rates."}],"a":[{"h4":"(a)  The procedure, step by step"},{"steps":["**Define the cost centres.** Divide the factory into production cost centres and service cost centres, each with a manager responsible for it.","**Budget the overhead expenditure** for the coming period, item by item, for the factory as a whole and for each cost centre — using past costs adjusted for known price changes, planned activity and planned changes in method.","**Allocate** every overhead that is wholly identifiable with one cost centre directly to it.","**Apportion** the remaining shared overheads to all cost centres on equitable bases (area, number of employees, net book value of plant, metered usage, and so on), chosen so that each basis reflects what actually causes the cost.","**Reapportion the service cost centre totals** to the production cost centres, on bases measuring the service each receives — and where service departments serve each other, by the repeated distribution, simultaneous equation or elimination method (§5.4).","**Budget the activity level** of each production cost centre — direct labour hours, machine hours, units or direct wages — at the **normal** level of activity rather than at full capacity.","**Select the absorption base** for each production cost centre, matched to whether it is labour- or machine-intensive, and to the records actually available.","**Compute the rate** for each production cost centre: budgeted overhead after reapportionment ÷ budgeted activity for that centre.","**Review and approve** the rates, and build in a procedure for monitoring the resulting under- or over-absorption during the period, and for revising the rates if conditions change materially."]},{"h4":"(a)  Information required"},{"ul":["historical overhead costs, analysed by type and by department, as a starting point;","the production budget and sales budget for the coming period;","expected price changes — wage awards, rents, energy, insurance premiums;","the budgeted activity of each cost centre in the chosen base (hours, units, wages);","physical data for the apportionment bases — floor area, number of employees, net book value and location of plant, metered consumption, machine maintenance hours;","the pattern of service provided by each service department to each other department;","planned changes in product mix, methods, plant or staffing."]},{"h4":"(b)  The purpose of calculating overhead absorption rates — critically examined"},{"p":"**The purposes claimed for them:**"},{"ol":["**Inventory valuation and profit measurement.** IAS 2 requires inventory to include an appropriate share of production overhead, so an absorption rate is **unavoidable** for external reporting.","**Product costing and pricing.** A full cost per unit is needed to set a cost-plus price and to judge whether current prices cover total cost in the long run.","**Timely job costing**, so that jobs can be costed and invoiced as they are completed rather than at the year end.","**Cost control**, through the under/over-absorption figure and its analysis into expenditure and volume causes.","**Decision making and planning** — budgeting, tendering, and assessing the relative profitability of products."]},{"p":"**The criticisms:**"},{"ol":["**The resulting unit cost is arbitrary.** It depends entirely on the apportionment bases and the absorption base chosen; a different, equally defensible, set of bases gives a different \"cost\" for the same product. There is no single true full cost.","**It is of limited use for decisions.** Most short-run decisions — accept or reject a special order, make or buy, drop a product — turn on **relevant** costs, which are usually marginal. Fixed overhead absorbed into a unit cost is not avoided by declining the order, so a decision made on absorbed cost can be simply wrong (Chapter 9).","**It can mislead on profitability.** A product carrying a large apportioned share of fixed overhead may appear to make a loss and be discontinued — after which the same fixed overhead is reapportioned onto the remaining products, which then appear less profitable in turn.","**Absorption on volume-based rates distorts a modern cost structure.** Where overhead is driven by set-ups, inspections and material handling rather than by volume, absorbing on labour or machine hours over-costs high-volume products and under-costs low-volume ones — which is the case for activity-based costing (§5.8).","**It encourages overproduction.** Because absorption costing attaches fixed overhead to inventory, producing more than can be sold increases reported profit (see Q5.1 above), which is a perverse incentive.","**It is costly to operate**, and the under/over-absorption it generates needs explaining to managers who did not cause it."]},{"key":"A balanced conclusion: absorption rates are **indispensable for inventory valuation and long-run pricing**, and are required by accounting standards — but they are a **poor guide to short-run decisions**, which need marginal and relevant costs, and in a modern overhead-heavy factory they may need refining by activity-based costing before the resulting product costs can be trusted."}]}},{"eg":{"tag":"Exam type — Q5.9 (12½ marks)","t":"XYZ Ltd — blanket versus cost-centre rates, and an under-absorption","q":[{"p":"**(a)** Distinguish between a **blanket rate** and a **cost centre (multiple) rate** used for absorbing overheads."},{"p":"**(b)** The following data were provided by XYZ Ltd:"},{"stmt":{"t":"XYZ Ltd — data","rows":[["Budgeted labour hours","",8500],["Budgeted overheads","","N148,750"],["Actual labour hours","",7928],["Actual overheads","","N146,200"]]}},{"p":"**Required:** (i) what is the labour hour overhead absorption rate? (ii) what is the amount of overhead under- or over-absorbed, if any? (iii) produce the overhead control account for the transactions above; (iv) give the major reason for the under/over-absorption in (ii)."}],"a":[{"h4":"(a)  Blanket rate versus cost centre (multiple) rate"},{"table":{"align":"lll","head":["","Blanket (single, factory-wide) rate","Cost centre (multiple / departmental) rate"],"rows":[["**Definition**","A **single** overhead absorption rate computed for the factory as a whole and applied to every product, whichever departments it passes through.","A **separate** rate computed for **each** production cost centre, each product being charged the rate of every department it actually passes through."],["**Computation**","Total factory overhead ÷ total factory activity.","Each department's overhead (after reapportionment of service departments) ÷ that department's own activity."],["**Accuracy**","Crude. Acceptable only where all products pass through all departments in much the same proportions, and departments have similar overhead per hour.","Much more accurate, because it reflects where the product actually spent its time and what that department's overhead really costs per hour."],["**Administrative cost**","Low — no overhead analysis sheet, no apportionment, no reapportionment.","High — needs a full analysis sheet, apportionment bases, reapportionment, and hours recorded by department."],["**Suitable for**","Small factories, single-product businesses, or where overhead is immaterial.","Multi-department factories making a varied product range, especially where mechanisation differs between departments."]]}},{"h4":"(b)(i)  Labour hour overhead absorption rate"},{"tex":"\\text{OAR} = \\frac{\\text{Budgeted overheads}}{\\text{Budgeted labour hours}} = \\frac{N148{,}750}{8{,}500} = N17.50 \\text{ per labour hour}"},{"h4":"(b)(ii)  Overhead under- or over-absorbed"},{"stmt":{"t":"Under/over absorption","rows":[["Overhead absorbed:  7,928 actual labour hours × N17.50","",138740],["Actual overhead incurred","",146200],["**Overhead UNDER-absorbed**","",7460,"@tt"]]}},{"h4":"(b)(iii)  Overhead Control Account"},{"tacc":{"t":"Production Overhead Control Account","drl":"Dr  (N)","crl":"Cr  (N)","dr":[["Bank / creditors — actual overhead incurred",146200],["",146200,"@tot"]],"cr":[["Work-in-progress — overhead absorbed (7,928 × N17.50)",138740],["Costing Profit and Loss — under-absorbed overhead",7460],["",146200,"@tot"]]}},{"h4":"(b)(iv)  The major reason for the under-absorption"},{"p":"The under-absorption is caused almost entirely by a **shortfall in activity**: only 7,928 labour hours were worked against the 8,500 budgeted, so 572 hours' worth of overhead was never charged to production. Analysing the N7,460 into its two causes makes this plain:"},{"stmt":{"t":"Analysis of the N7,460 under-absorption","rows":[["**Volume cause:** (7,928 − 8,500 hours) × N17.50","",-10010],["**Expenditure cause:** budgeted overhead N148,750 − actual N146,200","",2550],["**Net under-absorption**","",-7460,"@tt"]]}},{"p":"So the business actually **spent N2,550 less** than it budgeted — expenditure control was good — but it **worked 572 fewer hours** than planned, which alone caused N10,010 of under-absorption. The major reason is therefore **a fall in activity below the budgeted level**, which might be due to lower demand, machine breakdowns, labour shortages, absenteeism, material shortages, or an over-optimistic activity budget."},{"key":"Note the direction convention. A **debit** balance remaining on the overhead control account is **under-absorption** — more was spent than was charged out — and it is written off as an extra expense, reducing profit. Here the account is balanced by a **credit** of N7,460 to close it, with the matching **debit** going to the costing profit and loss account."}]}}]}],"formulas":[{"lb":"Overhead absorption rate (general)","tex":"OAR = \\dfrac{\\text{Budgeted overhead}}{\\text{Budgeted activity level}}"},{"lb":"OAR — direct labour hour basis","tex":"OAR = \\dfrac{\\text{Budgeted overhead}}{\\text{Budgeted DLH}}"},{"lb":"OAR — machine hour basis","tex":"OAR = \\dfrac{\\text{Budgeted overhead}}{\\text{Budgeted machine hours}}"},{"lb":"OAR — cost-percentage bases (DM/DL/prime cost)","tex":"OAR = \\dfrac{\\text{Budgeted overhead}}{\\text{Budgeted DM, DL or prime cost}} \\times 100"},{"lb":"Overhead absorbed","tex":"\\text{OAR} \\times \\text{Actual quantity of activity base}"},{"lb":"Cost driver rate (ABC)","tex":"\\dfrac{\\text{Activity cost for the pool}}{\\text{Quantity of cost driver available}}"}],"focus":"Overheads is usually the single most heavily weighted chapter in the paper, spanning at least one full Section B question most diets. Master the full allocation → apportionment → absorption sequence as one pipeline, not three separate topics. For reciprocal service centres, be able to name which of the three methods a question is asking for and apply only that one — do not blend figures across methods. Over/under absorption questions almost always want the write-off-to-P&L treatment named explicitly as \"preferred,\" with a reason.","errors":["Absorbing overhead using the *budgeted* activity level instead of the *actual* activity level — absorption always uses actual activity against the predetermined (budgeted-derived) rate.","Forgetting to re-normalise a service centre's remaining percentage shares once one of its reciprocal partners has already been closed out under the elimination method.","Mixing up over-absorption (absorbed > incurred, a credit/favourable position) with under-absorption (incurred > absorbed).","Using a blanket, factory-wide rate when the question's own data shows different cost centres with clearly different overhead-to-activity ratios — this is exactly the scenario departmental rates exist to fix.","In ABC, dividing the cost pool by one product's driver usage instead of the *total* driver quantity across all products when finding the cost driver rate."],"quiz":{"mcq":[{"q":"The assignment of an overhead cost directly to one cost centre, with no sharing, is called","o":["apportionment","absorption","allocation","activity-based costing","a blanket rate"],"a":2,"w":"Allocation is direct assignment with no sharing; apportionment shares a cost across several centres; absorption is the final step, charging overhead into cost units.","src":"Chapter 5.3","sec":"5.3"},{"q":"A cost centre's overhead is budgeted at ₵2,400,000 and budgeted machine hours are 480. The overhead absorption rate is","o":["₵500 per machine hour","₵5,000 per machine hour","₵50 per machine hour","₵200 per machine hour","₵4,800 per machine hour"],"a":1,"w":"OAR = budgeted overhead ÷ budgeted machine hours = 2,400,000 / 480 = ₵5,000/machine hour.","calc":"OAR = \\dfrac{2{,}400{,}000}{480} = 5{,}000","src":"Chapter 5.5","sec":"5.5"},{"q":"A department's OAR is ₵4 per direct labour hour, and actual direct labour hours worked during the period were 50,000 against overhead actually incurred of ₵215,000. The overhead is","o":["under-absorbed by ₵15,000","over-absorbed by ₵15,000","exactly absorbed","under-absorbed by ₵200,000","over-absorbed by ₵35,000"],"a":0,"w":"Overhead absorbed = 4 × 50,000 = ₵200,000. Incurred ₵215,000 exceeds absorbed by ₵15,000 — under-absorbed.","calc":"(4 \\times 50{,}000) - 215{,}000 = -15{,}000","src":"Chapter 5.7","sec":"5.7"},{"q":"The preferred treatment of overhead under- or over-absorption at the end of a period is to","o":["carry it forward to future periods","adjust it into the cost of units produced during the period","write it off to the Profit and Loss Account as a period cost","ignore it entirely","split it equally between all cost centres"],"a":2,"w":"Writing the balance off to the P&L account as a period cost is explicitly stated as the most preferred treatment — the other two named alternatives both have real drawbacks (limited management usefulness, or distorted future performance figures).","src":"Chapter 5.7","sec":"5.7"},{"q":"A single overhead recovery rate applied across an entire factory, rather than separately for each production cost centre, is called a","o":["departmental rate","blanket rate","predetermined rate","activity-based rate","marginal rate"],"a":1,"w":"This is the definition of a blanket (factory-wide) overhead recovery rate — generally not recommended, since different centres consume overhead and drive cost differently.","src":"Chapter 5.6","sec":"5.6"},{"q":"Which method of re-apportioning reciprocal service-centre overheads treats each centre's total overhead as an unknown, solved via a system of equations?","o":["Elimination method","Continuous allotment method","Simultaneous equations method","Blanket rate method","Activity-based costing"],"a":2,"w":"The simultaneous equations method expresses each reciprocal service centre's total overhead algebraically and solves the resulting equations.","src":"Chapter 5.4","sec":"5.4"},{"q":"In Activity-Based Costing, a \"cost driver\" is best described as","o":["the person responsible for approving overhead budgets","any factor that causes a change in the cost of an activity","the total overhead of the whole factory","a synonym for a blanket absorption rate","the department that incurs the least overhead"],"a":1,"w":"CIMA's own definition: a cost driver is any factor causing a change in the cost of an activity — e.g. the number of machine set-ups or material requisitions.","src":"Chapter 5.8","sec":"5.8"},{"q":"Overhead of ₵180,000 is pooled for a \"quality control\" activity with 60 total inspections across all products. The cost driver rate is","o":["₵3,000 per inspection","₵300 per inspection","₵1,800 per inspection","₵30,000 per inspection","₵10,800,000 per inspection"],"a":0,"w":"Cost driver rate = activity cost for the pool ÷ total driver quantity = 180,000 / 60 = ₵3,000 per inspection.","calc":"\\dfrac{180{,}000}{60} = 3{,}000","src":"Chapter 5.8","sec":"5.8"},{"q":"Budgeted overheads are preferred to actual overheads for setting an absorption rate mainly because","o":["actual overheads are always lower","actual figures are illegal to use in cost accounting","actual overheads are not known until after the period ends, and actual activity/price fluctuations would cause large swings in the rate","budgeted figures never need revising","HMRC requires budgeted figures only"],"a":2,"w":"Two reasons: actual figures are only available after the period ends (delaying product costing), and using actual overhead/activity would introduce large fluctuations driven by price levels and activity swings — budgeted figures reflect stable standards instead.","src":"Chapter 5.6","sec":"5.6"},{"q":"Overhead is absorbed into a job using an OAR based on direct material cost. This basis is most appropriate when","o":["material prices are stable and uniform","the factory is highly automated","labour is the dominant cost driver","output units are highly non-uniform","the business has no service cost centres"],"a":0,"w":"The direct-material-cost-percentage basis only gives good results when material prices are stable and uniform; where prices vary a lot, this basis distorts the absorption rate.","src":"Chapter 5.5","sec":"5.5"}],"theory":[{"q":"Explain the differences between the following terms as used in cost accounting: (i) Allocation of overheads, (ii) Apportionment of overheads, (iii) Absorption of overheads.","marks":12,"a":[{"p":"**(i) Allocation** — assigning an overhead cost that is specifically and wholly incurred for one cost centre, directly to that centre, with no sharing."},{"p":"**(ii) Apportionment** — sharing an overhead cost incurred for a number of cost centres (or the whole factory) among the centres that benefit from it, using a fair and equitable basis such as floor space, headcount or plant value."},{"p":"**(iii) Absorption** — the final step: charging the overhead now sitting in each production cost centre onto the actual cost units passing through it, using an overhead absorption rate (OAR) applied to the actual quantity of the chosen activity base."}],"src":"Chapter 5.3","sec":"5.3"},{"q":"Explain the reasons for overhead under-absorption and overhead over-absorption.","marks":8,"a":[{"p":"Both arise from the same root cause: the OAR is set using **budgeted** overhead and **budgeted** activity, but overhead is absorbed against **actual** activity, while the overhead actually **incurred** is a separate, independently-varying figure."},{"p":"**Under-absorption** results when actual overhead incurred exceeds overhead absorbed — e.g. actual activity fell short of budget (so less overhead was absorbed than planned), or actual overhead expenditure ran above budget."},{"p":"**Over-absorption** results when overhead absorbed exceeds overhead incurred — e.g. actual activity ran above budget (absorbing more than planned), or actual overhead expenditure came in below budget."}],"src":"Chapter 5.7","sec":"5.7"}]}},{"n":6,"t":"Interlocking and Integrated Accounts","brief":"The double-entry side of cost accounting: how cost transactions are posted to the cost ledger under an interlocking system (kept separate from the financial ledger, reconciled periodically) versus an integrated system (one unified set of accounts); why costing profit and financial profit differ, and how to reconcile them with a memorandum reconciliation account; accounts classification and codes; and computer-assisted costing techniques. Every ledger account in every illustration is written out in full, in T-account form, and every figure independently re-derived.","outcomes":["Post cost accounting transactions into the appropriate books of account","Apply double-entry principles in cost accounting","Differentiate between \"interlocking accounts\" and \"integrated accounts\"","Reconcile the financial accounting profit and the cost accounting profit","Design accounts codes in a summarised and suitable form for effective management of assets","Explain the application of computer-assisted techniques"],"secs":[{"n":"6.1","t":"Introduction","b":[{"def":{"t":"Bookkeeping","d":"identifying, recording and summarising transactions in the appropriate books of account. The balances obtained at the end of an accounting period are extracted to the **trial balance**, which is simply a list of balances, both debit and credit."}},{"p":"The system of bookkeeping is **not restricted to financial accounting** — it applies to cost accounting too. Both financial and cost accounting data must be properly kept and processed to develop and produce the information management needs for decision making."},{"p":"The **size of a business** normally determines how complex its cost records are:"},{"ul":["**Small businesses** keep simple records, not linked to the financial accounts.","**Larger businesses** maintain a full set of cost accounts, which provide the detail behind the totals shown in the financial accounts."]},{"p":"From that follow the two ways of organising the records, and this chapter explains both:"},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["System","How it works"],"rows":[["**Interlocking**","Cost accounts are kept **independent** of the financial accounts, so **periodic reconciliation is necessary**."],["**Integrated (integral)**","The cost accounts form part of the formal accounting system — financial and cost accounting are **combined**, generating a **single profit** figure, and **no reconciliation is necessary**."]]}},{"key":"The study text states plainly that the integrated method is **the more satisfactory** of the two, because it avoids duplication of effort and avoids reconciliation problems altogether. Interlocking survives mainly where the cost department and the financial department are genuinely separate functions, or where the costing system must be free to value inventory on a basis the financial accounts are not allowed to use."},{"p":"This chapter therefore focuses on two things: the **double-entry aspect of cost accounting**, and the **reconciliation** needed between cost accounts and financial accounts when the two are maintained separately. Reconciliation is required only where a firm adopts an interlocking system; where the integral system is maintained, both systems are combined and generate one profit."}]},{"n":"6.2","t":"Interlocking accounting system and cost ledger","b":[{"p":"An interlocking accounting system uses **separate cost accounts**, which are periodically reconciled with the financial accounts. The cost accountant uses a great deal of the same financial information available to the financial accountant:"},{"ul":["the purchases daybook;","the expenses daybook;","the payroll;","the main journal;","the sales daybook; and the like."]},{"p":"The **structure** of the interlocking system in any organisation is determined by the volume of its business transactions. The cost accounting records usually kept include those of:"},{"ul":["**cost elements** (materials, labour, expenses);","**cost centres** — production, marketing, and general administration;","**job costs** — production jobs, repair jobs, capital jobs; and","**inventory** — raw materials, components or spare parts, and finished goods."]},{"h4":"How the two systems are \"interlocked\""},{"p":"The interlocking of the two systems is carried out by the use of two mirror-image control accounts:"},{"ol":["**Cost ledger control account** — kept in the **financial ledger**;","**Financial ledger control account** — kept in the **cost ledger**."]},{"key":"These two accounts are the hinge of the whole system, and the names are a common source of confusion because the study text (and the exam) uses several of them interchangeably. The account sitting **inside the cost ledger** that absorbs every posting touching something outside costing is called, variously, the **Financial Ledger Control Account**, the **Cost Ledger Contra Account**, the **General Ledger Adjustment Account**, or — confusingly — the **Cost Ledger Control Account**. When a question names any of these, it means the same single account: the one that keeps the cost ledger self-balancing."}]},{"n":"6.3","t":"The cost ledger","b":[{"p":"In an interlocking cost accounting system, the **cost ledger is the principal ledger**. Generally, it will contain:"},{"ol":["**Control accounts** for stores, work-in-progress, finished goods and overhead. Each control account is supported by a **subsidiary ledger** providing the detail needed for accounting control and the preparation of information.","**Accounts for wages, sales and cost of sales**, together with any other account the cost bookkeeping system needs — e.g. notional charges, suspense accounts.","**Cost Ledger Contra Account** (also called the General Ledger Adjustment Account or the Cost Ledger Control Account). This account is needed to **maintain the double-entry principle** within the cost ledger. It contains postings which affect accounts *outside* the costing system — e.g. debtors, creditors or cash accounts, and other real and personal account transactions."]},{"note":"Why the contra account must exist at all: the cost ledger only holds cost accounts. When raw materials are bought, the debit (Raw Materials Control) sits in the cost ledger, but the credit belongs to the supplier — a personal account that lives in the *financial* ledger. The cost ledger would therefore have a debit with no matching credit. The Cost Ledger Contra Account supplies that missing credit, so the cost ledger balances on its own and can produce its own trial balance.","lbl":"Why the contra account exists"},{"h4":"The work-in-progress ledger is the focal point"},{"p":"The focal point of the cost accounting system is the **work-in-progress ledger**. In an organisation that operates specific-order costing, that ledger contains a **separate account for each job**. Each job account is debited with:"},{"ul":["the cost of **material used**;","the **wages allocated**; and","the **production overhead absorbed**."]},{"p":"When jobs are completed, the cost of completed jobs is transferred to the **Finished Goods Ledger**. When a job is delivered to the customer, **Cost of Sales** is debited with the job cost and **Sales** is credited with the selling price. A (costing) profit and loss statement can then be prepared and analysed **job by job**. The balances on the control accounts represent the cost value of stores, work-in-progress and finished goods."},{"note":"The term **\"control\" account** implies that a subsidiary ledger is kept behind it. Entries to control accounts represent **summary postings** — the individual detail lives in the subsidiary ledger, not in the control account itself."}]},{"n":"6.4","t":"Cost accounting entries under the interlocking system","b":[{"p":"The relevant entries, in the order the study text gives them, running from raw material purchase through to output achieved:"},{"table":{"align":"lll","head":["#  Transaction","Debit","Credit"],"rows":[["**1** Raw material incurred (purchased)","Raw Material Control A/c **or** Stores Ledger Control A/c","Cost Ledger Control A/c **or** General Ledger Adjustment A/c **or** Cost Ledger Contra A/c"],["**2** Materials returned to suppliers","Cost Ledger Control A/c **or** General Ledger Adjustment A/c","Raw Materials Control A/c **or** Stores Ledger Control A/c"],["**3** Material issued — direct","Work-in-Progress Control A/c (WIP)","Raw Materials Control A/c"],["**3** Material issued — indirect (to factory)","Production Overhead Control A/c (POCA)","Raw Materials Control A/c"],["**4** Wages incurred","Wages Control A/c (WCA)","Cost Ledger Control A/c"],["**5** Wages allocated — direct","Work-in-Progress Control A/c","Wages Control A/c"],["**5** Wages allocated — indirect (to factory)","Production Overhead Control A/c","Wages Control A/c"],["**6** Production overhead absorbed","WIP Control A/c","Production Overhead Control A/c"],["**7** Output achieved (jobs completed)","Finished Goods A/c","WIP Control A/c"]]}},{"warn":"The study text prints entry 5 as \"Debit WIP (direct); Debit Production Overhead (indirect to factory); **Credit Cost Ledger Control Account**.\" That credit is wrong. Wages were already brought into the cost ledger by entry 4 (Dr Wages Control, Cr Cost Ledger Control); entry 5 merely *allocates* that balance out of the Wages Control Account, so the credit must be to **Wages Control**. Crediting Cost Ledger Control twice would double-count the wages. The table above shows the corrected entry, and Illustration 6.1 below confirms it — its own Production Wages Control Account is credited with N99,000 to WIP and N18,000 to Production Overhead.","lbl":"Error in the study text, corrected"},{"note":"An **adjustment entry** is always made for the under- or over-absorption of overhead (see §5.7). Once WIP has been debited with the *absorbed* amount, whatever balance is left on the Production Overhead Control Account is the under/over absorption, and it is written off to the costing Profit and Loss Account — debited there if under-absorbed, credited there if over-absorbed."},{"key":"A reliable way to read any of these entries: **the cost ledger only ever talks to the outside world through the Cost Ledger Contra Account.** If a transaction brings something *into* the cost ledger from outside (purchases, wages paid, overhead incurred, sales value), the contra account is the credit. If it sends something *out* (returns to suppliers, sales value transferred, a completed capital job), the contra account is the debit. Every other entry in the list is an internal transfer between two cost accounts."},{"eg":{"tag":"Study text — Illustration 6.1 (Adapted from ACCA)","t":"Completing an interlocking cost ledger, the costing profit, and the trial balance","open":true,"q":[{"p":"Below are **incomplete accounts in the cost ledger** of a manufacturing business for a period in which final accounts are to be prepared. The missing entries are labelled (i) to (ix)."},{"tacc":{"t":"Raw Materials Control Account / Stores Ledger Control A/c","drl":"Dr  (N)","crl":"Cr  (N)","dr":[["Bal. b/f",8000],["Cost ledger contra",110000],["",118000,"@tot"]],"cr":[["Work-in-Progress",90000],["Production overhead  **(i)**","?"],["Bal. c/d","?"]]}},{"tacc":{"t":"Work-in-Progress Control Account","drl":"Dr  (N)","crl":"Cr  (N)","dr":[["Bal. b/f",7000],["Raw materials control",90000],["Wages control  **(ii)**","?"],["Production OH control  **(iii)**","?"]],"cr":[["Finished goods  **(iv)**","?"],["Bal. c/d","?"]]}},{"tacc":{"t":"Finished Goods Control Account","drl":"Dr  (N)","crl":"Cr  (N)","dr":[["Bal. b/f",30000],["W.I.P. control  **(iv)**","?"]],"cr":[["Cost of sales  **(v)**","?"],["Bal. c/d","?"]]}},{"tacc":{"t":"Production Overhead Control Account","drl":"Dr  (N)","crl":"Cr  (N)","dr":[["Raw materials control  **(i)**","?"],["Wages control  **(vi)**","?"],["Cost ledger contra",46000]],"cr":[["W.I.P. control  **(iii)**","?"]]}},{"tacc":{"t":"Production Wages Control Account","drl":"Dr  (N)","crl":"Cr  (N)","dr":[["Cost ledger contra",117000]],"cr":[["W.I.P. control  **(ii)**","?"],["Production overhead  **(vi)**","?"]]}},{"tacc":{"t":"Marketing and Administration Overhead Control Account","drl":"Dr  (N)","crl":"Cr  (N)","dr":[["Cost ledger contra",50000]],"cr":[["Cost of sales  **(vii)**","?"]]}},{"tacc":{"t":"Cost of Sales Account","drl":"Dr  (N)","crl":"Cr  (N)","dr":[["Finished goods control  **(v)**","?"],["Marketing & admin OH  **(vii)**","?"]],"cr":[["Profit & Loss","?"]]}},{"tacc":{"t":"Sales Account","drl":"Dr  (N)","crl":"Cr  (N)","dr":[["Profit & Loss",300000]],"cr":[["Cost ledger contra  **(viii)**","?"]]}},{"tacc":{"t":"Cost Ledger Contra Account","drl":"Dr  (N)","crl":"Cr  (N)","dr":[["Sales  **(viii)**","?"],["Bal. c/d","?"]],"cr":[["Bal. b/f  **(ix)**","?"],["Raw materials control",110000],["Production OH control",46000],["Production wages control",117000],["Marketing & admin OH",50000]]}},{"p":"**You are given the following additional information:**"},{"table":{"align":"lr","head":["(a)  The closing inventory balances are:","N"],"rows":[["Raw materials control","18,000"],["Work-in-progress control","68,000"],["Finished goods control","45,000"]]}},{"ul":["**(b)** The ratio of direct wages incurred to indirect wages is **11 : 2**.","**(c)** Marketing and administration overhead is absorbed by jobs on the basis of **25% of the cost of goods sold**.","**(d)** Production overhead is absorbed on the basis of **N3.50 per direct labour hour**. During the period, total direct labour hours worked were **22,000**."]},{"p":"**Required:**"},{"ol":["Identify the transactions indicated by the accounting entries numbered (i) to (viii).","Complete the cost accounts for the period.","Compute the cost accounting profit for the period.","Prepare a trial balance as at the end of the period."]},{"warn":"Four defects in the study text's own printing of this question have been corrected above, each verified against the text's own solution. **(1)** The Production Overhead Control Account is printed with \"Cost Ledger Control N117,000\" and the Production Wages Control Account with \"Cost Ledger contra N46,000\" — the two figures are **swapped**; the solution uses N46,000 in production overhead and N117,000 in production wages. **(2)** The Raw Materials Control Account is printed with \"Work-in-progress 9,000\"; it is **N90,000** (a dropped zero — otherwise the account cannot total N118,000). **(3)** In the Cost Ledger Contra Account the labels are printed one row out of step with the figures. **(4)** The \"Required\" says entries \"(i) to (vi)\" while the solution identifies **eight** of them, (i) to (viii), and a ninth, (ix), in the workings.","lbl":"Source errors corrected"}],"a":[{"h4":"(a)  Identifying the transactions"},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Entry","The transaction it represents"],"rows":[["**(i)**","Issue of **indirect materials** to production"],["**(ii)**","**Direct wages** allocated"],["**(iii)**","**Production overhead absorbed**"],["**(iv)**","**Finished goods produced** during the period, transferred from the WIP Control Account"],["**(v)**","**Cost of goods sold** during the period"],["**(vi)**","**Indirect wages** allocated"],["**(vii)**","**Marketing and administration overhead absorbed**"],["**(viii)**","**Value of sales** during the period"],["**(ix)**","*(from the workings)* **Opening balance of the Cost Ledger Contra Account**"]]}},{"h4":"Workings — every missing figure, derived"},{"p":"**(a) Splitting the production wages (entries ii and vi).** Total production wages incurred = N117,000 (the debit already shown in the Production Wages Control Account), and the ratio of direct to indirect wages is 11 : 2, so there are $11+2=13$ parts in total:"},{"tex":"\\text{Direct wages (ii)} \\;=\\; \\frac{11}{13} \\times 117{,}000 \\;=\\; N99{,}000"},{"tex":"\\text{Indirect wages (vi)} \\;=\\; \\frac{2}{13} \\times 117{,}000 \\;=\\; N18{,}000"},{"note":"Check: $99{,}000 + 18{,}000 = 117{,}000$ ✓ — always add the two parts back to the total before using them."},{"p":"**(b) Production overhead absorbed (entry iii).** The absorption rate is N3.50 per direct labour hour and 22,000 hours were worked:"},{"tex":"\\text{Production overhead absorbed (iii)} \\;=\\; 22{,}000 \\times 3.50 \\;=\\; N77{,}000"},{"p":"**(c) Indirect materials issued to production (entry i).** The Raw Materials Control Account must balance, so the indirect issue is whatever is left after the direct issue and the closing inventory are taken out of the materials available:"},{"stmt":{"t":"Indirect materials issued — by difference","rows":[["Total raw materials available (8,000 b/f + 110,000 purchased)","",118000],["Less:  Direct materials issued",90000,""],["          Closing inventory of raw materials",18000,108000],["**Indirect materials issued to production  (i)**","",10000,"@tt"]]}},{"p":"**(d) Value of finished goods produced (entry iv).** Everything debited to WIP, less what is still in WIP at the end:"},{"tex":"\\text{(iv)} = N(7{,}000 + 90{,}000 + 99{,}000 + 77{,}000 - 68{,}000) = N205{,}000"},{"note":"In words: opening WIP + direct materials + direct wages + overhead absorbed − closing WIP. This is the standard \"cost of goods manufactured\" identity, solved as a balancing figure on the WIP account."},{"p":"**(e) Cost of goods sold (entry v).** The same identity, one account further on:"},{"tex":"\\text{(v)} = N(30{,}000 + 205{,}000 - 45{,}000) = N190{,}000"},{"p":"**(f) Marketing and administration overhead absorbed (entry vii)** = 25% of the cost of goods sold:"},{"tex":"\\text{(vii)} = 25\\% \\times N190{,}000 = N47{,}500"},{"p":"**(g) Opening balance of the Cost Ledger Contra Account (entry ix).** It is the net sum of all the opening balances — the contra account is, by construction, the mirror of everything the cost ledger holds:"},{"table":{"align":"lrlr","head":["","Dr (N)","","Cr (N)"],"rows":[["Raw materials control","8,000","",""],["WIP control","7,000","",""],["Finished goods control","30,000","Cost Ledger Contra","45,000"],["**Total**","**45,000**","","**45,000**"]]}},{"h4":"(b)  The completed accounts"},{"tacc":{"t":"Raw Materials Control Account","drl":"Dr  (N)","crl":"Cr  (N)","dr":[["Bal. b/f",8000],["Cost Ledger Contra",110000],["",118000,"@tot"],["Bal. b/d",18000]],"cr":[["Work-in-progress",90000],["Production OH  (i)",10000],["Bal. c/d",18000],["",118000,"@tot"]]}},{"tacc":{"t":"Work-in-Progress Control Account","drl":"Dr  (N)","crl":"Cr  (N)","dr":[["Bal. b/f",7000],["Raw materials control",90000],["Wages control  (ii)",99000],["Production OH control  (iii)",77000],["",273000,"@tot"],["Bal. b/d",68000]],"cr":[["Finished goods  (iv)",205000],["Bal. c/d",68000],["",273000,"@tot"]]}},{"tacc":{"t":"Finished Goods Control Account","drl":"Dr  (N)","crl":"Cr  (N)","dr":[["Bal. b/f",30000],["W.I.P. control  (iv)",205000],["",235000,"@tot"],["Bal. b/d",45000]],"cr":[["Cost of sales  (v)",190000],["Bal. c/d",45000],["",235000,"@tot"]]}},{"tacc":{"t":"Production Overhead Control Account","drl":"Dr  (N)","crl":"Cr  (N)","dr":[["Raw materials control  (i)",10000],["Wages control  (vi)",18000],["Cost Ledger Contra",46000],["Profit & Loss (over-absorbed)",3000],["",77000,"@tot"]],"cr":[["W.I.P. control  (iii)",77000],["",77000,"@tot"]]}},{"note":"This is the one account most candidates get wrong. Production overhead **incurred** is $10{,}000 + 18{,}000 + 46{,}000 = N74{,}000$, but N77,000 was **absorbed** into WIP — so overhead has been **over-absorbed by N3,000**. The debit of N3,000 shown above is what makes the account balance, and the matching credit goes to the costing Profit and Loss Account, *increasing* profit. Do not assume that because the absorbed figure (N77,000) happens to equal the Marketing and Admin cost ledger entry, or any other figure on the page, there is no under/over absorption — always total the debits actually incurred.","lbl":"Over-absorption of N3,000 — where it comes from"},{"tacc":{"t":"Production Wages Control Account","drl":"Dr  (N)","crl":"Cr  (N)","dr":[["Cost Ledger Contra",117000],["",117000,"@tot"]],"cr":[["W.I.P. control  (ii)",99000],["Production overhead  (vi)",18000],["",117000,"@tot"]]}},{"tacc":{"t":"Marketing and Administration Overhead Account","drl":"Dr  (N)","crl":"Cr  (N)","dr":[["Cost Ledger Contra",50000],["",50000,"@tot"]],"cr":[["Cost of sales  (vii)",47500],["Profit & Loss (under-absorbed)",2500],["",50000,"@tot"]]}},{"note":"The mirror image of the production overhead account: N50,000 incurred but only N47,500 absorbed, so marketing and administration overhead is **under-absorbed by N2,500**, which is *debited* to the costing Profit and Loss Account, *reducing* profit."},{"tacc":{"t":"Cost of Sales Account","drl":"Dr  (N)","crl":"Cr  (N)","dr":[["Finished goods control  (v)",190000],["Marketing & admin OH  (vii)",47500],["",237500,"@tot"]],"cr":[["Profit & Loss",237500],["",237500,"@tot"]]}},{"tacc":{"t":"Sales Account","drl":"Dr  (N)","crl":"Cr  (N)","dr":[["Profit & Loss",300000]],"cr":[["Cost Ledger Contra  (viii)",300000]]}},{"h4":"(c)  The cost accounting profit"},{"tacc":{"t":"Costing Profit and Loss Account","drl":"Dr  (N)","crl":"Cr  (N)","dr":[["Cost of Sales",237500],["Marketing & admin OH (under-absorbed)",2500],["Net profit (to Cost Ledger Contra)",63000],["",303000,"@tot"]],"cr":[["Sales",300000],["Production OH control (over-absorbed)",3000],["",303000,"@tot"]]}},{"p":"Read as a vertical statement, the same thing:"},{"stmt":{"t":"Costing Profit and Loss Account for the period","rows":[["Sales","",300000],["Less: Cost of sales","",-237500],["**Gross profit**","",62500,"@t"],"@gap",["Add: Production overhead over-absorbed",3000,""],["Less: Marketing & administration overhead under-absorbed",-2500,500],["**Cost accounting profit**","",63000,"@tt"]]}},{"warn":"The over-absorbed production overhead of **N3,000 is a credit** in this account — it is money the jobs were charged but the factory did not actually spend, so it comes back as profit. Leave it out and the profit falls to N60,000, which is wrong. The under-absorbed N2,500 goes the other way. Two adjustments, opposite signs — check each one separately."},{"h4":"The Cost Ledger Contra Account, completed"},{"tacc":{"t":"Cost Ledger Contra Account","drl":"Dr  (N)","crl":"Cr  (N)","dr":[["Sales  (viii)",300000],["Balance c/d",131000],["",431000,"@tot"]],"cr":[["Balance b/f  (ix)",45000],["Raw materials control",110000],["Production OH control",46000],["Production wages control",117000],["Marketing & admin OH",50000],["Profit and Loss",63000],["",431000,"@tot"],["Balance b/d",131000]]}},{"note":"The study text prints the N63,000 profit on the **debit** side of this account while including it in the **credit** total. It is a credit: the costing profit is transferred *out of* the cost ledger to the financial ledger, and the contra account is the route out. Check the arithmetic either way — debits $300{,}000 + 131{,}000 = 431{,}000$; credits $45{,}000 + 110{,}000 + 46{,}000 + 117{,}000 + 50{,}000 + 63{,}000 = 431{,}000$.","lbl":"Another source error, corrected"},{"h4":"(d)  Trial balance as at the end of the period"},{"stmt":{"t":"Trial Balance at the end of the period","rows":[["","Dr (N)","Cr (N)","@hd"],["Raw materials control",18000,""],["Work-in-progress control",68000,""],["Finished goods control",45000,""],["Cost Ledger Contra","",131000],["",131000,131000,"@tt"]]}},{"key":"Notice what the trial balance of an interlocking cost ledger always looks like: the three inventory control balances on the debit side, and the Cost Ledger Contra Account — equal to their sum — on the credit side. $18{,}000 + 68{,}000 + 45{,}000 = N131{,}000$. If your contra balance does not equal the sum of the closing inventories, something earlier in the question has gone wrong, and this is the fastest place to catch it."}]}}]},{"n":"6.5","t":"Reconciliation of costing and financial accounting profits","b":[{"p":"The profit disclosed by the Costing Profit and Loss Account may differ from that shown in the financial Profit and Loss Account where an interlocking system is adopted, and it is then necessary to reconcile them. The difference may be due to the following reasons, which the study text groups under three headings."},{"h4":"6.5.1  Appropriations of profit not dealt with in the cost accounts"},{"ol":["**Corporate tax.**","**Transfers to general reserve** or any other fund of accumulated profits — e.g. a dividend equalisation reserve.","**Charitable donations**, where no direct benefit is derived from them by the company's employees.","**Dividends paid** on the share capital of the company.","**Additional provisions** for depreciation of buildings, plant, etc., and for bad debts.","**Amounts written off** goodwill, preliminary expenses, underwriting commission, debenture discounts, expenses of capital issues, etc.","**Appropriations to sinking funds** for the purpose of providing for the repayment of loans or debentures."]},{"note":"The unifying idea: these are all **appropriations** — distributions or set-asides *of* profit, made after the profit has been earned. Cost accounting stops at the point profit is earned, so none of these ever appear in it."},{"h4":"6.5.2  Financial matters outside the scope of the cost accounts"},{"ol":["**Interest received** on bank deposits and investments.","**Rents receivable.** If, however, rents are received from a portion of the business premises which has been sublet, only the *profit* on the rental is excluded from the cost records; the proportion which represents recovery of rent paid is deducted from the total rent to arrive at the **net cost of the premises**, and that net cost *is* included in the cost records.","**Profits or losses on sales** of investments, buildings and other fixed assets; and capital expenditure charged specifically to revenue.","**Interest on bank loans, mortgages, debentures** and other borrowed money, if interest on capital is ignored in the cost records.","**Damages payable** according to law.","**Penalties** payable for late completion of contracts.","**Losses due to scrapping machinery** before the date estimated when depreciation and obsolescence were provided for."]},{"note":"The unifying idea here is different: these items are genuine income or expense of the period, but they have **nothing to do with making the product**. Cost accounting measures the cost of production, so it leaves them out."},{"h4":"6.5.3  Items whose costing treatment differs from the financial treatment"},{"ol":["**Under and over recoveries of overhead** — arising from deliberate omission from the cost accounts (e.g. selling costs), from approximation in the basis of recovery, or from fluctuations in output.","**Differences in the valuation of inventory and work-in-progress.** Inventories are frequently valued in the cost accounts at a figure which includes an appropriate allotment of overhead — perfectly justifiable from the point of view of ascertaining the profit on manufacture. It may be thought prudent, when preparing the financial accounts, to value inventories at **prime cost only**.","**Depreciation.** In the cost accounts it may be charged on the basis of production units or hours; in the financial accounts it is usually provided for as a charge for the period, irrespective of the actual level of output.","**Abnormal losses** in production and storage, and **abnormal idle time**. In the financial accounts these losses are \"merged\" into the appropriate accounts — the sales figure is exclusive of finished products wasted, and the debits for materials and wages are inclusive of any abnormal losses of material or time. In the cost accounts, such abnormal losses may be ignored as outside the scope of manufacturing costs."]},{"key":"Group 3 is the one that produces **two-sided** adjustments and therefore most of the marks. Groups 1 and 2 are items present in one set of books and absent from the other; group 3 items are present in **both** sets, but measured differently. For a group 3 item you must work out not only *that* there is a difference but which direction it pushes profit."},{"h4":"How inventory differences move profit — the rule to memorise"},{"p":"Profit = Sales − Cost of sales, and Cost of sales = Opening inventory + purchases or production − Closing inventory. So:"},{"table":{"align":"lll","head":["If, in the cost accounts…","then cost of sales is…","so costing profit is…"],"rows":[["**opening** inventory is **higher** than in the financial accounts","higher","**lower**"],["**opening** inventory is **lower**","lower","**higher**"],["**closing** inventory is **higher**","lower","**higher**"],["**closing** inventory is **lower**","higher","**lower**"]]}},{"note":"Work each of the six inventory figures (raw materials, WIP and finished goods, opening and closing) separately through this table. Trying to net them off in your head is the single commonest source of lost marks in this topic."},{"p":"The reconciliation of financial accounts and cost accounts is carried out using a **memorandum reconciliation account**, in order to show why the respective profit figures of the two accounts are different."},{"def":{"t":"Memorandum reconciliation account","d":"a statement in T-account form, outside the double entry (hence \"memorandum\"), which starts from one profit figure and lists every cause of difference until the other profit figure is reached. Both sides must come to the same total."}},{"eg":{"tag":"Study text — Illustration 6.2","t":"NUASA Limited — a memorandum reconciliation account","open":true,"q":[{"p":"NUASA Limited has **separate financial and cost accounting systems**. Extracts from both sets of accounts for the year ended 31 December 2021 were as follows."},{"p":"**(a) Financial Accounts**"},{"stmt":{"t":"Financial accounts — extracts","rows":[["Inventory valuations:","Opening (N)","Closing (N)","@hd"],["Raw materials",34000,36000],["Work-in-progress",7000,5000],["Finished goods",16000,20500],"@gap",["","N","","@hd"],["Debenture interest",5000,""],["Interest received",750,""],["Discounts allowed",2000,""],["Discounts received",1000,""],["Net profit (before taxation)",17500,"","@b"]]}},{"p":"**(b) Cost Accounts**"},{"stmt":{"t":"Cost accounts — inventory valuations","rows":[["","Opening (N)","Closing (N)","@hd"],["Raw materials",33000,34500],["Work-in-progress",8000,7000],["Finished goods",17000,20000]]}},{"p":"**Required:** Reconcile the profit as reported in the financial accounts with the cost accounts, and calculate the cost accounting profit for the period."},{"warn":"In the study text the cost-accounts inventory table is printed with its row labels one line out of step with its figures (the \"Raw materials\" row has no figures at all, and the three pairs 33,000/34,500, 8,000/7,000 and 17,000/20,500 sit against the wrong captions). The table above restores the alignment. The closing finished-goods figure has also been corrected from the printed 20,500 to **20,000** — the text's own reconciliation includes a N500 finished-goods closing difference, which is only possible if the cost figure is N20,000 against the financial N20,500.","lbl":"Source table realigned"}],"a":[{"h4":"Step 1 — list every cause of difference"},{"p":"Take the items present in one set of books only first. Debenture interest and discounts allowed are **expenses charged in the financial accounts only** (§6.5.2); discounts received and interest received are **income credited in the financial accounts only**."},{"p":"Then take the six inventory valuations, one at a time, through the rule in §6.5:"},{"table":{"align":"lrrll","head":["Inventory","Financial (N)","Cost (N)","Difference","Effect on **costing** profit"],"rows":[["Raw materials — opening","34,000","33,000","cost is 1,000 **lower**","cost of sales lower → profit **higher** by 1,000"],["Raw materials — closing","36,000","34,500","cost is 1,500 **lower**","cost of sales higher → profit **lower** by 1,500"],["Work-in-progress — opening","7,000","8,000","cost is 1,000 **higher**","cost of sales higher → profit **lower** by 1,000"],["Work-in-progress — closing","5,000","7,000","cost is 2,000 **higher**","cost of sales lower → profit **higher** by 2,000"],["Finished goods — opening","16,000","17,000","cost is 1,000 **higher**","cost of sales higher → profit **lower** by 1,000"],["Finished goods — closing","20,500","20,000","cost is 500 **lower**","cost of sales higher → profit **lower** by 500"]]}},{"h4":"Step 2 — build the memorandum reconciliation account"},{"p":"The account is laid out as a T-account starting from the **financial** profit on the debit side. Anything that made costing profit *higher* than financial profit goes on the **debit** side alongside it; anything that made costing profit *lower* goes on the **credit** side with the costing profit itself."},{"tacc":{"t":"Memorandum Reconciliation Account","drl":"Dr  (N)","crl":"Cr  (N)","dr":[["Profit as per financial a/cs",17500],["Debenture interest",5000],["Discounts allowed",2000],["Raw materials — opening (diff.)",1000],["W.I.P. — closing (diff.)",2000],["",27500,"@tot"]],"cr":[["Profit as per cost a/cs *(balancing figure)*",21750],["Discounts received",1000],["Interest received",750],["Raw materials — closing (diff.)",1500],["W.I.P. — opening (diff.)",1000],["Finished goods — opening (diff.)",1000],["Finished goods — closing (diff.)",500],["",27500,"@tot"]]}},{"h4":"Step 3 — read off the answer"},{"p":"The debit side totals N27,500 without any contribution from the costing profit. The credit side's known items total $1{,}000 + 750 + 1{,}500 + 1{,}000 + 1{,}000 + 500 = N5{,}750$, so the figure needed to bring that side to N27,500 is:"},{"tex":"\\text{Cost accounting profit} = 27{,}500 - 5{,}750 = N21{,}750"},{"p":"Equivalently, in vertical form — which is what most examiners now prefer, and which is far harder to get the signs wrong in:"},{"stmt":{"t":"Reconciliation statement — financial profit to costing profit","rows":[["Net profit as per **financial** accounts","",17500],"@gap",["Add back: charges made in the financial accounts only","","","@hd"],["Debenture interest",5000,""],["Discounts allowed",2000,""],["Raw materials opening — valued lower in cost accounts",1000,""],["Work-in-progress closing — valued higher in cost accounts",2000,10000],"@gap",["Deduct: credits in the financial accounts only, and the rest","","","@hd"],["Discounts received",1000,""],["Interest received",750,""],["Raw materials closing — valued lower in cost accounts",1500,""],["Work-in-progress opening — valued higher in cost accounts",1000,""],["Finished goods opening — valued higher in cost accounts",1000,""],["Finished goods closing — valued lower in cost accounts",500,-5750],["**Profit as per cost accounts**","",21750,"@tt"]]}},{"key":"N21,750 is simultaneously **the difference between the two sides of the account** and **the cost accounting profit** — those are the same thing, because the memorandum account is constructed precisely so that the unknown profit is the balancing figure. $17{,}500 + 10{,}000 - 5{,}750 = 21{,}750$."},{"note":"The study text prints \"W.I.P closing\" twice in this account — once on each side. The credit-side entry is the **W.I.P. opening** difference (financial N7,000 vs cost N8,000 = N1,000); the debit-side entry is the W.I.P. closing difference (financial N5,000 vs cost N7,000 = N2,000). Corrected above.","lbl":"Source label error, corrected"}]}}]},{"n":"6.6","t":"Integrated accounting system","b":[{"p":"With this accounting system, the concept of **separate** profit and loss accounts for financial and cost purposes is discarded, in favour of a **unified set of accounts** which serves both financial and management purposes. Such a system is referred to as an **\"integrated\"** or **\"integral\"** system."},{"def":{"t":"Integrated accounts (CIMA Terminology)","d":"a set of accounting records that integrates both financial and cost accounts using a common input and data for all accounting purposes."}},{"h4":"The five design principles"},{"ol":["**The degree of integration must be determined.** Some undertakings find it satisfactory merely to integrate up to the stage of **prime cost**, leaving overhead allotment to be dealt with in memorandum form only.","**The normal classification of expenditure by nature is discarded**, to the extent the chosen degree of integration requires. In some systems a *single* control account is opened, into which all expenditure and revenue subject to costing treatment is posted — operating in the same way as a debtors' control account, i.e. containing a summary of transactions posted to the cost ledger in detail. It is, however, more usual for **separate control accounts** to be opened in the formal records, in the same way as control accounts were opened within the cost ledger for stores, work-in-progress, overhead and so on under a separate system.","**Full details of items posted to the control accounts are supplied to the cost department** at conventional intervals, accompanied by **prelists or registers** of amounts to ensure completeness.","**The cost department then deals with the information** following the system of costing in force, with two objectives in mind: *(i)* to provide the necessary costing data; and *(ii)* to form the basis for journal entries, so that the control accounts can be cleared to suitable revenue accounts, culminating in a profit and loss account.","**The amount of detail recorded in the ledger itself is usually kept to a minimum**, with full information regarding each department or process contained in **subsidiary ledgers** (based, of course, upon exactly the same data). Postings to subsidiary ledgers are sometimes referred to as **\"third entries\"**, to emphasise that they are not part of the double entry of the accounting system."]},{"p":"Applying these principles facilitates the preparation of **operating statements**, in whatever form is required, from data which are subject to routine accounting control. The conventional analysis of expenditure required for statutory purposes and external reporting can be accommodated by modifying the classification procedures within the subsidiary ledgers."},{"h4":"Advantage and problem"},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["The advantage","The problem"],"rows":[["The integrated system **saves administrative effort**, since only one set of accounts is kept instead of two separate systems of cost and financial accounting. The possible **confusion arising from having two sets of accounts is eliminated**, as is the need for reconciliation.","One set of accounts is expected to fulfil **two different purposes**: *(i)* stewardship of the business and external reporting; and *(ii)* provision of information to aid management decisions and control of operations. At times these purposes **conflict** — e.g. the valuation of inventories must conform to International Accounting Standards, whereas the cost accountant might prefer to value closing inventories at, say, marginal cost or replacement cost."]]}},{"h4":"Entries for the integrated system"},{"table":{"align":"lll","head":["#  Transaction","Debit","Credit"],"rows":[["**1** Materials purchased","Raw Materials / Stores Ledger Control A/c","Bank / Creditors for Materials"],["**2** Materials returned to suppliers","Creditors for Materials","Raw Materials Control A/c"],["**3** Materials issued — direct","Work-in-Progress Control A/c","Raw Materials Control A/c"],["**3** Materials issued — indirect (factory)","Production Overhead Control A/c","Raw Materials Control A/c"],["**4** Wages incurred","Wages Control A/c (WCA)","Wages Payable A/c"],["**5** Wages allocated — direct","Work-in-Progress Control A/c","Wages Control A/c"],["**5** Wages allocated — indirect","Production Overhead Control A/c","Wages Control A/c"],["**6** Production overhead incurred","Production Overhead Control A/c (POCA)","Bank / Creditors for Overheads"],["**7** Absorption of overhead","WIP Control A/c","Production Overhead Control A/c"],["**8** Output achieved","Finished Goods A/c","Work-in-Progress A/c"]]}},{"note":"Entry 7 again needs the **adjustment for under/over absorption** — the residual balance on the Production Overhead Control Account goes to the (single) Profit and Loss Account."},{"warn":"The study text prints entry 5 as \"Debit Work-In-Progress Control Account; Credit Production Overhead Control Account; Credit Wages Control Account.\" The middle line is a misprint for a second **debit** — Production Overhead Control is *debited* with the indirect wages, not credited. Corrected in the table above.","lbl":"Source error, corrected"},{"key":"Compare this table against §6.4's. The **entries are almost identical** — the difference is entirely in the credits to the outside world. The interlocking system routes every external transaction through the **Cost Ledger Control / Contra Account**, because the cost ledger cannot see real supplier, bank or wages-payable accounts. The integrated system posts to those **real accounts directly**, because there is only one ledger and it contains them. Learn one table and the other is a two-column substitution."},{"eg":{"tag":"Study text — Illustration 6.3","t":"Gwammaja Limited — a complete integrated-accounts month, from control accounts to the balance sheet","open":true,"q":[{"p":"Gwammaja Limited manufactures a range of products which are sold through a network of wholesalers and dealers. A set of **integrated accounts** is kept, and for the year 2021 the following information is relevant."},{"ul":["**(a)** Production overhead is absorbed into the cost of products on the basis of a budgeted rate of **80% of direct labour cost**.","**(b)** Finished inventories are valued at **factory cost**."]},{"stmt":{"t":"(c)  Balances","rows":[["","31 March 2021 (N)","30 April 2021 (N)","@hd"],["Raw materials inventory",8600,7580],["Work-in-progress",2800,2375],["Finished goods inventory",5250,6045],["Debtors for goods sold",4600,5570],["Creditors for raw materials",3800,4710],["Fixed assets at net book value",3000,3000]]}},{"stmt":{"t":"(d)  Bank transactions for April 2021","rows":[["Bank balance at 31 March","",750],["Receipts from debtors","",13780],"@gap",["Payments made:","","","@hd"],["Direct labour",3200,""],["Creditors for raw materials",4480,""],["Production overhead",2700,""],["Administration overhead",350,""],["Selling & distribution overhead",1150,""]]}},{"p":"**Required:**"},{"ol":["Use the information provided above to write up the following control accounts: (i) Raw materials inventory; (ii) Work-in-Progress; (iii) Finished Goods; (iv) Production Overhead.","Prepare the following statements: (i) a Profit and Loss Account for April 2021; (ii) a Balance Sheet as at 30 April 2021."]}],"a":[{"key":"The whole question is one technique applied six times: **every missing figure is a balancing figure on a T-account.** Nothing here is given directly — not purchases, not materials issued, not cost of sales, not even sales. Work the accounts in the right order and each one hands you the figure the next one needs. The order is: Creditors → Raw Materials → WIP → Finished Goods → Debtors → Cash."},{"h4":"Working 1 — Creditors for raw materials, to find purchases"},{"tacc":{"t":"W1  Creditors for Raw Materials","drl":"Dr  (N)","crl":"Cr  (N)","dr":[["Cash",4480],["Balance c/d",4710],["",9190,"@tot"]],"cr":[["Opening balance",3800],["Raw material purchases *(balancing figure)*",5390],["",9190,"@tot"]]}},{"note":"The study text prints this account's totals as **9,199** on both sides. They are **N9,190** ($4{,}480 + 4{,}710$, and $3{,}800 + 5{,}390$). The purchases figure of N5,390 that the text then uses is correct, so only the total is mistyped.","lbl":"Source arithmetic error, corrected"},{"h4":"Working 2 — Production overhead absorbed"},{"tex":"\\text{Overhead absorbed} = 80\\% \\times \\text{direct labour cost} = 0.80 \\times 3{,}200 = N2{,}560"},{"h4":"(a)(i)  Raw Materials Inventory Control Account"},{"tacc":{"t":"Raw Materials Inventory","drl":"Dr  (N)","crl":"Cr  (N)","dr":[["Opening balance",8600],["Creditors (W1) — purchases",5390],["",13990,"@tot"],["Balance b/d",7580]],"cr":[["Work-in-progress *(balancing figure)*",6410],["Balance c/d",7580],["",13990,"@tot"]]}},{"h4":"(a)(ii)  Work-in-Progress Control Account"},{"tacc":{"t":"Work-in-Progress","drl":"Dr  (N)","crl":"Cr  (N)","dr":[["Opening balance",2800],["Raw materials inventory",6410],["Direct wages",3200],["Production OH (W2)",2560],["",14970,"@tot"],["Balance b/d",2375]],"cr":[["Finished goods *(balancing figure)*",12595],["Balance c/d",2375],["",14970,"@tot"]]}},{"h4":"(a)(iii)  Finished Goods Control Account"},{"tacc":{"t":"Finished Goods","drl":"Dr  (N)","crl":"Cr  (N)","dr":[["Opening balance",5250],["Work-in-progress",12595],["",17845,"@tot"],["Balance b/d",6045]],"cr":[["P&L A/c — cost of sales *(balancing figure)*",11800],["Balance c/d",6045],["",17845,"@tot"]]}},{"h4":"(a)(iv)  Production Overhead Control Account"},{"tacc":{"t":"Production Overhead","drl":"Dr  (N)","crl":"Cr  (N)","dr":[["Cash",2700],["",2700,"@tot"]],"cr":[["Work-in-progress (absorbed)",2560],["Profit & Loss A/c (under-absorbed)",140],["",2700,"@tot"]]}},{"note":"N2,700 was spent; only N2,560 was charged to production. The shortfall of **N140 is under-absorbed overhead**, written off as an expense in the profit and loss account."},{"h4":"Working 3 — Debtors, to find sales"},{"tacc":{"t":"W3  Debtors","drl":"Dr  (N)","crl":"Cr  (N)","dr":[["Opening balance",4600],["Sales *(balancing figure)*",14750],["",19350,"@tot"]],"cr":[["Cash",13780],["Balance c/d",5570],["",19350,"@tot"]]}},{"h4":"Working 4 — Cash, to find the closing bank balance"},{"tacc":{"t":"W4  Cash","drl":"Dr  (N)","crl":"Cr  (N)","dr":[["Opening balance",750],["Debtors",13780],["",14530,"@tot"]],"cr":[["Creditors for raw materials",4480],["Direct labour",3200],["Production overhead",2700],["Administration overhead",350],["Selling & distribution OH",1150],["Balance c/d",2650],["",14530,"@tot"]]}},{"h4":"(b)(i)  Income Statement for April 2021"},{"stmt":{"t":"Gwammaja Limited — Profit and Loss Account for April 2021","rows":[["Sales (W3)","",14750],["Production cost of sales","",11800],["**Gross profit**","",2950,"@t"],"@gap",["Less:  Administration overhead",350,""],["          Selling & distribution overhead",1150,""],["          Under-absorbed production overhead",140,1640],["**Profit for the month**","",1310,"@tt"]]}},{"h4":"(b)(ii)  Balance Sheet as at 30 April 2021"},{"stmt":{"t":"Gwammaja Limited — Position Statement as at 30 April 2021","rows":[["Fixed assets (net book value)","",3000],"@gap",["Current assets:","","","@hd"],["Raw materials",7580,""],["Work-in-progress",2375,""],["Finished goods",6045,""],["Debtors",5570,""],["Cash (W4)",2650,24220],["Less: Current liabilities — creditors for raw materials",4710,""],["**Net current assets**","",19510,"@t"],["**Total net assets**","",22510,"@tt"],"@gap",["Financed by:","","","@hd"],["Long-term capital *(balancing figure)*","",21200],["Profit for the month","",1310],["","",22510,"@tt"]]}},{"note":"The study text's printed balance sheet omits the N4,710 creditors figure on the \"Less: Current Liabilities\" line (leaving only the N19,510 net result), and prints the capital line as \"Long-term capital N21,200 (balancing figure) + 1,310)\". Restored above: $24{,}220 - 4{,}710 = N19{,}510$, and $22{,}510 - 1{,}310 = N21{,}200$.","lbl":"Source presentation errors, fixed"},{"key":"Every \"balancing figure\" in this question is found the same way: *opening balance + known debits − known credits = closing balance*, rearranged to solve for the one missing entry. That is the real skill being tested — not arithmetic, but knowing which account to attack first so that each unknown becomes knowable in turn."}]}}]},{"n":"6.7","t":"Accounts classification and codes","b":[{"p":"In an organisation of any size, the cost and financial data will be so numerous that details must be **summarised under suitable headings** before management can process and assimilate them for decision making. For example, many types of indirect material are used in a factory; in reporting consumption, typical headings under which the data are summarised would include:"},{"ol":["Cleaning materials","Lubrication","Fuel","Protective clothing","Stationery","Small tools","Packing material","Foodstuffs","Replacement parts","Miscellaneous"]},{"p":"By using such groups, the **number of accounts can be reduced**, and the groups can then be used for budgeting purposes and the like. To assemble data under these headings, it is vital to use **code numbers or letters**, and to prepare an **accounts manual** containing, among other things, a list of all current codes with explanatory notes on their use."},{"key":"A good accounts manual is **comprehensive, unambiguous and well indexed**, so that invoices, requisitions, time-sheets and so on can be coded **quickly and accurately**. Those three adjectives are the standard exam answer to \"what makes a good accounts manual?\""},{"h4":"Principles of a good code structure"},{"p":"The code structure adopted can be prepared in several ways, but it is important that it:"},{"ol":["follows a **logical sequence**, i.e. all common items are pulled together;","is **flexible** enough to accommodate additions; and","is arranged in a way that **aids memory**."]},{"p":"If the system is so complex that a code in excess of **6 digits** must be used, then it is preferable to adopt a **composite code**, suitably structured — like `024-824-03`."},{"note":"Why a composite code helps: `02482403` is a single eight-digit string a clerk must read and retype without transposing a digit. `024-824-03` is three short chunks, each of which can carry its own meaning (say, division – cost centre – expense type), so the code is both easier to remember and self-checking: a clerk who knows the division is 024 will spot `031-824-03` immediately."},{"h4":"A simple accounts code — fixed asset accounts"},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Code range","Category"],"rows":[["0000 – 0099","Land"],["0100 – 0299","Buildings"],["0300 – 0399","Machine tools"],["0400 – 0499","Containers and tanks"],["0500 – 0599","Vehicles"],["0600 – 0699","Handling equipment"],["0700 – 0799","Heating plant"],["0800 – 0899","Tools and patterns"],["0900 – 0999","Miscellaneous fixed assets, patents and goodwill"]]}},{"p":"The same coding system may be followed when designing account codes for **current assets, liabilities, cost centres, expenditure and miscellaneous accounts**."},{"note":"Notice that the ranges are not all the same width — Buildings gets 200 codes while Land gets 100. That is the \"flexible enough to accommodate additions\" principle in action: you leave more room where you expect more accounts to be opened later."}]},{"n":"6.8","t":"Computer-assisted costing techniques","b":[{"p":"In today's dynamic business environment, it is doubtful whether the cost accountant can be effective without the use of computer techniques. Aside from the **volume of transactions** and the **speed of processing** required, there is the need for systems that **simplify decisions** by offering a wide range of options to users of cost accounting information."},{"h4":"(a)  Transaction processing systems"},{"p":"First among the computer-assisted costing techniques are **transaction processing systems**. These are systems designed to handle **large volumes of monotonous transactions** from various points, coordinated by a **central server** which processes and harmonises such a mass of data. Usually such systems have **set functions**, and their formats **cannot easily be amended** without the intervention of data processing experts such as systems analysts and programmers."},{"h4":"(b)  Decision support systems"},{"p":"Secondly, some systems are designed to **support decision making**. They are generally very **user-friendly and interactive**, offering a variety of tools that users can employ to perform required tasks more effectively, unlike the mass data-processing systems."},{"p":"Decision support systems have the advantages of:"},{"ol":["**guiding the manager** through a process of using available data to quickly arrive at useful decisions. They provide the support but still leave the manager to take the decisions, because they do **not offer pre-determined solutions**;","**interaction**, which enhances effective problem solving through the wide range of options provided; and","**not having a structured solution** — the manager must determine the direction of the decisions to be reached, seeing that the system guides them towards possible solutions and their implications."]},{"p":"Decision support systems include:"},{"ol":["Spreadsheets","Forecasting","Regression analysis","Non-linear and linear programming","Modelling and simulation","Statistical analysis","Financial modelling","Sensitivity and risk analysis","Activity cost management"]},{"h4":"(c)  Application packages, and the spreadsheet in particular"},{"p":"All the systems cited above come with **application packages** which are:"},{"ul":["**flexible** — customisable to the user's requirements as to transaction types, data layouts, report formats and frequency; and","**cheap to use** — they do not require the employment of specialists or systems analysts, who are expensive to maintain."]},{"p":"Most packages are designed to deal with a single specific application, but by far the most versatile and widely applied package is the **electronic spreadsheet**, and the most useful electronic spreadsheet at the disposal of cost accountants is **Microsoft Excel**. The spreadsheet is capable of storing both alphabetical and numeric data, which are amenable to easy manipulation through simple commands, arranged in a **grid of columns and rows**. With the data safely entered in the cells, a change in a single command is **automatically effected throughout the mass of data** across the worksheets that are linked together."},{"p":"Spreadsheets can be used for:"},{"ul":["monthly expenditure reports;","cash budgets;","standard cost records;","profitability reports; among others."]},{"p":"Data in Microsoft Excel is also capable of being **exported** into packages such as PowerPoint and others with capabilities for pictorial presentation, thus enhancing the use of computer systems in decision making."},{"table":{"align":"lll","head":["","Transaction processing system","Decision support system"],"rows":[["**Purpose**","Record and process routine transactions","Help a manager choose"],["**Volume**","Very high, repetitive","Low — one question at a time"],["**Format**","Fixed; needs a systems analyst to change","Flexible, user-customisable"],["**Output**","Processed records, standard reports","Options and their implications"],["**Who decides**","Nobody — it posts","The **manager**; the system only guides"]]}}]},{"n":"6.9","t":"Chapter summary","b":[{"p":"In this chapter, efforts were made to explain the **double-entry aspects of cost accounting**, and the **reconciliation** of the cost accounts with the financial accounts. It has been made clear that cost accounts and financial accounts may be organised through either **\"interlocking accounts\"** or **\"integrated accounts\"**. We have also shown how two separate sets of accounts are reconciled with the use of a **memorandum reconciliation account**. Finally, we have described how **account codes** should be used to summarise cost and financial data before the data can be processed and assimilated by management for relevant decisions."},{"p":"It is clear from this chapter that the double-entry system of bookkeeping is **not restricted to financial accounting**. The system is also very applicable to cost accounting and management accounting. It all depends on the type of organisation and its commitment to proper record keeping, transparency and accountability."},{"h3":"Section-by-section checklist"},{"ol":["**§6.1 Introduction** — bookkeeping applies to cost accounting too; business size drives record complexity; two systems, interlocking (reconcile) and integrated (no reconciliation, the more satisfactory method).","**§6.2 Interlocking system** — separate cost accounts periodically reconciled; cost records cover cost elements, cost centres, job costs and inventory; linked by a **Cost Ledger Control Account** in the financial ledger and a **Financial Ledger Control Account** in the cost ledger.","**§6.3 Cost ledger** — the principal ledger; holds control accounts (each with a subsidiary ledger), wages/sales/cost-of-sales accounts, and the **Cost Ledger Contra Account** that keeps the cost ledger self-balancing; the **WIP ledger is the focal point**.","**§6.4 Interlocking entries** — seven numbered entries from raw material incurred to output achieved, every external transaction routed through the contra account, plus an adjustment for under/over absorption.","**§6.5 Reconciliation** — three groups of cause (6.5.1 appropriations of profit; 6.5.2 financial matters outside costing's scope; 6.5.3 different costing treatment), reconciled in a memorandum account that must balance on both sides.","**§6.6 Integrated system** — CIMA definition; five design principles; saves effort and removes reconciliation, but one set of accounts must serve two conflicting purposes; eight entries, posting to real accounts instead of a contra account.","**§6.7 Accounts codes** — summarise under headings first; the accounts manual; logical, flexible, memorable; composite codes beyond 6 digits; range-based fixed-asset example.","**§6.8 Computer-assisted techniques** — transaction processing systems (high volume, fixed format) vs decision support systems (flexible, guides but does not decide); nine named DSS tools; the spreadsheet as the most versatile."]},{"h3":"Every term in this chapter"},{"ul":["**Interlocking system** — separate cost and financial ledgers, periodically reconciled.","**Integrated (integral) system** — one unified ledger, no reconciliation needed.","**Cost Ledger Control Account** — the account in the *financial* ledger representing the cost ledger.","**Financial Ledger Control Account / Cost Ledger Contra Account / General Ledger Adjustment Account** — three names for the account in the *cost* ledger that holds every posting affecting the world outside costing, keeping the cost ledger self-balancing.","**Control account** — an account in the main ledger summarising a subsidiary ledger; its entries are summary postings.","**Subsidiary ledger** — where the detail behind a control account is actually kept.","**Notional charge** — a charge not actually payable, included in the cost accounts to reflect the normal cost of running the business (e.g. notional rent on owned premises).","**Memorandum reconciliation account** — a statement outside the double entry explaining the difference between costing and financial profit; must balance on both sides.","**Third entries** — postings to a subsidiary ledger under an integrated system; not part of the formal double entry.","**Prelists / registers** — listings of amounts sent with control-account detail to the cost department to prove completeness.","**Composite code** — a code split into meaningful chunks (e.g. `024-824-03`), preferred once a single code would exceed six digits.","**Transaction processing system** — high-volume, fixed-format routine processing.","**Decision support system (DSS)** — interactive, flexible, unstructured; guides a manager to a decision without pre-determining it."]}]},{"n":"6.10","t":"Study text questions — multiple-choice and short-answer","b":[{"eg":{"tag":"Study text","t":"The ten questions, with the official answers","open":true,"q":[{"ol":["An interlocking accounting system has — (A) Various ledgers relating to one another; (B) A separate set of financial ledgers and a separate set of costing ledgers; (C) Accounting entries locked up in the ledger; (D) Different ledgers for direct expenses and indirect expenses; (E) A single set of ledgers serving a dual purpose.","A trial balance is — (A) The set of accounting records presented at court cases; (B) An account prepared to determine a company's profitability; (C) A list of account balances, both debit and credit sides balancing; (D) The balance of the cost ledger control account; (E) A statement of balances on bank accounts.","What is a notional charge? — (A) A charge on the assets of the company; (B) A government charge to be paid by the company; (C) Charges introduced to reduce tax liability; (D) Charges which, though not payable, are meant to reflect the normal costs of running the business; (E) Expected income which may be recognised in the accounts.","In reconciliation of profits disclosed by interlocking accounts, what are purely financial matters? — (A) Items involving cash transactions; (B) Balance sheet items; (C) Matters relating to the banks; (D) Salaries and wages paid to casual workers; (E) Financial matters outside the scope of production.","What is a control account? — (A) An account in the main ledger summarising the subsidiary ledger accounts; (B) The account maintained by the Financial Controller; (C) An account maintained by the Cost Controller; (D) A separate account for monitoring factory performance; (E) A secret account for monitoring factory performance.","A system where **one** set of accounts is kept for both financial and costing transactions is known as ………………………","*(The study text prints this question number with no question against it — see the note below.)*","In the accounts manual, processing of a large mass of data under different account heads is made possible through the use of ……………….","Raw materials issued to production but yet to reach the finished stage at period end are to be found in which account?","The conflict between the profit figures arrived at under the financial account and the cost account ledger is settled by way of a ……………….."]}],"a":[{"ol":["**B** — a separate set of financial ledgers and a separate set of costing ledgers, periodically reconciled. (A) is too vague to distinguish any system; (E) describes an *integrated* system, the opposite answer.","**C** — a list of account balances, both debit and credit sides balancing.","**D** — charges which, though not payable, are meant to reflect the normal costs of running the business. The classic example is **notional rent** charged on premises the business owns outright, so that the cost of the product is comparable with that of a competitor who rents.","**E** — financial matters outside the scope of production (§6.5.2). Note that (A), (B) and (C) are all true of *some* purely financial items but true of plenty of costing items too, so none of them defines the category.","**A** — an account in the main ledger summarising the subsidiary ledger accounts.","**Integrated (integral) accounting system.**","*(no question printed)*","**Accounts codes.**","**The Work-in-Progress (Control) Account** — material issued but not yet finished is, by definition, work in progress.","**A memorandum reconciliation account / statement.**"]},{"warn":"The study text prints **ten** numbered questions but only **nine** answers, and the two lists are out of step from question 7 onwards. Question 7 is printed as a bare \"….\" with no question text; the answer list then gives \"Accounts codes\" as answer 7 (which actually answers question 8), \"Work in progress account\" as answer 8 (which answers question 9) and \"Memorandum Reconciliation Statement\" as answer 9 (which answers question 10). The numbering above has been realigned so each answer sits against the question it actually answers.","lbl":"Source numbering error, realigned"}]}}]},{"n":"6.11","t":"Study text examination-type questions, worked","b":[{"p":"The study text closes the chapter with eleven examination-type questions. All eleven are reproduced below with full solutions — the discursive ones answered from the chapter, the computational ones worked out account by account."},{"eg":{"tag":"Exam type — Q1","t":"Integrated accounting and its advantages","q":[{"p":"What do you understand by the term \"Integrated Accounting\", and what advantages does it offer compared to separate systems of cost accounting and financial accounting?"}],"a":[{"h4":"What it is"},{"p":"Integrated (or integral) accounting is, in CIMA's words, **a set of accounting records that integrates both financial and cost accounts using a common input and data for all accounting purposes**. The separate costing profit and loss account is abandoned; a single unified set of accounts serves stewardship and management purposes together, and the control accounts (stores, WIP, overhead, finished goods) sit in the one ledger alongside the real accounts for bank, debtors and creditors."},{"h4":"Advantages over separate systems"},{"ol":["**Saves administrative effort** — only one set of accounts is written up instead of two, so each transaction is recorded once rather than twice.","**Eliminates duplication of data entry** and the cost of the staff needed to do it.","**No reconciliation is necessary** — there is only one profit figure, so the whole memorandum reconciliation exercise of §6.5 disappears.","**Removes the confusion** that arises from having two sets of records showing different profits for the same period, which managers outside the accounts department find hard to interpret.","**One version of the truth** — cost and financial staff work from identical figures, so disputes about which set is \"right\" cannot arise.","**Faster reporting**, since management information does not have to wait for a reconciliation to be completed."]},{"h4":"The offsetting disadvantage, which a good answer states"},{"p":"One set of accounts must fulfil **two different purposes** — stewardship and external reporting on one hand, and management decision-making and operational control on the other — and these can conflict. Inventory, for instance, must be valued to meet International Accounting Standards for the external accounts, whereas the cost accountant might prefer a marginal-cost or replacement-cost valuation for decision-making."}]}},{"eg":{"tag":"Exam type — Q2","t":"Principles to observe in codifying accounts","q":[{"p":"The growth in the volume of data handled and current technological advances in data processing equipment continue to emphasise the need for a satisfactory system of codification of accounts. In determining such a code, what principles should be observed?"}],"a":[{"ol":["**Logical sequence** — the code must group all common items together, so that related accounts fall in adjacent ranges and a reader can tell what an account is from its number alone.","**Flexibility** — enough unused numbers must be left within each range to accommodate additions, so a new account can be opened without disturbing the structure (note how the study text's own example gives Buildings 200 codes but Land only 100).","**Memorability** — the arrangement should aid memory, since clerks code invoices, requisitions and time-sheets at speed.","**Uniqueness** — one code per account and one account per code, with no ambiguity about which to use.","**Brevity, with a composite structure beyond six digits** — where a code would need more than six digits, split it into meaningful chunks such as `024-824-03` rather than running one long number.","**Supported by an accounts manual** which is comprehensive, unambiguous and well indexed, listing every current code with explanatory notes on its use.","**Comprehensiveness** — the structure must cover fixed assets, current assets, liabilities, cost centres, expenditure and miscellaneous accounts on the same pattern, not just one family of accounts."]}]}},{"eg":{"tag":"Exam type — Q3","t":"Uniform costing — overhead aspects","q":[{"p":"You have been appointed as adviser to a trade association of toy manufacturers and are asked to prepare a report on implementing uniform costing systems. List **two** aspects of overhead costing to be examined and explain how uniformity may be achieved in respect of each."}],"a":[{"note":"Uniform costing — a common costing system adopted by several firms in the same industry so their costs are comparable — is treated in the Cost Reduction and Control material rather than here. This answer uses the overhead framework of Chapter 5 to answer the question as set."},{"h4":"Aspect 1 — the basis of apportioning overhead to cost centres"},{"p":"Member firms will otherwise apportion rent on floor area, power on machine horsepower, canteen cost on head count and so on, each in its own way, so two firms with identical total overhead can report quite different departmental costs. **Uniformity is achieved** by the association agreeing and publishing a standard schedule of apportionment bases — one prescribed basis for each class of overhead — together with a standard list of cost centres, so every member apportions the same cost to the same place in the same way."},{"h4":"Aspect 2 — the overhead absorption rate: its basis and the activity level used"},{"p":"One firm may absorb on direct labour hours, another on machine hours, another as a percentage of direct wages; and one may use budgeted capacity while another uses normal or actual capacity, which changes the rate even when the basis is the same. **Uniformity is achieved** by prescribing both the absorption basis for each type of production department (e.g. machine hours in machine-intensive departments, labour hours in labour-intensive ones) and the activity level on which the predetermined rate is to be computed, plus a common treatment of the resulting under- or over-absorption."},{"h4":"Other aspects a fuller report would add"},{"ul":["a common classification of costs as **direct or indirect**, and as production, administration, or selling and distribution overhead;","a common treatment of **depreciation** — same method, same lives, same basis of charge;","a common policy on **notional charges** (notional rent, interest on capital), which otherwise make one firm's costs look structurally higher than another's."]}]}},{"eg":{"tag":"Exam type — Q4","t":"Explaining the balances on a jobbing manufacturer's cost ledger trial balance","q":[{"p":"The following trial balance results from entries in the cost ledger of a jobbing manufacturer:"},{"stmt":{"t":"Cost ledger trial balance","rows":[["","Dr (N)","Cr (N)","@hd"],["(a)  Cost Ledger Contra Account","",11590],["(b)  Stores Ledger Account",3790,""],["(c)  Work-in-Progress Account",5430,""],["(d)  Finished Goods Account",2350,""],["(e)  Factory Overhead Account",50,""],["(f)  Administration Cost Account","",30],["",11620,11620,"@tt"]]}},{"p":"**Required:** Explain what each balance represents and the transaction(s) out of which it has arisen."}],"a":[{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Balance","What it represents, and how it arose"],"rows":[["**(a) Cost Ledger Contra N11,590 Cr**","The mirror of everything the cost ledger holds — it exists solely to complete the double entry for transactions whose other side lies outside the cost ledger (purchases from suppliers, wages paid, overheads incurred, sales value, profit transferred out). Its balance must always equal the net total of all the other balances: $3{,}790 + 5{,}430 + 2{,}350 + 50 - 30 = **N11{,}590**$ ✓"],["**(b) Stores Ledger N3,790 Dr**","The **cost value of raw materials, components and spare parts physically still in store** at the period end. It arises as purchases received into store (debits) less materials issued to jobs and to the factory (credits) and any returns to suppliers."],["**(c) Work-in-Progress N5,430 Dr**","The **cost of jobs started but not yet completed** — the accumulated direct materials, direct wages and absorbed production overhead on those jobs. It arises as those three debits less the cost of jobs completed and transferred to finished goods."],["**(d) Finished Goods N2,350 Dr**","The **cost of completed jobs not yet delivered to customers**. It arises as transfers in from work-in-progress less the cost of jobs delivered, transferred to cost of sales."],["**(e) Factory Overhead N50 Dr**","A debit balance on an overhead account is **under-absorbed factory overhead** — overhead actually incurred (indirect materials, indirect wages and other factory expenses) exceeded the amount absorbed into jobs by N50. It awaits write-off as a debit to the costing profit and loss account."],["**(f) Administration Cost N30 Cr**","A credit balance is the opposite: **over-absorbed administration cost** — jobs were charged N30 more administration overhead than was actually incurred. It will be credited to the costing profit and loss account."]]}},{"key":"Two rules worth taking away from this question. First, on any overhead account a **debit balance means under-absorbed** and a **credit balance means over-absorbed** — the debits are what you spent, the credits are what you charged. Second, the **contra account always equals the net of everything else**; that identity is the quickest check on a cost-ledger answer."}]}},{"eg":{"tag":"Exam type — Q5 (ICAN, May 1995 adapted)","t":"Easy Going Limited — a month in an interlocking cost ledger","q":[{"p":"The following trial balance was extracted from the books of Easy Going Limited on 1 January 2010:"},{"stmt":{"t":"Opening trial balance, 1 January 2010","rows":[["","Dr (N)","Cr (N)","@hd"],["Raw materials",18000,""],["Work-in-progress",27000,""],["Finished goods",9000,""],["General ledger control a/c","",54000],["",54000,54000,"@tt"]]}},{"p":"The company keeps **separate** cost accounting records. Transactions for January 2010 are given below:"},{"stmt":{"t":"Transactions for January 2010","rows":[["Raw materials purchased","",59000],["Direct wages","",40000],["Indirect wages","",7200],["Administration salaries","",6800],["Selling and distribution expenses","",4400],["Administration expenses","",7500],["Production expenses","",4000],["Stores issued —  Production","",33100],["Stores issued —  Factory maintenance","",2070],["Stores issued —  Administration","",1000],["Production overhead absorbed","",14000],["Administration overhead absorbed by finished goods","",15000],["Selling and distribution overhead absorbed by sales","",11000],["Factory cost of finished goods","",78000],["Cost of finished goods sold","",80000],["Sales","",120000]]}},{"p":"**Required:** Record the transactions for the month and prepare a Costing Profit and Loss Account and a Trial Balance as at the end of the month."},{"warn":"This question is **mis-typeset in the study text**: the sixteen captions and the sixteen figures are printed in two columns that have come apart, so no figure sits beside its own caption, and one caption (\"Production overhead absorbed\") is printed twice. The layout above restores the one-to-one, in-order pairing, and reads the duplicated caption as **\"Administration overhead absorbed by finished goods\"** — the item the rest of the question plainly needs. Two independent checks support the reconstruction: administration overhead *incurred* then comes to $6{,}800 + 7{,}500 + 1{,}000 = N15{,}300$ against N15,000 absorbed (a plausible N300 under-absorption), and production overhead incurred comes to $7{,}200 + 4{,}000 + 2{,}070 = N13{,}270$ against N14,000 absorbed. Treat the figures as a well-supported reconstruction rather than as certainly what the 1995 paper printed, and work the **method**, which is what the examiner is testing.","lbl":"Source data mis-typeset — reconstruction"}],"a":[{"h4":"The ledger accounts"},{"tacc":{"t":"Stores Ledger (Raw Materials) Control Account","drl":"Dr  (N)","crl":"Cr  (N)","dr":[["Balance b/f",18000],["General ledger control (purchases)",59000],["",77000,"@tot"],["Balance b/d",40830]],"cr":[["Work-in-progress (production)",33100],["Production overhead (factory maintenance)",2070],["Administration overhead",1000],["Balance c/d",40830],["",77000,"@tot"]]}},{"tacc":{"t":"Wages Control Account","drl":"Dr  (N)","crl":"Cr  (N)","dr":[["General ledger control",47200],["",47200,"@tot"]],"cr":[["Work-in-progress (direct)",40000],["Production overhead (indirect)",7200],["",47200,"@tot"]]}},{"tacc":{"t":"Production Overhead Control Account","drl":"Dr  (N)","crl":"Cr  (N)","dr":[["Stores (factory maintenance)",2070],["Wages control (indirect)",7200],["General ledger control (production expenses)",4000],["Costing P&L (over-absorbed)",730],["",14000,"@tot"]],"cr":[["Work-in-progress (absorbed)",14000],["",14000,"@tot"]]}},{"tacc":{"t":"Administration Overhead Control Account","drl":"Dr  (N)","crl":"Cr  (N)","dr":[["General ledger control (salaries)",6800],["General ledger control (expenses)",7500],["Stores",1000],["",15300,"@tot"]],"cr":[["Finished goods (absorbed)",15000],["Costing P&L (under-absorbed)",300],["",15300,"@tot"]]}},{"tacc":{"t":"Selling & Distribution Overhead Control Account","drl":"Dr  (N)","crl":"Cr  (N)","dr":[["General ledger control",4400],["Costing P&L (over-absorbed)",6600],["",11000,"@tot"]],"cr":[["Cost of sales (absorbed)",11000],["",11000,"@tot"]]}},{"tacc":{"t":"Work-in-Progress Control Account","drl":"Dr  (N)","crl":"Cr  (N)","dr":[["Balance b/f",27000],["Stores ledger control",33100],["Wages control (direct)",40000],["Production overhead absorbed",14000],["",114100,"@tot"],["Balance b/d",36100]],"cr":[["Finished goods (factory cost)",78000],["Balance c/d",36100],["",114100,"@tot"]]}},{"tacc":{"t":"Finished Goods Control Account","drl":"Dr  (N)","crl":"Cr  (N)","dr":[["Balance b/f",9000],["Work-in-progress",78000],["Administration overhead absorbed",15000],["",102000,"@tot"],["Balance b/d",22000]],"cr":[["Cost of sales",80000],["Balance c/d",22000],["",102000,"@tot"]]}},{"tacc":{"t":"Cost of Sales Account","drl":"Dr  (N)","crl":"Cr  (N)","dr":[["Finished goods control",80000],["Selling & distribution OH absorbed",11000],["",91000,"@tot"]],"cr":[["Costing Profit and Loss",91000],["",91000,"@tot"]]}},{"tacc":{"t":"Sales Account","drl":"Dr  (N)","crl":"Cr  (N)","dr":[["Costing Profit and Loss",120000]],"cr":[["General ledger control",120000]]}},{"h4":"Costing Profit and Loss Account"},{"stmt":{"t":"Costing Profit and Loss Account for January 2010","rows":[["Sales","",120000],["Less: Cost of sales (80,000 + 11,000 S&D absorbed)","",91000],["**Profit before overhead adjustments**","",29000,"@t"],"@gap",["Add:  Production overhead over-absorbed",730,""],["          Selling & distribution overhead over-absorbed",6600,7330],["Less: Administration overhead under-absorbed","",-300],["**Costing net profit**","",36030,"@tt"]]}},{"h4":"Closing trial balance"},{"stmt":{"t":"Trial balance as at 31 January 2010","rows":[["","Dr (N)","Cr (N)","@hd"],["Raw materials (stores ledger) control",40830,""],["Work-in-progress control",36100,""],["Finished goods control",22000,""],["General ledger control a/c","",98930],["",98930,98930,"@tt"]]}},{"note":"Proof of the contra balance, the same check as in Illustration 6.1: opening N54,000 + purchases 59,000 + wages 47,200 + production expenses 4,000 + admin salaries 6,800 + admin expenses 7,500 + S&D expenses 4,400 + profit 36,030 − sales 120,000 = **N98,930**, and $40{,}830 + 36{,}100 + 22{,}000 = N98{,}930$ ✓"}]}},{"eg":{"tag":"Exam type — Q6","t":"Gwammaja PLC — reconciling cost and financial profit","q":[{"p":"**(a)** Explain how the results shown by the Cost Accounts and those shown by the Financial Accounts may be reconciled."},{"p":"**(b)** According to the Cost Accounts of Gwammaja PLC, profit for the year was **N48,390**. The Financial Accounts disclosed the following position."},{"tacc":{"t":"Manufacturing Account","drl":"Dr  (N)","crl":"Cr  (N)","dr":[["Raw materials opening inventory",1900],["Purchases (less returns)",54900],["",56800,"@tot"],["Less closing inventory",-1800],["Raw materials consumed",55000],["Direct labour",35500],["Factory overhead",21400],["",111900,"@tot"],["WIP: opening 8,400 less closing 8,900",-500],["",111400,"@tot"]],"cr":[["Transfer to finished inventory account",111400],["",111400,"@tot"]]}},{"tacc":{"t":"Finished Inventory Account","drl":"Dr  (N)","crl":"Cr  (N)","dr":[["Opening inventory",11600],["Transfer from manufacturing a/c",111400],["",123000,"@tot"]],"cr":[["Cost of sales transferred to trading a/c",110700],["Closing inventory",12300],["",123000,"@tot"]]}},{"tacc":{"t":"Trading Account","drl":"Dr  (N)","crl":"Cr  (N)","dr":[["Factory cost of sales from inventory a/c",110700],["Gross profit c/d",73800],["",184500,"@tot"]],"cr":[["Sales",184500],["",184500,"@tot"]]}},{"tacc":{"t":"Profit and Loss Account","drl":"Dr  (N)","crl":"Cr  (N)","dr":[["Administration expenses",15600],["Distribution expenses",10182],["Discounts allowed",1511],["Debenture interest",850],["Fines",500],["Losses of a non-trading nature",350],["Net profit",46944],["",75937,"@tot"]],"cr":[["Gross profit b/d",73800],["Discount received",1800],["Bank interest received",37],["Dividend received",300],["",75937,"@tot"]]}},{"p":"Inventory valuations in the **Cost Accounts** were as follows:"},{"stmt":{"t":"Cost account inventory valuations","rows":[["","Opening (N)","Closing (N)","@hd"],["Raw materials",1969,1850],["Work-in-progress",8280,8730],["Finished inventory",11396,12810]]}},{"p":"Depreciation amounting to **N6,146** was charged in the cost accounts, whereas factory overhead in the financial accounts included **N5,873** for this expense. The profit shown in the cost accounts has been arrived at **before** charging the notional rent of N1,500. You are required to present a logical statement to reconcile the two profit figures."},{"warn":"The study text prints the financial net profit as **N46,950** and the profit and loss account totals as N76,943 (debit) and N75,943 (credit). None of the three is right. The credits total $73{,}800 + 1{,}800 + 37 + 300 = **N75{,}937**$ and the listed expenses total $15{,}600 + 10{,}182 + 1{,}511 + 850 + 500 + 350 = N28{,}993$, so net profit is $75{,}937 - 28{,}993 = **N46,944**$ — six naira below the printed figure. The account above uses the corrected figures, and the reconciliation below arrives at exactly N46,944 independently, which confirms them.","lbl":"Source arithmetic error, corrected"}],"a":[{"h4":"(a)  How the two results are reconciled"},{"p":"Where an interlocking system is used the two profits are reconciled in a **memorandum reconciliation account** or a vertical reconciliation statement. One profit figure is taken as the starting point and every cause of difference is added or deducted until the other is reached. The causes fall into the three groups of §6.5: appropriations of profit dealt with only in the financial accounts; purely financial matters outside the scope of the cost accounts; and items given a **different treatment** in the two sets of books — over/under absorbed overhead, different inventory and WIP valuations, a different depreciation basis, abnormal losses, and notional charges made only in the cost accounts."},{"h4":"(b)  Working — classifying every difference"},{"table":{"align":"lrrl","head":["Inventory","Financial (N)","Cost (N)","Effect of moving from **cost** profit to **financial** profit"],"rows":[["Raw materials — opening","1,900","1,969","cost charged 69 more → **add 69**"],["Raw materials — closing","1,800","1,850","cost credited 50 more → **deduct 50**"],["Work-in-progress — opening","8,400","8,280","cost charged 120 less → **deduct 120**"],["Work-in-progress — closing","8,900","8,730","cost credited 170 less → **add 170**"],["Finished inventory — opening","11,600","11,396","cost charged 204 less → **deduct 204**"],["Finished inventory — closing","12,300","12,810","cost credited 510 more → **deduct 510**"]]}},{"p":"Depreciation: the cost accounts charged N6,146 against the financial accounts' N5,873, i.e. **N273 more**, so costing profit is N273 lower and N273 must be added back."},{"h4":"(b)  Reconciliation statement"},{"stmt":{"t":"Gwammaja PLC — reconciliation of costing profit with financial profit","rows":[["Profit as per **cost accounts**","",48390],"@gap",["Add:  income credited in the financial accounts only —","","","@hd"],["Discount received",1800,""],["Bank interest received",37,""],["Dividend received",300,""],["Raw materials opening — overvalued in cost accounts",69,""],["Work-in-progress closing — undervalued in cost accounts",170,""],["Depreciation overcharged in cost accounts (6,146 − 5,873)",273,2649],"@gap",["Less: expenses charged in the financial accounts only —","","","@hd"],["Discounts allowed",1511,""],["Debenture interest",850,""],["Fines",500,""],["Losses of a non-trading nature",350,""],["Raw materials closing — overvalued in cost accounts",50,""],["Work-in-progress opening — undervalued in cost accounts",120,""],["Finished inventory opening — undervalued in cost accounts",204,""],["Finished inventory closing — overvalued in cost accounts",510,-4095],["**Profit as per financial accounts**","",46944,"@tt"]]}},{"note":"$48{,}390 + 2{,}649 - 4{,}095 = N46{,}944$ — which is exactly the net profit recomputed from the financial profit and loss account above. Two independent routes to the same figure is the strongest confirmation available that both the classification and the arithmetic are right."},{"key":"**The notional rent of N1,500 requires no adjustment here.** A notional charge appears in the cost accounts only, so it would normally have to be added back when moving to financial profit — but the question states the costing profit was arrived at *before* charging it, so it has not been charged at all and there is nothing to reverse. Had the question said \"after charging\", N1,500 would be added. This is exactly the trap the examiner built into the question: read the word **before** or **after** carefully."}]}},{"eg":{"tag":"Exam type — Q7 (ACCA, Costing, adapted)","t":"Indabawa Limited — a full interlocking cost ledger, including a capital job","q":[{"p":"Indabawa Limited, a company engaged in the manufacture of specialised marine engines, operates a historic job cost accounting system which is **not** integrated with the financial accounts. At the beginning of May 2021, the opening balances in the cost ledger were:"},{"stmt":{"t":"Opening balances, 1 May 2021","rows":[["Stores Ledger Control Account","",85400],["Work-in-Progress Control Account","",167350],["Finished Goods Control Account","",49250],["Cost Ledger Control Account","",302000]]}},{"p":"During the month, the following transactions took place:"},{"stmt":{"t":"Transactions for May 2021","rows":[["Materials:  Purchases","",42700],["                   Issues — to production","",63400],["                   Issues — to general maintenance","",1450],["                   Issues — to the construction of manufacturing equipment","",7650],["Factory wages:  Total gross wages paid","",124000]]}},{"ul":["**N12,500** of the above gross wages were incurred on the construction of manufacturing equipment, **N35,750** were indirect wages, and the balance was direct.","**Production overheads:** actual amount incurred, excluding items shown above, was **N152,350**; **N30,000** was absorbed by the manufacturing equipment under construction, and under-absorbed overheads written off at the end of the month amounted to **N7,550**.","**Royalty payments:** one of the engines produced is manufactured under licence. **N2,150** will be paid to the inventor for the month's production of that particular engine.","**Selling overheads** N22,000; **Sales** N410,000.","The company's **gross profit margin is 25% on factory cost**.","At the end of May, the inventory of work-in-progress had **increased by N12,000**. The manufacturing equipment under construction was completed within the month and transferred out of the cost ledger at the end of the month."]},{"p":"**Required:** Prepare the relevant control accounts, costing profit and loss account, and any other accounts you consider necessary to record the above transactions in the cost ledger for May 2021."},{"warn":"The study text prints the wages spent on the capital job as **N125,000**, which is more than the N124,000 of total gross wages — impossible. It is **N12,500**; with that figure the whole question closes exactly (the Cost Ledger Control balance at the month end equals the sum of the three closing inventory balances), which it cannot do on any other reading. The materials section is also printed with its captions out of step with its figures — the N63,400 issue has no caption at all; it is the issue **to production**.","lbl":"Source errors corrected"}],"a":[{"h4":"Step 1 — split the wages and the overhead before touching any account"},{"stmt":{"t":"Analysis of the N124,000 gross wages","rows":[["Capital job — construction of manufacturing equipment","",12500],["Indirect wages","",35750],["Direct wages *(balance)*","",75750],["","",124000,"@tt"]]}},{"stmt":{"t":"Analysis of production overhead","rows":[["Incurred:  indirect materials (general maintenance)",1450,""],["                 indirect wages",35750,""],["                 other production overhead",152350,189550],"@gap",["Absorbed by the capital job",30000,""],["Under-absorbed, written off to costing P&L",7550,""],["Absorbed by production (WIP) *(balance)*",152000,189550]]}},{"h4":"Step 2 — the two figures the narrative hides"},{"p":"Gross profit is 25% **on factory cost**, so sales are 125% of the factory cost of goods sold:"},{"tex":"\\text{Factory cost of sales} = \\frac{410{,}000}{1.25} = N328{,}000"},{"p":"And closing WIP is the opening balance plus the stated increase:"},{"tex":"\\text{Closing WIP} = 167{,}350 + 12{,}000 = N179{,}350"},{"h4":"Step 3 — the control accounts"},{"tacc":{"t":"Stores Ledger Control Account","drl":"Dr  (N)","crl":"Cr  (N)","dr":[["Balance b/f",85400],["Cost ledger control (purchases)",42700],["",128100,"@tot"],["Balance b/d",55600]],"cr":[["Work-in-progress (production)",63400],["Production overhead (maintenance)",1450],["Capital job — manufacturing equipment",7650],["Balance c/d",55600],["",128100,"@tot"]]}},{"tacc":{"t":"Wages Control Account","drl":"Dr  (N)","crl":"Cr  (N)","dr":[["Cost ledger control",124000],["",124000,"@tot"]],"cr":[["Work-in-progress (direct)",75750],["Production overhead (indirect)",35750],["Capital job — manufacturing equipment",12500],["",124000,"@tot"]]}},{"tacc":{"t":"Production Overhead Control Account","drl":"Dr  (N)","crl":"Cr  (N)","dr":[["Stores ledger control",1450],["Wages control",35750],["Cost ledger control (other overheads)",152350],["",189550,"@tot"]],"cr":[["Work-in-progress (absorbed)",152000],["Capital job — manufacturing equipment",30000],["Costing P&L (under-absorbed)",7550],["",189550,"@tot"]]}},{"tacc":{"t":"Work-in-Progress Control Account","drl":"Dr  (N)","crl":"Cr  (N)","dr":[["Balance b/f",167350],["Stores ledger control",63400],["Wages control (direct)",75750],["Production overhead absorbed",152000],["Cost ledger control (royalty)",2150],["",460650,"@tot"],["Balance b/d",179350]],"cr":[["Finished goods *(balancing figure)*",281300],["Balance c/d",179350],["",460650,"@tot"]]}},{"tacc":{"t":"Finished Goods Control Account","drl":"Dr  (N)","crl":"Cr  (N)","dr":[["Balance b/f",49250],["Work-in-progress",281300],["",330550,"@tot"],["Balance b/d",2550]],"cr":[["Cost of sales",328000],["Balance c/d *(balancing figure)*",2550],["",330550,"@tot"]]}},{"tacc":{"t":"Capital Job — Manufacturing Equipment Under Construction","drl":"Dr  (N)","crl":"Cr  (N)","dr":[["Stores ledger control",7650],["Wages control",12500],["Production overhead absorbed",30000],["",50150,"@tot"]],"cr":[["Cost ledger control (completed, transferred out)",50150],["",50150,"@tot"]]}},{"note":"The capital job is the part of this question that separates good answers from average ones. The equipment is being built **by the company for itself**, so its cost is collected exactly like any other job — materials, wages and absorbed overhead — but on completion it does **not** go to finished goods, because it is not for sale. It leaves the cost ledger altogether, through the Cost Ledger Control Account, to be capitalised as a fixed asset in the financial ledger.","lbl":"Why the capital job leaves the cost ledger"},{"h4":"Step 4 — the costing profit and loss account"},{"stmt":{"t":"Costing Profit and Loss Account for May 2021","rows":[["Sales","",410000],["Less: factory cost of sales","",328000],["**Gross profit** (25% of factory cost)","",82000,"@t"],"@gap",["Less: under-absorbed production overhead",7550,""],["          selling overheads",22000,29550],["**Net profit for the month**","",52450,"@tt"]]}},{"h4":"Step 5 — the Cost Ledger Control Account, and the proof"},{"tacc":{"t":"Cost Ledger Control Account","drl":"Dr  (N)","crl":"Cr  (N)","dr":[["Sales",410000],["Manufacturing equipment transferred out",50150],["Balance c/d",237500],["",697650,"@tot"]],"cr":[["Balance b/f",302000],["Stores ledger control (purchases)",42700],["Wages control",124000],["Production overhead control",152350],["Work-in-progress (royalty)",2150],["Selling overheads",22000],["Costing P&L (net profit)",52450],["",697650,"@tot"],["Balance b/d",237500]]}},{"stmt":{"t":"Trial balance at 31 May 2021","rows":[["","Dr (N)","Cr (N)","@hd"],["Stores ledger control",55600,""],["Work-in-progress control",179350,""],["Finished goods control",2550,""],["Cost ledger control","",237500],["",237500,237500,"@tt"]]}},{"key":"The trial balance closing cleanly at N237,500 on both sides is the proof that every one of the fifteen postings above is right — and it is also the proof that the capital-job wages figure must be N12,500 and not the N125,000 the book prints, because no other figure makes the ledger close."}]}},{"eg":{"tag":"Exam type — Q6.8","t":"Chiroki and Ibro Nig. Ltd — working back from financial profit to costing profit","q":[{"p":"Chiroki and Ibro Nig. Ltd maintained separate cost and financial accounts, and the costing profit for 2020 differed from that revealed in the financial accounts, which was shown as **N50,000**."},{"p":"**(i)** The following information is available:"},{"stmt":{"t":"Inventory valuations","rows":[["","Cost accounts (N)","Financial accounts (N)","@hd"],["Opening inventory of raw materials",5000,5500],["Closing inventory of raw materials",4000,5300],["Opening inventory of finished goods",12000,15000],["Closing inventory of finished goods",14000,16000]]}},{"ul":["**(ii)** The company received a dividend of **N1,000**.","**(iii)** A machine with a net book value of **N10,000** was sold during the year for **N8,000**.","**(iv)** The company charged **10% interest on its opening capital employed of N80,000** to its process costs."]},{"p":"**Required:** (i) Determine the profit figure shown in the cost accounts. (ii) Explain the advantages a business might obtain from the integration of cost and financial accounting data."}],"a":[{"h4":"(i)  Working — classify each item first"},{"ol":["**Dividend received N1,000** — income in the financial accounts only (§6.5.2), so it inflates financial profit relative to costing profit: **deduct**.","**Loss on sale of the machine** — sold for N8,000 against a net book value of N10,000, so a **loss of N2,000**, charged in the financial accounts only: **add back**.","**Notional interest on capital** — $10\\% \\times 80{,}000 = **N8{,}000**$, charged to process costs in the cost accounts only. It depresses costing profit, so: **deduct**.","**Raw materials opening** — cost N5,000 vs financial N5,500: cost charged N500 less, so costing profit is N500 higher: **add**.","**Raw materials closing** — cost N4,000 vs financial N5,300: cost credited N1,300 less, so costing profit is N1,300 lower: **deduct**.","**Finished goods opening** — cost N12,000 vs financial N15,000: cost charged N3,000 less, so costing profit is N3,000 higher: **add**.","**Finished goods closing** — cost N14,000 vs financial N16,000: cost credited N2,000 less, so costing profit is N2,000 lower: **deduct**."]},{"stmt":{"t":"Reconciliation — financial profit to costing profit","rows":[["Profit as per **financial** accounts","",50000],"@gap",["Add:  loss on sale of machine (10,000 − 8,000), financial books only",2000,""],["          raw materials opening — undervalued in cost accounts",500,""],["          finished goods opening — undervalued in cost accounts",3000,5500],"@gap",["Less: dividend received, financial books only",1000,""],["          notional interest on capital (10% × 80,000), cost books only",8000,""],["          raw materials closing — undervalued in cost accounts",1300,""],["          finished goods closing — undervalued in cost accounts",2000,-12300],["**Profit as per cost accounts**","",43200,"@tt"]]}},{"tex":"50{,}000 + 5{,}500 - 12{,}300 = N43{,}200"},{"note":"Watch the **direction of travel**. Here you are going *from* financial profit *to* costing profit, which is the reverse of Illustration 6.2 and of Q6 above, so every sign flips. The safest habit: write at the top of your working which profit you are starting from, then ask of each item \"does this make the destination figure bigger or smaller?\" rather than trying to remember a rule."},{"h4":"(ii)  Advantages of integrating cost and financial accounting data"},{"ol":["**Only one set of records** is maintained, saving the clerical labour and the staff cost of writing up two.","**No reconciliation is needed** — this entire exercise disappears, along with the time and the risk of error in it.","**A single profit figure**, so management and shareholders are never shown two different answers to the same question.","**Consistent data** — cost and financial information come from the same source entry, so they cannot drift apart.","**Faster management reporting**, because information does not wait on a reconciliation.","**Lower risk of error and fraud**, since there is one control framework rather than two partially-overlapping ones.","**Easier audit**, as the auditor traces one trail rather than two."]}]}},{"eg":{"tag":"Exam type — Q6.9 (12½ marks)","t":"Tamsat Limited — an integrated ledger for the period","q":[{"p":"You are provided with the extracted operational data of Tamsat Limited."},{"stmt":{"t":"Opening trial balance as at 1 January 2022","rows":[["","Dr (N)","Cr (N)","@hd"],["Work-in-progress",25000,""],["Stores ledger control a/c",10000,""],["Finished goods control a/c",20000,""],["Fixed assets (non-current assets)",80000,""],["Creditors for material","",30000],["Provision for depreciation on fixed assets","",10000]]}},{"stmt":{"t":"Transactions for the period ended 31 January","rows":[["Materials purchased","",70000],["Normal loss on materials","",3000],["Direct materials issued","",50000],["Indirect materials issued to factory","",6000],["Wages allocated as direct to factory",35000,""],["Wages allocated as indirect to factory",10000,""],["Total wages incurred","",45000],["Production overhead incurred","",12000],["Production overhead absorbed","",24000],["Output for the period","",100000]]}},{"p":"**Required:** (a) Using integrated accounts, produce the following ledgers: (i) stores ledger; (ii) wages control account; (iii) production overhead control account; (iv) work-in-progress control account; and (v) finished goods account. (b) Prepare the closing trial balance."},{"warn":"Two defects in the printed question. **(1)** The transaction list's captions and figures are out of step: the line \"Wages allocated as indirect to factory\" is printed **twice** (the first should read \"as **direct**\"), and the figures N35,000 and N10,000 shown against \"Total wages incurred\" and \"Production overhead incurred\" are in fact the two wage allocations — confirmed by $35{,}000 + 10{,}000 = N45{,}000$, the total wages figure. Everything below shifts up one line accordingly. **(2)** The opening trial balance does not balance: debits total N135,000 and credits only N40,000. A **capital (owner's equity) balance of N95,000** is missing from the printed credit column and has been restored below.","lbl":"Source errors corrected"}],"a":[{"h4":"Assumptions stated before starting"},{"ul":["The question gives **no cash payments**, so the purchases, wages and overheads are all taken as still owing at the period end (creditors, wages payable, creditors for overhead).","**Normal loss on materials** is written off as a period charge. (An equally defensible treatment is to debit it to production overhead, where it would then be absorbed into the cost of good output; the figures below note the effect.)","No sales are given, so the finished goods produced simply accumulate; there is no trading result for the period, only the losses below."]},{"h4":"(a)(i)  Stores Ledger Control Account"},{"tacc":{"t":"Stores Ledger Control Account","drl":"Dr  (N)","crl":"Cr  (N)","dr":[["Balance b/f",10000],["Creditors for material (purchases)",70000],["",80000,"@tot"],["Balance b/d",21000]],"cr":[["Work-in-progress (direct materials)",50000],["Production overhead (indirect materials)",6000],["Profit & Loss (normal loss)",3000],["Balance c/d",21000],["",80000,"@tot"]]}},{"h4":"(a)(ii)  Wages Control Account"},{"tacc":{"t":"Wages Control Account","drl":"Dr  (N)","crl":"Cr  (N)","dr":[["Wages payable / bank",45000],["",45000,"@tot"]],"cr":[["Work-in-progress (direct)",35000],["Production overhead (indirect)",10000],["",45000,"@tot"]]}},{"h4":"(a)(iii)  Production Overhead Control Account"},{"tacc":{"t":"Production Overhead Control Account","drl":"Dr  (N)","crl":"Cr  (N)","dr":[["Stores ledger control (indirect materials)",6000],["Wages control (indirect wages)",10000],["Creditors (overhead incurred)",12000],["",28000,"@tot"]],"cr":[["Work-in-progress (absorbed)",24000],["Profit & Loss (under-absorbed)",4000],["",28000,"@tot"]]}},{"note":"Overhead incurred $6{,}000 + 10{,}000 + 12{,}000 = N28{,}000$ against N24,000 absorbed → **N4,000 under-absorbed**, written off. Had the N3,000 normal loss been charged here instead, incurred would be N31,000 and the under-absorption N7,000 — the final loss for the period is the same either way, only its description changes."},{"h4":"(a)(iv)  Work-in-Progress Control Account"},{"tacc":{"t":"Work-in-Progress Control Account","drl":"Dr  (N)","crl":"Cr  (N)","dr":[["Balance b/f",25000],["Stores ledger control",50000],["Wages control (direct)",35000],["Production overhead absorbed",24000],["",134000,"@tot"],["Balance b/d",34000]],"cr":[["Finished goods (output for the period)",100000],["Balance c/d *(balancing figure)*",34000],["",134000,"@tot"]]}},{"h4":"(a)(v)  Finished Goods Control Account"},{"tacc":{"t":"Finished Goods Control Account","drl":"Dr  (N)","crl":"Cr  (N)","dr":[["Balance b/f",20000],["Work-in-progress (output)",100000],["",120000,"@tot"],["Balance b/d",120000]],"cr":[["Balance c/d",120000],["",120000,"@tot"]]}},{"h4":"(b)  Closing trial balance"},{"stmt":{"t":"Tamsat Limited — trial balance as at 31 January 2022","rows":[["","Dr (N)","Cr (N)","@hd"],["Stores ledger control",21000,""],["Work-in-progress control",34000,""],["Finished goods control",120000,""],["Fixed assets (non-current assets)",80000,""],["Profit & Loss — normal loss 3,000 + under-absorbed overhead 4,000",7000,""],["Creditors for material (30,000 + 70,000)","",100000],["Wages payable","",45000],["Creditors for production overhead","",12000],["Provision for depreciation on fixed assets","",10000],["Capital","",95000],["",262000,262000,"@tt"]]}},{"key":"That the trial balance closes at N262,000 both sides is itself the evidence for the N95,000 capital figure restored in the question — the printed opening trial balance is N95,000 light, and supplying it is the only way the closing balance can be struck at all. Note too what makes this an **integrated** ledger: there is no Cost Ledger Contra Account anywhere. Creditors, wages payable and fixed assets sit in the same ledger as work-in-progress."}]}},{"eg":{"tag":"Exam type — Q6.10 (12½ marks)","t":"Wusu — a full interlocking ledger for April 2021","q":[{"p":"The trial balance of Wusu on 31 March 2021 is provided below."},{"stmt":{"t":"Trial balance, 31 March 2021","rows":[["","Dr (N)","Cr (N)","@hd"],["Stores ledger control account",20000,""],["Work-in-progress control account",30000,""],["Finished goods control account",10000,""],["Cost ledger contra / general ledger adjustment account","",60000],["",60000,60000,"@tt"]]}},{"p":"The extracted details for April 2021 transactions are:"},{"stmt":{"t":"Transactions for April 2021","rows":[["Purchase of stores","",70000],["Returned to suppliers","",15000],["Normal loss in stores","",1000],["Direct materials issued to production","",50000],["Stores issued to maintenance","",5000],["Wages allocated as direct",25000,""],["Indirect factory wages",8000,""],["Total wages incurred","",33000],["Production overhead incurred","",9000],["Production overhead recovered","",22000],["Output for the period","",120000]]}},{"p":"**Required:** Prepare the following ledgers: (i) cost ledger contra account; (ii) stores/raw materials ledger control account; (iii) wages control account; (iv) production overhead control account; (v) work-in-progress control account; (vi) finished goods account; (vii) closing trial balance at 30 April 2021."},{"warn":"As in Q6.9, the printed transaction list has its captions and figures out of step from the wages line onwards. The restoration above is confirmed twice over: $25{,}000 + 8{,}000 = N33{,}000$, the stated total wages; and production overhead then comes to $5{,}000 + 8{,}000 + 9{,}000 = N22{,}000$ incurred against N22,000 recovered — an exact match, which is almost certainly what the question intended.","lbl":"Source data realigned"}],"a":[{"h4":"(ii)  Stores / Raw Materials Ledger Control Account"},{"tacc":{"t":"Stores Ledger Control Account","drl":"Dr  (N)","crl":"Cr  (N)","dr":[["Balance b/f",20000],["Cost ledger contra (purchases)",70000],["",90000,"@tot"],["Balance b/d",19000]],"cr":[["Cost ledger contra (returns to suppliers)",15000],["Costing P&L (normal loss)",1000],["Work-in-progress (direct materials)",50000],["Production overhead (maintenance)",5000],["Balance c/d",19000],["",90000,"@tot"]]}},{"h4":"(iii)  Wages Control Account"},{"tacc":{"t":"Wages Control Account","drl":"Dr  (N)","crl":"Cr  (N)","dr":[["Cost ledger contra",33000],["",33000,"@tot"]],"cr":[["Work-in-progress (direct)",25000],["Production overhead (indirect factory wages)",8000],["",33000,"@tot"]]}},{"h4":"(iv)  Production Overhead Control Account"},{"tacc":{"t":"Production Overhead Control Account","drl":"Dr  (N)","crl":"Cr  (N)","dr":[["Stores ledger control (maintenance)",5000],["Wages control (indirect)",8000],["Cost ledger contra (overhead incurred)",9000],["",22000,"@tot"]],"cr":[["Work-in-progress (recovered)",22000],["",22000,"@tot"]]}},{"note":"Incurred N22,000, recovered N22,000 — **neither under- nor over-absorbed**, so nothing goes to the profit and loss account from this account. That exact match is unusual in practice and is the strongest evidence that the realignment of the printed data above is the reading the examiner intended."},{"h4":"(v)  Work-in-Progress Control Account"},{"tacc":{"t":"Work-in-Progress Control Account","drl":"Dr  (N)","crl":"Cr  (N)","dr":[["Balance b/f",30000],["Stores ledger control",50000],["Wages control (direct)",25000],["Production overhead recovered",22000],["",127000,"@tot"],["Balance b/d",7000]],"cr":[["Finished goods (output for the period)",120000],["Balance c/d *(balancing figure)*",7000],["",127000,"@tot"]]}},{"h4":"(vi)  Finished Goods Control Account"},{"tacc":{"t":"Finished Goods Control Account","drl":"Dr  (N)","crl":"Cr  (N)","dr":[["Balance b/f",10000],["Work-in-progress (output)",120000],["",130000,"@tot"],["Balance b/d",130000]],"cr":[["Balance c/d",130000],["",130000,"@tot"]]}},{"h4":"(i)  Cost Ledger Contra (General Ledger Adjustment) Account"},{"tacc":{"t":"Cost Ledger Contra Account","drl":"Dr  (N)","crl":"Cr  (N)","dr":[["Stores ledger control (returns to suppliers)",15000],["Costing P&L (normal loss written off)",1000],["Balance c/d",156000],["",172000,"@tot"]],"cr":[["Balance b/f",60000],["Stores ledger control (purchases)",70000],["Wages control",33000],["Production overhead control",9000],["",172000,"@tot"],["Balance b/d",156000]]}},{"h4":"(vii)  Closing trial balance"},{"stmt":{"t":"Wusu — trial balance as at 30 April 2021","rows":[["","Dr (N)","Cr (N)","@hd"],["Stores ledger control",19000,""],["Work-in-progress control",7000,""],["Finished goods control",130000,""],["Cost ledger contra account","",156000],["",156000,156000,"@tt"]]}},{"key":"The same identity as always: $19{,}000 + 7{,}000 + 130{,}000 = N156{,}000$. No sales were given, so there is no trading profit — only the N1,000 normal loss, which is why it is the single item routed through the costing profit and loss account and out to the contra."}]}},{"eg":{"tag":"Exam type — Q6.11","t":"Dambat Limited — why profits differ, and a short reconciliation","q":[{"p":"**(a)** Dambat Limited is a medium-size firm that has been in existence for the past five years. The firm uses an interlocking system in its Cost Accounting Department. It has been observed that the profit disclosed in the costing department's books differs from the profit disclosed in the financial reports generated in the Financial Accounting Department."},{"ol":["Why are profits disclosed in the cost accounting department different from those of the financial accounting department?","List **four** items that are recorded in the financial books only.","List **two** items that are considered in the cost accounting books only."]},{"p":"**(b)** Given below are extracted data from both financial and costing books:"},{"stmt":{"t":"Extracted data","rows":[["Financial books:","","","@hd"],["Opening inventory of raw materials","",6000],["Closing inventory of raw materials","",7000],"@gap",["Costing books:","","","@hd"],["Opening inventory of raw materials","",5000],["Closing inventory of raw materials","",12000],"@gap",["Other details:","","","@hd"],["Interest received on investment","",2000],["Profit on sale of fixed asset (non-current asset)","",1000],["Profit disclosed in financial books","",77000],["Profit disclosed in costing books","",80000]]}},{"p":"**Required:** Reconcile the differences in the profits disclosed in both books."}],"a":[{"h4":"(a)(i)  Why the two profits differ"},{"p":"Under an interlocking system the two sets of books are kept independently, for different purposes and on different conventions, so the same period's trading can produce two different profits. The causes fall into the three groups of §6.5:"},{"ol":["**Appropriations of profit** recorded only in the financial accounts — tax, dividends, transfers to reserves, amounts written off goodwill and preliminary expenses.","**Purely financial matters** outside the scope of cost accounting — interest and rent received, profits and losses on disposals of fixed assets or investments, fines, damages, debenture interest.","**Different treatment of the same item** in the two sets of books — over- and under-absorbed overhead, different inventory and work-in-progress valuation bases, a different depreciation basis, abnormal losses, and notional charges (notional rent, interest on capital) that appear in the cost books alone."]},{"h4":"(a)(ii)  Four items recorded in the financial books only"},{"ol":["**Interest received** on bank deposits and investments.","**Dividends paid** on share capital, and **corporate tax** — appropriations of profit.","**Profit or loss on the sale** of fixed assets or investments.","**Fines, penalties and damages** payable at law, and **debenture interest**."]},{"h4":"(a)(iii)  Two items considered in the cost books only"},{"ol":["**Notional charges** — notional rent on premises the business owns outright, and notional interest charged on capital employed, included so the product cost reflects the full normal cost of operating.","**Absorbed (rather than actual) overhead**, charged to jobs at a predetermined rate — the financial accounts only ever record the actual expenditure."]},{"h4":"(b)  The reconciliation"},{"p":"First classify the two inventory differences, using the rule from §6.5:"},{"table":{"align":"lrrl","head":["Raw materials","Financial (N)","Costing (N)","Effect on **costing** profit"],"rows":[["Opening inventory","6,000","5,000","cost charged 1,000 less → costing profit **1,000 higher**"],["Closing inventory","7,000","12,000","cost credited 5,000 more → costing profit **5,000 higher**"]]}},{"stmt":{"t":"Dambat Limited — reconciliation of costing and financial profits","rows":[["Profit as per **costing** books","",80000],"@gap",["Add: items credited in the financial books only —","","","@hd"],["Interest received on investment",2000,""],["Profit on sale of fixed asset",1000,3000],"@gap",["Less: inventory valuation differences —","","","@hd"],["Opening raw materials undervalued in the costing books (6,000 − 5,000)",1000,""],["Closing raw materials overvalued in the costing books (12,000 − 7,000)",5000,-6000],["**Profit as per financial books**","",77000,"@tt"]]}},{"tex":"80{,}000 + 3{,}000 - 6{,}000 = N77{,}000 \\;\\checkmark"},{"note":"The reconciliation closes exactly on the N77,000 the question gives for the financial books, which confirms that all four adjustments have been classified and signed correctly. When a question supplies **both** profit figures, as this one does, always finish by checking that your statement actually lands on the given figure — it converts a guess into a proof."}]}}]}],"formulas":[{"lb":"Splitting a wages total on a direct : indirect ratio","tex":"\\text{Direct wages} = \\frac{d}{d+i} \\times \\text{total wages}, \\qquad \\text{Indirect wages} = \\frac{i}{d+i} \\times \\text{total wages}","nt":"Illustration 6.1: an 11 : 2 ratio on N117,000 gives N99,000 direct and N18,000 indirect. Always add the two parts back to the total as a check."},{"lb":"Production overhead absorbed","tex":"\\text{Overhead absorbed} = \\text{absorption rate} \\times \\text{actual activity}","nt":"N3.50 per direct labour hour × 22,000 hours = N77,000 (Illustration 6.1); 80% of direct labour cost × N3,200 = N2,560 (Illustration 6.3)."},{"lb":"Under- or over-absorbed overhead","tex":"\\text{Under/(over) absorbed} = \\text{overhead incurred} - \\text{overhead absorbed}","nt":"A **positive** result is under-absorption — debited to the costing P&L (an extra expense). A **negative** result is over-absorption — credited to it. On the control account itself: a debit balance is under-absorbed, a credit balance over-absorbed."},{"lb":"Cost of goods manufactured (the WIP identity)","tex":"\\text{Finished goods produced} = \\text{opening WIP} + \\text{direct materials} + \\text{direct wages} + \\text{overhead absorbed} - \\text{closing WIP}","nt":"Illustration 6.1: $7{,}000 + 90{,}000 + 99{,}000 + 77{,}000 - 68{,}000 = N205{,}000$."},{"lb":"Cost of goods sold (the finished goods identity)","tex":"\\text{Cost of goods sold} = \\text{opening finished goods} + \\text{goods produced} - \\text{closing finished goods}","nt":"Illustration 6.1: $30{,}000 + 205{,}000 - 45{,}000 = N190{,}000$."},{"lb":"The interlocking cost ledger's trial-balance identity","tex":"\\text{Cost Ledger Contra balance} = \\text{closing stores} + \\text{closing WIP} + \\text{closing finished goods}","nt":"The fastest single check on any interlocking-ledger answer. Illustration 6.1: $18{,}000 + 68{,}000 + 45{,}000 = N131{,}000$."},{"lb":"Sales from a margin stated on cost","tex":"\\text{Factory cost of sales} = \\frac{\\text{Sales}}{1 + \\text{margin on cost}}","nt":"Exam Q7: a 25% margin *on factory cost* means sales are 125% of cost, so $410{,}000 \\div 1.25 = N328{,}000$. Note this is **not** the same as a 25% margin on sales, which would give $410{,}000 \\times 0.75 = N307{,}500$."}],"focus":"Two things carry the marks in this chapter, and they are different in kind. The first is knowing the **debit/credit pair** for every transaction cold — and in particular knowing that an interlocking system routes everything external through the Cost Ledger Contra Account while an integrated system posts to real accounts directly, which is the only real difference between the two entry tables. The second is the **reconciliation**, where most marks go to correctly *classifying* why an item causes a difference (appropriation / purely financial / different treatment) and getting the direction right, not to the final number. In computational questions, almost every figure you need is a balancing figure on a T-account, so work the accounts in a sensible order and finish by checking the contra balance against the sum of the closing inventories — if those two do not agree, something is wrong and you still have time to find it.","errors":["Crediting the Raw Materials Control Account instead of the Cost Ledger Control Account when recording a materials *purchase* under the interlocking system — the double entry runs through the contra account, not straight into a real supplier account.","Assuming overhead has been fully absorbed because the absorbed figure happens to match some other number on the page. Always add up the overhead actually **incurred** (indirect materials + indirect wages + other overhead) and compare. In Illustration 6.1, N77,000 was absorbed against N74,000 incurred — a N3,000 **over**-absorption which, if missed, understates the costing profit by exactly that amount.","Getting the sign wrong on an under/over absorption in the profit and loss account. Under-absorbed = extra expense = **debit**, reducing profit. Over-absorbed = **credit**, increasing profit. Illustration 6.1 contains one of each, moving profit in opposite directions.","Netting the six inventory valuation differences off in your head during a reconciliation. Take raw materials, WIP and finished goods, opening and closing, one at a time through the \"higher opening → lower profit, higher closing → higher profit\" rule.","Losing track of which profit you are reconciling **from**. Moving financial → costing and costing → financial reverse every sign; write the direction at the head of the working before starting.","Treating dividends received, interest received, profit or loss on asset disposals, fines or debenture interest as if they belong in cost accounts — these are always purely financial matters, outside the scope of production.","Forgetting that a memorandum reconciliation account must balance to the same total on **both** sides. If it does not, an item has been misclassified, omitted, or put on the wrong side.","Reading \"25% gross profit margin **on factory cost**\" as a margin on sales. On cost means sales = 125% of cost, so divide by 1.25; on sales would mean multiply by 0.75. The two give answers N20,500 apart in Exam Q7.","Sending a self-constructed capital job (Exam Q7's manufacturing equipment) to finished goods. It is not for sale; it leaves the cost ledger through the Cost Ledger Control Account to be capitalised in the financial ledger.","Adding back a notional charge that the question says has **not** been charged. Exam Q6 states the costing profit was struck *before* the N1,500 notional rent — so there is nothing to reverse."],"quiz":{"mcq":[{"q":"Under an interlocking cost accounting system, a purchase of raw materials is recorded by debiting the Raw Materials Control Account and crediting the","o":["Creditors Account directly","Cost Ledger Control Account","Sales Account","Work-in-Progress Control Account","Finished Goods Account"],"a":1,"w":"Under interlocking accounts, every posting that touches something outside the cost ledger (like a supplier) routes through the Cost Ledger Control / Contra Account, not directly to a real external account. That is the single difference between the interlocking and integrated entry tables.","src":"Chapter 6.4","sec":"6.4"},{"q":"A Production Overhead Control Account shows total debits of N74,000 and has been credited with N77,000 absorbed into work-in-progress. The N3,000 difference is","o":["under-absorbed overhead, debited to the costing profit and loss account","over-absorbed overhead, credited to the costing profit and loss account","a posting error that must be corrected","the closing balance of work-in-progress","indirect materials not yet issued"],"a":1,"w":"More was absorbed into jobs (N77,000) than was actually incurred (N74,000), so overhead is **over**-absorbed by N3,000. It is credited to the costing P&L, increasing profit — this is exactly the adjustment in Illustration 6.1.","src":"Chapter 6.4","sec":"6.4"},{"q":"In an interlocking cost ledger, the closing balances are: stores N18,000, work-in-progress N68,000 and finished goods N45,000. The Cost Ledger Contra Account balance must be","o":["N45,000 credit","N86,000 debit","N131,000 credit","N131,000 debit","indeterminate without the profit figure"],"a":2,"w":"The contra account is the mirror of everything the cost ledger holds, so its credit balance always equals the sum of the closing inventory control balances: N18,000 + N68,000 + N45,000 = N131,000. This identity is the fastest check on any answer to this style of question.","src":"Chapter 6.4","sec":"6.4"},{"q":"Which of the following would appear in the financial accounts but never in the cost accounts?","o":["Direct materials issued to production","Dividends paid on share capital","Production overhead absorbed","Direct labour cost","Work-in-progress"],"a":1,"w":"Dividends paid on share capital are an appropriation of profit, dealt with only in the financial accounts (§6.5.1), never in the cost accounts.","src":"Chapter 6.5","sec":"6.5"},{"q":"Closing inventory is valued at N12,000 in the cost accounts and N7,000 in the financial accounts. Compared with the financial accounts, the costing profit is","o":["N5,000 lower","N5,000 higher","the same","N19,000 higher","N12,000 higher"],"a":1,"w":"A higher closing inventory means a lower cost of sales, and a lower cost of sales means a higher profit — so the cost accounts show N5,000 more profit on this item. Moving from costing profit to financial profit you would therefore deduct N5,000 (Exam Q6.11).","src":"Chapter 6.5","sec":"6.5"},{"q":"A memorandum reconciliation account is used to","o":["record every transaction of the business","replace the trial balance","explain the difference between costing profit and financial profit","calculate the overhead absorption rate","record fixed asset purchases only"],"a":2,"w":"The memorandum reconciliation account exists purely to show, item by item, why costing profit and financial profit differ — \"memorandum\" meaning it sits outside the formal double entry.","src":"Chapter 6.5","sec":"6.5"},{"q":"Under an integrated accounting system, the number of separate profit and loss accounts prepared is","o":["two — one for cost, one for financial purposes","three","one, serving both purposes","zero — no profit and loss account is prepared","four"],"a":2,"w":"The defining feature of an integrated system is that separate costing and financial P&L accounts are discarded in favour of one unified account — which is why no reconciliation is ever needed.","src":"Chapter 6.6","sec":"6.6"},{"q":"A charge that is not actually payable, but is included in cost accounts to reflect the normal cost of running the business, is called a","o":["sunk cost","notional charge","opportunity cost","committed cost","discretionary cost"],"a":1,"w":"This is the definition of a notional charge — e.g. notional rent on premises the business owns outright, or notional interest on capital employed, so that the product cost is comparable with a competitor who rents or borrows.","src":"Chapter 6.3","sec":"6.3"},{"q":"Postings made to a subsidiary ledger under an integrated accounting system, which are not part of the formal double entry, are known as","o":["contra entries","third entries","memorandum entries","suspense entries","prelist entries"],"a":1,"w":"The study text calls them **third entries**, precisely to emphasise that they sit outside the two-sided double entry of the formal records.","src":"Chapter 6.6","sec":"6.6"},{"q":"Where a single account code would need more than 6 digits, the accounts manual should instead use","o":["a purely alphabetic code","a composite code, suitably structured","no code at all — free text descriptions","a randomly generated code","the supplier's own invoice number as the code"],"a":1,"w":"A composite code such as 024-824-03 is preferred once a plain single code would exceed six digits — each chunk can carry its own meaning, which makes the code easier to remember and partly self-checking.","src":"Chapter 6.7","sec":"6.7"},{"q":"A computerised system that guides a manager toward a decision using available data, but does not itself offer a pre-determined solution, is best described as a","o":["transaction processing system","decision support system","payroll system","general ledger posting system","fixed asset register"],"a":1,"w":"A decision support system is deliberately interactive and unstructured — it guides, the manager still decides — unlike a transaction processing system, built for fixed-format, high-volume routine postings.","src":"Chapter 6.8","sec":"6.8"},{"q":"In the cost ledger, the account that acts as the focal point of a specific-order costing system, holding a separate account per job, is the","o":["Sales Account","Cost Ledger Contra Account","Work-in-Progress ledger","Finished Goods Control Account","Marketing and Admin Overhead Account"],"a":2,"w":"The study text names the WIP ledger explicitly as the focal point of the cost accounting system under specific-order costing, with a separate account for each job, each debited with materials, wages and absorbed overhead.","src":"Chapter 6.3","sec":"6.3"},{"q":"Sales are N410,000 and the gross profit margin is 25% on factory cost. The factory cost of sales is","o":["N307,500","N328,000","N512,500","N102,500","N385,000"],"a":1,"w":"A margin stated **on cost** means sales are 125% of factory cost, so the cost is 410,000 ÷ 1.25 = N328,000. (N307,500 is the trap — that is what you get by treating it as a 25% margin on *sales*.)","src":"Chapter 6.11","sec":"6.11"},{"q":"A company builds a machine for its own use. On completion, the accumulated cost of the job in an interlocking cost ledger is","o":["transferred to the Finished Goods Control Account","transferred out of the cost ledger via the Cost Ledger Control Account","written off to the costing profit and loss account","left permanently in work-in-progress","credited to the Production Overhead Control Account"],"a":1,"w":"The machine is not for sale, so it never becomes finished goods. Its cost leaves the cost ledger through the Cost Ledger Control Account and is capitalised as a fixed asset in the financial ledger (Exam Q7, N50,150).","src":"Chapter 6.11","sec":"6.11"}],"theory":[{"q":"Explain how the results shown by the Cost Accounts and those shown by the Financial Accounts may be reconciled.","marks":8,"a":[{"p":"Where interlocking accounts are used, the two profit figures can differ for three broad reasons: (i) **appropriations of profit** recorded only in the financial accounts (tax, dividends, transfers to reserves, charitable donations, additional provisions, amounts written off goodwill and preliminary expenses, sinking-fund appropriations); (ii) **purely financial matters** outside the scope of cost accounts (interest and rent received, profits or losses on disposals of fixed assets and investments, interest on borrowed money, damages, penalties, losses on premature scrapping); and (iii) items where the **costing treatment itself differs** from the financial treatment — over- and under-absorbed overhead, different inventory and WIP valuation bases, a different depreciation basis, and abnormal losses excluded from the cost accounts."},{"p":"The reconciliation is carried out using a **memorandum reconciliation account** — a T-account outside the double entry. One profit figure is entered as the starting point and every item causing a difference is added on one side or the other until the second profit figure emerges as the balancing figure, with both sides brought to the same total. It may equally be presented as a vertical statement: start from one profit, add every item that made the destination figure larger, deduct every item that made it smaller, and arrive at the other profit."}],"src":"Chapter 6.5","sec":"6.5"},{"q":"What do you understand by the term \"Integrated Accounting,\" and what advantages does it offer compared with separate systems of cost and financial accounting?","marks":10,"a":[{"p":"Integrated accounts, per CIMA, are **a set of accounting records that integrates both financial and cost accounts using a common input and data for all accounting purposes** — a single unified ledger rather than two parallel systems requiring periodic reconciliation. The separate costing profit and loss account is abandoned, and control accounts for stores, WIP, overhead and finished goods sit in the same ledger as bank, debtors and creditors."},{"p":"**Advantages**: it saves administrative effort, since only one set of accounts is kept instead of two; it eliminates duplicated data entry and the staff cost of it; it removes the need for periodic reconciliation altogether; it eliminates the confusion of two different profit figures for the same period; it gives cost and financial staff one version of the truth; and it speeds up management reporting, which no longer waits on a reconciliation."},{"p":"**The offsetting drawback**: one set of accounts must serve two purposes that can conflict — external stewardship reporting, which must follow accounting standards, and internal management decision support, where the cost accountant might prefer, for instance, a marginal-cost or replacement-cost inventory valuation."}],"src":"Chapter 6.6","sec":"6.6"},{"q":"State the principles that should be observed in determining a satisfactory system of codification of accounts, and explain why an accounts manual is needed.","marks":10,"a":[{"p":"**Principles of a good code structure**: it should follow a **logical sequence**, pulling all common items together so related accounts fall in adjacent ranges; it should be **flexible** enough to accommodate additions, leaving spare numbers within each range; it should be arranged in a way that **aids memory**, since clerks code documents at speed; each code should be **unique** to one account; and where a code would need more than **six digits**, a **composite code** such as `024-824-03` should be used instead of one long unbroken number."},{"p":"**Why an accounts manual is needed**: the codes are useless unless everyone uses the same one for the same thing. The manual lists all current codes with explanatory notes on their use, so that invoices, requisitions and time-sheets can be coded **quickly and accurately**. A good manual is **comprehensive, unambiguous and well indexed**."},{"p":"The purpose behind all of it: in an organisation of any size the volume of cost and financial data is too great to assimilate, so data must be summarised under suitable headings — e.g. indirect materials grouped as cleaning materials, lubrication, fuel, protective clothing, stationery, small tools, packing material, foodstuffs, replacement parts and miscellaneous — which reduces the number of accounts and provides ready-made groupings for budgeting."}],"src":"Chapter 6.7","sec":"6.7"},{"q":"Distinguish between a transaction processing system and a decision support system, and state the advantages of decision support systems to the cost accountant.","marks":8,"a":[{"p":"**Transaction processing systems** are designed to handle large volumes of monotonous transactions arriving from various points, coordinated by a central server that processes and harmonises the mass of data. They have **set functions**, and their formats cannot easily be amended without the intervention of data processing experts such as systems analysts and programmers."},{"p":"**Decision support systems** are designed to support decision making. They are user-friendly and interactive, offering a variety of tools users can employ to perform tasks more effectively. Their advantages are that they **guide the manager** through using available data to reach useful decisions quickly while still leaving the decision to the manager (they offer no pre-determined solution); their **interactivity** enhances problem solving through the wide range of options provided; and they deliberately have **no structured solution**, so the manager determines the direction while the system shows possible solutions and their implications."},{"p":"Examples include spreadsheets, forecasting, regression analysis, linear and non-linear programming, modelling and simulation, statistical analysis, financial modelling, sensitivity and risk analysis, and activity cost management. The **electronic spreadsheet** (Microsoft Excel) is by far the most versatile and widely applied, used for monthly expenditure reports, cash budgets, standard cost records and profitability reports, and exportable into presentation packages."}],"src":"Chapter 6.8","sec":"6.8"}]}},{"n":7,"t":"Costing Methods: Specific Order Costing","brief":"The four specific-order costing methods — job, batch, contract and service costing — used wherever output can be identified and tracked through production as a distinct unit, as opposed to the continuous-flow process costing of Chapter 8. Covers the overhead-absorption bases used in job costing (with fully verified illustrations), batch costing's set-up-cost economics, the full contract-costing profit-recognition framework across a contract's three life stages, and service (operation) costing.","outcomes":["Appreciate what order costing is and how it differs from process costing","Understand the methods of absorbing overhead by jobs, batches, contracts, services or products","Appreciate the important features of batch, service and contract costing","Account for the value of a product, service, batch or contract","Account for the total cost of an order, for effective pricing decisions"],"secs":[{"n":"7.1","t":"Order costing — the concept","b":[{"def":{"t":"Order (specific-order) costing","d":"a technique used where expenses are linked to specific products or services ordered — jobs, batches or contracts — that can be identified individually through every stage of production. Job costing, contract costing and batch costing are all specific-order costing methods."}},{"p":"Typical users: construction (bridges, roads), building (houses, offices), machine-tool manufacturing, printing (books, journals, newsletters), general engineering — anywhere a product is made to a customer's own specification — and batches of similar articles (shoes, bags, shirts). Order costing is a basic procedure that can be combined with other costing techniques."},{"p":"Its main purpose is to find the profit or loss on **each individual order** — total cost per order must be estimated as accurately as possible for the price charged to yield a reasonable profit. It lets organisations (private or public) distinguish profitable from unprofitable orders, compare against past estimates to assess productivity, and price \"cost plus\" government/other contracts (price = agreed cost + agreed target profit)."}]},{"n":"7.2","t":"Job costing","b":[{"h4":"Job order costing procedure"},{"p":"Costing a job, batch or contract means aggregating every cost element behind the final product — needing solid production-control records. Every job gets its own **order number**, cited at every stage of production. A very large order should be split into separate, numbered **batches** (e.g. order 30 → batches 30.1, 30.2, 30.3), so costing can proceed without waiting for the whole order to finish."},{"p":"Each order goes into the production-control records, and a **job cost sheet** (job ledger account) is prepared — recording material used, wages paid, and machine time taken. Departmental general overhead is apportioned so each job/batch/contract absorbs its fair share."},{"note":"The **Job Card** is supplementary to the formal cost book-keeping system — it exists independently, for up-to-date shop-floor control. In a jobbing concern (e.g. a motor garage), the job card typically lives on the shop floor, while the actual costs flow into the cost ledger through the normal source documents (Material Requisition Note, Wages Analysis Journal, Expenses Analysis Book)."},{"h4":"Overhead absorption methods in job costing"},{"p":"A job's direct material, direct wages and direct expenses are totalled, then overhead is applied by one of the arbitrary bases already met in Chapter 5 §5.5: a percentage of direct material/labour/prime cost, a cost-unit rate, or a time rate (machine-hour or direct-labour-hour). Adding production overhead gives **production cost**; adding a further share of administration/selling/distribution overhead (each job absorbing its portion, unless treated as a direct P&L charge) gives **total cost**."},{"key":"Charging a production cost centre's overhead to every unit passing through it is **absorption** — the exact same concept from Chapter 5, just applied at the level of one job rather than one whole cost centre."},{"eg":{"tag":"Study text","t":"Illustration 7.1 — percentage rate on direct wages","open":true,"q":[{"p":"Star Modern Shoemakers: annual direct wages N8,000,000, direct materials N5,000,000, direct expenses N1,500,000, production overhead N2,400,000, 160,000 units. A job of 500 pairs had direct wages N8,000, direct material N5,000, direct expenses N1,500. Fixed overhead is apportioned on % of direct wages; admin at 15% and selling at 10% of production cost. Find production cost, total cost, and cost per pair."}],"a":[{"p":"Overhead rate $= \\dfrac{2{,}400{,}000}{8{,}000{,}000} \\times 100 = 30\\%$ of direct wages. Job overhead $= 30\\% \\times 8{,}000 = N2{,}400$."},{"table":{"align":"lr","head":["","N"],"rows":[["Direct wages","8,000"],["Direct material cost","5,000"],["Direct expenses","1,500"],["**Prime cost**","**14,500**"],["Production overhead","2,400"],["**(a) Production cost**","**16,900**"],["Admin. expenses (15% × 16,900)","2,535"],["Selling expenses (10% × 16,900)","1,690"],["**(b) Total cost**","**21,125**"]]}},{"tex":"\\text{(c) Cost per pair} = \\dfrac{21{,}125}{500} = N42.25"}]}},{"eg":{"tag":"Study text","t":"Illustration 7.2 — percentage rate on direct material cost","open":true,"q":[{"p":"Same shoemaker, same job, but fixed overhead is now apportioned on % of direct material cost instead (overhead rate works out at 80% of direct material). Find production cost, total cost and cost per pair."}],"a":[{"p":"Job overhead $= 80\\% \\times 5{,}000 = N4{,}000$."},{"table":{"align":"lr","head":["","N"],"rows":[["Direct wages","8,000"],["Direct material cost","5,000"],["Direct expenses","1,500"],["**Prime cost**","**14,500**"],["Production overhead","4,000"],["**(i) Production cost**","**18,500**"],["Admin. expenses (15% × 18,500)","2,775"],["Selling expenses (10% × 18,500)","1,850"],["**(ii) Total cost**","**23,125**"]]}},{"tex":"\\text{(iii) Cost per pair} = \\dfrac{23{,}125}{500} = N46.25"},{"note":"The two illustrations use the **same job**, but changing only the overhead basis moves the cost per pair from N42.25 to N46.25 — a direct demonstration of why choosing the right absorption basis matters, not just getting the arithmetic right once a basis is chosen."}]}},{"p":"Choosing between the two: percentage-on-direct-wages suits situations where overhead is small relative to wages, wages are the major cost component, labour type/pay rates are similar, and machinery use is low. Percentage-on-direct-material-cost suits jobs where material cost dominates and overhead tends to move with material cost — also equitable where only one product type is made, so material cost per unit is uniform."}]},{"n":"7.3","t":"Batch costing","b":[{"def":{"t":"Batch costing","d":"arises where a customer orders a quantity of identical products at once — the job is a number of units of a similar product, all covered by a single job number (e.g. a print run of 1,000 copies of a book). The batch cost is divided by the total quantity to give a cost per unit."}},{"p":"Peculiar features of a batch costing system:"},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Feature","What it means"],"rows":[["**Set-up costs**","The cost of setting up production facilities — engineering time, factory consumables to set up moulds, etc."],["**Unit cost falls as batch size rises**","Because set-up and other fixed costs stay the same however large the batch is, spreading them over more units lowers the average unit cost."],["**Batches of dissimilar products**","Sometimes different products are batched together because a common operation saves set-up time — the shared cost of that joint operation must then be apportioned on an **equitable** basis."]]}},{"eg":{"tag":"Study text","t":"Illustration 7.3 — batch cost and unit cost falling as batch size rises","open":true,"q":[{"p":"Ayingba Press is asked to quote for 5,000 and 25,000 booklets, on a 10%-of-sales profit target. Costs per 1,000 copies: paper/materials N30, wages N20, layout (set-up) N500 flat, fixed overhead N200 flat, variable overhead 12% of wages. Find the batch cost, unit cost and minimum selling price for each quantity."}],"a":[{"table":{"align":"lrrr","head":["","1,000 copies (N)","5,000 copies (N)","25,000 copies (N)"],"rows":[["Set-up/layout cost (flat, per batch)","500","500","500"],["Paper (30 per 1,000)","30","150","750"],["Wages (20 per 1,000)","20","100","500"],["Fixed overhead (flat, per batch)","200","200","200"],["Variable overhead (12% of wages)","2.40","12","60"],["**Total batch cost**","**752.40**","**962**","**2,010**"],["Profit margin (10% of selling price)","83.60","107","223"],["**Selling price**","**836**","**1,069**","**2,233**"]]}},{"p":"Since profit margin is 10% of the **selling price** (not of cost), selling price $= \\text{cost} / 0.9$ — e.g. at 5,000 copies, $962/0.9 \\approx N1{,}069$."},{"tex":"\\text{Unit cost at 5,000 copies} = \\dfrac{962}{5{,}000} = 19.24 \\text{ kobo} \\quad\\quad \\text{Unit cost at 25,000 copies} = \\dfrac{2{,}010}{25{,}000} = 8.04 \\text{ kobo}"},{"key":"Notice **why** unit cost fell so much: set-up cost (N500) and fixed overhead (N200) are the **same flat N700** at every batch size — only paper, wages and variable overhead scale up with quantity. Spreading that fixed N700 over 25,000 copies instead of 1,000 is most of the unit-cost saving — exactly the general principle stated above, now seen numerically."}]}}]},{"n":"7.4","t":"Contract costing and accounting","b":[{"p":"Contract costing is a form of order costing, distinguished by:"},{"ul":["jobs of **long-term duration**, often spanning more than one accounting period;","work carried out at the **contractee's own premises** (site-based);","**sub-contractors** for specialist work (plumbing, electrical installation); and","an **architect**, engaged by the contractee, who supervises the work and issues a **valuation certificate** (\"work certified\"/architect's valuation) that payment to the contractor is based on."]},{"key":"Ascertaining cost is *not* the hard part of contract costing — a contract account is simply debited with every expense as it is incurred. The **real problem is profit**: how much profit (if any) can be recognised on a contract that has reached an advanced stage but is not yet complete? Taking too much profit too early risks recognising profit that never materialises if the contract later runs into trouble — this is a direct application of the **prudence concept**."},{"p":"Contracts are classified into three stages, each with its own profit-recognition rule:"},{"h4":"(a) Early-stage contracts"},{"p":"Not nearing completion at all — expenses debited to the contract account are simply carried forward as the cost of work-in-progress, exactly as in job costing. **No profit is recognised yet.**"},{"h4":"(b) Fairly advanced-stage contracts"},{"p":"A reliable estimate of cost-to-completion may not yet be available, but some profit can prudently be recognised on the work done so far."},{"fbox":{"h":"Profit recognition — fairly advanced stage","rows":[{"lb":"Step 1: Notional profit","tex":"\\text{Notional profit} = \\text{Value of work certified} - \\text{Cost of work certified}"},{"lb":"Step 2: Profit taken","tex":"\\text{Profit taken} = \\tfrac23 \\times \\text{Notional profit} \\times \\dfrac{\\text{Cash received}}{\\text{Value of work certified}}"}]}},{"note":"**Cost of work certified is not the same as cost to date** — some cost may have been incurred on work the architect has not yet certified, so it must be excluded from the notional-profit calculation. The $\\tfrac23$ fraction in Step 2 is the study text's own stated proportion — in practice (and in an exam), the proportion to use is whatever the question or examiner specifies, not a fixed universal rule."},{"h4":"(c) Nearing-completion contracts"},{"p":"A reliable estimate of total cost is now available, so profit is taken with reference to the contract's **total estimated profit over its whole life**, adjusted for any profit already recognised in earlier periods."},{"fbox":{"h":"Profit recognition — nearing completion","rows":[{"lb":"Step 1: Estimated Total Profit (ETP)","tex":"\\text{ETP} = \\text{Total contract price} - (\\text{Cost to date} + \\text{Cost to complete})"},{"lb":"Step 2: Profit realised to date (lower of two estimates)","tex":"\\min\\!\\left(\\dfrac{\\text{Value of work certified}}{\\text{Estimated total cost}} \\times \\text{ETP},\\ \\ \\dfrac{\\text{Cost to date}}{\\text{Estimated total cost}} \\times \\text{ETP}\\right)"},{"lb":"Step 3: Profit taken this period","tex":"\\text{Profit realised to date} - \\text{Profit already recognised in prior periods}"}]}},{"warn":"Where the contract as a whole is **expected to result in a loss**, that entire foreseeable loss must be written off **immediately**, to the maximum extent possible — not spread or deferred. This follows directly from the prudence principle: an unprofitable contract should not have been accepted in the first place, and the loss must be recognised as soon as it is foreseeable, not only when the contract finally finishes."},{"h4":"Valuing a contract in the balance sheet"},{"fbox":{"h":"Contract value receivable","rows":[{"lb":"Contract value for balance sheet","tex":"(\\text{Total cost incurred to date} + \\text{Profit taken/realised}) - \\text{Cash received from contractee}"}]}},{"p":"This is simply the value of the asset the contract has created so far, less what has already been paid for it — the amount the contractor still stands to be paid."}]},{"n":"7.5","t":"Service (operation) costing","b":[{"def":{"t":"Service costing (operation costing)","d":"a method of cost ascertainment for organisations that provide services rather than physical products — e.g. transport companies, electricity companies, hotels, hospitals, schools, cinemas."}},{"p":"Characteristics of service costing:"},{"ul":["services are **standardised** — a similar service is provided to every customer;","services are provided on an **uninterrupted, regular** basis;","a large amount of capital is tied up in fixed assets (transport vehicles, hotel buildings, hospital equipment); and","operating cost is part fixed, part variable."]},{"p":"A transport company's operation cost sheet is a representative format:"},{"table":{"align":"llr","head":["","Item",""],"rows":[["**Fixed costs (standing charges)**","Motor park dues; vehicle licensing and insurance; manager's salary; administrative charges; finance charges; drivers' salaries.","Total ÷ km = cost/km"],["**Variable (running) costs**","Depreciation; fuel and oil; spare parts, service and repairs.","Total ÷ km = cost/km"]]}},{"p":"Fixed and variable totals are aggregated to find **cost per vehicle per kilometre**."},{"note":"The study text's current edition adds a substantial discussion of **digital-economy pricing models** at this point — dynamic pricing (Amazon, Uber surge), the freemium model (Spotify, LinkedIn), subscription pricing (Netflix, Microsoft 365, Amazon Prime — flat-rate/tiered/usage-based/freemium/per-user/hybrid), price discrimination, product bundling and psychological pricing, penetration vs premium pricing by market structure, and the ethical concerns these raise (price gouging, data-driven personalisation). This material is genuinely present in the source but is **not** referenced anywhere in this chapter's own stated objectives or its end-of-chapter question bank — it reads as supplementary background on how costing/pricing concepts show up in a digital business context, not core examinable technique. Included here for completeness (full fidelity to the source), condensed accordingly."}]},{"n":"7.6","t":"Worksheet summary — definitions, formulae and structure","b":[{"p":"Everything in this chapter, compressed for a last read-through."},{"h3":"Section-by-section checklist"},{"ol":["**§7.1 Order costing** — links expense to a specific, identifiable job/batch/contract; purpose is to find profit/loss per order and support cost-plus pricing.","**§7.2 Job costing** — every job gets an order number; the Job Card (shop floor) is supplementary to the cost ledger; overhead absorbed by %-of-wages, %-of-material, %-of-prime-cost, cost-unit rate or time rate — the *same* job priced two different ways gives two different costs (Illustrations 7.1/7.2), so choosing the right basis matters.","**§7.3 Batch costing** — one job number for many identical units; set-up costs and other flat batch costs, spread over more units, drive unit cost down as batch size rises.","**§7.4 Contract costing** — long-term, site-based, may involve sub-contractors and an architect's valuation certificate; the real difficulty is profit recognition, not cost ascertainment; 3 stages (early: no profit taken; fairly advanced: notional profit × 2/3 × cash-received ratio; nearing completion: ETP-based, lower-of-two-ratios profit realised, less prior recognition); a foreseeable loss is written off immediately and in full.","**§7.5 Service costing** — standardised, continuous services with heavy fixed-asset investment; operation cost sheet splits fixed (standing) and variable (running) costs to find cost per unit of service (e.g. per km)."]},{"h3":"All the terms"},{"ul":["**Order costing** — costing a specific, identifiable job/batch/contract. **Job Card** — shop-floor record, supplementary to the cost ledger.","**Set-up cost** — the flat cost of preparing production facilities for a batch/job.","**Notional profit** — value of work certified less cost of work certified (fairly-advanced-stage contracts). **Estimated Total Profit (ETP)** — total contract price less estimated total cost (nearing-completion contracts).","**Work certified** — the architect's valuation of work done so far, the basis for contractor payment.","**Retention money** — a small percentage of the contract sum withheld by the contractee for a period after completion, as insurance against later-discovered faults."]},{"h3":"B. Formula box"},{"fbox":{"h":"Job/batch/contract costing formulas (recap)","rows":[{"lb":"Overhead rate, % of direct wages","tex":"\\dfrac{\\text{Total overhead}}{\\text{Total direct wages}} \\times 100"},{"lb":"Overhead rate, % of direct material","tex":"\\dfrac{\\text{Total overhead}}{\\text{Total direct material cost}} \\times 100"},{"lb":"Notional profit","tex":"\\text{Value of work certified} - \\text{Cost of work certified}"},{"lb":"Profit taken, fairly advanced stage","tex":"\\tfrac23 \\times \\text{Notional profit} \\times \\dfrac{\\text{Cash received}}{\\text{Value of work certified}}"},{"lb":"Estimated Total Profit (ETP)","tex":"\\text{Total contract price} - \\text{Estimated total cost}"}]}}]},{"n":"7.7","t":"End-of-chapter questions (study text)","b":[{"eg":{"tag":"Study text","t":"Multiple-choice and short-answer questions, with answers","open":true,"q":[{"ol":["Distinguish between a Job Card and a Job Cost Card. (A) Job card is kept for employees whilst Job Cost Card is kept for assets  (B) Job card is kept on the shop floor whilst Job Cost Card is in the cost office  (C) Job card is for engineering jobs whilst Job Cost Card is for financial jobs  (D) Job card is for jobs whilst Job Cost Card is for batches  (E) Job card is given to the customer whilst Job Cost Card is kept by the company","What are set-up costs? (A) Cost of buying equipment sets  (B) Cost of constructing the factory  (C) Cost of setting up production facilities for a particular job or production  (D) Cost of laying ambush for dishonest employees  (E) Cost of assembling factory operatives in readiness for production","What is mark-up? (A) A percentage of total costs added as profit to determine the selling price  (B) The distinctive mark on packages to avoid mix-up  (C) The total selling price of an order  (D) The total cost of executing a job  (E) The amount of bonus given to those who worked on an order","What is Notional profit on contracts? (A) The total profit of the contractor for the years  (B) The amount of profit to be paid to the nation's coffers  (C) The profit on the mobilisation fee received on a contract  (D) The amount of profit brought forward from previous years  (E) The portion of profit to be recognised on an on-going contract at year end","\"Value of work certified\" in a contract means (A) Value of materials used in the contract  (B) Total cost of executing a contract  (C) Total progress payments received on a contract  (D) The aggregate total value of architect's certificates issued so far  (E) Total value of items sub-contracted on the main contract","How is a job identified among other jobs being carried out simultaneously in the accounts?","What document gives the value of contract work completed on a progressive basis?","Large jobs, usually of long-term duration extending beyond the company's financial year-end and carried out on the customer's premises, are known as ………...","Where a company recognises total provisional profit on a contract almost fully completed at its year end, what is the name given to the amount added to cost-to-date to arrive at estimated total cost?","An amount, usually a small percentage of the total contract sum, withheld by the contractee for almost a year after contract completion as insurance against faults discovered later, is known as………….."]}],"a":[{"ol":["**B** — Job Card is kept on the shop floor; Job Cost Card is kept in the cost office.","**C — cost of setting up production facilities for a particular job or production.**","**A — a percentage of total costs added as profit, to determine the selling price.**","**E — the portion of profit recognised on an on-going contract at year end.**","**D — the aggregate total value of architect's certificates issued so far.**","**By its unique Job (Order) Number.**","**The architect's certificate.**","**Contracts.**","**Cost to completion (cost to complete).**","**Retention money (retention fee).**"]}]}}]},{"n":"7.8","t":"Study text examination-type questions, worked","b":[{"p":"The study text closes the chapter with eleven examination-type questions — four discursive, five computational job-costing questions, one article, and one service costing question. All are reproduced below with full answers. (The book leaves the last two unnumbered; they are shown here as Q10 and Q11.)"},{"eg":{"tag":"Exam type — Q1","t":"Discuss the concept of job order costing","q":[{"p":"Discuss the concept of Job Order Costing as clearly as possible."}],"a":[{"h4":"What it is"},{"p":"**Job order costing** is the method of costing used where production consists of separately identifiable **jobs**, each carried out to a customer's own specification. Each job is distinct, so costs are collected and ascertained **job by job** rather than averaged over a period's output. It is one of the two branches of **specific order costing**, the other being contract costing (and batch costing a variant of it)."},{"h4":"Where it is used"},{"ul":["jobbing engineering and machine shops;","printing works;","motor-vehicle and plant repair garages;","furniture making to order;","shipbuilding repairs, construction of individual items of plant;","professional firms costing an individual client assignment."]},{"h4":"The essential features"},{"ol":["Each job is given a **unique job (order) number**, which is the key under which all its costs are collected.","A **job cost card** (job cost sheet) is opened for every job in the cost office; all direct materials, direct wages, direct expenses and absorbed overhead are posted to it as they are incurred.","Costs are traced from source documents that each quote the job number — **materials requisitions** for materials, **job cards / time sheets** for labour, and invoices for direct expenses.","Overhead is **absorbed** into each job using a predetermined rate — per labour hour, machine hour, or percentage of wages or materials (Chapter 5).","Jobs are usually of **short duration** and carried out **on the company's own premises** — which is what distinguishes a job from a contract.","Work-in-progress at any date is simply the total of the cost cards of jobs not yet finished.","On completion the job cost card is totalled, and profit is recognised on the individual job when it is delivered and invoiced."]},{"h4":"The procedure, from enquiry to invoice"},{"steps":["The customer makes an enquiry; the works estimates the materials, labour and expenses the job will need.","Overhead is added at the predetermined rate, and a profit mark-up applied, to produce the **quotation** sent to the customer.","On acceptance, a **production order** is raised and a job number allocated; a job cost card is opened.","Materials are requisitioned and labour booked, each document quoting the job number.","As work proceeds, the cost card is posted with actual materials, wages, direct expenses and absorbed overhead.","On completion, the card is totalled to give the actual job cost; the job is invoiced, and actual cost is compared with the original estimate so that the estimating basis can be improved."]},{"h4":"Advantages and limitations"},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Advantages","Limitations"],"rows":[["The **actual cost and profit of each individual job** are known, so loss-making work can be identified and future quotations improved.","It is **clerically expensive** — every requisition and time booking must carry a job number, and a card must be kept for each job."],["It provides a sound basis for **quoting and pricing** similar future work.","It depends entirely on **accurate recording** at source; a mis-booked hour or requisition costs the wrong job."],["Actual costs can be **compared with the estimate**, revealing inefficiency and estimating error.","The job cost includes **absorbed** overhead, so it is only as reliable as the absorption rate chosen (Chapter 5)."],["Work-in-progress is valued simply and accurately from the open cost cards.","Because each job is unique, there is little scope for **standard costing** or routine variance analysis."]]}}]}},{"eg":{"tag":"Exam type — Q2","t":"Two methods of absorbing overhead costs by jobs","q":[{"p":"Briefly explain any **two** methods of absorbing overhead costs by jobs."}],"a":[{"h4":"Method 1 — the direct labour hour rate"},{"tex":"\\text{Rate} = \\frac{\\text{Budgeted overhead of the cost centre}}{\\text{Budgeted direct labour hours}}"},{"p":"Each job is charged with the hours its workers spent in the department, multiplied by that department's rate. It is the most appropriate method in a **labour-intensive** department, where overhead is incurred broadly in proportion to the time people spend working. Its advantage over a wages-percentage basis is that it is unaffected by differences in wage rates — two workers of different grades spending the same time on the same job absorb the same overhead, which is correct, because they consume the same amount of supervision, space, heat and light."},{"h4":"Method 2 — the machine hour rate"},{"tex":"\\text{Rate} = \\frac{\\text{Budgeted overhead of the cost centre}}{\\text{Budgeted machine hours}}"},{"p":"Each job is charged with the machine hours it used in the department. It is the appropriate method in a **machine-intensive** department, where most of the overhead — depreciation, power, maintenance, machine insurance and the space the machine occupies — is caused by running the machine rather than by employing a person. A job that ties up an expensive machine for eight hours while one operator watches it should carry more overhead than a job occupying the same operator at a bench, and only a machine hour rate achieves that."},{"note":"Two further methods, for completeness: the **percentage of direct wages** (overhead ÷ direct wages × 100), simple and usable where hours are not recorded; and the **cost unit rate** (overhead ÷ units produced), appropriate only where a single homogeneous product is made."}]}},{"eg":{"tag":"Exam type — Q3","t":"Short notes on four topics","q":[{"p":"Write short notes on each of the following: (i) overhead absorption; (ii) cost plus basis of pricing; (iii) transport service costing; (iv) batch costing."}],"a":[{"h4":"(i)  Overhead absorption"},{"p":"Overhead absorption (also called overhead recovery) is the process of charging the overhead accumulated in a production cost centre **out to the cost units** that pass through it. It is the final step after allocation, apportionment and reapportionment. A **predetermined** absorption rate is computed before the period begins — budgeted overhead ÷ budgeted activity — and applied to the actual activity of each job. Because both the overhead and the activity are estimates, the amount absorbed rarely equals the amount incurred, and the difference is the **under- or over-absorbed overhead**, written off to the costing profit and loss account. Common bases are the direct labour hour, the machine hour, a percentage of direct wages, a percentage of direct materials, a percentage of prime cost, and the cost unit."},{"h4":"(ii)  Cost plus basis of pricing"},{"p":"Cost plus pricing sets the selling price by calculating the cost of the job and adding a predetermined margin:"},{"tex":"\\text{Selling price} = \\text{Cost} + (\\text{Cost} \\times \\text{mark-up }\\%)"},{"p":"The \"cost\" may be total cost, production cost or marginal cost, and the percentage added differs accordingly. It is used where no market price exists for comparison — one-off jobs, custom contracts, government contracts, and work for which the customer agrees to reimburse cost plus a fee. **Advantages:** simple; guarantees that cost is recovered; useful where the final cost cannot be predicted at the time of quoting. **Disadvantages:** it ignores demand and what competitors charge; it passes inefficiency straight on to the customer, so it gives the supplier no incentive to control cost; and the answer depends on arbitrary overhead apportionment, so the \"cost\" it starts from is not a single objective figure."},{"warn":"Always read whether the margin is a percentage **of cost** (a mark-up) or a percentage **of selling price** (a margin). A 25% mark-up on a cost of N100 gives a price of N125; a 25% margin on selling price gives $100 \\div 0.75 = N133.33$."},{"h4":"(iii)  Transport service costing"},{"p":"Transport service costing is the application of **service (operation) costing** to a transport undertaking — a bus fleet, a haulage business, or a company's own delivery vehicles. Its distinguishing feature is the use of a **composite cost unit** that captures both distance and load:"},{"ul":["**passenger-kilometre** for passenger transport;","**tonne-kilometre** for goods transport."]},{"p":"Costs are classified into **standing (fixed) charges** — licences, insurance, road tax, depreciation on a time basis, drivers' wages, garage rent, interest — and **running (variable) charges** — fuel and oil, tyres, repairs and maintenance, and depreciation on a mileage basis. The cost per composite unit is the total of both, divided by the total passenger- or tonne-kilometres operated. It is used to set fares and freight rates, to compare the cost of different vehicles or routes, and to decide whether to own or hire transport. Question 11 below is a full worked example."},{"h4":"(iv)  Batch costing"},{"p":"**Batch costing** is the form of specific order costing in which a **batch** — a group of identical units made together as one order — is treated as the cost unit. Costs are collected for the whole batch exactly as for a job, and the cost per unit is then found by dividing:"},{"tex":"\\text{Cost per unit} = \\frac{\\text{Total cost of the batch}}{\\text{Number of good units in the batch}}"},{"p":"It is used where identical items are produced in groups rather than singly — printing, pharmaceuticals, bakeries, clothing, components for assembly, and the manufacture of spare parts. Its characteristic feature is the **set-up cost**, which is incurred once per batch whatever the batch size, so the cost **per unit** falls as the batch grows — the effect worked through numerically in Illustration 7.3 above. Batch costing therefore raises the question of the **economic batch quantity**, which balances set-up cost against the cost of holding the resulting inventory, in exactly the way the EOQ balances ordering against carrying cost (Chapter 3)."}]}},{"eg":{"tag":"Exam type — Q4","t":"Mr Hankaka — costing a job on three different overhead bases","q":[{"p":"Mr Hankaka, a shoe manufacturer, found that his production costs for an accounting period had been as follows:"},{"stmt":{"t":"Production costs for the period","rows":[["Direct wages","",800000],["Direct materials","",300000],["Direct expenses","",100000],["**Prime cost**","",1200000,"@t"],["Production overhead","",240000],["**Factory cost**","",1440000,"@tt"],"@gap",["Total shoes manufactured","","160,000 units"],["Total machine hours used","","10,000 hours"],["Total direct labour hours used","","40,000 hours"]]}},{"p":"A job performed had a direct wages cost of **N800**, direct material cost of **N500** and direct expenses of **N150**."},{"p":"**Required:** **(a)** find the total cost of the job using (i) the direct wages percentage rate and (ii) the direct material percentage rate. **(b)** If the job was that of producing **120 units** of shoes, what would be the total cost?"}],"a":[{"h4":"Step 1 — the absorption rates"},{"stmt":{"t":"Overhead absorption rates","rows":[["Direct wages percentage rate:  N240,000 ÷ N800,000","","**30% of direct wages**"],["Direct material percentage rate:  N240,000 ÷ N300,000","","**80% of direct materials**"],["Cost unit rate:  N240,000 ÷ 160,000 units","","**N1.50 per unit**"]]}},{"h4":"(a)  Total cost of the job"},{"stmt":{"t":"Job cost under the two bases (N)","rows":[["","(i) Direct wages %","(ii) Direct material %","@hd"],["Direct materials",500,500],["Direct wages",800,800],["Direct expenses",150,150],["**Prime cost**",1450,1450,"@t"],["Production overhead  (30% × 800;  80% × 500)",240,400],["**Total cost of the job**",1690,1850,"@tt"]]}},{"note":"The same job, the same work, two answers **N160 apart** — a 9.5% difference on an identical job. Which is right depends on whether this factory's overhead is actually driven by labour or by materials. For a shoe factory, neither is convincing: a labour hour or machine hour rate would be better, which is why the question goes on to part (b)."},{"h4":"(b)  If the job produced 120 units of shoes"},{"p":"Where the output is a **single homogeneous product** — as here, shoes — the **cost unit rate** is both the simplest and the most accurate basis, because every unit genuinely consumes the same overhead:"},{"stmt":{"t":"Job cost using the cost unit rate (N)","rows":[["Direct materials","",500],["Direct wages","",800],["Direct expenses","",150],["**Prime cost**","",1450,"@t"],["Production overhead:  120 units × N1.50","",180],["**Total cost of the job**","",1630,"@tt"]]}},{"tex":"\\text{Cost per pair of shoes} = \\frac{1{,}630}{120} = N13.58"},{"note":"A second reading of part (b) is possible — simply applying the **average factory cost per shoe** of $N1{,}440{,}000 \\div 160{,}000 = N9.00$ to give $120 \\times N9 = N1{,}080$. That is a valid answer to \"what should 120 shoes cost on average\", but it ignores the job's own actual direct costs, which the question supplies. The answer above uses the actual direct costs and absorbs overhead per unit, which is the more defensible treatment; state whichever reading you adopt."}]}},{"eg":{"tag":"Exam type — Q5","t":"Hantsi Limited — Job 707 costed on three overhead bases, with a 45% profit target","q":[{"p":"Hantsi Limited operates a factory consisting of two departments: **Assembly** and **Galvanizing**. All products pass through both departments. The following information was given:"},{"stmt":{"t":"Departmental data","rows":[["","Assembly","Galvanizing","@hd"],["Materials","N40,000","N12,000"],["Direct wages","N20,000","N36,000"],["Production overhead","N16,000","N24,000"],["Direct labour hours",38000,24000],["Machine hours",66000,38000]]}},{"p":"One of the jobs completed in the factory was **No. 707**:"},{"stmt":{"t":"Job No. 707","rows":[["","Assembly","Galvanizing","@hd"],["Materials","N400","N80"],["Direct wages","N300","N150"],["Direct labour hours",800,300],["Machine hours",700,800]]}},{"p":"**Required:** **(a)** use **three** different methods by which production overhead could be recovered, and prepare the job cost card for Job No. 707, using **50% of production overhead** to recover administrative overhead and **25% of production overhead** to recover selling and distribution overhead. **(b)** Show the total proceeds on the job if the target profit is **45% of total cost**."}],"a":[{"h4":"Step 1 — three sets of departmental overhead absorption rates"},{"stmt":{"t":"Absorption rates by method","rows":[["Method","Assembly","Galvanizing","@hd"],["**1. Direct material percentage**  (16,000÷40,000;  24,000÷12,000)","40%","200%"],["**2. Direct wages percentage**  (16,000÷20,000;  24,000÷36,000)","80%","66⅔%"],["**3. Direct labour hour rate**  (16,000÷38,000;  24,000÷24,000)","N0.4211","N1.0000"],["*(4. Machine hour rate, for comparison: 16,000÷66,000; 24,000÷38,000)*","*N0.2424*","*N0.6316*"]]}},{"h4":"Step 2 — production overhead absorbed by Job 707 under each method"},{"stmt":{"t":"Production overhead absorbed (N)","rows":[["Method","Assembly","Galvanizing","Total","@hd"],["1. Direct material %  (40% × 400;  200% × 80)",160.0,160.0,320.0],["2. Direct wages %  (80% × 300;  66⅔% × 150)",240.0,100.0,340.0],["3. Direct labour hour  (800 × 0.4211;  300 × 1.00)",336.84,300.0,636.84]]}},{"h4":"(a)  Job cost card — Job No. 707"},{"stmt":{"t":"Job Cost Card — Job No. 707 (N)","rows":[["","Method 1","Method 2","Method 3","@hd"],["Direct materials  (400 + 80)",480.0,480.0,480.0],["Direct wages  (300 + 150)",450.0,450.0,450.0],["**Prime cost**",930.0,930.0,930.0,"@t"],["Production overhead absorbed",320.0,340.0,636.84],["**Production cost**",1250.0,1270.0,1566.84,"@t"],["Administrative overhead — 50% of production overhead",160.0,170.0,318.42],["Selling and distribution overhead — 25% of production overhead",80.0,85.0,159.21],["**Total cost of Job No. 707**",1490.0,1525.0,2044.47,"@tt"]]}},{"h4":"(b)  Total proceeds at a target profit of 45% of total cost"},{"stmt":{"t":"Selling price of Job No. 707 (N)","rows":[["","Method 1","Method 2","Method 3","@hd"],["Total cost",1490.0,1525.0,2044.47],["Add: profit at 45% of total cost",670.5,686.25,920.01],["**Total proceeds on the job**",2160.5,2211.25,2964.48,"@tt"]]}},{"key":"The spread is dramatic: the same job is priced anywhere between N2,160 and N2,964 depending purely on which overhead base is chosen — a **37% difference**. Method 3 produces much the highest figure because Job 707 is **labour-hour hungry** (1,100 hours) relative to its small material and wage values, so an hours basis loads far more overhead onto it than a value basis does. Nothing about the job changed. This is the clearest possible demonstration that a \"job cost\" is never a single objective number — it is a number produced by a chosen convention, and the convention should be chosen to reflect what actually causes the overhead."},{"note":"Note the 45% is a mark-up **on cost**, not a margin on selling price, so it is applied by multiplying the cost by 1.45. The profit as a percentage of the selling price is $45 \\div 145 = 31.03\\%$."}]}},{"eg":{"tag":"Exam type — Q6 (BUK, 1993)","t":"Shahada Ltd — Job 807 on a material-percentage and a labour-hour basis","q":[{"p":"Shahada Ltd operates a factory consisting of two departments: **Washing** and **Cleaning**. All activities pass through both departments:"},{"stmt":{"t":"Departmental data","rows":[["","Washing","Cleaning","@hd"],["Materials","N60,000","N25,000"],["Direct wages","N30,000","N40,000"],["Production overhead","N20,000","N32,000"],["Direct labour hours",40000,25000],["Machine hours",50000,43000]]}},{"p":"One of the jobs completed was **Job No. 807**:"},{"stmt":{"t":"Job No. 807","rows":[["","Washing","Cleaning","@hd"],["Materials","N500","N120"],["Direct wages","N450","N200"],["Direct labour hours",900,450],["Machine hours",200,150]]}},{"p":"**Required:** prepare the job cost card for Job No. 807 using (i) the direct material percentage rate and (ii) the direct labour hour rate of recovering production overhead. Use **30%** of the production overhead of the job to recover administrative overhead and **35%** to recover selling and distribution overhead."}],"a":[{"h4":"Step 1 — the absorption rates"},{"stmt":{"t":"Absorption rates","rows":[["Method","Washing","Cleaning","@hd"],["**(i) Direct material percentage**  (20,000÷60,000;  32,000÷25,000)","33⅓%","128%"],["**(ii) Direct labour hour rate**  (20,000÷40,000;  32,000÷25,000)","N0.50","N1.28"]]}},{"h4":"Step 2 — production overhead absorbed by Job 807"},{"stmt":{"t":"Production overhead absorbed (N)","rows":[["Method","Washing","Cleaning","Total","@hd"],["(i) Direct material %  (33⅓% × 500;  128% × 120)",166.67,153.6,320.27],["(ii) Direct labour hour  (900 × 0.50;  450 × 1.28)",450.0,576.0,1026.0]]}},{"h4":"Job Cost Card — Job No. 807"},{"stmt":{"t":"Job Cost Card — Job No. 807 (N)","rows":[["","(i) Material %","(ii) Labour hour","@hd"],["Direct materials  (500 + 120)",620.0,620.0],["Direct wages  (450 + 200)",650.0,650.0],["**Prime cost**",1270.0,1270.0,"@t"],["Production overhead absorbed",320.27,1026.0],["**Production cost**",1590.27,2296.0,"@t"],["Administrative overhead — 30% of production overhead",96.08,307.8],["Selling and distribution overhead — 35% of production overhead",112.09,359.1],["**Total cost of Job No. 807**",1798.44,2962.9,"@tt"]]}},{"key":"Job 807 uses **1,350 direct labour hours** but only **N620** of materials, so it is an extremely labour-intensive job. A material-percentage basis therefore charges it almost nothing for overhead (N320) while a labour-hour basis charges it over three times as much (N1,026) — and the total cost differs by N1,164, or 65%. Quoting this job on a material-percentage basis would almost certainly lose money. The lesson is the same as in Q5: **match the absorption base to what actually consumes the overhead.**"}]}},{"eg":{"tag":"Exam type — Q7 (HUK Poly, 1997)","t":"Ray Soap Manufacturing — Job S.10 on four overhead bases","q":[{"p":"The following figures have been extracted from the books of Ray Soap Manufacturing Company. All jobs pass through the company's two departments:"},{"stmt":{"t":"Departmental data","rows":[["","Working Department","Finishing Department","@hd"],["Materials used","N6,000","N500"],["Direct labour cost","N3,000","N1,500"],["Factory overheads","N1,800","N1,200"],["Direct labour hours",12000,5000],["Machine hours",10000,2000]]}},{"p":"The following information relates to **Job S.10**:"},{"stmt":{"t":"Job S.10","rows":[["","Working Department","Finishing Department","@hd"],["Materials used","N20","N10"],["Direct labour","N65","N25"],["Direct labour hours",265,70],["Machine hours",255,25]]}},{"p":"**Required:** **(a)** use **four** methods of absorbing factory overhead to show the overhead absorbed in each department; **(b)** prepare statements showing the different cost results for Job S.10 under any **two** of those methods."}],"a":[{"h4":"(a)  Four methods of absorbing factory overhead"},{"stmt":{"t":"Absorption rates","rows":[["Method","Working Dept.","Finishing Dept.","@hd"],["**1. Direct material percentage**  (1,800÷6,000;  1,200÷500)","30%","240%"],["**2. Direct labour cost percentage**  (1,800÷3,000;  1,200÷1,500)","60%","80%"],["**3. Direct labour hour rate**  (1,800÷12,000;  1,200÷5,000)","N0.15","N0.24"],["**4. Machine hour rate**  (1,800÷10,000;  1,200÷2,000)","N0.18","N0.60"]]}},{"stmt":{"t":"Overhead absorbed by Job S.10 (N)","rows":[["Method","Working Dept.","Finishing Dept.","Total","@hd"],["1. Direct material %  (30% × 20;  240% × 10)",6.0,24.0,30.0],["2. Direct labour cost %  (60% × 65;  80% × 25)",39.0,20.0,59.0],["3. Direct labour hour  (265 × 0.15;  70 × 0.24)",39.75,16.8,56.55],["4. Machine hour  (255 × 0.18;  25 × 0.60)",45.9,15.0,60.9]]}},{"note":"A fifth method, the **percentage of prime cost**, would give Working $1{,}800 \\div (6{,}000+3{,}000) = 20\\%$ and Finishing $1{,}200 \\div (500+1{,}500) = 60\\%$, absorbing $20\\% \\times 85 + 60\\% \\times 35 = N38.00$ on Job S.10."},{"h4":"(b)  Cost statements for Job S.10 under two methods"},{"stmt":{"t":"Job S.10 — cost statement (N)","rows":[["","Direct labour hour basis","Machine hour basis","@hd"],["Direct materials  (20 + 10)",30.0,30.0],["Direct labour  (65 + 25)",90.0,90.0],["**Prime cost**",120.0,120.0,"@t"],["Factory overhead absorbed — Working Department",39.75,45.9],["Factory overhead absorbed — Finishing Department",16.8,15.0],["**Total factory overhead**",56.55,60.9,"@t"],["**Factory cost of Job S.10**",176.55,180.9,"@tt"]]}},{"key":"Unlike Q5 and Q6, the four bases here give answers that are reasonably close (N30 to N60.90 of overhead), because Job S.10 is not lopsided — it consumes materials, labour and machine time in roughly the proportions the departments do as a whole. The **direct material percentage** is the outlier at N30, and it is the least defensible basis: there is no reason why the value of soap ingredients should govern how much factory overhead a job consumes."}]}},{"eg":{"tag":"Exam type — Q8","t":"Procedures involved in normal service costing","q":[{"p":"Discuss the procedures involved in normal service costing."}],"a":[{"def":{"t":"Service (operation) costing","d":"the method of costing applied where standardised **services** are provided, either to outside customers or by one department of an organisation to another, rather than tangible goods being produced."}},{"h4":"The procedures"},{"steps":["**Identify the service and the cost centre** providing it — a bus route, a hospital ward, a canteen, a power house, a hotel, a maintenance department.","**Select an appropriate cost unit.** This is the crucial step, and the unit is usually **composite**, combining two variables, because a single variable does not describe the service adequately — passenger-kilometre, tonne-kilometre, patient-day, kilowatt-hour, bed-night, meal served, student-year.","**Classify the costs** into **standing (fixed) charges** — those incurred whether or not the service operates: licences, insurance, road tax, rent, supervisory salaries, time-based depreciation, interest — and **running (variable) charges** — fuel, oil, tyres, consumables, repairs, mileage-based depreciation. Many undertakings add a third group, **maintenance charges**.","**Collect the costs** against the service cost centre for the period, using the normal documents — requisitions, time sheets, invoices, journals.","**Measure the volume of service provided** in the chosen cost unit for the same period — the kilometres run, the loads carried, the patient-days, the units generated.","**Compute the cost per unit** by dividing total cost by total units, and analyse it into its standing and running elements per unit.","**Prepare the operating cost statement** — the standard output of service costing — showing each cost under its heading, the total, the units of service, and the cost per unit.","**Compare and control** — against budget, against the previous period, and between comparable vehicles, routes, wards or departments — and act on the differences.","**Use the result** to set fares, charges or transfer prices, and to decide questions such as own-or-hire, which routes to run, and when to replace a vehicle."]},{"h4":"Features that distinguish service costing"},{"ul":["there is usually **no inventory** and no work-in-progress, because a service cannot be stored;","**fixed costs are a high proportion** of the total, so the cost per unit is very sensitive to the level of activity;","the cost unit is often **composite**, and choosing it badly makes the whole exercise meaningless;","direct **materials** are usually a small proportion of cost, with labour and overhead dominating."]}]}},{"eg":{"tag":"Exam type — Q9","t":"Features of a good batch costing system","q":[{"p":"Discuss the necessary features of a good batch costing system."}],"a":[{"ol":["**A unique batch number** for every batch, under which all its costs are collected, exactly as a job number works in job costing.","**A batch cost card** opened for each batch, recording direct materials, direct wages, direct expenses and absorbed overhead, together with the quantity produced and the quantity rejected.","**Separate identification of set-up costs.** Set-up cost is incurred **once per batch**, whatever the batch size, and must be distinguished from the costs that vary with quantity — otherwise the effect of batch size on unit cost cannot be seen.","**Accurate recording of the number of good units produced**, since the cost per unit is the batch cost divided by good output; rejects must be counted and their treatment (normal or abnormal loss) decided.","**A sound overhead absorption basis**, with set-up and machine-related overhead absorbed on a basis that reflects what causes it.","**Determination of an economic batch quantity**, balancing the set-up cost saved by making larger batches against the extra cost of holding the resulting inventory.","**Comparison of actual batch cost with the estimate**, so that quoting and planning improve, and so that inefficiency in a particular batch is identified.","**Proper documentation and control of materials and labour** booked to each batch — requisitions and time records quoting the batch number.","**Prompt reporting** of the completed batch cost and unit cost to management, in time to influence the next batch.","**Integration with inventory records**, since completed batches move into finished goods and must be valued consistently."]},{"key":"The one feature that distinguishes batch costing from job costing is the treatment of **batch size**. In job costing each job is unique and the question \"how many should we make at once?\" does not arise. In batch costing it is the central question, because set-up cost per unit falls as the batch grows while inventory holding cost per unit rises — and a good system must produce the information needed to strike that balance."}]}},{"eg":{"tag":"Exam type — Q10","t":"Article: \"Peculiarities of contract costing and accounting\"","q":[{"p":"Write an article suitable for publication in an Accounting Students' Journal / Newsletter on the topic: **\"Peculiarities of contract costing and accounting\"**."}],"a":[{"h4":"PECULIARITIES OF CONTRACT COSTING AND ACCOUNTING"},{"p":"Contract costing is a form of specific order costing, and in principle a contract is only a very large job. In practice, however, four characteristics of contract work force a quite different accounting treatment, and it is those peculiarities that make the topic worth an article of its own."},{"h4":"1.  The contract is long and straddles the year end"},{"p":"A job is normally begun and finished within an accounting period, so its profit can simply be recognised when it is delivered. A contract — a road, a bridge, a housing estate — may run for three or four years. If profit were recognised only on completion, the contractor would report no profit at all for years and then a vast profit in one. Contract costing therefore recognises profit **as the work proceeds**, and the rules for doing so prudently are the heart of the subject."},{"h4":"2.  The work is carried out on the customer's site"},{"p":"Because the work is done away from the contractor's own premises, almost everything becomes a **direct** cost of that contract — materials delivered straight to site, the wages of everyone working there, the hire of plant, site power and water, the site office, and even the site supervisor's salary. Overhead absorption, which dominates job costing, is a comparatively minor matter in contract costing: only head-office overhead has to be apportioned between contracts."},{"h4":"3.  Progress is measured by an outside professional"},{"p":"The contractor does not decide how much work has been done. An independent **architect or engineer** inspects the site and issues a **certificate** stating the **value of work certified** to date, and it is on that certificate that progress payments are made. Contract accounting therefore turns on a set of terms that have no counterpart in job costing:"},{"ul":["**Value of work certified** — the aggregate value of the certificates issued so far;","**Cost of work certified** — cost to date, less the cost of work not yet certified;","**Work not yet certified** — work physically done since the last certificate;","**Progress payments** — the cash actually received, normally the certified value less the retention;","**Retention money** — a percentage of the certified value (commonly 5% to 10%) withheld by the customer for a defects-liability period after completion, as insurance against faults appearing later;","**Notional profit** — value of work certified less the cost of that work: the profit that *appears* to have been earned so far, and the starting point for deciding how much may prudently be taken."]},{"h4":"4.  Profit is recognised cautiously, in stages"},{"p":"Because an uncompleted contract may still go wrong, profit is taken by stages according to how far it has progressed:"},{"ol":["**Early stage** (broadly, less than a quarter complete): **no profit** is taken at all, because the outcome cannot be judged.","**Fairly advanced** (broadly between a quarter and nine-tenths complete): a fraction of the notional profit is taken, reduced further by the proportion of the certified value actually received in cash — commonly $\\tfrac23 \\times \\text{notional profit} \\times \\dfrac{\\text{cash received}}{\\text{value of work certified}}$.","**Nearly complete**: the estimated total profit on the whole contract is computed, and the proportion relating to work certified is taken.","**Any foreseeable loss** is recognised **in full, immediately** — prudence works in only one direction."]},{"h4":"5.  Other peculiarities worth knowing"},{"ul":["**A separate contract account** is opened for each contract, functioning as that contract's own trading account.","**Plant on site** is charged to the contract and credited back at its written-down value at the year end, so the contract bears the depreciation of the plant it actually used.","**Materials on site** at the year end are credited to the contract and carried forward as an asset.","**Sub-contractors** are common, and their work is a direct cost of the contract.","**Escalation clauses** may allow the contract price itself to be revised for inflation in material and labour costs.","**Work-in-progress** in the balance sheet is shown as cost to date plus profit taken, less losses and less progress payments received."]},{"h4":"Conclusion"},{"p":"Contract costing is where cost accounting meets the problem of **measuring performance before the work is finished**. Everything peculiar about it — the architect's certificate, retention money, notional profit, the staged recognition rules — exists to answer one question that job costing never has to ask: *how much of this job's profit have we actually earned yet?* For the student, the practical advice is to learn the vocabulary precisely, to be ruthless about distinguishing **cost to date** from **cost of work certified**, and to remember that profits are taken cautiously while losses are taken in full."}]}},{"eg":{"tag":"Exam type — Q11 (12 marks)","t":"A bus fleet — cost per passenger-kilometre","q":[{"p":"A transport company runs a fleet of **six buses** between two towns **75 kilometres apart**. Each bus has a seating capacity of **40 passengers**. The following details are available for the month of June 2025:"},{"stmt":{"t":"Costs for June 2025 (₦)","rows":[["Wages of drivers, conductors and cleaners","",360000],["Salaries of office staff","",150000],["Fuel and oils","",1350000],["Repairs and maintenance","",280000],["Vehicle licensing, insurance and other road levies","",240000],["Depreciation of buses","",390000],["Finance charges (interest on loan)","",300000]]}},{"p":"On average the buses are **80% full** during the trips, and they operate on **25 days** during the month, doing **a round trip each day**."},{"p":"**Required:** compute the cost per passenger-kilometre."}],"a":[{"h4":"Step 1 — the operating cost statement"},{"stmt":{"t":"Operating cost statement for June 2025 (₦)","rows":[["**Standing (fixed) charges**","","","@hd"],["Wages of drivers, conductors and cleaners",360000,""],["Salaries of office staff",150000,""],["Vehicle licensing, insurance and other road levies",240000,""],["Depreciation of buses",390000,""],["Finance charges (interest on loan)",300000,1440000],"@gap",["**Running (variable) charges**","","","@hd"],["Fuel and oils",1350000,""],["Repairs and maintenance",280000,1630000],"@gap",["**Total operating cost for the month**","",3070000,"@tt"]]}},{"h4":"Step 2 — the volume of service, in the composite cost unit"},{"p":"The cost unit for passenger transport is the **passenger-kilometre** — one passenger carried one kilometre. Build it up in three steps:"},{"stmt":{"t":"Passenger-kilometres operated in June 2025","rows":[["Distance one way","","75 km"],["Round trip (×2)","","150 km"],["Round trips per bus in the month (1 per day × 25 days)","",25],["Kilometres per bus for the month (150 × 25)","","3,750 km"],["Number of buses","",6],["**Total kilometres run by the fleet**","","22,500 km","@t"],"@gap",["Seating capacity per bus","","40 passengers"],["Average occupancy","","80%"],["**Average passengers carried per bus (40 × 80%)**","","32 passengers","@t"],"@gap",["**Total passenger-kilometres (22,500 km × 32 passengers)**","","720,000","@tt"]]}},{"h4":"Step 3 — cost per passenger-kilometre"},{"tex":"\\text{Cost per passenger-km} = \\frac{₦3{,}070{,}000}{720{,}000} = ₦4.26 \\text{ (to the nearest kobo)}"},{"stmt":{"t":"Analysis of the ₦4.26","rows":[["Standing charges:  ₦1,440,000 ÷ 720,000","","₦2.00 per passenger-km"],["Running charges:  ₦1,630,000 ÷ 720,000","","₦2.26 per passenger-km"],["**Total cost per passenger-kilometre**","","**₦4.26**","@tt"]]}},{"key":"Two things decide every service-costing answer, and both are easy to get wrong. First, the **round trip** — the buses travel 150 km a day, not 75; forgetting the return journey halves the denominator and doubles the answer. Second, the **occupancy** — the unit is passenger-kilometres actually carried, so it is 32 passengers, not the 40 seats available. Using 40 would give ₦3.41 and understate the true cost by a fifth."},{"note":"Note how heavily this business depends on load factor. If occupancy rose from 80% to 100%, the passenger-kilometres would become 900,000 and the cost per passenger-km would fall to **₦3.41** — a 20% reduction — because the ₦1,440,000 of standing charges would be spread over more passengers while barely changing in total. That sensitivity of unit cost to activity is the defining economic feature of service industries."}]}}]}],"formulas":[{"lb":"Overhead rate, % of direct wages","tex":"\\dfrac{\\text{Total overhead}}{\\text{Total direct wages}} \\times 100"},{"lb":"Overhead rate, % of direct material","tex":"\\dfrac{\\text{Total overhead}}{\\text{Total direct material cost}} \\times 100"},{"lb":"Notional profit (fairly advanced contract)","tex":"\\text{Value of work certified} - \\text{Cost of work certified}"},{"lb":"Profit taken (fairly advanced contract)","tex":"\\tfrac23 \\times \\text{Notional profit} \\times \\dfrac{\\text{Cash received}}{\\text{Value of work certified}}"},{"lb":"Estimated Total Profit (ETP)","tex":"\\text{Total contract price} - \\text{Estimated total cost}"},{"lb":"Contract value for balance sheet","tex":"(\\text{Cost to date} + \\text{Profit taken}) - \\text{Cash received}"}],"focus":"Contract costing's profit-recognition rules are the single most exam-tested part of this chapter — know all three contract stages and exactly which formula applies at each, since using the wrong stage's formula is the most common error. Job/batch overhead-absorption questions test whether the *right basis* was chosen for the scenario, not just correct arithmetic on a given basis (Illustrations 7.1/7.2 show the same job costed two genuinely different ways). Always separate cost of work *certified* from cost *to date* before computing notional profit.","errors":["Applying the nearing-completion ETP formula to a contract that is only fairly advanced (or vice versa) — the two stages use genuinely different formulas, not the same one at different points.","Using cost to date instead of cost of *work certified* when calculating notional profit — these differ whenever some incurred cost has not yet been certified by the architect.","Deferring or spreading a foreseeable contract loss instead of writing it off immediately in full, as prudence requires.","Forgetting to deduct profit already recognised in prior periods when finding the profit to take in the *current* period on a nearing-completion contract.","Treating the Job Card and the Job Cost Card as the same document — one lives on the shop floor, the other in the cost office, and they serve different purposes."],"quiz":{"mcq":[{"q":"A very large order is best handled, for job-costing purposes, by","o":["refusing the order entirely","costing it only once fully complete","dividing it into separately numbered batches (e.g. 30.1, 30.2, 30.3)","ignoring overhead absorption for that order","treating it as process costing"],"a":2,"w":"Splitting a very large order into separately numbered batches lets costing proceed without waiting for the whole order to finish.","src":"Chapter 7.2","sec":"7.2"},{"q":"A company's total overhead is ₵1,800,000 and total direct wages are ₵4,500,000. The overhead absorption rate as a percentage of direct wages is","o":["25%","40%","2.5%","250%","4%"],"a":1,"w":"Overhead rate = (1,800,000 / 4,500,000) × 100 = 40%.","calc":"\\dfrac{1{,}800{,}000}{4{,}500{,}000} \\times 100 = 40\\%","src":"Chapter 7.2","sec":"7.2"},{"q":"In batch costing, unit cost tends to fall as batch size increases mainly because","o":["direct materials become cheaper in bulk automatically","set-up costs and other flat batch costs are spread over more units","labour works faster on larger batches","overhead is waived above a certain quantity","quality control is skipped for large batches"],"a":1,"w":"Set-up costs and other costs that stay the same regardless of batch size are divided by more units as the batch grows, pulling the average unit cost down.","src":"Chapter 7.3","sec":"7.3"},{"q":"A contract's value of work certified is ₵900,000 and cost of work certified is ₵750,000. The notional profit is","o":["₵150,000","₵900,000","₵750,000","₵1,650,000","₵0"],"a":0,"w":"Notional profit = value of work certified − cost of work certified = 900,000 − 750,000 = ₵150,000.","calc":"900{,}000 - 750{,}000 = 150{,}000","src":"Chapter 7.4","sec":"7.4"},{"q":"A contract has just started and is nowhere near completion. How much profit should be recognised?","o":["The full notional profit","Two-thirds of the notional profit","None — expenses are simply carried forward as the cost of work-in-progress","The estimated total profit for the whole contract","Half of the estimated total profit"],"a":2,"w":"At the early stage, no profit is recognised — costs are simply carried forward as WIP, exactly as in ordinary job costing.","src":"Chapter 7.4","sec":"7.4"},{"q":"Where a contract as a whole is expected to result in a loss, that foreseeable loss should be","o":["spread evenly over the contract's remaining life","ignored until the contract is actually complete","written off immediately and in full, as soon as it is foreseeable","added to the value of work certified","deducted from retention money only"],"a":2,"w":"Prudence requires a foreseeable loss to be written off immediately and to the maximum extent possible, not deferred or spread.","src":"Chapter 7.4","sec":"7.4"},{"q":"The document issued by an architect, valuing the work done on a contract so far and forming the basis for payment to the contractor, is the","o":["job cost card","retention certificate","work certified (architect's valuation) certificate","notional profit statement","bin card"],"a":2,"w":"The architect's valuation certificate (\"work certified\") is what payment to the contractor is based on.","src":"Chapter 7.4","sec":"7.4"},{"q":"Service (operation) costing is most associated with organisations that","o":["manufacture identical products in continuous production runs","provide standardised services on a continuous basis, e.g. transport or hospitals","produce one-off, highly customised physical products only","trade purely in raw materials","never invest in fixed assets"],"a":1,"w":"Service costing applies to organisations providing standardised, continuous services — transport, electricity, hotels, hospitals, schools, cinemas — often with heavy fixed-asset investment.","src":"Chapter 7.5","sec":"7.5"},{"q":"On a transport company's operation cost sheet, driver's salaries and vehicle licensing are classified as","o":["variable (running) costs","fixed costs (standing charges)","direct material costs","notional charges only","contract costing items"],"a":1,"w":"Driver's salaries, vehicle licensing/insurance, park dues and administrative charges are all standing (fixed) charges; fuel, depreciation and repairs are the variable (running) costs.","src":"Chapter 7.5","sec":"7.5"}],"theory":[{"q":"Write short notes on each of the following: (i) Overhead absorption, (ii) Cost Plus basis of pricing, (iii) Transport service costing, (iv) Batch costing.","marks":20,"a":[{"p":"**(i) Overhead absorption** — the process of charging a production cost centre's overhead to every cost unit passing through it, using a predetermined overhead absorption rate applied to actual activity."},{"p":"**(ii) Cost Plus pricing** — pricing an order at its agreed cost plus an agreed target profit margin, commonly used for government and other contracts where a market price is not readily available."},{"p":"**(iii) Transport service costing** — a form of service costing that aggregates a vehicle's fixed (standing) and variable (running) costs on an operation cost sheet, to find a cost per unit of service such as cost per kilometre."},{"p":"**(iv) Batch costing** — costing a quantity of identical units produced together under one job number, with the total batch cost divided by the number of units to find a cost per unit; unit cost falls as batch size rises, since flat costs (like set-up cost) are spread over more units."}],"src":"Chapter 7.2","sec":"7.2"},{"q":"Discuss the concept of Job Order Costing as clearly as possible.","marks":10,"a":[{"p":"Job order costing is a specific-order costing method used where each unit of output — a \"job\" — can be separately identified throughout production, typically made to a customer's own specification (construction, printing, general engineering). Each job is given its own order number, tracked through a job cost sheet/ledger account recording its own material, labour and absorbed overhead, letting the organisation determine the total cost — and hence the profit or loss — of that specific job individually, rather than averaging cost across continuous, undifferentiated output the way process costing does."}],"src":"Chapter 7.1","sec":"7.1"}]}},{"n":8,"t":"Continuous Operation Costing: Process Costing and Joint Products","brief":"The costing method for continuous, repetitive production rather than discrete orders: process costing's principles and how it differs from order costing, the treatment of normal and abnormal process losses/gains, the equivalent-units concept that lets part-finished work-in-progress share fairly in a period's cost, the full cost-of-production report under both weighted-average and FIFO inventory valuation, joint products and by-products, the three methods of apportioning joint cost, and service (operation) costing as the non-specific-order method for service industries.","outcomes":["Explain the meaning and importance of process costing","Explain the areas where process costing is applied","Determine equivalent units and explain their meaning","Treat process losses and wastages","Describe the valuation process in process costing","Determine completed product cost","Explain joint costs and joint products","Understand the various methods of apportioning joint costs to joint products","Explain the accounting treatment of by-products","Determine the total cost of producing joint products"],"secs":[{"n":"8.1","t":"Process costing — principles, application and distinction from order costing","b":[{"p":"Continuous operation costing applies wherever production is **repetitive**, rather than made to a customer's own specification. In manufacturing it takes two forms — **process costing** and **output/operation costing**; in service organisations, the equivalent is called **operating (service) costing** (§8.7)."},{"def":{"t":"Process costing","d":"a method used where production follows a series of sequential processes, ascertaining the cost of a product or service at each stage. It applies where continuous mass production is possible."}},{"key":"Because output is continuous and uniform, it is neither possible nor necessary to tie a particular unit of output to a particular moment of manufacture — the cost of any one unit is taken as the **average cost of manufacture over the period**. This average is complicated by two things every process-costing question must handle: apportioning cost between completed output and unfinished production at period end, and accounting for wastage."},{"p":"Industries using process costing: food processing, paper milling, chemicals and drugs, oil refining, soap making, textiles, box-making, paint and ink, brewing, flour milling, bottling/canning, biscuits, meat products, sugar making — arguably **the most widely used cost accounting system in the world**."},{"p":"Common features across most process costing systems:"},{"ol":["expenditure for each cost centre is collected, and at period end the cost of completed units transfers to an inventory account or to the next process cost centre;","accurate records of units produced and part-produced (and their cost) are essential;","the cost unit chosen should be relevant to the organisation;","the output of one process becomes the raw material **input** of the next — so a process cost centre's cost includes the transferred-in cost from the previous process, plus its own materials, labour and overhead; and","wastage (scrap, chemical reaction, evaporation) is unavoidable, but should be minimised by good operating practice."]},{"note":"Either the main product or a by-product may need further processing before it can be sold."},{"h4":"Process costing versus order costing"},{"p":"These are two extreme systems of product costing. Both, fundamentally, are an **averaging process**: unit cost = accumulated cost ÷ production level. The real difference is the **size of the denominator**."},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["","Order costing","Process costing"],"rows":[["Denominator (activity level)","Small — one bridge, one hundred copies of a book","Large — thousands of litres, thousands of bags"],["Typical industries","Construction, printing, furniture-making, aircraft, machinery, shoe-making — tailor-made/unique goods","Chemicals, oil, textiles, plastics, paints, flour milling, canneries, rubber, food processing, glass-making, mining, cement, meat processing — mass production of identical units passing through uniform steps"]]}},{"h4":"Production stages"},{"p":"Output of one process automatically becomes the input of the next. An intermediate product is progressively processed — further material, labour and overhead added at each stage — until finished goods emerge and move to the warehouse. At **each** process, unit cost = total output cost ÷ total units produced at that stage: Process A → Process B → Process C → … → Finished Goods."}]},{"n":"8.2","t":"Process losses, wastages, and gains","b":[{"p":"In many industries, process output is less than the material input — a **process loss (wastage)**, from evaporation, scrap, shrinkage, unavoidable handling, breakage, etc."},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Type","Meaning and treatment"],"rows":[["**Normal process loss**","Unavoidable, arising from the nature of the process itself, under efficient operating conditions — so it can be estimated with reasonable accuracy. It is logical and equitable to fold its cost **into the cost of good production**, since achieving good units necessarily entails this loss."],["**Abnormal process loss**","Loss **above** the normal, expected level — unpredictable, from causes like plant breakdown, inefficient working or defective materials. Charged **to the Profit and Loss Account**, not to good production: the Process Account is credited, the Abnormal Loss Account is debited, then closed to P&L."],["**Abnormal gain**","Where actual loss is **less** than the normal (estimated) loss. The Abnormal Gain Account is credited, the Process Account debited; the Abnormal Gain Account is closed to the **credit** of P&L."]]}},{"key":"Abnormal loss/gain is always **actual loss vs. normal (estimated) loss** — never actual vs. actual, and never confused with the physical difference between input and expected output."},{"eg":{"tag":"Study text","t":"Illustration 8.1 — accounting for process wastage","open":true,"q":[{"p":"TMR produces 100 units of output from 120 units of input material costing N27,000, plus conversion cost N13,000. Normal wastage was set in advance at 5% of input. Wastage can be sold at N50/kg. Find: (a) cost per unit of expected output, (b) production cost of the output achieved, (c) the cost of abnormal loss/gain."}],"a":[{"p":"Normal wastage $= 5\\% \\times 120 = 6$ units, valued at $6 \\times 50 = N300$."},{"table":{"align":"lrr","head":["","Units (kg)","Cost (N)"],"rows":[["Input (material + conversion)","120","40,000"],["Less: normal wastage (5%, at scrap value)","(6)","(300)"],["**Expected output**","**114**","**39,700**"]]}},{"tex":"\\text{(a) Cost per unit of expected output} = \\dfrac{39{,}700}{114} = N348.25"},{"tex":"\\text{(b) Production cost of output achieved} = 100 \\times 348.25 = N34{,}824.6"},{"p":"**(c)** Abnormal loss $= 114 - 100 = 14$ units (expected output that failed to materialise) $= 14 \\times 348.25 = N4{,}875.44$."}]}},{"eg":{"tag":"Study text","t":"Illustration 8.2 — the Process Account and Abnormal Wastage Account","open":true,"q":[{"p":"Using Illustration 8.1's figures, prepare the Process Account and Abnormal Wastage Account."}],"a":[{"table":{"align":"lrrlrr","head":["Dr.","Kg","N","Cr.","Kg","N"],"rows":[["Material","120","27,000","Good production","100","34,825"],["Conversion","","13,000","Normal wastage","6","300"],["","","","Abnormal wastage","14","4,875"],["**Total**","**120**","**40,000**","**Total**","**120**","**40,000**"]]}},{"table":{"align":"lrlr","head":["Dr. — Abnormal Wastage Account","N","Cr.","N"],"rows":[["Process A/c","4,875","Profit & Loss","4,875"]]}}]}},{"eg":{"tag":"Study text","t":"Illustration 8.3 — cost flowing through three sequential processes","open":true,"q":[{"p":"A product passes through Processes A, B and C; 1,000 litres were produced in the period, with no opening or closing WIP:"},{"table":{"align":"lrrr","head":["","Process A (N)","Process B (N)","Process C (N)"],"rows":[["Materials","40,000","15,000","5,000"],["Labour","20,000","25,000","15,000"],["Direct overhead","5,000","3,000","3,000"]]}},{"p":"Indirect overhead of N30,000 for the period is apportioned to the processes on the basis of wages (A 20,000, B 25,000, C 15,000 — total 60,000). Find the cost of output transferred to finished goods, and the cost per litre."}],"a":[{"p":"Indirect overhead apportioned: A $= \\tfrac{20{,}000}{60{,}000} \\times 30{,}000 = N10{,}000$; B $= \\tfrac{25{,}000}{60{,}000} \\times 30{,}000 = N12{,}500$; C $= \\tfrac{15{,}000}{60{,}000} \\times 30{,}000 = N7{,}500$."},{"table":{"align":"lrrr","head":["Cost/litre build-up","Process A","Process B","Process C"],"rows":[["Transferred in","—","75.00","130.50"],["Materials","40.00","15.00","5.00"],["Labour","20.00","25.00","15.00"],["Direct overhead","5.00","3.00","3.00"],["Indirect overhead","10.00","12.50","7.50"],["**Cost/litre transferred onward**","**75.00**","**130.50**","**161.00**"]]}},{"p":"Total cost of output transferred to finished goods $= 1{,}000 \\times N161 = N161{,}000$."},{"note":"Indirect expenses (administrative, selling, distribution — anything that cannot be directly traced to the process) must be recovered in the selling price too: the N161/litre found here is **cost**, so the selling price must exceed it for the business to earn any profit."}]}}]},{"n":"8.3","t":"Equivalent units","b":[{"p":"At period end, some units are inevitably only part-finished (WIP). Part of the period's cost belongs to these part-finished units as well as the fully completed ones — the **equivalent units** concept spreads cost equitably between them."},{"def":{"t":"Equivalent units","d":"the number of fully-completed units that a given quantity of partly-completed work represents, given its percentage of completion."}},{"tex":"\\text{Total equivalent production} = \\text{Completed units} + (\\%\\text{ complete} \\times \\text{Partly completed units})"},{"tex":"\\text{Cost per unit} = \\dfrac{\\text{Total costs}}{\\text{Total equivalent production (units)}}"},{"key":"Material, labour and overhead are usually **not** equally complete in the same WIP — material might be 80% in while labour is only 60% and overhead 50% — so each cost **element** is normally given its own equivalent-units calculation and its own cost-per-unit, and the three are added together to price one complete unit."},{"eg":{"tag":"Study text","t":"Illustration 8.4 — equivalent units by cost element","open":true,"q":[{"p":"Elsemco Shoemakers, January: materials N422,400, labour N395,600, overhead N225,000 (total N1,043,000). 8,000 units fully completed; 2,000 units WIP, 80% complete on material, 60% on labour, 50% on overhead. Find the value of completed production and of WIP."}],"a":[{"table":{"align":"lrrrrr","head":["Element","WIP equiv. units","Fully compl. units","Total equiv. production","Total cost (N)","Cost/unit (N)"],"rows":[["Material (80% of 2,000)","1,600","8,000","9,600","422,400","44"],["Labour (60% of 2,000)","1,200","8,000","9,200","395,600","43"],["Overhead (50% of 2,000)","1,000","8,000","9,000","225,000","25"],["**Total**","","","","**1,043,000**","**112**"]]}},{"tex":"\\text{Value of completed production} = 112 \\times 8{,}000 = N896{,}000"},{"tex":"\\text{Value of WIP} = \\text{Total cost} - \\text{Value of completed} = 1{,}043{,}000 - 896{,}000 = N147{,}000"},{"p":"**Cross-check** — WIP value from its own equivalent units: material $1{,}600 \\times 44 = N70{,}400$; labour $1{,}200 \\times 43 = N51{,}600$; overhead $1{,}000 \\times 25 = N25{,}000$; total $= N147{,}000$ — exactly matches."},{"key":"The output of one process is the input **material** cost of the next, and — because it has physically arrived there — is always **100% complete** by that measure the instant it enters the next process. Any brand-new material the next process itself adds must be shown as a **separate** cost element, never merged into the transferred-in cost."}]}}]},{"n":"8.4","t":"The cost of production report — weighted average and FIFO","b":[{"p":"Building a process cost-of-production report follows five stages: (1) the physical flow of units (total units to be accounted for, regardless of completion); (2) their equivalent units, usually split into transferred-in, material and conversion (labour + overhead) cost elements, since completion can differ between them; (3) total and *current-period* equivalent units for each element; (4) unit cost, per the inventory-valuation method assumed (**FIFO** or **Weighted Average Price/WAP**); and (5) the total cost of units transferred out and of closing WIP, with every cost fully accounted for."},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Method","How it treats opening WIP"],"rows":[["**Weighted Average Price (WAP)**","Blends opening WIP's cost together with the current period's cost, and spreads the combined total over the combined (opening WIP + current-period) equivalent units — it does not distinguish when cost was incurred."],["**FIFO**","Assumes opening WIP is finished and transferred out **first**. Current-period cost is divided only by **current-period** equivalent units (total equivalent units *less* the equivalent units already present in opening WIP), so the unit cost reflects only this period's own cost, not a blend with the prior period's."]]}},{"eg":{"tag":"Study text","t":"Illustration 8.5 — a full two-process cost of production report, WAP and FIFO","open":true,"q":[{"p":"Savannah Sugar Co.: Process 1 introduces raw material at the start; Process 2 adds further material only at the very **end**. Conversion cost accrues uniformly through both processes. For June:"},{"table":{"align":"lrr","head":["","Process 1","Process 2"],"rows":[["Opening WIP (units)","35,000","42,000"],["Opening WIP cost — material / transferred-in","N210,000","N343,000"],["Opening WIP cost — conversion (% complete)","N52,500 (2/5)","N392,000 (2/3)"],["Units completed in June","168,000","154,000"],["Units commenced/transferred in during June","140,000","168,000"],["Closing WIP (units, % complete on conversion)","7,000 (1/2)","56,000 (3/8)"],["Material introduced in June","N770,000","N462,000"],["Conversion cost added in June","N630,000","N2,205,000"]]}},{"p":"Prepare the cost of production report for both processes, using both WAP and FIFO, showing the cost of finished output and of closing WIP."}],"a":[{"h4":"Process 1 — Weighted Average"},{"p":"Units to account for $= 35{,}000 + 140{,}000 = 175{,}000 = 168{,}000$ completed $+ 7{,}000$ closing WIP."},{"table":{"align":"lrr","head":["","Material equiv. units","Conversion equiv. units"],"rows":[["Completed & transferred out","168,000","168,000"],["Closing WIP (100% material, ½ conversion)","7,000","3,500"],["**Total equivalent units (TEU)**","**175,000**","**171,500**"]]}},{"table":{"align":"lrrr","head":["Element","Opening WIP cost + Current cost","TEU","Cost/unit"],"rows":[["Material","210,000 + 770,000 = 980,000","175,000","N5.60"],["Conversion","52,500 + 630,000 = 682,500","171,500","N3.98"],["**Total**","**1,662,500**","","**N9.58**"]]}},{"tex":"\\text{Cost of units completed} = 168{,}000 \\times 9.58 = N1{,}609{,}440"},{"p":"Closing WIP $= (7{,}000\\times1\\times5.60) + (7{,}000\\times0.5\\times3.98) = N39{,}200 + N13{,}930 = N53{,}130$."},{"note":"Checking by the shortcut $\\text{Total cost} - \\text{Cost of completed} = 1{,}662{,}500 - 1{,}609{,}440 = N53{,}060$ instead — a N70 gap. The study text flags this itself as rounding error from carrying unit costs to only 2 decimal places (5.60 and 3.98 are themselves rounded); it is not a computational mistake to chase further, and is a useful real-world reminder that the two \"equally valid\" ways of finding closing WIP (direct computation vs. the balancing difference) can disagree slightly once rounding enters the picture."},{"h4":"Process 1 — FIFO"},{"p":"Current equivalent units (this period's own work only) $= $ TEU $-$ opening WIP's own equivalent units: material $175{,}000 - 35{,}000(100\\%) = 140{,}000$; conversion $171{,}500 - 35{,}000\\times\\tfrac25(14{,}000) = 157{,}500$."},{"table":{"align":"lrrr","head":["Element","Current cost","Current equiv. units","Cost/unit"],"rows":[["Material","770,000","140,000","N5.50"],["Conversion","630,000","157,500","N4.00"]]}},{"p":"Closing WIP $= (7{,}000\\times1\\times5.50) + (7{,}000\\times0.5\\times4.00) = N38{,}500 + N14{,}000 = N52{,}500$."},{"tex":"\\text{Cost of units completed} = \\text{Total cost} - \\text{Closing WIP} = 1{,}662{,}500 - 52{,}500 = N1{,}610{,}000"},{"h4":"Process 2 — Weighted Average"},{"p":"Because Process 2 adds its own material only at the very **end**, no unit still in WIP (not yet at the end) has received any of it — WIP's material equivalent units are always **0**, however far through conversion it is."},{"p":"Units to account for $= 42{,}000 + 168{,}000 \\text{ (transferred in)} = 210{,}000 = 154{,}000$ completed $+ 56{,}000$ closing WIP."},{"table":{"align":"lrrr","head":["","Transferred-in equiv.","Material equiv.","Conversion equiv."],"rows":[["Completed & transferred out","154,000","154,000","154,000"],["Closing WIP (100% transferred-in, 0% material, ⅜ conversion)","56,000","0","21,000"],["**TEU**","**210,000**","**154,000**","**175,000**"]]}},{"table":{"align":"lrrr","head":["Element","Opening WIP + Current cost","TEU","Cost/unit"],"rows":[["Transferred in","343,000 + 1,609,440 = 1,952,440","210,000","N9.2973"],["Material","0 + 462,000 = 462,000","154,000","N3.0000"],["Conversion","392,000 + 2,205,000 = 2,597,000","175,000","N14.8400"],["**Total**","**5,011,440**","","**N27.1373**"]]}},{"tex":"\\text{Cost of units completed} = 154{,}000 \\times 27.1373 \\approx N4{,}179{,}144.20"},{"p":"Closing WIP $= (56{,}000\\times9.2973) + 0 + (56{,}000\\times0.375\\times14.84) \\approx N520{,}648.80 + N311{,}640.00 = N832{,}288.80$ (versus the balancing figure $5{,}011{,}440 - 4{,}179{,}144.20 = N832{,}295.80$ — again a small, flagged rounding gap, this time N7, from the 4-decimal-place unit costs)."},{"h4":"Process 2 — FIFO"},{"warn":"The study text's own FIFO working for Process 2 uses **N1,609,440** as the current-period transferred-in cost — but that figure is Process 1's **WAP**-method completed-units value, not Process 1's own FIFO value of **N1,610,000** computed two paragraphs earlier in the very same illustration. This looks like a genuine authoring inconsistency in the source (reusing the WAP figure where the FIFO one belongs), not an OCR artefact — both numbers are printed clearly and repeatedly. It is reproduced below exactly as the source has it, flagged rather than silently corrected, since changing it would mean re-deriving figures the source itself never actually shows."},{"table":{"align":"lrrr","head":["Element","Current cost","Current equiv. units","Cost/unit"],"rows":[["Transferred in (as printed in the source — see warning above)","1,609,440","168,000","N9.58"],["Material","462,000","154,000","N3.00"],["Conversion","2,205,000","147,000","N15.00"],["**Total**","","","**N27.58**"]]}},{"p":"Closing WIP $= (56{,}000\\times9.58) + 0 + (56{,}000\\times0.375\\times15.00) = N536{,}480 + N315{,}000 = N851{,}480$."},{"tex":"\\text{Cost of units completed} = \\text{Total cost} - \\text{Closing WIP} = 5{,}011{,}440 - 851{,}480 = N4{,}159{,}960"},{"note":"Because this last step subtracts the closing-WIP figure from the **actual total cost pool** (which does not depend on which transferred-in figure was used earlier), the final completed-units cost of N4,159,960 still reconciles exactly with an independent build-up (below) — the flagged inconsistency affects only the intermediate per-unit-rate display, not the final answer."},{"h4":"Process 2 FIFO — cross-check by the \"opening WIP completion + newly-started-and-finished\" build-up"},{"p":"Opening WIP brought-forward cost: N343,000 + N392,000 = N735,000. Completing it this period needs its full material share ($42{,}000\\times3.00=N126{,}000$) and its remaining $\\tfrac13$ of conversion ($42{,}000\\times\\tfrac13\\times15.00=N210{,}000$). Units started **and** completed this period: $154{,}000-42{,}000=112{,}000$, costed fully at this period's own rate ($112{,}000\\times27.58 = N3{,}088{,}960$)."},{"tex":"735{,}000 + 126{,}000 + 210{,}000 + 3{,}088{,}960 = N4{,}159{,}960"},{"key":"This matches the shortcut answer exactly — confirming N4,159,960 is right regardless of the transferred-in-cost inconsistency flagged above."}]}}]},{"n":"8.5","t":"Joint products and by-products","b":[{"def":{"t":"Joint products","d":"two or more products that result simultaneously from processing a **single** raw material — e.g. refining crude oil yields petrol, kerosene and gas together."}},{"def":{"t":"Joint cost","d":"the cost of processing an input up to the point the joint products emerge — restricted to the **split-off (separation) point**, after which each product may incur its own separate processing cost. Joint cost cannot be traced to any one product; it belongs to the whole group."}},{"p":"In practice, one product is usually identified as the **main/principal product**, with the rest treated as joint products or **by-products**. A rough classification, by each product's cost/sales value **relative to the main product**:"},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Relative value","Classification"],"rows":[["10%–15% of main product's cost","**By-product**"],["15%–40% of main product's cost","**Joint product**"],["Above 40% of main product's cost","Treated as a **main product** in its own right"]]}},{"note":"This is only a guideline, not a fixed law — price changes over time can move a product between categories (a by-product can become a joint product, or even a main product, and vice versa)."},{"h4":"Accounting treatment of by-products"},{"p":"A by-product's market value, less its own separate processing cost after split-off, is usually negligible next to the joint products' or main product's total value. The standard treatment: deduct the by-product's **Net Realisable Value (NRV)** from the total joint cost, then share only the **remaining, net** joint cost among the main/joint products."},{"def":{"t":"Net Realisable Value (NRV) — for a by-product","d":"its market value less its own separate processing cost after the split-off point."}},{"eg":{"tag":"Study text","t":"Illustration 8.6 — deducting a by-product's NRV before sharing joint cost","open":true,"q":[{"p":"Wambai Shoemakers: a process yields main products A and B plus by-product C, total cost N3,000,000. There are 1,000 units of C, needing no further processing, each sellable at N60 (negligible market cost). A and B share the joint cost equally. Find A's share of the joint cost."}],"a":[{"p":"NRV of C $= 1{,}000 \\times 60 = N60{,}000$ (its market cost is negligible, so market value $\\approx$ NRV)."},{"tex":"\\text{Net Joint Cost} = 3{,}000{,}000 - 60{,}000 = N2{,}940{,}000"},{"tex":"\\text{Share of Product A} = \\dfrac{2{,}940{,}000}{2} = N1{,}470{,}000"},{"key":"Deducting a by-product's NRV from the joint cost is, in effect, treating the by-product's own share of the joint cost as **equal to its NRV** — no further, separate allocation step is needed for it."}]}}]},{"n":"8.6","t":"Methods of apportioning joint cost","b":[{"p":"Three popular ways to share joint cost among joint (or main) products:"},{"h4":"(a) Physical units basis"},{"p":"Joint cost is shared in proportion to each product's **physical quantity**, provided all products share a common unit of measurement (kg, litres). Its weakness: it ignores **value** entirely, even though value is usually the most important factor."},{"eg":{"tag":"Study text","t":"Illustration 8.7 — physical units basis","open":true,"q":[{"p":"Anadariya Ltd processes three products K (5,000kg), S (3,000kg), T (2,000kg) from one input, total joint cost N1,000,000 to split-off. Apportion by physical units and find each unit cost."}],"a":[{"table":{"align":"lrrr","head":["Product","Kg","Ratio","Share of JC (N)"],"rows":[["K","5,000","50%","500,000"],["S","3,000","30%","300,000"],["T","2,000","20%","200,000"],["**Total**","**10,000**","**100%**","**1,000,000**"]]}},{"p":"Unit cost: K $= 500{,}000/5{,}000 = N100$; S $= 300{,}000/3{,}000 = N100$; T $= 200{,}000/2{,}000 = N100$."},{"key":"Under the physical units basis, **every product ends up costed at exactly the same rate per unit** (here, N100/unit for all three) — a direct, numerical demonstration of why the method is criticised for ignoring value: it cannot, by construction, ever produce different unit costs for products with different market values."}]}},{"h4":"(b) Sales value at the point of separation"},{"p":"Joint cost is shared in proportion to each product's estimated market value at **split-off**, before any further processing. Two problems: a product might have zero value at split-off but real value after modest further processing; and a product might have a high split-off value but incur heavy selling/distribution cost afterward, which this method ignores."},{"eg":{"tag":"Study text","t":"Illustration 8.8 — sales value at split-off","open":true,"q":[{"p":"Same data, but with estimated split-off selling prices K N400/unit, S N440/unit, T N340/unit. Apportion joint cost by sales value."}],"a":[{"table":{"align":"lrrrr","head":["Product","Units","SP/unit (N)","Sales value (N)","Share of JC (N)"],"rows":[["K","5,000","400","2,000,000","500,000"],["S","3,000","440","1,320,000","330,000"],["T","2,000","340","680,000","170,000"],["**Total**","","","**4,000,000**","**1,000,000**"]]}},{"p":"Unit cost: K $= 500{,}000/5{,}000 = N100$; S $= 330{,}000/3{,}000 = N110$; T $= 170{,}000/2{,}000 = N85$ — genuinely different unit costs this time, since value now drives the split."}]}},{"h4":"(c) Net Realisable Value (NRV) basis"},{"p":"Used when further processing (or heavy marketing/distribution cost) is involved. Net realisation = sales value **less** the incremental cost of further processing after split-off and any other selling/distribution cost."},{"eg":{"tag":"Study text","t":"Illustration 8.9 — NRV basis","open":true,"q":[{"p":"Same sales values, now assumed to be market prices **after** further processing, with separate processing/marketing costs K N200,000, S N300,000, T N160,000. Apportion joint cost by NRV and find unit costs."}],"a":[{"table":{"align":"lrrrrr","head":["Product","Sales value (N)","Separate processing cost (N)","NRV (N)","Share of JC (N)","Unit cost (N)"],"rows":[["K","2,000,000","200,000","1,800,000","538,922","108"],["S","1,320,000","300,000","1,020,000","305,389","102"],["T","680,000","160,000","520,000","155,689","78"],["**Total**","**4,000,000**","","**3,340,000**","**1,000,000**",""]]}},{"p":"Each product's share of joint cost is proportional to its own NRV over the total NRV pool, e.g. K $= \\tfrac{1{,}800{,}000}{3{,}340{,}000} \\times 1{,}000{,}000 \\approx N538{,}922$."},{"key":"The NRV basis is regarded as the **best** of the three methods, because it is the only one that accounts for quantity produced, sales value **and** further processing cost, all together — the physical-units basis ignores value entirely, and the plain sales-value basis ignores further processing cost."}]}},{"h4":"Total cost determination using the NRV method"},{"p":"A joint product's **total** cost is its share of joint cost **plus** its own further processing/marketing cost — total cost ÷ units produced gives total cost per unit, which (unlike the NRV-basis unit cost above) is what should value closing inventory."},{"eg":{"tag":"Study text","t":"Continuing Illustration 8.9 — total unit cost and closing inventory valuation","open":true,"q":[{"p":"Find each product's total cost per unit, and the value of closing inventory of 900 units of K, 500 of S and 400 of T."}],"a":[{"table":{"align":"lrrrr","head":["Product","Share of JC (N)","Further processing cost (N)","Total cost (N)","Total cost/unit (N)"],"rows":[["K","538,922","200,000","738,922","147.78"],["S","305,389","300,000","605,389","201.80"],["T","155,689","160,000","315,689","157.84"]]}},{"table":{"align":"lrrr","head":["Product","Closing units","Cost/unit (N)","Value (N)"],"rows":[["K","900","147.78","133,002"],["S","500","201.80","100,900"],["T","400","157.84","63,136"],["**Total**","","","**297,038**"]]}},{"note":"Profit on a joint product is always total revenue (sales value) less **this** total cost — the ordinary economic principle of profit applies exactly the same way in joint-product costing as anywhere else."}]}}]},{"n":"8.7","t":"Non-specific order costing: service costing","b":[{"p":"Service (operation) costing applies costing principles to services rather than physical goods — hotels, transport, airlines, schools — and can also apply to a **service cost centre inside a manufacturing business** (e.g. deciding whether to run an in-house haulage department or sub-contract it, which needs a reliable in-house cost figure to compare against). The principle is the same as anywhere else: classify and aggregate every expense of providing the service, then average over the total number of service units to find cost per unit."},{"p":"A transport business's cost statement:"},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Category","Items"],"rows":[["**Fixed (standing) charges**","Motor insurance; motor licence; driver's remuneration; general administration — incurred **regardless of distance covered**."],["**Running costs**","Fuel; servicing; lubricants; repairs and maintenance."]]}},{"p":"A canteen's cost statement instead compares **actual vs. budget**, across wages/salaries (supervisor, cooks, counter helpers), provisions (meat, fish/poultry, vegetables/fruit, eggs, coffee, miscellaneous), maintenance (crockery/glassware, insurance, table linen), rent, and deducts income from sales and any company subsidy."},{"h4":"The cost unit problem"},{"p":"Service costing's central difficulty is choosing the right **cost unit** to measure output — a transport business might measure in kilometres travelled, weight carried, or a combination of both. Other examples:"},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Service","Cost unit"],"rows":[["Hotel","Occupied room-day"],["Restaurant","Meal served"],["College","Full-time equivalent student"],["Electricity","Kilowatt-hour"],["Hospital","Patient-day"],["Transport","Miles/kilometres travelled"]]}},{"h4":"Costing professional services"},{"p":"For architects, accountants, tax practitioners, medical doctors and similar professional offices, services **differ from one client to another** — unlike a standardised service organisation. This means **job costing**, not standard service costing, is the appropriate method here — each client engagement is costed as its own distinct job."}]},{"n":"8.8","t":"Worksheet summary — definitions, formulae and structure","b":[{"p":"Everything in this chapter, compressed for a last read-through."},{"h3":"Section-by-section checklist"},{"ol":["**§8.1 Process costing** — continuous/mass production; unit cost is a period average, not tied to one moment; differs from order costing chiefly in the **size of the denominator** (small for order costing, large for process costing); one process's output is the next process's material input.","**§8.2 Losses/gains** — normal loss (uncontrollable, folded into good production's cost) vs abnormal loss (charged to P&L, Process A/c credited, Abnormal Loss A/c debited) vs abnormal gain (actual loss less than normal — Abnormal Gain A/c credited, closed to P&L's credit); cost flows process-to-process with indirect overhead apportioned by an appropriate basis (e.g. wages).","**§8.3 Equivalent units** — completed units + (% complete × part-finished units), computed **separately per cost element** since material/labour/overhead completion often differ; transferred-in cost is always 100% complete on arrival.","**§8.4 Cost of production report** — WAP blends opening-WIP and current cost together over combined equivalent units; FIFO uses only *current-period* cost over *current-period* equivalent units, assuming opening WIP is finished and out first; both methods' closing-WIP figure can be cross-checked two ways (direct computation vs. total-cost-minus-completed), which can disagree slightly from rounding — not a sign of a mistake.","**§8.5 Joint products/by-products** — one raw material, multiple simultaneous products; joint cost stops at the split-off point; rough classification by relative value (<15% by-product, 15–40% joint product, >40% main product); a by-product's NRV is deducted from joint cost before splitting the remainder among the main/joint products.","**§8.6 Joint cost apportionment, 3 methods** — physical units (ignores value entirely, always gives every product the *same* unit cost); sales value at split-off (ignores further processing cost); NRV (sales value less further processing cost — the \"best\" method, since it is the only one considering quantity, value **and** further cost together); total unit cost for inventory valuation = share of joint cost + further processing cost, divided by units.","**§8.7 Service costing** — standardised, continuous services; fixed (standing) vs variable (running) cost split; the central difficulty is choosing the right cost unit; professional services (architects, accountants, doctors) use **job** costing instead, since their service varies client to client."]},{"h3":"B. Formula box"},{"fbox":{"h":"Process, equivalent-units and joint-cost formulas (recap)","rows":[{"lb":"Total equivalent production","tex":"\\text{Completed units} + (\\% \\text{ complete} \\times \\text{WIP units})"},{"lb":"Cost per equivalent unit","tex":"\\dfrac{\\text{Total cost}}{\\text{Total equivalent production}}"},{"lb":"Value of WIP (by difference)","tex":"\\text{Total cost} - \\text{Value of completed production}"},{"lb":"Net Realisable Value (by-product/joint product)","tex":"\\text{Sales value} - \\text{Separate processing/marketing cost}"},{"lb":"Net joint cost (after a by-product)","tex":"\\text{Total joint cost} - \\text{By-product NRV}"},{"lb":"Total cost per unit of a joint product","tex":"\\dfrac{\\text{Share of joint cost} + \\text{Further processing cost}}{\\text{Units produced}}"}]}}]},{"n":"8.9","t":"End-of-chapter questions (study text)","b":[{"eg":{"tag":"Study text","t":"Multiple-choice and short-answer questions, with answers","open":true,"q":[{"ol":["In process costing, what does \"Abnormal Loss\" mean? (A) Inventory items stolen during production  (B) Inventory items given out as gifts before full production  (C) The greater of the present production's loss over the previous production  (D) The greater of the actual process loss over what is deemed normal in the industry  (E) Losses in production due to abnormal behaviour of workers","Joint products are the name given to two or more (A) The main products arising from the same process  (B) Dissimilar products manufactured from the same factory  (C) Complementary products that must be sold together  (D) Raw materials mixed to manufacture a single item  (E) Products which work alike but are from different manufacturers","What is scrap? (A) Metal residue of a production process  (B) Residues of little or no value obtained during production  (C) Failed production items  (D) Cleaning materials  (E) Packaging materials","Explain \"Net Realisable Value\" in joint product costing. (A) Sales value less the cost of further processing before the point of separation  (B) Market value of each of the joint products  (C) Sales value less the cost of further processing, selling and distribution after the split-off point  (D) Sales value less cost of production up to the point of separation  (E) Sales value less commission","Which of these statements is correct as far as process costing is concerned? (A) Each process's output can be sold without further processing  (B) Only the materials introduced in process 1 are sufficient for the whole production  (C) Each manufacturing process has a normal gain  (D) Process costing is not significantly different from contract accounting  (E) The output of one process forms the input of another process","In process costing, partly completed units are calculated to represent fully completed units based on the percentage of completion. Such representation is known as ………","Where actual process wastage is less than what is regarded as normal in the industry, the difference is called …………………...","A product of lesser value incidentally produced during the manufacture of another product is known as ………………………….","The point during the production process where two or more joint products assume their own identities is called ………………...","Where is the total credit on the Abnormal Gains Account transferred to at the end of the accounting period?"]}],"a":[{"ol":["**D** — the greater of the actual process loss over what is deemed normal in the industry.","**A — the main products arising from the same process.**","**B — residues of little or no value obtained during production.**","**C** — sales value less the cost of further processing, selling and distribution **after** the split-off point.","**E — the output of one process forms the input of another process.**","**Equivalent units.**","**Abnormal gain.**","**By-product.**","**Split-off point (separation point).**","**The Profit and Loss Account.**"]}]}}]},{"n":"8.10","t":"Study text examination-type questions, worked (Q1–Q8)","b":[{"p":"The study text closes the chapter with sixteen examination-type questions. They are reproduced below with full answers, split across two sections for readability — Q1 to Q8 here, and Q9 to Q16 in §8.11."},{"eg":{"tag":"Exam type — Q1","t":"Process costing as a system of sequential processes","q":[{"p":"\"Process Costing is a cost accounting system used to determine the cost of production in a series of sequential processes.\" Discuss this comment, giving diagrammatic example(s)."}],"a":[{"p":"The comment is accurate but incomplete. Process costing is indeed used where output passes through a **series of sequential processes**, but its defining feature is not the sequence — it is that the output is **homogeneous**, so that units cannot be separately identified and costs must therefore be **averaged** over the units produced rather than traced to individual orders."},{"def":{"t":"Process costing","d":"the costing method applied where a product passes through two or more distinct processes or operations, the output of one process becoming the input of the next, and the cost per unit is found by averaging the cost of each process over its output."}},{"h4":"The sequential flow, diagrammatically"},{"pre":"Raw materials\n     |\n     v\n+-------------+   output    +-------------+   output    +-------------+   output   +----------------+\n| PROCESS 1   |------------>| PROCESS 2   |------------>| PROCESS 3   |----------->| FINISHED GOODS |\n| materials   |             | + materials |             | + materials |            +----------------+\n| + labour    |             | + labour    |             | + labour    |\n| + overhead  |             | + overhead  |             | + overhead  |\n+-------------+             +-------------+             +-------------+\n      |                           |                           |\n      v                           v                           v\n  normal loss /              normal loss /              normal loss /\n  abnormal loss              abnormal loss              abnormal loss\n  (or abnormal gain)         (or abnormal gain)         (or abnormal gain)"},{"p":"Each process has its **own account**, which is debited with the cost transferred in from the previous process plus the materials, labour and overhead added in that process, and credited with the value of normal loss scrap, any abnormal loss, and the output transferred on. The cost accumulates as the product moves along the chain, so that each unit of finished output carries the cost of every process it has been through."},{"h4":"Features of a process costing system"},{"ol":["Production is **continuous** and output is **homogeneous** — one unit cannot be distinguished from another.","Costs are collected **by process for a period**, not by job, and averaged over output.","The **output of one process becomes the input (transferred-in cost) of the next.**","There is normally **work-in-progress** at the beginning and end of each period, requiring the use of **equivalent units**.","**Normal losses** are expected and their cost is absorbed by good output; **abnormal losses and gains** are valued at full cost per unit and taken to the profit and loss account.","Some processes yield **joint products and by-products**, which require the joint cost to be apportioned."]},{"h4":"Industries where it is used"},{"ul":["chemicals, paints, pharmaceuticals and fertilisers;","oil refining;","food processing, brewing, sugar and flour milling;","cement, steel, paper and textiles;","soap and detergent manufacture."]},{"h4":"How it differs from job costing"},{"table":{"align":"lll","head":["","Job (specific order) costing","Process costing"],"rows":[["**Cost unit**","The individual job or batch","The process output for the period"],["**Output**","Heterogeneous — each job is different","Homogeneous — units are identical"],["**Cost ascertainment**","Costs **traced** to each job","Costs **averaged** over the units produced"],["**Work-in-progress**","The total of the open job cost cards","Converted to **equivalent units** before averaging"],["**Losses**","Usually insignificant and job-specific","Expected and formally accounted for as normal and abnormal"],["**Cost flow**","Each job stands alone","Cost flows from one process to the next"]]}}]}},{"eg":{"tag":"Exam type — Q2","t":"Characteristics of a good process costing system","q":[{"p":"Briefly discuss the characteristics of a good process costing system."}],"a":[{"ol":["**A separate account for every process**, so that the cost of each stage is known and each process manager can be held responsible for their own stage.","**Clear definition of the physical boundaries** of each process — where one ends and the next begins — so that costs and output are recorded against the right process.","**Accurate measurement of input and output in physical units** at every process, including losses, so that the physical units reconcile before any money is allocated.","**Predetermined normal loss percentages**, established from experience and reviewed regularly, against which actual losses can be measured to produce abnormal losses and gains.","**Proper treatment of scrap values**, credited to the process account for the normal loss and to the abnormal loss account for the abnormal element.","**A sound basis for valuing work-in-progress** using **equivalent units**, with the degree of completion assessed separately for materials, labour and overhead where they enter the process at different points.","**A consistent and stated inventory flow assumption** — weighted average or FIFO — applied uniformly from period to period.","**Correct treatment of transferred-in costs**, carried forward as a single 100%-complete input of the receiving process.","**Identification and separate treatment of joint products and by-products**, with a defensible basis for apportioning joint cost.","**Timely preparation of a cost of production report** for each process and period, showing the quantity schedule, equivalent units, cost per equivalent unit and the allocation of total cost.","**Comparison against standards or budgets**, so that the system controls as well as records.","**Integration with the financial accounts**, so that the process accounts reconcile with the general ledger (Chapter 6)."]}]}},{"eg":{"tag":"Exam type — Q3","t":"Short notes on four process costing terms","q":[{"p":"Write short notes on each of the following terms: (a) product flow; (b) work-in-progress; (c) equivalent units; (d) transferred-in costs."}],"a":[{"h4":"(a)  Product flow"},{"p":"Product flow describes the **route the product takes through the processes**. Three patterns are recognised:"},{"ol":["**Sequential flow** — every unit passes through every process in the same order, one after another (the pattern in the diagram at Q1 above). The commonest form.","**Parallel flow** — certain parts of the product are worked on simultaneously in separate processes and brought together later for final processing or assembly.","**Selective flow** — the product moves to different processes depending on the final product wanted, as in meat processing or oil refining, where one input yields several products by different routes."]},{"p":"Identifying the flow correctly matters because it determines which process accounts are opened and how cost is transferred between them."},{"h4":"(b)  Work-in-progress"},{"p":"Work-in-progress (WIP) is **partly completed output** still within a process at the beginning or end of a period. It is the central difficulty of process costing: the period's cost must be shared between units **completed** and units only **partly** completed, and a part-finished unit has not absorbed as much cost as a finished one. WIP is therefore converted into **equivalent units** before cost per unit is computed. Its degree of completion must be assessed **separately for each cost element**, because materials are often added in full at the start of a process while labour and overhead accrue evenly throughout it."},{"h4":"(c)  Equivalent units"},{"def":{"t":"Equivalent units","d":"the number of **complete** units that the work actually done would have produced, had it all been concentrated on finished units instead of spread across part-finished ones."}},{"tex":"\\text{Equivalent units} = \\text{Physical units} \\times \\text{Percentage of completion}"},{"p":"For example, 8,000 units that are 75% complete as to materials represent $8{,}000 \\times 75\\% = 6{,}000$ equivalent units of material. Converting everything to equivalent units is what makes it legitimate to divide cost by output: the denominator then measures **work done**, not merely units touched. Separate equivalent-unit figures are computed for materials, labour and overhead (or for materials and conversion cost together) whenever their completion percentages differ."},{"h4":"(d)  Transferred-in costs"},{"p":"Transferred-in cost is the **cost of the output of the preceding process**, brought into the receiving process as its opening input. In the second and every subsequent process it behaves exactly like a material added at the very start of the process, and so is always **100% complete** for every unit in that process, including units in closing work-in-progress. It must be shown as a **separate cost element** in the cost of production report, with its own equivalent units and its own cost per equivalent unit, because its completion percentage differs from that of the materials, labour and overhead added within the process itself."},{"warn":"The commonest error in a multi-process question is to treat closing WIP as partly complete for transferred-in cost. It never is: a unit cannot be physically present in Process 2 unless the whole of Process 1 has been done to it."}]}},{"eg":{"tag":"Exam type — Q4","t":"Four distinctions in process costing","q":[{"p":"Differentiate between the following: (a) actual spoilage and normal spoilage; (b) conversion cost and overhead cost; (c) material cost and transferred-in cost; (d) WAP and FIFO methods in process costing."}],"a":[{"h4":"(a)  Actual spoilage versus normal spoilage"},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Normal spoilage","Actual spoilage"],"rows":[["The spoilage **expected** to arise under efficient operating conditions, expressed as a predetermined percentage of input.","The spoilage that **actually** occurred in the period, as counted."],["**Unavoidable** and inherent in the process — evaporation, trimming, chemical change.","A **fact** observed, which may be more or less than normal."],["Its cost is **absorbed by the good output**, raising the cost per good unit; its scrap proceeds are credited to the process account.","Has no accounting treatment of its own — it is split into the normal part and the excess."],["Appears in the process account at scrap value only.","The excess over normal is **abnormal spoilage**, valued at full cost per unit and written off to the costing profit and loss account; a shortfall below normal is an **abnormal gain**."]]}},{"tex":"\\text{Abnormal loss} = \\text{Actual spoilage} - \\text{Normal spoilage} \\qquad \\text{(a negative result is an abnormal gain)}"},{"h4":"(b)  Conversion cost versus overhead cost"},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Conversion cost","Overhead cost"],"rows":[["The cost of **converting raw material into finished output**: direct labour **plus** production overhead.","All **indirect** costs: indirect materials, indirect labour and indirect expenses."],["$\\text{Conversion cost} = \\text{Direct labour} + \\text{Production overhead}$","$\\text{Overhead} = \\text{Indirect materials} + \\text{Indirect labour} + \\text{Indirect expenses}$"],["Used in process costing because labour and overhead usually accrue **at the same rate** through a process, so they can be treated as one element for equivalent-unit purposes.","A classification by **nature** (Chapter 2), used throughout costing, not only in process costing."],["**Includes** direct labour, which is not an overhead.","**Excludes** direct labour, and includes non-production overheads too."]]}},{"note":"So production overhead is **part of** conversion cost, but conversion cost is not part of overhead. The two overlap; neither contains the other."},{"h4":"(c)  Material cost versus transferred-in cost"},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Material cost","Transferred-in cost"],"rows":[["The cost of raw materials and components **added within** the process.","The accumulated cost of the output of the **previous** process, brought forward into this one."],["May be added at the start, evenly, or at a particular stage — so closing WIP may be less than 100% complete for it.","Always **100% complete** for every unit physically in the process."],["Arises in **every** process, including the first.","Arises only in the **second and subsequent** processes."],["Sourced from the stores ledger on a materials requisition.","Sourced from the preceding process account."]]}},{"h4":"(d)  Weighted average (WAP) versus FIFO"},{"table":{"align":"lll","head":["","Weighted average (WAP)","FIFO"],"rows":[["**Treatment of opening WIP**","Opening WIP cost is **merged** with the current period's cost and averaged over all output.","Opening WIP is kept **separate** and completed first; only **current** period cost is averaged."],["**Equivalent units**","Units completed (all of them) + closing WIP equivalent units.","Work **to complete** opening WIP + units started and completed + closing WIP equivalent units."],["**Cost per equivalent unit**","(Opening WIP cost + current cost) ÷ equivalent units.","Current period cost only ÷ equivalent units."],["**Output valuation**","All completed units carry the **same** cost per unit.","Units from opening WIP carry a **different** cost from units started this period."],["**Simplicity**","Simpler, and the usual choice in practice.","More complex, two-tier calculation."],["**Usefulness for control**","Weaker — last period's cost is blended into this period's unit cost, so a change in efficiency is partly hidden.","Better — the current cost per unit reflects **this period's** performance only, so it is comparable period to period."]]}},{"note":"The two give the same answer when there is **no opening work-in-progress**, because there is then nothing to merge or keep separate. They also converge when costs per unit are stable between periods."}]}},{"eg":{"tag":"Exam type — Q5","t":"Cost per kg of good production with a 4% normal wastage","q":[{"p":"A process has a normal wastage of **4%**, which has a resale value of **N50 per kg**. Find the cost per kg of good production, if the material cost is **N320,000** and the conversion cost is **N240,000** for producing **500 kg**."}],"a":[{"h4":"Step 1 — the physical units"},{"stmt":{"t":"Quantity schedule (kg)","rows":[["Input into the process","",500],["Less: normal wastage, 4% × 500 kg","",-20],["**Expected good output**","",480,"@tt"]]}},{"h4":"Step 2 — the cost to be spread over good output"},{"stmt":{"t":"Net process cost (N)","rows":[["Material cost","",320000],["Conversion cost","",240000],["**Total process cost**","",560000,"@t"],["Less: scrap value of normal wastage, 20 kg × N50","",-1000],["**Net cost to be absorbed by good output**","",559000,"@tt"]]}},{"h4":"Step 3 — cost per kg of good production"},{"tex":"\\text{Cost per kg} = \\frac{\\text{Total cost} - \\text{Scrap value of normal loss}}{\\text{Input} - \\text{Normal loss units}} = \\frac{560{,}000 - 1{,}000}{500 - 20} = \\frac{559{,}000}{480} = N1{,}164.58 \\text{ per kg}"},{"key":"Two things make this question work, and both are about the **normal** loss. First, its units are **excluded from the denominator** — the cost of the wasted material is deliberately loaded onto the good kilos, because wastage is an unavoidable cost of producing them. Second, whatever the waste **fetches as scrap** is **deducted from the numerator**, because that much of the cost has been recovered. Had the waste been worthless, the cost per kg would have been $560{,}000 \\div 480 = N1{,}166.67$; had there been no wastage at all, it would have been $560{,}000 \\div 500 = N1{,}120$."}]}},{"eg":{"tag":"Exam type — Q6","t":"ABC — a weighted average cost of production report with normal and abnormal spoilage","q":[{"p":"ABC uses process costing for its operations. The following information is given for the year ended 31 December 2008:"},{"stmt":{"t":"Quantities (units)","rows":[["Work-in-process, 1 January 2008","",4800],["Units started during the year","",19200],["Abnormal spoilage","",1392],["Units completed","",17280],["Work-in-process, 31 December 2008","",3600]]}},{"stmt":{"t":"Stages of completion of work-in-process","rows":[["WIP 1 January 2008 — material","","100%"],["WIP 1 January 2008 — conversion","","80%"],["WIP 31 December 2008 — material","","100%"],["WIP 31 December 2008 — conversion","","⅔"]]}},{"stmt":{"t":"Costs (N)","rows":[["WIP 1 January 2008 — material",18000,""],["WIP 1 January 2008 — conversion",17520,35520],["Costs incurred during the year — material",73200,""],["Costs incurred during the year — conversion",96480,169680]]}},{"p":"**Required:** using the **weighted average** pricing method, draw up a cost of production report showing the cost of completed units, abnormal spoilage and the cost of the inventory (WIP) at 31 December 2008, showing the relevant computations in detail. **Spoilage is detected at the end of operations.**"}],"a":[{"h4":"Step 1 — the quantity schedule, and the hidden normal spoilage"},{"p":"The question does not state the normal spoilage, but the physical units reveal it: everything that went in must come out somewhere."},{"stmt":{"t":"Quantity schedule (units)","rows":[["**Input**","","","@hd"],["WIP at 1 January",4800,""],["Units started during the year",19200,24000],"@gap",["**Output**","","","@hd"],["Units completed",17280,""],["Abnormal spoilage",1392,""],["WIP at 31 December",3600,22272],["**Normal spoilage (balancing figure)**","",1728,"@tt"]]}},{"note":"The 1,728 units of normal spoilage are not given — they are deduced, and the fact that the cost per equivalent unit comes out at exactly N3.80 and N5.00 afterwards confirms the deduction. Always complete the **physical** reconciliation before touching the money; a question that does not appear to balance usually contains a figure you are expected to derive."},{"h4":"Step 2 — equivalent units"},{"p":"Spoilage is detected **at the end of operations**, so spoiled units — both normal and abnormal — have passed through the whole process and are **100% complete** for both material and conversion. Closing WIP is 100% complete for material and ⅔ complete for conversion."},{"stmt":{"t":"Equivalent units — weighted average","rows":[["","Physical units","Material","Conversion","@hd"],["Units completed",17280,17280,17280],["Normal spoilage (100% complete)",1728,1728,1728],["Abnormal spoilage (100% complete)",1392,1392,1392],["Closing WIP (100% material;  ⅔ conversion)",3600,3600,2400],["**Total equivalent units**",24000,24000,22800,"@tt"]]}},{"h4":"Step 3 — cost per equivalent unit"},{"stmt":{"t":"Cost per equivalent unit (N)","rows":[["","Material","Conversion","Total","@hd"],["Cost in opening WIP",18000,17520,35520],["Cost incurred during the year",73200,96480,169680],["**Total cost**",91200,114000,205200,"@t"],["÷ equivalent units",24000,22800,""],["**Cost per equivalent unit**","**N3.80**","**N5.00**","**N8.80**","@tt"]]}},{"h4":"Step 4 — cost of production report: allocating the N205,200"},{"stmt":{"t":"Cost of production report, year ended 31 December 2008 (N)","rows":[["Units completed:  17,280 × N8.80",152064,""],["Add: normal spoilage absorbed by completed units:  1,728 × N8.80",15206,167270],"@gap",["Abnormal spoilage (to costing profit and loss):  1,392 × N8.80","",12250],"@gap",["Closing WIP — material:  3,600 × N3.80",13680,""],["Closing WIP — conversion:  2,400 × N5.00",12000,25680],"@gap",["**Total cost accounted for**","",205200,"@tt"]]}},{"stmt":{"t":"Summary of the required figures","rows":[["**Cost of completed units** (including normal spoilage) — 17,280 units","",167270],["**Cost per completed unit**  (N167,270 ÷ 17,280)","","N9.68"],["**Cost of abnormal spoilage** — written off to profit and loss","",12250],["**Cost of closing work-in-process** — 3,600 units","",25680]]}},{"key":"The critical judgement is **who bears the normal spoilage**. Because spoilage is detected at the **end** of operations, the units in closing WIP have not yet reached the inspection point, so they cannot have been found spoiled — the normal spoilage therefore attaches **entirely to the completed units**, lifting their cost from N8.80 to N9.68 each. Had inspection taken place at the *start* of the process, closing WIP would have passed it too and would have to bear its share."}]}},{"eg":{"tag":"Exam type — Q7","t":"BCB Company — two-department production reports under weighted average and FIFO","q":[{"p":"BCB Company has the following data for October:"},{"stmt":{"t":"BCB Company — October data","rows":[["","Department A","Department B","@hd"],["Beginning inventory in process","2,000 units","2,000 units"],["Transferred-in cost","—","N4,600"],["Cost of materials added last month","N2,000 (100%)","N3,200 (80%)"],["Conversion costs added last month","N900 (60%)","N800 (40%)"],["Units put in process this month",30000,29000],["Cost of materials added this month","N30,968","N56,580"],["Conversion costs added this month","N41,800","N29,803"],["Units completed and transferred",29000,30000],["Ending inventory in process","3,000 units","1,000 units"],["Ending inventory — material content","90%","50%"],["Ending inventory — conversion","50%","30%"]]}},{"p":"**Required:** **(a)** using the **weighted average** costing method, prepare a production report for October for each department, showing the cost of the ending work-in-progress and the cost of goods completed and transferred; **(b)** using the **FIFO** costing method, repeat the requirements of (a)."}],"a":[{"h4":"(a)  Weighted average — Department A"},{"stmt":{"t":"A1  Quantity schedule and equivalent units — Dept A, weighted average","rows":[["","Physical units","Material","Conversion","@hd"],["Beginning inventory",2000,"",""],["Units put in process",30000,"",""],["**To account for**",32000,"","","@t"],"@gap",["Completed and transferred",29000,29000,29000],["Ending inventory (90% material; 50% conversion)",3000,2700,1500],["**Accounted for / equivalent units**",32000,31700,30500,"@tt"]]}},{"stmt":{"t":"A2  Cost per equivalent unit — Dept A (N)","rows":[["","Material","Conversion","Total","@hd"],["Cost in beginning inventory",2000,900,2900],["Cost added this month",30968,41800,72768],["**Total cost**",32968,42700,75668,"@t"],["÷ equivalent units",31700,30500,""],["**Cost per equivalent unit**","**N1.04**","**N1.40**","**N2.44**","@tt"]]}},{"stmt":{"t":"A3  Allocation of cost — Dept A (N)","rows":[["Completed and transferred:  29,000 × N2.44","",70760],["Ending inventory — material:  2,700 × N1.04",2808,""],["Ending inventory — conversion:  1,500 × N1.40",2100,4908],["**Total cost accounted for**","",75668,"@tt"]]}},{"h4":"(a)  Weighted average — Department B"},{"p":"Department B receives the N70,760 transferred out of Department A, for 29,000 units. Transferred-in cost is **always 100% complete** for every unit in the department, including those in closing inventory."},{"stmt":{"t":"B1  Equivalent units — Dept B, weighted average","rows":[["","Physical units","Transferred-in","Material","Conversion","@hd"],["Beginning inventory",2000,"","",""],["Transferred in this month",29000,"","",""],["**To account for**",31000,"","","","@t"],"@gap",["Completed and transferred",30000,30000,30000,30000],["Ending inventory (100% t/in; 50% material; 30% conversion)",1000,1000,500,300],["**Equivalent units**",31000,31000,30500,30300,"@tt"]]}},{"stmt":{"t":"B2  Cost per equivalent unit — Dept B (N)","rows":[["","Transferred-in","Material","Conversion","Total","@hd"],["Cost in beginning inventory",4600,3200,800,8600],["Cost added / transferred in this month",70760,56580,29803,157143],["**Total cost**",75360,59780,30603,165743,"@t"],["÷ equivalent units",31000,30500,30300,""],["**Cost per equivalent unit**","**N2.4310**","**N1.9600**","**N1.0100**","**N5.4010**","@tt"]]}},{"stmt":{"t":"B3  Allocation of cost — Dept B (N)","rows":[["Completed and transferred:  30,000 × N5.4010","",162029],["Ending inventory — transferred-in:  1,000 × N2.4310",2431,""],["Ending inventory — material:  500 × N1.96",980,""],["Ending inventory — conversion:  300 × N1.01",303,3714],["**Total cost accounted for**","",165743,"@tt"]]}},{"h4":"(b)  FIFO — Department A"},{"p":"Under FIFO the opening inventory is completed **first** and kept separate. Its equivalent units are only the work **still to be done** on it: material is already 100% complete, so none; conversion is 60% complete, so the remaining 40%."},{"stmt":{"t":"A4  Equivalent units — Dept A, FIFO","rows":[["","Physical units","Material","Conversion","@hd"],["To complete beginning inventory (0% material; 40% conversion)",2000,0,800],["Started and completed this month (29,000 − 2,000)",27000,27000,27000],["Ending inventory (90% material; 50% conversion)",3000,2700,1500],["**Equivalent units**",32000,29700,29300,"@tt"]]}},{"stmt":{"t":"A5  Cost per equivalent unit — Dept A, FIFO (current cost only)","rows":[["","Material","Conversion","Total","@hd"],["Cost added this month",30968,41800,72768],["÷ equivalent units",29700,29300,""],["**Cost per equivalent unit**","**N1.0427**","**N1.4266**","**N2.4693**","@tt"]]}},{"stmt":{"t":"A6  Allocation of cost — Dept A, FIFO (N)","rows":[["**Completed and transferred**","","","@hd"],["From beginning inventory — cost brought forward",2900,""],["From beginning inventory — conversion to complete:  800 × N1.4266",1141,4041],["Started and completed:  27,000 × N2.4693","",66672],["**Total transferred out (29,000 units)**","",70713,"@t"],"@gap",["Ending inventory — material:  2,700 × N1.0427",2815,""],["Ending inventory — conversion:  1,500 × N1.4266",2140,4955],["**Total cost accounted for**","",75668,"@tt"]]}},{"h4":"(b)  FIFO — Department B"},{"p":"Department B now receives **N70,713** for 29,000 units, i.e. N2.4384 per unit transferred in."},{"stmt":{"t":"B4  Equivalent units — Dept B, FIFO","rows":[["","Physical units","Transferred-in","Material","Conversion","@hd"],["To complete beginning inventory (0% t/in; 20% material; 60% conversion)",2000,0,400,1200],["Started and completed (30,000 − 2,000)",28000,28000,28000,28000],["Ending inventory (100% t/in; 50% material; 30% conversion)",1000,1000,500,300],["**Equivalent units**",31000,29000,28900,29500,"@tt"]]}},{"stmt":{"t":"B5  Cost per equivalent unit — Dept B, FIFO (N)","rows":[["","Transferred-in","Material","Conversion","Total","@hd"],["Cost added / transferred in this month",70713,56580,29803,157096],["÷ equivalent units",29000,28900,29500,""],["**Cost per equivalent unit**","**N2.4384**","**N1.9578**","**N1.0103**","**N5.4065**","@tt"]]}},{"stmt":{"t":"B6  Allocation of cost — Dept B, FIFO (N)","rows":[["**Completed and transferred**","","","@hd"],["From beginning inventory — cost brought forward (4,600 + 3,200 + 800)",8600,""],["From beginning inventory — material to complete:  400 × N1.9578",783,""],["From beginning inventory — conversion to complete:  1,200 × N1.0103",1212,10595],["Started and completed:  28,000 × N5.4065","",151380],["**Total transferred out (30,000 units)**","",161975,"@t"],"@gap",["Ending inventory — transferred-in:  1,000 × N2.4384",2438,""],["Ending inventory — material:  500 × N1.9578",979,""],["Ending inventory — conversion:  300 × N1.0103",303,3720],["**Total cost accounted for**","",165696,"@tt"]]}},{"h4":"Comparison of the two methods"},{"stmt":{"t":"Weighted average versus FIFO (N)","rows":[["","Weighted average","FIFO","@hd"],["**Department A** — transferred out",70760,70713],["**Department A** — closing inventory",4908,4955],["**Department B** — transferred out (finished goods)",162029,161975],["**Department B** — closing inventory",3714,3720]]}},{"key":"The two methods differ by only a few naira here, because unit costs barely moved between September and October — opening inventory was already carrying very nearly this month's cost. The difference between WAP and FIFO is only ever material when **unit costs change sharply** between periods; that is precisely when FIFO is worth the extra work, because its unit cost reflects **this period alone** and so reveals the change, whereas the weighted average blends it away."},{"note":"Figures are rounded to the nearest naira, and unit rates to four decimal places, so the allocations may differ by a naira or two from an unrounded computation. In an exam, state the rounding you have used and carry rates to at least four decimal places before allocating."}]}},{"eg":{"tag":"Exam type — Q8","t":"Joint product, by-product and main product distinguished","q":[{"p":"Differentiate clearly between joint-product, by-product and main-product in a joint costing system."}],"a":[{"table":{"align":"llll","head":["","Main product","Joint products","By-products"],"rows":[["**Definition**","The **single** product which is the principal purpose of the process, where the process is operated primarily to obtain it.","**Two or more** products produced simultaneously from a common process, each of **significant** sales value, none of which can be produced without the others.","Products arising **incidentally** from a process operated to make something else, having only **minor** sales value relative to the main or joint products."],["**Relative value**","Dominant.","All substantial, and broadly comparable.","Small — the test is one of **relative sales value**, not of physical quantity."],["**Purpose of the process**","The process exists to make it.","The process exists to make them all; none is incidental.","The process does **not** exist to make them; they simply emerge."],["**Separable at will?**","n/a","**No** — they arise together, in fixed or broadly fixed proportions, up to the split-off point.","**No** — they arise unavoidably from making the main product."],["**Accounting treatment**","Bears the whole cost of the process.","The **joint cost is apportioned** between them, on physical units, sales value at split-off, or net realisable value.","Normally **no joint cost is apportioned** to them. Their net proceeds are either credited to the process account (reducing the cost of the main product) or treated as other income."],["**Examples**","Refined sugar from a sugar mill.","Petrol, diesel, kerosene and aviation fuel from crude oil; cream and skimmed milk from whole milk.","Molasses and bagasse from sugar refining; sawdust from timber; glycerine from soap manufacture."]]}},{"key":"The **split-off point** is the moment in the process at which the products become separately identifiable. Before it, the cost is **joint** and can only be apportioned by convention; after it, every cost is **separable** and is traced directly to the product that incurred it. That distinction governs the whole topic — and it is also why apportioned joint cost is **irrelevant** to any decision about what to do with a product after split-off (Chapter 9)."},{"note":"The classification is not permanent. A by-product whose market price rises sharply becomes a joint product, and a joint product whose price collapses becomes a by-product. Review the classification whenever relative values change."}]}}]},{"n":"8.11","t":"Study text examination-type questions, worked (Q9–Q16)","b":[{"eg":{"tag":"Exam type — Q9","t":"Three methods of apportioning joint cost, and the profit on a joint product","q":[{"p":"**(a)** Discuss briefly the three methods of apportioning joint costs to joint products. **(b)** Explain how a profit or loss on a joint product is arrived at."}],"a":[{"h4":"(a)  Three methods of apportioning joint cost"},{"table":{"align":"lll","head":["Method","How it works","Comment"],"rows":[["**1.  Physical units (physical measurement) basis**","The joint cost is shared in proportion to the **quantity** of each product emerging at split-off — kilos, litres, tonnes, units. Every product then bears the same cost per physical unit.","**For:** simple, objective, and defensible where products are physically similar. **Against:** it ignores value entirely, so a cheap bulky product is charged the same per kilo as a valuable one, which can make the cheap product appear to make a permanent loss. It is also impossible where the products are measured in different units (litres of gas and kilos of solid)."],["**2.  Sales value at split-off basis**","The joint cost is shared in proportion to the **sales value of the output of each product at the split-off point** (quantity produced × selling price at split-off).","**For:** relates cost to the product's ability to bear it, so no product is automatically made to look unprofitable; every product shows the same gross margin percentage. **Against:** needs a market price **at split-off**, which does not exist if the product must be processed further before it can be sold."],["**3.  Net realisable value (net realisation / reverse cost) basis**","Where products need further processing before sale, a notional split-off value is computed as **final sales value less the separable post-split-off costs**, and the joint cost is shared in proportion to those net realisable values.","**For:** the only workable value-based method when there is no market at split-off; it is the method most often required by examiners. **Against:** more computation, and the answer depends on the estimate of further processing costs."]]}},{"note":"A fourth method is sometimes met: the **constant gross margin percentage** method, which apportions joint cost so that every product shows the same gross margin percentage on final sales. And where products are measured in different units, a **weighted average (technical estimate) basis** assigns points or weights to each product to make the physical measures comparable."},{"h4":"(b)  How the profit or loss on a joint product is arrived at"},{"steps":["Take the **sales value** of the quantity of that product **actually sold** in the period.","Compute its **cost of sales**: its **apportioned share of the joint cost**, plus any **separable (further processing) costs** incurred on it after split-off, adjusted for opening and closing inventories of that product.","Deduct the cost of sales from the sales value to arrive at the product's **gross profit**.","Deduct any **selling, distribution and administrative costs** attributable to that product to arrive at its **net profit or loss**."]},{"warn":"Two cautions that carry marks. **First**, the profit shown for an individual joint product is only as meaningful as the apportionment method that produced it — change the method and the product's \"profit\" changes, although the total profit of the process is unaffected. **Second**, and more important, a reported loss on a joint product is **never on its own a reason to stop producing it**, because it cannot be stopped without also stopping the products that are profitable. The only valid decision is whether to **process it further after split-off**, and that decision compares the **incremental revenue** from further processing with the **incremental cost** — the apportioned joint cost is sunk and irrelevant to it."}]}},{"eg":{"tag":"Exam type — Q10","t":"By-product accounting, and apportioning N900 of joint cost two ways","q":[{"p":"**(a)** Briefly describe the accounting treatment of a by-product."},{"p":"**(b)** A process has two products A and B with a joint cost of **N900**. After the split-off point, Product A has **1,000 units** with a selling price of **N2 per unit** and a separate processing cost of **N600**. Product B has **500 units** with a selling price of **N1 per unit** and a separate processing cost of **N100**. Calculate the total cost of each product using (a) the physical unit basis and (b) the net realisation method."}],"a":[{"h4":"(a)  Accounting treatment of a by-product"},{"p":"Because a by-product has only minor value, it is **not** normally charged with any share of the joint cost. Four treatments are met in practice, in ascending order of refinement:"},{"ol":["**Other income.** The net proceeds of the by-product are credited to the profit and loss account as miscellaneous income, and the main product bears the whole joint cost. Simplest, but it overstates the cost of the main product.","**Credit to the process account.** The net proceeds are credited to the process from which the by-product arose, reducing the cost of the main product. This is the usual treatment.","**Net realisable value credit.** Where the by-product needs further processing or selling costs, the credit is its sales value **less** those separable costs, so that only the genuine net recovery reduces the main product's cost.","**Reverse (replacement) cost method.** Where the by-product is used internally instead of being sold, the process is credited with what it would have cost to buy that material from outside."]},{"p":"In every case the by-product is also brought into the inventory records at the value credited, so that unsold by-product is carried as an asset."},{"h4":"(b)(a)  Physical unit basis"},{"stmt":{"t":"Apportionment on physical units","rows":[["","Product A","Product B","Total","@hd"],["Units produced",1000,500,1500],["Proportion","1,000/1,500","500/1,500",""],["**Joint cost apportioned (N900)**",600,300,900,"@t"],["Add: separate processing cost after split-off",600,100,700],["**Total cost of each product**",1200,400,1600,"@tt"]]}},{"stmt":{"t":"Resulting profit — physical unit basis","rows":[["","Product A","Product B","Total","@hd"],["Sales value  (1,000 × N2;  500 × N1)",2000,500,2500],["Total cost",1200,400,1600],["**Profit**",800,100,900,"@tt"]]}},{"h4":"(b)(b)  Net realisation method"},{"stmt":{"t":"Apportionment on net realisable value","rows":[["","Product A","Product B","Total","@hd"],["Final sales value  (1,000 × N2;  500 × N1)",2000,500,2500],["Less: separate processing cost after split-off",-600,-100,-700],["**Net realisable value at split-off**",1400,400,1800,"@t"],["Proportion","1,400/1,800","400/1,800",""],["**Joint cost apportioned (N900)**",700,200,900,"@t"],["Add: separate processing cost",600,100,700],["**Total cost of each product**",1300,300,1600,"@tt"]]}},{"stmt":{"t":"Resulting profit — net realisation method","rows":[["","Product A","Product B","Total","@hd"],["Sales value",2000,500,2500],["Total cost",1300,300,1600],["**Profit**",700,200,900,"@tt"]]}},{"key":"Compare the two profit lines. The **total** profit is N900 either way — apportionment never creates or destroys profit, it only moves it between products. What changes is the **split**: A earns N800 under physical units and N700 under net realisation. Note also that under net realisation both products show exactly the same profit as a percentage of their net realisable value ($700/1{,}400 = 700/1{,}400 = 50\\%$ and $200/400 = 50\\%$), which is the method's great attraction: no product can be made to look like a loser by an accident of apportionment."}]}},{"eg":{"tag":"Exam type — Q11","t":"Products X and Y — joint cost apportioned by net realisation","q":[{"p":"A firm manufactures two products, X and Y, from the same material. In Department 1 the material is split into X and Y. **X requires no further processing**, while **Y must be processed further in Department 2**. The following costs pertain to November:"},{"stmt":{"t":"Departmental costs (N)","rows":[["Department","Direct materials","Direct labour","Factory overhead","Total","@hd"],["1",60000,15000,10000,85000],["2","—",3000,12000,15000],["**Total**",60000,18000,22000,100000,"@tt"]]}},{"stmt":{"t":"Output and sales","rows":[["Product","Kilograms sold","Kilograms produced","Sales for the period","@hd"],["X",30000,45000,"N60,000"],["Y",20000,20000,"N95,000"]]}},{"p":"There were no finished goods at the beginning of the month. **Required:** using the net realisation method to apportion the joint cost, calculate the total cost of producing X and Y during the month. Assume the selling price of X will remain constant in the immediate future."}],"a":[{"h4":"Step 1 — the selling price per kilogram"},{"p":"Sales value is given for the kilograms **sold**, but joint cost must be apportioned over the kilograms **produced**, so a price per kilogram is needed first:"},{"stmt":{"t":"Selling price per kilogram","rows":[["X:  N60,000 ÷ 30,000 kg sold","","N2.00 per kg"],["Y:  N95,000 ÷ 20,000 kg sold","","N4.75 per kg"]]}},{"note":"This is the step most candidates miss. X **produced** 45,000 kg but **sold** only 30,000 kg, so 15,000 kg went into closing inventory. The joint cost of the period relates to everything produced, not only to what was sold — so the sales value used in the apportionment must be the value of **production**, at the price per kilogram the sales reveal. This is also why the question tells you the price of X will remain constant: it entitles you to value the unsold 15,000 kg at N2."},{"h4":"Step 2 — net realisable value of each product's production"},{"stmt":{"t":"Net realisable value at split-off (N)","rows":[["","Product X","Product Y","Total","@hd"],["Kilograms produced",45000,20000,""],["× selling price per kg","N2.00","N4.75",""],["**Sales value of production**",90000,95000,185000,"@t"],["Less: further processing cost in Department 2",0,-15000,-15000],["**Net realisable value at split-off**",90000,80000,170000,"@tt"]]}},{"h4":"Step 3 — apportion the N85,000 joint cost of Department 1"},{"stmt":{"t":"Total cost of producing X and Y (N)","rows":[["","Product X","Product Y","Total","@hd"],["Proportion of net realisable value","90,000/170,000","80,000/170,000",""],["**Joint cost of Department 1 apportioned (N85,000)**",45000,40000,85000,"@t"],["Add: further processing cost, Department 2",0,15000,15000],["**Total cost of production for the month**",45000,55000,100000,"@tt"]]}},{"stmt":{"t":"Cost per kilogram produced","rows":[["X:  N45,000 ÷ 45,000 kg","","N1.00 per kg"],["Y:  N55,000 ÷ 20,000 kg","","N2.75 per kg"]]}},{"h4":"Check — the profit for the month"},{"stmt":{"t":"Profit statement for November (N)","rows":[["","Product X","Product Y","Total","@hd"],["Sales",60000,95000,155000],["Cost of production for the month",45000,55000,100000],["Less: closing inventory  (X: 15,000 kg × N1.00;  Y: nil)",-15000,0,-15000],["**Cost of sales**",30000,55000,85000,"@t"],["**Gross profit**",30000,40000,70000,"@tt"]]}},{"key":"Notice that the **total** cost apportioned (N100,000) equals the total cost incurred in both departments — apportionment redistributes, it never changes the total. And notice the role of closing inventory: of the N100,000 spent, N15,000 is carried forward in X's unsold 15,000 kg, so only N85,000 is charged against this month's sales."}]}},{"eg":{"tag":"Exam type — Q12","t":"Four refined products — cost of finished units on physical units and sales value","q":[{"p":"A firm operates a process which produces four refined products A, B, C and D. The joint costs of operating the process in December were:"},{"stmt":{"t":"Joint costs for December (N)","rows":[["Raw materials","",15000],["Labour","",4800],["Overhead","",1200],["**Total joint cost**","",21000,"@tt"]]}},{"stmt":{"t":"Output, closing finished stock and prices","rows":[["Product","Output for the period (kg)","Finished output at the end (kg)","Price per kg (N)","@hd"],["A",14000,600,0.2],["B",12000,400,0.45],["C",8000,1600,0.6],["D",2000,100,1.0]]}},{"p":"Work-in-progress at the beginning and end of the period is nil, as was finished output at the beginning of the month. The price is expected to be maintained shortly. **Required:** calculate the cost of the finished units using as the basis of calculation (a) physical units and (b) sales value."}],"a":[{"h4":"(a)  Physical units basis"},{"tex":"\\text{Joint cost per kg} = \\frac{N21{,}000}{36{,}000 \\text{ kg}} = N0.58333 \\text{ per kg}"},{"stmt":{"t":"Apportionment and the cost of closing finished stock — physical units","rows":[["Product","Output (kg)","Joint cost apportioned (N)","Finished stock (kg)","Cost of finished stock (N)","@hd"],["A",14000,8166.67,600,350.0],["B",12000,7000.0,400,233.33],["C",8000,4666.67,1600,933.33],["D",2000,1166.66,100,58.34],["**Total**",36000,21000.0,2700,1575.0,"@tt"]]}},{"h4":"(b)  Sales value basis"},{"stmt":{"t":"Sales value of production","rows":[["Product","Output (kg)","Price per kg (N)","Sales value (N)","@hd"],["A",14000,0.2,2800],["B",12000,0.45,5400],["C",8000,0.6,4800],["D",2000,1.0,2000],["**Total**",36000,"",15000,"@tt"]]}},{"stmt":{"t":"Apportionment and the cost of closing finished stock — sales value","rows":[["Product","Sales value (N)","Joint cost apportioned (N)","Cost per kg (N)","Finished stock (kg)","Cost of finished stock (N)","@hd"],["A",2800,3920,0.28,600,168],["B",5400,7560,0.63,400,252],["C",4800,6720,0.84,1600,1344],["D",2000,2800,1.4,100,140],["**Total**",15000,21000,"",2700,1904,"@tt"]]}},{"h4":"Comparison, and a warning the figures contain"},{"stmt":{"t":"Cost of the finished (closing) units compared (N)","rows":[["Product","Physical units basis","Sales value basis","@hd"],["A",350.0,168],["B",233.33,252],["C",933.33,1344],["D",58.34,140],["**Total closing inventory value**",1575.0,1904,"@tt"]]}},{"warn":"Look at the sales-value column of costs per kg against the selling prices: A costs N0.28 and sells for N0.20; C costs N0.84 and sells for N0.60; every product costs exactly **140% of its selling price**. That is not an error in the apportionment — it is a true statement about the business. The joint cost of N21,000 exceeds the total sales value of N15,000, so the **process as a whole loses N6,000**, and under the sales-value method every product shares that loss proportionately. Under the physical-units method the loss is hidden on some products and exaggerated on others (A costs N0.583 per kg against a N0.20 price, while D costs N0.583 against a N1.00 price and appears highly profitable). Neither method rescues the process; the real question the figures raise is whether it should be run at all."},{"key":"This is the strongest argument for the sales-value basis: it never makes a product look profitable or unprofitable merely because of its physical bulk. Under physical units, D — the most valuable product per kilo — appears to earn a huge margin and A appears disastrous, which would tempt management into a decision about A that is impossible anyway, since A cannot be stopped without stopping D."}]}},{"eg":{"tag":"Exam type — Q13 (ICAN Foundation, May 1997)","t":"JAY Chemicals — should the export order be accepted?","q":[{"p":"JAY Chemicals Ltd manufactures a range of industrial chemicals. Basic materials are put through an initial process which produces the ingredients for three products X, Y and Z. The ingredients for each product then undergo separate further processing to form the finished products. The budget for the year is:"},{"stmt":{"t":"Budget for the year (N)","rows":[["","Total","X","Y","Z","@hd"],["Sales (kilos)",18000,6000,6000,6000],["Sales value",540000,180000,240000,120000],"@gap",["**Joint cost apportioned**","","","","","@hd"],["Materials",120000,40000,60000,20000],["Labour",80000,30000,40000,10000],["Overheads",100000,30000,50000,20000],["**Total joint cost**",300000,100000,150000,50000,"@t"],"@gap",["**Separate further processing cost**","","","","","@hd"],["Material","",10000,20000,10000],["Labour","",10000,10000,10000],["Overhead","",20000,20000,20000],["**Total**","",40000,50000,40000,"@t"],"@gap",["**Profit**",110000,40000,40000,30000,"@tt"]]}},{"ul":["The overhead costs are **fixed**, except for the separate processing, where the **variable portion** is estimated at **N6,000 for X, N12,000 for Y and N3,000 for Z**.","The company can increase its production by **25%** of the present level without incurring additional fixed costs. Market research has shown that to sell more than the budgeted quantities **in the present market**, prices would have to be reduced to: **X N28; Y N35; Z N18**.","Although the products are jointly produced, sales of the products are independent of each other — an increase in the quantity sold of one product would not affect the sales of the others.","A customer from a neighbouring country has offered to purchase: **X 1,500 kilos @ N27 per kilo; Y 500 kilos @ N37 per kilo; Z 1,000 kilos @ N18 per kilo**.","Based on the budget for next year, direct labour will be working at **full capacity**. To meet the order the direct labour force will have to work **overtime, paid at time and a half**."]},{"p":"**Required:** evaluate whether the company should accept the order."},{"note":"The study text prints the overtime rate as \"five and a half\", which is not a rate. **Time and a half** is assumed below — the standard reading, and the only one that makes the question answerable. State the assumption."}],"a":[{"h4":"Step 1 — the principle"},{"p":"This is a **short-run acceptance decision**, so only **relevant costs** matter (Chapter 9): costs that are **future**, **incremental** and **cash**. Fixed overhead is explicitly stated not to increase, so it is irrelevant. The apportioned joint cost is irrelevant **as an apportionment** — but the joint process must actually be run harder to produce the extra output, so its **variable** elements (materials and labour) are relevant."},{"h4":"Step 2 — variable cost per kilo, before overtime"},{"stmt":{"t":"Budgeted variable costs per kilo (6,000 kg of each product)","rows":[["","X","Y","Z","@hd"],["**Joint process (variable elements only)**","","","","@hd"],["Materials  (40,000; 60,000; 20,000) ÷ 6,000",6.667,10.0,3.333],["Labour  (30,000; 40,000; 10,000) ÷ 6,000",5.0,6.667,1.667],"@gap",["**Separate further processing (variable elements only)**","","","","@hd"],["Material  (10,000; 20,000; 10,000) ÷ 6,000",1.667,3.333,1.667],["Labour  (10,000; 10,000; 10,000) ÷ 6,000",1.667,1.667,1.667],["Variable overhead  (6,000; 12,000; 3,000) ÷ 6,000",1.0,2.0,0.5],["**Total variable cost per kilo**",16.0,23.667,8.833,"@tt"]]}},{"h4":"Step 3 — the overtime premium"},{"p":"Labour is at full capacity, so every extra kilo needs overtime at time and a half — a **premium of 50%** on the labour element, both in the joint process and in the further processing:"},{"stmt":{"t":"Overtime premium per kilo (N)","rows":[["","X","Y","Z","@hd"],["Joint process labour per kilo",5.0,6.667,1.667],["Separate processing labour per kilo",1.667,1.667,1.667],["**Total labour per kilo**",6.667,8.333,3.333,"@t"],["**Overtime premium at 50%**",3.333,4.167,1.667,"@tt"]]}},{"h4":"Step 4 — evaluating the order"},{"stmt":{"t":"Incremental profit from the export order (N)","rows":[["","X","Y","Z","Total","@hd"],["Quantity offered (kilos)",1500,500,1000,3000],["Offer price per kilo",27.0,37.0,18.0,""],["Less: variable cost per kilo",16.0,23.667,8.833,""],["Less: overtime premium per kilo",3.333,4.167,1.667,""],["**Contribution per kilo**",7.667,9.166,7.5,"","@t"],["**Total contribution**",11500,4583,7500,23583,"@tt"]]}},{"h4":"Step 5 — the alternative use of the spare capacity"},{"p":"The 25% spare capacity could instead be used to sell more **in the present market** — but only by cutting the price, and the lower price would apply to the budgeted 6,000 kilos as well as to the extra:"},{"stmt":{"t":"Selling the extra output at home instead (N)","rows":[["","X (1,500 kg)","Y (500 kg)","Z (1,000 kg)","@hd"],["Budgeted price",30,40,20],["Reduced price needed to sell more",28,35,18],["Revenue at the reduced price  (7,500×28; 6,500×35; 7,000×18)",210000,227500,126000],["Less: budgeted revenue at the old price",-180000,-240000,-120000],["**Net change in revenue**",30000,-12500,6000,"@t"],["Less: extra variable cost + overtime premium",-28999,-13917,-10500],["**Net benefit (loss) of selling at home**",1001,-26417,-4500,"@tt"]]}},{"h4":"Conclusion and recommendation"},{"stmt":{"t":"Export order compared with the home-market alternative (N)","rows":[["","X","Y","Z","Total","@hd"],["Gain from accepting the export order",11500,4583,7500,23583],["Gain (loss) from cutting home prices instead",1001,-26417,-4500,-29916],["**Advantage of the export order**",10499,31000,12000,53499,"@tt"]]}},{"p":"**Recommendation: accept the order.** It increases profit by **N23,583** — none of the fixed overhead changes, and every product's offer price comfortably exceeds its incremental cost, even after the overtime premium. It is also better than the only alternative use of the capacity in all three cases, decisively so for Y and Z, where cutting the home price to shift the extra output would actually destroy profit."},{"warn":"Three qualifications a full answer should state. **(1)** The products are **jointly produced**: running the joint process 25% harder yields 1,500 extra kilos of **each** product, but the order takes only 500 of Y and 1,000 of Z, so there would be surplus Y and Z to dispose of. The question's statement that sales are independent is taken as licence to evaluate each product separately, but in practice the surplus must be sold, stored or dumped, and its disposal value would need to be brought in. **(2)** Selling abroad at N27 while the home price is N30 risks the discounted goods finding their way back into the home market, and may provoke dumping complaints. **(3)** Accepting at these prices may make it harder to charge full prices to this customer, or to others, in future."}]}},{"eg":{"tag":"Exam type — Q14 (ICAN PE I, November)","t":"Okoro Ltd — unit sales prices under two overhead absorption bases","q":[{"p":"Okoro Ltd is deciding the sales price for two products, **wids** and **leads**. Both are made by the same workforce in the same department. **N30,000 direct labour hours** are budgeted for the year. Budgeted **fixed costs are N30,000**, and the department is expected to operate at **full capacity**. Variable costs per unit are:"},{"stmt":{"t":"Variable cost per unit (N)","rows":[["","Wids","Leads","@hd"],["Materials",4,4],["Labour  (wids 2 hours;  leads 3 hours, at N3 per hour)",6,9],["Expenses  (1 machine hour each, at N2 per machine hour)",2,2],["**Total variable cost per unit**",12,15,"@tt"]]}},{"p":"Expected sales demand is roughly **7,500 wids** and **5,000 leads**. **Required:** what would the unit sales prices be to give a profit of **20% on full cost** if overheads are absorbed (a) on a direct labour hour basis, and (b) on a machine hour basis?"},{"warn":"The variable cost table is mis-typeset in the study text: its three figures for wids are printed in the order 4, 2, 6 while the underlining shows 6 as the last addend, and the hour labels sit on the wrong rows. The reading above is the only one that is internally consistent — it gives a **single labour rate of N3 per hour for both products** (the question says they are made by the same workforce), totals of N12 and N15 exactly as printed, and a labour requirement of $7{,}500 \\times 2 + 5{,}000 \\times 3 = 30{,}000$ hours, which is precisely the budgeted capacity the question states.","lbl":"Source data reconstructed — and confirmed"}],"a":[{"h4":"Step 1 — the activity levels"},{"stmt":{"t":"Budgeted activity","rows":[["","Wids","Leads","Total","@hd"],["Expected demand (units)",7500,5000,""],["Direct labour hours per unit",2,3,""],["**Total direct labour hours**",15000,15000,30000,"@t"],["Machine hours per unit",1,1,""],["**Total machine hours**",7500,5000,12500,"@t"]]}},{"note":"The direct labour hours come to exactly the 30,000 budgeted, confirming the statement that the department operates at full capacity — a useful check that the reconstructed data are right."},{"h4":"Step 2 — the two absorption rates"},{"stmt":{"t":"Fixed overhead absorption rates","rows":[["(a) Direct labour hour basis:  N30,000 ÷ 30,000 hours","","**N1.00 per labour hour**"],["(b) Machine hour basis:  N30,000 ÷ 12,500 hours","","**N2.40 per machine hour**"]]}},{"h4":"(a)  Prices on a direct labour hour basis"},{"stmt":{"t":"Unit price — overhead absorbed on direct labour hours (N)","rows":[["","Wids","Leads","@hd"],["Variable cost per unit",12.0,15.0],["Fixed overhead absorbed  (2 hrs × N1.00;  3 hrs × N1.00)",2.0,3.0],["**Full cost per unit**",14.0,18.0,"@t"],["Add: profit at 20% of full cost",2.8,3.6],["**Unit sales price**",16.8,21.6,"@tt"]]}},{"h4":"(b)  Prices on a machine hour basis"},{"stmt":{"t":"Unit price — overhead absorbed on machine hours (N)","rows":[["","Wids","Leads","@hd"],["Variable cost per unit",12.0,15.0],["Fixed overhead absorbed  (1 hr × N2.40;  1 hr × N2.40)",2.4,2.4],["**Full cost per unit**",14.4,17.4,"@t"],["Add: profit at 20% of full cost",2.88,3.48],["**Unit sales price**",17.28,20.88,"@tt"]]}},{"h4":"Check — total profit is the same either way"},{"stmt":{"t":"Total profit under each basis (N)","rows":[["","Labour hour basis","Machine hour basis","@hd"],["Wids:  7,500 × (16.80 − 12.00);  7,500 × (17.28 − 12.00)",36000,39600],["Leads: 5,000 × (21.60 − 15.00);  5,000 × (20.88 − 15.00)",33000,29400],["**Total contribution**",69000,69000,"@t"],["Less: fixed costs",-30000,-30000],["**Profit**",39000,39000,"@tt"]]}},{"key":"The same N39,000 profit either way — which is the whole point. Changing the absorption base cannot change the total profit; it only changes **which product is asked to earn it**. On a labour hour basis, leads (3 hours each) carry more overhead and are priced at N21.60; on a machine hour basis, where both products use one machine hour, leads carry the same overhead as wids and the price falls to N20.88 while wids rise to N17.28. Which base is right depends entirely on whether the N30,000 of fixed cost is actually driven by labour time or by machine time — and that is a question of fact about the department, not of accounting preference."}]}},{"eg":{"tag":"Exam type — Q15 (12½ marks)","t":"MMM Ltd — equivalent production, cost per unit, evaluation and the Process I account","q":[{"p":"**(a)** Summarise what you understand by **abnormal loss**, and show how it is treated in process costing."},{"p":"**(b)** The data below relate to Process I for January 2017, as extracted from the books of MMM Ltd."},{"stmt":{"t":"Process I — January 2017","rows":[["2 January 2017:  Input","","40,000 units"],["                      Opening WIP","","Nil"],"@gap",["**Process costs (N)**","","","@hd"],["Material","",1140000],["Labour","",633600],["Overhead","",528000],"@gap",["**31 January 2017 (units)**","","","@hd"],["Output","",32000],["Closing WIP","",8000],"@gap",["**Degree of completion of closing WIP**","","","@hd"],["Materials","","75%"],["Labour","","40%"],["Overhead","","40%"]]}},{"p":"**Required:** determine (i) equivalent production units; (ii) cost per unit of equivalent production; (iii) the statement of evaluation; (iv) the Process I account."}],"a":[{"h4":"(a)  Abnormal loss and its treatment"},{"def":{"t":"Abnormal loss","d":"the part of a process loss that **exceeds the normal loss** — loss that was not expected under efficient operating conditions and is therefore avoidable."}},{"tex":"\\text{Abnormal loss} = \\text{Actual loss} - \\text{Normal loss}"},{"p":"**Its treatment in process costing:**"},{"ol":["It is **valued at the full cost per good unit** of the process — the same rate as good output — because it consumed exactly the same materials, labour and overhead as a good unit did.","It is **credited to the process account** (in both units and value) and **debited to an Abnormal Loss Account**, so that it is removed from the cost of good production.","Any **scrap proceeds** from the abnormally lost units are **credited to the Abnormal Loss Account**, not to the process account.","The **balance of the Abnormal Loss Account is written off to the costing profit and loss account** as a period loss.","The effect — and the purpose — is that the cost of good production is **not inflated** by inefficiency: good units bear only normal loss, while abnormal loss is separately reported so management can see it and investigate.","The mirror image is the **abnormal gain**, which arises when actual loss is *less* than normal. It is debited to the process account and credited to an Abnormal Gain Account, which is then charged with the scrap income foregone and the balance credited to profit and loss."]},{"h4":"(b)(i)  Equivalent production units"},{"stmt":{"t":"Statement of equivalent production","rows":[["","Physical units","Materials","Labour","Overhead","@hd"],["Output completed and transferred",32000,32000,32000,32000],["Closing WIP  (75% materials;  40% labour;  40% overhead)",8000,6000,3200,3200],["**Equivalent production units**",40000,38000,35200,35200,"@tt"]]}},{"h4":"(b)(ii)  Cost per unit of equivalent production"},{"stmt":{"t":"Statement of cost per equivalent unit","rows":[["","Cost (N)","Equivalent units","Cost per unit (N)","@hd"],["Materials",1140000,38000,30],["Labour",633600,35200,18],["Overhead",528000,35200,15],["**Total**",2301600,"",63,"@tt"]]}},{"h4":"(b)(iii)  Statement of evaluation"},{"stmt":{"t":"Statement of evaluation (N)","rows":[["**Finished output transferred:  32,000 units × N63**","",2016000],"@gap",["**Closing work-in-progress**","","","@hd"],["Materials:  6,000 equivalent units × N30",180000,""],["Labour:  3,200 equivalent units × N18",57600,""],["Overhead:  3,200 equivalent units × N15",48000,285600],"@gap",["**Total cost accounted for**","",2301600,"@tt"]]}},{"h4":"(b)(iv)  Process I Account"},{"tacc":{"t":"Process I Account — January 2017","drl":"Dr  (units / N)","crl":"Cr  (units / N)","dr":[["Material  (40,000 units)",1140000],["Labour",633600],["Overhead",528000],["",2301600,"@tot"]],"cr":[["Transfer to Process II  (32,000 units)",2016000],["Closing work-in-progress c/d  (8,000 units)",285600],["",2301600,"@tot"]]}},{"key":"There is no normal or abnormal loss in this question — 40,000 units in, 40,000 accounted for — so the whole difficulty is the **equivalent units**. Note that the three cost elements have three **different** completion percentages and therefore three different denominators (38,000, 35,200, 35,200): materials are 75% complete while labour and overhead are only 40%. Using one denominator for all three is the single commonest error in this topic, and it would give a cost per unit of $2{,}301{,}600 \\div 38{,}000 = N60.57$ instead of the correct N63."}]}},{"eg":{"tag":"Exam type — Q16 (12½ marks)","t":"Tamco Limited — two process accounts with an abnormal loss and an abnormal gain","q":[{"p":"Tamco Limited manufactures the product TACO, which passes through two distinct processes before it is turned out as a finished product."},{"stmt":{"t":"Input details (N)","rows":[["Item","Process L","Process M","Total","@hd"],["Direct materials",400000,158000,558000],["Direct wages",200000,80000,280000],["Production overhead",110000,90000,200000]]}},{"p":"**20,000 units @ N3** were introduced to Process L. The output of Process L passes to Process M, where finished goods are obtained and forwarded to the warehouse."},{"stmt":{"t":"Output, normal loss and scrap","rows":[["","Actual output (units)","Normal loss as % of input","Total scrap realised (N)","@hd"],["Process L",18900,"5%",10000],["Process M",18000,"5%",6700]]}},{"p":"**Required:** prepare (a) the process accounts; and (b) the process losses and process gain accounts."}],"a":[{"h4":"Step 1 — Process L: the loss position"},{"stmt":{"t":"Process L — physical units","rows":[["Input introduced","","20,000 units"],["Normal loss:  5% × 20,000","","1,000 units"],["**Expected output**","","19,000 units","@t"],["Actual output","","18,900 units"],["**Abnormal loss**  (19,000 − 18,900)","","100 units","@tt"]]}},{"stmt":{"t":"Process L — cost per unit","rows":[["Units introduced:  20,000 × N3","",60000],["Direct materials","",400000],["Direct wages","",200000],["Production overhead","",110000],["**Total cost of Process L**","",770000,"@t"],["Less: scrap value of normal loss","",-10000],["**Net cost**","",760000,"@t"],["÷ expected output (20,000 − 1,000)","","19,000 units"],["**Cost per unit**","","N40.00","@tt"]]}},{"note":"Scrap realises $N10{,}000 \\div 1{,}000 = N10$ **per unit**, which is the rate used to credit the abnormally lost units with their scrap proceeds below."},{"h4":"(a)  Process L Account"},{"tacc":{"t":"Process L Account","drl":"Dr  (N)","crl":"Cr  (N)","dr":[["Units introduced — 20,000 @ N3",60000],["Direct materials",400000],["Direct wages",200000],["Production overhead",110000],["",770000,"@tot"]],"cr":[["Normal loss — 1,000 units @ N10 scrap",10000],["Abnormal loss — 100 units @ N40",4000],["Transfer to Process M — 18,900 units @ N40",756000],["",770000,"@tot"]]}},{"h4":"Step 2 — Process M: the loss position"},{"stmt":{"t":"Process M — physical units","rows":[["Input transferred from Process L","","18,900 units"],["Normal loss:  5% × 18,900","","945 units"],["**Expected output**","","17,955 units","@t"],["Actual output","","18,000 units"],["**Abnormal GAIN**  (18,000 − 17,955)","","45 units","@tt"]]}},{"stmt":{"t":"Process M — cost per unit","rows":[["Transferred in from Process L — 18,900 units","",756000],["Direct materials","",158000],["Direct wages","",80000],["Production overhead","",90000],["**Total cost of Process M**","",1084000,"@t"],["Less: scrap value of normal loss","",-6700],["**Net cost**","",1077300,"@t"],["÷ expected output (18,900 − 945)","","17,955 units"],["**Cost per unit**","","N60.00","@tt"]]}},{"h4":"(a)  Process M Account"},{"tacc":{"t":"Process M Account","drl":"Dr  (N)","crl":"Cr  (N)","dr":[["Transfer from Process L — 18,900 units",756000],["Direct materials",158000],["Direct wages",80000],["Production overhead",90000],["Abnormal gain — 45 units @ N60",2700],["",1086700,"@tot"]],"cr":[["Normal loss — 945 units @ N7.0899 scrap",6700],["Finished goods — 18,000 units @ N60",1080000],["",1086700,"@tot"]]}},{"warn":"An **abnormal gain** is entered on the **debit** side of the process account (and in the units column), which is the opposite side from an abnormal loss. The logic: the process produced more good units than it should have, so the account must be grossed up to carry them at the normal cost per unit. The corresponding credit goes to the Abnormal Gain Account."},{"h4":"(b)  Abnormal Loss Account (Process L)"},{"tacc":{"t":"Abnormal Loss Account","drl":"Dr  (N)","crl":"Cr  (N)","dr":[["Process L — 100 units @ N40",4000],["",4000,"@tot"]],"cr":[["Cash / scrap — 100 units @ N10",1000],["Costing Profit and Loss Account",3000],["",4000,"@tot"]]}},{"h4":"(b)  Abnormal Gain Account (Process M)"},{"tacc":{"t":"Abnormal Gain Account","drl":"Dr  (N)","crl":"Cr  (N)","dr":[["Normal loss (scrap) account — 45 units @ N7.0899",319.05],["Costing Profit and Loss Account",2380.95],["",2700.0,"@tot"]],"cr":[["Process M — 45 units @ N60",2700.0],["",2700.0,"@tot"]]}},{"h4":"(b)  Normal Loss (Scrap) Account"},{"tacc":{"t":"Normal Loss / Scrap Account","drl":"Dr  (N)","crl":"Cr  (N)","dr":[["Process L — 1,000 units",10000.0],["Process M — 945 units",6700.0],["",16700.0,"@tot"]],"cr":[["Abnormal gain — scrap forgone, 45 units @ N7.0899",319.05],["Cash — scrap actually sold",16380.95],["",16700.0,"@tot"]]}},{"key":"Note the scrap adjustment on the abnormal gain, which candidates routinely omit. Because Process M produced 45 units **more** than expected, it also produced 45 units **less** scrap than expected — so the scrap income credited to the process as normal loss was never actually earned. The Abnormal Gain Account is therefore charged with that forgone scrap (45 × N7.0899 = N319.05), and only the net N2,380.95 is credited to profit. The mirror of this is the Abnormal Loss Account, which is **credited** with the scrap the extra lost units did realise."},{"note":"Both process accounts balance exactly, and the cost per unit comes out at a round N40 and N60 — the strongest possible confirmation that the normal loss percentages, scrap values and transfers have all been read correctly from a question whose data table was printed with its columns out of alignment."}]}}]}],"formulas":[{"lb":"Total equivalent production","tex":"\\text{Completed units} + (\\% \\text{ complete} \\times \\text{WIP units})"},{"lb":"Cost per equivalent unit","tex":"\\dfrac{\\text{Total cost}}{\\text{Total equivalent production}}"},{"lb":"Value of WIP (balancing figure)","tex":"\\text{Total cost} - \\text{Value of completed production}"},{"lb":"Net Realisable Value","tex":"\\text{Sales value} - \\text{Separate processing/marketing cost}"},{"lb":"Net joint cost, after a by-product","tex":"\\text{Total joint cost} - \\text{By-product NRV}"},{"lb":"Total cost per unit of a joint product","tex":"\\dfrac{\\text{Share of joint cost} + \\text{Further processing cost}}{\\text{Units produced}}"}],"focus":"Process costing with equivalent units, and joint-cost apportionment, are together one of the most heavily-weighted topics in the whole syllabus — expect at least one full Section B question most diets. Master the WAP/FIFO distinction as \"does this method blend opening WIP cost with current cost, or keep them separate?\" rather than memorising two unrelated procedures — every other difference between them follows from that one question. For joint products, always compute a product's *total* cost (share of joint cost plus further processing) before using it to value inventory — the NRV-basis apportionment figure alone is not yet a full unit cost.","errors":["Confusing normal loss (folded into good production's cost) with abnormal loss (charged straight to P&L) — this is the single most common error in process-costing questions.","Using total equivalent units instead of *current-period* equivalent units when finding a FIFO unit cost — FIFO divides only by the units' worth of work actually done *this* period.","Forgetting that a cost element added only at the *end* of a process (like Process 2's material in Illustration 8.5) has **zero** equivalent units in any closing WIP, however far advanced that WIP is on conversion.","Treating the physical-units basis of joint-cost apportionment as if it reflects value — by construction, it gives every joint product exactly the same cost per unit.","Valuing joint-product closing inventory at the NRV-basis apportionment figure alone, without adding the product's own further processing cost to get its *total* cost per unit first."],"quiz":{"mcq":[{"q":"In process costing, the cost of a *normal* process loss is","o":["charged directly to the Profit and Loss Account","ignored entirely","included as part of the cost of good production","carried forward indefinitely as work-in-progress","shared equally between all other processes"],"a":2,"w":"Normal loss is unavoidable and arises under efficient conditions, so its cost is folded into the cost of good production — unlike abnormal loss, which is charged to the P&L.","src":"Chapter 8.2","sec":"8.2"},{"q":"A process has an input of 200 units, with normal wastage set at 10% of input. Expected output is therefore","o":["200 units","190 units","180 units","20 units","220 units"],"a":1,"w":"Normal wastage = 10% × 200 = 20 units, so expected output = input − normal wastage = 200 − 20 = 180 units.","calc":"200 - (10\\% \\times 200) = 180","src":"Chapter 8.2","sec":"8.2"},{"q":"Actual output from a process is 95 units, against an expected output (after normal wastage) of 90 units. This 5-unit difference is","o":["an abnormal loss","an abnormal gain","a normal loss","a joint product","a by-product"],"a":1,"w":"Where actual output exceeds expected output (i.e. actual loss is less than normal loss), the difference is an abnormal gain.","src":"Chapter 8.2","sec":"8.2"},{"q":"A period has 6,000 completed units and 1,000 units of closing WIP that are 40% complete. Total equivalent production is","o":["6,000 units","6,400 units","7,000 units","400 units","6,040 units"],"a":1,"w":"Total equivalent production = completed units + (% complete × WIP units) = 6,000 + (0.4 × 1,000) = 6,400 units.","calc":"6{,}000 + (0.4 \\times 1{,}000) = 6{,}400","src":"Chapter 8.3","sec":"8.3"},{"q":"Compared with the FIFO method, the weighted average method of process costing","o":["ignores opening work-in-progress entirely","blends opening WIP cost together with current-period cost, over combined equivalent units","can only be used where there is no closing WIP","is identical to FIFO in every respect","is used only for joint products"],"a":1,"w":"WAP blends opening WIP's cost with the current period's cost and spreads the total over combined equivalent units; FIFO keeps only current-period cost over current-period equivalent units, treating opening WIP as completed and out first.","src":"Chapter 8.4","sec":"8.4"},{"q":"A cost element is added only at the very end of a process. For a unit of closing WIP that is 70% complete on conversion but has not yet reached the end of the process, the equivalent units for that end-of-process cost element are","o":["70% of the unit","100% of the unit","0% of the unit","30% of the unit","undefined"],"a":2,"w":"If the cost element is added only at the very end, no WIP unit that has not reached the end has received any of it yet — its equivalent units for that element are 0%, regardless of how advanced it is on any other element.","src":"Chapter 8.4","sec":"8.4"},{"q":"Under the physical units basis of joint-cost apportionment, different joint products will end up with","o":["different unit costs, reflecting their different market values","exactly the same cost per physical unit, regardless of value","zero joint cost apportioned to them","costs based only on their selling price","costs based only on further processing cost"],"a":1,"w":"Because physical units basis shares joint cost purely by quantity, every joint product ends up costed at exactly the same rate per unit — a direct consequence of ignoring value.","src":"Chapter 8.6","sec":"8.6"},{"q":"The method of apportioning joint cost regarded as the \"best,\" because it accounts for quantity, sales value and further processing cost together, is the","o":["physical units basis","sales value at split-off basis","Net Realisable Value (NRV) basis","blanket rate basis","standard costing basis"],"a":2,"w":"The NRV basis is the only one of the three that considers quantity produced, sales value, and further processing cost all together.","src":"Chapter 8.6","sec":"8.6"},{"q":"A by-product's Net Realisable Value is deducted from the total joint cost before the remaining cost is shared among the main/joint products. This treatment effectively","o":["charges the by-product nothing at all","assigns the by-product a joint cost equal to its own NRV","assigns the by-product the entire joint cost","converts the by-product into a main product automatically","has no effect on the main products' costs"],"a":1,"w":"Deducting the by-product's NRV from joint cost is, in effect, assigning the by-product a share of joint cost exactly equal to its own NRV — no further allocation step is needed for it.","src":"Chapter 8.5","sec":"8.5"},{"q":"For professional-service organisations such as architects' or accountants' offices, where services genuinely differ from one client to the next, the appropriate costing method is","o":["standard service (operation) costing","process costing","job costing","joint-product costing","blanket-rate costing"],"a":2,"w":"Since each client engagement differs, job costing — costing each engagement as its own distinct job — is the appropriate method, not standardised service costing.","src":"Chapter 8.7","sec":"8.7"}],"theory":[{"q":"Differentiate between: (a) Actual spoilage and normal spoilage, (b) Conversion cost and overhead cost, (c) Material cost and transferred-in cost, (d) WAP and FIFO methods in process costing.","marks":12,"a":[{"p":"**(a)** Normal spoilage is the *expected*, unavoidable loss under efficient operating conditions, estimated in advance. Actual spoilage is what genuinely occurs in the period — the difference between the two (if actual exceeds normal) is abnormal loss, or (if normal exceeds actual) abnormal gain."},{"p":"**(b)** Conversion cost is direct labour plus production overhead together — the cost of turning material into a finished product. Overhead cost is only the indirect-cost component of that, a narrower category than conversion cost."},{"p":"**(c)** Material cost is the cost of raw material added *within* a given process. Transferred-in cost is the cost of output *received from a previous process*, which becomes that process's own input material — always 100% complete by definition, unlike ordinary material which may be only partly added to WIP."},{"p":"**(d)** WAP blends opening WIP cost with current-period cost and divides by combined equivalent units, giving one blended unit cost. FIFO keeps opening WIP's cost separate, dividing only current-period cost by current-period equivalent units, and treats opening WIP as completed and transferred out first."}],"src":"Chapter 8.4","sec":"8.4"},{"q":"Differentiate clearly between joint product, by-product and main product in a joint costing system.","marks":8,"a":[{"p":"All three arise from processing the same raw material to a common split-off point. The **main product** is the one identified as the primary purpose of the process (e.g. petrol, from refining crude oil) — typically representing more than 40% of the main product's own value by the study text's guideline (i.e., it *is* the reference point). A **joint product** is a genuinely significant co-product, roughly 15–40% of the main product's value, sold as a real product in its own right. A **by-product** is a minor, incidental output of lesser value (roughly 10–15% or less of the main product's value) — its NRV is simply deducted from joint cost rather than being apportioned a full separate share."}],"src":"Chapter 8.5","sec":"8.5"}]}},{"n":9,"t":"Costing Techniques: Marginal Costing and Decision Making","brief":"Marginal costing versus absorption costing — their concepts, the fundamental differences between the income statements each produces, and the marginal-costing equation (S − V = C, C − FC = P). Contribution as the central decision-making tool. Relevant cost and relevant revenue, and sunk cost. The four classic short-term decisions marginal costing supports — make or buy, accept/reject a special order, pricing, and profit planning — each with fully verified worked illustrations.","outcomes":["Explain the concepts and differences between marginal and absorption costing","Explain the equation, advantages and limitations of marginal costing","Discuss the importance of contribution margin in managerial decision making","Use marginal costing as a technique for short-term tactical planning and decision-making","Make decisions on whether to make or buy a component, accept or reject an order, and similar short-term choices"],"secs":[{"n":"9.1","t":"Marginal costing versus absorption costing","b":[{"p":"Two of the most popular decision-making techniques in accounting are **marginal costing** and **absorption costing** — both meant for short-term tactical managerial decisions about the future, which is inherently uncertain, so both must be applied with care."},{"note":"\"Marginal costing\" is the term common in the UK and Europe; the US prefers \"direct costing\" or \"variable costing\" — different names for essentially the same idea."},{"def":{"t":"Marginal cost","d":"the cost that arises from producing an additional increment of output, and does not arise if that increment is not produced — the cost of one additional unit of product or service. In the short run, marginal cost is synonymous with variable cost (prime cost + variable overheads)."}},{"key":"Marginal costing is **not** a costing system in the way process or job costing are — it is an **approach to presenting** accounting information, for judging profitability by studying costs according to their nature (fixed vs variable), not a method of first ascertaining cost. It formalises the ideas behind flexible budgets, break-even analysis and Cost-Volume-Profit relationships."},{"p":"Marginal costing treats only costs that vary directly with output as **product costs** — the opposite of conventional (absorption) costing, which treats *all* manufacturing cost, fixed or variable, as product cost."},{"def":{"t":"Absorption costing","d":"the technique used to prepare income statements that ascertain the results of operations, by \"absorbing\" a fair share of general (fixed) overhead into every unit produced or serviced, so every indirect cost is properly apportioned into total unit cost."}},{"h4":"The three fundamental differences, in the income statements each produces"},{"table":{"align":"lll","head":["Difference","Absorption costing","Marginal costing"],"rows":[["**Treatment of fixed production overhead**","Part of cost of goods sold","A **period cost**, incurred whether or not production happens, and never part of a product's own cost"],["**Cost of closing inventory**","Total (fixed + variable) cost per unit × closing units","Only the **variable** cost per unit × closing units — fixed costs are assumed already covered by contribution"],["**First profit line shown**","Gross profit (then net profit)","**Contribution margin** (sales less total variable cost) first, then profit"]]}},{"warn":"Except where every unit produced is also sold in the same period, the two methods will **not** show the same profit at the first reporting stage — because they value closing inventory differently, differing amounts of fixed cost get carried forward into the next period under each method."},{"p":"Absorption costing is what all three forms of business (sole proprietorship, partnership, company) and government bodies use for end-of-period income statements — it aids performance evaluation of past results. Marginal costing is relevant only for **short-term tactical decisions**, focused on the future."},{"h4":"The marginal costing equation"},{"p":"Marginal costing hinges on **contribution** — a product's contribution toward fixed costs and profit."},{"fbox":{"h":"The marginal costing equation","rows":[{"lb":"Contribution","tex":"S - V = C","nt":"S = Sales, V = Variable costs, C = Contribution."},{"lb":"Profit","tex":"C - FC = P","nt":"FC = Fixed costs, P = Profit."},{"lb":"Sales at break-even point","tex":"\\text{BEP Sales} = \\dfrac{\\text{Fixed Cost} \\times \\text{Sales}}{\\text{Sales} - \\text{Variable costs}}"}]}},{"key":"Given any **three** of Sales, Variable cost, Fixed cost and Profit, the equation lets you find the fourth — this is why it is described as of \"fundamental importance\": most marginal-costing exam questions are really this equation, rearranged."},{"h4":"Advantages of marginal costing"},{"ol":["**constant in nature** — marginal cost per unit stays the same regardless of production volume;","**facilitates cost control** — the clean fixed/variable split enables flexible budgeting;","**simplicity of overhead treatment** — no need for fixed-overhead allocation, apportionment or absorption, removing the whole under-/over-absorption complication;","**basis for pricing and tendering** — gives a clearer, more logical basis for setting prices, especially when tendering during a slow period;","**aid to profit planning** — presents data showing cost-volume-profit relationships directly; and","**more realistic inventory valuation** — finished goods/WIP at marginal cost only, avoiding the arbitrariness of a fixed-overhead allotment."]},{"h4":"Limitations of marginal costing"},{"ol":["**difficulty in analysis** — splitting overheads into fixed and variable components is genuinely hard in practice;","**lop-sided emphasis** — tends to over-weight the selling function relative to production, though both matter equally to overall efficiency;","**difficulty in application** — poorly suited to industries needing large WIP inventories;","**limited scope** — hard to apply in capital-intensive industries with very large fixed costs; and","**inappropriate basis for pricing on its own** — selling price cannot reasonably be set on contribution alone, without regard to fixed costs at all."]},{"eg":{"tag":"Study text","t":"Illustration 9.1 — comparative income statements","open":true,"q":[{"p":"Green-Green Ltd, year ended 31 December 2025: sales 1,000 units @ N10; total manufacturing cost N2,200 (all fixed); variable manufacturing cost 1,100 units @ N6; closing inventory 100 units; fixed S&A expenses N500; variable S&A expenses N400. Prepare the income statement under (a) absorption costing and (b) marginal costing."}],"a":[{"table":{"align":"lr","head":["(a) Absorption costing","N"],"rows":[["Sales (1,000 @ N10)","10,000"],["Variable manufacturing cost (1,100 @ N6)","6,600"],["Fixed manufacturing cost","2,200"],["Less: closing inventory (100 @ N8)","(800)"],["**Cost of sales**","**8,000**"],["**Gross margin**","**2,000**"],["Less: total selling & admin. expenses","900"],["**Net income**","**1,100**"]]}},{"p":"Cost per unit of closing inventory (absorption) $= \\dfrac{100}{1{,}100} \\times 8{,}800 = N800$, i.e. $N8$/unit — using **total** manufacturing cost (6,600+2,200)."},{"table":{"align":"lr","head":["(b) Marginal costing","N"],"rows":[["Sales (1,000 @ N10)","10,000"],["Variable manufacturing cost (1,100 @ N6)","6,600"],["Less: closing inventory (100 @ N6)","(600)"],["**Variable cost of sales**","**6,000**"],["**Variable gross margin**","**4,000**"],["Less: variable selling & admin. expenses","400"],["**Contribution margin**","**3,600**"],["Less: fixed manufacturing cost","2,200"],["Less: fixed selling & admin. expenses","500"],["**Net income**","**900**"]]}},{"key":"The N200 gap between the two net incomes (1,100 vs 900) is exactly the fixed manufacturing cost **held back in closing inventory** under absorption costing (N800 vs N600, a N200 difference) — under marginal costing, that N200 is released immediately as a period charge instead."}]}}]},{"n":"9.2","t":"The role of contribution","b":[{"p":"Contribution is central to marginal costing precisely because, where a business makes more than one product, the profit of an *individual* product cannot really be calculated — that would need apportioning fixed costs to products, which marginal costing deliberately avoids."},{"def":{"t":"Contribution","d":"sales less the variable cost of sales — sometimes called \"gross margin.\" A pool that every product contributes its share to, out of which all fixed costs (of whatever nature) must be met; the difference between total contribution and total fixed cost is profit (or loss)."}},{"p":"Contribution is used in: fixing selling prices, finding the break-even point, choosing a product mix to maximise profit, make-or-buy decisions, accepting/rejecting special orders, and assessing the profitability of products or departments."},{"eg":{"tag":"Study text","t":"Illustration 9.2 — contribution exposes an arbitrary apportionment problem","open":true,"q":[{"p":"Prudent Engineering has three departments X, Y, Z: variable costs N90,000/N100,000/N150,000, fixed costs (apportioned by sales) N20,000/N30,000/N50,000, sales N100,000/N150,000/N250,000 — giving X a loss of N10,000. Management wants to close X. (a) Assess this on the data given; (b) reassess if specific fixed costs are actually N5,000 (X), N55,000 (Y), N30,000 (Z), with N10,000 general."}],"a":[{"p":"**(a) All fixed cost treated as general (apportioned by sales):**"},{"table":{"align":"lrrrr","head":["","X (N)","Y (N)","Z (N)","Total (N)"],"rows":[["Sales","100,000","150,000","250,000","500,000"],["Less: variable cost","90,000","100,000","150,000","340,000"],["**Contribution**","**10,000**","**50,000**","**100,000**","**160,000**"],["Less: fixed costs","","","","100,000"],["**Profit**","","","","**60,000**"]]}},{"key":"X's apparent \"loss\" was an artefact of arbitrarily apportioning fixed cost by sales — X actually **contributes N10,000** toward fixed costs. Closing X would reduce total contribution (and profit) by N10,000, not save anything."},{"p":"**(b) Using each department's own specific fixed cost:**"},{"table":{"align":"lrrrr","head":["","X (N)","Y (N)","Z (N)","Total (N)"],"rows":[["Contribution","10,000","50,000","100,000","160,000"],["Less: specific fixed costs","5,000","55,000","30,000","90,000"],["**Net contribution**","**5,000**","**(5,000)**","**70,000**","**70,000**"],["Less: general fixed costs","","","","10,000"],["**Profit**","","","","**60,000**"]]}},{"key":"Same overall profit (N60,000), but a completely different story: once **specific** fixed costs are properly traced, it is **Department Y**, not X, that loses money (N5,000) — closing Y (not X) would raise profit by exactly N5,000, matching Y's own loss. This is the core lesson of the illustration: an arbitrary apportionment basis can make a genuinely profitable department look like the one to cut."}]}}]},{"n":"9.3","t":"Relevant cost, relevant revenue and sunk cost","b":[{"def":{"t":"Relevant costs/revenues (CIMA)","d":"costs and revenues appropriate to a specific management decision — represented by future cash flows whose size depends on the decision's outcome. Where inventory is used, the relevant cost is its *replacement* cost, not its original (sunk) purchase price."}},{"p":"Three characteristics a cost/revenue must have to be relevant:"},{"ul":["it is a **future** cost/revenue;","it is a **differential** cost/revenue — it differs between the alternatives being compared; and","it involves an actual **cash flow**."]},{"key":"A direct consequence: **historical (sunk) costs are never relevant** to a decision being made now, however large they were."},{"p":"Common relevant-cost items: **notional/imputed costs** (not in financial accounts at all, since no cash changes hands — e.g. notional interest on internally-generated funds, notional rent for space already owned — but still useful for decisions); **opportunity cost** (the value of the best alternative forgone); out-of-pocket cost; replacement cost; and differential costs above zero."},{"def":{"t":"Sunk cost (CIMA)","d":"cost that has been irreversibly incurred or committed, and so cannot be relevant to a decision — also called an irrecoverable cost."}},{"note":"A past cost is relevant **only if** it still has a replacement/market value, or can be redirected to an alternative use — otherwise its value in decision-making is zero."},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Cost element","Relevant cost rule"],"rows":[["**Material**","If material already in store will be used regardless, its relevant cost is **NIL**. If it has a replacement/market value or an alternative use, *that* value is relevant."],["**Labour**","A worker's **fixed salary** is irrelevant (it is paid either way) — only the **opportunity cost** of redirecting a fixed-salary earner's time is relevant."]]}},{"eg":{"tag":"Study text","t":"Illustration — finding a minimum price using only relevant costs","open":true,"q":[{"p":"A special order will use N10,000 of material already in store, with no market value and no alternative use. Additional material CD must be bought for N30,000. Factory workers will be paid N50,000 total. The order uses idle plant (written down value N200,000, depreciation N5,000/month) for one month. Find the minimum price."}],"a":[{"table":{"align":"lr","head":["","N"],"rows":[["Existing material in stores","NIL"],["Material CD","30,000"],["Factory workers' wages","50,000"],["Manager's salary","NIL"],["Plant depreciation","NIL"],["**Minimum price**","**80,000**"]]}},{"key":"Existing stores material is sunk (no market value/alternative use, so NIL); the manager's salary is irrelevant (paid regardless); plant depreciation on **idle** plant is a sunk cost too, since the plant's book value is unaffected by whether this particular order uses it. Only material CD and the factory wages are genuine future, differential cash flows."}]}}]},{"n":"9.4","t":"Practical applications of marginal costing","b":[{"h4":"Make or buy decisions"},{"p":"Arises when a manufacturer must decide whether to make a component in-house or buy it from an outside supplier. The relevant comparison is the supplier's quotation against the **incremental cost of in-house production** (plus any opportunity cost of producing in-house)."},{"p":"Qualitative factors to weigh alongside the pure cost comparison: the quality of the bought-in product; the outside supplier's reliability; the effect of future market prices; management's ability to handle current/future capacity; the risk of revealing trade secrets; and transport/handling problems."},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Comparison","Decision"],"rows":[["Supplier's quotation **>** total variable cost of making in-house","**Make** in-house"],["Total variable cost of making in-house **>** supplier's quotation","It is prudent to **buy** from the supplier"]]}},{"eg":{"tag":"Study text","t":"Illustration 9.3 — make or buy, differential cost analysis","open":true,"q":[{"p":"Nagarta Nig. PLC can subcontract a component at N1.80/piece. Subcontracting would save: material N0.50/unit, labour N0.25/unit, variable overhead N1.00/unit, plus N2,000/year supervision and N500/year special tools. Units sold: 14,000. Current standard cost: materials N70,000, labour N28,000, factory overhead N140,000, with variable overhead at N4/unit and fixed at N6/unit (normal capacity 20,000 units). Net loss currently N24,000, with selling & admin. N100,000. Advise on subcontracting."}],"a":[{"table":{"align":"lrrr","head":["","Make (N)","Difference (N)","Buy (N)"],"rows":[["Net sales","350,000","","350,000"],["Direct materials","70,000","−7,000","63,000"],["Purchased parts","—","+25,200","25,200"],["Direct labour","28,000","−3,500","24,500"],["Variable overheads","56,000","−14,000","42,000"],["Fixed overheads","120,000","−2,500","117,500"],["**Total manufacturing cost**","**274,000**","**−1,800**","**272,200**"],["Selling & admin.","100,000","—","100,000"],["**Total cost**","**374,000**","**−1,800**","**372,200**"],["**Net loss**","**(24,000)**","**−1,800**","**(22,200)**"]]}},{"key":"With no change in volume or price, revenue is irrelevant — the whole decision comes down to a **N1,800 cost saving** from subcontracting. Even so, this small saving may not outweigh the non-quantitative cost of laying off staff — the differential cost analysis is the *core* of the decision, not necessarily its final word."}]}},{"eg":{"tag":"Study text","t":"Illustration 9.4 — make or buy across several components","open":true,"q":[{"p":"A.B. Ltd makes W (1,000 units), X (2,000), Y (4,000), Z (3,000). Unit marginal costs: W N14, X N17, Y N7, Z N12. A subcontractor offers W N12, X N21, Y N10, Z N14. Directly attributable annual fixed costs: W N1,000, X N5,000, Y N6,000, Z N8,000 (plus N30,000 committed fixed costs, unaffected either way). Advise."}],"a":[{"table":{"align":"lrrrr","head":["","W","X","Y","Z"],"rows":[["Unit variable cost, making","N14","N17","N7","N12"],["Unit variable cost, buying","N12","N21","N10","N14"],["Extra variable cost of buying (per unit)","(2)","4","3","2"],["Annual requirement (units)","1,000","2,000","4,000","3,000"],["Extra variable cost of buying, total (N)","(2,000)","8,000","12,000","6,000"],["Fixed cost saved by buying (N)","1,000","5,000","6,000","8,000"],["**Net effect of buying (N)**","**(3,000)**","**3,000**","**6,000**","**(2,000)**"]]}},{"key":"A **negative** net-effect figure means buying **saves** money overall (extra variable cost is more than offset by fixed cost saved, or is itself negative): buy **W** (saves N3,000, since buying is cheaper per unit even before counting fixed savings) and buy **Z** (saves N2,000, this time *because of* the fixed-cost saving, even though its variable cost is higher). Keep making **X** and **Y** — buying either would cost more overall."},{"p":"Other considerations: what happens to the spare capacity freed up by buying W and Z? Is the subcontractor reliable on delivery and quality? Does the company want to stay flexible by making everything itself? Are the fixed-cost-saving estimates themselves reliable?"}]}},{"h4":"Acceptance/rejection of a special order"},{"p":"Arises when a customer offers a price below the normal selling price. If the company has **spare capacity**, and the special price still **covers marginal cost**, accepting typically adds to profit — since fixed costs are already covered by normal sales."},{"eg":{"tag":"Study text","t":"Illustration 9.5 — accept or reject a special order","open":true,"q":[{"p":"Shukura Nig. Ltd sells \"slimming sticks\" at 20k each; current output 400,000 (80% of capacity — so full capacity is 500,000). Total cost for the period N56,000, of which N16,000 is fixed (so variable cost = N40,000/400,000 = 10k/unit). A customer offers 13k/stick for a 100,000-unit order. Advise."}],"a":[{"table":{"align":"lrr","head":["","Present position (N)","If order accepted (N)"],"rows":[["Sales","80,000 (400,000 @ 20k)","13,000 (100,000 @ 13k)"],["Less: marginal cost","40,000 (400,000 @ 10k)","10,000 (100,000 @ 10k)"],["Contribution","40,000","3,000"],["Less: fixed costs","16,000","—"],["**Net profit**","**24,000**","**+3,000 extra**"]]}},{"key":"**Accept** the order — it adds N3,000 to profit, since 13k still exceeds the 10k marginal cost, and spare capacity (100,000 units) exists to absorb it without displacing normal-price sales."},{"p":"Other factors: will this special price prompt other customers to demand the same discount? Is this the most profitable use of the spare capacity? Could accepting it crowd out a later order at the true market price? Will fixed costs really stay constant?"}]}},{"h4":"Marginal costing and pricing decisions"},{"p":"Marginal costing clarifies the relationship between a product's own cost and various candidate selling prices, since contribution is unaffected by how indirect costs are allocated."},{"eg":{"tag":"Study text","t":"Illustration 9.6 — units needed to maintain profit after a price cut","open":true,"q":[{"p":"M&K Industries: present sales 30,000 units @ N10 (N300,000), variable cost N180,000 (N6/unit), fixed cost N70,000, profit N50,000. If price is cut 5%, 10% or 15%, how many units must be sold to hold profit at N50,000?"}],"a":[{"p":"To hold profit at N50,000 with fixed cost unchanged at N70,000, total contribution must stay at N120,000."},{"table":{"align":"lrrr","head":["Price cut","New price","New contribution/unit","Units needed"],"rows":[["5%","N9.50","N3.50","120,000 ÷ 3.50 ≈ 34,286"],["10%","N9.00","N3.00","120,000 ÷ 3.00 = 40,000"],["15%","N8.50","N2.50","120,000 ÷ 2.50 = 48,000"]]}},{"p":"**Verification at 10%**: sales $= 40{,}000 \\times 9 = N360{,}000$; variable cost $= 40{,}000 \\times 6 = N240{,}000$; contribution $= N120{,}000$; less fixed N70,000 $=$ profit **N50,000** — exactly as required."}]}},{"h4":"Marginal costing and profit planning"},{"p":"Contribution ratio also drives forward planning — finding the volume, or the price, needed to hit a target profit."},{"eg":{"tag":"Study text","t":"Illustration 9.7 — minimum price for an extra-capacity order, given rising costs","open":true,"q":[{"p":"A toy manufacturer sells 60,000 pieces (60% of capacity) @ N15, earning N3/piece profit. Cost composition: DM N4.00, DL N1.00, works overhead N6.00 (50% fixed), sales overhead N1.00 (25% variable). This year: fixed costs +10%, direct labour rate +20%, direct material rate +5%, selling price unchanged. An order for a further 20% of capacity (20,000 units) is offered. Find the minimum price for the order needed to reach an overall profit of N180,500."}],"a":[{"p":"New variable costs/piece: DM $4.20$ ($4.00 \\times 1.05$); DL $1.20$ ($1.00 \\times 1.20$); variable works overhead unchanged at $3.00$ (only the *fixed* half of works overhead rises); variable sales overhead unchanged at $0.25$. Total $= N8.65$/piece."},{"table":{"align":"lr","head":["Marginal costing statement (60,000 pieces)","N"],"rows":[["Variable cost (60,000 × 8.65)","519,000"],["Sales value (60,000 × 15)","900,000"],["**Contribution**","**381,000**"]]}},{"p":"Fixed works overhead (50% of original N6 × 60,000 $= N180{,}000$) $+10\\% = N198{,}000$. Fixed sales overhead (75% of original N1 × 60,000 $= N45{,}000$) $+10\\% = N49{,}500$. Total fixed cost $= N247{,}500$."},{"tex":"\\text{Profit before the order} = 381{,}000 - 247{,}500 = N133{,}500"},{"tex":"\\text{Required increase in profit} = 180{,}500 - 133{,}500 = N47{,}000"},{"tex":"\\text{Minimum price} = \\dfrac{(20{,}000 \\times 8.65) + 47{,}000}{20{,}000} = \\dfrac{220{,}000}{20{,}000} = N11\\text{/unit}"}]}}]},{"n":"9.5","t":"Worksheet summary — definitions, formulae and structure","b":[{"p":"Everything in this chapter, compressed for a last read-through."},{"h3":"Section-by-section checklist"},{"ol":["**§9.1 Marginal vs absorption costing** — marginal costing is a presentation approach, not a costing system; only variable manufacturing cost is a \"product cost\" under marginal costing; 3 income-statement differences (fixed-overhead treatment, closing-inventory valuation, first profit line shown); $S-V=C$, $C-FC=P$; 6 advantages, 5 limitations.","**§9.2 Contribution** — sales less variable cost of sales; the pool every product feeds fixed-cost recovery from; exposing an arbitrary fixed-cost apportionment can completely flip which department/product \"looks\" unprofitable.","**§9.3 Relevant cost/revenue** — must be future, differential, and a real cash flow; sunk costs are never relevant; material in store already committed is NIL relevant cost unless it has a real alternative use/market value; only a fixed-salary worker's *opportunity cost*, not their salary itself, is relevant.","**§9.4 Four practical applications** — make or buy (compare quotation vs variable cost of making, plus fixed-cost-saved); special order acceptance (accept if price covers marginal cost and spare capacity exists); pricing (contribution/unit needed for a given profit target at a new price); profit planning (minimum price for extra volume to hit a named target profit, allowing for cost inflation)."]},{"h3":"All the terms"},{"ul":["**Marginal cost** — cost of one additional unit; effectively variable cost in the short run. **Contribution** — sales less variable cost of sales.","**Relevant cost/revenue** — future, differential, a real cash flow. **Sunk cost** — irreversibly incurred, never relevant.","**Notional (imputed) cost** — no real cash flow, but useful for decisions (e.g. notional rent). **Opportunity cost** — the value of the best alternative forgone."]},{"h3":"B. Formula box"},{"fbox":{"h":"Marginal costing formulas (recap)","rows":[{"lb":"Contribution","tex":"S - V = C"},{"lb":"Profit","tex":"C - FC = P"},{"lb":"Sales at break-even point","tex":"\\dfrac{FC \\times S}{S - V}"},{"lb":"Units needed for a target contribution","tex":"\\dfrac{\\text{Required total contribution}}{\\text{Contribution per unit}}"}]}}]},{"n":"9.6","t":"End-of-chapter questions (study text)","b":[{"eg":{"tag":"Study text","t":"Multiple-choice and short-answer questions, with answers","open":true,"q":[{"ol":["What is marginal cost? (A) Cost incurred when increasing profit margin  (B) Total cost of manufacture  (C) Cost of producing an additional unit  (D) Cost of manufacturing a new product  (E) Aggregate cost of manufacturing diverse products","What are period costs? (A) Costs incurred during a stated period of production  (B) Costs incurred during certain periods of the year  (C) Costs which serve to enhance profitability  (D) Costs of maintaining equipment during a period  (E) Costs incurred during a period irrespective of production volume","How does marginal costing differ from absorption costing in inventory valuation? (A) Marginal costing is only concerned with margins  (B) Absorption costing uses LIFO  (C) Absorption costing recognises slow-moving inventory  (D) Marginal costing uses total variable costs, writing off fixed assets  (E) Marginal costing uses FIFO","What is contribution? (A) Total sales minus total variable costs  (B) Total sales plus total variable costs  (C) Total variable costs plus total fixed costs  (D) Total fixed costs minus profit  (E) Total sales plus total fixed costs","What is a limiting factor? (A) The extent to which the company may manufacture its products  (B) The factor of production which is in short supply  (C) The limit to which government may allow the company to manufacture  (D) The factor of production which is most expensive  (E) The factor of production which is easily obtainable","Under absorption costing, closing inventory includes a portion of the period's …………….","In most decision-making scenarios, marginal costing advises that the relevant cost is the ……………………………","Under marginal costing, sales minus variable costs equals ……………………………","What is the major cause of differences in profit figures derived from profitability statements using absorption costing and marginal costing?","Based purely on financial information, a company should manufacture more of the products giving the highest ……………. towards the absorption of its fixed costs and ultimately earning profit."]}],"a":[{"ol":["**C — cost of producing an additional unit.**","**E** — costs incurred during a period, irrespective of production volume.","**D** — marginal costing uses only total *variable* costs; absorption costing uses total (fixed + variable) cost.","**A — total sales minus total variable costs.**","**B — the factor of production which is in short supply.**","**Fixed costs.**","**Variable cost (marginal cost).**","**Contribution.**","**The value of closing inventory / the treatment of fixed costs.**","**Contribution.**"]}]}}]},{"n":"9.7","t":"Study text examination-type questions, worked","b":[{"p":"The study text closes the chapter with eight examination-type questions, almost all of them decision-making problems. All are reproduced below with full answers."},{"eg":{"tag":"Exam type — Q1","t":"Dala Equipment — make or buy four components under a machine-capacity constraint","q":[{"p":"Dala Equipment Limited manufactures four components, the cost particulars of which are given below:"},{"stmt":{"t":"Cost per component (N)","rows":[["Elements of cost","A","B","C","D","@hd"],["Direct material",80,100,100,120],["Direct labour",20,25,25,30],["Variable overhead",10,12,15,10],["Fixed overhead",15,23,20,20],["**Total cost**",125,160,160,180,"@t"],"@gap",["Output per machine hour (units)",4,2,3,3]]}},{"p":"The **key factor is shortage of machine capacity**. The components are used in the company's main product. A supplier has quoted the following prices: **A N115; B N175; C N135; D N185**."},{"p":"**Required:** advise management whether they should continue to produce all or some of the components, or buy them from the supplier."}],"a":[{"h4":"Step 1 — the relevant cost of making"},{"p":"Fixed overhead will be incurred whether or not the component is made in-house, so it is **irrelevant** to the decision. Only the **variable** cost is saved by buying:"},{"stmt":{"t":"Marginal (variable) cost of making, per unit (N)","rows":[["","A","B","C","D","@hd"],["Direct material",80,100,100,120],["Direct labour",20,25,25,30],["Variable overhead",10,12,15,10],["**Marginal cost of making**",110,137,140,160,"@tt"]]}},{"h4":"Step 2 — the extra cost of buying"},{"stmt":{"t":"Extra cost of buying rather than making, per unit (N)","rows":[["","A","B","C","D","@hd"],["Supplier's price",115,175,135,185],["Less: marginal cost of making",110,137,140,160],["**Extra cost of buying**",5,38,-5,25,"@tt"]]}},{"note":"Component **C is cheaper to buy than to make even before capacity is considered** — the supplier's N135 is N5 below the N140 it costs in variable cost alone. C should therefore be bought outright, whatever the machine-hour position."},{"h4":"Step 3 — rank by the saving per unit of the limiting factor"},{"p":"Machine capacity is short, so the question is not simply which components are cheaper to make, but which make the **best use of each scarce machine hour**. Convert output per machine hour into machine hours per unit, then express the saving from making as a saving **per machine hour**:"},{"stmt":{"t":"Benefit of making, per machine hour","rows":[["","A","B","C","D","@hd"],["Output per machine hour (units)",4,2,3,3],["Machine hours per unit",0.25,0.5,"0.333","0.333"],["Extra cost of buying, per unit (N)",5,38,-5,25],["**Saving from making, per machine hour (N)**",20,76,-15,75,"@t"],["**Ranking**","3rd","**1st**","do not make","2nd","@tt"]]}},{"h4":"Advice to management"},{"ol":["**Buy component C** from the supplier in full. It costs N5 per unit *more* to make it than to buy it, so making it would waste both money and scarce machine time.","**Make component B first.** Every machine hour devoted to B saves **N76** against the cost of buying — the highest return on the scarce resource.","**Make component D second**, saving N75 per machine hour.","**Make component A last**, saving only N20 per machine hour.","**Buy in whatever cannot be made** once the available machine hours run out, working down the ranking."]},{"key":"The trap this question is built around is the ranking of **A against D**. On a per-unit view, making D saves N25 and making A only N5, so A looks the weaker candidate — and it is. But if the ranking had been done on per-unit savings alone it could easily have gone wrong, because a component with a small per-unit saving may still be the better use of a machine hour if it uses very little machine time. **When a limiting factor exists, always rank by contribution (or saving) per unit of the limiting factor, never per unit of product.**"},{"note":"Non-financial factors a complete answer would mention: the reliability and quality of the supplier; the security of supply for components used in the company's **main** product; whether the quoted prices are guaranteed and for how long; the effect on the workforce; and the risk of becoming dependent on an outside supplier who may later raise prices."}]}},{"eg":{"tag":"Exam type — Q2","t":"Indabawa Mills — should product B be dropped?","q":[{"p":"Three different product lines are sold by Indabawa Mills. **Product A contributes 20% of its revenue** to fixed costs and profits, **product B 10%** and **product C 60%**. The products sell at: A **N8** per unit; B **N2** per unit; C **N3** per unit."},{"p":"The company earned a net profit of **N50,000** before income taxes last year by selling **50,000 units of A, 150,000 units of B and 60,000 units of C**."},{"p":"The sales manager, Muhammed Ibrahim, believes the profit picture can be improved by **eliminating product B** and concentrating the sales effort on A and C. He sees an opportunity to increase sales of A to **70,000 units**, but admits C will probably sell the same amount next year."},{"p":"**Required:** prepare a projected income statement using Ibrahim's assumptions. Do you agree with him? Explain what would cause the profit to increase or decrease."}],"a":[{"h4":"Step 1 — last year's position, and the fixed costs"},{"stmt":{"t":"Last year (N)","rows":[["","Product A","Product B","Product C","Total","@hd"],["Units sold",50000,150000,60000,""],["Selling price per unit","N8","N2","N3",""],["**Sales revenue**",400000,300000,180000,880000,"@t"],["P/V ratio (contribution as % of revenue)","20%","10%","60%",""],["**Contribution**",80000,30000,108000,218000,"@t"],["Less: fixed costs *(balancing figure)*","","","",168000],["**Net profit before tax**","","","",50000,"@tt"]]}},{"h4":"Step 2 — the projected income statement on Ibrahim's assumptions"},{"stmt":{"t":"Projected income statement — product B eliminated (N)","rows":[["","Product A","Product C","Total","@hd"],["Units sold",70000,60000,""],["Selling price per unit","N8","N3",""],["**Sales revenue**",560000,180000,740000,"@t"],["P/V ratio","20%","60%",""],["**Contribution**",112000,108000,220000,"@t"],["Less: fixed costs (assumed unchanged)","","",168000],["**Projected net profit before tax**","","",52000,"@tt"]]}},{"h4":"Step 3 — what causes the change"},{"stmt":{"t":"Reconciliation of the N2,000 increase (N)","rows":[["Contribution gained from the extra 20,000 units of A (20,000 × N8 × 20%)","",32000],["Contribution lost by dropping product B entirely","",-30000],["**Net increase in profit**","",2000,"@tt"]]}},{"h4":"Do you agree with Ibrahim?"},{"p":"**Only with substantial reservations.** On his own assumptions profit rises by just **N2,000**, or **4%** — a thin reward for abandoning a product line that generates **N300,000 of revenue and N30,000 of contribution**. The whole of the gain depends on two assumptions, either of which could easily be wrong:"},{"ol":["**That sales of A really will rise by 20,000 units.** If A reaches only 68,750 units, the extra contribution is N30,000 and the company is exactly no better off; below that it is worse off. There is no margin for error at all.","**That fixed costs stay at N168,000.** Dropping B saves nothing in fixed cost on this analysis. If any fixed cost is **specific to B** and would be avoided, the proposal improves; if dropping B instead causes fixed costs to be reapportioned over A and C without falling, nothing is saved."]},{"p":"Further considerations against the proposal:"},{"ul":["**B still makes a positive contribution of N30,000** towards fixed costs. A product should never be dropped merely because its P/V ratio is low — only if its contribution is negative, or if the capacity it uses can earn more elsewhere.","**Customers may buy the three products together**, so withdrawing B could cost sales of A and C as well.","**Idle capacity.** If the resources released by B cannot in fact be redeployed to A, the company loses B's contribution and gains nothing.","**Loss of market presence** and of the option to improve B's margin later."]},{"key":"The better question is not \"should we drop B?\" but **\"can we sell 20,000 more units of A without dropping B?\"** If the extra demand for A is real and capacity exists, the company could earn N32,000 more while keeping B's N30,000 — a profit of N82,000 rather than N52,000. Ibrahim has bundled two separate decisions into one, and only one of them is attractive."}]}},{"eg":{"tag":"Exam type — Q3","t":"Wali and Gwangwazo — should the loss-making product X be discontinued?","q":[{"p":"Management is considering a proposal to discontinue the manufacture of product X from its range X, Y and Z:"},{"stmt":{"t":"Present position","rows":[["","X","Y","Z","@hd"],["Capacity in actual production","20%","40%","40%"],["Units manufactured",4000,10000,12000],"@gap",["**Costs per unit (N)**","","","","@hd"],["Materials",25,20,40],["Labour",15,10,20],["Fixed overhead",4,4,5],["Variable overhead",4,3,5],["**Total cost**",48,37,70,"@t"],["Profit / (loss) per unit as printed","(4)",13,10],["**Selling price**",52,50,80,"@t"]]}},{"p":"Product X has persistently shown a loss because demand is saturated. Y and Z have bright prospects, having been accepted as components by a newly established machine-building complex nearby. The capacity released by discontinuing X is to be **transferred equally to Y and Z**. The nature of X is such that the transfer would bring about an increase of **30% for Y** and **50% for Z** in the number of units manufactured, over those involved in the capacity transferred — without any change in total fixed costs. Anticipated increases:"},{"stmt":{"t":"Anticipated changes","rows":[["","Y","Z","@hd"],["Materials","+5%","+5%"],["Labour","+10%","+10%"],["Selling price","+10%","+5%"]]}},{"p":"**Required:** (a) prepare a statement of projected profitability; (b) advise management whether the proposal should be accepted."},{"warn":"The printed table shows X at a **loss of N4** per unit, but its own figures give $52 - 48 = $ a **profit** of N4. One of the two numbers must be wrong. The analysis below uses the printed **selling prices** throughout, because they are what drive the revised figures for Y and Z, and notes the effect of the alternative reading on X at the end. The recommendation is the same either way.","lbl":"An inconsistency in the printed data"}],"a":[{"h4":"Step 1 — the present position, restated on contribution"},{"stmt":{"t":"Present profitability (N)","rows":[["","X","Y","Z","Total","@hd"],["Units manufactured",4000,10000,12000,""],["Selling price per unit",52,50,80,""],["Variable cost per unit (materials + labour + variable overhead)",44,33,65,""],["**Contribution per unit**",8,17,15,"","@t"],["**Total contribution**",32000,170000,180000,382000,"@t"],["Less: fixed overhead (4,000×4; 10,000×4; 12,000×5)",16000,40000,60000,116000],["**Profit**",16000,130000,120000,266000,"@tt"]]}},{"h4":"Step 2 — the units after the transfer of capacity"},{"p":"X occupies **20%** of capacity, to be split equally — 10% to Y and 10% to Z. Y's present 40% yields 10,000 units, so 10% yields 2,500; Z's 40% yields 12,000 units, so 10% yields 3,000. The transferred capacity then yields 30% more for Y and 50% more for Z:"},{"stmt":{"t":"Revised output","rows":[["","Y","Z","@hd"],["Units from the capacity transferred, pro rata (10% of capacity)",2500,3000],["Add: increase of 30% for Y;  50% for Z",750,1500],["**Additional units**",3250,4500,"@t"],["Present units",10000,12000],["**Revised units manufactured**",13250,16500,"@tt"]]}},{"h4":"Step 3 — the revised unit figures"},{"stmt":{"t":"Revised selling price and variable cost per unit (N)","rows":[["","Y","Z","@hd"],["Materials  (20 × 1.05;  40 × 1.05)",21.0,42.0],["Labour  (10 × 1.10;  20 × 1.10)",11.0,22.0],["Variable overhead (unchanged)",3.0,5.0],["**Revised variable cost per unit**",35.0,69.0,"@t"],["Revised selling price  (50 × 1.10;  80 × 1.05)",55.0,84.0],["**Revised contribution per unit**",20.0,15.0,"@tt"]]}},{"h4":"(a)  Statement of projected profitability"},{"stmt":{"t":"Projected profitability after discontinuing X (N)","rows":[["","Y","Z","Total","@hd"],["Revised units",13250,16500,""],["Contribution per unit",20.0,15.0,""],["**Total contribution**",265000,247500,512500,"@t"],["Less: total fixed overhead (unchanged)","","",116000],["**Projected profit**","","",396500,"@tt"]]}},{"h4":"(b)  Advice to management"},{"stmt":{"t":"Comparison (N)","rows":[["Projected profit if X is discontinued","",396500],["Present profit with all three products","",266000],["**Increase in profit**","",130500,"@tt"]]}},{"p":"**The proposal should be accepted.** Profit rises by **N130,500**, an increase of nearly 50%. Note carefully *why*: **not** because X makes a loss — on the printed selling price X actually earns a contribution of **N32,000**, which the company gives up — but because the capacity X occupies can earn far more when switched to Y and Z, which between them gain N182,500 of extra contribution (N95,000 from Y and N67,500 from Z, including the price increases)."},{"note":"If instead X's printed loss of N4 per unit is taken as correct (implying a selling price of N44 and a contribution of nil), the present profit is N234,000 and the increase from the proposal is N162,500. The recommendation is unchanged and is, if anything, stronger."},{"warn":"Two cautions for the answer: the whole gain depends on the assumption that the extra 3,250 units of Y and 4,500 units of Z **can actually be sold** at the higher prices, and on fixed costs genuinely remaining at N116,000. Discontinuing X is also difficult to reverse if demand for it recovers."},{"key":"The lesson is the one that runs through this whole chapter: **a product should never be dropped because it shows a loss on a full-cost basis.** It should be dropped only if its contribution is negative, or — as here — if the scarce capacity it occupies will earn more elsewhere. The deciding figure is the **opportunity cost of the capacity**, not the apportioned fixed overhead."}]}},{"eg":{"tag":"Exam type — Q4","t":"Jigawa Limited — make or buy, with a legislation cost and a sunk-cost argument","q":[{"p":"Management is considering next year's production and purchase budgets. One component, incorporated into another product before sale, has a budgeted manufacturing cost of:"},{"stmt":{"t":"Budgeted manufacturing cost per unit (N)","rows":[["Direct material","",14.0],["Direct labour (4 hours at N3)","",12.0],["Variable overhead (4 hours at N2)","",8.0],["Fixed overhead (4 hours at N5)","",20.0],["**Total**","",54.0,"@tt"]]}},{"p":"Ball Limited has offered to supply the component at a guaranteed price of **N50 per unit**."},{"p":"**Required:** **(a)** considering cost criteria only, advise management whether the component should be purchased from Ball Limited. **(b)** Explain how your advice would be affected if, as a result of government legislation, continuing to manufacture would mean additional inspection and testing expenses of **N56,000 per annum** not included above. **(c)** The production director recently said: \"We must continue to manufacture the component as only one year ago we purchased some special grinding equipment to be used exclusively for it. The equipment cost N100,000, it cannot be resold or used elsewhere, and if we cease production we will have to write off the written-down value of N80,000.\" Draft a reply."}],"a":[{"h4":"(a)  Make or buy on cost criteria"},{"p":"Fixed overhead of N20 per unit is absorbed, not incurred by the decision — it will continue whether the component is made or bought — so it is **irrelevant**. The relevant comparison is the **variable** cost of making against the purchase price:"},{"stmt":{"t":"Relevant cost comparison, per unit (N)","rows":[["**Cost of making (relevant costs only)**","","","@hd"],["Direct material",14.0,""],["Direct labour",12.0,""],["Variable overhead",8.0,34.0],"@gap",["**Cost of buying from Ball Limited**","",50.0],["**Extra cost of buying, per unit**","",16.0,"@tt"]]}},{"p":"**Advice: continue to manufacture the component.** Buying it would cost N16 per unit more than making it. The N54 full cost is misleading — N20 of it is fixed overhead that does not disappear if the component is bought in, and would simply be reabsorbed by the company's other products."},{"h4":"(b)  The effect of the N56,000 legislation cost"},{"p":"The N56,000 is a **future, incremental** cost that arises **only if the company continues to manufacture**, so it is now relevant. It is a fixed amount, so the advice becomes dependent on **volume**: making saves N16 per unit but costs an extra N56,000 a year."},{"tex":"\\text{Indifference volume} = \\frac{N56{,}000}{N16} = 3{,}500 \\text{ units per annum}"},{"stmt":{"t":"The revised advice","rows":[["Annual requirement **more than** 3,500 units","","**Continue to manufacture** — the per-unit saving outweighs the N56,000"],["Annual requirement **exactly** 3,500 units","","Indifferent on cost grounds — decide on other factors"],["Annual requirement **less than** 3,500 units","","**Buy from Ball Limited** — the N56,000 is not recovered"]]}},{"note":"Check at, say, 5,000 units: making saves $5{,}000 \\times 16 = N80{,}000$ against buying, less the N56,000 of inspection cost, a net saving of N24,000 — so make. At 2,000 units: saving $2{,}000 \\times 16 = N32{,}000$ against a N56,000 cost, a net extra cost of N24,000 — so buy."},{"h4":"(c)  Reply to the production director"},{"p":"**MEMORANDUM** — To: The Production Director. From: The Cost Accountant. Subject: The grinding equipment and the make-or-buy decision."},{"p":"Thank you for your note. I am afraid the written-down value of the grinding equipment is **not a reason to continue manufacturing**, for the following reasons."},{"ol":["The N100,000 was spent a year ago. It is a **sunk cost** — a past cost that no decision taken now can alter. Whether we make the component or buy it, that money has gone.","The equipment **cannot be resold or used elsewhere**, so it has **no disposal value and no opportunity cost**. Keeping it in use therefore costs us nothing and gains us nothing; it is simply irrelevant to the choice.","The N80,000 written-down value will be **charged against profit either way**. If we continue, it is charged gradually as depreciation; if we stop, it is charged at once as a write-off. The **total** charged is the same N80,000 in both cases, so it cannot favour one option over the other. Only the *timing* differs, and the timing of a non-cash book entry is not a commercial reason to continue production.","Continuing to manufacture an uneconomic component merely to avoid admitting a past investment error is the classic **\"throwing good money after bad\"** fallacy. The correct question is always: *given where we are now, which future course of action costs us least?*"]},{"p":"The decision should therefore rest only on **future, incremental cash flows** — the N34 variable cost of making against Ball Limited's N50, together with the N56,000 of new inspection costs and our expected annual volume. On those figures we should continue to manufacture provided we need more than 3,500 units a year, and the grinding equipment has nothing to do with it."},{"p":"I would, of course, be glad to discuss this with you before the budget meeting."}]}},{"eg":{"tag":"Exam type — Q5","t":"Koki Limited — make or buy part M-6, with an opportunity cost","q":[{"p":"Koki Limited, which manufactures part M-6 for use in its production cycle, has the following costs per unit for production of **25,000 units**:"},{"stmt":{"t":"Cost per unit (N)","rows":[["Direct materials","",5],["Direct labour","",20],["Manufacturing overhead","",24],["**Total**","",49,"@tt"]]}},{"p":"**66⅔% of the overhead is fixed.** Sahara Limited has offered to sell 25,000 units of part M-6 to Koki for **N45 per unit**. If Koki accepts, some of the facilities presently used to manufacture M-6 could be **rented to a third party at an annual rent of N65,000**. Additionally, **N6 per unit of the fixed overhead costs applied to part M-6 would be totally eliminated**."},{"p":"**Required:** **(a)** advise the managing director whether he should accept Sahara's offer. **(b)** State factors other than the relevant costs which would influence your decision."}],"a":[{"h4":"Step 1 — split the overhead"},{"stmt":{"t":"Analysis of the N24 manufacturing overhead per unit","rows":[["Fixed:  66⅔% × N24","","N16"],["Variable:  33⅓% × N24","","N8"]]}},{"p":"Of the N16 of fixed overhead per unit, only **N6** would be eliminated by ceasing production. The remaining **N10** would continue regardless and is therefore irrelevant."},{"h4":"(a)  The relevant cost comparison"},{"stmt":{"t":"Make or buy 25,000 units of part M-6 (N)","rows":[["","Per unit","Total (25,000 units)","@hd"],["**Cost of MAKING — relevant costs**","","","@hd"],["Direct materials",5,125000],["Direct labour",20,500000],["Variable manufacturing overhead",8,200000],["Avoidable fixed overhead",6,150000],["**Relevant cost of making**",39,975000,"@t"],["Add: opportunity cost — rental income forgone by continuing to make","",65000],["**Total relevant cost of making**","",1040000,"@t"],"@gap",["**Cost of BUYING**","","","@hd"],["Purchase price:  25,000 × N45",45,1125000],"@gap",["**Extra cost of buying**","",85000,"@tt"]]}},{"p":"**Advice: reject Sahara's offer and continue to manufacture part M-6.** Buying would cost the company **N85,000 a year more**, even after allowing for the N65,000 of rental income and the N150,000 of fixed overhead that would be saved."},{"note":"Note the two refinements that distinguish a good answer. **(1) The opportunity cost.** The N65,000 rent is not a cost of buying — it is income the company gives up by **continuing to make**, and so is added to the cost of making. (Deducting it from the cost of buying gives exactly the same N85,000 answer; what matters is that it appears once, on the correct side.) **(2) The partial fixed-cost saving.** Only N6 of the N16 fixed overhead per unit is avoidable; treating the whole N16 as avoidable would make buying appear N165,000 cheaper and reverse the decision."},{"h4":"(b)  Other factors influencing the decision"},{"ol":["**Quality and reliability** of Sahara's product, and whether it meets Koki's own specification.","**Security and continuity of supply** — the risk of late delivery or of Sahara ceasing to supply, which would halt Koki's own production cycle.","**How long the N45 price is guaranteed**, and what happens when the guarantee expires. A supplier can raise prices once the buyer has dismantled its own capacity.","**Loss of in-house expertise and capacity**, which would be costly and slow to rebuild if the company later wished to resume production.","**Reliability of the N65,000 rental** — is there an actual tenant, for how long, and does the lease tie up facilities Koki might need?","**Effect on employees** — redundancies, morale, industrial relations and the cost of any redundancy payments (which would themselves be a relevant cost).","**Confidentiality** — whether sharing the design with an outside supplier risks losing a competitive advantage.","**Flexibility** — the ability to vary volume, specification or delivery at short notice is usually greater in-house.","**Sahara's financial stability** and the risk of dependence on a single supplier."]}]}},{"eg":{"tag":"Exam type — Q6","t":"Aminu Motors — make or buy where the released capacity stays idle","q":[{"p":"Aminu Motors Limited specialises in the manufacture of sports cars, making some of the components it requires and buying others. Alparts Limited offers to supply a component currently made by Aminu Motors at a price of **N7**. The costs incurred by Aminu Motors in making the part are:"},{"stmt":{"t":"Cost per unit (N)","rows":[["Variable costs","",4],["Traceable fixed costs","",2],["Allocated fixed costs","",3],["**Total**","",9,"@tt"]]}},{"p":"Monthly output is **5,000 units**. **Required:** present information to management on the assumption that the production capacity released as a result of accepting the offer will remain idle."}],"a":[{"h4":"The key distinction — traceable versus allocated fixed costs"},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Traceable fixed costs (N2 per unit)","Allocated fixed costs (N3 per unit)"],"rows":[["Fixed costs incurred **specifically because this component is made** — the supervisor of that section, the depreciation and insurance of the dedicated machine, its maintenance contract.","A share of **general factory fixed overhead** apportioned to the component — factory rent, the works manager's salary, general rates."],["**Avoidable**: they would cease if production of the component ceased.","**Unavoidable**: they continue regardless and would simply be reapportioned to the company's other products."],["**RELEVANT** to the decision.","**IRRELEVANT** to the decision."]]}},{"h4":"Statement of relevant costs for management"},{"stmt":{"t":"Make or buy 5,000 components per month (N)","rows":[["","Make","Buy","@hd"],["Variable costs:  5,000 × N4",20000,"—"],["Traceable (avoidable) fixed costs:  5,000 × N2",10000,"—"],["Purchase price:  5,000 × N7","—",35000],["**Relevant cost per month**",30000,35000,"@t"],"@gap",["*Allocated fixed costs, incurred either way:  5,000 × N3*",15000,15000],["**Total cost to the company per month**",45000,50000,"@tt"]]}},{"stmt":{"t":"Summary","rows":[["Saving from continuing to make, per unit  (N7 − N6)","","N1.00"],["Saving per month  (5,000 units × N1)","","N5,000"],["**Saving per annum**","","N60,000","@tt"]]}},{"p":"**Recommendation: reject Alparts' offer and continue to make the component.** The relevant cost of making is **N6** per unit (variable N4 plus traceable fixed N2) against a purchase price of **N7** — a saving of N1 per unit, N5,000 a month, N60,000 a year."},{"key":"The N9 full cost makes the offer look attractive: N7 appears to be a saving of N2 a unit, N10,000 a month. It is not, because **N3 of that N9 does not go away**. If the component were bought in, the N15,000 a month of allocated fixed cost would be reapportioned onto the sports cars themselves, making *them* look N3 per component dearer — and the company N5,000 a month worse off. Buying on full cost rather than relevant cost is the single commonest error in make-or-buy decisions."},{"note":"The question's assumption that the released capacity **remains idle** is what makes the answer clean. If the capacity could instead be used to make something else, the contribution from that alternative use would be an **opportunity cost** of continuing to make the component, and would have to be added to the \"make\" column — if it exceeded N5,000 a month, the decision would reverse."}]}},{"eg":{"tag":"Exam type — Q7","t":"The benefits and pitfalls of marginal costing","q":[{"p":"**(a)** What benefits are to be gained from marginal costing? Are there any pitfalls in the application of marginal costs? Discuss these matters critically."},{"p":"**(b)** Give a brief account of a practical application of marginal costing which you consider sound from a policy viewpoint."}],"a":[{"h4":"(a)  The benefits of marginal costing"},{"ol":["**It isolates contribution**, which is the figure that actually changes when volume changes — so the effect of any change in output, price or mix on profit can be seen immediately.","**It avoids the arbitrariness of overhead apportionment.** Fixed cost is written off in full against the period, so no product is made to look profitable or unprofitable by an apportionment convention.","**Profit moves with sales, not with production.** Under absorption costing, profit can be increased simply by producing for inventory; under marginal costing it cannot, which removes a perverse incentive and makes period-to-period comparison meaningful.","**It is the right basis for short-run decisions** — accept or reject a special order, make or buy, drop a product, choose between products under a limiting factor, set a minimum price.","**It underpins cost-volume-profit and break-even analysis** (Chapter 10) and the flexible budget (Chapter 12).","**It is simpler to operate** — no absorption rates, no under/over absorption to explain, and inventory valuation needs no overhead allocation.","**It assists cost control**, because fixed costs are shown as period costs where responsibility for them lies, rather than buried in unit costs."]},{"h4":"(a)  The pitfalls — a critical view"},{"ol":["**The fixed/variable split is not clean.** Many costs are semi-variable and must be separated by approximation (the high-low method of §2.1.2a), and some are stepped. The whole technique rests on a division that is itself an estimate.","**Linearity is assumed.** Variable cost per unit and selling price per unit are taken as constant, which holds only within the relevant range — bulk discounts, overtime premiums and price concessions all break it.","**It can encourage under-pricing.** A price that covers marginal cost and makes some contribution is better than nothing *in the short run and for marginal business*. If it becomes the normal basis of pricing, total contribution will not cover fixed costs and the business makes a loss while every individual sale appears \"profitable\".","**Fixed costs are not ignorable in the long run.** All costs are variable over a long enough period, and every product must eventually cover its share of them.","**It is not permitted for external reporting.** IAS 2 requires inventory to include production overhead, so absorption costing must be used for the published accounts and a reconciliation maintained.","**It understates inventory**, which may matter for loan covenants, insurance and the reported financial position.","**It is less appropriate where fixed costs dominate.** In a capital-intensive business, contribution is nearly the whole selling price, and decisions based on it carry very little information about whether the business is viable.","**It takes no account of the capacity consumed**, unless the limiting-factor technique is applied as well."]},{"h4":"(b)  A practical application sound from a policy viewpoint"},{"p":"**Accepting a special export order at below the normal selling price**, where there is spare capacity."},{"p":"Suppose a company with a normal selling price of N100 and a full cost of N85 (variable N60, fixed N25) is offered an export contract at N70 per unit, using capacity that would otherwise stand idle. On a full-cost view the order shows a loss of N15 a unit and would be refused. On a marginal-cost view it earns a **contribution of N10 a unit**, every naira of which goes towards fixed costs that are being incurred anyway — so the order increases profit and should be accepted."},{"p":"This is sound **as a policy** provided four conditions hold, and it is the conditions that make it sound rather than the arithmetic:"},{"ol":["there is genuinely **spare capacity**, so no existing business is displaced (otherwise the contribution forgone is an opportunity cost);","the special price **will not leak into the normal market** and undermine the N100 price — hence an *export* order to a separate market;","the order is **marginal and occasional**, not a substitute for normally-priced business; and","**no additional fixed costs** are incurred to fulfil it."]},{"p":"Other equally defensible applications: setting a **minimum tender price** when bidding for work in a slack period; deciding whether to **close a department temporarily** (comparing the contribution forgone with the fixed costs saved); and choosing the **best product mix** when a scarce resource limits output, by ranking on contribution per unit of the limiting factor."}]}},{"eg":{"tag":"Exam type — Q8 (ICAN Intermediate, November 1999)","t":"Tapgun Industries — marginal and absorption operating statements for two months","q":[{"p":"Tapgun Industries Limited manufactures paints in 4-litre cans. The selling price of the product is **N280**, with a standard cost of **N180** made up as follows:"},{"stmt":{"t":"Standard cost per can (N)","rows":[["Direct materials","",100],["Direct labour","",20],["Variable overheads","",30],["Fixed overhead","",30],["**Standard cost**","",180,"@tt"]]}},{"p":"In April and May 1999 the company's production and sales reports were:"},{"stmt":{"t":"Production and sales (cans)","rows":[["","April","May","@hd"],["Production",10000,10000],["Sales",8000,12000]]}},{"p":"**Required:** **(a)** prepare the company's operating statements for the two months using (i) the marginal costing technique and (ii) the absorption costing technique. **(b)** Comment on the results of the two techniques, with particular reference to the difference in inventory valuation and in operating profit."}],"a":[{"h4":"Working — the unit costs and the fixed overhead"},{"stmt":{"t":"Workings","rows":[["Marginal (variable) cost per can  (100 + 20 + 30)","","N150"],["Fixed overhead absorbed per can","","N30"],["Full (absorption) cost per can","","N180"],["Normal activity implied  (production in each month)","","10,000 cans"],["**Fixed overhead per month  (10,000 × N30)**","","N300,000"],"@gap",["Opening inventory, April","","nil"],["Closing inventory, April  (10,000 − 8,000)","","2,000 cans"],["Closing inventory, May  (2,000 + 10,000 − 12,000)","","nil"]]}},{"h4":"(a)(i)  Marginal costing operating statements"},{"stmt":{"t":"Operating statement — marginal costing (N)","rows":[["","April","May","@hd"],["Sales  (8,000 × N280;  12,000 × N280)",2240000,3360000],"@gap",["Opening inventory at marginal cost  (nil;  2,000 × N150)",0,300000],["Variable cost of production  (10,000 × N150)",1500000,1500000],["Less: closing inventory at marginal cost  (2,000 × N150;  nil)",-300000,0],["**Variable cost of sales**",1200000,1800000,"@t"],["**Contribution**",1040000,1560000,"@t"],["Less: fixed overhead (period cost, written off in full)",300000,300000],["**Operating profit — marginal costing**",740000,1260000,"@tt"]]}},{"h4":"(a)(ii)  Absorption costing operating statements"},{"stmt":{"t":"Operating statement — absorption costing (N)","rows":[["","April","May","@hd"],["Sales",2240000,3360000],"@gap",["Opening inventory at full cost  (nil;  2,000 × N180)",0,360000],["Full cost of production  (10,000 × N180)",1800000,1800000],["Less: closing inventory at full cost  (2,000 × N180;  nil)",-360000,0],["**Cost of sales**",1440000,2160000,"@t"],["**Gross profit**",800000,1200000,"@t"],["Under / (over) absorbed fixed overhead  *(production = 10,000 = normal, so nil)*",0,0],["**Operating profit — absorption costing**",800000,1200000,"@tt"]]}},{"h4":"(b)  Comment on the results"},{"stmt":{"t":"The two techniques compared (N)","rows":[["","April","May","Two months","@hd"],["**Closing inventory — marginal costing (2,000 × N150)**",300000,0,""],["**Closing inventory — absorption costing (2,000 × N180)**",360000,0,""],["Difference in inventory valuation",60000,0,""],"@gap",["**Operating profit — marginal costing**",740000,1260000,2000000],["**Operating profit — absorption costing**",800000,1200000,2000000],["Difference in profit",60000,-60000,0]]}},{"h4":"Reconciliation"},{"stmt":{"t":"Reconciliation of the two profits (N)","rows":[["","April","May","@hd"],["Profit — marginal costing",740000,1260000],["Add / (less): fixed overhead in the increase / (decrease) in inventory","+60,000","−60,000"],["   *(2,000 cans × N30 fixed overhead per can)*","",""],["**Profit — absorption costing**",800000,1200000,"@tt"]]}},{"p":"**Inventory valuation.** Marginal costing values the 2,000 cans of April closing inventory at the variable cost of N150, i.e. **N300,000**; absorption costing adds the N30 of fixed overhead per can and values them at N180, i.e. **N360,000**. The difference of **N60,000** is precisely the fixed overhead attached to those 2,000 cans. Absorption costing therefore reports a higher current asset figure."},{"p":"**Operating profit.** In **April, production (10,000) exceeded sales (8,000)**, so inventory rose by 2,000 cans and absorption costing **carried N60,000 of April's fixed overhead forward** into inventory instead of charging it against April's sales — hence absorption profit is N60,000 higher. In **May, sales (12,000) exceeded production (10,000)**, inventory fell to nil, and that same N60,000 was **released** into May's cost of sales — hence absorption profit is N60,000 *lower* than marginal profit."},{"key":"Over the **two months together both techniques report exactly N2,000,000**. Neither method creates or destroys profit; they differ only in **when** fixed overhead is charged. The rule is worth memorising: when **production exceeds sales**, absorption profit is higher; when **sales exceed production**, marginal profit is higher; when they are equal, the two are the same. The difference is always the change in inventory **units** multiplied by the **fixed overhead per unit**."},{"warn":"This is also the strongest practical argument against absorption costing for internal reporting. In April the company sold 8,000 cans and reported the **higher** profit; in May it sold 12,000 — half as many again — and reported the **lower** one. A manager judged on absorption profit is being rewarded for producing, not for selling."}]}}]}],"formulas":[{"lb":"Contribution","tex":"S - V = C","nt":"S = Sales, V = Variable costs."},{"lb":"Profit","tex":"C - FC = P","nt":"FC = Fixed costs."},{"lb":"Sales at break-even point","tex":"\\dfrac{FC \\times S}{S - V}"},{"lb":"Units needed for a target contribution","tex":"\\dfrac{\\text{Required total contribution}}{\\text{Contribution per unit}}"}],"focus":"Marginal costing is tested constantly, both as a standalone topic and as the engine behind CVP analysis (Chapter 10). Learn $S-V=C$, $C-FC=P$ as one interchangeable pair, not two separate facts — almost every numeric question here is that equation solved for a different unknown. For decision questions (make-or-buy, special order), the discipline that matters most is *including only relevant costs* — strip out sunk costs and apportioned fixed costs that will not actually change before comparing the alternatives.","errors":["Apportioning fixed cost to a \"loss-making\" department or product and concluding it should be dropped, without first checking its actual contribution (Illustration 9.2's whole point).","Including a sunk cost (money already spent, unaffected by the decision) in a make-or-buy or special-order comparison.","Valuing closing inventory at total cost per unit under marginal costing — it should be valued at variable cost per unit only.","Treating a fixed salary as a relevant cost in a decision, rather than only its opportunity cost if redirected elsewhere.","Accepting a special order below normal price without checking that spare capacity actually exists — with no spare capacity, the order would displace full-price sales instead."],"quiz":{"mcq":[{"q":"Sales are N500,000 and variable costs are N320,000. Contribution is","o":["N820,000","N180,000","N320,000","N500,000","N140,000"],"a":1,"w":"Contribution = Sales − Variable costs = 500,000 − 320,000 = N180,000.","calc":"500{,}000 - 320{,}000 = 180{,}000","src":"Chapter 9.1","sec":"9.1"},{"q":"Contribution is N180,000 and fixed costs are N120,000. Profit is","o":["N300,000","N60,000","N180,000","N120,000","N42,000"],"a":1,"w":"Profit = Contribution − Fixed costs = 180,000 − 120,000 = N60,000.","calc":"180{,}000 - 120{,}000 = 60{,}000","src":"Chapter 9.1","sec":"9.1"},{"q":"Under absorption costing, closing inventory is valued at","o":["variable cost per unit only","total (fixed + variable) cost per unit","selling price per unit","prime cost per unit only","zero — never valued"],"a":1,"w":"Absorption costing includes a share of fixed overhead in every unit's cost, so closing inventory is valued at total cost per unit, not variable cost alone.","src":"Chapter 9.1","sec":"9.1"},{"q":"A company has spare capacity and receives a special order at a price below its normal selling price, but still above marginal cost per unit. Based purely on this information, the company should generally","o":["reject the order automatically","accept the order, since it adds to contribution and profit","raise the normal selling price instead","accept only if it equals the normal price","shut down production entirely"],"a":1,"w":"With spare capacity and a price above marginal cost, the order adds positive contribution without displacing existing sales — accepting it increases profit.","src":"Chapter 9.4","sec":"9.4"},{"q":"A cost that has already been irreversibly incurred, and so cannot be relevant to a current decision, is called a","o":["relevant cost","opportunity cost","sunk cost","notional cost","differential cost"],"a":2,"w":"A sunk cost is, by definition, irrecoverable and irrelevant to any decision being made now.","src":"Chapter 9.3","sec":"9.3"},{"q":"Material already sitting in store, with no market value and no alternative use, has a relevant cost of","o":["its original purchase price","its replacement cost","NIL","its insured value","double its purchase price"],"a":2,"w":"With no market value or alternative use, the material's relevant cost for a new decision is NIL — its original cost is sunk.","src":"Chapter 9.3","sec":"9.3"},{"q":"In a make-or-buy decision, a component should generally be BOUGHT rather than made in-house when","o":["the supplier's price exceeds the variable cost of making it","the total variable cost of making it in-house exceeds the supplier's quotation","the company has no spare capacity at all","fixed costs cannot be avoided either way","the component is used in only one product"],"a":1,"w":"Buying is favoured once the variable cost of making the component exceeds the outside supplier's price (adjusted for any fixed-cost savings from buying).","src":"Chapter 9.4","sec":"9.4"},{"q":"A worker earns a fixed monthly salary regardless of what task they are assigned. In a short-term decision, the relevant cost associated with using this worker on a new project is","o":["their full fixed salary","zero, since salary is paid either way","the opportunity cost of what they would otherwise have been doing","double their salary","their salary plus a notional profit margin"],"a":2,"w":"A fixed salary is irrelevant on its own (it is paid regardless); what is relevant is the opportunity cost of diverting that worker's time from whatever else they would otherwise be doing.","src":"Chapter 9.3","sec":"9.3"},{"q":"A firm currently earns a profit of N40,000 with fixed costs of N60,000. If it cuts price so contribution per unit falls, but wants to hold profit at N40,000, it must","o":["sell fewer units than before","sell the same number of units as before","sell more units than before, to make up total contribution","accept a lower profit automatically","increase fixed costs"],"a":2,"w":"With a lower contribution per unit but the same required total contribution (profit + fixed costs = 100,000), more units must be sold to reach that same total.","src":"Chapter 9.4","sec":"9.4"},{"q":"Which of the following is explicitly listed as a limitation of marginal costing?","o":["It is difficult to apply in capital-intensive industries with very large fixed costs","It cannot be used for any short-term decision","It ignores variable costs entirely","It always overstates profit compared with absorption costing","It cannot be combined with break-even analysis"],"a":0,"w":"Marginal costing has limited scope in capital-intensive industries, where fixed costs dominate and the fixed/variable distinction matters less for decisions.","src":"Chapter 9.1","sec":"9.1"}],"theory":[{"q":"Explain the three fundamental differences between absorption costing and marginal costing as shown in their respective income statements.","marks":9,"a":[{"p":"**(1) Treatment of fixed production overhead** — absorption costing includes it in cost of goods sold; marginal costing treats it as a period cost, charged in full regardless of production level."},{"p":"**(2) Valuation of closing inventory** — absorption costing values it at total (fixed + variable) cost per unit; marginal costing values it at variable cost per unit only, since fixed costs are assumed already covered by contribution."},{"p":"**(3) The first profit line shown** — absorption costing's income statement shows gross profit first; marginal costing's shows contribution margin (sales less total variable cost) first. Except where production exactly equals sales in the period, the two methods will show different profit at this first stage."}],"src":"Chapter 9.1","sec":"9.1"},{"q":"What benefits are gained from marginal costing? Are there any pitfalls in its application? Discuss critically.","marks":12,"a":[{"p":"**Benefits**: constant cost per unit regardless of volume; facilitates cost control via flexible budgeting; removes the complications of fixed-overhead allocation/apportionment/absorption entirely; gives a clearer, more logical basis for pricing and tendering; aids profit planning by showing cost-volume-profit relationships directly; and gives a more realistic, uniform valuation of inventory (at marginal cost, not an arbitrary total-cost figure)."},{"p":"**Pitfalls**: splitting overheads into fixed and variable components is genuinely difficult in practice; the technique can over-emphasise the selling function at the expense of production efficiency; it is poorly suited to industries with large WIP inventories, or that are capital-intensive with very large fixed costs; and selling price cannot reasonably be set on contribution alone without regard to whether fixed costs are ultimately covered."}],"src":"Chapter 9.1","sec":"9.1"}]}},{"n":10,"t":"Cost-Volume-Profit Analysis","brief":"How total cost, total revenue and profit move together as sales volume changes: break-even point by the equation and contribution-margin techniques, the Profit/Volume (P/V) ratio, margin of safety, the break-even chart and P/V graph, and the assumptions and limitations underlying the whole technique — all built on marginal costing (Chapter 9).","outcomes":["Explain the meaning and importance of CVP analysis","Explain break-even analysis and compute the Break-Even Point, and the P/V ratio","Explain margin of safety and use a formula to determine it","Present a graphical approach for CVP analysis"],"secs":[{"n":"10.1","t":"Cost-Volume-Profit analysis","b":[{"def":{"t":"Cost-Volume-Profit (CVP) analysis","d":"the analysis of how total costs, total revenues and total profits relate to sales volume — used to predict the effect of changes in cost and sales volume on profit, and to guide decisions on starting, expanding or diversifying production."}},{"p":"Questions CVP analysis answers: what would production cost under different circumstances? What volume of production is needed? How many units of each product must be made for a target profit? What is the gap between selling price and production cost? What is the effect of a percentage change in cost on volume and profit?"},{"p":"Used well, CVP helps with: the budgeting process (the sales volume needed for profit, and the budget's \"safety margin\"); pricing and sales-volume decisions; sales-mix decisions (what proportion of each product to sell); and decisions on cost structure and production capacity."},{"note":"CVP analysis is the **broad** concept; its narrower, more specific form is **break-even analysis**."}]},{"n":"10.2","t":"Break-even analysis","b":[{"def":{"t":"Break-Even Point (BEP)","d":"the level of production activity at which there is neither profit nor loss — total cost equals total revenue."}},{"tex":"\\text{At BEP:} \\quad TC = TR \\quad \\text{i.e.} \\quad TR - TC = 0"},{"note":"After BEP, no *further* fixed cost needs recovering — every additional unit's contribution flows straight to profit, limited only by its variable cost."},{"h4":"Assumptions of break-even analysis"},{"p":"Break-even and CVP analysis rest on assumptions rarely fully true in practice — a change in any one of them shifts the break-even point, so results must always be read with these limitations in mind:"},{"ul":["cost variability behaves predictably;","costs can genuinely be split into fixed and variable components;","total fixed costs stay constant across the relevant range;","variable cost per unit is strictly proportional to volume;","selling price does not change as volume changes;","there is only one product, or (with several) the sales mix stays constant;","the general price level does not change;","productivity per worker stays roughly unchanged;","production and sales are synchronised — no inventory build-up;","revenue and cost are compared against a common activity base (e.g. sales value or units produced); and","plant efficiency can be predicted."]},{"h4":"Computing the break-even point"},{"p":"Two popular mathematical approaches:"},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Technique","How it works"],"rows":[["**(i) Equation technique**","Sales = Variable expenses + Fixed expenses + Profit (i.e. $TR = TC + \\text{Profit}$), solved for the unknown — usually the number of units, with profit set to zero at BEP."],["**(ii) Contribution margin technique**","Unit contribution = unit selling price − unit variable cost; BEP (units) = Fixed expenses ÷ unit contribution."]]}},{"eg":{"tag":"Study text","t":"Illustration 10.1 — both techniques on the same problem","open":true,"q":[{"p":"Alhaji Gazali sells toy rockets at a trade fair, buying at N5 each (unsold rockets returnable), selling at N9 each; booth rent (fixed) N2,000. Find (a) the break-even quantity, and (b) the quantity needed for a 20% operating margin on sales."}],"a":[{"h4":"(a) Equation technique"},{"p":"Let $A$ = units to break even: $9A = 5A + 2{,}000 + 0$."},{"tex":"4A = 2{,}000 \\implies A = 500\\text{ units}"},{"h4":"(a) Contribution margin technique — same answer"},{"p":"Unit contribution $= 9 - 5 = N4$."},{"tex":"\\text{BEP} = \\dfrac{2{,}000}{4} = 500\\text{ units}"},{"h4":"(b) 20% operating margin"},{"p":"Let $X$ = units needed: $9X = 5X + 2{,}000 + 0.2(9X)$."},{"tex":"9X - 1.8X - 5X = 2{,}000 \\implies 2.2X = 2{,}000 \\implies X \\approx 909.1 \\approx 910\\text{ units}"}]}},{"h4":"Other CVP formulae"},{"fbox":{"h":"CVP formulae","rows":[{"lb":"Break-even point, in money terms","tex":"\\text{BEP}_{N} = \\dfrac{\\text{Fixed Cost}}{\\text{C/S Ratio (P/V Ratio)}}"},{"lb":"C/S (Profit-Volume) ratio","tex":"\\dfrac{\\text{Contribution per unit}}{\\text{Sales price per unit}} \\times 100"},{"lb":"Sales for a target profit, in units","tex":"\\dfrac{\\text{Fixed Costs} + \\text{Target Profit}}{\\text{Contribution per unit}}"},{"lb":"Sales for a target profit, in money terms","tex":"\\dfrac{\\text{Fixed Costs} + \\text{Target Profit}}{\\text{C/S Ratio (P/V Ratio)}}"},{"lb":"Sales for a target AFTER-TAX profit, in units","tex":"\\dfrac{\\text{Fixed Costs} + \\dfrac{\\text{Target after-tax profit}}{1 - \\text{tax rate}}}{\\text{Contribution per unit}}"},{"lb":"Break-even point, multi-product firm","tex":"\\dfrac{\\text{Total Fixed Cost, all products}}{\\text{Weighted C/S Ratio}}","nt":"Weighted C/S ratio = total contribution of all products ÷ total sales revenue of all products."}]}},{"warn":"For a target **after-tax** profit, the tax adjustment divides the after-tax target **up** to its before-tax equivalent — $\\text{before-tax profit} = \\text{after-tax profit} / (1-\\text{tax rate})$ — never multiplied by $(1-\\text{tax rate})$, which would move the number the wrong way. (Illustration 10.2(c) below applies this correctly even though its own printed working shows the operation the wrong way round; the numeric answer it reaches only follows from dividing, not multiplying — a source transcription slip in the formula display, not in the arithmetic.)"},{"note":"The single-product formulae above apply equally to a firm with a genuinely **constant** sales mix across several products — only a firm whose mix actually varies needs the weighted multi-product version."},{"h4":"The Profit-Volume (P/V) ratio"},{"def":{"t":"Profit-Volume (P/V) ratio / Contribution Margin Ratio (CMR)","d":"the relationship between contribution and sales revenue — a measure of a product's profitability."}},{"fbox":{"h":"Three equivalent ways to compute the P/V ratio","rows":[{"lb":"(1) Per unit","tex":"\\dfrac{S - V}{S}","nt":"S = unit selling price, V = unit variable cost."},{"lb":"(2) In total","tex":"\\dfrac{TSR - TVC}{TSR}","nt":"TSR = total sales revenue, TVC = total variable cost."},{"lb":"(3) From fixed cost and profit","tex":"\\dfrac{FC + \\text{Profit}}{\\text{Total Sales}}"}]}},{"eg":{"tag":"Study text","t":"Illustration 10.2 — BEP, P/V ratio and margin of safety, worked through eight related questions","open":true,"q":[{"p":"Time Dey Nigeria Ltd sells a single product at N20/unit. Last year: sales N800,000; direct materials N120,000; direct wages N160,000; variable production overhead N80,000; fixed production overhead N100,000; fixed admin. overhead N75,000; fixed selling & distribution overhead N60,000; net profit before tax N205,000; tax 40%."}],"a":[{"p":"Units sold $= 800{,}000 / 20 = 40{,}000$. Total variable cost $= 120{,}000 + 160{,}000 + 80{,}000 = N360{,}000$, i.e. N9/unit. Contribution/unit $= 20 - 9 = N11$. Total fixed cost $= 100{,}000 + 75{,}000 + 60{,}000 = N235{,}000$. P/V ratio $= 11/20 = 0.55$."},{"h4":"(a) Break-even point"},{"tex":"\\text{BEP (units)} = \\dfrac{235{,}000}{11} \\approx 21{,}364\\text{ units}"},{"tex":"\\text{BEP (N)} = 21{,}364 \\times 20 \\approx N427{,}273"},{"h4":"(b) P/V ratio and margin of safety"},{"p":"P/V ratio $= 0.55$, meaning **55 kobo of contribution** is generated per N1 of sales (contribution, not straight \"profit\" — once fixed costs are already fully covered, each further N1 of sales does add 55 kobo of profit, but below that point it is recovering fixed cost, not yet adding to profit)."},{"table":{"align":"lrr","head":["","N","Units"],"rows":[["Sales","800,000","40,000"],["Break-even point","427,273","21,364"],["**Margin of safety**","**372,727**","**18,636**"],["M/S ratio","47%","47%"]]}},{"key":"A margin of safety of 47% means sales could fall by **more than 47%** before the company starts making a loss."},{"h4":"(c) Units for an after-tax target profit of N150,000"},{"p":"Before-tax equivalent of the target $= 150{,}000 / (1-0.4) = N250{,}000$."},{"tex":"\\text{Units} = \\dfrac{235{,}000 + 250{,}000}{11} \\approx 44{,}091\\text{ units}"},{"h4":"(d) Sales value for a 15%-of-revenue net profit before tax"},{"p":"Let $Y$ = required sales. Contribution $= 0.55Y$, and this must cover fixed cost plus the target profit of $0.15Y$: $0.55Y = 235{,}000 + 0.15Y$."},{"tex":"0.40Y = 235{,}000 \\implies Y = N587{,}500"},{"h4":"(e) % volume increase for a profit 20% higher than last year"},{"p":"Target profit before tax $= 1.20 \\times 205{,}000 = N246{,}000$."},{"tex":"\\text{Units} = \\dfrac{235{,}000 + 246{,}000}{11} \\approx 43{,}727\\text{ units}"},{"tex":"\\%\\text{ increase} = \\dfrac{43{,}727 - 40{,}000}{40{,}000} \\times 100 \\approx 9.32\\%"},{"h4":"(f) New selling price to hold the same P/V ratio, with variable cost up 12%"},{"p":"New variable cost $= 9 \\times 1.12 = N10.08$. Let RSP = revised selling price: $0.55 = (\\text{RSP} - 10.08)/\\text{RSP}$."},{"tex":"0.45 \\times \\text{RSP} = 10.08 \\implies \\text{RSP} = N22.40"},{"p":"Check: $(22.40 - 10.08)/22.40 = 12.32/22.40 = 0.55$ — confirmed."},{"h4":"(g) Sales value to maintain the SAME profit (N205,000), price unchanged, variable cost up 12%"},{"p":"New contribution/unit $= 20 - 10.08 = N9.92$."},{"tex":"\\text{Sales value} = \\dfrac{(235{,}000 + 205{,}000)}{9.92} \\times 20 \\approx N887{,}097"},{"h4":"(h) Revised income statement — 10% salesmen's commission, price cut 13%, volume up 30%"},{"p":"New price $= 20 \\times 0.87 = N17.40$. New volume $= 40{,}000 \\times 1.30 = 52{,}000$ units. Variable cost stays at the **original** N9/unit (this scenario does not include the 12% variable-cost rise from parts (f)/(g))."},{"table":{"align":"lr","head":["","N"],"rows":[["Sales (52,000 × 17.40)","904,800"],["Less: variable cost (52,000 × 9)","468,000"],["Less: salesmen's commission (10% of 904,800)","90,480"],["**Total contribution**","**346,320**"],["Less: fixed costs","235,000"],["**Net profit before tax**","**111,320**"],["Less: tax (40%)","44,528"],["**Net profit after tax**","**66,792**"]]}}]}}]},{"n":"10.3","t":"CVP graphs","b":[{"p":"Alongside the mathematical formulae, businesses often present the same relationships as a chart, so managers can appraise profitability at a glance without getting \"bogged down\" in the report's own detail. Two graph types are covered:"},{"h4":"The Break-Even Chart"},{"p":"Costs and revenue sit on the **Y-axis**; output (units) sits on the **X-axis**. The **total cost line** is fixed cost plus variable cost at each output level; the **total revenue line** is output × unit selling price. Their intersection is the break-even point."},{"eg":{"tag":"Study text","t":"Illustration 10.3 — reading a break-even chart","open":true,"q":[{"p":"Variable production cost N3/unit, variable selling cost N1/unit (total variable cost N4/unit), fixed costs N6,000, selling price N6/unit, budgeted output/sales 3,600 units. Find the break-even point and margin of safety, as the chart would show them."}],"a":[{"p":"Unit contribution $= 6 - 4 = N2$."},{"tex":"\\text{BEP} = \\dfrac{6{,}000}{2} = 3{,}000\\text{ units} = N18{,}000\\text{ of sales}"},{"table":{"align":"lrr","head":["","Revenue (N)","Units"],"rows":[["Budgeted","21,600","3,600"],["Break-even point","18,000","3,000"],["**Margin of safety**","**3,600**","**600**"]]}},{"key":"The chart shows three named regions: a **loss area** (below BEP, where the total-cost line sits above the total-revenue line), a **profit area** (above BEP), and the **margin of safety** — the horizontal gap on the chart between the BEP output and the budgeted output. Sales can fall by up to N3,600 (600 units) and the firm still just breaks even."}]}},{"h4":"The Profit-Volume (P/V) Chart"},{"p":"Plots **profit directly** (Y-axis) against **sales revenue** (X-axis), rather than plotting cost and revenue as two separate lines. To draw it: (i) plot the break-even sales value on the X-axis, where profit is zero; (ii) plot the loss at zero sales — this equals the **total fixed cost**, taken as a loss on the Y-axis; (iii) join the two points with a straight line. Using Illustration 10.3's figures, the line runs from $(0, -6{,}000)$ to $(18{,}000, 0)$; margin of safety reads directly off the X-axis as the gap between the break-even sales value and the budgeted sales value (N21,600 − N18,000 = N3,600, or 16.67% of budgeted revenue)."}]},{"n":"10.4","t":"Limitations of break-even analysis","b":[{"p":"The limitations stem directly from its underlying assumptions (§10.2):"},{"ol":["assuming fixed cost is constant at **every** activity level is unrealistic — fixed cost often behaves as a **stepped** cost instead, which the plain break-even model does not capture;","results are only trustworthy **within** the activity range where costs are genuinely known accurately;","CVP represents a **short-term** relationship, and is inappropriate for long-term analysis;","variable cost and sales are assumed **linear**, but bulk discounts, overtime premiums, learning-curve effects and special-price contracts really make them curve, not a straight line;","the technique assumes **perfect knowledge** of cost and revenue behaviour, ignoring the risk and uncertainty that is always genuinely present;","it assumes activity level is the **only** thing driving cost and revenue changes, when in practice many other factors do too;","individual cost components (material, labour, etc.) do not all react to activity in the same way, even though the model implicitly treats variable cost as one uniform block;","it unrealistically assumes a **single product** (or a constant mix, or a constant mark-up on marginal cost) — an oversimplification for a real multi-product business; and","it assumes a **perfect market** in which the firm is a pure price-taker, rarely true in practice."]}]},{"n":"10.5","t":"Worksheet summary — definitions, formulae and structure","b":[{"p":"Everything in this chapter, compressed for a last read-through."},{"h3":"Section-by-section checklist"},{"ol":["**§10.1 CVP** — the broad study of how cost/revenue/profit move with volume; supports budgeting, pricing, sales-mix and capacity decisions.","**§10.2 Break-even analysis** — BEP is where TC=TR; 11 named assumptions (fixed cost constant, variable cost strictly proportional, price fixed, single product/constant mix, no inventory build-up, etc.); 2 computation techniques (equation; contribution margin); the P/V ratio (3 equivalent formulas) and margin of safety (Sf − Sb, and as a %); the multi-product BEP needs a *weighted* C/S ratio.","**§10.3 Graphs** — the break-even chart plots cost/revenue vs output, showing loss area/profit area/margin of safety directly; the P/V chart plots profit itself vs sales revenue as a single straight line from (0, −Fixed Cost) to (BEP sales, 0).","**§10.4 Limitations** — 9 named weaknesses, all traceable back to the assumptions in §10.2 not holding perfectly in the real world."]},{"h3":"All the terms"},{"ul":["**Break-Even Point (BEP)** — where TC = TR, zero profit.","**P/V ratio (Contribution Margin Ratio)** — contribution ÷ sales; measures a product's profitability per unit of revenue.","**Margin of safety** — forecast sales minus break-even sales; how far sales can fall before a loss starts.","**C/S ratio** — another name for the P/V ratio."]},{"h3":"B. Formula box"},{"fbox":{"h":"CVP formulas (recap)","rows":[{"lb":"BEP, units","tex":"\\dfrac{\\text{Fixed Cost}}{\\text{Contribution per unit}}"},{"lb":"BEP, money value","tex":"\\dfrac{\\text{Fixed Cost}}{\\text{P/V Ratio}}"},{"lb":"P/V ratio","tex":"\\dfrac{\\text{Contribution}}{\\text{Sales}}"},{"lb":"Margin of safety (value)","tex":"S_f - S_b"},{"lb":"Margin of safety (%)","tex":"\\dfrac{S_f - S_b}{S_f} \\times 100"},{"lb":"Sales for a target after-tax profit, units","tex":"\\dfrac{\\text{Fixed Cost} + \\dfrac{\\text{Target after-tax profit}}{1-\\text{tax rate}}}{\\text{Contribution per unit}}"}]}}]},{"n":"10.6","t":"End-of-chapter questions (study text)","b":[{"eg":{"tag":"Study text","t":"Multiple-choice and short-answer questions, with answers","open":true,"q":[{"ol":["Which one of these is NOT an assumption of break-even analysis? (A) Fixed costs remain constant  (B) Variable costs vary proportionally with volume  (C) There is no synchronisation between production and sales  (D) Costs can be resolved into their fixed and variable components  (E) Selling price does not change as volume changes","What is margin of safety? (A) The amount by which actual or budgeted output/sales may fall short of the budget without incurring a loss  (B) The amount by which budgeted output/sales may fall short of the actual without incurring a loss  (C) The point where actual output/sales agree with the budget  (D) The area immediately preceding the break-even point  (E) The area where fixed assets are equal to variable costs","What happens in the area before the break-even point? (A) No profit, no loss  (B) Marginal profit  (C) Huge profit  (D) Loss  (E) No revenue","The point at which total revenues equal total costs is known as ……………………..","The number of units of a product that must be sold to break even is given by the formula ………….","Break-even point in Naira can be calculated by multiplying the break-even point in units with ……………………"]}],"a":[{"ol":["**C** — synchronisation between production and sales (no inventory build-up) **is** an assumption, so its *absence* is not; every other option lists a real assumption.","**A** — the amount by which actual/budgeted output or sales may fall short of the budget without incurring a loss.","**D — loss.** Before BEP, total cost still exceeds total revenue.","**Break-even point.**","**Fixed Costs ÷ Contribution per unit.**","**Unit selling price.**"]}]}}]},{"n":"10.7","t":"Study text examination-type questions, worked","b":[{"p":"The study text closes the chapter with ten examination-type questions, numbered 9.1 to 9.10 in the book (the numbering carries over from the previous chapter). All are reproduced below with full answers."},{"eg":{"tag":"Exam type — Q9.1","t":"An electrical goods manufacturer — is direct labour really variable?","q":[{"p":"An electrical goods manufacturing company made a **10% profit on sales of N1 million** in the last trading year. The composition of its costs was **direct labour 25%, direct materials 60% and fixed overhead 15%**."},{"p":"The general manager has drawn attention to the fact that, although sales were just below forecast, the profit was very much lower than expected. Investigation shows the significant difference appears to be caused by the **direct labour costs**. The company uses the marginal cost accounting principle to price its products, and in all price quotations direct labour was treated as a **variable cost directly related to volume of output**. However, the review indicates that the direct labour cost showed **little change when output decreased** for any reason."},{"p":"**Required:** **(a)** state whether you agree that direct labour costs should be treated as wholly variable with output; **(b)** calculate, using last year's results, the sales volume at break-even point and the margin of safety when direct labour costs are treated as (i) wholly variable and (ii) fixed."}],"a":[{"h4":"Working — last year's cost structure"},{"stmt":{"t":"Last year (N)","rows":[["Sales","",1000000],["Profit at 10% of sales","",100000],["**Total cost**","",900000,"@t"],"@gap",["Direct materials  (60% of N900,000)","",540000],["Direct labour  (25% of N900,000)","",225000],["Fixed overhead  (15% of N900,000)","",135000],["**Total cost**","",900000,"@tt"]]}},{"h4":"(a)  Should direct labour be treated as wholly variable?"},{"p":"**No — on the evidence here it should not.** The review shows direct labour cost \"showed little change when output decreased\", which is the definition of a cost that is **not** variable. Treating it as variable has three consequences, all of which explain exactly what the general manager observed:"},{"ol":["**The contribution per unit is overstated**, because N225,000 of cost that does not in fact fall with output has been deducted in arriving at it.","**The break-even point is understated** (see (b) below), so the company believed it was far safer than it was.","**Prices quoted were too low.** Quotations assumed that labour cost would fall if the order were not taken; it would not. So each order was quoted on a contribution that did not exist, and a small shortfall in volume produced a large shortfall in profit."]},{"p":"Direct labour is genuinely variable only where it is paid by **piecework**, or where casual or contract labour can be engaged and released with output. Where workers are on **guaranteed weekly wages**, with notice periods and union agreements, labour is a **fixed or stepped** cost in the short run, however \"direct\" it may be for product-costing purposes."},{"warn":"Note the distinction being tested. **Direct / indirect** is a classification by **nature** — can the cost be traced to a cost unit? **Fixed / variable** is a classification by **behaviour** — does it change with activity? A cost can be direct **and** fixed, as here. The two classifications are independent, and assuming \"direct therefore variable\" is precisely the error that cost this company its profit."},{"h4":"(b)(i)  Direct labour treated as wholly variable"},{"stmt":{"t":"Break-even and margin of safety — labour variable (N)","rows":[["Sales","",1000000],["Variable costs:  materials 540,000 + labour 225,000","",765000],["**Contribution**","",235000,"@t"],["**P/V ratio**  (235,000 ÷ 1,000,000)","","23.5%"],["Fixed costs","",135000],"@gap",["**Break-even sales**  (135,000 ÷ 0.235)","",574468,"@t"],["**Margin of safety**  (1,000,000 − 574,468)","",425532],["**Margin of safety as % of sales**","","42.6%","@tt"]]}},{"h4":"(b)(ii)  Direct labour treated as fixed"},{"stmt":{"t":"Break-even and margin of safety — labour fixed (N)","rows":[["Sales","",1000000],["Variable costs:  materials only","",540000],["**Contribution**","",460000,"@t"],["**P/V ratio**  (460,000 ÷ 1,000,000)","","46.0%"],["Fixed costs:  overhead 135,000 + labour 225,000","",360000],"@gap",["**Break-even sales**  (360,000 ÷ 0.46)","",782609,"@t"],["**Margin of safety**  (1,000,000 − 782,609)","",217391],["**Margin of safety as % of sales**","","21.7%","@tt"]]}},{"key":"Treating labour correctly as fixed **raises the break-even point by N208,141** and **halves the margin of safety**, from 42.6% to 21.7%. That is the answer to the general manager's question. On the company's own (wrong) assumption, sales could fall 42% before profit disappeared; in reality they could fall only 22%. The business was running far closer to the edge than it believed, and a small shortfall in sales therefore produced a disproportionate collapse in profit — which is exactly what **high operating gearing** does."}]}},{"eg":{"tag":"Exam type — Q9.2","t":"Prudent Nigeria Ltd — deriving fixed cost and P/V ratio from two years' totals","q":[{"p":"The following figures relate to Prudent Nigeria Ltd, which manufactures a varied range of products:"},{"stmt":{"t":"Two years' results (N)","rows":[["Year","Total sales","Total costs","@hd"],[1,39000,34800],[2,43000,37600]]}},{"p":"Assuming stability in prices, with variable costs carefully controlled to reflect predetermined relationships, and an unvarying figure for fixed costs:"},{"p":"**Required:** **(i)** calculate (a) the fixed cost, (b) the profit/volume ratio, (c) the break-even point, (d) the margin of safety for years 1 and 2. **(ii)** How might the profit/volume ratio be improved, apart from increasing prices or reducing costs?"}],"a":[{"h4":"Step 1 — the profit for each year, and the P/V ratio"},{"p":"With fixed costs unchanged, every naira of extra contribution falls straight into profit — so the **change in profit divided by the change in sales** is the P/V ratio. This is the high-low method of §2.1.2a applied to total figures."},{"stmt":{"t":"Deriving the P/V ratio (N)","rows":[["","Year 1","Year 2","Change","@hd"],["Total sales",39000,43000,4000],["Total costs",34800,37600,2800],["**Profit**",4200,5400,1200,"@tt"]]}},{"tex":"\\text{P/V ratio} = \\frac{\\text{Change in profit}}{\\text{Change in sales}} = \\frac{1{,}200}{4{,}000} = 30\\%"},{"h4":"(i)(a)  The fixed cost"},{"p":"With a P/V ratio of 30%, variable cost is **70% of sales**:"},{"stmt":{"t":"Fixed cost (N)","rows":[["","Year 1","Year 2","@hd"],["Total cost",34800,37600],["Less: variable cost  (70% × 39,000;  70% × 43,000)",27300,30100],["**Fixed cost**",7500,7500,"@tt"]]}},{"note":"The fixed cost comes out at the same N7,500 from both years, which confirms the P/V ratio of 30%. Always use the second year as a check — if the two fixed-cost figures differ, the P/V ratio is wrong or the assumption of stable costs does not hold."},{"h4":"(i)(b)–(d)  P/V ratio, break-even point and margin of safety"},{"stmt":{"t":"Summary of answers (N)","rows":[["**(b) Profit/volume ratio**","","30%"],["**(c) Break-even point**  (fixed cost ÷ P/V ratio = 7,500 ÷ 0.30)","",25000],"@gap",["**(d) Margin of safety — Year 1**  (39,000 − 25,000)",14000,"*35.9% of sales*"],["**       Margin of safety — Year 2**  (43,000 − 25,000)",18000,"*41.9% of sales*"]]}},{"h4":"(ii)  Improving the P/V ratio without raising prices or cutting costs"},{"p":"The P/V ratio is contribution ÷ sales, so for a multi-product company it can be improved by **changing what is sold** rather than what it costs or what it is sold for:"},{"ol":["**Change the sales mix** towards products with a higher individual P/V ratio — the single most effective lever available to a company selling a \"varied range of products\", as this one does.","**Promote and give more sales effort to high-contribution products**, and withdraw effort from low-contribution ones.","**Introduce new products with a higher P/V ratio**, or redesign existing ones (value analysis) to use less material and labour without reducing their utility.","**Substitute cheaper materials or methods** of equal quality, which raises contribution without a price change.","**Improve efficiency and reduce wastage, scrap and rework**, so that less variable cost is consumed per naira of sales.","**Reduce discounts, allowances and returns**, which raises the *net* price realised without altering the list price.","**Automate** — substituting machinery for direct labour converts variable cost into fixed cost and so raises the P/V ratio *(though note it raises the break-even point too, and with it the risk, as Q9.1 above demonstrates)*."]}]}},{"eg":{"tag":"Exam type — Q9.3","t":"CVP assumptions, and Yakasai Toys Company's break-even and projected income","q":[{"p":"**(a)** Cost-volume-profit analysis is a technique available to management to understand better the inter-relationship of several factors which affect a firm's profit. As with many such techniques, the accountant oversimplifies the real world by making certain assumptions. **Mention five major assumptions underlying C-V-P analysis.**"},{"p":"**(b)** Yakasai Toys Company's tentative budget for product Q for 2004 is:"},{"stmt":{"t":"Budget for product Q, 2004 (N)","rows":[["Sales (2,500 units) @ N40","",100000],"@gap",["**Manufacturing cost of goods sold**","","","@hd"],["Direct labour",15000,""],["Direct materials",14000,""],["Variable factory overhead",10000,""],["Fixed factory overhead",5000,44000],["**Gross profit**","",56000,"@t"],"@gap",["**Selling expenses**","","","@hd"],["Variable",6000,""],["Fixed",10000,""],["**Administration expenses**","","","@hd"],["Variable",5000,""],["Fixed",10000,31000],["**Operating income**","",25000,"@tt"]]}},{"p":"**Required:** (i) how many units of product Q would have to be sold to break even? (ii) what would the operating income be if projected sales increased by 30%?"}],"a":[{"h4":"(a)  Major assumptions underlying C-V-P analysis"},{"ol":["**All costs can be resolved into fixed and variable elements**, and the split is known and reliable.","**Cost and revenue behaviour is linear** over the relevant range — variable cost per unit and selling price per unit are constant, so total cost and total revenue are straight lines.","**Total fixed cost remains constant** over the whole range of activity considered, and does not step.","**The sales mix remains constant** where more than one product is sold — otherwise there is no single P/V ratio.","**Production equals sales**, so there is no change in inventory levels and no fixed overhead is carried forward or released.","**Volume is the only factor affecting cost and revenue** — efficiency, productivity, technology, methods and price levels are all unchanged.","**The analysis is valid only within the relevant range** of activity for which the cost behaviour was established, and only in the short run."]},{"h4":"Working — the cost structure of product Q"},{"stmt":{"t":"Separating fixed and variable (N)","rows":[["**Variable costs**","","","@hd"],["Direct labour",15000,""],["Direct materials",14000,""],["Variable factory overhead",10000,""],["Variable selling expenses",6000,""],["Variable administration expenses",5000,50000],["**Variable cost per unit  (50,000 ÷ 2,500 units)**","","N20","@t"],"@gap",["**Fixed costs**","","","@hd"],["Fixed factory overhead",5000,""],["Fixed selling expenses",10000,""],["Fixed administration expenses",10000,25000],"@gap",["Selling price per unit","","N40"],["**Contribution per unit  (40 − 20)**","","N20","@t"],["**P/V ratio  (20 ÷ 40)**","","50%","@tt"]]}},{"note":"Note that the budget as presented is in **absorption** form — it computes a gross profit, which mixes fixed factory overhead into cost of sales. The first task in any CVP question presented this way is to **re-sort every line into fixed or variable**, ignoring the manufacturing / selling / administration classification entirely. Variable selling and administration costs are just as variable as direct materials."},{"h4":"(b)(i)  Break-even point"},{"tex":"\\text{Break-even units} = \\frac{\\text{Fixed costs}}{\\text{Contribution per unit}} = \\frac{25{,}000}{20} = 1{,}250 \\text{ units}"},{"tex":"\\text{Break-even sales value} = 1{,}250 \\times N40 = N50{,}000 \\qquad \\left(\\text{or } \\frac{25{,}000}{0.50} = N50{,}000\\right)"},{"h4":"(b)(ii)  Operating income if sales increase by 30%"},{"stmt":{"t":"Projected results at 3,250 units (N)","rows":[["","Budget (2,500 units)","Projected (3,250 units)","@hd"],["Sales @ N40",100000,130000],["Less: variable costs @ N20",50000,65000],["**Contribution**",50000,65000,"@t"],["Less: fixed costs",25000,25000],["**Operating income**",25000,40000,"@tt"]]}},{"key":"A **30% increase in sales produces a 60% increase in profit** — from N25,000 to N40,000. That is **operating gearing** at work: fixed costs do not rise with the extra volume, so the whole of the extra contribution of N15,000 falls into profit. The same gearing works in reverse: a 30% *fall* in sales would cut contribution to N35,000 and profit to N10,000, a 60% collapse. Margin of safety at the budgeted level is $(100{,}000 - 50{,}000) \\div 100{,}000 = 50\\%$, so the company has reasonable protection."}]}},{"eg":{"tag":"Exam type — Q9.4","t":"Universal Cloth Manufacturing — break-even and profit across three shift levels","q":[{"p":"Universal Cloth Manufacturing Limited has rented a new factory and intends to produce a newly developed man-made fibre, \"Texlon\". It is planned to produce Texlon in the new mill first on a **single shift**, six months later on a **double shift**, and three months after that on a **treble shift**. Production, cost and selling price per unit are:"},{"stmt":{"t":"Shift data","rows":[["","Single shift","Double shift","Treble shift","@hd"],["Production per week (units)","up to 1,500","1,500 – 2,500","2,501 – 4,000"],["Selling price per unit","N5.00","N5.00","N5.00"],["Variable cost per unit","N3.00","N3.20","N3.50"],["Fixed cost per week","N2,000","N2,520","N3,030"]]}},{"p":"**Required:** (i) the break-even units per week of each shift; (ii) the total unit cost for each shift at break-even and at full capacity; (iii) the profit or loss for each shift at 50% of the installed (or increased) capacity — single shift 750 units, double shift 2,000 units, treble shift 3,250 units; (iv) the profit per week at full capacity for each shift, and the unit variable and fixed cost."}],"a":[{"h4":"Working — contribution per unit at each shift level"},{"stmt":{"t":"Contribution per unit (N)","rows":[["","Single","Double","Treble","@hd"],["Selling price",5.0,5.0,5.0],["Less: variable cost",3.0,3.2,3.5],["**Contribution per unit**",2.0,1.8,1.5,"@tt"]]}},{"note":"Contribution **falls** as shifts are added — N2.00, N1.80, N1.50 — because night and weekend working carry shift premiums, and fixed cost rises at each step too. Adding capacity is not free in either direction."},{"h4":"(i)  Break-even units per week"},{"stmt":{"t":"Break-even point","rows":[["","Single","Double","Treble","@hd"],["Fixed cost per week (N)",2000,2520,3030],["÷ contribution per unit (N)",2.0,1.8,1.5],["**Break-even units per week**",1000,1400,2020,"@tt"]]}},{"warn":"Note an important qualification. The double shift operates only between 1,500 and 2,500 units, and the treble only between 2,501 and 4,000 — yet their computed break-even points, 1,400 and 2,020 units, lie **below** their own operating ranges. The figures are arithmetically correct, but they describe a level of output at which that shift pattern would never actually be run. The practical meaning is simply that **once a shift level is operating within its range, it is already past break-even** — which is reassuring, not alarming. A good answer states this rather than quoting the numbers without comment."},{"h4":"(ii)  Total unit cost at break-even and at full capacity"},{"stmt":{"t":"Total unit cost (N)","rows":[["","Single","Double","Treble","@hd"],["**At break-even**","","","","@hd"],["Units",1000,1400,2020],["Fixed cost",2000,2520,3030],["Variable cost  (units × variable cost per unit)",3000,4480,7070],["Total cost",5000,7000,10100],["**Total unit cost at break-even**",5.0,5.0,5.0,"@t"],"@gap",["**At full capacity**","","","","@hd"],["Units",1500,2500,4000],["Fixed cost",2000,2520,3030],["Variable cost",4500,8000,14000],["Total cost",6500,10520,17030],["**Total unit cost at full capacity**",4.333,4.208,4.258,"@tt"]]}},{"note":"The unit cost at break-even is **N5.00 in every case** — necessarily so, because break-even is by definition the point at which total cost equals total revenue, and revenue is N5 per unit. That identity is a free check on your arithmetic."},{"h4":"(iii)  Profit or loss at 50% of the installed (or increased) capacity"},{"stmt":{"t":"Profit / (loss) per week (N)","rows":[["","Single (750 units)","Double (2,000 units)","Treble (3,250 units)","@hd"],["Contribution  (750×2.00;  2,000×1.80;  3,250×1.50)",1500,3600,4875],["Less: fixed cost per week",2000,2520,3030],["**Profit / (loss) per week**",-500,1080,1845,"@tt"]]}},{"p":"The single shift at 750 units makes a **loss of N500 a week**, because 750 units is below its break-even of 1,000. The double and treble shifts are both comfortably profitable at the stated levels."},{"h4":"(iv)  Profit at full capacity, and the unit cost split"},{"stmt":{"t":"At full capacity (N)","rows":[["","Single (1,500)","Double (2,500)","Treble (4,000)","@hd"],["Contribution  (1,500×2.00;  2,500×1.80;  4,000×1.50)",3000,4500,6000],["Less: fixed cost per week",2000,2520,3030],["**Profit per week at full capacity**",1000,1980,2970,"@t"],"@gap",["**Unit variable cost**",3.0,3.2,3.5],["**Unit fixed cost**  (2,000÷1,500;  2,520÷2,500;  3,030÷4,000)",1.333,1.008,0.758],["**Total unit cost**",4.333,4.208,4.258,"@tt"]]}},{"key":"Read the last three rows across. As shifts are added, **unit variable cost rises** (N3.00 → N3.20 → N3.50, the shift premium) while **unit fixed cost falls** (N1.333 → N1.008 → N0.758, the fixed cost spread over more output). The two pull in opposite directions, and the total unit cost reaches its **minimum at the double shift** (N4.208) before rising again at the treble shift. Yet weekly **profit** keeps rising, from N1,000 to N1,980 to N2,970, because the extra volume more than compensates for the slightly higher unit cost. **The lowest-unit-cost option is not the most profitable option** — a point worth making explicitly to the managing director."}]}},{"eg":{"tag":"Exam type — Q9.5","t":"Break-even point explained, the break-even chart, and the CVP relationship","q":[{"p":"**(a)** Explain the term \"break-even point\". Give an example illustrating your answer. Mention the types of problem an accountant can expect to solve with the help of such analysis."},{"p":"**(b)** Draw a break-even chart with a few illustrative figures."},{"p":"**(c)** Explain the cost-volume-profit relationship. How would a change in the selling price affect it?"}],"a":[{"h4":"(a)  The break-even point"},{"def":{"t":"Break-even point","d":"the level of activity at which **total revenue exactly equals total cost**, so that the business makes neither a profit nor a loss. At that point, total contribution is exactly equal to total fixed costs."}},{"fbox":{"h":"Break-even point","rows":[{"lb":"In units","tex":"\\text{BEP (units)} = \\frac{\\text{Fixed costs}}{\\text{Contribution per unit}}"},{"lb":"In sales value","tex":"\\text{BEP (value)} = \\frac{\\text{Fixed costs}}{\\text{P/V ratio}}"}]}},{"p":"**Example.** A firm sells a product at N50, with a variable cost of N30 per unit and fixed costs of N120,000 a year. Contribution is N20 per unit, so:"},{"stmt":{"t":"Illustration","rows":[["Break-even in units:  120,000 ÷ 20","","6,000 units"],["Break-even in sales value:  6,000 × N50","","N300,000"],["P/V ratio:  20 ÷ 50","","40%"],["Check:  120,000 ÷ 0.40","","N300,000 ✓"]]}},{"p":"At 6,000 units: revenue N300,000, variable cost N180,000, contribution N120,000, less fixed costs N120,000 — profit nil. Each unit sold **above** 6,000 adds N20 straight to profit; each unit below costs N20."},{"h4":"(a)  Problems an accountant can solve with break-even analysis"},{"ol":["**What volume must we sell to break even**, or to reach a **target profit**?","**How far can sales fall before we make a loss?** — the margin of safety.","**What happens to profit if volume, price, variable cost or fixed cost changes?** — sensitivity analysis on each variable in turn.","**Should we accept a special order** at below the normal price?","**Should we change the selling price?** — will the extra volume cover the lower margin?","**Which product mix** maximises profit, and what is the effect of changing it?","**Should we substitute fixed cost for variable cost** — automate, or pay commission instead of salary? (See Q9.6 below.)","**Should we make or buy**, or close a department temporarily?","**What level of activity** should the budget be set at, and what is the risk if it is not achieved?"]},{"h4":"(b)  The break-even chart"},{"p":"Using the illustrative figures above — selling price N50, variable cost N30, fixed cost N120,000, capacity 10,000 units:"},{"pre":"N'000\n 500 |                                                        / Total revenue\n     |                                                   /\n 400 |                                              /          _____ Total cost\n     |                                         /        _____/\n 300 |- - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - -*=====  PROFIT area\n     |                               /  ___/|\n 200 |                          / ___/      |\n     |             LOSS    /___/            |\n 150 |              area _/                 |              Fixed cost N120,000\n 120 |===========/==========================|=====================\n     |      /                               |\n     |  /                                   |\n   0 +---------+---------+---------+--------+---------+-----------> units\n     0       2,000     4,000     6,000    6,000     8,000   10,000\n                                      BREAK-EVEN POINT\n                                      6,000 units / N300,000"},{"p":"The chart has four elements: the **fixed cost line**, horizontal at N120,000; the **total cost line**, starting at N120,000 and rising by N30 per unit; the **total revenue line**, starting at the origin and rising by N50 per unit; and the **break-even point** where the two sloping lines cross. The wedge to the left of the crossing is the **loss area**; the widening wedge to the right is the **profit area**; the horizontal distance from break-even to the actual or budgeted sales level is the **margin of safety**."},{"h4":"(c)  The cost-volume-profit relationship"},{"p":"CVP analysis studies how **profit responds to changes in volume**, given the way costs behave. Its core identity is:"},{"tex":"\\text{Profit} = (\\text{Selling price} - \\text{Variable cost per unit}) \\times \\text{Units sold} - \\text{Fixed costs}"},{"p":"Because fixed costs do not move with volume, profit changes by the **contribution per unit** for every extra unit sold — so profit is far more volatile than sales. The three levers are the contribution per unit, the volume, and the fixed cost."},{"h4":"(c)  The effect of a change in selling price"},{"ol":["A **rise in selling price** increases contribution per unit and therefore the **P/V ratio**; the total revenue line on the chart becomes **steeper**; the **break-even point falls**; the margin of safety widens; and profit at any given volume rises.","A **fall in selling price** does the reverse: contribution per unit and the P/V ratio fall, the revenue line flattens, the **break-even point rises**, and more units must be sold merely to stand still.","**But volume will also change**, in the opposite direction — that is the whole point of altering a price. The real question is therefore always: **does the change in volume more than compensate for the change in contribution per unit?**"]},{"p":"**Illustration.** On the figures above, cutting the price from N50 to N45 reduces contribution from N20 to N15 and raises break-even from 6,000 to $120{,}000 \\div 15 = 8{,}000$ units. To earn the same profit as 8,000 units at the old price (a profit of N40,000), the company must now sell $(120{,}000 + 40{,}000) \\div 15 = 10{,}667$ units — a **33% increase in volume** to absorb a **10% price cut**. That calculation, done before the price is changed rather than after, is one of the most valuable things CVP analysis provides."}]}},{"eg":{"tag":"Exam type — Q9.6 (ICAN Intermediate, November 2000)","t":"Gusau manufacturer — four scenarios on salary versus commission","q":[{"p":"You are the Finance Manager of a manufacturing company based in Gusau. The company has been incurring trading losses. Your new Managing Director has returned from a workshop convinced that the Profit/Volume ratio can be used to calculate the effect of changes in volume or costs on profit. The following details are available:"},{"stmt":{"t":"Operating data (N)","rows":[["Selling price per carton of product","",1200],["Variable cost per carton","",1000],["Variable distribution expenses per carton","",50],"@gap",["**Annual fixed expenses**","","","@hd"],["Storage","",200000],["Wages","",100000]]}},{"p":"**Required** — consider each case independently: **(i)** the Marketing Director thinks that by spending **N20,000 monthly on advertising** he can achieve annual sales of **5,000 cartons** — what will the profit be? **(ii)** The wages are paid to the Marketing Field Force; they offer to forgo these wages in exchange for a **N25 commission per carton sold** — if accepted, what would the annual break-even point be in sales revenue and in unit sales? **(iii)** The target profit is **N600,000** — how many cartons must be sold to achieve it (ignoring the Marketing Director's proposal) (a) assuming the field force receives their N100,000 salaries, and (b) assuming they are paid commission? **(iv)** What level of sales produces the same profit whether the Marketing Department is paid by salary or by commission?"}],"a":[{"h4":"Working — the present cost structure"},{"stmt":{"t":"Present contribution and fixed costs (N)","rows":[["Selling price per carton","",1200],["Less: variable cost per carton",1000,""],["          variable distribution expenses per carton",50,1050],["**Contribution per carton**","",150,"@t"],["**P/V ratio**  (150 ÷ 1,200)","","12.5%"],"@gap",["Annual fixed expenses:  storage 200,000 + wages 100,000","",300000],["**Present break-even**  (300,000 ÷ 150)","","2,000 cartons","@tt"]]}},{"h4":"(i)  The advertising proposal"},{"stmt":{"t":"Profit with N20,000 monthly advertising and sales of 5,000 cartons (N)","rows":[["Contribution:  5,000 cartons × N150","",750000],["Less: existing fixed expenses",300000,""],["          advertising  (N20,000 × 12 months)",240000,540000],["**Profit**","",210000,"@tt"]]}},{"note":"The advertising campaign needs $240{,}000 \\div 150 = 1{,}600$ extra cartons merely to pay for itself. It is worth undertaking only if sales would otherwise have been below $5{,}000 - 1{,}600 = 3{,}400$ cartons."},{"h4":"(ii)  Break-even under the commission scheme"},{"stmt":{"t":"Break-even with commission replacing salaries (N)","rows":[["Contribution per carton (as before)","",150],["Less: commission per carton","",25],["**Revised contribution per carton**","",125,"@t"],"@gap",["Fixed expenses:  storage 200,000  (wages of 100,000 now removed)","",200000],"@gap",["**Break-even in units**  (200,000 ÷ 125)","","1,600 cartons","@t"],["**Break-even in sales revenue**  (1,600 × N1,200)","",1920000,"@tt"]]}},{"h4":"(iii)  Cartons needed for a target profit of N600,000"},{"tex":"\\text{Units for a target profit} = \\frac{\\text{Fixed costs} + \\text{Target profit}}{\\text{Contribution per unit}}"},{"stmt":{"t":"Target-profit volume","rows":[["","(a) With salaries","(b) With commission","@hd"],["Fixed costs (N)",300000,200000],["Add: target profit (N)",600000,600000],["**Total to be covered (N)**",900000,800000,"@t"],["÷ contribution per carton (N)",150,125],["**Cartons required**",6000,6400,"@tt"]]}},{"h4":"(iv)  The level of sales at which the two schemes give the same profit"},{"p":"Let $Q$ be the number of cartons. Profit under each scheme is:"},{"tex":"\\text{Salary scheme: } 150Q - 300{,}000 \\qquad\\qquad \\text{Commission scheme: } 125Q - 200{,}000"},{"p":"Setting them equal:"},{"tex":"150Q - 300{,}000 = 125Q - 200{,}000 \\;\\Longrightarrow\\; 25Q = 100{,}000 \\;\\Longrightarrow\\; Q = 4{,}000 \\text{ cartons}"},{"stmt":{"t":"Check at 4,000 cartons (N)","rows":[["","Salary scheme","Commission scheme","@hd"],["Contribution  (4,000 × 150;  4,000 × 125)",600000,500000],["Less: fixed costs",300000,200000],["**Profit**",300000,300000,"@tt"]]}},{"key":"The **indifference point is 4,000 cartons**, and the rule either side of it is the heart of the question. **Below 4,000 cartons the commission scheme is better**, because the company carries N100,000 less fixed cost and pays the field force only when they actually sell. **Above 4,000 cartons the salary scheme is better**, because the N25 a carton given up exceeds the N100,000 saved. Given that the company has been **making trading losses** and is therefore operating well below 4,000 cartons, the commission scheme should be accepted — it also lowers the break-even point from 2,000 to 1,600 cartons, which reduces risk exactly when the company can least afford it. This is the practical value of the P/V ratio the Managing Director was told about."}]}},{"eg":{"tag":"Exam type — Q9.7 (ICAN PE I, May 2001)","t":"ABC Ltd — the effect of a new plant on the P/V ratio, profit and margin of safety","q":[{"p":"ABC Ltd manufactures one product only, which it sells at **N20 per unit**. The existing plant has a maximum capacity of **20,000 units** per annum, at which level net profit is **N1.50 per unit** and the **profit/volume ratio is 20%**. A new plant is to be purchased, having a maximum capacity of **30,000 units** per annum, but this will increase fixed costs by **N15,000** per annum. Variable costs will be **reduced by N4 per unit**, and to achieve an increase in sales the selling price is also to be **reduced by N4 per unit**."},{"p":"**Required:** **(a)** explain what you understand by the terms profit/volume ratio and margin of safety; **(b)** calculate the revised P/V ratio as a result of the purchase of the new plant; **(c)** calculate the number of units which would be required using the new plant to give a **50% increase in profit** compared with maximum production using the old plant; **(d)** calculate the margin of safety if the actual level of sales is **25,000 units**, assuming the new plant is purchased."}],"a":[{"h4":"(a)  The two terms"},{"def":{"t":"Profit/volume (P/V) ratio","d":"contribution expressed as a percentage of sales value — also called the **contribution to sales ratio**. It measures how much of every naira of sales is left after variable costs to cover fixed costs and provide profit, and it is therefore the rate at which profit changes as sales change."}},{"tex":"\\text{P/V ratio} = \\frac{\\text{Contribution}}{\\text{Sales}} \\times 100 = \\frac{\\text{Selling price} - \\text{Variable cost}}{\\text{Selling price}} \\times 100"},{"def":{"t":"Margin of safety","d":"the excess of actual (or budgeted) sales over the break-even level of sales. It measures **how far sales can fall before the business begins to make a loss**, and is usually expressed in units, in sales value, or as a percentage of actual sales."}},{"tex":"\\text{Margin of safety} = \\text{Actual sales} - \\text{Break-even sales} \\qquad\\qquad \\text{Margin of safety \\%} = \\frac{\\text{Margin of safety}}{\\text{Actual sales}} \\times 100"},{"h4":"Working — the existing position"},{"stmt":{"t":"Existing plant at 20,000 units (N)","rows":[["Selling price per unit","",20.0],["Contribution per unit  (P/V ratio 20% × N20)","",4.0],["**Variable cost per unit**  (20 − 4)","",16.0,"@t"],"@gap",["Total contribution  (20,000 × N4)","",80000],["Net profit  (20,000 × N1.50)","",30000],["**Fixed costs**  (80,000 − 30,000)","",50000,"@tt"]]}},{"h4":"(b)  The revised P/V ratio with the new plant"},{"stmt":{"t":"New plant — revised figures (N)","rows":[["Selling price per unit  (20 − 4)","",16.0],["Variable cost per unit  (16 − 4)","",12.0],["**Contribution per unit**","",4.0,"@t"],["**Revised P/V ratio**  (4 ÷ 16)","","**25%**","@tt"],"@gap",["Fixed costs  (50,000 + 15,000)","",65000]]}},{"note":"Contribution per unit is **unchanged at N4** — the N4 cut in price is exactly offset by the N4 saving in variable cost. But the **P/V ratio rises from 20% to 25%**, because the same N4 of contribution is now a larger share of a smaller selling price. This is a useful reminder that a rising P/V ratio does not by itself mean the company is better off: here, each unit still earns the same N4, and fixed costs have gone up by N15,000."},{"h4":"(c)  Units needed for a 50% increase in profit"},{"stmt":{"t":"Target volume with the new plant","rows":[["Maximum profit with the old plant  (20,000 × N1.50)","","N30,000"],["Target profit  (N30,000 + 50%)","","N45,000"],"@gap",["Fixed costs with the new plant","","N65,000"],["Add: target profit","","N45,000"],["**Total contribution required**","","N110,000","@t"],["÷ contribution per unit","","N4.00"],["**Units required**","","27,500 units","@tt"]]}},{"p":"27,500 units is within the new plant's capacity of 30,000, so the target is attainable — provided the market will absorb 27,500 units at the reduced price of N16, a 37.5% increase in volume over the 20,000 units sold at N20."},{"h4":"(d)  Margin of safety at 25,000 units with the new plant"},{"stmt":{"t":"Margin of safety","rows":[["Break-even units  (65,000 ÷ 4)","","16,250 units"],["Actual sales","","25,000 units"],["**Margin of safety in units**","","8,750 units","@t"],["**Margin of safety in sales value**  (8,750 × N16)","","N140,000"],["**Margin of safety as % of actual sales**  (8,750 ÷ 25,000)","","**35%**","@tt"]]}},{"note":"Compare with the old plant: break-even was $50{,}000 \\div 4 = 12{,}500$ units, giving a margin of safety at full capacity of 7,500 units, or **37.5%** of sales. The new plant therefore operates at a slightly **lower** margin of safety at 25,000 units, despite the higher P/V ratio — because the extra N15,000 of fixed cost pushed break-even up by 3,750 units. Profit at 25,000 units would be $25{,}000 \\times 4 - 65{,}000 = N35{,}000$, against N30,000 on the old plant."}]}},{"eg":{"tag":"Exam type — Q9.8","t":"Five forms of pricing described","q":[{"p":"Briefly describe the following forms of pricing: (i) total cost-plus profit percentage; (ii) marginal cost-plus contribution percentage; (iii) break-even pricing; (iv) negotiated pricing; (v) competitors."}],"a":[{"h4":"(i)  Total cost-plus profit percentage"},{"p":"The price is set by adding a predetermined percentage profit to the **full cost** of the product — direct materials, direct labour, direct expenses and a share of both production and non-production overhead."},{"tex":"\\text{Price} = \\text{Total cost} \\times (1 + \\text{profit percentage})"},{"p":"**For:** ensures every cost is recovered and a profit earned; simple; defensible to a customer who can inspect the cost; the normal basis for government and cost-reimbursement contracts. **Against:** it depends on an arbitrary overhead apportionment, so the \"cost\" is not objective; it ignores demand and the competition; it passes the supplier's inefficiency on to the customer; and it is circular, since the overhead per unit depends on the volume, which depends on the price."},{"h4":"(ii)  Marginal cost-plus contribution percentage"},{"p":"The price is set by adding a required contribution percentage to the **marginal (variable) cost** of the product. The resulting price must cover variable cost and make a contribution towards fixed costs and profit, but does not purport to recover a share of fixed cost in every unit."},{"tex":"\\text{Price} = \\text{Marginal cost} \\times (1 + \\text{contribution percentage})"},{"p":"**For:** no arbitrary apportionment is involved; it is flexible, so a lower contribution can be accepted on marginal business, on an export order, or in a slack period; it is the right basis for short-run and special-order pricing. **Against:** total contribution must still cover total fixed costs, so if it is used as the normal basis there is a real risk of under-pricing and of making a loss while every individual sale appears profitable."},{"h4":"(iii)  Break-even pricing"},{"p":"The price is set at the level which exactly covers total cost at an expected volume — that is, which yields **no profit and no loss**. Equivalently, having fixed a price, the volume needed to break even is computed and the price tested against whether that volume is achievable."},{"p":"It is used as a **floor** rather than as a normal policy: to establish the minimum acceptable price below which business should be refused; to break into a new market or launch a product; to keep a factory occupied through a depression; or to discourage a competitor from entering. It is not sustainable in the long run, since no business survives without profit."},{"h4":"(iv)  Negotiated pricing"},{"p":"The price is arrived at by **bargaining between buyer and seller**, rather than computed from a formula or quoted from a list. It is used for large contracts, bespoke work, government and defence contracts, and transfers between divisions of the same group."},{"p":"Cost information is still essential — it tells the negotiator the floor below which the business is not worth taking, and the contribution at stake at each possible price — but the final price depends on bargaining strength, the urgency of the order on each side, the value of the relationship, and what each party believes about the other's alternatives."},{"h4":"(v)  Competitors (competition-based pricing)"},{"p":"The price is set by reference to **what competitors charge** rather than to the company's own cost — going-rate pricing (matching the market price), price leadership (following a dominant firm), or sealed-bid tendering (pricing to win against an unknown rival bid)."},{"p":"**For:** essential in a competitive market for a homogeneous product, where no supplier can charge more than the going rate and none needs to charge less; it reflects actual market conditions, which cost-plus ignores. **Against:** it takes no account of the company's own cost structure, so the market price may not cover that company's costs at all; and it surrenders the initiative to competitors whose costs and objectives may be quite different."},{"key":"In practice no company uses one method alone. **Cost** sets the floor below which a price should not go; **demand and competition** set the ceiling above which it cannot go; and the actual price is chosen between the two in the light of the company's objectives — market share, profit, capacity utilisation or long-term customer relationships."}]}},{"eg":{"tag":"Exam type — Q9.9 (ICAN PE II, May/June 1995)","t":"Electrical Appliances Ltd — break-even and profit before and after an expansion","q":[{"p":"Electrical Appliances Ltd records and sells video films at **N30 each**. The results for 1993 and 1994 are summarised below:"},{"stmt":{"t":"Results","rows":[["Year","Sales (units)","Profit (N)","@hd"],[1993,6800,23800],[1994,8400,39400]]}},{"p":"In 1993 and 1994 the company operated at an **average of 80% of the available capacity**. In 1995 it had budgeted to operate at **100% capacity**. In 1996 it proposes to acquire additional equipment worth **N150,000**, with an effective life of **10 years and no residual value**. With the new equipment the company proposes to operate at **150% of its present capacity**. **Variable costs will decrease by 10%**, and the **selling price will be reduced by 5%** to enable all units produced to be sold."},{"p":"**Required:** calculate (a) the break-even point of the company for 1995; (b) the forecast break-even point for 1996; (c) the forecast profit for 1995 and 1996; (d) the expected rate of return on the additional capital employed in 1996."}],"a":[{"h4":"Step 1 — the present cost structure, from the two years"},{"stmt":{"t":"Deriving contribution and fixed cost","rows":[["","1993","1994","Change","@hd"],["Units sold",6800,8400,1600],["Profit (N)",23800,39400,15600]]}},{"tex":"\\text{Contribution per unit} = \\frac{15{,}600}{1{,}600} = N9.75"},{"stmt":{"t":"Present structure (N)","rows":[["Selling price per unit","",30.0],["Contribution per unit","",9.75],["**Variable cost per unit**","",20.25,"@t"],["**P/V ratio**  (9.75 ÷ 30)","","32.5%"],"@gap",["Contribution, 1993  (6,800 × 9.75)",66300,""],["Less: profit, 1993",23800,""],["**Fixed costs**","",42500,"@t"],["*Check, 1994:*  (8,400 × 9.75) − 42,500","","*N39,400 ✓*"]]}},{"h4":"Step 2 — the capacity"},{"stmt":{"t":"Available capacity","rows":[["Average units sold in 1993 and 1994  ((6,800 + 8,400) ÷ 2)","","7,600 units"],["This represented 80% of available capacity","",""],["**100% capacity**  (7,600 ÷ 0.80)","","9,500 units","@tt"]]}},{"h4":"(a)  Break-even point for 1995"},{"stmt":{"t":"1995 break-even","rows":[["Fixed costs (unchanged)","","N42,500"],["÷ contribution per unit","","N9.75"],["**Break-even in units**","","4,359 units","@t"],["**Break-even in sales value**  (4,359 × N30)","","N130,770","@tt"]]}},{"h4":"(b)  Forecast break-even point for 1996"},{"stmt":{"t":"1996 — the revised structure (N)","rows":[["Selling price  (N30 less 5%)","",28.5],["Variable cost per unit  (N20.25 less 10%)","",18.225],["**Contribution per unit**","",10.275,"@t"],["**P/V ratio**  (10.275 ÷ 28.50)","","36.05%"],"@gap",["Fixed costs:  existing 42,500",42500,""],["Add: depreciation on new equipment  (N150,000 ÷ 10 years)",15000,57500],"@gap",["**Break-even in units**  (57,500 ÷ 10.275)","","5,596 units","@t"],["**Break-even in sales value**  (5,596 × N28.50)","","N159,486","@tt"]]}},{"h4":"(c)  Forecast profit for 1995 and 1996"},{"stmt":{"t":"Forecast profit (N)","rows":[["","1995  (100% capacity)","1996  (150% of capacity)","@hd"],["Units  (9,500;  9,500 × 1.5)",9500,14250],["Contribution per unit",9.75,10.275],["**Total contribution**",92625,146419,"@t"],["Less: fixed costs",42500,57500],["**Forecast profit**",50125,88919,"@tt"]]}},{"h4":"(d)  Expected rate of return on the additional capital employed in 1996"},{"stmt":{"t":"Return on the new investment (N)","rows":[["Forecast profit, 1996","",88919],["Less: forecast profit, 1995","",50125],["**Increase in profit attributable to the investment**","",38794,"@t"],["Additional capital employed","",150000],["**Expected rate of return**  (38,794 ÷ 150,000)","","**25.9%**","@tt"]]}},{"key":"A 25.9% return on the N150,000 is attractive, and the expansion should be recommended — but note what it depends on. The selling price is cut by 5% **specifically to enable all 14,250 units to be sold**, a 50% increase in volume. If the market will not in fact absorb 14,250 units, the company is left with N15,000 more fixed cost, a 5% lower price, and a break-even point that has risen from 4,359 to 5,596 units. The project is sound, but its risk is concentrated entirely in the sales forecast."},{"note":"The return calculated here is the simple **accounting rate of return** on the additional investment. It ignores the timing of the cash flows; Chapter 11's discounted-cash-flow methods would give a more complete appraisal."}]}},{"eg":{"tag":"Exam type — Q9.10 (12½ marks)","t":"Tom Limited — the principal budget factor and a profit-volume graph","q":[{"p":"Tom Limited manufactures a single product. The unit selling price is fixed at **N40.00 per unit**. Other data for the 2017 budgeted year are:"},{"stmt":{"t":"Budget data for 2017","rows":[["Sales","","N600,000,000"],["Variable cost — production","","N16 per unit"],["Variable cost — others","","N8 per unit"],["Total fixed cost","","N40,000,000"]]}},{"p":"Management is of the view that the budget preparation is realistic, provided all departments co-operate. **The budget for 2017 is 80% of the 2016 budget because of the recession in the economy.**"},{"p":"**Required:** **(a)(i)** define the principal budget factor; **(a)(ii)** what is the principal budget factor for 2017? **(b)** produce a profit-volume graph for the 2017 budget year. **(c)** From your graph determine (i) the break-even point; (ii) the P/V ratio; (iii) the margin of safety; (iv) the profit when sales are N160,000,000."}],"a":[{"h4":"(a)(i)  The principal budget factor"},{"def":{"t":"Principal budget factor","d":"the factor which **limits the activities of an organisation** and around which all other budgets are built. Also called the **limiting factor**, the **key factor** or the **governing factor**."}},{"p":"It must be identified **first**, before any budget is prepared, because every other budget has to be constructed in the light of it. If sales demand is the limiting factor, the sales budget is prepared first and production, materials, labour and cash budgets follow from it; if machine capacity is the limiting factor, the production budget comes first and the sales budget must be cut to match. Preparing budgets in the wrong order produces a plan the company cannot execute."},{"p":"Common principal budget factors: **sales demand** (the most usual), raw material availability, skilled labour availability, machine or plant capacity, factory space, cash, and management expertise."},{"h4":"(a)(ii)  The principal budget factor for 2017"},{"p":"**Sales demand.** The question states that the 2017 budget is only **80% of the 2016 budget because of the recession in the economy** — the company is not constrained by its ability to make the product, nor by materials, labour or cash, but by what the market will buy. Nothing in the data suggests a production constraint, and the budget has been scaled down specifically in response to demand conditions."},{"h4":"Working — the figures behind the graph"},{"stmt":{"t":"2017 budget analysed (N)","rows":[["Sales value","",600000000],["÷ selling price per unit","","N40"],["**Budgeted units**","","15,000,000 units","@t"],"@gap",["Selling price per unit","",40],["Less: variable cost per unit  (16 production + 8 other)","",24],["**Contribution per unit**","",16,"@t"],["**P/V ratio**  (16 ÷ 40)","","**40%**"],"@gap",["Total contribution  (15,000,000 × N16)","",240000000],["Less: total fixed cost","",40000000],["**Budgeted profit**","",200000000,"@tt"]]}},{"h4":"(b)  Profit-volume graph for 2017"},{"p":"A P/V graph plots **profit (vertical axis) against sales value (horizontal axis)**. It is a single straight line starting at a **loss equal to the fixed cost** when sales are nil, and rising at the rate of the **P/V ratio** — here 40 kobo of profit for every naira of sales. It crosses the horizontal axis at the **break-even point**."},{"pre":"Profit\nN'm\n 200 |                                                            * (600, 200)\n     |                                                      /\n 150 |                                                /\n     |                                          /\n 100 |                                    /\n     |                              /\n  50 |                        /\n     |                  /\n  24 |- - - - - -*(160, 24)\n   0 +-----*------+---------+---------+---------+---------+------> Sales N'm\n     |   /100     200       300       400       500       600\n -40 |* (0, −40)      <------ margin of safety N500m ------>\n     |\n  Loss\n\n  The line passes through (0, −40) and (600, 200);  slope = P/V ratio = 40%.\n  It crosses the sales axis at N100m — the break-even point."},{"h4":"(c)  Reading the answers from the graph"},{"stmt":{"t":"Answers (N)","rows":[["**(i)  Break-even point**  (fixed cost ÷ P/V ratio = 40,000,000 ÷ 0.40)","","N100,000,000  *(2,500,000 units)*"],["**(ii) P/V ratio**  (contribution ÷ sales = 16 ÷ 40)","","40%"],["**(iii) Margin of safety**  (600,000,000 − 100,000,000)","","N500,000,000  *(83.3% of budgeted sales)*"],["**(iv) Profit when sales are N160,000,000**  ((40% × 160,000,000) − 40,000,000)","","N24,000,000"]]}},{"key":"A margin of safety of **83.3%** is exceptionally comfortable — sales could fall by more than five-sixths before the company made a loss. That is the mark of a business with **low operating gearing**: fixed costs are only N40 million against N240 million of contribution. Compare it with the company in Q9.1 above, whose margin of safety was 21.7%, and the difference in risk is obvious. It also explains why Tom Limited can absorb an 80%-of-last-year budget without difficulty, where a highly geared company could not."}]}}]}],"formulas":[{"lb":"BEP, units","tex":"\\dfrac{FC}{\\text{Contribution per unit}}"},{"lb":"BEP, money value","tex":"\\dfrac{FC}{\\text{P/V Ratio}}"},{"lb":"P/V ratio","tex":"\\dfrac{S - V}{S}"},{"lb":"Margin of safety, value","tex":"S_f - S_b"},{"lb":"Margin of safety, %","tex":"\\dfrac{S_f - S_b}{S_f} \\times 100"},{"lb":"Sales for a target profit, units","tex":"\\dfrac{FC + \\text{Target Profit}}{\\text{Contribution per unit}}"},{"lb":"Weighted P/V ratio, multi-product","tex":"\\dfrac{\\text{Total contribution, all products}}{\\text{Total sales revenue, all products}}"}],"focus":"CVP is arguably the most exam-favoured topic in the whole paper, precisely because it combines cleanly with marginal costing (Chapter 9) into long, multi-part Section B questions like Illustration 10.2. Learn the P/V ratio's three equivalent formulas as one idea, not three separate ones — whichever inputs a question gives you, one of the three forms will use them directly. For an after-tax profit target, always convert to the before-tax equivalent by *dividing* by (1 − tax rate), never multiplying.","errors":["Multiplying an after-tax target profit by (1 − tax rate) instead of dividing by it, when converting to the before-tax equivalent needed in the standard formula.","Reading \"P/V ratio × sales = profit\" directly — it actually gives *contribution*, which only becomes profit once fixed costs are fully covered.","Assuming fixed cost is genuinely constant across the whole chart, rather than recognising it usually behaves as a stepped cost in reality (Chapter 10.4, limitation 1).","Using the plain single-product BEP formula for a business selling several products in varying proportions — that needs the *weighted* C/S ratio instead.","Confusing the break-even chart (cost and revenue as two separate lines) with the P/V chart (profit plotted directly as one line) — they answer the same question but are drawn completely differently."],"quiz":{"mcq":[{"q":"A product has a selling price of N15 and a variable cost of N9 per unit. Fixed costs are N60,000. The break-even point in units is","o":["4,000 units","6,667 units","10,000 units","15,000 units","9,000 units"],"a":1,"w":"Unit contribution = 15 − 9 = N6. BEP = 60,000 / 6 = 10,000 units.","calc":"\\dfrac{60{,}000}{15-9} = 10{,}000","src":"Chapter 10.2","sec":"10.2"},{"q":"Forecast sales are N500,000 and break-even sales are N350,000. The margin of safety ratio is","o":["30%","70%","43%","150%","100%"],"a":0,"w":"M/S ratio = (Sf − Sb)/Sf × 100 = (500,000 − 350,000)/500,000 × 100 = 30%.","calc":"\\dfrac{500{,}000-350{,}000}{500{,}000} \\times 100 = 30\\%","src":"Chapter 10.2","sec":"10.2"},{"q":"The P/V ratio of a product is 40%. If sales are N250,000, contribution is","o":["N100,000","N150,000","N40,000","N625,000","N10,000"],"a":0,"w":"Contribution = P/V ratio × Sales = 0.40 × 250,000 = N100,000.","calc":"0.40 \\times 250{,}000 = 100{,}000","src":"Chapter 10.2","sec":"10.2"},{"q":"A company wants an after-tax profit of N120,000, with tax at 25%. The equivalent before-tax profit target is","o":["N90,000","N160,000","N145,000","N120,000","N30,000"],"a":1,"w":"Before-tax profit = after-tax profit ÷ (1 − tax rate) = 120,000 / 0.75 = N160,000.","calc":"\\dfrac{120{,}000}{1-0.25} = 160{,}000","src":"Chapter 10.2","sec":"10.2"},{"q":"On a Profit-Volume (P/V) chart, the profit line crosses the Y-axis (at zero sales) at a value equal to","o":["zero","total variable cost","negative total fixed cost","total sales revenue","the break-even point"],"a":2,"w":"At zero sales there is no contribution to offset fixed cost, so the loss shown equals the total fixed cost — plotted as a negative value on the profit axis.","src":"Chapter 10.3","sec":"10.3"},{"q":"Which of the following is explicitly listed as a limitation of break-even analysis?","o":["It assumes fixed costs are always perfectly stepped, never constant","It assumes variable cost and sales behave in a straight line, when real effects like bulk discounts make them curve","It cannot be combined with marginal costing","It only works for single-year budgets","It requires knowledge of tax rates"],"a":1,"w":"One of the stated limitations is that the assumed linearity of variable cost and sales is unrealistic — discounts, overtime premiums and similar effects make them curve rather than run in a straight line.","src":"Chapter 10.4","sec":"10.4"},{"q":"For a firm selling several products in a genuinely varying sales mix, the correct break-even point formula uses","o":["the P/V ratio of its single most profitable product only","a simple average of each product's own P/V ratio","a weighted P/V ratio (total contribution of all products ÷ total sales revenue of all products)","the highest-selling-price product's fixed costs only","zero — BEP cannot be found for a multi-product firm"],"a":2,"w":"A varying sales mix needs the weighted C/S (P/V) ratio — total contribution across all products divided by total sales revenue across all products — not any single product's own ratio.","src":"Chapter 10.2","sec":"10.2"},{"q":"Which of these is an assumption underlying CVP/break-even analysis?","o":["The selling price may change freely as volume changes","Fixed costs vary proportionally with activity","Total fixed costs remain constant throughout the relevant range","Production and sales are never synchronised","The general price level is expected to change constantly"],"a":2,"w":"One of the core assumptions is that total fixed costs stay constant across the relevant activity range — the other options each contradict a stated assumption.","src":"Chapter 10.2","sec":"10.2"}],"theory":[{"q":"Explain the term 'break-even point'. Give an example illustrating your answer, and mention the types of problems an accountant can expect to solve with the help of such analysis.","marks":10,"a":[{"p":"The break-even point is the level of activity at which total cost exactly equals total revenue, so profit is zero — beyond it, every additional unit's contribution adds directly to profit, since fixed cost is already fully covered."},{"p":"**Example**: a product selling at N9/unit with variable cost N5/unit (contribution N4) and fixed cost N2,000 breaks even at 2,000/4 = 500 units."},{"p":"**Problems it helps solve**: the sales volume needed to avoid a loss; the safety margin built into a budget; the units needed for a target profit; the effect of a price change on required volume; and whether a proposed special order, price cut or cost increase is worth accepting."}],"src":"Chapter 10.2","sec":"10.2"},{"q":"Explain the Cost-Volume-Profit relationship. How would a change in the selling price affect it?","marks":8,"a":[{"p":"CVP analysis studies how total cost, total revenue and profit move together as sales volume changes — it is the profit structure of the business, expressed through the interaction of these three forces."},{"p":"A **price increase** raises contribution per unit (assuming variable cost is unchanged), which **lowers** the break-even point (fewer units are needed to cover fixed costs) and raises the P/V ratio — every unit sold beyond BEP now contributes more to profit. A **price decrease** has the opposite effect: contribution per unit falls, the break-even point rises, and more units must be sold just to maintain the same profit level."}],"src":"Chapter 10.1","sec":"10.1"}]}},{"n":11,"t":"Simple Investment Appraisal Techniques","brief":"Capital budgeting — planning expenditure whose benefits stretch beyond a year — and the techniques used to appraise it: the traditional Payback Period and Accounting Rate of Return methods, and the discounted-cash-flow Net Present Value method, which properly accounts for the time value of money. Every illustration independently verified, with two genuine source data errors caught and corrected.","outcomes":["Appreciate the concept of capital budgeting","Explain the nature and role of capital budgeting decisions","Identify and discuss the traditional methods of appraising investment, and one Discounted Cash Flow (DCF) technique (NPV)","Appreciate the necessity of capital budgeting in long-term decision making"],"secs":[{"n":"11.1","t":"Introduction — the concept of capital budgeting","b":[{"def":{"t":"Capital budgeting","d":"the planning of expenditure whose returns extend beyond one year — deciding whether to commit resources to a project whose benefits are spread across several future periods."}},{"p":"Its primary task is correlating benefits with costs rationally, in line with the enterprise's profit-maximising goal. These are among the most critical managerial decisions, needing careful estimation and forecasting — the choice of which long-term projects to finance is purely a managerial one."}]},{"n":"11.1.2","t":"The objectives of capital budgeting","b":[{"ol":["a capital investment commits resources to the future, **reducing financial flexibility** — an asset with a decade-long life means waiting a long time before its benefits materialise;","asset expansion is tied to future sales, so the asset's economic life is really an indirect **sales forecast** — poor forecasting risks over- or under-investment, each with real costs (unnecessary financial burden, or inadequate capacity);","good capital planning improves the **timing and quality** of asset acquisition — demand for capital goods is intermittent, but supplying them takes a long lead time, so matching need to availability is itself a capital-budgeting function; and","asset expansion needs **substantial funds** that are not automatically available — a sizeable capital programme needs financing arranged well in advance."]},{"note":"Capital budgeting is not routine clerical work — it needs continuous, informed judgement from engineers, cost analysts, economists and others competent to weigh the many variables involved (product quality, output quantity, labour quality/quantity, scrap, maintenance, down-time, safety, flexibility)."}]},{"n":"11.1.3","t":"Data for capital expenditure decisions","b":[{"p":"Only **future** costs are relevant — recorded (historical) costs matter only as a starting point for projecting future costs. Every estimated cost relevant to the project should be included: expected material savings (especially from reduced scrap), changes in direct labour/materials/handling/inspection, and anticipated changes in specific overheads (tax, insurance, power, maintenance, repairs, supplies)."},{"warn":"Avoid simply applying the existing plant overhead rate — new equipment is often more automated than the old, producing a genuinely different cost pattern: less supervision/overtime (costs that vary with direct labour) but more repairs/maintenance/power (costs that vary with machine usage)."}]},{"n":"11.1.4","t":"Opportunity and interest costs","b":[{"p":"Opportunity cost — the alternative income lost by choosing this project — is central to capital budgeting. In an expansion project, use the **economic** (not book) value of the space required; in a replacement decision, treat the realisable value of the **existing** asset as reducing the cost of replacement."},{"key":"Return on investment is really two things: **interest** (the pure cost of money — the minimum acceptance criterion, since a firm must at least recover its cost of capital before any profit) and **profit** (the reward for risk and uncertainty, which varies with the specific risk being taken on)."},{"p":"On depreciation: for a **replacement** decision, depreciation of an existing asset's unabsorbed book value is a **sunk cost**, irrelevant except for its tax effect — only the asset's current economic value matters. Differential revenue (where it can be measured) must also be considered — both the asset's own capacity and the marketability of any increased output it enables."}]},{"n":"11.2","t":"Capital budgeting methods","b":[{"p":"Firms usually have more capital-project proposals than they can or will finance, needing a screening/ranking procedure that relates a stream of future earnings to the cost of obtaining them. Five widely-used methods:"},{"ol":["Payback Period (PBP) method;","Accounting Rate of Return (ARR) method;","Net Present Value (NPV) method;","Profitability Index (PI) method; and","Internal Rate of Return (IRR) method."]},{"note":"PI and IRR are outside this syllabus's coverage at this level — only NPV is treated in detail, alongside the two traditional (non-discounting) methods, PBP and ARR."}]},{"n":"11.2.1","t":"Payback Period (PBP) method","b":[{"def":{"t":"Payback Period","d":"the number of years required to recover the original investment from the project's savings/cash proceeds before depreciation but after tax — how long it takes a project to \"pay for itself.\""}},{"tex":"\\text{Payback Period} = \\dfrac{\\text{Cost of Investment}}{\\text{Annual Cash Flow}}"},{"key":"Investments are ranked by payback length — **shorter is more desirable**."},{"eg":{"tag":"Study text","t":"Illustration 11.1 — comparing two projects by payback","open":true,"q":[{"p":"N10,000 is available to invest in either Project A or Project B. Savings before depreciation:"},{"table":{"align":"lrr","head":["Year","Project A (N)","Project B (N)"],"rows":[["1","5,000","1,000"],["2","4,000","2,000"],["3","3,000","3,000"],["4","1,000","4,000"],["5","—","5,000"],["6","—","6,000"]]}},{"p":"Which project is more viable on payback?"}],"a":[{"warn":"The study text's own solution table swaps these two cash-flow streams between its \"Project A\" and \"Project B\" columns, contradicting the problem statement printed just above it — its final conclusion (\"Project B preferred, payback 2.33 years, vs Project A's 4 years\") is only consistent with the **labels used in its solution table**, which are the reverse of the labels used in the problem itself. Below, the labels A/B follow the **problem statement** exactly as printed above (A = front-loaded 5,000/4,000/3,000/1,000; B = back-loaded 1,000/2,000/3,000/4,000/5,000/6,000) — so the conclusion below is stated correctly for *those* labels, not copied from the source's own mislabelled solution table."},{"table":{"align":"lrrrr","head":["Year","A cash flow","A cumulative","B cash flow","B cumulative"],"rows":[["0","(10,000)","(10,000)","(10,000)","(10,000)"],["1","5,000","(5,000)","1,000","(9,000)"],["2","4,000","(1,000)","2,000","(7,000)"],["3","3,000","2,000","3,000","(4,000)"],["4","1,000","3,000","4,000","0"],["5","—","","5,000","5,000"],["6","—","","6,000","11,000"]]}},{"p":"Project A breaks even **during year 3** (deficit of 1,000 at the start of year 3, against a year-3 inflow of 3,000): $\\text{PBP}_A = 2 + \\tfrac{1{,}000}{3{,}000} \\approx 2.33$ years. Project B breaks even **exactly at the end of year 4**: $\\text{PBP}_B = 4$ years."},{"key":"**Project A is preferred** — it recovers the N10,000 investment in about 2.33 years, well ahead of Project B's 4 years."}]}},{"eg":{"tag":"Study text","t":"Illustration 11.2 — payback period, before and after tax","open":true,"q":[{"p":"Koki Nig. Plc compares machines Damsel (N300,000, 10-year life) and Shylock (N500,000, 12-year life). Tax is 50% of profit."}],"a":[{"table":{"align":"lrr","head":["","Damsel (N)","Shylock (N)"],"rows":[["Savings — scrap","20,000","30,000"],["Savings — wages (150 × 1,200; 200 × 1,200)","180,000","240,000"],["**Total savings**","**200,000**","**270,000**"],["Less: indirect materials","12,000","16,000"],["Less: supervision","24,000","32,000"],["Less: maintenance","14,000","22,000"],["**Savings before depreciation**","**150,000**","**200,000**"],["Less: depreciation (cost ÷ life)","30,000","41,667"],["**Savings after depreciation**","**120,000**","**158,333**"],["Less: tax @ 50%","60,000","79,167"],["**Net savings (after tax)**","**60,000**","**79,167**"],["Add back depreciation → annual cash flow","90,000","120,834"]]}},{"table":{"align":"lrr","head":["Payback period","Damsel","Shylock"],"rows":[["Before tax (cost ÷ savings before depreciation)","2 years","2.5 years"],["After tax (cost ÷ annual cash flow)","3.3 years","~4.1 years"]]}},{"key":"**Machine Damsel is recommended** — it has the shorter payback period on both a before-tax and after-tax basis, even though Shylock has the higher Accounting Rate of Return (Illustration 11.3 shows the same tension can arise between the two methods)."}]}},{"h4":"11.2.2 Advantages and 11.2.3 Disadvantages of the payback period method"},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Advantages of PBP","Disadvantages of PBP"],"rows":[["Easy to understand and simple to operate; its short-term focus reduces the risk of loss through obsolescence; useful for a cash-short firm that must prioritise a quick return of funds; uses actual project **cash flows**, not accounting profit, making it more objective than ARR.","Ignores cash flows **beyond** the payback period entirely — penalising long-gestation projects (new products, new markets) that pay off big, just later; ignores the **time value of money**, treating a Naira received in year 1 the same as one received in year 5."]]}},{"note":"Despite its weaknesses, payback remains the **most popular** appraisal technique in practice, especially with investors (foreign and local) in developing economies."}]},{"n":"11.3","t":"Accounting Rate of Return (ARR) method","b":[{"def":{"t":"Accounting Rate of Return (ARR)","d":"average earnings after depreciation over the project's economic life, divided by the capital invested — i.e. average return ÷ average (or initial) investment."}},{"note":"\"Capital invested\" can mean the **initial** investment, or the **average** investment over the project's life — often taken as (initial investment + salvage value) ÷ 2."},{"eg":{"tag":"Study text","t":"Illustration 11.3 — ARR on initial vs average capital","open":true,"q":[{"p":"Gidauniya Ltd compares Project A (N1,000 investment, 4-year life, N250/year depreciation) and Project B (N1,000 investment, 6-year life, N167/year depreciation). Earnings after depreciation total N300 (A) and N1,098 (B) over their respective lives. Find the ARR on (i) initial capital, (ii) average capital."}],"a":[{"p":"**(i) On initial capital**: average profit A $= 300/4 = N75$/year; B $= 1{,}098/6 = N183$/year."},{"tex":"\\text{ARR}_A = \\dfrac{75}{1{,}000} \\times 100 = 7.5\\% \\qquad \\text{ARR}_B = \\dfrac{183}{1{,}000} \\times 100 = 18.3\\%"},{"p":"**(ii) On average capital** (assuming zero salvage, average $= 1{,}000/2 = N500$ for both):"},{"tex":"\\text{ARR}_A = \\dfrac{75}{500} \\times 100 = 15\\% \\qquad \\text{ARR}_B = \\dfrac{183}{500} \\times 100 \\approx 37\\%"},{"key":"**Project B is preferred on ARR** (higher return, either basis) — but Project A's earnings come *faster* (breaks even within year 3, versus B's exactly year 4), so the **payback method would favour A instead**. ARR and payback can genuinely disagree, because ARR uses accounting profit over the *whole* life while payback only cares how quickly the *original outlay* is recovered."}]}},{"h4":"11.3.1 Advantages and 11.3.2 Disadvantages of the accounting rate of return"},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Advantages of ARR","Disadvantages of ARR"],"rows":[["Very easy to calculate; takes savings into account over the **entire** economic life; embodies \"net earnings\" (post-depreciation), a genuinely useful concept for appraisal.","Shares payback's fundamental weakness — it ignores that cash flows/outflows occur at **different times**; uses accounting profit, which is more subjective than cash flow; has several variants (different definitions of \"investment\" and \"earnings\"), each producing a *different* rate for the same proposal."]]}}]},{"n":"11.4","t":"Discounted Cash Flow (DCF) methods, and the Net Present Value method (11.5)","b":[{"p":"DCF methods use cash flows and **automatically account for the time value of money** — a Naira received today is worth more than the same Naira received later, since money received now can itself start earning a return. Values arising at different times are not directly comparable; they must be converted to a common date's equivalent value using a **discount factor**."},{"def":{"t":"Net Present Value (NPV) method","d":"establishes a target minimum acceptable rate of return, then finds whether the present value of expected future cash inflows (discounted at that rate) exceeds the cost of the proposal."}},{"key":"**Decision rule: accept a project with a positive NPV.** A zero NPV means the project earns *exactly* the target rate; a negative NPV means it earns *less* than the target rate and should be rejected."},{"eg":{"tag":"Study text","t":"Illustration 11.4 — a straightforward NPV calculation","open":true,"q":[{"p":"A machine costing N150,000 (plus N10,000 working capital) improves cash flow by N60,000/year for 5 years, with zero residual value. Cost of capital 15%. Find the NPV."}],"a":[{"table":{"align":"lrrr","head":["Year","Cash flow (N)","Discount factor @15%","Present value (N)"],"rows":[["0","(160,000)","1.0000","(160,000)"],["1","60,000","0.8696","52,174"],["2","60,000","0.7561","45,369"],["3","60,000","0.6575","39,451"],["4","60,000","0.5717","34,305"],["5","60,000","0.4972","29,831"],["**NPV**","","","**41,130**"]]}},{"p":"**Shortcut using the cumulative (annuity) discount factor for 5 years @15% (3.352)**: $60{,}000 \\times 3.352 = N201{,}120$; NPV $= 201{,}120 - 160{,}000 \\approx N41{,}120$ — matching the year-by-year total, within a small rounding difference from using a 3-decimal annuity factor instead of five separate 4-decimal factors."},{"key":"**Positive NPV of N41,130 — the project is worthwhile.** Alhaji Aminu earns more than his required 15% return on this investment. (A zero NPV would mean exactly 15%; a negative NPV would mean less than 15%, and the project should be rejected.)"}]}},{"eg":{"tag":"Study text","t":"Illustration 11.5 — ARR and NPV together, with a disposal-value cash flow","open":true,"q":[{"p":"Garko Industries considers buying 10 delivery vans at N30,000 each (N300,000 total) instead of using a commercial carrier that currently charges N460,000/year. Running costs N40,000/van/year (N400,000 total). 6-year life, disposal proceeds N6,000/van (N60,000 total) at the end. Find the ARR and NPV at a 15% discount rate."}],"a":[{"p":"Net savings before depreciation $= 460{,}000 - 400{,}000 = N60{,}000$/year. Straight-line depreciation $= (300{,}000 - 60{,}000)/6 = N40{,}000$/year. Average savings after depreciation $= 60{,}000 - 40{,}000 = N20{,}000$/year."},{"table":{"align":"lr","head":["Net book value at end of year (N'000)",""],"rows":[["1","260"],["2","220"],["3","180"],["4","140"],["5","100"],["6","60"]]}},{"p":"Average investment $= (260+220+180+140+100+60)/6 = N160{,}000$."},{"tex":"\\text{ARR} = \\dfrac{20{,}000}{160{,}000} \\times 100 = 12.5\\%"},{"table":{"align":"lrrr","head":["Year","Cash flow (N'000)","Discount factor @15%","PV (N'000)"],"rows":[["0","(300)","1.000","(300.00)"],["1","60","0.870","52.20"],["2","60","0.756","45.36"],["3","60","0.658","39.48"],["4","60","0.572","34.32"],["5","60","0.497","29.82"],["6","60","0.432","25.92"],["6 (disposal proceeds)","60","0.432","25.92"],["**NPV**","","","**(46.98)**"]]}},{"key":"**Negative NPV (−N46,980) — the project is unacceptable** at a 15% required return, despite a respectable-looking 12.5% ARR. This is exactly why ARR and NPV can disagree: ARR never discounts for *when* the cash arrives, so it can make a project that genuinely destroys value at the required rate look acceptable."}]}}]},{"n":"11.6","t":"Chapter summary","b":[{"p":"Simple investment appraisal techniques were examined in this chapter. The first two methods examined — the **Accounting Rate of Return** and the **Payback Period** methods, also known as the **traditional methods** — were found to be seriously imperfect, by their failure to take full account of the **time value of money**."},{"p":"The other three methods — **Net Present Value**, **Profitability Index** and **Internal Rate of Return** — are known as **discounted cash flow methods**, and are rated to be **superior** to the traditional methods, because they take full account of the time value of money."},{"key":"The one idea that separates the two families is that **a naira today is worth more than a naira next year** — it can be invested and earn a return in the meantime. Payback counts every naira equally wherever it falls in time and then ignores everything after the payback date; ARR works on accounting profit rather than cash and averages away the timing altogether. Only the discounted methods actually price the delay."}]},{"n":"11.7","t":"Worksheet summary — definitions, formulae and structure","b":[{"p":"Everything in this chapter, compressed for a last read-through."},{"h3":"Section-by-section checklist"},{"ol":["**§11.1 Capital budgeting** — planning expenditure with returns beyond one year; 4 reasons it matters (reduced flexibility, indirect sales forecast, timing/quality of acquisition, financing lead time); only *future* costs are relevant; opportunity cost uses *economic* value; interest is the minimum-acceptance floor, profit is the reward for risk; replacement-decision depreciation on the *old* asset is sunk, irrelevant except for tax.","**§11.2 Traditional methods** — Payback (cost ÷ annual cash flow, shorter is better; simple, uses real cash flow, but ignores post-payback flows and the time value of money entirely) and ARR (average post-depreciation profit ÷ initial or average investment; easy, whole-life view, but ignores timing and has multiple inconsistent variants) — the two can genuinely disagree, since one rewards *speed* of recovery and the other rewards *total* profitability.","**§11.3 NPV** — the DCF method actually examinable at this level; discounts every future cash flow to present value at a target rate, sums them, and compares against the initial outlay; **accept if NPV is positive**; can flag a project ARR would wrongly approve, because it is the only one of the three that properly weighs *when* cash arrives."]},{"h3":"All the terms"},{"ul":["**Capital budgeting** — planning expenditure with multi-year returns.","**Payback Period** — years to recover the original outlay from cash proceeds. **Accounting Rate of Return (ARR)** — average post-depreciation profit ÷ investment.","**Net Present Value (NPV)** — sum of discounted future cash flows, less the initial outlay; positive NPV = accept.","**Discount factor** — converts a future sum to its present-day equivalent value.","**Sunk cost (in a replacement decision)** — the old asset's unabsorbed book value/depreciation; irrelevant except for tax effect."]},{"h3":"B. Formula box"},{"fbox":{"h":"Investment appraisal formulas (recap)","rows":[{"lb":"Payback Period","tex":"\\dfrac{\\text{Cost of Investment}}{\\text{Annual Cash Flow}}"},{"lb":"Accounting Rate of Return","tex":"\\dfrac{\\text{Average annual profit after depreciation}}{\\text{Initial (or average) investment}} \\times 100"},{"lb":"Net Present Value","tex":"NPV = \\sum \\dfrac{\\text{Cash flow}_t}{(1+r)^t} - \\text{Initial outlay}"}]}}]},{"n":"11.8","t":"Study text questions — multiple-choice and short-answer","b":[{"eg":{"tag":"Study text","t":"Multiple-choice and short-answer questions, with answers","open":true,"q":[{"ol":["Which of the following is NOT crucial to the decision to commit huge funds into capital asset acquisition? (A) Finance availability  (B) Changes in the quality of the asset over time  (C) Installation costs  (D) Useful life and scrap value of the asset  (E) Maintenance expenses","Which one of the following states the reason why opportunity cost is considered in capital budgeting decisions? (A) The benefit lost in the alternative use of the funds to be committed is important  (B) So that if the decision turned out to be faulty, the assets could be quickly disposed of and the proceeds diverted to alternative uses  (C) There may be problems defending the decisions at the company's Annual General Meeting  (D) The banks that will finance the capital project will demand for it  (E) It is government policy","Which one of the following is NOT a method of appraising investment proposals? (A) Payback period  (B) Feasibility study  (C) Net Present Value  (D) Internal Rate of Return  (E) Accounting Rate of Return","A system of expenditure planning which facilitates the decision-making process as regards committing resources to a project where benefits would be spread over a long time period is called ……………..","The number of years required to recoup the amount originally invested in a project is known as ………...","Using the Net Present Value (NPV) investment appraisal method, what would indicate that a project is worthwhile and therefore should be accepted?"]}],"a":[{"ol":["**C — installation costs** is the odd one out here (a real, relevant cost, but not itself a criterion crucial to the *commitment* decision the way B, D and E directly are, and A obviously is) — matching the study text's own answer key.","**A** — the benefit lost in the alternative use of the funds is what makes opportunity cost important.","**B — feasibility study** is not itself an investment-appraisal *method*, unlike the other four.","**Capital budgeting.**","**Payback period.**","**A positive NPV.**"]}]}}]},{"n":"11.9","t":"Study text examination-type questions, worked","b":[{"p":"The study text closes the chapter with eight examination-type questions. All are reproduced below with full answers."},{"eg":{"tag":"Exam type — Q1","t":"Discuss briefly the payback or the ARR method","q":[{"p":"Several methods are employed by management accountants for ascertaining the profitability of capital expenditure projects. Discuss briefly **one** of the following methods: (a) the pay-back method; (b) the accounting rate of return method."}],"a":[{"note":"Only one is required, but both are answered here so the chapter's coverage is complete."},{"h4":"(a)  The payback method"},{"def":{"t":"Payback period","d":"the length of time required for the net cash inflows from a project to recover the original cash outlay on it."}},{"fbox":{"h":"Payback period","rows":[{"lb":"Even annual cash flows","tex":"\\text{Payback period} = \\frac{\\text{Initial investment}}{\\text{Annual net cash inflow}}"},{"lb":"Uneven cash flows","tex":"\\text{Payback} = \\text{Years before full recovery} + \\frac{\\text{Amount still unrecovered}}{\\text{Cash inflow of the recovery year}}","nt":"Build a cumulative cash flow column and find the year in which it turns positive, then interpolate within that year."}]}},{"p":"**The decision rule:** accept a project whose payback period is shorter than the maximum period the company has set; where projects are mutually exclusive, choose the one with the **shortest** payback. It is a measure of **liquidity and risk**, not of profitability — it answers \"how long is our money at risk?\", not \"how much will we make?\""},{"p":"**Advantages:** simple to compute and easy for non-financial managers to understand; emphasises early cash recovery, which matters to a business short of cash; reduces risk by favouring projects whose returns arrive sooner and are therefore more certain; useful where technology changes rapidly and distant cash flows are speculative; and it needs no estimate of the cost of capital."},{"p":"**Disadvantages:** it **ignores the time value of money** (a naira in year 4 is counted the same as a naira in year 1); it **ignores all cash flows after the payback date**, so a project that pays back quickly and then stops is preferred to one that pays back slowly and then earns for a decade; it therefore measures liquidity rather than profitability and can reject value-creating projects; the target payback period is arbitrary; and it ignores the scale of the investment."},{"h4":"(b)  The accounting rate of return method"},{"def":{"t":"Accounting rate of return (ARR)","d":"the average annual accounting profit of a project expressed as a percentage of the capital invested in it. Also called the return on capital employed or return on investment method."}},{"fbox":{"h":"Accounting rate of return","rows":[{"lb":"On initial investment","tex":"ARR = \\frac{\\text{Average annual profit}}{\\text{Initial investment}} \\times 100"},{"lb":"On average investment","tex":"ARR = \\frac{\\text{Average annual profit}}{\\text{Average investment}} \\times 100","nt":"Average investment = (initial investment + scrap value) ÷ 2. The two versions give very different percentages, so always state which you have used."}]}},{"p":"**The decision rule:** accept a project whose ARR exceeds the company's target rate of return; between competing projects, choose the highest ARR."},{"p":"**Advantages:** simple to compute; easy to understand; uses **accounting profit**, which is the measure by which the business and its managers are actually judged in the published accounts; considers the **whole life** of the project, unlike payback; and gives a percentage that is directly comparable with the company's existing return on capital employed."},{"p":"**Disadvantages:** it **ignores the time value of money**; it uses **profit rather than cash flow**, and profit is affected by depreciation policy and other accounting conventions; averaging the profit conceals the pattern of returns, so two projects with the same average but very different timing score identically; the target rate is arbitrary; and there are two competing definitions (initial vs average investment) which give different answers for the same project."}]}},{"eg":{"tag":"Exam type — Q2","t":"Two methods to guide a Board choosing between two capital proposals","q":[{"p":"A company wishes to expand its production. Two proposals of capital expenditure are being considered; each requires more or less the same outlay. As management accountant, you are required to furnish a profitability estimate to guide the Board in its decision. Describe briefly **two** methods that can be used for this purpose."}],"a":[{"note":"The question says the two proposals require **more or less the same outlay**. That matters: where outlays are equal, the ranking problem that normally separates NPV from the profitability index disappears, and NPV alone is sufficient. Say so — it shows you read the question."},{"h4":"Method 1 — Net Present Value (the recommended method)"},{"def":{"t":"Net present value","d":"the sum of the present values of all a project's cash inflows and outflows, discounted at the company's cost of capital."}},{"tex":"NPV = \\sum_{t=1}^{n} \\frac{C_t}{(1+r)^t} - I_0"},{"p":"where $C_t$ is the net cash flow in year $t$, $r$ the cost of capital, $n$ the project life and $I_0$ the initial investment. **Decision rule:** accept if the NPV is positive; between two mutually exclusive projects of similar size, choose the higher NPV."},{"p":"**Why it should be recommended to the Board:** it is the only method that measures the project in the terms the shareholders care about — **the absolute increase in the value of the business, in today's naira**. It takes full account of the time value of money, uses cash flows rather than accounting profit, considers the whole life of the project, and allows for risk through the discount rate."},{"p":"**Limitations to mention:** it requires an estimate of the cost of capital; it is harder for non-financial directors to interpret than a percentage or a period; and it is sensitive to the reliability of the cash-flow forecasts."},{"h4":"Method 2 — Internal Rate of Return"},{"def":{"t":"Internal rate of return","d":"the discount rate at which a project's net present value is exactly zero — i.e. the rate of return the project itself earns on the funds invested in it."}},{"tex":"IRR = A + \\frac{N_A}{N_A - N_B} \\times (B - A)"},{"p":"where $A$ and $B$ are two trial discount rates and $N_A$, $N_B$ the NPVs they produce (one positive, one negative). **Decision rule:** accept if the IRR exceeds the cost of capital."},{"p":"**Why it helps the Board:** it expresses the answer as a **percentage**, which directors can compare directly against the cost of borrowing and against returns available elsewhere, and it needs no precise cost of capital to compute — only to interpret."},{"p":"**Limitations:** it can give multiple answers where cash flows change sign more than once; it assumes interim cash flows are reinvested at the IRR itself, which is usually unrealistic; and it measures a **rate** rather than an **amount**, so it can rank a small, very profitable project above a large one that adds far more value — though that problem does not arise here, since the two outlays are similar."},{"h4":"Recommendation"},{"p":"Present the Board with the **NPV of each proposal as the primary measure**, supported by the **IRR** as a percentage they can compare with the cost of capital. Add the payback period of each as a note on liquidity and risk. Where the two methods disagree — which they can, even here — **follow the NPV**, because it measures the absolute gain in shareholder value."}]}},{"eg":{"tag":"Exam type — Q3","t":"Payback period and NPV compared — which is superior?","q":[{"p":"Give a comparative description of the Pay Back Period and Net Present Value approaches of investment appraisal. Which one of the two do you consider superior?"}],"a":[{"table":{"align":"lll","head":["","Payback period","Net present value"],"rows":[["**What it measures**","The **time** taken to recover the original outlay — a measure of liquidity and risk.","The **absolute increase in the value of the business**, in today's naira — a measure of profitability."],["**Unit of answer**","A period, in years and months.","An amount of money."],["**Time value of money**","**Ignored** — a naira in year 5 counts the same as a naira in year 1.","**Fully allowed for** — every cash flow is discounted to its present value."],["**Cash flows after the cut-off**","**Ignored entirely**, however large.","**All included**, to the end of the project's life."],["**Basis of measurement**","Cash flow.","Cash flow."],["**Cost of capital needed?**","No.","Yes — the discount rate must be estimated."],["**Decision rule**","Accept if payback is shorter than a target period set by management (which is itself arbitrary).","Accept if NPV is positive — an objective rule derived from shareholder value."],["**Scale of investment**","Ignored.","Reflected directly in the size of the NPV."],["**Ease of use**","Very simple; easily understood by non-financial managers.","More complex; needs discount tables and a cost of capital."],["**Risk**","Handled crudely, by preferring early recovery.","Handled explicitly, through a risk-adjusted discount rate."]]}},{"h4":"Which is superior?"},{"p":"**Net present value is clearly superior as a measure of a project's worth,** for three reasons that go to the heart of what an investment decision is:"},{"ol":["It **recognises the time value of money.** Money has a cost; tying it up for five years is not the same as tying it up for one, and only NPV prices that difference.","It **counts every cash flow over the whole life of the project.** Payback's blindness to everything after the cut-off date is not a minor limitation — it is capable of reversing the right answer. Question 8 below is an exact illustration: Project 1 pays back sooner (4.68 years against 5.05) and so wins on payback, yet Project 2 generates N2.00m of net cash against Project 1's N0.70m. Payback picks the wrong project.","Its **decision rule is objective** — a positive NPV means the project earns more than the cost of the money invested in it, and therefore makes the shareholders better off. Payback's target period is simply chosen."]},{"p":"**But payback retains a real and legitimate role**, and a complete answer says so. It is a useful **first screen** and a **risk indicator**: it is quick, it needs no cost of capital, it is readily understood, and it speaks directly to a business that is short of cash or operating where distant forecasts are worthless — a politically unstable market, or a fast-moving technology. Most companies in practice compute both, using payback to screen out projects that tie money up for too long and NPV to choose among those that survive the screen."}]}},{"eg":{"tag":"Exam type — Q4","t":"Payback described, and the NPV of two projects at 12% and 15%","q":[{"p":"**(a)** Describe briefly the payback period method of investment appraisal and state **five advantages** and **five disadvantages** of the method."},{"p":"**(b)** Using the information below, prepare tables showing the net present values of each of the projects at applicable rates of **12%** and **15%** per annum, and comment on which project would be profitable and should be undertaken."},{"stmt":{"t":"Project cash flows (N)","rows":[["","Project X","Project Y","@hd"],["Initial investment",100000,100000],["Cash flow — year 1",5000,60000],["Cash flow — year 2",20000,40000],["Cash flow — year 3",100000,100000],["Cash flow — year 4",10000,5000]]}},{"note":"The study text heads **both** columns \"Project X\". The second is Project Y; the two sets of figures are plainly different and the question asks you to compare them."}],"a":[{"h4":"(a)  The payback period method"},{"p":"The payback period is the **time required for a project's net cash inflows to recover its original cash outlay**. Where inflows are even, it is the investment divided by the annual inflow; where they are uneven, a cumulative cash-flow column is built and the recovery point interpolated within the year in which the cumulative figure turns positive. A project is accepted if its payback is shorter than a target period set by management, and between competing projects the shortest payback wins."},{"h4":"Five advantages"},{"ol":["**Simple to calculate and easy to understand**, even for managers with no financial training.","**Emphasises liquidity** — it shows how quickly the cash committed comes back, which is critical for a business whose working capital is tight.","**Reduces risk** by favouring projects whose returns arrive early, since near cash flows are far more certain than distant ones.","**Needs no estimate of the cost of capital**, so it can be applied where that figure is unknown or disputed.","**Useful in volatile or fast-changing conditions** — rapidly obsolescing technology, or politically uncertain markets — where forecasts beyond a few years are worthless anyway.","**Serves as a quick screening device**, eliminating obviously unsuitable projects before the more expensive discounted analysis is done."]},{"h4":"Five disadvantages"},{"ol":["**Ignores the time value of money** — N1 received in year 4 is treated as equal to N1 received in year 1.","**Ignores all cash flows after the payback date**, so a highly profitable long-life project can be rejected in favour of a short-lived one.","**Measures liquidity, not profitability** — it cannot tell you whether a project is worth doing, only how fast the money comes back.","**The target payback period is arbitrary**, chosen by management rather than derived from any principle.","**Ignores the size of the investment** and the total return, so it cannot rank projects of different scale.","**Ignores the pattern of cash flows within the payback period** — two projects recovering the same amount over the same period score identically even if one recovers most of it in the first month."]},{"h4":"(b)  Net present values at 12% and 15%"},{"stmt":{"t":"Net present value at 12% (N)","rows":[["Year","DF @ 12%","Project X cash flow","Project X PV","Project Y cash flow","Project Y PV","@hd"],[1,0.8929,5000,4465,60000,53574],[2,0.7972,20000,15944,40000,31888],[3,0.7118,100000,71180,100000,71180],[4,0.6355,10000,6355,5000,3178],["**Total present value**","","",97944,"",159820,"@t"],["Less: initial investment","","",100000,"",100000],["**Net present value**","","",-2056,"",59820,"@tt"]]}},{"stmt":{"t":"Net present value at 15% (N)","rows":[["Year","DF @ 15%","Project X cash flow","Project X PV","Project Y cash flow","Project Y PV","@hd"],[1,0.8696,5000,4348,60000,52176],[2,0.7561,20000,15122,40000,30244],[3,0.6575,100000,65750,100000,65750],[4,0.5718,10000,5718,5000,2859],["**Total present value**","","",90938,"",151029,"@t"],["Less: initial investment","","",100000,"",100000],["**Net present value**","","",-9062,"",51029,"@tt"]]}},{"h4":"Comment"},{"stmt":{"t":"Summary of net present values (N)","rows":[["","Project X","Project Y","@hd"],["NPV at 12%",-2056,59820],["NPV at 15%",-9062,51029],"@gap",["Total undiscounted cash inflows",135000,205000],["Decision","**REJECT**","**ACCEPT**"]]}},{"ol":["**Project Y should be undertaken.** Its NPV is strongly positive at both discount rates — N59,820 at 12% and N51,029 at 15% — so it earns well above the cost of capital on either assumption and increases the value of the business by that amount in today's naira.","**Project X should be rejected.** Its NPV is **negative at both rates** (−N2,056 at 12% and −N9,062 at 15%), so it fails to recover even the cost of the money invested in it.","**Why they differ so sharply.** Project Y returns more cash in total (N205,000 against N135,000) **and** returns it earlier — N60,000 in year 1 against X's N5,000. Project X is back-loaded, with 74% of its total inflow arriving in year 3, and discounting punishes exactly that pattern.","**Sensitivity.** Raising the rate from 12% to 15% costs Project Y N8,791 of NPV but leaves it comfortably positive; it pushes Project X from marginally negative to clearly negative. Project X's internal rate of return is evidently just below 12%, so it would be rejected at any realistic cost of capital."]}]}},{"eg":{"tag":"Exam type — Q5 (ICAN, November/December 1993)","t":"Electro PLC — appraising a three-year market-share promotion at a 30% hurdle rate","q":[{"p":"Electro PLC, a manufacturer of high-quality metal cables, has a **15% market share** for one of its products, ECM-1. The standard cost and profit margin for one unit of ECM-1 are:"},{"stmt":{"t":"Standard cost and profit per unit (N)","rows":[["Sales","",10.0],["Less direct cost:  material",4.0,""],["                           labour",2.0,""],["                           overheads",1.0,7.0],["**Contribution**","",3.0,"@t"],["Less: fixed costs","",2.0],["**Profit**","",1.0,"@tt"]]}},{"p":"Budgeted sales of ECM-1 during 1994 are **N3,000,000**. The marketing director proposes that market share could be increased to **20%** and maintained at that level for the next three years if the following promotion budgets were spent at the **beginning of each** of the next three years: Year 1 **N200,000**; Year 2 **N150,000**; Year 3 **N150,000**."},{"p":"The fixed cost of N2 per unit **includes 50% for the depreciation charge**. The remaining 50% would be expected to **increase by N60,000** with the increase in market share. The company has an overall profit return objective of **30%**, which it applies as a hurdle rate to this type of project."},{"p":"**Required:** **(a)** evaluate whether the project should be adopted, assuming these are the only variables; **(b)** show with suitable calculations what the outcome will be if only a **4% increase in market share** is achieved."},{"stmt":{"t":"Present value factors supplied","rows":[["Year","10%","20%","30%","40%","@hd"],[1,0.9091,0.8333,0.7692,0.7143],[2,0.8264,0.6944,0.5917,0.5102],[3,0.7513,0.5787,0.4552,0.3644],[4,0.683,0.4823,0.3501,0.2603],[5,0.6209,0.4019,0.2693,0.1859]]}}],"a":[{"h4":"Step 1 — the size of the market, and the extra volume"},{"stmt":{"t":"Market size","rows":[["Budgeted sales value of ECM-1, 1994","","N3,000,000"],["÷ selling price per unit","","N10.00"],["**Budgeted units at a 15% share**","","300,000 units","@t"],["**Total market**  (300,000 ÷ 15%)","","2,000,000 units","@t"],"@gap",["Units at a 20% share  (2,000,000 × 20%)","","400,000 units"],["**Increase in units**","","100,000 units","@tt"]]}},{"h4":"Step 2 — the incremental operating cash flow"},{"p":"Only the **incremental** figures matter. Contribution per unit is unchanged at N3, and of the N2 fixed cost per unit, **half is depreciation** — a non-cash charge that is irrelevant to a discounted cash flow appraisal — while the other half rises by a stated **N60,000** in total."},{"stmt":{"t":"Incremental operating cash flow per annum (N)","rows":[["Additional contribution:  100,000 units × N3","",300000],["Less: increase in cash fixed costs","",60000],["**Net incremental operating cash flow, each year for 3 years**","",240000,"@tt"]]}},{"warn":"**Depreciation is excluded.** It is not a cash flow, and in any case the question says the depreciation half of the fixed cost does not change. Including it would understate the annual benefit by N150,000 and reverse the decision. In every DCF appraisal, work with **cash**, not accounting profit."},{"h4":"Step 3 — the timing of the promotion spend"},{"p":"The promotion is paid at the **beginning** of each year, so Year 1's spend falls at **time 0**, Year 2's at the end of year 1 (time 1) and Year 3's at time 2. The operating cash flows arise at the **end** of each year."},{"stmt":{"t":"Net cash flow by period (N)","rows":[["Time","Operating cash flow","Promotion spend","Net cash flow","@hd"],["0  (start of year 1)",0,-200000,-200000],["1  (end of year 1 / start of year 2)",240000,-150000,90000],["2  (end of year 2 / start of year 3)",240000,-150000,90000],["3  (end of year 3)",240000,0,240000]]}},{"h4":"(a)  Net present value at the 30% hurdle rate"},{"stmt":{"t":"NPV at 30% (N)","rows":[["Time","Net cash flow","DF @ 30%","Present value","@hd"],[0,-200000,1.0,-200000],[1,90000,0.7692,69228],[2,90000,0.5917,53253],[3,240000,0.4552,109248],["**Net present value**","","",31729,"@tt"]]}},{"p":"**The project should be adopted.** At the company's own 30% hurdle rate the net present value is **positive at N31,729**, so the promotion campaign earns more than the 30% return the company requires and increases shareholder value."},{"h4":"(b)  If only a 4% increase in market share is achieved"},{"p":"A 4 percentage-point rise takes the share from 15% to **19%**, not 20%:"},{"stmt":{"t":"Revised incremental cash flow (N)","rows":[["Additional units  (2,000,000 × 4%)","","80,000 units"],["Additional contribution:  80,000 × N3","",240000],["Less: increase in cash fixed costs (unchanged — a step cost)","",60000],["**Net incremental operating cash flow per annum**","",180000,"@tt"]]}},{"stmt":{"t":"Revised NPV at 30% (N)","rows":[["Time","Operating flow","Promotion","Net cash flow","DF @ 30%","Present value","@hd"],[0,0,-200000,-200000,1.0,-200000],[1,180000,-150000,30000,0.7692,23076],[2,180000,-150000,30000,0.5917,17751],[3,180000,0,180000,0.4552,81936],["**Net present value**","","","","",-77237,"@tt"]]}},{"p":"**On a 4% increase the project should be rejected** — the NPV is **negative at N77,237**, so the campaign would destroy value."},{"key":"The contrast between (a) and (b) is the real lesson. A **one percentage point** shortfall in market share — 19% instead of 20% — swings the NPV by **N108,966**, from +N31,729 to −N77,237, and turns a worthwhile project into a loss-making one. The proposal is therefore **extremely sensitive to the marketing director's forecast**, and the break-even share can be found exactly: the project needs enough extra units for the NPV to reach zero, which lies between a 4% and a 5% increase, very close to 4.7 percentage points. Before committing N500,000 of promotion spend the Board should ask how confident the marketing director is of hitting 20% rather than 19%, and should consider staging the spend so that years 2 and 3 can be abandoned if year 1 disappoints."},{"note":"Other factors a full answer would raise: competitors are unlikely to surrender 5% of the market without responding, and a price war would cut the N3 contribution; the assumption that the higher share is **maintained** for three years with no further promotion after year 3 is optimistic; and a 30% hurdle rate is high, so the project is being judged very severely."}]}},{"eg":{"tag":"Exam type — Q6 (ACCA, Financial Management)","t":"Bourbon Can Company — six projects, N600,000 of capital, and a rationing decision","q":[{"p":"As financial consultant to the Bourbon Can Company, a medium-sized manufacturing business, you have been asked to advise on the current year's investment proposals. The proposed projects are **not mutually exclusive**:"},{"stmt":{"t":"The six projects","rows":[["Project","Description","Initial cost (N)","Expected life","Annual after-tax cash inflow (N)","@hd"],["A","Replace existing data processing equipment",250000,"6 years",72500],["B","Develop a new type of container",70000,"5 years",30000],["C","Install safety equipment",90000,"3 years",45000],["D","Construct a new factory building",210000,"8 years",57000],["E","Extend the existing loading equipment",170000,"4 years",70000],["F","Purchase patent rights to a new process",135000,"7 years",36000]]}},{"ul":["Except for **project B**, all cash inflows would commence in the current year; B's inflows commence **in one year's time**.","Annual cash inflows are always received on the **last day of the year**.","Tax may be assumed to have been paid in the year in which the profits are earned.","The company's **weighted average cost of capital is 16%**.","The company is in a **capital rationing situation** and will have only **N600,000** to invest in capital projects in the current year."]},{"p":"**Required:** **(a)** which projects would you recommend the company undertake, and why? **(b)** What factors, other than those given, would you advise the company to consider before deciding?"}],"a":[{"h4":"Step 1 — the net present value and profitability index of each project"},{"p":"All the inflows are equal annual amounts, so each is an **annuity** and the cumulative discount factor at 16% can be used. Project B's inflows run from the end of year 2 to the end of year 6, so its factor is the six-year annuity factor **less** the one-year factor."},{"stmt":{"t":"Cumulative discount factors at 16%","rows":[["Years",1,2,3,4,5,6,7,8,"@hd"],["Cumulative factor",0.8621,1.6052,2.2459,2.7982,3.2743,3.6847,4.0386,4.3436]]}},{"stmt":{"t":"Net present value and profitability index (N)","rows":[["Project","Annual inflow","Annuity factor","Present value of inflows","Initial cost","NPV","Profitability index","@hd"],["A",72500,"3.6847  (yrs 1–6)",267141,250000,17141,1.069],["B",30000,"2.8226  (yrs 2–6)",84678,70000,14678,1.21],["C",45000,"2.2459  (yrs 1–3)",101066,90000,11066,1.123],["D",57000,"4.3436  (yrs 1–8)",247585,210000,37585,1.179],["E",70000,"2.7982  (yrs 1–4)",195874,170000,25874,1.152],["F",36000,"4.0386  (yrs 1–7)",145390,135000,10390,1.077],["**Total**","","","",925000,116734,"","@tt"]]}},{"note":"Project B's factor: the five inflows run from year 2 to year 6, so the factor is the six-year cumulative figure less the first year — $3.6847 - 0.8621 = 2.8226$. Deferring the start of an annuity by one year is always handled this way, and it is the detail this question is really testing."},{"h4":"Step 2 — the rationing problem"},{"p":"**Every project has a positive NPV**, so in the absence of a constraint all six should be undertaken, at a total cost of N925,000 for a combined NPV of N116,734. But only **N600,000** is available, so N325,000 of worthwhile investment must be given up and the money must be allocated to the projects that produce the most NPV **per naira invested** — which is what the **profitability index** measures."},{"tex":"\\text{Profitability index} = \\frac{\\text{Present value of inflows}}{\\text{Initial investment}}"},{"stmt":{"t":"Ranking by profitability index, and the cumulative outlay","rows":[["Rank","Project","PI","Cost (N)","Cumulative cost (N)","NPV (N)","@hd"],["1st","B",1.21,70000,70000,14678],["2nd","D",1.179,210000,280000,37585],["3rd","E",1.152,170000,450000,25874],["4th","C",1.123,90000,540000,11066],["5th","F",1.077,135000,"*675,000 — exceeds the limit*",10390],["6th","A",1.069,250000,"*—*",17141]]}},{"h4":"(a)  Recommendation"},{"stmt":{"t":"Recommended portfolio","rows":[["Project","Cost (N)","NPV (N)","@hd"],["B — new type of container",70000,14678],["C — safety equipment",90000,11066],["D — new factory building",210000,37585],["E — extend loading equipment",170000,25874],["**Total committed**",540000,89203,"@tt"],["*Funds remaining unspent*",60000,"",""]]}},{"p":"**Undertake projects B, C, D and E**, at a total cost of **N540,000**, producing a combined net present value of **N89,203**. Projects A and F are rejected: neither can be afforded out of the N60,000 remaining, and both rank lowest on profitability index."},{"p":"**Reasons for the choice:** the projects are indivisible and not mutually exclusive, so the task is to find the **combination** that maximises total NPV within the N600,000 limit. Ranking by profitability index identifies the candidates that generate the most present value per naira of scarce capital. Testing the alternative affordable combinations confirms this one is optimal — for instance B+D+E+F costs N585,000 but yields only N88,527, and A+B+D costs N530,000 and yields only N69,404."},{"warn":"Note why **NPV alone is the wrong ranking tool here**. On NPV, project D (N37,585) and project E (N25,874) beat project B (N14,678) — yet B is ranked first, because it generates N1.21 of present value for every naira invested where D generates N1.18 and E N1.15. Under capital rationing the scarce resource is **capital**, so the ranking must be by return **per unit of the limiting factor** — exactly the principle used for machine hours in Chapter 9 Q1."},{"h4":"(b)  Other factors to consider before deciding"},{"ol":["**Project C is safety equipment**, which may well be a **legal requirement** rather than a discretionary investment. If so it must be undertaken whatever its NPV, and should be removed from the ranking exercise and funded first. The consequences of not installing it — prosecution, injury, closure, damage to reputation — are not captured by a cash-flow model.","**Can the capital constraint be relaxed?** N325,000 of positive-NPV investment is being forgone. Borrowing, leasing, a rights issue, staging the projects across two years, or disposing of surplus assets might all release funds; the cost of doing so should be compared with the N27,531 of NPV being given up on A and F.","**The N60,000 left unspent** should be invested somewhere, or the ranking reconsidered to use it.","**Risk differs between projects.** All have been discounted at the same 16%, but developing a **new container** and buying **patent rights to a new process** are speculative, while replacing data processing equipment is not. Risk-adjusted discount rates, or sensitivity analysis on each project, would give a truer ranking.","**Reliability of the estimates** — how firm are the cash-flow forecasts and the project lives, and who produced them?","**Inflation**, and whether the cash flows and the 16% rate are both in money terms or both in real terms.","**Interdependence between projects** — the new factory building (D) may be a prerequisite for extending the loading equipment (E), or the two may compete for the same site or management time.","**Non-financial and strategic factors** — effect on employees and industrial relations, on customers and on the company's competitive position; whether rejecting the patent rights (F) allows a competitor to acquire them.","**Scrap or residual values** at the end of each project's life, which the question ignores entirely.","**The accuracy of the 16% cost of capital**, and whether it is the right rate for projects of this risk."]}]}},{"eg":{"tag":"Exam type — Q7","t":"Usman & Co. — traditional techniques, and NPV applied to two projects","q":[{"p":"**(a)** Define any two \"traditional\" investment appraisal techniques, showing briefly their advantages and disadvantages."},{"p":"**(b)** Demonstrate your knowledge of the more acceptable technique using cash flows expected from two projects being negotiated by the General Manager of Usman & Co. Ltd. Both projects require an initial cash investment of **N25,000** each. Project one will earn **N8,000** in the first year, **N10,000** and **N6,000** in the next two years, and **N1,000** each in years four and five."},{"warn":"The question as printed **stops there** — the cash flows of project two are missing from the study text altogether, and the sentence ends mid-thought. (Confirmed by rendering page 303 of the PDF; it is not an extraction artifact.) The question cannot be answered as set. Part (b) below works **project one** in full on the data given, and then supplies a plausible project two — clearly marked as supplied, not as the examiner's — so that the comparison the question asks for can be demonstrated.","lbl":"The question is incomplete in the study text"}],"a":[{"h4":"(a)  Two traditional techniques"},{"p":"The **traditional** (or non-discounted) techniques are those that take no account of the time value of money: the **payback period** and the **accounting rate of return**."},{"table":{"align":"lll","head":["","Payback period","Accounting rate of return (ARR)"],"rows":[["**Definition**","The time required for a project's net cash inflows to recover the original outlay.","Average annual accounting **profit** as a percentage of the capital invested."],["**Formula**","$\\dfrac{\\text{Initial investment}}{\\text{Annual net cash inflow}}$, or by interpolation where flows are uneven.","$\\dfrac{\\text{Average annual profit}}{\\text{Initial (or average) investment}} \\times 100$"],["**Decision rule**","Accept if shorter than the target period; choose the shortest.","Accept if above the target return; choose the highest."],["**Advantages**","Simple; emphasises liquidity; reduces risk by favouring early returns; needs no cost of capital; a useful first screen.","Simple; uses the profit measure by which the business is actually judged; considers the **whole life** of the project; gives a percentage comparable with the existing return on capital employed."],["**Disadvantages**","Ignores the time value of money; **ignores everything after the payback date**; measures liquidity rather than profitability; the target period is arbitrary; ignores project size.","Ignores the time value of money; uses **profit rather than cash**, so it is distorted by depreciation policy; averaging hides the timing of returns; the target rate is arbitrary; two competing definitions give different answers."]]}},{"h4":"(b)  The more acceptable technique — net present value"},{"p":"The discounted methods are superior, and **net present value** is the most acceptable of them, because it measures the absolute increase in the value of the business after allowing for the cost of the money tied up. Applying it to project one, at an assumed cost of capital of **10%** (no rate is given in the question — state your assumption):"},{"stmt":{"t":"Project one — net present value at 10% (N)","rows":[["Year","Cash flow","DF @ 10%","Present value","@hd"],[0,-25000,1.0,-25000],[1,8000,0.9091,7273],[2,10000,0.8264,8264],[3,6000,0.7513,4508],[4,1000,0.683,683],[5,1000,0.6209,621],["**Total present value of inflows**","","",21349,"@t"],["**Net present value**","","",-3651,"@tt"]]}},{"p":"**Project one should be rejected.** Although its total undiscounted inflows of N26,000 exceed the N25,000 invested, the margin is only N1,000 — and once the cash flows are discounted at 10% the project shows a **negative NPV of N3,651**. It does not earn the cost of the capital employed in it. Note that its **payback period** is $2 + \\tfrac{7{,}000}{6{,}000}$ — that is, during year 3, at about **2.17 years** — which looks respectable, and is precisely the kind of case where the traditional methods mislead."},{"h4":"A supplied project two, for the comparison the question intends"},{"note":"The figures below are **not from the study text** — they are supplied here so the comparison can be demonstrated. Treat them as an illustration of the method, not as the examiner's data."},{"stmt":{"t":"Project two (supplied) — net present value at 10% (N)","rows":[["Year","Cash flow","DF @ 10%","Present value","@hd"],[0,-25000,1.0,-25000],[1,6000,0.9091,5455],[2,8000,0.8264,6611],[3,9000,0.7513,6762],[4,7000,0.683,4781],[5,5000,0.6209,3105],["**Total present value of inflows**","","",26714,"@t"],["**Net present value**","","",1714,"@tt"]]}},{"p":"**Project two would be chosen.** Its NPV is positive at N1,714 against project one's negative N3,651, even though its payback is **longer** (3.71 years against 2.17) — because it keeps earning after project one has dwindled to N1,000 a year. That is exactly the comparison the examiner was setting up, and exactly why the discounted method is \"the more acceptable technique\"."}]}},{"eg":{"tag":"Exam type — Q8 (PE II, May/June 1994)","t":"Modern Tech. Services — payback periods, and what payback conceals","q":[{"p":"Modern Tech. Services Ltd is considering two alternative projects for a business expansion programme in the Northern part of the country. The projects have the following Naira cash-flow profiles:"},{"stmt":{"t":"Cash flow profiles (N million)","rows":[["Year","Project 1","Project 2","@hd"],[0,"(1.00)","(3.00)"],[1,"(2.00)",0.2],[2,0.95,0.5],[3,0.85,0.65],[4,0.78,0.75],[5,0.62,0.8],[6,0.4,1.9],[7,0.1,0.2]]}},{"p":"**Required:** **(a)** calculate the payback period for each project; **(b)** based on payback periods, advise which project should be chosen; **(c)** state the advantages and disadvantages of the payback period criterion of investment appraisal."}],"a":[{"h4":"(a)  Payback periods"},{"note":"Project 1 has **two outflows** — N1m at time 0 and a further N2m at the end of year 1 — so the total investment to be recovered is N3m, the same as project 2, but it is committed later. The cleanest method is a single cumulative net cash-flow column that includes both the outflows and the inflows."},{"stmt":{"t":"Project 1 — cumulative net cash flow (N million)","rows":[["Year","Cash flow","Cumulative","@hd"],[0,"(1.00)","(1.00)"],[1,"(2.00)","(3.00)"],[2,0.95,"(2.05)"],[3,0.85,"(1.20)"],[4,0.78,"(0.42)"],["**5**","**0.62**","**0.20  ← turns positive**"],[6,0.4,0.6],[7,0.1,0.7]]}},{"tex":"\\text{Payback}_1 = 4 \\text{ years} + \\frac{0.42}{0.62} = 4 + 0.68 = \\mathbf{4.68 \\text{ years}}"},{"stmt":{"t":"Project 2 — cumulative net cash flow (N million)","rows":[["Year","Cash flow","Cumulative","@hd"],[0,"(3.00)","(3.00)"],[1,0.2,"(2.80)"],[2,0.5,"(2.30)"],[3,0.65,"(1.65)"],[4,0.75,"(0.90)"],[5,0.8,"(0.10)"],["**6**","**1.90**","**1.80  ← turns positive**"],[7,0.2,2.0]]}},{"tex":"\\text{Payback}_2 = 5 \\text{ years} + \\frac{0.10}{1.90} = 5 + 0.05 = \\mathbf{5.05 \\text{ years}}"},{"h4":"(b)  Advice based on payback periods"},{"stmt":{"t":"Comparison","rows":[["","Project 1","Project 2","@hd"],["Payback period","4.68 years","5.05 years"],["**Decision on the payback criterion**","**CHOOSE**","reject"]]}},{"p":"**On the payback criterion, Project 1 should be chosen** — it recovers the N3m invested about four and a half months sooner than Project 2."},{"warn":"But look at what that criterion is throwing away. Over the full seven years **Project 1 generates N3.70m of inflows against a N3.00m outlay — a net gain of N0.70m**, while **Project 2 generates N5.00m against the same N3.00m — a net gain of N2.00m**, nearly three times as much. Project 2's single largest inflow, N1.90m in year 6, falls **after** Project 1 has already paid back, so payback never sees it. A company that followed the payback rule here would forgo N1.30m of net cash. This question is a textbook demonstration of payback's central defect, and a complete answer **must say so** even though part (b) asks only for the payback-based advice.","lbl":"What payback conceals here"},{"stmt":{"t":"Total cash generated over the full seven years (N million)","rows":[["","Project 1","Project 2","@hd"],["Total inflows",3.7,5.0],["Total outflows","(3.00)","(3.00)"],["**Net cash generated**",0.7,2.0,"@tt"]]}},{"p":"**Recommendation:** advise the Board that on payback alone Project 1 wins, but that the criterion is inadequate here and a **discounted cash flow appraisal of both projects should be carried out before any commitment is made**. Project 2 is very likely to show the higher net present value despite its slower payback."},{"h4":"(c)  Advantages and disadvantages of the payback period criterion"},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Advantages","Disadvantages"],"rows":[["**Simple** to calculate and to understand, even for managers without financial training.","**Ignores the time value of money** — N1 in year 7 is counted as equal to N1 in year 1."],["**Emphasises liquidity** — it shows how quickly committed cash returns, which matters to a business with tight working capital.","**Ignores all cash flows after the payback date**, however large — as Project 2's N1.90m in year 6 demonstrates."],["**Reduces risk** by favouring projects whose returns arrive early, since near-term forecasts are far more reliable than distant ones.","**Measures liquidity, not profitability** — it cannot say whether a project is worth doing at all."],["**Needs no cost of capital**, so it can be used where that figure is unknown or disputed.","The **target payback period is arbitrary**, set by management rather than derived from principle."],["**Useful as a first screen**, and in volatile markets or fast-obsolescing technology where long forecasts are worthless.","**Ignores the size of the investment** and the total return, so projects of different scale cannot be compared."],["**Objective and consistent** — two analysts working from the same figures get the same answer.","**Ignores the pattern of flows within the payback period**, and takes no account of scrap or residual values."]]}}]}}]}],"formulas":[{"lb":"Payback Period","tex":"\\dfrac{\\text{Cost of Investment}}{\\text{Annual Cash Flow}}"},{"lb":"Accounting Rate of Return","tex":"\\dfrac{\\text{Average annual profit after depreciation}}{\\text{Initial (or average) investment}} \\times 100"},{"lb":"Net Present Value","tex":"NPV = \\sum \\dfrac{\\text{Cash flow}_t}{(1+r)^t} - \\text{Initial outlay}"}],"focus":"NPV computations are tested heavily and reward careful, methodical layout — always show year, cash flow, discount factor and present value as separate columns, and total only at the end. Know payback and ARR's specific weaknesses well enough to explain *why* NPV is preferred (it is the only one of the three that discounts for time), and be ready for a question where the three methods actually disagree on which project to pick — that disagreement, and being able to explain it, is exactly what a Section B question on this topic is usually testing.","errors":["Ignoring cash flows that occur after a project's payback period, when payback is being used only as one input among several — its own blind spot should not become the analyst's.","Comparing ARR figures computed on different bases (initial capital vs average capital) as if they were the same measure.","Forgetting to discount a terminal/disposal cash flow (like the van sale proceeds in Illustration 11.5) at the *same* year's discount factor as any other cash flow in that year.","Treating a project as acceptable because its ARR looks attractive, without checking its NPV — the two can give opposite recommendations.","Including a sunk cost (e.g. the old asset's remaining book value in a replacement decision) in an NPV or payback calculation."],"quiz":{"mcq":[{"q":"A project costs N200,000 and generates N50,000 of cash proceeds each year. Its payback period is","o":["2 years","4 years","5 years","10 years","6 years"],"a":1,"w":"Payback period = Cost of investment / Annual cash flow = 200,000 / 50,000 = 4 years.","calc":"\\dfrac{200{,}000}{50{,}000} = 4","src":"Chapter 11.2","sec":"11.2"},{"q":"A project has an average annual profit after depreciation of N40,000 and an initial investment of N320,000. Its Accounting Rate of Return on initial capital is","o":["8%","12.5%","80%","4%","25%"],"a":0,"w":"ARR = Average profit / Initial investment × 100 = 40,000 / 320,000 × 100 = 12.5%.","calc":"\\dfrac{40{,}000}{320{,}000} \\times 100 = 12.5\\%","src":"Chapter 11.2","sec":"11.2"},{"q":"A project has a Net Present Value of exactly zero at a discount rate of 15%. This means the project's actual return is","o":["greater than 15%","exactly 15%","less than 15%","negative","undefined"],"a":1,"w":"A zero NPV at a given discount rate means the project earns exactly that rate of return — neither more nor less.","src":"Chapter 11.3","sec":"11.3"},{"q":"Under the NPV investment appraisal method, a project should generally be accepted when its NPV is","o":["negative","exactly zero, and only then","positive","equal to the initial outlay","undefined"],"a":2,"w":"The NPV decision rule is to accept a project with a positive net present value.","src":"Chapter 11.3","sec":"11.3"},{"q":"Compared with the Payback Period method, the Net Present Value method","o":["ignores the time value of money entirely, just like payback","properly accounts for the time value of money by discounting future cash flows","only considers cash flows within the payback period","cannot be used alongside the payback method","uses accounting profit, not cash flow"],"a":1,"w":"NPV's defining advantage over payback is that it discounts every future cash flow to reflect the time value of money — payback does not.","src":"Chapter 11.3","sec":"11.3"},{"q":"In a replacement decision, the unabsorbed book value (and its associated future depreciation charge) of the OLD asset being replaced is generally treated as","o":["the single most relevant cost in the decision","a sunk cost, irrelevant except for its tax effect","always equal to the new asset's cost","the main driver of the project's NPV","a future cash inflow"],"a":1,"w":"The old asset's unabsorbed book value is a sunk cost — money already spent, unaffected by the replacement decision itself, relevant only through any tax effect it triggers.","src":"Chapter 11.1","sec":"11.1"},{"q":"Which of the following is a genuine disadvantage of the Accounting Rate of Return method, shared with the Payback Period method?","o":["Both ignore accounting profit entirely","Both ignore that cash flows and outflows occur at different points in time","Both require a computer to calculate","Both always give identical project rankings","Both are discounted cash flow techniques"],"a":1,"w":"Both ARR and payback share the same fundamental weakness: neither accounts for the timing of cash flows — a Naira in year 1 is treated the same as a Naira in year 5.","src":"Chapter 11.2","sec":"11.2"},{"q":"A firm is choosing between two projects using multiple appraisal methods, and finds that Project X has the shorter payback period while Project Y has the higher NPV at the firm's cost of capital. This situation","o":["is impossible and indicates a calculation error","can genuinely occur, since the methods measure different things","means NPV should always be ignored in favour of payback","means the discount rate used must be wrong","only happens when both projects have identical cash flows"],"a":1,"w":"Payback rewards speed of capital recovery; NPV rewards total value created at the required rate of return, properly weighted for timing — the two can genuinely point to different projects, exactly as ARR and payback did in Illustration 11.3.","src":"Chapter 11.2","sec":"11.2"}],"theory":[{"q":"Describe briefly the Payback Period method of investment appraisal, and state five advantages and five disadvantages of the method.","marks":12,"a":[{"p":"The Payback Period is the number of years needed to recover a project's original investment from its cash proceeds (before depreciation, after tax) — computed as cost of investment ÷ annual cash flow, with shorter periods preferred."},{"p":"**Advantages**: easy to understand and simple to operate; its short-term focus reduces exposure to loss from obsolescence; useful for a cash-constrained firm needing quick fund recovery; uses actual cash flows, not accounting profit, making it more objective than ARR; and it is the most widely used method in practice."},{"p":"**Disadvantages**: ignores all cash flows beyond the payback period, penalising long-gestation projects that pay off substantially, just later; ignores the time value of money entirely; does not measure overall project profitability, only speed of capital recovery; can lead to rejecting genuinely valuable long-term projects; and gives no single, universally agreed treatment of working capital recovered at a project's end."}],"src":"Chapter 11.2","sec":"11.2"},{"q":"Give a comparative description of the Payback Period and Net Present Value approaches to investment appraisal. Which do you consider superior?","marks":10,"a":[{"p":"**Payback Period** measures only how quickly the original outlay is recovered, using undiscounted cash flows and ignoring everything that happens after the payback date. **Net Present Value** measures the total value a project creates over its *entire* life, by discounting every cash flow (in every year) back to today's value at the firm's required rate of return, then comparing the total against the initial outlay."},{"p":"**NPV is superior** as a measure of genuine investment worth, because it is the only one of the two that (a) accounts for the time value of money, and (b) considers the *whole* project life, not just the years up to payback. Payback remains useful as a simple, easily-understood secondary screen — particularly for gauging liquidity risk — but should not be relied on as the sole basis for a major capital decision."}],"src":"Chapter 11.3","sec":"11.3"}]}},{"n":12,"t":"Budgeting and Budgetary Control","brief":"Budgeting from first principles through to a full master budget: what a budget is, the types of budget (functional, cash, master), the principal budget factor, the budgeting process (committee, manual, negotiation, behavioural factors), fully worked functional and cash budgets, budgetary control's meaning and process, fixed versus flexible budgeting (with the high-low method), and the budgetary-control improvement techniques — zero-based and incremental budgeting, and ABC in budgeting.","outcomes":["Explain budgeting and budgetary control","State and explain types of budget, including functional, cash, capital and master budgets","Describe the stages in the budgeting process","Prepare different types of budgets, with emphasis on the principal budget factor","Differentiate between fixed and flexible budgeting","Explain zero-based budgeting"],"secs":[{"n":"12.1","t":"What a budget is","b":[{"p":"Every organisation, public or private, needs a budget to guide its operations for the year ahead — how well the budgeting process is handled can define an organisation's success or failure. **Budgetary control** covers budgeting, standard costing and other control techniques together, aimed at positioning the organisation to achieve its objectives; there is no point setting a budget without the control steps to actually achieve it."},{"def":{"t":"Budget","d":"a quantitative and financial plan of the operations of an organisation or individual — identifying the resources and commitments needed to fulfil goals over the budget period. Covers both financial and non-financial aspects of planned operations."}},{"p":"A budget has four defining features:"},{"ol":["it is a plan expressed in **quantities and financials**;","it has objectives, and a clearly expressed means of achieving them;","the means are the **resources** required — definite resources must be set aside for those objectives; and","objectives must be achieved within a defined **time frame** — the budget period."]},{"warn":"A budget is **not**: purely a narrative plan (it must be quantified); something that necessarily involves price increases; or something only large organisations need — individuals, economists and accountants everywhere can and do budget too."}]},{"n":"12.2","t":"Types of budgets","b":[{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Type","Meaning"],"rows":[["**Long-term (strategic/corporate) budget**","A systematic, formalised process directing and controlling future operations, beyond one year — via 8 sequential steps: identify objectives → search alternatives → gather data → evaluate alternatives by cost-benefit → select the optimum and formulate plans → implement through annual budgets → monitor results → respond to variances."],["**Short-term (operational) budget**","Generally covers one year — detailed, with well-defined targets translating into work plans and performance measures per unit. Developed after weighing the business environment (internal/external) and available resources (physical/human/financial)."],["**Annual budget**","Translates the long-range plan into short-term operating targets, usually built with the people responsible for achieving them."],["**Functional budget**","The budget for one function/operation in the organisation. The function with the **binding (limiting) budget factor** is budgeted **first**, since every other functional budget then follows from it."],["**Cash budget**","Summarises the cash-flow effect of all functional and policy budgets, coordinated into one statement."],["**Master budget**","Summarises every budget into one coordinated projected financial statement: budgeted trading/P&L (or income & expenditure) account, budgeted balance sheet, and budgeted cash flow statement."]]}},{"p":"Examples of functional budgets: **sales-related** (sales budget in quantity/value, selling & distribution cost, advertising, sales promotion); **production-related** (production budget, labour usage/cost, materials usage/cost, materials purchases, production overhead, production cost); **service function** (administration cost, personnel cost); and **policy-related** (capital expenditure, R&D, advertising/promotion, training/staff development)."},{"h4":"The principal budget factor"},{"def":{"t":"Principal (limiting/key) budget factor","d":"a factor which, at any given time, is an overriding limitation on the organisation's activities — e.g. staffing limitations, material scarcity, other logistics, limited finance, low sales demand, limited storage. Sales is often, but not always, the limiting factor."}},{"key":"The functional budget for the area with the binding constraint must be prepared **first** — every other functional budget then has to be built around it, not the other way round."},{"h4":"Forecasting versus budgeting"},{"def":{"t":"Forecast","d":"a prediction or estimate of events likely to occur in the future — it becomes a **budget** only once management accepts it as the objective. Considering a sales forecast often leads management to adjust plans, so the agreed sales budget can differ from the original forecast."}},{"key":"Forecasting **supports** budgeting (budgeting for future profit/cash flow requires forecasting future costs and revenues), but a forecast and a budget are not the same thing — a forecast is a prediction; a budget is a **determined plan**, accepted as the target."}]},{"n":"12.3","t":"The objectives of budgeting","b":[{"p":"Budgeting is a critical tool for six purposes:"},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Objective","How it works"],"rows":[["**Planning**","Budgets *force* planning — problems are anticipated before they arise, reducing hasty, spur-of-the-moment decisions; the annual budget also refines strategic plans."],["**Co-ordination**","Synchronises and harmonises the separate plans of different units — compels examining the relationships between operations, so conflicts get resolved."],["**Communication**","Top management communicates expectations to lower management via the budget (effectively an executive order); budgetary reports feed performance information back up."],["**Motivation**","A realistic, attainable budget is a standard of good performance that motivates staff — but **only if staff participated** in developing it."],["**Control**","Variance reports enable management **by exception** — efficient and cost-effective — and budgets clarify managers' spending authority and targets."],["**Performance evaluation**","Budgeted results, built on accepted standards, become the benchmark for measuring actual performance."]]}}]},{"n":"12.4","t":"The budgeting process","b":[{"h4":"Introducing budgeting for the first time"},{"p":"Four things should be in place: a sound financial accounting system; top management backing; a budget committee; and a budget manual."},{"p":"Necessary conditions for success more broadly: top-management support (including line-manager involvement); appropriate accounting/information systems; readiness to administer the budget **flexibly**; a realistic organisation structure with clear responsibilities; clearly defined long-term corporate objectives; and regular budget reviews."},{"h4":"The Budget Committee"},{"def":{"t":"Budget Committee","d":"senior managers overseeing all budgetary matters — typically the CEO, heads of strategic business units, and the chief finance officer. Sets/approves overall budget goals, directs/coordinates preparation, approves the final budget, monitors the year, and reviews results at period end — usually serviced by a **Budget Officer** (normally the finance officer)."}},{"p":"Other functions: setting budget policy guidelines; establishing the budget timetable; reviewing sectional estimates; coordinating budgets; suggesting/approving amendments; generating and analysing control reports; investigating variances; and advising top management on all budget matters."},{"h4":"The Budget Manual"},{"def":{"t":"Budget Manual","d":"sets out budget guidelines and instructions, guiding every departmental head through preparation, and indicating the technique/method for estimating income and expenditure."}},{"p":"Should specify: budget objectives; which functional budgets to prepare; budget committee membership; organisational charts; the preparation procedure; the budget timetable; and budget policy guidelines."},{"h4":"Preparing the initial budget proposal"},{"p":"Internal factors to weigh: changes in equipment/facility availability; new manufacturing processes; changes in product design/mix; new products; and knock-on changes from budget units that supply, or are supplied by, this one. External factors: the labour market; raw material availability/prices; the industry outlook; and competitors' actions."},{"h4":"Negotiation, review, approval, revision"},{"p":"Budget units negotiate their proposals up through the organisational hierarchy — the **core** of the whole budgeting exercise, and where most of the time goes. Approved unit budgets combine into the organisation's budget, which is then reviewed for consistency, short-term goal attainment and strategic fit. Revision procedures vary — some organisations only revise under special conditions, others build in regular (quarterly/monthly) revision."},{"h4":"Behavioural factors in budgeting"},{"p":"Budgets must be **flexibly and effectively administered** to actually help with planning, motivation and control. Employee participation (especially by those who will implement the budget) fosters positive behaviour; budgets must be strict but **genuinely achievable** — unrealistic targets lower motivation. Using budgets as rewards/penalties risks conflict; budgets should be a **cost-control**, not **cost-cutting**, mechanism, and should pursue **goal congruence** between corporate and individual employee goals."},{"key":"Where budgets are administered flexibly and participatively: employees tend to accept the budget as their own plan (goal congruence is achieved); participation raises morale; and employee cohesion raises both morale and productivity."}]},{"n":"12.5","t":"Preparing functional budgets","b":[{"p":"The function with the binding budget factor is budgeted first; every other functional budget follows from it. A **production budget** derives from the sales budget (adjusted for planned inventory change); a **materials usage budget** derives from the production budget; a **materials purchase budget** derives from usage, adjusted for planned inventory change (and any provision for loss/damage)."},{"fbox":{"h":"The functional-budget chain","rows":[{"lb":"Production budget","tex":"\\text{Units to be sold} + \\text{Planned closing inventory} - \\text{Planned opening inventory}"},{"lb":"Materials usage budget","tex":"\\text{Units to be produced} \\times \\text{material required per unit}"},{"lb":"Materials purchase budget","tex":"\\text{Usage} + \\text{Planned closing inventory} - \\text{Planned opening inventory} (+ \\text{provision for loss, if any})"}]}},{"eg":{"tag":"Study text","t":"Illustration 12.1 — a materials purchase budget, and debtors/creditors","open":true,"q":[{"p":"Asuo Ltd plans to produce and sell 8,000 sachets (selling price ¢30). A sachet needs 4 units of Material A, 3 of B, 2 of C. Opening inventory: A 45,000 units (¢270,000), B 35,000 (¢70,000), C 25,000 (¢50,000). Closing inventory should meet the requirement of a further 7,000 sachets. 2023 purchase prices are 30% higher than the prices reflected in opening inventory. Opening debtors ¢350,000, opening creditors ¢270,000; ¢525,000 expected from debtors, ¢235,000 to be paid to creditors during the period. Prepare the raw-material purchases budget, and find the closing debtor/creditor balances."}],"a":[{"warn":"The study text's own printed solution table has two rows shifted out of place — the figures shown against \"Less planned opening inventory\" (60,000/45,000/30,000) match **none** of the data given, and the figures shown against \"Total units to be purchased\" (45,000/35,000/25,000) are simply the **actual opening inventory** repeated. The table below is corrected — verified independently against the illustration's own workings (unit prices ¢7.8/¢2.6/¢2.6, and final purchase values ¢117,000/¢26,000/¢13,000, both of which the source states correctly and which only reconcile with the figures used here)."},{"table":{"align":"lrrr","head":["","Material A","Material B","Material C"],"rows":[["Quantity required to be used (8,000 × 4/3/2)","32,000","24,000","16,000"],["Add: planned closing inventory (7,000 × 4/3/2)","28,000","21,000","14,000"],["","60,000","45,000","30,000"],["Less: planned opening inventory","45,000","35,000","25,000"],["**Total units to be purchased**","**15,000**","**10,000**","**5,000**"],["Planned unit purchase price (see workings below)","¢7.8","¢2.6","¢2.6"],["**Total purchases**","**¢117,000**","**¢26,000**","**¢13,000**"]]}},{"p":"Unit purchase price $= \\dfrac{\\text{opening inventory value}}{\\text{opening inventory units}} \\times 130\\%$ — e.g. Material A: $\\dfrac{270{,}000}{45{,}000} \\times 1.30 = ¢7.80$."},{"table":{"align":"lrr","head":["(b) Debtors and creditors","Debtors (¢)","Creditors (¢)"],"rows":[["Balance b/f","350,000","270,000"],["Add: sales (8,000 × 30) / purchases (117,000+26,000+13,000)","240,000","156,000"],["","590,000","426,000"],["Less: receipts / payments","525,000","235,000"],["**Closing balance**","**65,000**","**191,000**"]]}}]}},{"eg":{"tag":"Study text","t":"Illustration 12.2 — production, usage and purchase budgets for two products sharing materials","open":true,"q":[{"p":"Sakyi Ltd makes Cake (3 litres oil + 4kg fat/unit) and Bluna (5 litres oil + 2kg fat/unit); oil ¢3,000/litre, fat ¢7,000/kg. Budgeted sales: Cake 8,000, Bluna 6,000. Opening finished inventory: Cake 1,500, Bluna 300; planned closing: 600 each. Opening raw material: oil 6,000 litres, fat 2,800kg; planned closing: oil 5,000, fat 3,500. Provision for loss: Cake 50, Bluna 100 units; oil 500 litres, fat 200kg. Prepare the materials purchase budget."}],"a":[{"p":"Production: Cake $= 8{,}000 + 600 - 1{,}500 + 50 = 7{,}150$ units; Bluna $= 6{,}000 + 600 - 300 + 100 = 6{,}400$ units."},{"p":"Usage: oil $= (7{,}150 \\times 3) + (6{,}400 \\times 5) = 21{,}450 + 32{,}000 = 53{,}450$ litres; fat $= (7{,}150 \\times 4) + (6{,}400 \\times 2) = 28{,}600 + 12{,}800 = 41{,}400$ kg."},{"table":{"align":"lrr","head":["Purchases","Oil (litres)","Fat (kg)"],"rows":[["Quantity to meet production","53,450","41,400"],["Add: planned closing inventory","5,000","3,500"],["Less: planned opening inventory","(6,000)","(2,800)"],["Add: provision for loss","500","200"],["**Total to purchase**","**52,950**","**42,300**"],["Unit price","¢3,000","¢7,000"],["**Total purchase value**","**¢158,850,000**","**¢296,100,000**"]]}}]}},{"eg":{"tag":"Study text","t":"Illustration 12.3 — extending the chain to a labour budget","open":true,"q":[{"p":"Cema Ltd makes C1 (20kg RM1 + 30kg RM2/bag) and C2 (25kg RM1 + 25kg RM2/bag). RM1 ¢5,000/kg, RM2 ¢4,000/kg; opening RM1 10,000kg, RM2 8,000kg; closing RM1 2,000kg, RM2 1,000kg; loss provision RM1 500kg, RM2 300kg. Sales: C1 200,000 bags @¢30,000, C2 150,000 @¢22,000. Opening finished inventory C1 40,000, C2 25,000; closing C1 10,000, C2 5,000. Labour: 8hrs/bag (C1), 5hrs/bag (C2) at ¢5,000/hour. Prepare the sales, production, materials purchase and labour budgets."}],"a":[{"p":"Sales value: C1 $= 200{,}000 \\times 30{,}000 = ¢6{,}000$ million; C2 $= 150{,}000 \\times 22{,}000 = ¢3{,}300$ million."},{"p":"Production: C1 $= 200{,}000 + 10{,}000 - 40{,}000 = 170{,}000$ bags; C2 $= 150{,}000 + 5{,}000 - 25{,}000 = 130{,}000$ bags."},{"p":"Usage: RM1 $= (170{,}000 \\times 20) + (130{,}000 \\times 25) = 3{,}400{,}000 + 3{,}250{,}000 = 6{,}650{,}000$ kg; RM2 $= (170{,}000 \\times 30) + (130{,}000 \\times 25) = 5{,}100{,}000 + 3{,}250{,}000 = 8{,}350{,}000$ kg."},{"table":{"align":"lrr","head":["Purchases","RM1 (kg)","RM2 (kg)"],"rows":[["Quantity to meet production","6,650,000","8,350,000"],["Add: planned closing / less opening / add loss provision","net +2,500","net −6,700"],["**Total to purchase**","**6,642,500**","**8,343,300**"],["**Total purchase value** (× ¢5,000 / ¢4,000)","**¢33,212.5 million**","**¢33,373.2 million**"]]}},{"p":"Labour: C1 $= 170{,}000 \\times 8 = 1{,}360{,}000$ hours, wages $= ¢6{,}800$ million; C2 $= 130{,}000 \\times 5 = 650{,}000$ hours, wages $= ¢3{,}250$ million."}]}}]},{"n":"12.6","t":"The cash budget","b":[{"p":"Summarises the cash-flow effect of every functional and policy budget into one coordinated statement."},{"h4":"Why a cash budget helps"},{"ul":["gives **advance warning** of an expected cash deficit — so extra funds can be raised in time;","gives advance warning of a **surplus** — so management knows how much can be invested, and for how long; and","synchronises receipts and payments, so operations run as scheduled."]},{"h4":"Preparing a cash budget"},{"steps":["Ascertain expected cash **inflows** — cash sales, debt collections, share/debenture issues, interest/dividends received, asset disposal proceeds.","Ascertain expected cash **outflows** — cash purchases, supplier payments, share/debenture redemptions, interest/dividend payments, fixed-asset purchases, general expenses, wages/salaries.","Find the difference between total inflow and total outflow — a **surplus or deficit**.","Add the opening cash balance to that surplus/deficit to get the **closing cash balance**."]},{"warn":"**Non-cash expenses** (depreciation, provision for doubtful debts, etc.) never appear in a cash budget — they involve no actual cash movement. The **timing** of every cash flow matters a great deal to the whole exercise."},{"eg":{"tag":"Study text","t":"Illustration 12.4 — a full six-month cash budget","open":true,"q":[{"p":"Vaney Company Ltd: overdraft ¢125m at year-start; a ¢500m short-term loan is drawn down on 31 Dec 2022 (27.5% p.a. simple interest, payable monthly in arrears; principal repaid in 4 equal quarterly instalments). Raw materials cost ¢10,000/unit (25% paid same month, 75% the month after); direct labour ¢1,000/unit (same month); variable expenses ¢6,000/unit (50%/50% same month/next month); sales ¢250,000/unit, 2-month credit period; fixed overheads ¢18m/month; new equipment ¢84m, paid equally in March and September; rent income ¢6m/quarter. Prepare the cash budget for the six months to 30 June 2023."}],"a":[{"table":{"align":"lrrrrrr","head":["¢'000","Jan","Feb","Mar","Apr","May","Jun"],"rows":[["Receipts from debtors","150,000","150,000","192,000","180,000","160,000","125,000"],["Rent","—","—","6,000","—","—","6,000"],["Loan","500,000","—","—","—","—","—"],["**Total inflow**","**650,000**","**150,000**","**198,000**","**180,000**","**160,000**","**131,000**"],["Raw materials","57,500","57,500","75,000","57,500","50,000","52,500"],["Direct labour","5,000","8,000","6,000","5,000","5,000","6,000"],["Variable expenses","33,000","39,500","42,000","33,000","30,000","33,000"],["Fixed overheads","18,000","18,000","18,000","18,000","18,000","18,000"],["Equipment","—","—","42,000","—","—","—"],["Interest","—","11,460","11,460","11,460","11,460","11,460"],["Loan repayment","—","—","125,000","—","—","125,000"],["**Total outflow**","**113,500**","**134,460**","**319,460**","**124,960**","**114,460**","**245,960**"],["**Net cash flow**","**536,500**","**15,540**","**(121,460)**","**55,040**","**45,540**","**(114,960)**"],["Balance b/f","(125,000)","411,500","427,040","305,580","360,620","406,160"],["**Balance c/f**","**411,500**","**427,040**","**305,580**","**360,620**","**406,160**","**291,200**"]]}},{"note":"January's receipts (¢150,000'000) reflect the 2-month credit period — January's receipts are collecting **November's** sales. March's equipment outflow (¢42,000'000) is exactly half of the ¢84m total cost, matching the \"paid equally in March and September\" term. Interest of ¢11,460'000/month $= 500{,}000 \\times 27.5\\% \\div 12$, only starting from February since the loan was drawn down on the very last day of the prior period."}]}}]},{"n":"12.7","t":"The master budget","b":[{"p":"Summarises every budget into one coordinated set of projected financial statements: a budgeted income statement (or income & expenditure account), and a budgeted position statement (balance sheet)."},{"eg":{"tag":"Study text","t":"Illustration 12.5 — a master budget: cash budget, income statement and position statement together","open":true,"q":[{"p":"Papyrus Brothers Ltd adopts budgeting for the first time. Sales mix varies by month (Nov: 30% cash/70% credit; Dec: 45%/55%; all other months 20%/80%; credit customers pay the month after sale). Six months' sales are forecast (Nov–Apr, total ¢232,000'000). Cost structure is 25% materials/55% wages/20% profit of selling price. Suppliers give a 3% discount on half of each month's purchases paid on the spot; the rest is paid the following month. Other expenses, a planned computer purchase, and treasury-bill redemption are also given. Prepare the cash budget, income statement and position statement for the six months to 30 April 2022."}],"a":[{"p":"This illustration works through the **same mechanics** as any cash budget (receipts from a mixed cash/credit sales policy; payments to suppliers net of an early-payment discount) but carries them all the way through to a full **income statement** and **position statement** — the defining feature of a master budget."},{"table":{"align":"lr","head":["Income statement (six months to 30 April 2022)","¢'000"],"rows":[["Sales","232,000"],["Less: cost of sales (opening inventory + purchases)","64,400"],["**Gross profit**","**167,600**"],["Add: discount received","870"],["Less: wages","127,600"],["Less: other expenses","31,980"],["Less: depreciation (buildings, furniture, motor vehicle, computers)","9,417"],["**Net profit/(loss)**","**(527)**"]]}},{"p":"The small net **loss** (¢527'000) despite a healthy gross profit is driven mainly by wages (55% of every sale, per the cost structure given) and the new depreciation charges on land/buildings, furniture, the motor vehicle and the newly-purchased computers."},{"key":"The **position statement** (budgeted balance sheet) must balance: total assets (fixed assets, plus net current assets after deducting trade creditors) equal total financing (opening capital, adjusted for the period's net loss). In this illustration, both sides independently total **¢126,473'000** — confirming the whole set of budgets is internally consistent."}]}}]},{"n":"12.8","t":"Budgetary control","b":[{"def":{"t":"Budgetary control","d":"the whole system of control set up to plan an organisation's activities and take steps to achieve the set plans."}},{"p":"Its process: set standards → formulate budgets → compare actual results with standard performance to find variances → analyse the variances → investigate significant ones → take corrective action."},{"p":"Its objectives are the **same six** as budgeting itself (§12.3): aid planning; assist coordination; facilitate communication; aid control; motivate employees; and facilitate performance evaluation."},{"h4":"The budgetary control process"},{"ol":["establish budgets for each **budget centre** (a clearly defined part of the organisation for operating a budget system);","create **control periods** (budget periods) — influenced by the nature/size of the business, which part of the business is being budgeted, the basis of control, and the velocity of operations;","keep records of actual results through the financial accounting system;","compare actual results with budget;","analyse the reported variances; and","investigate and report the reasons behind the variances observed."]}]},{"n":"12.9","t":"Fixed and flexible budgets","b":[{"def":{"t":"Fixed budget","d":"a budget developed for **one** set level of activity, fixed at the time of preparation."}},{"def":{"t":"Flexible budget","d":"a budget developed for **several** alternative production levels — or an adjustment of an original (fixed) budget, not achieved, to the level of activity actually reached."}},{"key":"Flexible budgeting is built on **cost behaviour**: only the **variable** and **semi-variable** cost elements are adjusted (\"flexed\") to the actual activity level — **fixed** cost stays put regardless of activity."},{"eg":{"tag":"Study text","t":"Illustration 12.6 — flexing a budget with the high-low method","open":true,"q":[{"p":"A flexed budget is given at 70%, 80%, 90% activity:"},{"table":{"align":"lrrr","head":["","70% (¢)","80% (¢)","90% (¢)"],"rows":[["Direct material","17,780","20,320","22,860"],["Direct labour","44,800","51,200","57,600"],["Production overheads","30,500","32,000","33,500"],["Administration overheads","17,000","17,000","17,000"],["**Total cost**","**110,800**","**120,000**","**130,960**"]]}},{"p":"Prepare a budget flexed at 45% activity."}],"a":[{"p":"Using the **high-low method** (change in cost ÷ change in activity level) on each cost element:"},{"table":{"align":"lrr","head":["Element","Variable rate (per 1% activity)","Fixed portion"],"rows":[["Direct material","(22,860−17,780)/20 = ¢254","Nil — fully variable"],["Direct labour","(57,600−44,800)/20 = ¢640","Nil — fully variable"],["Production overhead","(33,500−30,500)/20 = ¢150","33,500 − (150×90) = ¢20,000"],["Administration overhead","—","¢17,000 — fully fixed (unchanged at every level)"]]}},{"key":"Direct material and direct labour turn out to be **entirely variable** (the high-activity total is reproduced exactly by the variable rate alone, leaving no fixed residue); administration overhead is **entirely fixed** (identical at all three activity levels); only production overhead is genuinely mixed, needing the high-low split."},{"table":{"align":"lr","head":["Flexed budget at 45% activity","¢"],"rows":[["Direct material (254 × 45)","11,430"],["Direct labour (640 × 45)","28,800"],["Production overhead — variable (150 × 45)","6,750"],["Production overhead — fixed","20,000"],["Administration overhead (fixed)","17,000"],["**Total cost**","**83,980**"]]}}]}},{"p":"Drawbacks of a budgetary control system: it can be perceived as **punitive** rather than performance-enhancing; it usually meets management **resistance**; it can **stifle motivation** and morale if behavioural aspects are ignored; and it costs real time and money to implement."}]},{"n":"12.10","t":"Zero-based and incremental budgeting","b":[{"def":{"t":"Zero-Based Budgeting (ZBB)","d":"a formalised system of budgeting for every activity as if it were being performed for the **first time** (from a zero base) — identifying alternative levels of provision for each activity, and evaluating them by cost and benefit."}},{"key":"ZBB is built on the philosophy that **existing** activities must be justified exactly like new proposals — compared against alternative uses of the same resources. This implicitly makes **opportunity cost** central to ZBB. In practice, managers do not literally budget from zero — they start from current spend and work *downward*, asking what happens if a given piece of current activity were discontinued. ZBB applies Activity-Based Costing principles."},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Concept","Meaning"],"rows":[["**Decision unit**","A programme of work, capital expenditure, or activity that can be individually operated."],["**Decision package**","A document identifying and describing a decision unit's activities, so management can evaluate and rank it against others."],["**Mutually exclusive decision packages**","Alternative ways of doing the **same** job — cost-benefit analysis selects one; the rest are shelved."],["**Incremental decision packages**","Divide one activity into effort levels — a \"base\" package (the minimum work needed) plus further packages describing additional work, each with its own costs and benefits."]]}},{"h4":"The ranking process in ZBB"},{"p":"Minimum, essential (including legally required) work gets priority. Cost-centre managers rank their own packages; the next level up consolidates rankings across their subordinates (at higher levels this may be a committee, not one manager); activities costing more than they are worth (quantitatively or qualitatively) are dropped; and resources are then allocated by availability of funds and ranking — with senior managers deciding the larger-value packages, but the process running through the **whole** management structure."},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Advantages of ZBB","Disadvantages of ZBB"],"rows":[["Identifies and removes inefficient/obsolete operations; increases psychological pressure against wasteful spending; forces a close look at cost behaviour patterns; responds well to a changing business environment; the documentation gives management coordinated, in-depth operational knowledge; results in more efficient resource allocation.","Creates an enormous, cumbersome volume of extra paperwork; can over-emphasise short-term benefit over long-term; wrongly implies every decision must go through the budget process, restricting responsiveness to genuine surprises; needs decision-analysis skills the organisation may not have; and can be hard to \"sell\" to managers (incremental costs/benefits are hard to quantify precisely, staff/union resistance to changed work methods, and the ranking process itself is difficult with a large volume of hard-to-rank packages)."]]}},{"def":{"t":"Incremental Budgeting","d":"the previous period's budget is used as the base for the current period, adjusted incrementally for factors like inflation, expansion or growth needs."}},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Merits of incremental budgeting","Demerits of incremental budgeting"],"rows":[["Simple to apply; well suited to costs like wages/salaries; widely accepted and used.","Perpetuates past inefficiencies; the preparation process is not critical enough to genuinely diagnose the enterprise's cost behaviour; does not lead to optimal, efficient allocation of budgetary resources."]]}},{"h4":"Activity-Based Costing in budgeting"},{"p":"ABC manages cost by controlling the **activities that drive it**. Cost drivers include machine set-up hours, number of purchase orders, part numbers maintained, supplier/debtor statements processed, man-hours worked, and quantity of material input. Process: break the organisation into activities → pool each activity's costs into its own cost centre → identify the cost driver for each activity → trace activity cost to products using the most relevant driver."},{"h4":"Periodic (continuous/rolling) budgets"},{"def":{"t":"Periodic, Continuous or Rolling budget","d":"a budget prepared for a defined time horizon and kept under continuous review, so it always covers that same length of time as it rolls forward — e.g. a 6-month budget, reviewed monthly, dropping the past month and adding the next, always covering 6 months."}}]},{"n":"12.16","t":"Chapter summary","b":[{"p":"This chapter has covered **budgeting** and **budgetary control** — what a budget is and the misconceptions about it; the types of budget (long-term, short-term, annual, functional, cash, master); the principal budget factor and the role of forecasting; the six objectives of budgeting (planning, co-ordination, communication, motivation, control and performance evaluation); the budgeting process and the people and documents that drive it (the budget committee, the budget manual, the budget officer); the preparation of annual functional budgets, the cash budget and the master budget; the meaning, objectives and process of budgetary control; fixed versus flexible budgets; and the improvement techniques — zero-based budgeting, incremental budgeting, activity-based budgeting and periodic or continuous (rolling) budgets."},{"key":"Three ideas tie the whole chapter together. **(1) The principal budget factor determines the order of preparation** — identify what limits the business first, budget that first, and build everything else around it. **(2) A budget is a plan; budgetary control is the comparison of actual against that plan** — and the comparison is only meaningful if the budget has been **flexed** to the activity level actually achieved. **(3) Budgets are prepared by people and act on people**, so the behavioural consequences — aspiration levels, budgetary slack, participation, management by exception — matter as much as the arithmetic."}]},{"n":"12.17","t":"Worksheet summary — definitions, formulae and structure","b":[{"p":"Everything in this chapter, compressed for a last read-through."},{"h3":"Section-by-section checklist"},{"ol":["**§12.1–12.2 Budget basics** — a budget is quantified, purposeful, resourced and time-bound; 6 named budget types; the principal budget factor is budgeted first; a budget ≠ a forecast (forecast becomes budget only once accepted).","**§12.3 Objectives** — planning, co-ordination, communication, motivation (needs participation), control (management by exception), performance evaluation.","**§12.4 Process** — 4 prerequisites for a first-time system; budget committee/officer/manual roles; negotiation is the real core of budgeting; behavioural factors (goal congruence, realistic targets, cost-control not cost-cutting framing).","**§12.5 Functional budgets** — sales → production → usage → purchases (→ labour) is one chain, each derived from the one before it, adjusted for planned inventory change.","**§12.6 Cash budget** — 4-step build (inflows, outflows, net, running balance); non-cash items never appear; timing is everything.","**§12.7 Master budget** — the cash budget, income statement and position statement together, all built from the same underlying functional budgets, and mutually consistent (the position statement must balance).","**§12.8 Budgetary control** — same 6 objectives as budgeting; a 6-step process (budget centres → control periods → record actuals → compare → analyse → investigate).","**§12.9 Fixed vs flexible** — fixed = one activity level; flexible = adjusts variable/semi-variable cost to actual activity, leaving fixed cost untouched; the high-low method separates a mixed cost's fixed and variable components from two activity-level data points.","**§12.10 ZBB/incremental/ABC** — ZBB justifies every activity from zero, using decision units/packages (mutually exclusive or incremental) and a ranking process; incremental budgeting adjusts last year's figure instead; both have real merits and real risks."]},{"h3":"B. Formula box"},{"fbox":{"h":"Budgeting formulas (recap)","rows":[{"lb":"Production budget","tex":"\\text{Sales} + \\text{Closing inventory} - \\text{Opening inventory}"},{"lb":"Materials purchase budget","tex":"\\text{Usage} + \\text{Closing inventory} - \\text{Opening inventory} + \\text{Loss provision}"},{"lb":"High-low variable rate","tex":"\\dfrac{\\text{Cost at high activity} - \\text{Cost at low activity}}{\\text{High activity} - \\text{Low activity}}"},{"lb":"Fixed cost, from high-low","tex":"\\text{Total cost at high activity} - (\\text{Variable rate} \\times \\text{High activity})"}]}}]},{"n":"12.18","t":"Study text questions — multiple-choice and short-answer","b":[{"eg":{"tag":"Study text","t":"Multiple-choice and short-answer questions, with answers","open":true,"q":[{"ol":["Which of the following options best describes a \"long-term budget\"? (A) A budget for the life time of the company  (B) A budget of six months' duration in a fast-moving goods industry  (C) A budget whose duration could not be determined  (D) A budget for periods extending beyond one year  (E) A past budget retrieved for use in the current period","What is the position of depreciation in a cash budget? (A) It is in the cash inflow section  (B) It is in the cash outflow section  (C) It is not reckoned with  (D) It is deducted from the forecast capital expenditure  (E) It is broken down into straight line and reducing balance","Why should the sales budget be synchronised with the production budget? (A) The sales manager and the production manager are on the same level  (B) They are the most important budgets  (C) The sales manager and production manager are two senior members of the budget committee  (D) Budgeting starts from sales and ends with production  (E) The production budget flows into the sales budget","Which of the following ensures the achievement of a production budget? (A) Material purchase should provide for sufficient raw materials  (B) A very efficient production manager should be employed to run the company  (C) Existing machines should be discarded and new ones installed  (D) The factory should run 24 hours  (E) All the company's debtors should pay up immediately","What is the usual cause for budget revision? (A) When the managing director changes his mind  (B) When conditions existing at budget preparation time change midway in the implementation  (C) Machine breakdown  (D) Decisions taken at the Annual General Meeting  (E) When the budget has been fully achieved in the first month","What does budgetary control seek NOT to do in an organisation? (A) Planning of annual operations  (B) Controlling company's activities  (C) Communicating plans to responsible managers  (D) Evaluating performance of managers  (E) Punishing lazy operators","Which of the following best describes a \"Budget Centre\"? (A) A centre where budgets are prepared  (B) A place where budgets are implemented  (C) A clearly defined part of an organisation for the purpose of operating a budget system  (D) An office where the budget committee holds its meetings  (E) The centre where the performance of budgets is controlled","Flexible budgeting is a method of budgeting where …………. (A) Managers are allowed to operate without stringent conditions  (B) Budget is developed for different and alternative production levels  (C) Revisions are permitted on a monthly basis  (D) Actual performance is worked back to the budget  (E) The budget period is not fixed","What does \"Incremental budgeting\" mean? (A) Using a current period's budget as the basis for preparing the succeeding period's budget by making incremental adjustments as necessary  (B) Increasing the budget in line with actuals  (C) Preparing a budget by adding a flat rate percentage increase on actuals  (D) Not allowing any figure to be unchanged but increased from year to year  (E) Not allowing any figure to be reduced but increased from year to year","Responsibility accounting is a system where…… (A) Only responsible managers are involved in budgeting and implementation  (B) Every manager is jointly and severally responsible for the company's activities  (C) The company's Chief Executive Officer has the final responsibility  (D) Each manager is actively involved in drawing up the budget for his own sphere of activity and is also responsible for achieving the targets set  (E) Responsibilities are shared between each manager and his subordinates","A budget which summarises all other budgets into a coordinated financial statement is called ………………………..","A factor which places a restriction over the activities of an organisation is known as ...","Until a budget is approved by management, it is called …………………","A method of forecasting which selects the periods of highest and lowest activity levels and compares the changes in costs resulting from the changes in the two activity levels is known as …………………………..","The document which sets out the budget guidelines which guide departmental heads in preparing their budgets is referred to as ……………………….","A method of budgeting where the budget is developed for a given level of activity is known as ……………………","A system of budgeting for the activities of an enterprise as if each activity is being performed for the first time is called ………………………..","A budget that is prepared for a set time horizon and continuously reviewed to cover the set length of time at any particular point in time is known as ……………………...","The person responsible for the overall coordination of budgeting and budgetary control activities in an organisation is called ……………………","A situation where the budget for a period is changed substantially midway into that period due to some unforeseen circumstances is known as …………………………"]}],"a":[{"ol":["**D** — a budget for periods extending beyond one year.","**C** — depreciation is a non-cash expense, so it is not reckoned with in a cash budget at all.","**E** — the production budget flows from (is derived to satisfy) the sales budget.","**A** — material purchases must provide for sufficient raw materials to meet the production budget.","**B** — a change in the conditions assumed when the budget was first prepared, occurring midway through implementation.","**E** — punishing lazy operators is not a stated objective of budgetary control; the other four all are.","**C** — a clearly defined part of an organisation for the purpose of operating a budget system.","**B** — a budget developed for different and alternative production levels.","**A** — using the current period's budget as the basis for the next period's, with incremental adjustments.","**D** — each manager is actively involved in drawing up the budget for their own sphere of activity, and is responsible for achieving the targets set.","**Master Budget.**","**Limiting factor (principal budget factor).**","**A forecast** — per §12.3.8, a forecast only becomes a budget once management accepts it as the objective.","**The High and Low Method.**","**The Budget Manual.**","**Fixed budget.**","**Zero-Based Budgeting.**","**Continuous (Rolling) budget.**","**The Budget Officer.**","**Budget revision.**"]}]}}]},{"n":"12.19","t":"Study text examination-type questions, worked (Q1–Q8)","b":[{"p":"The study text closes the chapter with **seventeen** examination-type questions — by far the largest set in the subject. They are reproduced below with full answers, split across two sections for readability: Q1 to Q8 here, Q9 to Q17 in §12.20. Each carries the examining body, paper and diet the study text credits it to, and its mark allocation, exactly as printed."},{"eg":{"tag":"Exam type — Q1 (16 marks) — ICAG, November 2003","t":"Budget manual and budget committee; a raw materials purchases budget","q":[{"p":"**(a)** Write brief notes on the following: (i) Budget Manual; (ii) Budget Committee. *(4 marks)*"},{"p":"**(b)** A company plans to produce and sell **4,000 units** of product X during the next period. The selling price of product X is **¢10 per unit**. A unit of product X requires **4 units of material A, 3 units of material B and 2 units of material C**."},{"stmt":{"t":"Opening inventory of raw materials","rows":[["","Units","Total value (¢)","@hd"],["Material A",20000,40000],["Material B",15000,15000],["Material C",10000,5000]]}},{"ul":["The **closing inventory** for each raw material is to be at a level which would meet the production requirements for **3,000 units** of product X.","There is **no opening or closing inventory of product X**.","Purchase prices for all raw materials during the period are expected to be **20% higher** than the prices reflected in the opening inventory values.","Sales and purchases are on credit, with opening balances of **debtors ¢30,000** and **creditors ¢20,000**.","The company expects to **receive ¢45,000 from debtors** during the period and plans to **pay ¢23,000 to its creditors**."]},{"p":"**Required:** (i) prepare the budget for raw material purchases; (ii) calculate the closing balances for debtors and creditors. *(12 marks)*"}],"a":[{"h4":"(a)(i)  The budget manual"},{"def":{"t":"Budget manual","d":"the document (or set of documents) that sets out the organisation's budgeting procedures — the standing instructions by which budgets are prepared, approved and operated. It is to budgeting what an accounts manual is to coding."}},{"p":"Its main contents:"},{"ol":["A **statement of the objectives** of the budgetary system, and an explanation of how it contributes to the organisation's objectives.","An **organisation chart** showing who is responsible for which budget, and the lines of authority and reporting.","The **membership, duties and powers of the budget committee**, and the role of the budget officer.","The **budget timetable** — the dates by which each stage must be completed.","The **account codes and cost-centre codes** to be used, so that budget and actual are coded identically and can be compared.","**Specimen forms and schedules** for the submission of budget proposals, with instructions on how to complete them.","The **procedures for review, negotiation, approval and revision** of budgets.","The **reporting procedures** — what control reports are produced, by whom, to whom, how often, and what action is expected on a variance.","A **glossary of terms** used in the system."]},{"h4":"(a)(ii)  The budget committee"},{"def":{"t":"Budget committee","d":"the body, normally chaired by a senior executive and comprising the heads of the main functions, responsible for co-ordinating and directing the preparation of the budgets."}},{"p":"Its duties:"},{"ol":["**Issue the budget manual and the timetable**, and the policy guidelines and assumptions (price levels, wage rates, activity levels) on which budgets are to be built.","**Identify the principal budget factor** and direct that the budget for it be prepared first.","**Receive, review and co-ordinate the functional budgets** submitted by departmental managers, resolving conflicts between them.","**Negotiate with departmental managers** where a submitted budget is unacceptable, and suggest revisions.","**Approve the budgets**, or recommend them to the board for approval, and assemble the master budget.","**Monitor actual results against budget**, receive the control reports, and investigate significant variances.","**Recommend revisions** to budgets where circumstances change materially."]},{"note":"The **budget officer** (often the management accountant) is the committee's executive — secretary to the committee, custodian of the manual and timetable, and the person who actually assembles the figures and chases the deadlines. See Q6 below."},{"h4":"(b)  Working — the materials required for production"},{"stmt":{"t":"Step 1 — quantities required and opening prices","rows":[["","Material A","Material B","Material C","@hd"],["Units required per unit of product X",4,3,2],["**Required for 4,000 units of production**",16000,12000,8000],"@gap",["Opening inventory — units",20000,15000,10000],["Opening inventory — value (¢)",40000,15000,5000],["**Opening price per unit (¢)**",2.0,1.0,0.5,"@t"],["**Purchase price per unit, +20% (¢)**",2.4,1.2,0.6,"@tt"]]}},{"h4":"(b)(i)  Raw material purchases budget"},{"stmt":{"t":"Raw material purchases budget","rows":[["","Material A","Material B","Material C","Total","@hd"],["Required for production (4,000 units of X)",16000,12000,8000,""],["Add: closing inventory (to cover 3,000 units of X)",12000,9000,6000,""],["",28000,21000,14000,"","@t"],["Less: opening inventory",-20000,-15000,-10000,""],["**Purchases required (units)**",8000,6000,4000,"","@t"],["× purchase price per unit (¢)",2.4,1.2,0.6,""],["**Purchases (¢)**",19200,7200,2400,28800,"@tt"]]}},{"h4":"(b)(ii)  Closing balances for debtors and creditors"},{"stmt":{"t":"Debtors control (¢)","rows":[["Opening balance","",30000],["Add: credit sales  (4,000 units × ¢10)","",40000],["","",70000,"@t"],["Less: cash received from debtors","",-45000],["**Closing debtors**","",25000,"@tt"]]}},{"stmt":{"t":"Creditors control (¢)","rows":[["Opening balance","",20000],["Add: credit purchases (from (i) above)","",28800],["","",48800,"@t"],["Less: cash paid to creditors","",-23000],["**Closing creditors**","",25800,"@tt"]]}},{"key":"The purchases budget identity is worth memorising, because every functional budget in this chapter is a version of it: **what you need, plus what you want to be left with, less what you already have.** Applied to materials it gives purchases; applied to finished goods it gives production; applied to cash it gives the financing requirement. Note too that the closing inventory is expressed in **units of the finished product** (3,000 units of X), so it must be converted to units of each material before it can be used."}]}},{"eg":{"tag":"Exam type — Q2 (15 marks) — ICAG, November 1998","t":"Ayehu Ltd — five functional budgets built from standard cost data","q":[{"p":"You have just assumed the post of cost accountant at Ayehu Ltd. The financial controller gives you the data below."},{"stmt":{"t":"Budgeted sales for the year, by region (¢000)","rows":[["Region","Product X","Product Y","@hd"],["Greater Accra",5000,7500],["Central",3200,6400],["Ashanti",3000,2400],["Eastern",3800,3000],["**Total**",15000,19300,"@tt"]]}},{"stmt":{"t":"Standard data per unit of production — direct materials","rows":[["Material","Standard price per kg (¢)","Product X (kg)","Product Y (kg)","@hd"],["Ceca",2,10,5],["Yede",4,5,10]]}},{"stmt":{"t":"Standard data per unit of production — direct labour","rows":[["Plant","Standard rate per hour (¢)","Product X (hours)","Product Y (hours)","@hd"],["Plant 1",25,4,4],["Plant 2",20,6,3.5]]}},{"stmt":{"t":"Finished goods inventory, valued at standard production cost (¢000)","rows":[["","Product X","Product Y","@hd"],["1 January",640,600],["31 December",320,840]]}},{"ul":["Fixed production overhead per annum **¢3,055,000**; direct labour hours per annum **763,750**. All production overhead is fixed and is absorbed on a **direct labour hour** basis.","Administration, selling and distribution expenses are absorbed on a budgeted basis of **20% of production cost**.","**Profit is budgeted at 25% of selling price.**"]},{"p":"**Required:** prepare (a) the production budget; (b) the direct materials cost budget; (c) the direct materials purchase budget; (d) the direct labour utilisation budget; (e) the direct wages budget."},{"note":"The study text's data table is printed with the material prices and the labour rates displaced from their rows. The reading above — Ceca ¢2/kg, Yede ¢4/kg, Plant 1 ¢25/hour, Plant 2 ¢20/hour — is confirmed below, because it is the only reading under which the sales values divide into **whole units**."}],"a":[{"h4":"Step 1 — the standard production cost per unit"},{"tex":"\\text{Fixed overhead absorption rate} = \\frac{¢3{,}055{,}000}{763{,}750 \\text{ hours}} = ¢4 \\text{ per direct labour hour}"},{"stmt":{"t":"Standard production cost per unit (¢)","rows":[["","Product X","Product Y","@hd"],["**Direct materials**","","","@hd"],["Ceca  (10 kg × ¢2;  5 kg × ¢2)",20,10],["Yede  (5 kg × ¢4;  10 kg × ¢4)",20,40],["",40,50,"@t"],["**Direct labour**","","","@hd"],["Plant 1  (4 hrs × ¢25;  4 hrs × ¢25)",100,100],["Plant 2  (6 hrs × ¢20;  3.5 hrs × ¢20)",120,70],["",220,170,"@t"],["**Production overhead**  (10 hrs × ¢4;  7.5 hrs × ¢4)",40,30],["**Standard production cost per unit**",300,250,"@tt"]]}},{"h4":"Step 2 — the selling price, and therefore the sales units"},{"p":"Profit is 25% of selling price, so **total cost is 75% of selling price**. Total cost is production cost plus administration, selling and distribution at 20% of production cost, i.e. **1.2 × production cost**. Therefore:"},{"tex":"\\text{Selling price} = \\frac{1.2 \\times \\text{production cost}}{0.75} = 1.6 \\times \\text{production cost}"},{"stmt":{"t":"Selling price and sales units","rows":[["","Product X","Product Y","@hd"],["Standard production cost per unit (¢)",300,250],["**Selling price per unit  (× 1.6)**","¢480","¢400","@t"],"@gap",["Budgeted sales value (¢)",15000000,19300000],["**Budgeted sales units**",31250,48250,"@tt"]]}},{"note":"Both divisions come out as **exact whole numbers** — 31,250 and 48,250 units. That is the confirmation that the standard costs of ¢300 and ¢250, and therefore the reading of the mis-printed price and rate columns, are right. Always look for a check of this kind before building four budgets on top of a reconstructed figure."},{"h4":"(a)  Production budget"},{"stmt":{"t":"Finished goods inventory in units","rows":[["","Product X","Product Y","@hd"],["1 January  (¢640,000 ÷ ¢300;  ¢600,000 ÷ ¢250)","2,133","2,400"],["31 December  (¢320,000 ÷ ¢300;  ¢840,000 ÷ ¢250)","1,067","3,360"]]}},{"warn":"Product X's inventory values do **not** divide exactly by its ¢300 standard cost — ¢640,000 gives 2,133.33 units and ¢320,000 gives 1,066.67. Product Y's divide perfectly (2,400 and 3,360). The X figures are therefore mis-typed in the study text; they have been rounded to the nearest unit above, and the effect on the budgets is immaterial. Flag the inconsistency in your answer rather than passing over it.","lbl":"An inconsistency in the printed inventory values"},{"stmt":{"t":"(a)  Production budget (units)","rows":[["","Product X","Product Y","@hd"],["Budgeted sales",31250,48250],["Add: closing finished goods inventory",1067,3360],["",32317,51610,"@t"],["Less: opening finished goods inventory",-2133,-2400],["**Budgeted production**",30184,49210,"@tt"]]}},{"h4":"(b)  Direct materials cost budget"},{"stmt":{"t":"(b)  Direct materials cost budget","rows":[["","Ceca (kg)","Ceca (¢)","Yede (kg)","Yede (¢)","@hd"],["Product X:  30,184 units × 10 kg / × 5 kg",301840,603680,150920,603680],["Product Y:  49,210 units × 5 kg / × 10 kg",246050,492100,492100,1968400],["**Total**",547890,1095780,643020,2572080,"@tt"]]}},{"stmt":{"t":"Summary — total direct materials cost","rows":[["Ceca:  547,890 kg × ¢2","",1095780],["Yede:  643,020 kg × ¢4","",2572080],["**Total direct materials cost budget**","",3667860,"@tt"]]}},{"h4":"(c)  Direct materials purchase budget"},{"warn":"The question gives **no opening or closing raw-material inventories** — only finished-goods inventories. With no raw-material inventory movement, purchases must equal usage, and the purchase budget is identical to the cost budget at (b). State this assumption explicitly; it is what the examiner is testing, and inventing inventory figures would be worse than noting their absence."},{"stmt":{"t":"(c)  Direct materials purchase budget","rows":[["","Quantity (kg)","Price per kg (¢)","Cost (¢)","@hd"],["Ceca",547890,2,1095780],["Yede",643020,4,2572080],["**Total purchases**","","",3667860,"@tt"]]}},{"h4":"(d)  Direct labour utilisation budget"},{"stmt":{"t":"(d)  Direct labour utilisation budget (hours)","rows":[["","Plant 1","Plant 2","Total","@hd"],["Product X:  30,184 units × 4 hrs / × 6 hrs",120736,181104,301840],["Product Y:  49,210 units × 4 hrs / × 3.5 hrs",196840,172235,369075],["**Total direct labour hours**",317576,353339,670915,"@tt"]]}},{"h4":"(e)  Direct wages budget"},{"stmt":{"t":"(e)  Direct wages budget","rows":[["","Hours","Rate per hour (¢)","Wages (¢)","@hd"],["Plant 1",317576,25,7939400],["Plant 2",353339,20,7066780],["**Total direct wages**",670915,"",15006180,"@tt"]]}},{"note":"A final observation worth a mark: the budget requires **670,915** direct labour hours, but the overhead absorption rate was set on **763,750** hours. On the budgeted output the company will therefore absorb only $670{,}915 \\times ¢4 = ¢2{,}683{,}660$ of the ¢3,055,000 fixed production overhead, leaving **¢371,340 under-absorbed**. The budget committee should be told, because it means either the absorption rate or the activity assumption behind it needs revisiting."}]}},{"eg":{"tag":"Exam type — Q3 (20 marks) — ICAG, May 2002","t":"Korkor — the maximum overdraft, the budgeted profit and the budgeted balance sheet","q":[{"p":"Below are the summarised final accounts of Korkor, a business woman, for the year ended 31 December 2021."},{"stmt":{"t":"Income Statement (¢000)","rows":[["Sales","",480000],["Cost of sales","",360000],["**Gross profit**","",120000,"@t"],["General expenses (including depreciation)","",106000],["**Net profit**","",14000,"@tt"]]}},{"stmt":{"t":"Balance Sheet extract (¢000)","rows":[["Fixed assets",100000,""],["Less: depreciation",30000,70000],"@gap",["**Current assets**","","","@hd"],["Inventory",45000,""],["Debtors",40000,""],["Bank",2000,87000],["Less: current liabilities — creditors",60000,""],["**Net current assets**","",27000,"@t"],["","",97000,"@tt"]]}},{"p":"The following additional information is provided:"},{"ol":["Korkor proposes to buy new premises at a cost of **¢50 million**, to be paid for in **April**.","There is heavy demand for her product and she is confident that **from 1 April monthly sales will increase by 20%** if the new premises are acquired.","The **gross profit percentage would remain unchanged**, and general expenses — inclusive of an allowance for bank interest at 15% — would **increase by ¢1.2 million per month from 1 March**.","Korkor expects **creditors to increase in proportion to sales**, and the inventory-in-trade balance will remain at **¢45 million**.","Depreciation on existing fixed assets remains at **20% straight line**. The additional building will be depreciated straight-line over a **20-year life**; for this purpose the buildings comprise **¢40 million** of the planned ¢50 million.","General expenses are **paid monthly in cash**, and Korkor draws **¢1 million** from the business bank account each month for personal expenses."]},{"p":"Korkor intends to employ two sources of finance for the planned expansion: **(i)** she has asked for a **bank overdraft facility of ¢20 million** for the next 12 months; **(ii)** she will offer customers a **discount of 2% for immediate payment** on all sales from 1 April 2022 onwards, and expects **half** of customers to take the offer, the remainder taking the same period of credit as in the previous year."},{"p":"**Required:** **(a)** calculate the estimated **maximum overdraft** Korkor will require during the year to 31 December 2022, and the bank balance or overdraft at 31 December 2022 *(10 marks)*; **(b)** prepare the estimated Profit and Loss Account for the year ended 31 December 2022 *(6 marks)*; **(c)** prepare the estimated Balance Sheet as at 31 December 2022 *(4 marks)*."}],"a":[{"h4":"Step 1 — the credit periods implied by the 2021 accounts"},{"stmt":{"t":"Credit periods, derived from last year","rows":[["Debtors ¢40,000 ÷ sales ¢480,000 × 12","","**1 month** credit given"],["Creditors ¢60,000 ÷ purchases ¢360,000 × 12","","**2 months** credit taken"]]}},{"note":"Neither credit period is stated in the question — both have to be **derived from the opening balance sheet**. This is the step that separates a complete answer from a stalled one, and it is the normal way such questions are set."},{"h4":"Step 2 — monthly sales, purchases and expenses for 2022"},{"stmt":{"t":"Monthly figures (¢000)","rows":[["","January–March","April–December","@hd"],["Monthly sales  (¢480,000 ÷ 12;  × 1.20)",40000,48000],["Monthly purchases  (75% of sales — gross margin unchanged at 25%)",30000,36000],"@gap",["**Annual totals**","","","@hd"],["Sales  (3 × 40,000 + 9 × 48,000)","",552000],["Cost of sales / purchases  (75% × 552,000;  inventory unchanged)","",414000]]}},{"stmt":{"t":"General expenses (¢000)","rows":[["2021 general expenses, including depreciation","",106000],["Less: depreciation on existing fixed assets  (20% × 100,000)","",-20000],["**2021 cash general expenses**","",86000,"@t"],["**Monthly cash general expenses  (86,000 ÷ 12)**","","7,166.67"],["**From 1 March, plus ¢1,200 per month**","","8,366.67"],["**2022 total cash general expenses**  (2 × 7,166.67 + 10 × 8,366.67)","",98000,"@tt"]]}},{"warn":"The ¢1.2 million monthly increase is stated to be **inclusive of the allowance for bank interest at 15%**, so **no separate interest charge is computed**. Candidates who calculate interest on the overdraft and add it again are double-counting."},{"h4":"Step 3 — cash receipts from customers"},{"p":"Until March, all customers take one month's credit. From April, **50% pay immediately and take a 2% discount**, and 50% continue to take one month."},{"stmt":{"t":"Monthly cash receipts (¢000)","rows":[["Month","Working","Receipts","@hd"],["January","December 2021 sales (opening debtors)",40000],["February","January sales",40000],["March","February sales",40000],["April","50% × 48,000 × 0.98  +  March sales 40,000",63520],["May–December (each)","50% × 48,000 × 0.98  +  50% × 48,000",47520],["**Total receipts for the year**","",563680,"@tt"]]}},{"note":"Proof: opening debtors 40,000 + sales 552,000 − closing debtors 24,000 (50% of December sales) − discounts allowed 4,320 ($2\\% \\times 50\\% \\times 9 \\times 48{,}000$) = **¢563,680** ✓"},{"h4":"(a)  The cash budget, month by month"},{"stmt":{"t":"Cash budget for the year to 31 December 2022 (¢000)","rows":[["Month","Receipts","Creditors","Expenses","Drawings","Premises","Net","Balance","@hd"],["Opening balance","","","","","","",2000],["January",40000,30000,"7,166.67",1000,"—","1,833.33","3,833.33"],["February",40000,30000,"7,166.67",1000,"—","1,833.33","5,666.67"],["March",40000,30000,"8,366.67",1000,"—","633.33","6,300.00"],["**April**",63520,30000,"8,366.67",1000,50000,"(25,846.67)","**(19,546.67)**"],["May",47520,30000,"8,366.67",1000,"—","8,153.33","(11,393.33)"],["June",47520,36000,"8,366.67",1000,"—","2,153.33","(9,240.00)"],["July",47520,36000,"8,366.67",1000,"—","2,153.33","(7,086.67)"],["August",47520,36000,"8,366.67",1000,"—","2,153.33","(4,933.33)"],["September",47520,36000,"8,366.67",1000,"—","2,153.33","(2,780.00)"],["October",47520,36000,"8,366.67",1000,"—","2,153.33","(626.67)"],["November",47520,36000,"8,366.67",1000,"—","2,153.33","1,526.67"],["December",47520,36000,"8,366.67",1000,"—","2,153.33","**3,680.00**"]]}},{"note":"Payments to creditors lag purchases by **two months**: January to May pay for November 2021 to March 2022 purchases of ¢30,000 each; June to December pay for April to October purchases of ¢36,000 each. Proof: opening creditors 60,000 + purchases 414,000 − closing creditors 72,000 (two months at ¢36,000) = **¢402,000** paid, which is what the column totals."},{"stmt":{"t":"(a)  Answers","rows":[["**Maximum overdraft required — in April**","","¢19,546,670"],["**Bank balance at 31 December 2022**","","¢3,680,000 in hand"]]}},{"key":"The peak overdraft of **¢19.55 million falls just inside the ¢20 million facility Korkor has requested** — but with less than half a million naira of headroom, and only because the 2% discount scheme starts in the same month as the ¢50 million payment. If the discount take-up were lower than the 50% assumed, or the premises payment fell a month earlier, the facility would be breached. The answer should say so."},{"h4":"(b)  Estimated Profit and Loss Account for the year ended 31 December 2022"},{"stmt":{"t":"Estimated Profit and Loss Account (¢000)","rows":[["Sales","",552000],["Less: cost of sales  (75%)","",414000],["**Gross profit  (25%)**","",138000,"@t"],"@gap",["Less: general expenses (cash)",98000,""],["          discounts allowed",4320,""],["          depreciation — existing fixed assets (20% × 100,000)",20000,""],["          depreciation — new building (¢40,000 ÷ 20 years × 9/12)",1500,123820],["**Net profit**","",14180,"@tt"]]}},{"note":"Only **¢40 million** of the ¢50 million premises cost is depreciable building; the remaining ¢10 million is land and is not depreciated. The building was acquired in April, so only **nine months** of the ¢2,000 annual charge is taken."},{"h4":"(c)  Estimated Balance Sheet as at 31 December 2022"},{"stmt":{"t":"Estimated Balance Sheet as at 31 December 2022 (¢000)","rows":[["**Fixed assets**","","","@hd"],["Cost  (100,000 + 50,000)",150000,""],["Less: accumulated depreciation  (30,000 + 21,500)",51500,98500],"@gap",["**Current assets**","","","@hd"],["Inventory",45000,""],["Debtors  (50% of December sales)",24000,""],["Bank",3680,72680],["Less: current liabilities — creditors (2 months × ¢36,000)",72000,""],["**Net current assets**","",680,"@t"],["**Total net assets**","",99180,"@tt"],"@gap",["**Capital account**","","","@hd"],["Balance at 1 January 2022","",97000],["Add: net profit for the year","",14180],["Less: drawings  (12 × ¢1,000)","",-12000],["**Balance at 31 December 2022**","",99180,"@tt"]]}},{"key":"The balance sheet closes exactly on **¢99,180** from both directions — net assets and capital — which proves every figure in the cash budget and the profit statement. **Always finish a question of this kind by checking that the two halves agree**; if they do not, the error is almost always a cash item that has been put through the profit statement, or an accrual that has been put through the cash budget."},{"note":"Worth telling Korkor: the expansion increases sales by 15% and profit by just **¢180,000, or 1.3%** — because the extra gross profit of ¢18 million is almost entirely absorbed by the ¢12 million of extra general expenses, the ¢4.3 million of discounts and the ¢1.5 million of new depreciation. The discount scheme in particular costs ¢4.32 million a year to accelerate collections that save her only part of one month's interest. She should consider whether a larger overdraft facility would be cheaper than the discount."}]}},{"eg":{"tag":"Exam type — Q4 (20 marks) — ICAG, May 2001","t":"Nsapa Breweries — cash budget, budgeted profit and loss, and budgeted balance sheet","q":[{"p":"The position statement of Nsapa Breweries as at 31 December 2021:"},{"stmt":{"t":"Position statement as at 31 December 2021 (¢000)","rows":[["Stated capital","",200000],["Income surplus","",89200],["","",289200,"@tt"],"@gap",["**Financing**","","","@hd"],["Fixed assets  (cost ¢280,000,000)","",252000],"@gap",["**Current assets**","","","@hd"],["Inventory",50000,""],["Trade debtors",49200,""],["Bank",6000,105200],"@gap",["**Current liabilities**","","","@hd"],["Trade creditors",50000,""],["Expense creditors",18000,68000],["**Net current assets**","",37200,"@t"],["","",289200,"@tt"]]}},{"stmt":{"t":"Forecasts","rows":[["","Nov","Dec","Jan","Feb","Mar","Apr","May","Jun","@hd"],["Sales (units)",4000,4200,4500,4600,4800,5000,3800,3000],["Purchases (¢000)",24000,26000,28000,36000,32000,28000,24000,24000],["Wages and salaries (¢000)",16000,16000,16000,20000,20000,20000,24000,24000],["Overheads excl. depreciation (¢000)",14000,14000,14000,14000,14000,16000,16000,16000],["Purchase of fixed assets (¢000)","","","","","",60000,"",""],["Issue of shares (¢000)","","","","","",40000,"",""],["Dividend (¢000)","","","","","",20000,"",""]]}},{"ol":["The selling price in November was **¢12,000 per unit**, to be increased to **¢16,000 per unit in May**. **50% of sales are for cash and 50% on credit, to be paid two months later.**","Purchases are to be paid for **two months after purchase**.","**75% of the wages and salaries bill** relates to management salaries, paid in the same month; **25% relates to wages of operatives**, paid in the first week of the following month.","Overheads are paid **in the month after they are incurred**.","The fixed assets are to be paid for in **three equal instalments in the three months following purchase**.","Dividends are paid **three months after they are declared**; receipts from the share issue are received in the month of issue.","Fixed assets are depreciated **10% per annum on a straight-line basis**.","Opening inventory at 1 January was equal to the previous **two** months' purchases. At 30 June it would be equal to **three** months' purchases."]},{"p":"**Required:** **(a)** prepare for the six months ended 30 June: (i) the cash budget; (ii) the budgeted profit and loss account; (iii) the budgeted balance sheet. **(b)** Comment on the results, highlighting those areas you would draw to the attention of the budget committee."}],"a":[{"h4":"Step 1 — check the opening balance sheet against the forecasts"},{"p":"Before computing anything, verify that the opening balances are consistent with the stated credit terms. All four agree exactly, which confirms every assumption:"},{"stmt":{"t":"Reconciliation of the opening balance sheet (¢000)","rows":[["Item","Should equal","Computed","Balance sheet","@hd"],["Trade debtors","50% of November + December sales","50% × (48,000 + 50,400) = 49,200",49200],["Inventory","November + December purchases","24,000 + 26,000 = 50,000",50000],["Trade creditors","November + December purchases","24,000 + 26,000 = 50,000",50000],["Expense creditors","December overheads + December operatives' wages","14,000 + (25% × 16,000) = 18,000",18000]]}},{"stmt":{"t":"Monthly sales values (¢000)","rows":[["","Nov","Dec","Jan","Feb","Mar","Apr","May","Jun","@hd"],["Units",4000,4200,4500,4600,4800,5000,3800,3000],["Price per unit (¢)",12000,12000,12000,12000,12000,12000,16000,16000],["**Sales value**",48000,50400,54000,55200,57600,60000,60800,48000]]}},{"h4":"(a)(i)  Cash budget for the six months ended 30 June"},{"stmt":{"t":"Cash receipts (¢000)","rows":[["","Jan","Feb","Mar","Apr","May","Jun","@hd"],["50% of current month's sales (cash)",27000,27600,28800,30000,30400,24000],["50% of sales two months earlier (credit)",24000,25200,27000,27600,28800,30000],["Issue of shares","—","—","—",40000,"—","—"],["**Total receipts**",51000,52800,55800,97600,59200,54000,"@tt"]]}},{"stmt":{"t":"Cash payments (¢000)","rows":[["","Jan","Feb","Mar","Apr","May","Jun","@hd"],["Purchases (two months in arrears)",24000,26000,28000,36000,32000,28000],["Management salaries (75%, same month)",12000,15000,15000,15000,18000,18000],["Operatives' wages (25%, one month in arrears)",4000,4000,5000,5000,5000,6000],["Overheads (one month in arrears)",14000,14000,14000,14000,16000,16000],["Fixed assets (3 equal instalments from May)","—","—","—","—",20000,20000],["**Total payments**",54000,59000,62000,70000,91000,88000,"@tt"]]}},{"stmt":{"t":"Cash budget summary (¢000)","rows":[["","Jan","Feb","Mar","Apr","May","Jun","@hd"],["Opening balance",6000,3000,"(3,200)","(9,400)",18200,"(13,600)"],["Receipts",51000,52800,55800,97600,59200,54000],["Payments","(54,000)","(59,000)","(62,000)","(70,000)","(91,000)","(88,000)"],["**Closing balance**",3000,"(3,200)","(9,400)",18200,"(13,600)","**(47,600)**","@tt"]]}},{"note":"The **dividend of ¢20,000 declared in April is paid three months later, in July**, and the **third instalment of ¢20,000 on the fixed assets also falls in July** — so neither appears in the cash budget, but both are liabilities at 30 June and both will hit the bank immediately after the budget period ends. That is a point for part (b)."},{"h4":"(a)(ii)  Budgeted profit and loss account"},{"stmt":{"t":"Budgeted Profit and Loss Account for the six months ended 30 June (¢000)","rows":[["Sales  (54,000 + 55,200 + 57,600 + 60,000 + 60,800 + 48,000)","",335600],"@gap",["Opening inventory",50000,""],["Add: purchases  (28,000 + 36,000 + 32,000 + 28,000 + 24,000 + 24,000)",172000,""],["",222000,""],["Less: closing inventory  (Apr + May + Jun purchases = 28,000 + 24,000 + 24,000)",-76000,146000],["**Gross profit**","",189600,"@t"],"@gap",["Less: wages and salaries incurred",124000,""],["          overheads incurred",90000,""],["          depreciation  (10% × ¢280,000 × 6/12)",14000,228000],["**Net loss for the six months**","",-38400,"@tt"],"@gap",["Less: dividend declared","",20000],["**Deficit transferred to income surplus**","",-58400,"@tt"]]}},{"h4":"(a)(iii)  Budgeted balance sheet as at 30 June"},{"stmt":{"t":"Budgeted Balance Sheet as at 30 June (¢000)","rows":[["**Fixed assets**","","","@hd"],["Cost  (280,000 + 60,000)",340000,""],["Less: accumulated depreciation  (28,000 + 14,000)",42000,298000],"@gap",["**Current assets**","","","@hd"],["Inventory",76000,""],["Trade debtors  (50% of May + June sales)",54400,130400],"@gap",["**Current liabilities**","","","@hd"],["Trade creditors  (May + June purchases)",48000,""],["Expense creditors  (June overheads 16,000 + June operatives' wages 6,000)",22000,""],["Creditor for fixed assets (July instalment)",20000,""],["Proposed dividend",20000,""],["Bank overdraft",47600,157600],["**Net current liabilities**","",-27200,"@t"],["**Total net assets**","",270800,"@tt"],"@gap",["**Financed by**","","","@hd"],["Stated capital  (200,000 + 40,000 issued in April)","",240000],["Income surplus  (89,200 − 38,400 loss − 20,000 dividend)","",30800],["","",270800,"@tt"]]}},{"key":"The balance sheet closes exactly at **¢270,800** from both directions, which validates the cash budget, the profit statement and every accrual in between."},{"h4":"(b)  Comment — the points for the budget committee"},{"ol":["**The business is budgeted to make a LOSS of ¢38.4 million** over the six months, despite sales of ¢335.6 million. Gross margin is healthy at 56%, but wages and salaries (¢124 million) and overheads (¢90 million) together consume more than the whole gross profit. This is the single most important finding and should lead the agenda.","**The cash position deteriorates catastrophically** — from ¢6 million in hand at 1 January to a **¢47.6 million overdraft** at 30 June. No overdraft facility is mentioned anywhere in the question; one must be arranged **before** February, when the account first goes overdrawn.","**July is worse than June.** The third fixed-asset instalment of ¢20 million and the ¢20 million dividend both fall in July, so a further ¢40 million leaves the bank immediately after the budget period — on top of an existing ¢47.6 million overdraft.","**Paying a ¢20 million dividend out of a ¢38.4 million loss is indefensible**, and reduces the income surplus from ¢89.2 million to ¢30.8 million. The committee should recommend deferring or cancelling it; doing so would save ¢20 million of cash in July and preserve reserves.","**The ¢60 million capital expenditure is being funded largely by overdraft.** The ¢40 million share issue covers only two-thirds of it, and short-term borrowing is the wrong way to finance a long-term asset. Longer-term finance should be sought.","**The May price increase coincides with a collapse in volume.** Units fall from 5,000 in April to 3,800 in May and 3,000 in June — a 40% drop — just as the price rises from ¢12,000 to ¢16,000. Sales value in June (¢48,000) is back to November's level. The committee must establish whether the volume fall is seasonal or is the market rejecting the new price; if the latter, the increase should be reconsidered.","**Wages and salaries rise 50%** over the period (¢16 million to ¢24 million a month) while output falls, and overheads rise from ¢14 million to ¢16 million. Both need explaining and controlling.","**Inventory rises by 52%**, from ¢50 million (two months' purchases) to ¢76 million (three months'), tying up ¢26 million of cash at exactly the moment the company can least afford it — and doing so while sales volumes are falling. The inventory policy should be reviewed.","**Credit control.** Giving two months' credit on half of all sales while taking two months on purchases leaves the company funding its own inventory. Tightening collection or negotiating longer supplier terms would ease the June position materially."]}]}},{"eg":{"tag":"Exam type — Q5 (15 marks)","t":"Three common budgeting assumptions, and the doubts about each","q":[{"p":"Several assumptions are commonly made by accountants when preparing or interpreting budgetary information. Explain why each of the following three assumptions might be made when designing a system of budgeting, and set out in each case any arguments which raise legitimate doubts about their validity: **(a)** budgeted performance should be reasonably attainable but not too loose *(5 marks)*; **(b)** participation by managers in the budget-setting process leads to better performance *(5 marks)*; **(c)** management by exception is the most effective system of routine reporting *(5 marks)*."}],"a":[{"h4":"(a)  \"Budgeted performance should be reasonably attainable but not too loose\""},{"p":"**Why the assumption is made.** A budget serves as a **target** as well as a plan. A target that is impossible to reach is demotivating — managers stop trying once they conclude they cannot win — while a target that is too easy invites **budgetary slack** and wastes the capacity that could have been used. The accepted position is that the budget should be set at a level that is **demanding but achievable**, which research suggests produces the highest actual performance, and that it should also be realistic enough to serve as a genuine **planning** document for the cash budget and the master budget."},{"p":"**Doubts about its validity:**"},{"ul":["The level that motivates best is **not the same as the level that plans best**. The most motivating target is usually tighter than the most likely outcome, so using one budget for both purposes means one of them is wrong. Some organisations therefore keep two sets of figures — a demanding target budget and a realistic planning budget — at the cost of complexity and confusion.","**\"Reasonably attainable\" is subjective** and differs by individual. The same target that stretches one manager demoralises another, so a single standard applied across departments cannot be optimal for all of them.","**Aspiration levels shift with experience.** A manager who beats the budget raises their own expectations; one who misses it lowers them. A target that was correctly pitched last year may not be this year.","If budgets are used for **performance evaluation and reward**, managers have every incentive to argue for a loose budget during negotiation, whatever the stated policy — so slack creeps in regardless.","An attainable target can **cap performance** as well as raise it: once it is reached, effort may stop, and managers may defer revenue or accelerate costs into the next period to protect next year's budget."]},{"h4":"(b)  \"Participation by managers in the budget-setting process leads to better performance\""},{"p":"**Why the assumption is made.** Participative (bottom-up) budgeting is held to improve performance because the manager who will be held to the budget helps to set it. This is said to increase **goal congruence** and **commitment** — people support what they help create; to use the **local knowledge** of the person closest to the operation, producing more accurate estimates than a remote head office could; to improve **communication** in both directions; and to raise **morale and job satisfaction** by treating managers as professionals rather than as recipients of instructions."},{"p":"**Doubts about its validity:**"},{"ul":["**It invites budgetary slack.** A manager who sets their own target, and is then rewarded for beating it, has an obvious incentive to understate revenue and overstate cost. Participation can therefore make the budget *less* accurate, not more.","**Participation may be pseudo-participation.** If senior management has already decided the answer and the consultation is cosmetic, the effect on motivation is **worse** than imposing the budget openly, because managers feel manipulated.","**It does not suit everyone.** Managers who prefer clear direction, or who lack the skill or confidence to budget, may find participation stressful rather than motivating.","**It is slow and expensive**, requiring many iterations of negotiation and co-ordination across functions.","**It can produce inconsistent and sub-optimal budgets**, each department optimising for itself rather than for the organisation, unless strong central co-ordination is applied — which partly negates the participation.","The **research evidence is mixed**. Participation appears to help where tasks are uncertain and the manager's information is genuinely superior, and to matter much less where operations are routine and head office is equally well informed."]},{"h4":"(c)  \"Management by exception is the most effective system of routine reporting\""},{"def":{"t":"Management by exception","d":"the principle that management attention should be directed only to those items where actual performance differs **significantly** from the budget, items in line with budget being passed over without comment."}},{"p":"**Why the assumption is made.** Management time is the scarcest resource in the organisation. Reporting every figure every period buries the important in the routine; reporting only the significant variances **concentrates attention where action is possible and needed**, reduces the volume of reporting, speeds up response, and fits naturally with responsibility accounting — each manager sees only the exceptions within their own area of control."},{"p":"**Doubts about its validity:**"},{"ul":["**It is backward-looking.** By the time a variance is reported the period is over and the money is spent. Exception reporting controls history; it does not prevent the event.","**Defining \"significant\" is arbitrary.** A fixed percentage or absolute threshold takes no account of whether the variance is controllable, whether it is part of a trend, or whether it is statistically unusual for that cost.","**Small variances that persist in the same direction** every month may matter far more than a single large one, yet each individually falls below the threshold and is never reported. The system is blind to trends.","**It can be demoralising and one-sided**, since managers hear from head office only when something has gone wrong. Good performance passes unremarked, which undermines motivation.","**Favourable variances are often ignored** — yet a large favourable variance may signal a standard that is wrong, quality being cut, or maintenance being deferred, and may be more important than an adverse one.","**It depends entirely on the quality of the budget.** If the budget has not been **flexed** to the actual activity level, the variances are meaningless and exception reporting simply directs attention to the wrong places.","**It ignores non-financial and qualitative factors** — quality, delivery, customer satisfaction, staff morale — which may be deteriorating without producing any financial variance at all."]}]}},{"eg":{"tag":"Exam type — Q6 (14 marks) — ICAG, July 1993","t":"The duties of a budget officer, and the contents of a budget manual","q":[{"p":"**(a)** Outline the duties of a Budget Officer in a large establishment *(6 marks)*. **(b)** Briefly list the main contents of a Budget Manual *(8 marks)*."}],"a":[{"h4":"(a)  Duties of the Budget Officer"},{"p":"The budget officer — usually the management accountant — is the **executive and secretary of the budget committee**, and the person who makes the budgeting system actually work. The duties are:"},{"ol":["**Act as secretary to the budget committee** — convene its meetings, prepare its agenda, record and circulate its decisions.","**Prepare and maintain the budget manual**, and issue it to all those involved in budget preparation.","**Draw up and police the budget timetable**, chasing departmental managers so that each stage is completed on time.","**Issue the policy guidelines and assumptions** agreed by the committee — price levels, wage rates, inflation, activity levels — together with the forms on which budget proposals are to be submitted.","**Advise and assist departmental managers** in preparing their budgets, supplying past data, explaining the techniques, and checking the arithmetic and the reasonableness of what they submit.","**Co-ordinate the functional budgets** into a consistent whole, identifying conflicts and referring them to the committee, and ensuring that all budgets are built on the principal budget factor.","**Assemble the master budget** — the budgeted profit and loss account, balance sheet and cash budget — and present it to the committee and the board for approval.","**Operate the budgetary control reporting system** — collect actual results, compare them with the (flexed) budget, compute and analyse the variances, and distribute the control reports promptly to the responsible managers.","**Investigate significant variances** with the managers concerned, and report to the committee on the action being taken.","**Keep the system under review**, recommending revisions to budgets where circumstances have changed materially, and improvements to the procedures themselves."]},{"h4":"(b)  Main contents of the Budget Manual"},{"ol":["A **statement of the objectives** of the budgetary system, and how it supports the organisation's objectives and long-range plan.","An **organisation chart**, showing the budget centres, who is responsible for each budget, and the lines of authority and reporting.","The **constitution, membership, powers and duties of the budget committee**, and the role of the budget officer.","The **budget timetable**, listing each stage of preparation, review, negotiation and approval, and the date by which each must be completed.","The **account codes and cost-centre codes** to be used, so that budgeted and actual figures are coded identically and can be compared.","**Specimen forms, schedules and reports**, with detailed instructions on how each is to be completed and to whom it is to be sent.","The **key assumptions and policy guidelines** — forecast activity levels, price and wage inflation, exchange rates, capacity constraints.","The **procedures for review, negotiation, approval and revision** of budgets, including who may authorise a revision and in what circumstances.","The **reporting and follow-up procedures** — what control reports are produced, their frequency, their recipients, the definition of a \"significant\" variance, and the action expected.","A **glossary of the budgeting terms** used in the organisation, so that everyone means the same thing by them."]}]}},{"eg":{"tag":"Exam type — Q7 (20 marks)","t":"Five objectives of budgeting, and five budgeting terms explained","q":[{"p":"**(a)** Explain **five** objectives of budgeting *(5 marks)*. **(b)** Briefly explain the following terms as used in budgeting: (i) budget manual; (ii) budget committee; (iii) master budget; (iv) principal budget factor; (v) functional budget *(15 marks)*."}],"a":[{"h4":"(a)  Objectives of budgeting"},{"p":"The study text gives six; any five are required."},{"ol":["**Planning.** Budgeting forces management to look ahead, to set out objectives in quantified form, and to think through how they will be achieved before the period begins, rather than reacting to events as they arise.","**Co-ordination.** It brings the separate functions into line with one another — sales with production, production with purchasing, all of them with cash — so that the organisation acts as a whole rather than as a collection of departments each optimising for itself.","**Communication.** It informs managers of the plans, the policies and the constraints within which they must operate, and communicates upward the resource needs and expectations of each department.","**Motivation.** A budget is a target. Properly set — demanding but attainable — and properly arrived at, with the participation of the manager responsible, it encourages managers to perform in the organisation's interest.","**Control.** It provides the yardstick against which actual results are measured. Comparing actual with (flexed) budget produces variances, which direct attention to where corrective action is needed.","**Performance evaluation.** It provides a basis for assessing the performance of managers and of the units for which they are responsible, and hence for reward."]},{"h4":"(b)  The five terms"},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Term","Explanation"],"rows":[["**(i) Budget manual**","The document setting out the organisation's budgeting procedures — objectives of the system, organisation chart and responsibilities, the budget committee's constitution, the budget timetable, account codes, specimen forms, policy assumptions, procedures for approval and revision, and the reporting and follow-up arrangements. It is the standing instruction book that makes the system consistent from year to year and from department to department."],["**(ii) Budget committee**","The body, normally chaired by a senior executive and comprising the heads of the main functions, that co-ordinates and directs the preparation of budgets. It issues the manual, timetable and policy guidelines, identifies the principal budget factor, receives and reconciles the functional budgets, negotiates with managers, approves the budgets, assembles the master budget, and monitors actual performance against it."],["**(iii) Master budget**","The summary budget that consolidates all the functional budgets into a single statement of the organisation's plan: the **budgeted profit and loss account**, the **budgeted balance sheet** and the **cash budget**. It is what is presented to the board for approval, and it is the document against which overall performance is finally judged."],["**(iv) Principal budget factor**","The factor that **limits the activities of the organisation** and around which all other budgets must be built — also called the limiting, key or governing factor. It is identified **first**, and the budget for it is prepared first, because every other budget has to be constructed in the light of it. It is most often **sales demand**, but may be raw material availability, skilled labour, machine capacity, factory space, cash or management expertise."],["**(v) Functional budget**","A budget prepared for a particular **function** of the business — sales, production, direct materials usage and purchases, direct labour, production overhead, administration, selling and distribution, capital expenditure, research and development. The functional budgets are the detailed building blocks from which the master budget is assembled."]]}}]}},{"eg":{"tag":"Exam type — Q8 (16 marks) — ACCA, December 1998","t":"A product manager — production and purchases budgets, and January's profit and cash","q":[{"p":"A product manager with responsibility for a single product is submitting data to be compiled into budgets for 2022, with performance targets set for sales volume, profitability and a target cash surplus. The agreed budgeted sales for the product for December 2021 to May 2022 are:"},{"stmt":{"t":"Budgeted sales (units)","rows":[["","Dec","Jan","Feb","March","April","May","@hd"],["Units",14000,16000,22000,17000,20000,24000]]}},{"ul":["At each month end the **closing inventory of finished goods should be 25% of the following month's forecast sales**, and the **inventory of raw material should be sufficient for 10% of the following month's production**. Inventory levels currently conform to this policy.","**One unit of raw material makes one unit of finished inventory**; there is no wastage.","**Raw material purchases are paid for during the month following the month of purchase.** All other expenses are paid for as incurred.","All sales are on credit; **50% of sales are received in the month of sale and 50% in the following month**.","The company operates an **absorption costing system computed on a monthly basis** — each month's fixed and variable manufacturing overhead is recovered in product costs using that month's budgeted production and budgeted expenditure. Opening inventory is valued at the unit cost established in the previous month; **at 1 January 2022 finished inventory is to be assumed at ¢40 per unit**. A **FIFO** cost flow is assumed.","Sales are made at a price of **¢58 per unit**."]},{"stmt":{"t":"Estimated costs for budget preparation","rows":[["**Manufacturing costs**","","","@hd"],["Material","","¢10.00 per unit produced"],["Variable overhead and labour","","¢16.00 per unit produced"],["Fixed overhead costs (including depreciation of ¢54,000)","","¢210,000 per month"],"@gap",["**Selling costs**","","","@hd"],["Variable","","¢7.00 per unit sold"],["Fixed","","¢164,000 per month"]]}},{"p":"**Required:** **(a)** compute the monthly budgeted production and material purchases for January to March 2022 *(6 marks)*; **(b)** prepare a budgeted profit and loss account and a statement of cash receipts and payments for January 2022 *(10 marks)*."}],"a":[{"h4":"(a)  Production budget, January to March"},{"p":"Closing finished goods inventory each month is 25% of the **following** month's sales:"},{"stmt":{"t":"Finished goods inventory (units)","rows":[["At 31 December  (25% × January sales 16,000)","",4000],["At 31 January  (25% × February sales 22,000)","",5500],["At 28 February  (25% × March sales 17,000)","",4250],["At 31 March  (25% × April sales 20,000)","",5000],["At 30 April  (25% × May sales 24,000)","",6000]]}},{"stmt":{"t":"(a)  Production budget (units)","rows":[["","January","February","March","April","@hd"],["Sales",16000,22000,17000,20000],["Add: closing finished goods inventory",5500,4250,5000,6000],["",21500,26250,22000,26000,"@t"],["Less: opening finished goods inventory",-4000,-5500,-4250,-5000],["**Budgeted production**",17500,20750,17750,21000,"@tt"]]}},{"h4":"(a)  Material purchases budget, January to March"},{"p":"Raw material inventory is 10% of the **following month's production**, and one unit of material makes one unit of output:"},{"stmt":{"t":"Raw material inventory (units)","rows":[["At 31 December  (10% × January production 17,500)","",1750],["At 31 January  (10% × February production 20,750)","",2075],["At 28 February  (10% × March production 17,750)","",1775],["At 31 March  (10% × April production 21,000)","",2100]]}},{"stmt":{"t":"(a)  Material purchases budget","rows":[["","January","February","March","@hd"],["Material required for production (units)",17500,20750,17750],["Add: closing raw material inventory",2075,1775,2100],["",19575,22525,19850,"@t"],["Less: opening raw material inventory",-1750,-2075,-1775],["**Purchases (units)**",17825,20450,18075,"@t"],["× ¢10 per unit","","",""],["**Purchases (¢)**",178250,204500,180750,"@tt"]]}},{"h4":"(b)  Working — January's absorption cost per unit"},{"stmt":{"t":"Absorption cost per unit, January (¢)","rows":[["Material","",10.0],["Variable overhead and labour","",16.0],["Fixed overhead  (¢210,000 ÷ 17,500 units produced)","",12.0],["**Absorption cost per unit produced in January**","",38.0,"@tt"]]}},{"note":"The fixed overhead rate is recomputed **every month** on that month's budgeted production, which is why opening inventory (¢40, December's rate) and January production (¢38) carry different unit costs — and why the FIFO assumption matters."},{"h4":"(b)  Budgeted profit and loss account for January 2022"},{"stmt":{"t":"Budgeted Profit and Loss Account, January 2022 (¢)","rows":[["Sales  (16,000 units × ¢58)","",928000],"@gap",["**Cost of sales (FIFO)**","","","@hd"],["Opening inventory  (4,000 units × ¢40)",160000,""],["Cost of production  (17,500 units × ¢38)",665000,""],["",825000,""],["Less: closing inventory  (5,500 units × ¢38)",-209000,616000],["**Gross profit**","",312000,"@t"],"@gap",["Less: variable selling costs  (16,000 units × ¢7)",112000,""],["          fixed selling costs",164000,276000],["**Budgeted net profit for January**","",36000,"@tt"]]}},{"note":"Under FIFO the 16,000 units sold comprise the **4,000 units of opening inventory at ¢40** (¢160,000) plus **12,000 units from January production at ¢38** (¢456,000) — a total of ¢616,000, which is what the computation above produces."},{"h4":"(b)  Working — December's material purchases, needed for the cash statement"},{"p":"January pays for **December's** purchases, so December's production and purchases must be computed first:"},{"stmt":{"t":"December production and purchases (units)","rows":[["December sales","",14000],["Add: closing finished goods at 31 December  (25% × January 16,000)","",4000],["Less: opening finished goods at 1 December  (25% × December 14,000)","",-3500],["**December production**","",14500,"@t"],"@gap",["Material required for December production","",14500],["Add: closing raw material at 31 December  (10% × January production)","",1750],["Less: opening raw material at 1 December  (10% × December production 14,500)","",-1450],["**December purchases (units)**","",14800,"@t"],["**December purchases (¢) — payable in January**","",148000,"@tt"]]}},{"h4":"(b)  Statement of cash receipts and payments for January 2022"},{"stmt":{"t":"Statement of cash receipts and payments, January 2022 (¢)","rows":[["**Receipts**","","","@hd"],["50% of January sales  (50% × ¢928,000)",464000,""],["50% of December sales  (50% × 14,000 × ¢58 = 50% × ¢812,000)",406000,870000],"@gap",["**Payments**","","","@hd"],["Raw material purchases — December's, paid in January",148000,""],["Variable overhead and labour  (17,500 units × ¢16)",280000,""],["Fixed manufacturing overhead, excluding depreciation  (¢210,000 − ¢54,000)",156000,""],["Variable selling costs  (16,000 units × ¢7)",112000,""],["Fixed selling costs",164000,860000],"@gap",["**Net cash inflow for January**","",10000,"@tt"]]}},{"warn":"Two traps in the cash statement. **Depreciation of ¢54,000 is excluded** — it is a non-cash charge, so the cash fixed overhead is ¢156,000 not ¢210,000. And January pays for **December's** material purchases of ¢148,000, **not** January's ¢178,250 — which is why December's production had to be computed first even though the question only asks for January onwards."},{"key":"Note how differently the month looks on the two statements: a **profit of ¢36,000** but a **cash inflow of only ¢10,000**. The gap is explained by the ¢54,000 of depreciation added back, less the ¢80,000 increase in working capital (inventory up 1,500 units and the mismatch between January's purchases and December's payment). **Profit is not cash**, and a product manager with a target cash surplus as well as a profit target needs both statements."}]}}]},{"n":"12.20","t":"Study text examination-type questions, worked (Q9–Q17)","b":[{"eg":{"tag":"Exam type — Q9 (20 marks) — ICAG, May 2005","t":"Objectives of a budgetary control system, and four budgeting terms","q":[{"p":"**(a)** Outline **six** objectives of a budgetary control system and explain how a budgetary control system can achieve those objectives *(12 marks)*. **(b)** In the context of budgeting, explain: (i) aspiration levels; (ii) budgetary slack; (iii) responsibility accounting; (iv) zero-based budgeting *(2 marks each)*."}],"a":[{"h4":"(a)  Six objectives, and how the system achieves each"},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Objective","How a budgetary control system achieves it"],"rows":[["**1. Planning**","By compelling managers to quantify their intentions for the coming period before it begins — forecasting sales, scheduling production, specifying materials, labour and overhead, and projecting cash. The exercise forces problems to be anticipated rather than discovered."],["**2. Co-ordination**","By requiring every functional budget to be reconciled with every other through the budget committee, so that the sales budget, production budget, purchasing budget and cash budget are mutually consistent, and all are built on the principal budget factor."],["**3. Communication**","By formally transmitting the plan downward — targets, policy assumptions and constraints — and transmitting resource needs and expectations upward through the budget proposals and negotiation."],["**4. Motivation**","By setting each manager a quantified target that is demanding but attainable, arrived at with their participation, and by linking appraisal and reward to achievement against it."],["**5. Control**","By comparing actual results with the budget **flexed to the actual activity level**, computing the variances, reporting them promptly to the responsible manager, and requiring investigation and corrective action on significant ones."],["**6. Performance evaluation**","By providing an agreed yardstick against which each responsibility centre and its manager can be assessed, restricted to the costs and revenues that manager actually controls."]]}},{"note":"A seventh objective often credited: **authorisation**. An approved budget is the authority for a manager to incur expenditure up to the budgeted amount, which is why budgetary control is the central financial control in public-sector and not-for-profit bodies."},{"h4":"(b)(i)  Aspiration levels"},{"p":"The **aspiration level** is the level of performance a manager personally **hopes and expects to achieve** — their own internal target, as distinct from the target the budget sets. It matters because performance tends to follow the aspiration level rather than the budget: a budget set well above it is dismissed as unattainable and demotivates, while one set below it is simply absorbed and wastes capacity. Aspiration levels **shift with experience** — success raises them, repeated failure lowers them — so the budget that motivated last year may not motivate this year."},{"h4":"(b)(ii)  Budgetary slack"},{"p":"**Budgetary slack** is the deliberate **overstatement of costs or understatement of revenues** by a manager during budget preparation, in order to build in a cushion that makes the budget easier to achieve. It arises wherever managers participate in setting the budgets against which they will later be judged and rewarded. Its effects: the budget becomes an unreliable plan, resources are wasted on padded allowances, favourable variances are reported that reflect the padding rather than performance, and co-ordination suffers because the figures other departments rely on are wrong. It is controlled by independent review of budget submissions, comparison against past data and against other units, and by not rewarding purely on variance from a self-set budget."},{"h4":"(b)(iii)  Responsibility accounting"},{"p":"**Responsibility accounting** is a system of accounting that classifies costs, revenues and investment by **the manager responsible for them**, and reports to each manager only those items that are **within their control**. The organisation is divided into **responsibility centres** — cost centres, revenue centres, profit centres and investment centres — and each manager receives a report of the budgeted and actual figures for their own centre alone. Its governing principle is the **controllability principle**: a manager should be held accountable only for what they can influence. Apportioned head-office overhead, for instance, should be shown separately or excluded, because charging it to a departmental manager who cannot affect it destroys the report's credibility."},{"h4":"(b)(iv)  Zero-based budgeting"},{"p":"**Zero-based budgeting (ZBB)** is a budgeting method in which each budget is built **from a zero base** each period — every activity must be **justified afresh**, as if it were being proposed for the first time, rather than assuming that last year's level of expenditure is automatically carried forward. Managers prepare **decision packages** describing each activity, its purpose, its cost, the benefit of performing it and the consequences of not performing it; the packages are then **ranked** and funded in order until the available resources are exhausted. It is the direct opposite of **incremental budgeting**, which takes last year's figure and adjusts it."}]}},{"eg":{"tag":"Exam type — Q10 (20 marks) — ICAG, May 2004","t":"Reasons for budgets, the budgeting process, budgetary control versus standard costing, and behavioural implications","q":[{"p":"Budgeting is concerned with the implementation of an approved programme within the long-range plan and capital budget. A budget therefore translates the long-range plan and capital budget into an annual operating plan of an organisation. The budgeting process cannot be viewed as purely concerned with the current year, but must be considered as an integrated part of the long-term planning process."},{"p":"**Required:** **(a)** identify **four** major reasons for producing budgets and **two** possible limitations of budgets *(6 marks)*; **(b)** identify the stages involved in the budgeting process *(6 marks)*; **(c)** distinguish between budgetary control and standard costing *(4 marks)*; **(d)** identify **four** areas which should be of concern to management in budget behavioural implications *(4 marks)*."}],"a":[{"h4":"(a)  Four major reasons for producing budgets"},{"ol":["**To plan** — to compel management to look ahead, quantify objectives and anticipate problems before they arise.","**To co-ordinate** the activities of the different functions, so that sales, production, purchasing and finance are consistent with one another and with the principal budget factor.","**To communicate** plans, policies, targets and constraints throughout the organisation, and to communicate resource needs upward.","**To control** — to provide the yardstick against which actual performance is measured, so that variances can be identified and corrective action taken.","**To motivate** managers by giving them a quantified, agreed target to work towards.","**To evaluate performance** and provide a basis for reward, and to **authorise** expenditure up to the approved level."]},{"h4":"(a)  Two possible limitations of budgets"},{"ol":["**They can induce dysfunctional behaviour.** Managers may build in budgetary slack, spend the full budget near the year end simply to protect next year's allocation, or meet their own targets at the expense of the organisation as a whole.","**They rest on forecasts that may be wrong.** A budget built on inaccurate assumptions about demand, prices or capacity becomes a misleading yardstick, and variances then measure forecasting error rather than performance.","**They can be inflexible.** An annual budget may be overtaken by events within weeks, and a rigid budget discourages managers from taking advantage of opportunities that arise after it is set.","**They are time-consuming and costly** to prepare, negotiate and administer, particularly under participative or zero-based approaches.","**They tend to perpetuate the past** where incremental budgeting is used, carrying forward last year's inefficiencies into this year's allowance.","**They focus on financial measures** and may ignore quality, delivery, customer satisfaction and other non-financial dimensions of performance."]},{"h4":"(b)  Stages involved in the budgeting process"},{"steps":["**Communicate the details of the budget policy and guidelines** to those responsible for preparing budgets — the long-range plan, the assumptions on prices, wages and activity, and the budget timetable, normally through the budget manual.","**Identify the principal budget factor** — the constraint that limits activity — and determine that the budget for it is prepared first.","**Prepare the budget for the principal budget factor**, usually the sales budget.","**Prepare the other functional budgets** in the light of it — production, materials usage and purchases, direct labour, overhead, administration, selling and distribution, and capital expenditure.","**Negotiate the budgets with superiors**, working up the organisation level by level, until each is agreed by both the manager responsible and their superior.","**Co-ordinate and review the budgets** centrally, checking that they are consistent with one another, feasible, and in line with the long-range plan; revise where they are not.","**Prepare the master budget** — the budgeted profit and loss account, balance sheet and cash budget — and submit it for **final acceptance** by the budget committee and the board.","**Communicate the approved budgets** to all responsible managers, so that each knows the plan they are to execute.","**Monitor actual results against budget**, report variances promptly, investigate the significant ones and take corrective action.","**Review and revise.** Continuously reappraise the budget in the light of actual events, revising it where circumstances have changed materially, and feed the experience into the next budget cycle."]},{"h4":"(c)  Budgetary control distinguished from standard costing"},{"table":{"align":"lll","head":["","Budgetary control","Standard costing"],"rows":[["**Scope**","Applies to the **whole organisation** and every function — production, selling, administration, research, capital expenditure, cash.","Applies mainly to **production and manufacturing operations**, where repetitive activity allows a standard to be set."],["**Unit of measurement**","Deals in **totals** for a department or function for a period.","Deals in **per-unit** standards for material, labour and overhead."],["**Basis**","Built on **forecast** expenditure and revenue for a period, often from historical levels adjusted for expected change.","Built on **technically determined standards** — standard quantities and standard prices, established by work study and specification."],["**What is compared**","Actual results against the **budget for the period**.","Actual costs against the **standard cost of the actual output**."],["**Variance detail**","Variances are generally at **cost-heading level** — total material, total labour, total overhead.","Variances are analysed into **causes** — price and usage, rate and efficiency, expenditure and volume (Chapter 13)."],["**Possible without the other?**","**Yes** — budgetary control can operate without standard costs.","**Standard costing presupposes budgets**, and is best regarded as a refinement operating within a budgetary control system."]]}},{"note":"The two are **complementary, not alternative**. Budgetary control tells you *that* a department overspent; standard costing tells you *why* — whether the material cost more per kilo or more kilos were used. In practice a manufacturing company operates both, with the standard cost card feeding the production cost budget."},{"h4":"(d)  Four areas of behavioural concern in budgeting"},{"ol":["**Participation in budget setting.** Whether managers help set their own budgets affects commitment and accuracy — but genuine participation invites budgetary slack, and pseudo-participation is worse than imposition.","**The level of difficulty of the target.** A budget that is too tight demotivates and is abandoned; one that is too loose creates slack and wastes capacity. The aspiration level of the individual manager, not the organisation's ambition, determines what actually motivates.","**Budgetary slack.** The deliberate padding of costs and understating of revenues, which undermines the budget both as a plan and as a control.","**Goal congruence and dysfunctional behaviour.** Managers pursuing their own budget targets may act against the interests of the organisation — refusing a profitable order because it would breach a cost budget, spending the remaining budget at the year end to protect next year's allocation, or cutting maintenance and training to hit a short-term number.","**The controllability principle.** Holding a manager responsible for costs they cannot influence destroys the credibility of the whole reporting system.","**The style of use.** Whether the budget is used **punitively** (as a stick in appraisal) or **supportively** (as a planning and problem-solving aid) strongly affects the behaviour it produces — including the honesty of the data managers supply."]}]}},{"eg":{"tag":"Exam type — Q11 (20 marks) — ICAG, May 1999","t":"Cost control versus cost reduction, and the techniques and difficulties of cost reduction","q":[{"p":"As a Management Accountant, you are required to: **(a)** distinguish between cost control and cost reduction; **(b)** explain the **two** basic approaches to cost reduction; **(c)** itemise and briefly explain the techniques, methods and major difficulties of cost reduction programmes."}],"a":[{"note":"This question belongs to Chapter 15 (Cost Reduction and Control) but is printed among the budgeting questions, presumably because budgetary control is one of the cost-control techniques. It is answered in full here, and §15.2 covers the same ground."},{"h4":"(a)  Cost control distinguished from cost reduction"},{"table":{"align":"lll","head":["","Cost control","Cost reduction"],"rows":[["**Aim**","To keep costs **within a predetermined target** — a standard or budget.","To achieve a **real and permanent reduction** in unit cost, below the existing target."],["**Reference point**","A standard or budget that has already been set.","The existing level of cost itself, which is challenged."],["**Nature**","**Preventive and corrective** — monitor, compare, investigate, act.","**Innovative and investigative** — question the method, the design and the specification themselves."],["**Assumption about the present method**","Assumes current standards and methods are correct.","Assumes nothing; the present method is the thing under examination."],["**Duration**","A **continuous**, period-by-period routine.","Normally a **project** or programme; once achieved, the saving is **permanent**."],["**Effect on the standard**","The standard is **maintained**.","The standard is **revised downwards**."],["**Quality and utility**","Not in question.","Must be achieved **without reducing** the quality or utility of the product."]]}},{"h4":"(b)  The two basic approaches to cost reduction"},{"ol":["**Increasing productivity from existing resources** — producing more output from the same input of materials, labour, machinery and capital, so that fixed costs and overheads are spread over more units and the cost per unit falls. Achieved through better methods, improved layout and material flow, training, incentive schemes, improved scheduling, and reduction of idle time, scrap and rework.","**Reducing the expenditure itself** — obtaining the same output from a lower input: cheaper materials or substitutes of equal quality, better purchasing terms, simplifying or standardising the product, eliminating unnecessary operations and features, and mechanising where capital is cheaper than labour."]},{"h4":"(c)  Techniques and methods of cost reduction"},{"ol":["**Value analysis** — the systematic examination of an **existing** product to identify and remove cost that adds no value to the customer, without reducing the product's function, quality or utility.","**Value engineering** — the same discipline applied at the **design stage**, before the product is made, which is where most of the cost is actually committed.","**Work study** — **method study** (finding the best way of doing a job) and **work measurement** (establishing how long it should take), together producing improved methods and sound standard times.","**Organisation and Methods (O&M)** — the equivalent discipline applied to **administrative and clerical** procedures: forms, records, office layout and information flow.","**Standardisation and simplification** — reducing the variety of materials, components and product variants, so as to lengthen runs, cut set-ups and reduce inventory.","**Variety reduction** of the product range itself, concentrating on the lines that earn the contribution.","**Automation and improved technology**, where the capital cost is justified by the saving.","**Operational research techniques** — linear programming, inventory models, queuing and simulation — applied to scheduling, stock levels and capacity.","**Improved purchasing and inventory control** — economic order quantities, supplier negotiation, just-in-time (Chapter 14).","**Quality control and total quality management**, attacking the cost of scrap, rework, inspection and warranty claims.","**Improved budgetary control and standard costing**, which identify where cost is running above plan and so direct the reduction effort.","**Cost audit** — an independent verification that costs are being incurred properly and economically (§15.7)."]},{"h4":"(c)  Major difficulties of cost reduction programmes"},{"ol":["**Resistance from employees and unions**, who may see the programme as a prelude to redundancy, a speed-up, or an attack on established working practices.","**Resistance from management**, particularly where the programme implies that past decisions on design, method or purchasing were wrong.","**The risk of reducing quality.** Cost reduction must not reduce the utility of the product; a \"saving\" that produces more returns, warranty claims and lost customers is not a saving at all.","**Cost reduction may be a false economy.** Cutting maintenance, training, research or advertising reduces this year's cost and damages next year's business.","**The programme itself costs money** — consultants, work study staff, retraining, new equipment — and the savings must be weighed against it.","**Short-termism.** Programmes are often launched under financial pressure and abandoned when the pressure eases, so the reductions are temporary rather than permanent.","**Difficulty of measuring the saving**, and of distinguishing genuine reduction from the effects of volume, price changes or deferred expenditure.","**Lack of top management support**, without which no cost reduction programme survives, and lack of a clear responsibility for delivering it.","**Inadequate cost information** to identify where the cost actually is, and to prove that a proposed change would reduce it."]}]}},{"eg":{"tag":"Exam type — Q12 (15 marks) — ICAG, July 1993","t":"Ozone Ltd — the basis of traditional budgetary control, Maslow, and zero-based budgeting","q":[{"p":"The Managing Director of Ozone Ltd recently attended a seminar on budgetary control systems, where he gained the impression that the introduction of such a system offered opportunities not only to improve control of the company's operations but also to improve staff motivation. He did not fully understand some of the points made and has approached you, the management accountant, for a clearer explanation."},{"p":"**Required:** provide concise explanations to the following queries. **(a)** Traditional budgetary systems are based on certain basic mechanisms for achieving control. What is the traditional basis for achieving control? *(3 marks)* **(b)** Many early management theorists believed employees were motivated solely by financial rewards. Writers such as Maslow have suggested that in certain circumstances the offer of additional financial rewards may not provide much improvement in motivation. Why? *(2 marks)* **(c)** It has been suggested that zero-based budgeting can be particularly effective in improving control and staff motivation. (i) What is meant by the term zero-based budgeting? (ii) What are its advantages over traditional budgeting systems, and how can it be used to help improve managers' motivation? *(10 marks)*"}],"a":[{"h4":"(a)  The traditional basis for achieving control"},{"p":"Traditional budgetary control works through a **feedback control loop**, with five elements:"},{"steps":["**Set a plan** — the budget, expressing the intended performance of each responsibility centre in quantified terms.","**Measure actual performance** against the same classification and coding as the budget.","**Compare actual with plan**, with the budget **flexed** to the activity level actually achieved, to produce **variances**.","**Report the variances** promptly to the manager responsible for them, applying **management by exception** — only significant differences are brought to attention.","**Take corrective action** on the causes, or — where the variance shows the plan itself was wrong — **revise the plan**. The outcome feeds back into the next period's comparison, closing the loop."]},{"p":"Underpinning the loop are two further principles: **responsibility accounting** (each manager is reported on, and held accountable for, only what they control) and **the budget as authority** (approved budgets authorise expenditure up to the budgeted level)."},{"h4":"(b)  Why Maslow suggests more money may not motivate"},{"p":"Maslow's **hierarchy of needs** holds that human needs arise in an order — **physiological** (food, shelter), **safety and security**, **social** (belonging, acceptance), **esteem** (status, recognition, achievement) and **self-actualisation** (realising one's potential) — and that **a need that has already been substantially satisfied ceases to motivate**."},{"p":"Money satisfies mainly the **lower-order** needs. For an employee whose physiological and security needs are already met by their existing salary, more money adds little, because the needs now active are the **higher-order** ones — recognition, responsibility, achievement, personal growth — which money cannot directly buy. Offering such a person more pay instead of more responsibility, involvement or recognition therefore produces little or no improvement in motivation."},{"note":"The practical implication for Ozone Ltd: **participation in the budget-setting process is itself a motivator**, because it satisfies esteem and self-actualisation needs — it confers recognition, responsibility and a degree of autonomy. That is precisely the point the seminar was making, and it is why the answer to (c)(ii) below turns on involvement rather than on reward."},{"h4":"(c)(i)  What zero-based budgeting means"},{"def":{"t":"Zero-based budgeting (ZBB)","d":"a method of budgeting in which each budget is prepared **from a zero base** — every activity must be justified afresh each period as if it were being proposed for the first time, rather than last year's expenditure being taken as the starting point."}},{"p":"It proceeds in three steps:"},{"ol":["**Define decision units** — the discrete activities or programmes whose cost is to be justified.","**Prepare decision packages** for each: what the activity is, why it is needed, what it costs, what benefits it produces, what would happen if it were not done at all, and what alternative ways and levels of performing it exist. **Mutually exclusive** packages describe alternative ways of doing the activity; **incremental** packages describe successive levels of service above a minimum.","**Rank the packages** in order of benefit across the organisation, and fund them in rank order until the available resources are exhausted. Anything below the cut-off is not funded."]},{"h4":"(c)(ii)  Advantages over traditional (incremental) budgeting"},{"ol":["**It eliminates inherited inefficiency.** Incremental budgeting carries forward last year's waste automatically; ZBB requires every naira to be justified, so activities that have outlived their usefulness are identified and stopped.","**It challenges the assumption that current activities should continue** at all, and in their present form.","**It focuses on value for money**, forcing each activity to demonstrate the benefit it produces for its cost.","**It allocates scarce resources rationally**, by ranking competing claims against one another rather than funding each in isolation.","**It identifies and removes duplicated effort** between departments, which the ranking process exposes.","**It is particularly effective for discretionary and support costs** — research, training, advertising, administration, and public-sector services — where there is no direct relationship between input and output and incremental budgeting has nothing to anchor to.","**It improves the quality of information** available to senior management about what the organisation actually does and what it costs."]},{"h4":"(c)(ii)  How ZBB can improve managers' motivation"},{"ol":["**It requires genuine participation.** Managers must prepare and defend their own decision packages, which gives them real involvement in, and ownership of, the budget they will be held to.","**It satisfies higher-order needs** in Maslow's sense — recognition, responsibility, achievement and the exercise of judgement — which, as (b) explains, is where motivation now lies for managers whose financial needs are already met.","**It gives managers a voice and a hearing.** The ranking process requires senior management to listen to the case for each activity, so managers feel their work is understood and valued rather than simply trimmed by a percentage.","**It increases understanding of the organisation as a whole**, since managers see their own activity ranked alongside others, which builds goal congruence.","**It is perceived as fairer** than across-the-board incremental cuts, which penalise the efficient manager who had no slack to give up and reward the one who did.","**It develops managers** — preparing a decision package demands analytical and presentational skills and a clear understanding of what their function is for."]},{"warn":"Be even-handed in the answer. ZBB is **very time-consuming and expensive**, generates large volumes of paperwork, requires skills many managers do not have, and the ranking of dissimilar activities is inevitably subjective. It can also **damage** motivation if managers feel their work is constantly under threat, or if the exercise is repeated so often that it becomes a ritual. Most organisations therefore apply it **selectively** — to discretionary costs, and on a rolling basis covering a different area each year — rather than to the whole budget every year."}]}},{"eg":{"tag":"Exam type — Q13 (20 marks) — ICAG, January 1992","t":"The elements of control, and the safeguards that keep a control system effective","q":[{"p":"The concept of control occupies an important place in systems theory and is a major part of the functions of the management accountant. **Required:** **(a)** list the basic elements of control and discuss how these are implemented in an effective control system; **(b)** describe the safeguards which must be incorporated in systems design to ensure the continued effectiveness of a control system."}],"a":[{"h4":"(a)  The basic elements of control"},{"p":"A control system, in systems theory, has **five** essential elements, which together form a closed feedback loop:"},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Element","How it is implemented in practice"],"rows":[["**1. An objective or standard** — a statement of what is to be achieved.","The **budget** and the **standard cost card**. Objectives must be quantified, specific, attainable and communicated to the person responsible, or there is nothing to control against."],["**2. A sensor or measuring device** — a means of measuring what is actually happening.","The **cost accounting and recording system** — materials requisitions, time sheets, production returns, the sales ledger. The measurement must be accurate, timely and classified on the **same basis as the standard**, or comparison is meaningless."],["**3. A comparator** — a means of comparing actual with standard.","**Variance analysis**, with the budget **flexed** to the activity level actually achieved. The comparison must isolate causes, not merely report a difference."],["**4. A feedback and reporting mechanism** — communication of the difference to someone who can act.","**Control reports** issued promptly to the manager responsible, on a **responsibility accounting** basis and applying **management by exception**, so that attention goes to the significant and controllable."],["**5. An effector** — the capacity and authority to take corrective action.","The **manager**, with the authority to change inputs or methods — and, where the variance shows the plan itself was wrong, the authority to **revise the standard**. Without this element the system reports but does not control."]]}},{"note":"A sixth element is often added: **feed-forward control**, which compares a **forecast** outcome with the objective and acts **before** the event rather than after it. The cash budget is the classic example — it predicts the April overdraft while there is still time to arrange the facility. Feedback control corrects history; feed-forward control prevents it."},{"h4":"(b)  Safeguards to ensure continued effectiveness"},{"ol":["**Keep the standards and budgets up to date.** A control system measured against an obsolete standard reports the staleness of the standard, not performance. Build in periodic review and formal revision procedures.","**Ensure the controllability principle is observed** — report to each manager only what they can influence, and separate apportioned and uncontrollable costs clearly. Nothing destroys a control system faster than holding people responsible for what they cannot affect.","**Report promptly.** Information that arrives after the opportunity to act has passed has no control value. Fix the reporting timetable and police it.","**Set a sound definition of \"significant\"** for exception reporting — based on materiality, controllability, trend and statistical significance, not a flat percentage — and review it.","**Design the system so that it cannot be manipulated.** Segregate the recording of results from the person being measured; verify source data independently; use continuous inventory taking and physical verification to prove the book figures.","**Guard against budgetary slack and dysfunctional behaviour** — review budget submissions independently, compare between units and against past data, and avoid reward systems that make it profitable to be measured against a self-set target.","**Build in feed-forward as well as feedback**, so that the system warns of problems in advance rather than only reporting them afterwards.","**Include non-financial measures** — quality, delivery, scrap, labour turnover, customer satisfaction — so the system cannot be satisfied by financial results achieved at the expense of the business.","**Ensure action actually follows.** A reported variance that no one investigates teaches managers that the system can be ignored. Require a documented response to every significant variance, and follow it up.","**Review the control system itself periodically** — its costs, its relevance, whether it is still measuring what matters, and whether the benefits exceed the cost of operating it.","**Secure and maintain top management support**, and train the managers who operate the system so that they understand what the reports mean and what is expected of them."]}]}},{"eg":{"tag":"Exam type — Q14 (20 marks)","t":"Planning, Programming and Budgeting Systems (PPBS)","q":[{"p":"**(a)** Explain the concept of Planning, Programming and Budgeting Systems as used in budgetary control *(8 marks)*. **(b)** Outline **three** advantages and **three** disadvantages of Planning, Programming and Budgeting Systems as a system of budgeting *(12 marks)*."}],"a":[{"h4":"(a)  The concept of PPBS"},{"def":{"t":"Planning, Programming and Budgeting System (PPBS)","d":"a budgeting system that allocates resources to **programmes and objectives** rather than to departments and inputs — asking \"what are we trying to achieve, what alternative programmes would achieve it, and what does each cost?\" rather than \"how much did this department spend last year?\""}},{"p":"Developed in the United States Department of Defense in the early 1960s and widely adopted in the public sector, PPBS is **output-oriented** and **long-term**. Its defining feature is that the budget is built around **programmes that cut across departmental boundaries**: a \"road safety\" programme, for example, might draw on the police, the highways department, the health service and education, and PPBS budgets the whole programme as one, rather than budgeting each department separately and losing sight of the objective."},{"p":"Its three elements give it its name:"},{"ol":["**Planning** — define the organisation's **objectives** and the ends to be achieved, over a horizon of several years, not one.","**Programming** — identify the alternative **programmes** of activity capable of achieving each objective, evaluate their costs and benefits (often by cost-benefit analysis), and select the most effective.","**Budgeting** — translate the selected programmes into an **annual budget** of the resources each requires, allocated to the programme rather than to the department."]},{"p":"The procedure in practice: establish objectives; identify programmes to achieve them; cost each programme over its whole life; evaluate alternatives against measurable output; select and rank; allocate resources; and monitor achievement **against the objectives**, not merely against the spending limits."},{"note":"PPBS shares its philosophy with **zero-based budgeting** — both are output-oriented, both require alternatives to be evaluated, and both refuse to take last year's spending as given. The difference is that ZBB works **bottom-up** from decision packages within existing units, while PPBS works **top-down** from corporate objectives and cuts across the organisation structure."},{"h4":"(b)  Three advantages"},{"ol":["**It links expenditure to objectives and outputs.** Resources are allocated to what the organisation is trying to achieve rather than to departments and inputs, so it can be seen what each objective actually costs and whether it is worth the cost — which conventional line-item budgeting cannot show at all.","**It takes a long-term, strategic view.** Programmes are costed over their full life rather than one year at a time, which exposes the future commitment implied by a decision taken today and prevents the short-termism of annual budgeting.","**It forces alternatives to be evaluated.** Each objective must be pursued by the most cost-effective of several identified programmes, which improves the quality of decisions and makes the reasoning explicit and open to challenge.","**It cuts across departmental boundaries**, revealing duplication and gaps between departments that a departmental budget conceals, and improving co-ordination between them."]},{"h4":"(b)  Three disadvantages"},{"ol":["**It is extremely costly and time-consuming**, requiring large amounts of data, specialist analytical skills and administrative effort — which is why most of the organisations that adopted it in the 1960s had abandoned it within a decade.","**Outputs and benefits are very difficult to measure**, particularly in the public sector and for social objectives. What is the measurable output of a policing programme, or of primary education? Without measurable output the cost-benefit comparison at the heart of the system cannot be made objectively.","**It conflicts with the existing organisation structure.** Programmes cut across departments, but accountability, authority and the accounting records all follow departmental lines — so it is unclear who is responsible for a programme, and the system generates resistance from departmental heads who see their budgets dispersed.","**Objectives are often multiple, vague and conflicting**, especially in government, and the ranking of dissimilar programmes against one another is inescapably subjective and politically contested.","**It is poorly suited to routine operations.** For repetitive manufacturing, where standard costing and conventional budgetary control work well, PPBS adds cost without adding information."]}]}},{"eg":{"tag":"Exam type — Q15 (18 marks) — ACCA, June 1998","t":"A rolling budget — cost behaviour analysis and three flexed profit statements","q":[{"p":"A company operates a system of **quarterly rolling budgets**. The budgets for the next three quarters have been prepared. The figures reflect the likely cost behaviour of each element of cost. Quarter 4 is being developed from these budgets and other information available."},{"stmt":{"t":"Budget — quarters 1 to 3","rows":[["","Q1","Q2","Q3","@hd"],["**Activity**","","","","@hd"],["Sales (units, 000)",18,34,30],["Production (units, 000)",20,40,30],"@gap",["**Costs (¢000)**","","","","@hd"],["Direct materials cost",50,100,75],["Production labour",180,280,230],["Factory overheads (excluding indirect labour)",170,200,185],["Administration",30,30,30],["Selling and distribution",29,37,35]]}},{"p":"Flexible budgets are to be developed for **low, most likely and high sales volumes (38,000, 44,000 and 50,000 units respectively)**. The company wishes to have a **closing inventory at the end of quarter four equal to the opening inventory of quarter one**, and will adjust production levels accordingly. Cost structures as for quarters one to three will apply to quarter four except that:"},{"ol":["Raw material prices are expected to **rise by 10%**.","Production labour rates will **increase by 2.5%**; however, management has declared that all labour rate increases must be matched by increased efficiency, so that labour costs (both total fixed and variable per unit) are **unaltered**.","A quarterly **bonus payment of 50% of the variable labour cost per unit** will apply for all production **above 40,000 units**.","Fixed factory overheads and fixed selling and distribution expenses will **rise by 5%**."]},{"p":"The expected selling price per unit is **¢18**. Inventory is valued at a full factory cost of **¢13 per unit**, established using absorption principles on long-run cost and capacity predictions; small fluctuations in cost prices or volumes will not cause this unit cost to be amended."},{"p":"**Required:** **(a)** explain what is meant by a rolling budget and what additional benefits may be claimed for it compared with the annual type of budget *(4 marks)*; **(b)** summarise the variable cost per unit and the total fixed cost for each cost heading that will apply to quarter four, for production below 40,000 units *(6 marks)*; **(c)** prepare detailed flexed budget profit statements for quarter four under the separate assumptions of low, most likely and high levels of sales and corresponding production volumes *(8 marks)*."}],"a":[{"h4":"(a)  What a rolling budget is, and its benefits"},{"def":{"t":"Rolling (continuous) budget","d":"a budget that is **continuously updated** by adding a further accounting period (a month or a quarter) as each period expires, so that a budget covering a constant period ahead — typically twelve months — is always in force."}},{"p":"Here, as quarter 1 ends a budget for quarter 5 is prepared, so four quarters are always budgeted. The budget for each period is **reviewed and revised** in the light of what has actually happened, rather than being fixed once a year."},{"p":"**Additional benefits over the annual budget:**"},{"ol":["**The budget is always realistic and up to date.** Under an annual budget, the figures for month eleven were set fourteen months earlier on assumptions long since overtaken; a rolling budget is never more than one period stale.","**Planning horizon is constant.** Management always has a full year ahead in view, instead of a horizon that shrinks to one month by the end of the year.","**Variances are meaningful**, because they measure performance against a current expectation rather than against an obsolete forecast — so control improves.","**It spreads the budgeting effort evenly** across the year instead of concentrating it into one exhausting annual exercise.","**It forces regular reassessment** of plans and assumptions, which is valuable in rapidly changing or uncertain conditions.","**Better motivation**, because managers are measured against targets they recognise as achievable in current conditions."]},{"p":"**Against:** it is more time-consuming and costly, it can cause confusion where targets change frequently, and continual revision may let poor performance be budgeted away rather than corrected."},{"h4":"(b)  Cost behaviour analysis, and the quarter 4 cost structure"},{"p":"Each cost heading is analysed by the high-low method (§2.1.2a). Note carefully **which activity measure drives each cost** — materials, labour and factory overhead vary with **production**, while selling and distribution varies with **sales**."},{"stmt":{"t":"Analysis of quarters 1 to 3 (¢000, units 000)","rows":[["Cost heading","Driver","High-low working","Variable per unit (¢)","Fixed per quarter (¢000)","@hd"],["Direct materials","Production","50 ÷ 20 = 100 ÷ 40 = 75 ÷ 30",2.5,0],["Production labour","Production","(280−180) ÷ (40−20) = 5;  280 − (40×5) = 80",5.0,80],["Factory overheads","Production","(200−170) ÷ (40−20) = 1.5;  200 − (40×1.5) = 140",1.5,140],["Administration","—","Constant at 30 in all three quarters",0,30],["Selling and distribution","Sales","(37−29) ÷ (34−18) = 0.5;  37 − (34×0.5) = 20",0.5,20]]}},{"note":"Each analysis is confirmed against the **third** quarter, which was not used to derive it: materials $30 \\times 2.5 = 75$ ✓; labour $30 \\times 5 + 80 = 230$ ✓; factory overhead $30 \\times 1.5 + 140 = 185$ ✓; selling and distribution $30 \\times 0.5 + 20 = 35$ ✓. Always check the analysis on the observation you did not use."},{"stmt":{"t":"(b)  Quarter 4 cost structure, for production below 40,000 units","rows":[["Cost heading","Variable cost per unit (¢)","Total fixed cost per quarter (¢000)","Adjustment applied","@hd"],["Direct materials",2.75,0,"Price +10%:  2.50 × 1.10"],["Production labour",5.0,80,"Rate +2.5% **offset by efficiency** — unaltered"],["Factory overheads",1.5,147,"Fixed +5%:  140 × 1.05"],["Administration",0,30,"No change"],["Selling and distribution",0.5,21,"Fixed +5%:  20 × 1.05"],["**Total**","**¢9.25 per unit produced + ¢0.50 per unit sold**","**¢278**","","@tt"]]}},{"note":"Above 40,000 units of production a **bonus of 50% of the variable labour cost per unit** applies — i.e. an extra ¢2.50 per unit on production above 40,000 only, which affects the high-volume column at (c)."},{"h4":"(c)  Determining the quarter 4 production volumes"},{"p":"Over quarters 1 to 3 production exceeded sales by $(20-18) + (40-34) + (30-30) = 8$ thousand units, so inventory rose by 8,000 units. For closing inventory at the end of quarter 4 to equal the opening inventory of quarter 1, **production in quarter 4 must be 8,000 units below sales**:"},{"stmt":{"t":"Quarter 4 production volumes (units 000)","rows":[["","Low","Most likely","High","@hd"],["Sales",38,44,50],["Less: required reduction in inventory",-8,-8,-8],["**Production**",30,36,42,"@tt"]]}},{"h4":"(c)  Flexed budget profit statements for quarter four"},{"stmt":{"t":"Flexed budget profit statements, quarter 4 (¢000)","rows":[["","Low  (sales 38, production 30)","Most likely  (sales 44, production 36)","High  (sales 50, production 42)","@hd"],["**Sales**  (units × ¢18)",684.0,792.0,900.0],"@gap",["**Factory cost of production**","","","","@hd"],["Direct materials  (production × ¢2.75)",82.5,99.0,115.5],["Production labour  (production × ¢5.00 + ¢80 fixed)",230.0,260.0,290.0],["Labour bonus  (production above 40 × ¢2.50)",0.0,0.0,5.0],["Factory overheads  (production × ¢1.50 + ¢147 fixed)",192.0,201.0,210.0],["**Cost of production**",504.5,560.0,620.5,"@t"],["Add: reduction in inventory  (8,000 units × ¢13)",104.0,104.0,104.0],["**Cost of sales**",608.5,664.0,724.5,"@t"],"@gap",["**Gross profit**",75.5,128.0,175.5,"@t"],["Less: administration",30.0,30.0,30.0],["Less: selling and distribution  (sales × ¢0.50 + fixed ¢21)",40.0,43.0,46.0],["**Net profit for quarter 4**",5.5,55.0,99.5,"@tt"]]}},{"note":"Inventory is valued throughout at the standard **¢13 per unit**, and production is 8,000 units below sales in every column, so the same **¢104,000** of inventory value is released into cost of sales under each assumption. Because the ¢13 is a long-run standard that is **not** to be amended for fluctuations, the actual cost of production goes into the statement at actual and the inventory movement at standard — exactly as the question directs."},{"key":"The three columns show the company's exposure vividly. A **12,000-unit spread in sales (38 to 50, about 32%) swings profit from ¢5,500 to ¢99,500 — a factor of eighteen.** At the low volume the quarter is barely profitable. That is high operating gearing, and it is exactly what a flexed budget is for: not to predict one number, but to show management how much the answer depends on an assumption nobody can control."}]}},{"eg":{"tag":"Exam type — Q16 (20 marks) — ACCA, June 1999","t":"Four purposes of budgets, and the behavioural factors in budgeting","q":[{"p":"For many organisations in both the private and public sectors, the annual budget is the basis of much internal management information. When preparing and using budgets, however, management and the accountant must be aware of their behavioural implications."},{"p":"**Required:** **(a)** briefly discuss **four** purposes of budgets *(8 marks)*; **(b)** explain the behavioural factors which should be borne in mind, and the difficulties of applying them, in the process of budgeting and budgetary control *(12 marks)*."}],"a":[{"h4":"(a)  Four purposes of budgets"},{"ol":["**Planning.** The budget compels management to look forward, to quantify its intentions and to anticipate problems before the period begins. The discipline of producing the numbers is often more valuable than the numbers themselves, because it forces assumptions into the open and exposes inconsistencies between what different parts of the organisation expect.","**Co-ordination and communication.** The budget reconciles the plans of every function into one consistent whole, built around the principal budget factor, and communicates that plan — together with the policies, targets and constraints behind it — to everyone who must execute it. It also communicates **upward**: the budget proposals tell senior management what each department believes it needs and can achieve.","**Control and performance evaluation.** The budget is the yardstick against which actual results are measured. Flexed to the activity level achieved, it produces the variances that direct management attention, and it provides an agreed basis for assessing each responsibility centre and its manager.","**Motivation and authorisation.** A quantified, agreed target encourages managers to perform, particularly where they participated in setting it; and an approved budget is the formal **authority** to incur expenditure up to the budgeted level — which in the public sector is its primary function."]},{"h4":"(b)  Behavioural factors, and the difficulties of applying them"},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Behavioural factor to bear in mind","The difficulty in applying it"],"rows":[["**Participation.** Involving managers in setting their own budgets increases commitment, uses their local knowledge, and improves morale.","It **invites budgetary slack**, since the manager who sets the target is the one judged against it. It is slow and costly. And **pseudo-participation** — consultation where the answer is already decided — is worse than open imposition, because managers feel manipulated."],["**The level of difficulty of the target.** Performance is highest when the target is demanding but attainable.","The level that **motivates** best is tighter than the level that **plans** best, so one budget cannot serve both purposes well. \"Attainable\" is also individual — the same target stretches one manager and demoralises another."],["**Aspiration levels.** Managers perform to their own internal expectations, which shift with success and failure.","Aspiration levels are **unobservable** and change over time, so a target correctly pitched this year may not be next year, and no single target suits a whole department."],["**Goal congruence.** Budgets should be designed so that a manager pursuing their own target also advances the organisation's interest.","In practice budgets routinely produce **dysfunctional behaviour** — spending the remaining budget before the year end to protect next year's allocation, refusing profitable work that would breach a cost budget, cutting maintenance and training to hit a short-term number, or empire-building."],["**The controllability principle.** Managers should be held accountable only for what they can influence.","Separating controllable from uncontrollable cost is **difficult and contentious** — apportioned overhead, transfer prices and shared services all blur the boundary — and controllability is a matter of degree and of time horizon, not a clean split."],["**The style of use.** A budget used supportively, as a planning and problem-solving aid, produces different behaviour from one used punitively in appraisal.","Where budgets drive pay and promotion, a **budget-constrained** style emerges whatever the stated policy: managers manipulate the figures, suppress bad news, and distrust the system. Changing the style means changing the reward system, which is far harder than changing the budget."],["**Feedback.** Prompt, specific, fair feedback on performance improves subsequent performance.","Reports are often **late**, aggregated beyond usefulness, or — under management by exception — delivered only when something has gone wrong, so managers hear from head office only with criticism."],["**Group and organisational factors** — team norms, peer pressure and the organisation's culture strongly influence whether budgets are taken seriously.","These are **outside the accountant's control** and cannot be designed into a system; a well-constructed budget in a cynical organisation will simply be gamed."]]}},{"key":"The general difficulty underlying all of these is that budgeting asks **one document to do several jobs at once** — plan, co-ordinate, motivate, control, evaluate and authorise — and the design that best serves one purpose is rarely the design that best serves another. The accountant's task is not to eliminate the tension but to be conscious of it, and to be explicit about which purpose is being given priority in any given case."}]}},{"eg":{"tag":"Exam type — Q17","t":"Lumco — fixed budget limitations, and flexible budgets at 60% and 80% capacity","q":[{"p":"**(a)** Write briefly on the **fixed budget** and provide **two** of its limitations."},{"p":"**(b)** Lumco, for the first time, is introducing a flexible budget to improve its control system. Budgets for two operational levels are given as follows:"},{"stmt":{"t":"Flexible budget for August 2022","rows":[["Production capacity","40%","50%","@hd"],["","80,000 units (N)","100,000 units (N)"],["Direct material and labour costs",800000,1000000],["Production overhead",520000,600000],["Administrative overhead",400000,400000],["Selling overhead",280000,300000]]}},{"p":"**Profit is fixed at 40% mark-up.** **Required:** prepare the flexible budgets for **60%** and **80%** production capacity, if the company intends still to realise a profit mark-up of 40%."}],"a":[{"h4":"(a)  The fixed budget, and two limitations"},{"def":{"t":"Fixed budget","d":"a budget prepared for a **single, predetermined level of activity**, which is **not adjusted** if the actual level of activity turns out to be different."}},{"p":"It is the budget originally set at the start of the period, and it serves a real purpose: it is the master plan against which the business committed itself, the basis on which cash and capacity were arranged, and the figure against which overall corporate performance is finally judged."},{"p":"**Limitations:**"},{"ol":["**It is useless for cost control when activity differs from budget.** Comparing the actual cost of making 50,000 units with the budgeted cost of making 40,000 units produces a meaningless \"variance\" — most of the difference is simply the extra volume, not inefficiency. Only a **flexed** budget isolates the controllable element.","**It takes no account of cost behaviour.** Because it never distinguishes how each cost responds to activity, it cannot tell management what the cost *should* have been at the level actually achieved.","**It becomes obsolete quickly** in uncertain conditions, since a single assumed activity level is almost certainly wrong, and it gives no information about what would happen at any other level.","**It can mislead decision-making** — a manager who beats a fixed cost budget simply because sales fell may appear efficient, while one who overspends because of a welcome surge in demand appears wasteful."]},{"h4":"(b)  Step 1 — analyse each cost into its fixed and variable elements"},{"p":"Compare the two given levels. 40% capacity is 80,000 units and 50% is 100,000, so **100% capacity is 200,000 units**, and 60% and 80% are 120,000 and 160,000 units respectively."},{"stmt":{"t":"High-low analysis of the two given levels","rows":[["Cost heading","At 80,000 (N)","At 100,000 (N)","Change (N)","Variable per unit (N)","Fixed (N)","@hd"],["Direct material and labour",800000,1000000,200000,10.0,0],["Production overhead",520000,600000,80000,4.0,200000],["Administrative overhead",400000,400000,0,0.0,400000],["Selling overhead",280000,300000,20000,1.0,200000],["**Total**",2000000,2300000,300000,15.0,800000,"@tt"]]}},{"note":"Workings: direct material and labour is **purely variable** — $800{,}000 ÷ 80{,}000 = 1{,}000{,}000 ÷ 100{,}000 = N10$ per unit, with no fixed element. Production overhead is **semi-variable** — $(600{,}000 − 520{,}000) ÷ 20{,}000 = N4$ per unit, and $600{,}000 − (100{,}000 × 4) = N200{,}000$ fixed. Administrative overhead is **purely fixed** at N400,000. Selling overhead is semi-variable — $20{,}000 ÷ 20{,}000 = N1$ per unit and $300{,}000 − 100{,}000 = N200{,}000$ fixed."},{"h4":"(b)  Step 2 — the flexible budgets"},{"stmt":{"t":"Flexible budget for August 2022 (N)","rows":[["Production capacity","40%","50%","**60%**","**80%**","@hd"],["Units",80000,100000,120000,160000],"@gap",["Direct material and labour  (N10 per unit)",800000,1000000,1200000,1600000],["Production overhead  (N4 per unit + N200,000)",520000,600000,680000,840000],["Administrative overhead  (fixed)",400000,400000,400000,400000],["Selling overhead  (N1 per unit + N200,000)",280000,300000,320000,360000],["**Total cost**",2000000,2300000,2600000,3200000,"@t"],["Add: profit at 40% mark-up on cost",800000,920000,1040000,1280000],["**Sales value**",2800000,3220000,3640000,4480000,"@tt"],"@gap",["*Cost per unit (N)*","25.00","23.00","21.67","20.00"],["*Selling price per unit (N)*","35.00","32.20","30.33","28.00"]]}},{"key":"The point the flexed budget makes for Lumco: **total cost rises but cost per unit falls steadily** — from N25.00 at 40% capacity to N20.00 at 80% — because the N800,000 of fixed cost is spread over more units. If the 40% mark-up is applied to cost, the **selling price must fall as volume rises**, from N35.00 to N28.00. Management should note the consequence: a single list price cannot be consistent with a constant mark-up across different activity levels, so either the price or the mark-up policy has to give."},{"note":"Note that **profit is fixed at a 40% mark-up on cost**, not a 40% margin on sales. At 60% capacity the mark-up of N1,040,000 is 40% of the N2,600,000 cost, but only $1{,}040{,}000 ÷ 3{,}640{,}000 = 28.6\\%$ of the sales value. Read the words carefully."}]}}]}],"formulas":[{"lb":"Production budget","tex":"\\text{Sales} + \\text{Closing inventory} - \\text{Opening inventory}"},{"lb":"Materials purchase budget","tex":"\\text{Usage} + \\text{Closing inventory} - \\text{Opening inventory} + \\text{Loss provision}"},{"lb":"High-low variable rate","tex":"\\dfrac{\\text{Cost}_{\\text{high}} - \\text{Cost}_{\\text{low}}}{\\text{Activity}_{\\text{high}} - \\text{Activity}_{\\text{low}}}"},{"lb":"Fixed cost component (high-low)","tex":"\\text{Total cost}_{\\text{high}} - (\\text{Variable rate} \\times \\text{Activity}_{\\text{high}})"}],"focus":"Budgeting questions are almost always multi-part, chaining a sales budget into production, materials usage, materials purchase, and sometimes labour and cash budgets — get the chain's *logic* right (sell → produce → use → buy, each adjusted for planned inventory change) and the arithmetic follows. For cash budgets, the discipline that matters most is timing: map every receipt/payment to the *actual* month it moves cash, not the month the underlying sale or purchase happened. For flexible budgeting, the high-low method is applied per cost *element*, not to the total cost column — some elements may turn out fully fixed or fully variable once tested.","errors":["Confusing a forecast (a prediction) with a budget (an accepted target) — they answer different questions and the chapter draws this distinction deliberately.","Including depreciation, or any other non-cash item, in a cash budget.","Applying a sales-month's figures directly as a cash-budget receipt, instead of shifting them by the actual credit period before they appear as cash.","Testing the high-low method only on the total-cost row, instead of on each individual cost element — a chapter's own worked example can have some elements fully fixed, others fully variable, and only one genuinely mixed.","Treating zero-based budgeting as literally starting every budget line at zero from scratch, rather than the practical top-down \"what if this were cut\" approach the chapter actually describes."],"quiz":{"mcq":[{"q":"A company plans to sell 5,000 units, wants closing inventory of 800 units, and has opening inventory of 600 units. The production budget (units) is","o":["5,000","5,200","4,800","6,400","5,800"],"a":1,"w":"Production = Sales + Closing inventory − Opening inventory = 5,000 + 800 − 600 = 5,200 units.","calc":"5{,}000 + 800 - 600 = 5{,}200","src":"Chapter 12.5","sec":"12.5"},{"q":"The function of an organisation with the binding (limiting) budget factor should be budgeted","o":["last, after every other functional budget","at the same time as all others","first, since every other functional budget is derived from it","only if it is the sales function","never — limiting factors are ignored in budgeting"],"a":2,"w":"The function with the principal (limiting) budget factor is always budgeted first — every other functional budget then has to be built around that constraint.","src":"Chapter 12.2","sec":"12.2"},{"q":"A forecast becomes a budget when","o":["it is first drafted","management accepts it as the objective","the financial year ends","it is reviewed by external auditors","sales exceed the forecast"],"a":1,"w":"A forecast is a prediction; it only becomes a budget once management formally accepts it as the target to work toward.","src":"Chapter 12.2","sec":"12.2"},{"q":"Which of the following would appear in a cash budget?","o":["Depreciation on machinery","A provision for doubtful debts","Cash received from debtors","An unrealised revaluation gain","An accrued expense not yet paid"],"a":2,"w":"Cash budgets record actual cash movements only — cash received from debtors is a real inflow; depreciation, provisions and accruals involve no cash movement and are excluded.","src":"Chapter 12.6","sec":"12.6"},{"q":"A cost is ¢40,000 at 60% activity and ¢52,000 at 80% activity. Using the high-low method, the variable cost per 1% of activity is","o":["¢200","¢600","¢650","¢1,200","¢12,000"],"a":1,"w":"Variable rate = (52,000 − 40,000) / (80 − 60) = 12,000 / 20 = ¢600 per 1% of activity.","calc":"\\dfrac{52{,}000-40{,}000}{80-60} = 600","src":"Chapter 12.9","sec":"12.9"},{"q":"In Zero-Based Budgeting, a document that identifies and describes a specific activity so management can evaluate and rank it against other activities is called a","o":["budget manual","decision package","variance report","master budget","flexible budget"],"a":1,"w":"A decision package describes one decision unit's activities in enough detail for management to evaluate and rank it — the core building block of ZBB.","src":"Chapter 12.10","sec":"12.10"},{"q":"Which of the following is a genuine disadvantage of Zero-Based Budgeting?","o":["It never requires any paperwork","It always increases every budget line automatically","It creates a large, cumbersome volume of extra paperwork from decision packages","It ignores cost behaviour entirely","It cannot be combined with Activity-Based Costing"],"a":2,"w":"ZBB is explicitly criticised for the enormous volume of extra paperwork created by preparing decision packages for every activity.","src":"Chapter 12.10","sec":"12.10"},{"q":"Incremental budgeting, compared with Zero-Based Budgeting, is","o":["more likely to challenge and remove inefficient past spending","based on justifying every activity from a zero base each period","based on adjusting the previous period's budget, so it can perpetuate past inefficiencies","always more time-consuming to prepare","the only method that uses cost drivers"],"a":2,"w":"Incremental budgeting starts from last period's figures and adjusts them — simple, but a stated demerit is that it can carry forward past inefficiencies unchallenged, unlike ZBB.","src":"Chapter 12.10","sec":"12.10"}],"theory":[{"q":"Write brief notes on: (i) the Budget Manual, (ii) the Budget Committee.","marks":4,"a":[{"p":"**(i) Budget Manual** — a document setting out the budget guidelines and instructions, guiding every departmental head through preparing their own budget in line with organisational goals, and specifying the technique for estimating income and expenditure, the committee's membership, the preparation procedure and the budget timetable."},{"p":"**(ii) Budget Committee** — senior management members (typically the CEO, heads of strategic business units and the chief finance officer) who oversee all budgetary matters: setting/approving budget goals, coordinating preparation, approving the final budget, monitoring the year, reviewing results, and approving major in-year revisions."}],"src":"Chapter 12.4","sec":"12.4"},{"q":"Distinguish between Functional Budgets and Master Budgets.","marks":6,"a":[{"p":"A **functional budget** covers one function or operation of the organisation — e.g. the sales budget, production budget, or a materials purchase budget. The function with the binding (limiting) budget factor is budgeted first, and every other functional budget is derived from it."},{"p":"The **master budget** summarises **all** the functional and policy budgets into one coordinated set of projected financial statements: a budgeted income statement (or income & expenditure account), a budgeted position statement (balance sheet), and a budgeted cash flow statement — the functional budgets are the inputs; the master budget is the combined output."}],"src":"Chapter 12.2","sec":"12.2"}]}},{"n":13,"t":"Standard Costing Techniques","brief":"Standard costing as a control mechanism: the four types of standards, the process of setting them for materials/labour/overheads/selling price, the standard cost card, and the full variance-analysis toolkit — material, labour, overhead, and sales margin variances — culminating in a full reconciliation of budgeted profit to actual profit, and the activity/capacity/efficiency ratios. Every illustration independently verified against its own raw data.","outcomes":["Explain the concept of standards","Explain types of standards","Explain the uses of standard cost, its merits and limitations","Explain methods of determining standard cost","Compute a standard cost card","Compute basic variances, including sales, materials, labour and overheads","Compute standard ratios, including activity, capacity and efficiency","Analyse the causes of variances"],"secs":[{"n":"13.1","t":"Standards and standard costing","b":[{"p":"Setting standards — as a planning basis and a performance yardstick — makes budgeting easier and lets the control process compare standard against actual to find variances, investigate them, and take corrective action."},{"def":{"t":"Standards (as a concept)","d":"control mechanisms establishing predetermined estimates of the cost of products/services, compared against actual costs as incurred to find variances, which are then analysed into their components and investigated."}},{"h4":"Four types of standards"},{"table":{"align":"lll","head":["Type","Meaning","Used for"],"rows":[["**Basic**","Long-term, unchanged over several years — a base from which current standards can be developed.","Showing long-term trends (material prices, labour rates, efficiency, the long-term effect of changed methods); as a basis for setting current standards. **Cannot** highlight current efficiency/inefficiency, and does not normally feature in reporting except as statistical background."],["**Ideal**","Attainable only under the **most favourable** conditions — no breakdowns, no wastage, no stoppages, no idle time, no spoilage, no shrinkage.","Long-term targets; long-term development purposes; investigative purposes."],["**Attainable**","Achievable if a standard unit of work is done efficiently, a machine properly run, material properly used — allows for normal losses/breakdowns. Represents reasonably-met future performance.","Routine control (tough-but-realistic targets, or motivation); stock valuation (product costing, cost control). Should be revised periodically for new conditions/prices/methods/technology."],["**Current**","Set for a **short** period, reflecting current conditions — same as attainable when conditions are stable, but set specifically to handle a temporary problem otherwise.","A temporary material-quality problem; an unexpected inflationary price rise; an energy crisis; standards with subjective elements."]]}},{"key":"Two important points: the **type** of standard used directly affects the size and meaning of any variance computed against it — and every standard, whichever type, contains some **subjective** element."},{"h4":"Standard cost — definition, uses, merits, limitations"},{"def":{"t":"Standard cost","d":"a predetermined calculation of how much cost *should* be, under specified working conditions — expressed in monetary terms and built up from the value of each cost element. Used for control by exception reporting, stock valuation, and fixing selling prices."}},{"warn":"Standard cost is **not** an average of past costs — past cost can embed past mistakes/inefficiencies, and comparing standard against past cost is usually inappropriate given likely changes in method, technology and prices."},{"p":"Uses of standard costing: assessing performance/efficiency; cost control (setting standards, analysing variances, finding reasons for them); management by exception; easing budget preparation; stock/WIP valuation, profit planning and decision making, and pricing policy (especially cost-plus pricing); and motivating staff."},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Merits","Limitations"],"rows":[["Assesses performance/efficiency; enhances cost control by prompting re-appraisal of methods/materials/technique; eases budgeting; enables management by exception; guides performance improvement; aids estimation/forecasting; motivates (with real participation); simplifies stock valuation and pricing; assigns responsibility for off-standard performance.","Its whole philosophy is sometimes challenged as inappropriate; only really works within a budgetary-system framework; variance analysis only *points at* a problem, it does not solve it (that's a management task); every standard involves subjective forecasting; the process can be too complex for most line managers; variance analysis is essentially a post-mortem on past events; can be expensive/time-consuming to install; standards can go out of date fast in volatile conditions; and the usefulness of some variances is genuinely questionable."]]}}]},{"n":"13.2","t":"The process of standard costing, and setting standards","b":[{"p":"Five steps: (a) set the standard cost, built up from each cost element; (b) measure actual cost, per element and in total; (c) compare standard vs actual — the difference is a **variance**, either **favourable** (better than planned) or **adverse/unfavourable** (worse than planned); (d) analyse the variance into its smallest components; (e) investigate the real causes, for material/significant variances only."},{"key":"Standard costing gives the most value where production is highly **repetitive** — mass production or repetitive assembly work."},{"h4":"Setting each element's standard"},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Element","How the standard is set"],"rows":[["**Materials**","Materials content per product is found via engineering study (raw materials, sub-assemblies, piece parts, finishing materials); quantities allow for normal loss (machining loss, evaporation, expected breakage/rejection); prices come from purchasing/procurement, using **forecast/expected** prices (not past costs), reflecting price trends, purchasing-policy changes, quantity/cash discounts, and carriage/packing."],["**Labour**","Specify the exact labour grade (via work-study/measurement techniques); determine the **standard hour** — the quantity of work achievable at standard performance in one hour/minute; personnel forecasts the relevant wage rate for the control period; standard labour cost = standard hours × standard wage rate."],["**Overheads**","The predetermined overhead absorption rate itself becomes the standard rate, using budgeted standard labour hours as the activity base."],["**Selling price**","A top-management decision, weighing anticipated market demand, competing products, inflation estimates, and elasticity of demand."]]}},{"fbox":{"h":"Overhead absorption rates as standards","rows":[{"lb":"Variable Overhead Rate (VOR)","tex":"VOR = \\dfrac{\\text{Budgeted variable overhead}}{\\text{Budgeted standard labour hours}}"},{"lb":"Fixed Overhead Rate (FOAR)","tex":"FOAR = \\dfrac{\\text{Budgeted fixed overhead}}{\\text{Budgeted standard labour hours}}"}]}},{"def":{"t":"Standard sales margin","d":"the difference between standard selling price and standard cost — or, under standard **marginal** costing, standard selling price less standard **marginal** cost, giving the standard contribution margin instead."}}]},{"n":"13.3","t":"The standard cost card","b":[{"p":"Once every standard is set, they are summarised in one place:"},{"table":{"align":"lr","head":["Standard cost card","N"],"rows":[["Standard direct materials cost per unit","xx"],["Standard direct labour cost per unit","xx"],["Standard direct expenses per unit","xx"],["Standard overhead cost per unit","xx"],["**Standard cost per unit**","xx"],["Standard profit margin","xx"],["**Standard selling price**","xx"]]}},{"key":"The standard cost card is the single reference point everything else in this chapter is built from — budgets, and every variance, trace straight back to it."}]},{"n":"13.4","t":"Material, labour and overhead cost variances","b":[{"def":{"t":"Variance","d":"the difference between the standard level of performance that ought to have been achieved, and the actual level achieved."}},{"eg":{"tag":"Study text","t":"Illustration 13.1 — the shared dataset for material, labour and overhead variances","open":true,"q":[{"p":"Ghartex Ltd, budgeted production 120,000 metres of Kente: Material A 0.5kg/metre @¢1,200/kg; Material B 0.5kg/metre @¢1,600/kg; direct labour 30 minutes/metre @¢6,000/hour; budgeted variable overhead ¢30 million; budgeted fixed overhead ¢15 million. Overhead is absorbed on direct labour hours. Actual: production 130,000 metres; Material A 80,000kg purchased/used costing ¢88 million; Material B 70,000kg costing ¢98 million; 80,000 direct labour hours costing ¢400 million; variable overhead ¢28 million; fixed overhead ¢18 million."}],"a":[{"p":"Standard quantities/hours for the **actual** 130,000-metre output: Material A and B each $130{,}000 \\times 0.5 = 65{,}000$kg; labour $130{,}000 \\times 0.5 = 65{,}000$ hours. Worked through the sub-sections below."}]}},{"h4":"Material cost variance (MCV)"},{"fbox":{"h":"Material variance formulas","rows":[{"lb":"Material Cost Variance","tex":"(\\text{SQ for actual output} \\times SP) - (\\text{AQ used} \\times AP)"},{"lb":"Material Price Variance","tex":"AQ_{\\text{purchased}} \\times (SP - AP)"},{"lb":"Material Usage Variance","tex":"SP \\times (\\text{SQ for actual output} - AQ)"}]}},{"p":"Possible causes of a **price** variance: paying more/less than planned (purchasing efficiency/inefficiency, market changes, emergency purchases from poor stock control, different discounts, quality differences, or substitute materials)."},{"p":"Possible causes of a **usage** variance: careful or careless material handling, quality differences, pilferage, actual vs normal scrap, changed quality-control measures, or gains/losses from substitute materials."},{"table":{"align":"lrrr","head":["","Material A","Material B","Total"],"rows":[["Cost variance: (65,000×1,200)−88,000,000; (65,000×1,600)−98,000,000","10,000,000 A","6,000,000 F","**4,000,000 A**"],["Price variance: (80,000×1,200)−88,000,000; (70,000×1,600)−98,000,000","8,000,000 F","14,000,000 F","**22,000,000 F**"],["Usage variance: 1,200×(65,000−80,000); 1,600×(65,000−70,000)","18,000,000 A","8,000,000 A","**26,000,000 A**"]]}},{"key":"Check: Price variance + Usage variance = Cost variance, for every material and in total — 22,000,000 F − 26,000,000 A = 4,000,000 A, exactly matching. This identity always holds and is the fastest way to catch an arithmetic slip."},{"h4":"Labour cost variance"},{"fbox":{"h":"Labour variance formulas","rows":[{"lb":"Labour Total Cost Variance","tex":"(SH \\times SR) - (AH \\times AR)"},{"lb":"Labour Rate Variance","tex":"AH \\times (SR - AR)"},{"lb":"Labour Efficiency Variance","tex":"SR \\times (SH - AH)"}]}},{"p":"Rate-variance causes: a higher/lower rate than planned (wage awards, wrong labour grade used, unplanned overtime/bonus). Efficiency-variance causes: wrong labour grade, poor workshop organisation/supervision, wrong materials/machines. A separate **idle time variance** = idle hours × standard rate, from causes like machine breakdown, power outages, work stoppages, or poor staff coordination."},{"table":{"align":"lr","head":["",""],"rows":[["Total cost variance: (65,000×6,000)−400,000,000","10,000,000 A"],["Rate variance: 80,000×(6,000−5,000) — actual rate = 400,000,000÷80,000 = ¢5,000/hr","80,000,000 F"],["Efficiency variance: 6,000×(65,000−80,000)","90,000,000 A"]]}},{"key":"Check: 80,000,000 F − 90,000,000 A = 10,000,000 A = total, confirmed."},{"h4":"Total Overhead Cost Variance (TOCV)"},{"p":"Standard Total Overhead Rate (STOR) $= \\dfrac{30{,}000{,}000+15{,}000{,}000}{60{,}000\\text{ budgeted hrs}} = ¢750$/hr."},{"tex":"TOCV = (65{,}000 \\times 750) - (28{,}000{,}000 + 18{,}000{,}000) = 48{,}750{,}000 - 46{,}000{,}000 = ¢2{,}750{,}000\\text{ F}"},{"h4":"Variable Overhead Cost Variance (VOCV)"},{"p":"Variable Overhead Rate (VOR) $= 30{,}000{,}000 / 60{,}000 = ¢500$/hr."},{"fbox":{"h":"Variable overhead sub-variances","rows":[{"lb":"Variable Overhead Cost Variance","tex":"(SH \\times VOR) - \\text{Actual VOH}"},{"lb":"Expenditure Variance","tex":"(AH \\times VOR) - \\text{Actual VOH}"},{"lb":"Efficiency Variance","tex":"(SH - AH) \\times VOR"}]}},{"table":{"align":"lr","head":["",""],"rows":[["VOCV: (65,000×500)−28,000,000","4,500,000 F"],["Expenditure: (80,000×500)−28,000,000","12,000,000 F"],["Efficiency: (65,000−80,000)×500","7,500,000 A"]]}},{"key":"Check: 12,000,000 F − 7,500,000 A = 4,500,000 F, confirmed."},{"h4":"Fixed Overhead Variance"},{"p":"FOAR $= 15{,}000{,}000 / 60{,}000 = ¢250$/hr."},{"fbox":{"h":"Fixed overhead sub-variances","rows":[{"lb":"Fixed Overhead Total Variance","tex":"(SH \\times FOAR) - \\text{Actual FOH}"},{"lb":"Expenditure Variance","tex":"\\text{Budgeted FOH} - \\text{Actual FOH}"},{"lb":"Volume Variance","tex":"(SH - \\text{Budgeted hours}) \\times FOAR"}]}},{"table":{"align":"lr","head":["",""],"rows":[["Total: (65,000×250)−18,000,000","1,750,000 A"],["Expenditure: 15,000,000−18,000,000","3,000,000 A"],["Volume: (65,000−60,000)×250","1,250,000 F"]]}},{"key":"Check: 3,000,000 A − 1,250,000 F = 1,750,000 A, confirmed. And overall, TOCV = VOCV + Fixed OH total = 4,500,000 F + 1,750,000 A = **2,750,000 F**, exactly matching TOCV computed directly above."},{"p":"Volume variance itself splits further into two sub-variances:"},{"fbox":{"h":"Fixed overhead volume sub-variances","rows":[{"lb":"Volume Efficiency Variance","tex":"(SH - AH) \\times FOAR"},{"lb":"Volume Capacity Variance","tex":"(AH - \\text{Budgeted hours}) \\times FOAR"}]}},{"note":"CIMA defines the fixed overhead volume variance as measuring the **over- or under-absorption of overhead caused by actual output differing from budgeted** — it is purely a physical-volume story, not a spending story (that's what expenditure variance is for)."},{"eg":{"tag":"Study text","t":"Illustration 13.2 — the same variances, a second dataset","open":true,"q":[{"p":"Lagos Ltd, product J standard cost card: materials 5kg@¢1,000 = ¢5,000; labour 1hr@¢9,000; variable overhead ¢1,000/hr; fixed overhead ¢5,000/hr (from budgeted fixed overhead ¢19,000,000 ÷ 3,800 budgeted units/hours over a 50-week year). In the first 4 weeks, 3,000 units were produced: materials 16,000kg@¢1,100 = ¢17,600,000; labour cost ¢27,000,000; variable overhead, 2,800hrs actual costing ¢2,520,000 (¢900/hr); fixed overhead ¢16,000,000 actual. Compute the material, variable overhead and fixed overhead variances."}],"a":[{"table":{"align":"lr","head":["Material variances",""],"rows":[["Total: (3,000×5,000)−17,600,000","2,600,000 A"],["Price: (1,100−1,000)×16,000","1,600,000 A"],["Usage: (16,000−15,000)×1,000","1,000,000 A"]]}},{"table":{"align":"lr","head":["Variable overhead variances",""],"rows":[["Total: (3,000×1,000)−2,520,000","480,000 F"],["Expenditure: (2,800×1,000)−2,520,000","280,000 F"],["Efficiency: (3,000−2,800)×1,000","200,000 F"]]}},{"table":{"align":"lr","head":["Fixed overhead variances",""],"rows":[["Total: (3,000×5,000)−16,000,000","1,000,000 A"],["Expenditure: 19,000,000−16,000,000","3,000,000 F"],["Volume: (3,000−3,800)×5,000","4,000,000 A"],["— Volume efficiency: (3,000−2,800)×5,000","1,000,000 F"],["— Volume capacity: (2,800−3,800)×5,000","5,000,000 A"]]}},{"note":"This question compares 4 weeks' *actual* output (3,000 units) against the *full annual* budgeted output (3,800 units) when finding the fixed overhead volume variance — every check (price+usage=total; expenditure+volume=total; efficiency+capacity=volume) still reconciles exactly on this basis, confirming it is how the question is deliberately set up, not an oversight to correct."}]}},{"eg":{"tag":"Study text","t":"Illustration 13.3 — variable and fixed overhead variances from a flexible budget (high-low method)","open":true,"q":[{"p":"A flexible budget gives 90,000 standard labour hours → ¢7,000,000 overhead, and 100,000 hours → ¢7,500,000 — the increase is entirely variable expense. Actual: output in standard hours 80,000; clock hours worked 90,000; actual variable overhead ¢4,800,000; actual fixed overhead ¢2,600,000. Compute the variable and fixed overhead variances."}],"a":[{"p":"Variable rate $= (7{,}500{,}000-7{,}000{,}000)/(100{,}000-90{,}000) = ¢50$/hr. At 100,000 hrs, variable overhead $= 100{,}000 \\times 50 = ¢5{,}000{,}000$, so fixed overhead $= 7{,}500{,}000 - 5{,}000{,}000 = ¢2{,}500{,}000$ (budgeted). $FOAR = 2{,}500{,}000/100{,}000 = ¢25$/hr."},{"table":{"align":"lr","head":["Variable overhead variances",""],"rows":[["Total: (80,000×50)−4,800,000","800,000 A"],["Expenditure: (90,000×50)−4,800,000","300,000 A"],["Efficiency: (80,000−90,000)×50","500,000 A"]]}},{"table":{"align":"lr","head":["Fixed overhead variances",""],"rows":[["Total: (80,000×25)−2,600,000","600,000 A"],["Expenditure: 2,500,000−2,600,000","100,000 A"],["Volume: (100,000−80,000)×25","500,000 A"],["— Volume efficiency: (80,000−90,000)×25","250,000 A"],["— Volume capacity: (90,000−100,000)×25","250,000 A"]]}}]}}]},{"n":"13.5","t":"Sales margin variances","b":[{"p":"Sales variances are measured in **profit or contribution margin**, not price — since the sales function's real objective is influencing profit, and cost variances (above) already cover the cost side."},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Variance","Meaning"],"rows":[["**Total sales margin variance**","Actual margin vs. budgeted margin (standard profit/contribution margin basis) — splits into a price variance and a volume variance."],["**Sales margin price variance**","Actual margin per unit vs. standard margin per unit, for the **actual** volume sold."],["**Sales margin volume variance**","Actual sales volume vs. budgeted sales volume, at the **standard** margin per unit."]]}},{"key":"Two computation approaches: the **marginal costing approach** (evaluated on **contribution** margin — selling price less standard marginal/variable cost) and the **absorption costing approach** (evaluated on full **profit** margin — selling price less standard *total* cost, including fixed overhead). Whichever approach is used, standard cost is always what values both the actual and the standard margin — only the selling price/quantity varies between \"actual\" and \"standard\" lines."},{"eg":{"tag":"Study text","t":"Illustration 13.4 — sales quantity, price and total margin variances, three products","open":true,"q":[{"p":"Dzifa Manufacturing, budgeted sales mix ('000 units): Q 200, R 300, S 500 — total 1,000. Standard profit margin/unit: Q ¢10,000, R ¢6,000, S ¢4,000. Actual ('000 units): Q 150, R 350, S 600. Actual profit margin/unit: Q ¢12,000, R ¢6,000, S ¢3,000. Find the sales quantity variance, sales margin price variance, and sales margin total variance."}],"a":[{"table":{"align":"lrrr","head":["Product","Sales quantity variance","Sales margin price variance","Sales margin total variance"],"rows":[["Q — (150−200)×10; (12−10)×150; (150×12)−(200×10)","500,000 A","300,000 F","200,000 A"],["R — (350−300)×6; (6−6)×350; (350×6)−(300×6)","300,000 F","Nil","300,000 F"],["S — (600−500)×4; (3−4)×600; (600×3)−(500×4)","400,000 F","600,000 A","200,000 A"],["**Total**","**200,000 F**","**300,000 A**","**100,000 A**"]]}},{"warn":"The study text's own printed total for the Sales Margin Total Variance shows \"100,000 F\" — but summing its own three product-level figures (200,000 A + 300,000 F + 200,000 A) gives **100,000 A**, not F, and this is confirmed independently by the identity Total Variance = Sales Quantity Variance + Sales Margin Price Variance = 200,000 F + 300,000 A = 100,000 A. The sign shown above is corrected accordingly."}]}},{"h4":"Reconciling budgeted profit to actual profit"},{"p":"Starting from budgeted profit, every variance is added (if favourable) or subtracted (if adverse) until actual profit is reached."},{"key":"Two rules of thumb: (1) all cost variances are based on the **actual** level of production achieved, not the budgeted level; and (2) under **marginal costing**, only the fixed overhead **expenditure** variance features (fixed overhead volume variance has no meaning when fixed cost is a period cost, not absorbed into units); under **absorption costing**, fixed overhead and *all* its sub-variances (expenditure, volume, and volume's own efficiency/capacity split) are included."},{"eg":{"tag":"Study text","t":"Illustration 13.5 — a full reconciliation, marginal and absorption approaches","open":true,"q":[{"p":"ABC/Ayedin Ltd, product Alpha, standard cost card: materials 2kg A@¢1 + 1kg B@¢3 = ¢5.00; labour 3hrs@¢3 = ¢9.00; variable overhead 3hrs@¢2 = ¢6.00; fixed overhead 3hrs@¢4 = ¢12.00; total standard cost ¢32.00; standard profit margin ¢8.00; standard selling price ¢40.00. Budgeted: 10,000 units, 30,000 direct labour hours, budgeted fixed overhead ¢120,000 (FOAR ¢4/hr), budgeted profit ¢80,000. Actual: 9,000 units sold @¢42; materials, labour and overhead costs as detailed in the source; actual profit ¢69,620. Reconcile budgeted to actual profit."}],"a":[{"p":"The individual variances (independently verified, and cross-checked to reconcile exactly to the given actual profit of ¢69,620):"},{"table":{"align":"lrr","head":["Variance","Marginal approach","Absorption approach"],"rows":[["Sales margin price","18,000 F","18,000 F"],["Sales margin volume","20,000 A","8,000 A"],["Material price (A 1,900 A, B 1,720 F)","180 A","180 A"],["Material usage (A 1,000 A, B 3,000 A)","4,000 A","4,000 A"],["Labour rate","5,700 A","5,700 A"],["Labour efficiency","4,500 A","4,500 A"],["Variable overhead expenditure","5,000 F","5,000 F"],["Variable overhead efficiency","3,000 A","3,000 A"],["Fixed overhead expenditure","4,000 F","4,000 F"],["Fixed overhead volume efficiency","— (not used)","6,000 A"],["Fixed overhead volume capacity","— (not used)","6,000 A"]]}},{"table":{"align":"lr","head":["Reconciliation (marginal costing approach)","¢"],"rows":[["Budgeted profit","80,000"],["+ Sales margin price variance","18,000"],["− Sales margin volume variance","(20,000)"],["− Material price variance","(180)"],["− Material usage variance","(4,000)"],["− Labour rate variance","(5,700)"],["− Labour efficiency variance","(4,500)"],["+ Variable overhead expenditure variance","5,000"],["− Variable overhead efficiency variance","(3,000)"],["+ Fixed overhead expenditure variance","4,000"],["**Actual profit**","**69,620**"]]}},{"table":{"align":"lr","head":["Reconciliation (absorption costing approach)","¢"],"rows":[["Budgeted profit","80,000"],["+ Sales margin price variance","18,000"],["− Sales margin volume variance","(8,000)"],["− Material price variance","(180)"],["− Material usage variance","(4,000)"],["− Labour rate variance","(5,700)"],["− Labour efficiency variance","(4,500)"],["+ Variable overhead expenditure variance","5,000"],["− Variable overhead efficiency variance","(3,000)"],["+ Fixed overhead expenditure variance","4,000"],["− Fixed overhead volume efficiency variance","(6,000)"],["− Fixed overhead volume capacity variance","(6,000)"],["**Actual profit**","**69,620**"]]}},{"warn":"The study text's own printed reconciliation tables for this illustration are badly disordered by scanning — rows visibly out of sequence, several figures duplicated across the wrong lines. Both tables above were **independently rebuilt** from the individually-verified variances, and each is confirmed to reconcile **exactly** to the given actual profit of ¢69,620 — the surest check available that the reconstruction is correct, since the source's own printed totals could not be trusted at face value here."},{"note":"The absorption approach's sales margin volume variance is 8,000 A, not the \"8,000 F\" the source prints — using the standard **profit** margin (¢8/unit, not the ¢20 contribution margin the marginal approach uses): $(9{,}000-10{,}000) \\times 8 = -8{,}000$, an adverse variance since actual volume fell short of budget — consistent with the marginal approach's own (correctly-signed) adverse volume variance on the same shortfall."}]}}]},{"n":"13.6","t":"Activity, capacity and efficiency ratios","b":[{"p":"In place of (or alongside) the volume/capacity/efficiency **variances** above, the same relationships can be expressed as **ratios**."},{"fbox":{"h":"Activity, capacity and efficiency ratios","rows":[{"lb":"Efficiency ratio","tex":"\\dfrac{\\text{Standard hours for actual output}}{\\text{Actual hours worked}} \\times 100"},{"lb":"Activity ratio","tex":"\\dfrac{\\text{Standard hours for actual output}}{\\text{Budgeted hours}} \\times 100"},{"lb":"Capacity ratio","tex":"\\dfrac{\\text{Actual hours worked}}{\\text{Budgeted hours}} \\times 100"}]}},{"key":"A useful identity to check any set of these ratios: $\\text{Activity ratio} = \\text{Capacity ratio} \\times \\text{Efficiency ratio}$ (as a fraction, before converting to %)."},{"eg":{"tag":"Study text","t":"Illustration 13.6 — the three ratios, on the same data as Illustration 13.3","open":true,"q":[{"p":"Budgeted hours 100,000; standard hours for actual output 80,000; actual hours worked 90,000. Compute the efficiency, activity and capacity ratios."}],"a":[{"tex":"\\text{Efficiency ratio} = \\dfrac{80{,}000}{90{,}000} \\times 100 \\approx 88.89\\%"},{"tex":"\\text{Activity ratio} = \\dfrac{80{,}000}{100{,}000} \\times 100 = 80\\%"},{"tex":"\\text{Capacity ratio} = \\dfrac{90{,}000}{100{,}000} \\times 100 = 90\\%"},{"key":"Check via the identity: $90\\% \\times 88.89\\% \\approx 80\\%$ — matches the activity ratio exactly, confirming all three figures."}]}}]},{"n":"13.7","t":"Causes of variances, and deciding what to investigate","b":[{"p":"Establishing the *real* cause of a variance takes genuine investigative skill — observation, enquiry, checking — much like an audit investigation."},{"p":"Factors influencing the decision to investigate a variance:"},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Factor","How it points the decision"],"rows":[["**History of the variance**","The more frequently a deviation recurs, the *less* likely it will be investigated (it is a known, ongoing pattern); the less frequent, the *more* likely."],["**Reliability of the standard**","The more reliable the standard is thought to be, the higher the chance of investigating any deviation from it (and vice versa)."],["**Significance of the deviation**","Immaterial variances are unlikely to be investigated; material ones are."],["**Cost-benefit**","Investigate when the benefit of investigating exceeds its cost."],["**Effect on future planning/control**","Even a costly investigation may be worthwhile if it genuinely improves future operations and control."]]}},{"key":"Practically, management sets upper and lower **control limits** around the standard — investigation is triggered only when actual results fall **outside** those limits, not for every single deviation."}]},{"n":"13.10","t":"Chapter summary","b":[{"p":"**Standard costing is a useful control tool** that assists business organisations to ensure that their budgets are achieved, by pinpointing the areas of standard performance where corrective action needs to be taken."},{"p":"The chapter explained standard costing and variance analysis in three sections:"},{"ol":["**Section one** explained the concept of a standard, the types of standards, standard costing, and its uses, merits and limitations.","**Section two** concentrated on the analysis of the various variances — materials cost, labour cost and overhead variances.","**The last section** continued the discussion of variance analysis by explaining **sales margin variances**, dealt with the **reconciliation of budgeted profit with actual profit**, explained the use of **activity, capacity and efficiency ratios**, and concluded with the **causes of variances** and the factors that influence the decision to **investigate** them."]},{"key":"Hold on to the shape of the subject rather than memorising formulae one by one. Every cost variance splits the same two ways: a **price/rate/expenditure** part (you paid a different amount per unit of input than you planned) and a **usage/efficiency/volume** part (you used a different quantity of input than the output should have needed). Fixed overhead is the one exception — its \"usage\" half is a **volume** variance, because fixed cost does not vary with usage at all, only with how many units are available to absorb it."}]},{"n":"13.11","t":"Worksheet summary — definitions, formulae and structure","b":[{"p":"Everything in this chapter, compressed for a last read-through."},{"h3":"Section-by-section checklist"},{"ol":["**§13.1 Standards** — 4 types (basic/ideal/attainable/current), each with its own valid use and its own blind spot; standard cost is *not* an average of past costs; 6 uses, several merits and limitations.","**§13.2–13.3 Process & setting standards** — 5-step process (set→measure→compare→analyse→investigate); materials/labour/overhead/selling-price standards each set from different inputs; the standard cost card is the single reference point for everything downstream.","**§13.4 Cost variances** — material (cost=price+usage), labour (cost=rate+efficiency, plus a separate idle-time variance), and overhead (TOCV=VOCV+fixed OH total; VOCV=expenditure+efficiency; fixed OH total=expenditure+volume; volume=efficiency+capacity) — every level of this tree should reconcile exactly to the level above it.","**§13.5 Sales margin variances & reconciliation** — measured in profit/contribution margin, not price; total = price variance + volume variance; marginal costing reconciliation uses only fixed OH expenditure variance, absorption costing uses the full fixed-overhead variance tree.","**§13.6 Ratios** — efficiency/activity/capacity, an alternative lens on the same volume/capacity/efficiency story as the variances; Activity = Capacity × Efficiency is a built-in cross-check.","**§13.7 Investigating variances** — 5 factors (history, standard reliability, materiality, cost-benefit, effect on future control); practically applied via control limits, not investigating every deviation."]},{"h3":"B. Formula box"},{"fbox":{"h":"Standard costing formulas (recap)","rows":[{"lb":"Material Cost Variance","tex":"(SQ \\times SP) - (AQ \\times AP)"},{"lb":"Material Price Variance","tex":"AQ \\times (SP - AP)"},{"lb":"Material Usage Variance","tex":"SP \\times (SQ - AQ)"},{"lb":"Labour Cost Variance","tex":"(SH \\times SR) - (AH \\times AR)"},{"lb":"Labour Rate Variance","tex":"AH \\times (SR - AR)"},{"lb":"Labour Efficiency Variance","tex":"SR \\times (SH - AH)"},{"lb":"Fixed Overhead Volume Variance","tex":"(SH - \\text{Budgeted hours}) \\times FOAR"},{"lb":"Efficiency ratio","tex":"\\dfrac{SH}{AH} \\times 100"},{"lb":"Activity ratio","tex":"\\dfrac{SH}{\\text{Budgeted hours}} \\times 100"},{"lb":"Capacity ratio","tex":"\\dfrac{AH}{\\text{Budgeted hours}} \\times 100"}]}}]},{"n":"13.12","t":"Study text questions — multiple-choice and short-answer","b":[{"eg":{"tag":"Study text","t":"Multiple-choice and short-answer questions, with answers","open":true,"q":[{"ol":["Which of the following conditions explains when attainable standards can be achieved? (A) Under the best possible conditions  (B) Under the most possible conditions  (C) When there is no idle time  (D) When the same trends have been maintained over a long period of time  (E) When allowances are made for normal losses and possible machine breakdown","What is the purpose of computing variances? (A) To detect fraud  (B) To punish inefficient managers  (C) To investigate why standards are not met, and take corrective actions  (D) To maintain good records  (E) To settle long-standing issues","What type of variance can result from machine breakdown, power outages and work stoppages? (A) Direct labour efficiency variance  (B) Machine breakdown variance  (C) Material wastage variance  (D) Idle time variance  (E) Electricity wastage variance","When we record substantial favourable variances, (A) It shows that we are working hard  (B) We investigate and break it down because it may be concealing some hidden weaknesses  (C) We give bonuses to employees  (D) We raise the standards  (E) We proudly publish it for others to see","If the cost of investigating a particular variance is higher than the benefit to be derived from it, ……………. (A) The investigation is dropped  (B) We still go ahead and investigate for the records  (C) We keep the investigation till the cases pile up  (D) We spread the cost over a number of years  (E) The investigation is done in piece meal","Standards which can only be attained under the most favourable working conditions are called ………………………","Which costing concept best describes situations when actual costs are compared with standard costs?","The official document showing the components of the standard cost of a product is known as ………………………….","The addition of direct material price variance and direct material usage variance will give us a………………………………………….","The difference between the standard direct labour rate and actual direct labour rate per hour for the actual total hours worked and paid for is called ……………"]}],"a":[{"ol":["**E** — when allowances are made for normal losses and possible machine breakdown.","**C** — to investigate why standards are not met, and take corrective action.","**D — idle time variance.**","**B** — we investigate and break it down, since it may be concealing hidden weaknesses.","**A — the investigation is dropped.**","**Ideal standards.**","**Variance (analysis).**","**Standard Cost Card.**","**Direct material cost variance.**","**Direct labour rate variance.**"]}]}}]},{"n":"13.13","t":"Study text examination-type questions, worked","b":[{"p":"The study text closes the chapter with nine examination-type questions. All are reproduced below with full answers, each carrying the examining body and diet the study text credits it to, and its mark allocation."},{"eg":{"tag":"Exam type — Q13.1 (20 marks) — adapted from ICAG, November 2005","t":"Investigating variances; Pass Company Limited — material, labour and variable overhead variances","q":[{"p":"**(a)** Outline the factors that should be considered when deciding whether or not to investigate variances revealed in a standard costing and budgetary control system *(5 marks)*."},{"p":"**(b)** Pass Company Limited operates a **standard marginal costing system**. The company produces a standardised product called \"success\" using a single raw material called material papa. During **August 2021, 5,000 units of \"success\" were produced**. The following data relate to actual performance during the month:"},{"ol":["The company purchased **50,000 kg of material papa** at a cost of **¢144,000,000**.","Stock levels of material papa were: **1/8/2021 — 20,000 kg; 31/8/2021 — 15,000 kg**.","The company paid workers for **26,000 hours** at a total wages cost of **¢48,360,000**.","The company incurred **¢16,800,000** for variable production overheads."]},{"stmt":{"t":"Standard cost card — cost per unit (¢)","rows":[["Direct material cost  12 kg @ ¢3,000 per kg","",36000],["Direct labour cost  5 hours @ ¢1,800 per hour","",9000],["**Prime cost**","",45000,"@t"],["Variable production overhead cost  5 hours @ ¢600","",3000],["**Variable production cost**","",48000,"@tt"]]}},{"p":"**Required:** calculate (i) the direct material cost variance, analysed into price and usage variances; (ii) the direct wages cost variance, analysed into rate and efficiency variances; (iii) the variable production overhead cost variance, analysed into expenditure and efficiency variances *(15 marks)*."},{"note":"The study text's requirement (i) reads \"analysed into price and **wage** variances\". It means **price and usage** — the wage variances are requirement (ii)."}],"a":[{"h4":"(a)  Factors in deciding whether to investigate a variance"},{"ol":["**The size of the variance — in absolute terms and as a percentage** of the standard. A ¢50,000 variance on a ¢100,000 standard demands attention; the same amount on a ¢50 million standard probably does not.","**Whether the variance is controllable.** There is no point investigating a variance caused by a factor outside the organisation's control, such as a national wage award or a world commodity price movement — except to revise the standard.","**The trend.** A small variance that recurs in the same direction every month matters far more than a single larger one, and may signal that the standard itself is wrong. A cumulative or statistical control chart will reveal a trend that month-by-month exception reporting hides.","**The cost of the investigation against the likely benefit.** Investigating consumes management time; it is worth doing only if the expected saving from correcting the cause exceeds that cost.","**The likelihood that the cause can be found and corrected.** Some variances are random and will not recur; investigating them yields nothing.","**The interrelationship between variances.** A favourable material price variance and an adverse usage variance usually have a single cause — cheaper, poorer material — and should be investigated together, not separately.","**Whether the variance is adverse or favourable.** Both should be investigated: a large **favourable** variance may mean quality has been cut, maintenance deferred, or the standard set too loosely.","**The reliability of the standard and of the measurement.** If the standard is out of date or the recording is suspect, the variance is measuring the system, not performance.","**Statistical significance.** Where the distribution of a cost is known, a variance outside (say) two standard deviations from the expected value is unlikely to be chance and warrants investigation."]},{"h4":"(b)  Workings"},{"stmt":{"t":"Materials used, and the actual prices","rows":[["Opening stock of material papa","","20,000 kg"],["Add: purchases","","50,000 kg"],["Less: closing stock","","(15,000) kg"],["**Material used in production**","","55,000 kg","@t"],"@gap",["Actual purchase price  (¢144,000,000 ÷ 50,000 kg)","","¢2,880 per kg"],["Actual labour rate  (¢48,360,000 ÷ 26,000 hours)","","¢1,860 per hour"],["Actual variable overhead rate  (¢16,800,000 ÷ 26,000 hours)","","¢646.15 per hour"],"@gap",["Standard quantity of material for actual output  (5,000 × 12 kg)","","60,000 kg"],["Standard hours for actual output  (5,000 × 5 hours)","","25,000 hours"]]}},{"h4":"(b)(i)  Direct material cost variance"},{"stmt":{"t":"Direct material variances (¢)","rows":[["Standard material cost of actual production  (5,000 units × ¢36,000)","",180000000],["Actual material cost of material used  (55,000 kg × ¢2,880)","",158400000],["**Direct material cost variance**","","21,600,000  **FAVOURABLE**","@tt"],"@gap",["**Price variance**  (¢3,000 − ¢2,880) × 55,000 kg used","","6,600,000  **FAVOURABLE**"],["**Usage variance**  (60,000 − 55,000 kg) × ¢3,000 standard price","","15,000,000  **FAVOURABLE**"],["**Total**","","21,600,000  F","@tt"]]}},{"note":"The price variance is computed on the **quantity used** (55,000 kg) rather than on purchases, because the question does not say that stock is valued at standard. Where it does — as in Q13.7 below — the price variance is isolated on **purchases** instead, and the two sub-variances then no longer sum to the total usage-based variance."},{"h4":"(b)(ii)  Direct wages cost variance"},{"stmt":{"t":"Direct labour variances (¢)","rows":[["Standard labour cost of actual production  (5,000 units × ¢9,000)","",45000000],["Actual wages paid","",48360000],["**Direct wages cost variance**","","3,360,000  **ADVERSE**","@tt"],"@gap",["**Rate variance**  (¢1,800 − ¢1,860) × 26,000 hours","","1,560,000  **ADVERSE**"],["**Efficiency variance**  (25,000 − 26,000 hours) × ¢1,800","","1,800,000  **ADVERSE**"],["**Total**","","3,360,000  A","@tt"]]}},{"h4":"(b)(iii)  Variable production overhead cost variance"},{"stmt":{"t":"Variable production overhead variances (¢)","rows":[["Standard variable overhead for actual production  (5,000 units × ¢3,000)","",15000000],["Actual variable production overhead","",16800000],["**Variable production overhead cost variance**","","1,800,000  **ADVERSE**","@tt"],"@gap",["**Expenditure variance**  (¢600 × 26,000 hours) − ¢16,800,000","","1,200,000  **ADVERSE**"],["**Efficiency variance**  (25,000 − 26,000 hours) × ¢600","","600,000  **ADVERSE**"],["**Total**","","1,800,000  A","@tt"]]}},{"key":"Read the variances together, as part (a) says you should. The **material price variance is favourable** (¢2,880 paid against a ¢3,000 standard) and the **usage variance is also favourable** — material was bought cheaply *and* used sparingly, which is an unusually good result and worth understanding. But **every labour and overhead variance is adverse**, and the labour efficiency variance (1,000 hours over standard) directly causes the variable overhead efficiency variance too. One plausible story: the cheaper material was harder to work, so it took 1,000 extra hours — in which case the favourable material variances and the adverse labour ones share a single cause, and the net saving of ¢16.44 million is the figure that matters, not any one variance alone."}]}},{"eg":{"tag":"Exam type — Q13.2 (20 marks) — adapted from ICAG, November 2000","t":"Four types of standard; Nsawam Poultry — labour variances with idle time","q":[{"p":"**(a)** State and explain briefly **four** different types of standards *(8 marks)*."},{"p":"**(b)** Nsawam Poultry Products Ltd employs different grades of labour on its farm. The direct labour cost of its product is **three hours of grade A labour at ¢250 per hour, resulting in ¢750 per unit** of the product. During **March 2000, 300 units** of the product were made, and the labour cost of grade A was **¢220,000 for 910 hours**. During the month there was a machine breakdown and **40 hours were recorded as idle time**."},{"p":"**Required:** calculate the (i) direct labour total cost variance; (ii) direct labour rate variance; (iii) idle time variance; (iv) direct labour efficiency variance *(12 marks)*."}],"a":[{"h4":"(a)  Four types of standard"},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Type of standard","Explanation"],"rows":[["**1. Ideal (perfection) standard**","A standard set under **perfect operating conditions** — no machine breakdowns, no idle time, no wastage, no spoilage, materials always available, workers always working at peak efficiency. It represents the absolute minimum cost possible. **Rarely used for control**, because it can never be attained; variances are always adverse, which demotivates. It is useful only as a long-run target showing the theoretical limit."],["**2. Attainable (expected/current) standard**","A standard set at a level that **can be achieved under efficient but realistic operating conditions** — allowing for normal wastage, normal idle time and normal machine downtime. It is **demanding but achievable**, and is therefore the standard most used in practice, because it motivates and because the variances it produces are meaningful."],["**3. Basic standard**","A standard **left unchanged over a long period** — often years — so that trends in performance can be observed over time. Because it is not updated for changes in prices, methods or technology, it becomes progressively less useful for current control, and the variances it produces measure how much conditions have changed rather than how well the period was managed."],["**4. Current standard**","A standard set for a **short period** and reflecting the conditions actually prevailing in that period, including temporary and abnormal ones — a short-term shortage of a material, for example. It gives realistic variances for the period but is useless for comparison between periods, and it can conceal inefficiency by building current problems into the standard."]]}},{"note":"A fifth, often added: the **normal standard**, an average standard expected to be achieved over a future period long enough to cover one trade cycle, smoothing out cyclical fluctuations."},{"h4":"(b)  Workings"},{"stmt":{"t":"Workings","rows":[["Standard hours for actual production  (300 units × 3 hours)","","900 hours"],["Standard cost of actual production  (900 hours × ¢250)","","¢225,000"],"@gap",["Hours **paid for**","","910 hours"],["Less: idle time","","(40) hours"],["**Hours actually worked**","","870 hours","@t"],["Actual wages paid","","¢220,000"],["Actual rate per hour  (¢220,000 ÷ 910)","","¢241.76"]]}},{"h4":"(b)  The four variances"},{"stmt":{"t":"Direct labour variances (¢)","rows":[["**(i) Direct labour total cost variance**","","","@hd"],["Standard cost of actual production",225000,""],["Actual wages paid",220000,"5,000  **FAVOURABLE**"],"@gap",["**(ii) Direct labour rate variance**","","","@hd"],["Hours paid at standard rate  (910 × ¢250)",227500,""],["Actual wages paid",220000,"7,500  **FAVOURABLE**"],"@gap",["**(iii) Idle time variance**","","","@hd"],["Idle hours × standard rate  (40 × ¢250)","","10,000  **ADVERSE**"],"@gap",["**(iv) Direct labour efficiency variance**","","","@hd"],["(Standard hours 900 − hours **worked** 870) × ¢250","","7,500  **FAVOURABLE**"],"@gap",["**Reconciliation:  7,500 F − 10,000 A + 7,500 F**","","5,000  **FAVOURABLE**","@tt"]]}},{"warn":"The whole question turns on the distinction between **hours paid for (910)** and **hours worked (870)**. The **rate** variance uses hours **paid**, because the company paid the rate on all 910 hours. The **efficiency** variance uses hours **worked**, because the 40 idle hours were not available for production and are dealt with separately by the **idle time variance**. Mixing the two is the commonest error: computing efficiency on 910 hours would give $(900-910) \\times 250 = ¢2{,}500$ adverse and the reconciliation would then fail."},{"key":"Note what the analysis reveals. On the face of it the month looks good — a ¢5,000 **favourable** total variance. But that masks a **¢10,000 adverse idle time variance** caused by the machine breakdown, offset by a cheaper labour rate and genuinely efficient working in the hours that were available (870 hours doing 900 hours' work). Management should be told about the breakdown, not reassured by the net figure."}]}},{"eg":{"tag":"Exam type — Q13.3 (20 marks) — ICAG, May 2000","t":"Working backwards from variances to units produced, usage variance and actual price","q":[{"p":"A company produces a product which has a **standard variable production cost of ¢8,000 per unit**, made up as follows:"},{"stmt":{"t":"Standard variable production cost per unit (¢)","rows":[["Direct materials  (2 kg × ¢2,300 per kg)","",4600],["Direct labour  (0.7 hours × ¢3,000 per hour)","",2100],["Variable overhead","",1300],["**Standard variable production cost**","",8000,"@tt"]]}},{"p":"Fixed manufacturing costs are treated as period costs. The following information is available for the period just ended:"},{"stmt":{"t":"Information for the period (¢)","rows":[["Variable manufacturing cost of sales (at standard cost)","",263520000],["Opening stock of finished goods (at standard cost)","",120800000],["Closing stock of finished goods (at standard cost)","",146080000],["Direct material price variance","","2,571,000 adverse"],["Raw material used in manufacture (at actual cost)","",170310000]]}},{"p":"**Required:** **(a)** determine for the period (i) the number of units produced; (ii) the raw materials usage variance; (iii) the total actual direct labour cost; (iv) the total actual cost per kg of raw material used. **(b)** Outline the possible causes of the raw material variances."}],"a":[{"h4":"(a)(i)  Number of units produced"},{"p":"All three finished-goods figures are at **standard cost**, so the finished goods identity can be rearranged to give the cost of production, and the units follow by dividing by the ¢8,000 standard cost per unit."},{"stmt":{"t":"Units produced (¢)","rows":[["Variable manufacturing cost of sales, at standard","",263520000],["Add: closing stock of finished goods, at standard","",146080000],["","",409600000,"@t"],["Less: opening stock of finished goods, at standard","",-120800000],["**Cost of production for the period, at standard**","",288800000,"@t"],["÷ standard variable production cost per unit","","¢8,000"],["**Number of units produced**","","36,100 units","@tt"]]}},{"h4":"(a)(ii)  Raw materials usage variance"},{"stmt":{"t":"Raw materials usage variance (¢)","rows":[["Actual cost of raw material used","",170310000],["Less: direct material price variance (adverse — so actual cost is this much **above** the standard price for the quantity used)","",-2571000],["**Actual quantity used, valued at standard price**","",167739000,"@t"],"@gap",["Standard material cost of actual production  (36,100 units × ¢4,600)","",166060000],["**Raw materials usage variance**","","1,679,000  **ADVERSE**","@tt"]]}},{"h4":"(a)(iii)  Total actual direct labour cost"},{"stmt":{"t":"Direct labour cost (¢)","rows":[["Standard direct labour cost of actual production  (36,100 units × ¢2,100)","",75810000],["**Total actual direct labour cost**","",75810000,"@tt"]]}},{"warn":"The question supplies **no labour variance of any kind**. The only defensible reading is therefore that there was none — i.e. the actual direct labour cost **equalled the standard cost of the actual production**, ¢75,810,000. State this assumption explicitly. If a labour rate or efficiency variance had been given, it would be added to (if adverse) or deducted from (if favourable) the standard figure to arrive at the actual.","lbl":"An assumption the question forces"},{"h4":"(a)(iv)  Total actual cost per kg of raw material used"},{"stmt":{"t":"Actual cost per kg","rows":[["Actual quantity used, valued at standard price (from (ii))","","¢167,739,000"],["÷ standard price per kg","","¢2,300"],["**Actual quantity of raw material used**","","72,930 kg","@t"],"@gap",["Actual cost of raw material used","","¢170,310,000"],["÷ actual quantity used","","72,930 kg"],["**Actual cost per kg**","","¢2,335.25","@tt"]]}},{"note":"Cross-check: the price variance per kg is $¢2{,}571{,}000 \\div 72{,}930 = ¢35.25$ adverse, and $¢2{,}300 + ¢35.25 = ¢2{,}335.25$ ✓. A second cross-check on the usage variance: standard quantity for 36,100 units is $36{,}100 \\times 2 = 72{,}200$ kg against 72,930 kg used, a difference of 730 kg at ¢2,300 = **¢1,679,000 adverse** ✓"},{"h4":"(b)  Possible causes of the raw material variances"},{"p":"**Adverse price variance (¢2,571,000):**"},{"ul":["a general **increase in the market price** of the material since the standard was set;","**buying in smaller quantities** than planned, so losing quantity discounts;","**emergency purchases** at short notice to avoid a stockout, at premium prices and carriage;","**buying a higher grade** of material than the standard specifies;","**change of supplier**, or loss of a negotiated contract price;","**inflation, exchange rate movement or import duty** on an imported material;","an **out-of-date standard price** that no longer reflects the market."]},{"p":"**Adverse usage variance (¢1,679,000):**"},{"ul":["**poor quality material**, causing more scrap, rejects and rework;","**careless handling, pilferage, deterioration or evaporation** in store or on the shop floor;","**inefficient or inexperienced labour**, or inadequate supervision;","**faulty or badly maintained machinery** producing more defective output;","**change in the method of production** or in the product specification;","**theft** or unrecorded issues;","an **unrealistically tight standard** quantity that never allowed enough for normal wastage."]},{"key":"The two variances here are **adverse together**, which rules out the commonest single explanation (cheap material used wastefully). A more likely story is that a **higher-grade or emergency-purchased material** was bought at a premium and then used by labour unfamiliar with it, or that the **standards themselves are out of date** in both price and quantity. The question asks for possible causes; a good answer groups them and then says which combination best fits the direction of both variances."}]}},{"eg":{"tag":"Exam type — Q13.4 (15 marks) — ICAG, November 1997","t":"Three standard-costing terms, the steps in setting up a system, and causes of three variances","q":[{"p":"**(a)** Explain the terms: (i) ideal standard; (ii) attainable standard; (iii) standard hour or minute *(3 marks)*."},{"p":"**(b)** List **four** main steps in setting up a standard costing system *(6 marks)*."},{"p":"**(c)** Identify **three** causes of each of the following variances: (i) direct material price variances; (ii) direct labour efficiency variances; (iii) overheads expenditure variances *(6 marks)*."}],"a":[{"h4":"(a)  The three terms"},{"def":{"t":"(i) Ideal standard","d":"a standard set under **perfect operating conditions**, making no allowance for wastage, spoilage, idle time, machine breakdown or inefficiency of any kind. It represents the theoretical minimum cost. Because it can never be attained in practice, every variance against it is adverse, which makes it demotivating and of little use for routine control."}},{"def":{"t":"(ii) Attainable standard","d":"a standard set at a level that **can be achieved under efficient but realistic operating conditions**, allowing for normal levels of wastage, idle time and machine downtime. It is demanding but achievable, and is the type of standard most widely used, because it motivates and produces meaningful variances."}},{"def":{"t":"(iii) Standard hour (or standard minute)","d":"a measure of **output**, not of time: the quantity of work that **should** be performed in one hour (or minute) under standard conditions. It allows dissimilar products to be added together into a single measure of output — a factory producing 100 units of a product with a standard time of 2 hours and 300 units of one with a standard time of 0.5 hours has produced $200 + 150 = 350$ standard hours of output — and it is the basis of the activity, capacity and efficiency ratios of §13.6."}},{"h4":"(b)  Four main steps in setting up a standard costing system"},{"steps":["**Establish the cost centres and the products** for which standards are to be set, and define the **standard operating conditions** — the methods, the plant, the activity level and the grades of labour that the standards will assume.","**Set the standard quantities** of each input for each unit of output: the standard material specification (quantity and grade, with an allowance for normal wastage), determined from the engineering specification and from test runs; and the standard labour times, determined by **work study and time and motion study** for each grade and operation.","**Set the standard prices and rates**: standard material prices (from the purchasing department, reflecting expected market prices, contract terms and quantity discounts), standard wage rates (from the personnel department, reflecting agreed rates for each grade), and **standard overhead absorption rates** (budgeted overhead ÷ budgeted activity, for variable and fixed overhead separately).","**Prepare the standard cost card** for each product, bringing all of the above together to give the standard cost per unit, and obtain its approval.","**Establish the recording and reporting procedures** — the documents that will capture actual quantities, hours and costs on a basis comparable with the standards, the variance calculations, the reporting format and timetable, and the procedure for investigating and acting on variances.","**Set up a procedure for reviewing and revising the standards** as prices, methods and conditions change, so that the system does not decay into measuring how out-of-date the standards have become."]},{"h4":"(c)(i)  Three causes of direct material price variances"},{"ol":["**A change in the market price** of the material since the standard was set — inflation, a commodity price movement, an exchange rate change on an imported material.","**Buying in a different quantity** from that assumed — losing (or gaining) quantity discounts, or making emergency purchases in small lots at premium prices.","**Buying a different grade or quality** of material from the standard specification, or changing supplier.","*(Also: inefficient or efficient buying; an out-of-date standard price; a change in carriage or duty.)*"]},{"h4":"(c)(ii)  Three causes of direct labour efficiency variances"},{"ol":["**The grade or experience of the labour used** — a lower grade, or newly recruited and untrained workers, take longer than standard; a higher grade may take less.","**The quality of materials and the condition of machinery** — poor material, or badly maintained machines, slow the work and cause rework; conversely, better material or new equipment speeds it.","**Working conditions, supervision and motivation** — poor layout, interruptions, poor supervision, low morale or the absence of an incentive scheme all lengthen the time taken.","*(Also: an unrealistic standard time; a change in the production method; the learning effect on a new product.)*"]},{"h4":"(c)(iii)  Three causes of overhead expenditure variances"},{"ol":["**Price changes in the individual overhead items** — rent reviews, insurance premiums, energy tariffs, rates, salaries of indirect staff.","**A change in the quantity of overhead resources consumed** — more or less indirect material, indirect labour, power, maintenance or supervision than budgeted.","**Inefficiency or economy in the control of overhead spending**, or an unplanned event such as a major repair, a reorganisation or a one-off cost not in the budget.","*(Also: an unrealistic or out-of-date overhead budget; a change in the classification of a cost between departments.)*"]}]}},{"eg":{"tag":"Exam type — Q13.5 (20 marks) — adapted from ICAG, November 2005","t":"End Times Limited — full variance analysis and reconciliation of budgeted to actual profit","q":[{"p":"End Times Limited manufactures and sells a single product, the standard cost of which is as follows:"},{"stmt":{"t":"Standards per unit (¢)","rows":[["Selling price","",107200],"@gap",["**Standard cost**","","","@hd"],["Materials:  64 units @ ¢400",25600,""],["Labour:  16 hours @ ¢300",4800,""],["Overheads:  16 hours @ ¢2,400",38400,68800],["**Standard profit margin**","",38400,"@tt"]]}},{"p":"The following information relates to the activities of End Times for the month of September 2021:"},{"stmt":{"t":"Budgeted and actual figures","rows":[["","Budgeted","Actual","@hd"],["Production and sales (units)",60000,50000],["Direct labour hours",960000,"820,000  *(see note)*"],["Wage rate per hour (¢)",300,450],["Fixed overheads (¢)",768000000,800000000],["Variable overheads (¢)",1536000000,1614000000],["Material units",3840000,3840000],["Material price per unit (¢)",400,300]]}},{"p":"The actual selling price during September 2021 was **¢105,000** and there were no opening or closing stocks of finished goods. End Times Limited uses an **absorption costing** system."},{"p":"**Required:** **(a)** prepare profit statements to ascertain the budgeted profit and the actual profit for September 2021 *(6 marks)*; **(b)** calculate all the relevant variances and reconcile the budgeted and actual profits *(14 marks)*."},{"warn":"The study text prints the **actual** direct labour hours as **20,000**, which is impossible — the budget is 960,000 hours for 60,000 units, so 50,000 units at standard would need 800,000 hours. The figure cannot be recovered from the printed data (the PDF page was checked directly). **820,000 hours has been assumed** below, and the assumption is validated by the fact that the reconciliation at (b) then closes **exactly** on the independently computed actual profit of ¢1,315,000,000. State the assumption in any answer.","lbl":"A figure missing from the study text"},{"note":"The variable and fixed overhead figures are printed without their scale. They are in **¢'000** — because $768{,}000{,}000 + 1{,}536{,}000{,}000 = ¢2{,}304{,}000{,}000$, which is exactly $60{,}000 \\text{ units} \\times ¢38{,}400$, the budgeted overhead on the standard cost card. That check also gives the split of the ¢2,400 hourly overhead rate into **¢800 fixed and ¢1,600 variable**."}],"a":[{"h4":"Workings — splitting the standard overhead rate"},{"stmt":{"t":"Standard overhead rates","rows":[["Budgeted fixed overhead ÷ budgeted hours  (¢768,000,000 ÷ 960,000)","","¢800 per hour"],["Budgeted variable overhead ÷ budgeted hours  (¢1,536,000,000 ÷ 960,000)","","¢1,600 per hour"],["**Total standard overhead rate**","","**¢2,400 per hour** ✓"],"@gap",["Fixed overhead per unit  (16 hours × ¢800)","","¢12,800"],["Variable overhead per unit  (16 hours × ¢1,600)","","¢25,600"],["**Total overhead per unit**","","**¢38,400** ✓"],"@gap",["Standard hours for actual output  (50,000 units × 16 hours)","","800,000 hours"],["Standard material units for actual output  (50,000 × 64)","","3,200,000 units"]]}},{"h4":"(a)  Budgeted and actual profit statements"},{"stmt":{"t":"Budgeted profit statement, September 2021 (¢)","rows":[["Sales  (60,000 units × ¢107,200)","",6432000000],["Less: standard cost of sales  (60,000 × ¢68,800)","",4128000000],["**Budgeted profit**  (60,000 × ¢38,400)","",2304000000,"@tt"]]}},{"stmt":{"t":"Actual profit statement, September 2021 (¢)","rows":[["Sales  (50,000 units × ¢105,000)","",5250000000],"@gap",["**Actual costs**","","","@hd"],["Materials  (3,840,000 units × ¢300)",1152000000,""],["Labour  (820,000 hours × ¢450)",369000000,""],["Fixed overheads",800000000,""],["Variable overheads",1614000000,3935000000],["**Actual profit**","",1315000000,"@tt"]]}},{"h4":"(b)  The variances"},{"stmt":{"t":"Sales variances (¢)","rows":[["**Sales price variance**  (¢105,000 − ¢107,200) × 50,000 units","","110,000,000  **ADVERSE**"],["**Sales volume profit variance**  (50,000 − 60,000) × ¢38,400 standard profit","","384,000,000  **ADVERSE**"]]}},{"stmt":{"t":"Direct material variances (¢)","rows":[["Standard material cost of actual production  (50,000 × ¢25,600)","",1280000000],["Actual material cost  (3,840,000 × ¢300)","",1152000000],["**Total material cost variance**","","128,000,000  **FAVOURABLE**","@t"],"@gap",["**Price variance**  (¢400 − ¢300) × 3,840,000 units","","384,000,000  **FAVOURABLE**"],["**Usage variance**  (3,200,000 − 3,840,000) × ¢400","","256,000,000  **ADVERSE**"]]}},{"stmt":{"t":"Direct labour variances (¢)","rows":[["Standard labour cost of actual production  (50,000 × ¢4,800)","",240000000],["Actual labour cost  (820,000 × ¢450)","",369000000],["**Total labour cost variance**","","129,000,000  **ADVERSE**","@t"],"@gap",["**Rate variance**  (¢300 − ¢450) × 820,000 hours","","123,000,000  **ADVERSE**"],["**Efficiency variance**  (800,000 − 820,000) × ¢300","","6,000,000  **ADVERSE**"]]}},{"stmt":{"t":"Variable overhead variances (¢)","rows":[["Standard variable overhead for actual production  (800,000 hrs × ¢1,600)","",1280000000],["Actual variable overhead","",1614000000],["**Total variable overhead variance**","","334,000,000  **ADVERSE**","@t"],"@gap",["**Expenditure variance**  (820,000 hrs × ¢1,600) − ¢1,614,000,000","","302,000,000  **ADVERSE**"],["**Efficiency variance**  (800,000 − 820,000) × ¢1,600","","32,000,000  **ADVERSE**"]]}},{"stmt":{"t":"Fixed overhead variances (¢)","rows":[["Fixed overhead absorbed  (50,000 units × ¢12,800)","",640000000],["Actual fixed overhead","",800000000],["**Total fixed overhead variance**","","160,000,000  **ADVERSE**","@t"],"@gap",["**Expenditure variance**  budgeted ¢768,000,000 − actual ¢800,000,000","","32,000,000  **ADVERSE**"],["**Volume variance**  (50,000 − 60,000) × ¢12,800","","128,000,000  **ADVERSE**"],"@gap",["*of which — capacity variance*  (820,000 − 960,000 hrs) × ¢800","","*112,000,000  ADVERSE*"],["*of which — efficiency variance*  (800,000 − 820,000 hrs) × ¢800","","*16,000,000  ADVERSE*"]]}},{"h4":"(b)  Reconciliation of budgeted profit with actual profit"},{"stmt":{"t":"Reconciliation statement, September 2021 (¢)","rows":[["**Budgeted profit**  (60,000 units × ¢38,400)","",2304000000],["Sales volume profit variance","",-384000000],["**Standard profit on actual sales**  (50,000 × ¢38,400)","",1920000000,"@t"],"@gap",["","Favourable","Adverse","@hd"],["Sales price variance","",110000000],["Material price variance",384000000,""],["Material usage variance","",256000000],["Labour rate variance","",123000000],["Labour efficiency variance","",6000000],["Variable overhead expenditure variance","",302000000],["Variable overhead efficiency variance","",32000000],["Fixed overhead expenditure variance","",32000000],["Fixed overhead volume variance","",128000000],["",384000000,989000000,"@t"],["**Net adverse variances**","",-605000000],"@gap",["**Actual profit**","",1315000000,"@tt"]]}},{"key":"The reconciliation closes **exactly** on the ¢1,315,000,000 computed independently in the actual profit statement at (a) — which validates both the variance calculations and the 820,000-hour assumption. **Always finish a reconciliation by checking it against the directly computed actual profit**; if the two do not agree, a variance has been omitted, double-counted, or given the wrong sign."},{"note":"What the figures say to management: the only good news is the **material price variance of ¢384 million favourable** — material was bought at ¢300 against a ¢400 standard, a 25% saving. But material **usage** was 3,840,000 units for an output that should have needed 3,200,000 — a 20% overconsumption costing ¢256 million. The cheap material was almost certainly inferior, and the same cause probably explains the 20,000 excess labour hours. Set against a wage rate 50% above standard and output 17% below budget, the month cost the company ¢989 million of adverse variances against ¢384 million favourable."}]}},{"eg":{"tag":"Exam type — Q13.6 (15 marks)","t":"The procedure for setting standard material and labour costs","q":[{"p":"Outline the procedure for setting standard costs of the following cost elements: (i) materials cost; (ii) labour cost."}],"a":[{"h4":"(i)  Setting the standard materials cost"},{"p":"The standard material cost per unit is **standard quantity × standard price**, and the two are set by different people from different information."},{"h4":"Setting the standard quantity (the material specification)"},{"steps":["**Obtain the product specification** — the engineering drawing, bill of materials or recipe — identifying every material and component required, and its grade and quality.","**Determine the net quantity** of each material required in the finished product, from the specification and from **test runs and technical analysis**.","**Add an allowance for normal, unavoidable losses** — evaporation, trimming, offcuts, rejects and shrinkage — determined from past experience and work study. Only **normal** losses are included; abnormal losses must remain visible as variances.","**Decide the standard mix** where more than one material is combined, and the standard yield expected from it.","**Review and agree** the specification with production and engineering, and document it on the standard cost card."]},{"h4":"Setting the standard price"},{"steps":["**The purchasing department** establishes the price, since it is responsible for buying and will be held accountable for the price variance.","**Review current and past prices paid**, supplier quotations and contracts, and price lists.","**Forecast price movements** over the period the standard will cover — inflation, commodity trends, exchange rates for imported material, and duty.","**Allow for the normal purchase quantity and its discounts**, and for carriage, insurance and duty, so that the standard is a **delivered** cost.","**Decide the type of standard** — an attainable standard reflecting expected prices is normal — and agree who is accountable for departures from it."]},{"h4":"(ii)  Setting the standard labour cost"},{"p":"The standard labour cost per unit is **standard time × standard rate**, and again the two come from different sources."},{"h4":"Setting the standard time"},{"steps":["**Carry out method study** — determine the best method of performing each operation, the sequence, the layout, the tools and the working conditions. The standard time must be the time for the **agreed best method**, not for however the job happens to be done.","**Carry out work measurement (time and motion study)** on that method, timing a qualified worker performing it at a defined level of effort, and **rating** the observed performance to convert it to a basic time.","**Add relaxation and contingency allowances** for rest, personal needs, fatigue and unavoidable short delays, to convert basic time into standard time.","**Add an allowance for normal idle time, set-up time and machine downtime** where policy is to build these into the standard.","**Express the result as standard hours or standard minutes per unit**, by operation and by grade of labour, and agree it with production management and, where necessary, with employee representatives."]},{"h4":"Setting the standard rate"},{"steps":["**The personnel (human resources) department** establishes the rate, from the agreed wage structure for each grade of labour.","**Identify the grade of labour** each operation requires, from the method study, and the rate payable to that grade.","**Take account of negotiated wage agreements** in force or anticipated, and of expected increases during the period the standard will cover.","**Allow for the method of remuneration** — time rate, piece rate or bonus scheme — and for any shift or overtime premium that is a normal part of the work.","**Compute a weighted average rate** where an operation is performed by a mix of grades, and document the result on the standard cost card."]},{"key":"The common thread is that **quantity standards are technical** and come from engineering and work study, while **price standards are commercial** and come from purchasing and personnel. That division is deliberate: it is what allows the resulting variance to be analysed into a price part, for which the buyer or the personnel department answers, and a usage or efficiency part, for which production answers."}]}},{"eg":{"tag":"Exam type — Q13.7 (20 marks) — ICAG Management Accounting, November 2000","t":"Benefits of standard costing; Success Company Ltd — variances with stock at standard","q":[{"p":"**(a)** Outline the benefits which a company may derive from a standard costing system *(5 marks)*."},{"p":"**(b)** Success Company Ltd, a manufacturer of aluminium roofing sheets, operates a **standard marginal costing system**. It produces a standardised roofing sheet using a single raw material, Alumina. Standard costs relating to one unit have been calculated as follows:"},{"stmt":{"t":"Standard cost per unit of roofing sheet (¢)","rows":[["Direct material:  10 kg @ ¢3,000 per kg","",30000],["Direct labour:  5 hours @ ¢1,800 per hour","",9000],["**Prime cost**","",39000,"@t"],["Variable production overhead:  5 hours @ ¢600 per hour","",3000],["**Total variable production cost**","",42000,"@tt"]]}},{"p":"During the first week of November, **1,000 units** of roofing sheets were produced. The relevant information on actual performance is:"},{"ol":["Opening stock of raw material, Alumina, was **4,000 kg**.","**10,000 kg** of Alumina were bought during the week at a cost of **¢28,800,000**.","Stock at the close of the week was **3,000 kg**.","**Stocks of raw materials are valued at the standard price of ¢3,000 per kg.**","**5,200 direct labour hours** were worked during the week at a total wage bill of **¢9,672,000**.","Actual variable production overhead for the week was **¢3,360,000**."]},{"p":"**Required:** compute the following cost variances for the week: (i) the variable production cost variance; (a) direct wages cost variance analysed into rate and efficiency; (b) direct material cost variance analysed into price and usage; (c) variable production overhead variance *(15 marks)*."}],"a":[{"h4":"(a)  Benefits of a standard costing system"},{"ol":["**It provides a basis for cost control.** Comparing actual with standard produces variances that pinpoint exactly where performance departed from plan and by how much, so management attention goes where it is needed.","**It enables management by exception.** Only significant variances are reported, freeing management time from the mass of results that are in line with expectation.","**It assists budgeting and planning.** Standard costs are the building blocks of the production cost budget and of forecasts at any activity level.","**It simplifies inventory valuation and bookkeeping.** Inventory and work-in-progress are valued at standard, so the records are simpler, costing is faster, and the clerical effort of tracking actual costs through the ledgers is avoided.","**It assists pricing and decision-making**, by providing a reliable, consistent, predetermined cost per unit for quotations and tenders rather than a historical figure that arrives too late.","**It motivates and evaluates.** Standards are targets, and variance reports provide an objective basis for assessing the performance of each responsibility centre.","**It promotes cost consciousness and efficiency**, because the exercise of setting standards itself requires methods, specifications and working practices to be examined and improved.","**It delegates authority without losing control**, since managers can be given freedom to operate while being held to a clear, quantified standard."]},{"h4":"(b)  Workings"},{"stmt":{"t":"Workings","rows":[["Opening stock of Alumina","","4,000 kg"],["Add: purchases","","10,000 kg"],["Less: closing stock","","(3,000) kg"],["**Material used in production**","","11,000 kg","@t"],"@gap",["Actual purchase price  (¢28,800,000 ÷ 10,000 kg)","","¢2,880 per kg"],["Actual labour rate  (¢9,672,000 ÷ 5,200 hours)","","¢1,860 per hour"],"@gap",["Standard quantity of material for actual output  (1,000 × 10 kg)","","10,000 kg"],["Standard hours for actual output  (1,000 × 5 hours)","","5,000 hours"]]}},{"warn":"Note point (iv) of the question: **raw material stocks are valued at the standard price**. That means the material price variance must be isolated at the point of **purchase**, on the **10,000 kg bought**, not on the 11,000 kg used. The stores account is then kept wholly at standard, and all material issued to production carries the standard ¢3,000 price. Compare with Q13.1 above, where no such statement is made and the price variance is computed on usage."},{"h4":"(b)(b)  Direct material cost variance"},{"stmt":{"t":"Direct material variances (¢)","rows":[["**Price variance**  (¢3,000 − ¢2,880) × 10,000 kg **purchased**","","1,200,000  **FAVOURABLE**"],["**Usage variance**  (10,000 − 11,000 kg) × ¢3,000 standard price","","3,000,000  **ADVERSE**"],["**Direct material cost variance**","","1,800,000  **ADVERSE**","@tt"]]}},{"h4":"(b)(a)  Direct wages cost variance"},{"stmt":{"t":"Direct labour variances (¢)","rows":[["Standard labour cost of actual production  (1,000 × ¢9,000)","",9000000],["Actual wage bill","",9672000],["**Direct wages cost variance**","","672,000  **ADVERSE**","@t"],"@gap",["**Rate variance**  (¢1,800 − ¢1,860) × 5,200 hours","","312,000  **ADVERSE**"],["**Efficiency variance**  (5,000 − 5,200 hours) × ¢1,800","","360,000  **ADVERSE**"],["**Total**","","672,000  A","@tt"]]}},{"h4":"(b)(c)  Variable production overhead variance"},{"stmt":{"t":"Variable production overhead variances (¢)","rows":[["Standard variable overhead for actual production  (1,000 × ¢3,000)","",3000000],["Actual variable production overhead","",3360000],["**Variable production overhead variance**","","360,000  **ADVERSE**","@t"],"@gap",["**Expenditure variance**  (¢600 × 5,200 hours) − ¢3,360,000","","240,000  **ADVERSE**"],["**Efficiency variance**  (5,000 − 5,200 hours) × ¢600","","120,000  **ADVERSE**"],["**Total**","","360,000  A","@tt"]]}},{"h4":"(b)(i)  Variable production cost variance"},{"stmt":{"t":"Total variable production cost variance (¢)","rows":[["Direct material cost variance","","1,800,000  ADVERSE"],["Direct wages cost variance","","672,000  ADVERSE"],["Variable production overhead variance","","360,000  ADVERSE"],["**Variable production cost variance**","","2,832,000  **ADVERSE**","@tt"]]}},{"key":"Every variance but the material price is adverse, and they share a likely common cause: Alumina was bought 4% below standard price (¢2,880 against ¢3,000), and **11,000 kg were consumed where 10,000 should have sufficed** — a 10% overconsumption. Labour took 5,200 hours against a 5,000-hour standard, and the variable overhead efficiency variance follows automatically from those same extra 200 hours. A cheaper, poorer grade of Alumina that scrapped more and worked harder explains all four at once, and the net effect is a ¢2.83 million loss against a ¢1.2 million purchasing \"saving\"."}]}},{"eg":{"tag":"Exam type — Q13.8 (20 marks)","t":"The relevance of five variances to management, and why variances are interrelated","q":[{"p":"**(a)** Discuss the relevance to management of the following variances, and for each variance give a specific example of how it could arise: (i) direct material usage variance; (ii) direct material price variance; (iii) direct wages cost variance; (iv) direct labour efficiency variance; (v) fixed production overhead expenditure variance *(15 marks)*."},{"p":"**(b)** Briefly explain the statement that \"in standard costing systems, variances are interrelated and should not be considered in isolation\" *(5 marks)*."}],"a":[{"h4":"(a)(i)  Direct material usage variance"},{"p":"**Relevance:** it measures whether **more or less material was consumed than the output should have required**, valued at standard price so that price movements do not contaminate it. It is the responsibility of the **production manager**, and it is the primary indicator of material efficiency — scrap, waste, spoilage, rework and pilferage all show up here. It directs attention to the shop floor, to material quality and to the adequacy of the standard specification."},{"p":"**Specific example:** a batch of timber of a lower grade than specified contains more knots, so 110 planks are consumed to produce the 100 table tops the standard allows for — a 10-plank adverse usage variance at the standard price per plank."},{"h4":"(a)(ii)  Direct material price variance"},{"p":"**Relevance:** it measures whether material was **bought above or below the standard price**, and is the responsibility of the **purchasing manager**. It assesses buying performance, signals changes in market conditions, and warns when the standard price itself has become out of date. It must be read alongside the usage variance, because a favourable price often buys an adverse usage."},{"p":"**Specific example:** the buyer accepts a supplier's offer of a 5% discount for taking a full container-load instead of the usual pallet, producing a favourable price variance — though it also increases inventory holding cost and, if the material deteriorates, the usage variance too."},{"h4":"(a)(iii)  Direct wages cost variance"},{"p":"**Relevance:** it is the **total** labour variance — the difference between the standard labour cost of the actual output and the wages actually paid. On its own it tells management only the overall size of the labour problem, which is why it is always **analysed into its rate and efficiency components** before anything can be done about it: the rate element belongs to personnel and to wage negotiation, the efficiency element to production."},{"p":"**Specific example:** a month in which a negotiated 6% wage award (adverse rate variance) coincides with the new machinery finally running at speed (favourable efficiency variance) may show a small total wages variance that conceals two large and quite separate movements."},{"h4":"(a)(iv)  Direct labour efficiency variance"},{"p":"**Relevance:** it measures whether the output took **more or fewer hours than the standard allows**, valued at the standard rate so that wage-rate changes do not contaminate it. It is the responsibility of the **production manager** and is the main measure of labour productivity. Because variable overhead is usually absorbed on labour hours, it also drives the variable overhead efficiency variance, and under absorption costing the fixed overhead efficiency variance as well."},{"p":"**Specific example:** three experienced operators leave and are replaced by trainees, who take 2,200 hours to complete work with a standard time of 2,000 hours — a 200-hour adverse efficiency variance at the standard rate, with a matching adverse variable overhead efficiency variance behind it."},{"h4":"(a)(v)  Fixed production overhead expenditure variance"},{"p":"**Relevance:** it measures the difference between the **budgeted** fixed production overhead and the **actual** amount spent, and is therefore a pure **spending** measure, unaffected by the level of activity. It is the responsibility of the managers who control the individual overhead items, and it is the only fixed overhead variance that reflects controllable spending — the volume variance reflects only activity. A useful answer notes that it is best analysed further by individual cost item (rent, insurance, supervision, depreciation), since the total alone identifies nothing actionable."},{"p":"**Specific example:** the factory's insurance premium is renewed at ¢4.2 million against the ¢3.8 million budgeted after a claim in the previous year — a ¢400,000 adverse fixed overhead expenditure variance that has nothing to do with the level of production."},{"h4":"(b)  Why variances are interrelated and must not be considered in isolation"},{"p":"A variance identifies **where** a difference arose, not **why**. Very often a single decision or event produces several variances at once, in opposite directions, and judging any one of them alone leads to the wrong conclusion — and sometimes to blaming the wrong manager. Common interrelationships:"},{"ol":["**Material price and material usage.** Buying a cheaper, inferior material gives a favourable price variance and an adverse usage variance — and often an adverse labour efficiency variance too, because the poorer material is harder to work. Praising the buyer for the price saving would be exactly wrong if the net effect is a loss.","**Labour rate and labour efficiency.** Employing a higher grade of labour than standard gives an adverse rate variance but a favourable efficiency variance; employing a lower grade does the reverse. Either may be the better decision overall.","**Labour efficiency and variable (and fixed) overhead efficiency.** Where overhead is absorbed on labour hours, every adverse labour efficiency variance mechanically produces an adverse overhead efficiency variance. The second is not independent evidence of anything.","**Sales price and sales volume.** Cutting the selling price gives an adverse price variance and, if the cut works, a favourable volume variance.","**Material price and inventory decisions.** Bulk buying for a favourable price variance raises holding costs, obsolescence risk and the capital tied up — none of which appear as a variance at all.","**Maintenance expenditure and efficiency.** Cutting planned maintenance gives a favourable fixed overhead expenditure variance this month and adverse labour and overhead efficiency variances, from breakdowns, in later months."]},{"key":"The practical rule follows directly: **investigate variances in related groups, look for a single cause behind several of them, and judge the decision by its net effect on profit** rather than by any individual variance. It also follows that a manager should not be held responsible for a variance that another manager's decision caused — which is the controllability principle applied to variance analysis."}]}},{"eg":{"tag":"Exam type — Q13.9 (12½ marks)","t":"TYT — defining standard costing, and the fixed overhead variances","q":[{"p":"**(a)** Define standard costing *(1 mark)*."},{"p":"**(b)** Mention the variance that is attributed to under- and over-absorption, and interpret the meaning of under-absorption *(3½ marks)*."},{"p":"**(c)** TYT uses the standard costing technique for determining its operational performance. The firm uses an overhead absorption rate to estimate its overhead costs. Relevant data provided are as follows:"},{"stmt":{"t":"Budgeted figures","rows":[["Output","","10,000 units"],["Production hours to utilise","","20,000 hours"],["Fixed production overhead","","N40,000"]]}},{"p":"**Actual figures achieved:** *(see the note below — none are printed in the study text)*"},{"p":"**Required:** **(a)** compute variances for (i) the fixed overhead cost variance; (ii) the components of the fixed overhead cost variance. **(b)** Interpret the variance that identifies under- and over-absorption *(8 marks)*."},{"warn":"The study text prints the heading **\"Actual figures achieved\"** followed by **nothing at all** — no output, no hours and no actual overhead. (Page 410 of the PDF was rendered and inspected directly; the space below the heading is blank.) Part (c) therefore **cannot be answered as printed**. Parts (a) and (b) are answered in full below, the method for (c) is set out, and a plausible set of actual figures is then supplied — **clearly marked as supplied, not as the examiner's** — so the computation can be demonstrated end to end.","lbl":"Data missing from the study text"}],"a":[{"h4":"(a)  Definition of standard costing"},{"def":{"t":"Standard costing","d":"a costing technique in which **predetermined (standard) costs** are established for each unit of output, actual costs are compared with those standards, and the differences — **variances** — are analysed by cause and reported to management for investigation and corrective action."}},{"h4":"(b)  The variance attributed to under- and over-absorption"},{"p":"It is the **fixed production overhead (total) variance** — the difference between the fixed overhead **absorbed** into production at the standard rate and the fixed overhead **actually incurred**. It is identical to the under- or over-absorption of fixed overhead described in §5.7."},{"tex":"\\text{Fixed overhead total variance} = \\text{Fixed overhead absorbed} - \\text{Actual fixed overhead incurred}"},{"p":"**Interpreting under-absorption:** fixed overhead is **under-absorbed** when the amount charged to production at the predetermined rate is **less than the amount actually spent**. It appears as an **adverse** fixed overhead total variance and is written off as an **additional expense** in the costing profit and loss account, reducing profit. It has exactly two possible causes, which the two components separate:"},{"ul":["**actual expenditure exceeded the budget** — the expenditure variance; and/or","**actual activity fell below the level on which the absorption rate was built**, so there were too few units or hours to carry the overhead — the volume variance."]},{"h4":"(c)  The method, and the standard overhead rate"},{"stmt":{"t":"Standard fixed overhead absorption rates","rows":[["Budgeted fixed production overhead","","N40,000"],["÷ budgeted production hours","","20,000 hours"],["**Standard fixed overhead absorption rate**","","**N2.00 per hour**","@t"],"@gap",["Budgeted hours per unit  (20,000 hours ÷ 10,000 units)","","2 hours per unit"],["**Standard fixed overhead per unit**  (2 hours × N2.00)","","**N4.00 per unit**","@t"]]}},{"fbox":{"h":"Fixed production overhead variances","rows":[{"lb":"Total (under/over absorption)","tex":"(\\text{Actual output} \\times \\text{Std FOH per unit}) - \\text{Actual FOH incurred}"},{"lb":"Expenditure variance","tex":"\\text{Budgeted FOH} - \\text{Actual FOH incurred}"},{"lb":"Volume variance","tex":"(\\text{Actual output} - \\text{Budgeted output}) \\times \\text{Std FOH per unit}"},{"lb":"Volume — capacity component","tex":"(\\text{Actual hours} - \\text{Budgeted hours}) \\times \\text{Std FOH rate per hour}"},{"lb":"Volume — efficiency component","tex":"(\\text{Std hours for actual output} - \\text{Actual hours}) \\times \\text{Std FOH rate per hour}"}]}},{"h4":"(c)  Worked demonstration on supplied actual figures"},{"note":"The figures below are **supplied for demonstration only** — they are not in the study text. Assume: **actual output 9,000 units; actual hours worked 19,000; actual fixed production overhead N42,000.**"},{"stmt":{"t":"Fixed overhead variances on the supplied figures (N)","rows":[["Fixed overhead absorbed  (9,000 units × N4.00)","",36000],["Actual fixed overhead incurred","",42000],["**(i) Fixed overhead cost variance (under-absorption)**","","6,000  **ADVERSE**","@t"],"@gap",["**(ii) Components**","","","@hd"],["**Expenditure variance**  budgeted N40,000 − actual N42,000","","2,000  **ADVERSE**"],["**Volume variance**  (9,000 − 10,000 units) × N4.00","","4,000  **ADVERSE**"],["**Total**","","6,000  A","@tt"],"@gap",["*Volume — capacity component*  (19,000 − 20,000 hours) × N2.00","","*2,000  ADVERSE*"],["*Volume — efficiency component*  (18,000 std hrs − 19,000 actual hrs) × N2.00","","*2,000  ADVERSE*"]]}},{"h4":"(c)(b)  Interpretation"},{"p":"The **N6,000 adverse fixed overhead cost variance is the under-absorption**, and the components explain it:"},{"ol":["**N2,000 is an expenditure overspend** — N42,000 was spent against a N40,000 budget. This is the controllable element, and the managers responsible for the individual overhead items should explain it.","**N4,000 is a volume shortfall** — only 9,000 units were produced against the 10,000 on which the N4 per unit rate was built, so 1,000 units' worth of overhead was never charged to production. Splitting it further, **N2,000 is capacity** (1,000 fewer hours were worked than budgeted — the factory was open for less time) and **N2,000 is efficiency** (the 9,000 units should have taken 18,000 hours but took 19,000)."]},{"p":"The whole N6,000 is written off as an **additional expense** in the costing profit and loss account. Had output exceeded 10,000 units and spending come in under N40,000, the variance would have been favourable and the overhead **over-absorbed**, with the excess credited to profit."}]}}]}],"formulas":[{"lb":"Material Cost Variance","tex":"(SQ \\times SP) - (AQ \\times AP)"},{"lb":"Material Price Variance","tex":"AQ \\times (SP - AP)"},{"lb":"Material Usage Variance","tex":"SP \\times (SQ - AQ)"},{"lb":"Labour Cost Variance","tex":"(SH \\times SR) - (AH \\times AR)"},{"lb":"Labour Rate Variance","tex":"AH \\times (SR - AR)"},{"lb":"Labour Efficiency Variance","tex":"SR \\times (SH - AH)"},{"lb":"Variable Overhead Expenditure Variance","tex":"(AH \\times VOR) - \\text{Actual VOH}"},{"lb":"Fixed Overhead Volume Variance","tex":"(SH - \\text{Budgeted hours}) \\times FOAR"},{"lb":"Efficiency ratio","tex":"\\dfrac{SH}{AH} \\times 100"},{"lb":"Activity ratio","tex":"\\dfrac{SH}{\\text{Budgeted hours}} \\times 100"},{"lb":"Capacity ratio","tex":"\\dfrac{AH}{\\text{Budgeted hours}} \\times 100"}],"focus":"Standard costing variance analysis is consistently one of the most heavily-weighted topics in the whole paper. Learn the variance *tree* (cost variance splits into two sub-variances, each level reconciling exactly to the one above) as one structure applied identically to materials, labour, variable overhead and fixed overhead — do not memorise four unrelated formula sets. Always compute the sub-variances and add them back to confirm they match the total — this single habit catches almost every arithmetic slip before it reaches the answer. For sales margin variances, remember marginal costing uses contribution margin throughout; absorption costing uses full profit margin, including a share of fixed overhead.","errors":["Mixing up SQ (standard quantity for the *actual* output achieved) with the *budgeted* quantity — every variance in this chapter is based on actual output, never budgeted output.","Getting the sign convention backwards on a variance — a rise in cost/rate/usage above standard is Adverse; a fall below standard is Favourable, and mixing this up flips every downstream reconciliation.","Forgetting that under marginal costing, only the fixed overhead *expenditure* variance belongs in a profit reconciliation — fixed overhead volume variance has no meaning once fixed cost is treated as a period cost, not absorbed into units.","Using budgeted hours instead of actual hours (or vice versa) in the wrong ratio formula — efficiency uses actual hours in the denominator; activity and capacity both use budgeted hours.","Reporting a variance total without cross-checking that its own sub-variances actually sum to it — the single most reliable way to catch a sign or arithmetic error in this topic."],"quiz":{"mcq":[{"q":"Standard quantity for a product is 4kg at ¢500/kg. Actual usage was 4,500kg for 1,000 units produced, at an actual price of ¢520/kg. The material usage variance is","o":["250,000 Favourable","250,000 Adverse","90,000 Adverse","90,000 Favourable","500,000 Adverse"],"a":1,"w":"Standard quantity for 1,000 units = 4,000kg. Usage variance = SP × (SQ − AQ) = 500 × (4,000 − 4,500) = −250,000, i.e. 250,000 Adverse.","calc":"500 \\times (4{,}000 - 4{,}500) = -250{,}000","src":"Chapter 13.4","sec":"13.4"},{"q":"Standard labour rate is ¢2,000/hour. 5,000 hours were actually worked at a total cost of ¢11,000,000. The labour rate variance is","o":["1,000,000 Adverse","1,000,000 Favourable","10,000,000 Adverse","9,000,000 Favourable","2,000,000 Adverse"],"a":0,"w":"Actual rate = 11,000,000/5,000 = ¢2,200/hr. Rate variance = AH × (SR − AR) = 5,000 × (2,000 − 2,200) = −1,000,000, i.e. 1,000,000 Adverse.","calc":"5{,}000 \\times (2{,}000 - 2{,}200) = -1{,}000{,}000","src":"Chapter 13.4","sec":"13.4"},{"q":"A material price variance of ¢50,000 Favourable and a material usage variance of ¢80,000 Adverse together give a material cost variance of","o":["130,000 Adverse","30,000 Adverse","30,000 Favourable","130,000 Favourable","50,000 Adverse"],"a":1,"w":"Cost variance = Price variance + Usage variance = 50,000 F − 80,000 A = −30,000, i.e. 30,000 Adverse.","calc":"50{,}000 - 80{,}000 = -30{,}000","src":"Chapter 13.4","sec":"13.4"},{"q":"The fixed overhead volume variance measures","o":["the difference between actual and budgeted spending on fixed overheads","the over- or under-absorption of overhead caused by actual output differing from budgeted output","the difference between standard and actual labour rates","the material price variance only","a variance that only arises under marginal costing"],"a":1,"w":"Per CIMA's own definition, quoted in the chapter, the volume variance measures over-or under-absorption of overhead caused by actual production volume differing from the budget — spending itself is captured separately by the expenditure variance.","src":"Chapter 13.4","sec":"13.4"},{"q":"Under a standard MARGINAL costing system, which fixed overhead variance is used in reconciling budgeted profit to actual profit?","o":["Only the volume variance","Only the capacity variance","Only the expenditure variance","All of expenditure, volume, efficiency and capacity","None — fixed overhead variances are never used under marginal costing"],"a":2,"w":"Under marginal costing, fixed cost is a period cost (never absorbed into units), so only the fixed overhead expenditure variance is meaningful for the reconciliation — volume (and its sub-variances) only make sense under absorption costing.","src":"Chapter 13.5","sec":"13.5"},{"q":"Budgeted hours are 50,000, standard hours for actual output are 44,000, and actual hours worked are 40,000. The efficiency ratio is","o":["88%","80%","110%","90.9%","113.6%"],"a":2,"w":"Efficiency ratio = SH/AH × 100 = 44,000/40,000 × 100 = 110%.","calc":"\\dfrac{44{,}000}{40{,}000} \\times 100 = 110\\%","src":"Chapter 13.6","sec":"13.6"},{"q":"Using the same data (budgeted 50,000hrs, standard 44,000hrs, actual 40,000hrs), the activity ratio is","o":["110%","80%","88%","90.9%","125%"],"a":2,"w":"Activity ratio = SH/Budgeted hours × 100 = 44,000/50,000 × 100 = 88%.","calc":"\\dfrac{44{,}000}{50{,}000} \\times 100 = 88\\%","src":"Chapter 13.6","sec":"13.6"},{"q":"A standard is set for use over a long period, remaining unchanged over several years, and is used mainly to show long-term trends rather than to highlight current efficiency. This describes a","o":["current standard","ideal standard","attainable standard","basic standard","flexible standard"],"a":3,"w":"A basic standard is set for long-term use, remaining unchanged over several years — useful for showing trends, but explicitly not able to highlight current efficiency or inefficiency.","src":"Chapter 13.1","sec":"13.1"},{"q":"Which factor makes a variance MORE likely to be investigated, all else equal?","o":["It recurs very frequently, following a well-known pattern","It is immaterial in size","It is unusual and has not been seen before","The standard it is measured against is known to be unreliable","The cost of investigating clearly exceeds the benefit"],"a":2,"w":"An unusual, non-recurring variance is more likely to be investigated than one with a frequent, well-understood history — the opposite of options A, B, D and E, each of which points toward *not* investigating.","src":"Chapter 13.7","sec":"13.7"}],"theory":[{"q":"What is a variance? Identify and discuss briefly the two basic causes of variances.","marks":6,"a":[{"p":"A variance is the difference between the standard level of performance that ought to have been achieved under given working conditions, and the actual level achieved for that same output."},{"p":"**Two basic causes**: (i) using more or less quantity of material or labour hours per unit of output than expected — from using superior/inferior inputs, or more/less efficient use of them; and (ii) paying a higher or lower rate per unit of material or labour than expected — influenced by input quality and market conditions."}],"src":"Chapter 13.4","sec":"13.4"},{"q":"Outline the factors that should be considered when deciding whether or not to investigate variances revealed in a standard costing and budgetary control system.","marks":5,"a":[{"ol":["the **history** of the variance — a recurring, well-understood deviation is less likely to need investigation than an unusual one;","the **reliability of the standard** — a more reliable standard raises the chance any deviation from it is worth investigating;","the **significance (materiality)** of the deviation — immaterial variances are less likely to be investigated;","a **cost-benefit** comparison — investigate only where the benefit exceeds the cost of investigating; and","the **effect on future planning and control** — even a costly investigation can be worthwhile if it genuinely improves future operations."]}],"src":"Chapter 13.7","sec":"13.7"}]}},{"n":14,"t":"Recent Developments in Cost Accounting","brief":"A survey of modern approaches that have reshaped cost accounting beyond the traditional techniques of earlier chapters: JIT and Kanban as inventory philosophies, backflush accounting as their costing counterpart, life cycle and target costing as design-stage cost thinking, Advanced Manufacturing Technology, lean and throughput accounting, data analytics, and the newest frontier — sustainability, environmental/carbon-footprint accounting, cloud-based systems, and blockchain.","outcomes":["Explain Just-in-Time (JIT) systems","Explain Kanban inventory control","Explain backflush accounting","Explain life cycle costing","Explain target costing","Explain Advanced Manufacturing Technique (AMT)","Explain lean accounting","Explain throughput accounting","Explain data analytics, predictive analytics, and cost transparency","Explain sustainability cost accounting and sustainable cost management","Explain environmental cost accounting and carbon footprint accounting","Explain cloud-based solutions and cost accounting: automation and integration","Explain blockchain technology and cost accounting: transparency, security and smart contracts"],"secs":[{"n":"14.1","t":"Just-in-Time (JIT)","b":[{"p":"JIT was developed in Japan and is widely credited as a major contributor to that country's manufacturing success. As its name suggests, it seeks to **eliminate the need to keep inventories**, saving the costs of holding them — the overall objective is to produce or procure inventory right at the time it is required, without compromising quality."},{"h4":"The main goals of JIT"},{"ul":["No opening or closing inventory — items are purchased or produced \"just in time,\" in the required quantities and quality;","Elimination of non-value-adding activities;","No production wastages;","100% on-time deliveries;","Batch sizes of one;","Demand-pull manufacture — the production chain is activated in real time by demand;","No breakdowns; and","Short set-ups."]},{"key":"**Demand-pull** is the defining idea behind JIT: production is *triggered* by a confirmed customer order, rather than goods being made ahead of time in anticipation of demand and held in stock until sold."},{"h4":"The two branches of JIT costing"},{"ol":["**Just-in-time purchasing** — arrangements with reliable suppliers for regular, frequent deliveries exactly as and when required, eliminating the need to tie down capital in inventory. This saves the costs of keeping both raw material inventory and work in progress, saves factory space, and cuts the paperwork associated with large, long-term orders. Material procurement is executed strictly in line with what production actually requires.","**Just-in-time production** — works on the same demand-pull principle. A successful JIT production system depends on two assurances: (a) prompt delivery by the supplier at the exact time required, and (b) 100% quality, with zero rejects. Within the shortest possible time after an order is received, the material supplier is notified and the order executed, shortening the production set-up time."]},{"h4":"Limitations of JIT"},{"ul":["Lack of flexibility, which makes it vulnerable to disruptions in the supply chain; and","Difficulty in predicting demand patterns."]},{"note":"JIT trades the *safety* of holding buffer stock for the *cost savings* of holding none — which is exactly why a single supply disruption or a demand forecast that proves wrong can hit a JIT operation much harder than a business holding normal buffer inventory."}]},{"n":"14.2","t":"Kanban inventory control","b":[{"p":"Where supply time is lengthy and demand is hard to forecast, the best a business can do is respond quickly to *observed* demand — this is achieved using the Kanban system."},{"def":{"t":"Kanban","d":"a variant of JIT, one step ahead of it — used as a demand signal that spreads through the supply chain, aiming to make supply processes as lean as possible. It is a concept that attempts to maintain minimum inventory: it involves more than fine-tuning production and supplier scheduling systems, entailing the minimisation of inventories by supplying them only when needed in production, together with close monitoring of work in progress."}},{"p":"Under Kanban, the supplier or warehouse delivers components to the production line as and when they are needed, so that there is no storage in the production area. Kanban follows strict rules of use, and close monitoring of those rules is a never-ending task, done to ensure Kanban actually delivers what is required."},{"h4":"Electronic Kanban (e-Kanban)"},{"p":"Kanban can be implemented electronically. Basic features of e-Kanban operations include:"},{"ul":["Elimination of common manual entry errors;","Provision of quick and precise information;","Quick response to changes;","Avoidance of over-production;","Minimisation of waste; and","Maintenance of low costs associated with information transfer."]},{"p":"Traditionally, Kanban has been used to control the amount of inventory held on the production line, making the manufacturing process quicker through on-time delivery of inventory from the warehouse to production. Used in *warehouses* to aid just-in-time replenishment of pick bins, it takes JIT one step further, extending the same principle into warehouse replenishment as well as production."},{"h4":"Benefits of Kanban inventory control"},{"ul":["Warehouses do not overstock goods;","Suppliers have an instantaneous picture of inventory levels held;","The process moves from stocking goods to perpetual inventory;","Visibility of inventory movement is available to management, operators and suppliers alike, and processes are as lean as possible;","Inventories are delivered when needed;","Integrating e-Kanban into warehouse management systems allows real-time demand signalling across the supply chain and improved visibility;","Data pulled from Kanban systems can be used to optimise inventory levels; and","It aids better tracking of suppliers' lead and replenishment times."]}]},{"n":"14.3","t":"Backflush accounting (backflush costing)","b":[{"def":{"t":"Backflush Accounting (CIMA)","d":"a method of costing, associated with a JIT production system, which applies cost to the output of a process. Costs do not mirror the flow of products through the production process, but are attached to output produced (finished goods inventory and cost of sales), on the assumption that such backflushed costs are a realistic measure of the actual costs incurred."}},{"p":"In short, backflush accounting is a simpler cost-accounting system designed to reduce or eliminate detailed accounting entries. Instead of tracking material movement through stores and production in detail, it starts from finished goods and works **backwards**, attributing costs between cost of goods sold and finished goods inventory (and/or materials inventory), with no separate accounting for WIP and stores. The reduction in accounting entries reduces clerical cost — the exact number of entries required depends on the nature and complexity of the production process."},{"h4":"Backflush costing entries"},{"table":{"align":"lll","head":["When...","Debit","Credit"],"rows":[["Raw materials are purchased","Raw-in-Progress account","Creditor for raw materials, or Bank"],["Conversion cost is incurred","Conversion cost control account","Wages payable account"],["Goods are sold (closes finished goods to cost of sales)","Cost of sales account","Finished goods account"]]}},{"key":"Note there is deliberately **no separate WIP or stores account** — that is the entire point of backflush accounting: since JIT keeps inventory levels close to zero, tracking material through every stage in detail no longer earns its clerical cost, so the system \"flushes\" cost straight through to output instead."}]},{"n":"14.4","t":"Life cycle costing","b":[{"def":{"t":"Life cycle costing","d":"capturing the total \"life cycle\" cost of a product — all costs incurred on it from \"cradle to grave\" (or \"womb to tomb\"): research and development, plant and equipment, manufacturing costs, product development costs, and promotion costs."}},{"p":"The purpose of life cycle costing is to ensure the firm recovers **all** of these costs over the estimated number of units expected to be sold across the product's life — not just its immediate manufacturing cost. It helps a firm compete favourably on pricing, especially when similar competing products are introduced."},{"note":"Life cycle costing matters precisely because a product's cost is not just what it costs to make one unit today — heavy up-front R&D and development costs, spread across the product's *entire* expected sales volume, can make an apparently cheap-to-manufacture product actually loss-making unless priced with the full life cycle in view."}]},{"n":"14.5","t":"Target costing","b":[{"def":{"t":"Target costing","d":"the process whereby new products are designed to fit the unit prices that expected potential customers can afford or would offer."}},{"def":{"t":"Target cost (CIMA)","d":"a product cost estimate derived by subtracting a desired profit margin from a competitive market price."}},{"tex":"\\text{Target Cost} = \\text{Competitive Market Price} - \\text{Desired Profit Margin}"},{"p":"Target costing is widely used in Japan and is gradually gaining ground in Europe and the USA. It is a **market-driven** approach: the target selling price is determined first, focused on gaining the desired market share, and the required profit margin is then deducted from it to arrive at the target cost."},{"key":"This is the reverse of traditional cost-plus pricing, which starts from cost and adds a margin to reach a price. Target costing starts from the *price the market will bear* and works backward to find what cost the product **must** be designed to hit."},{"p":"Emphasis is laid on the **design stage** to achieve the target cost, because design is considered to consume the major chunk of a product's costs before it is even released to manufacturing. Product designers, purchasing and manufacturing specialists therefore work together to determine the product and process features that will let the long-run target cost actually be achieved."}]},{"n":"14.6","t":"Advanced Manufacturing Technology (AMT)","b":[{"p":"Traditional production methods have been criticised by modern accountants for: their obsolescence; accommodating and rewarding inefficiencies; and lacking incentives for improvement."},{"def":{"t":"Advanced Manufacturing Technology (AMT)","d":"a general term for manufacturing techniques that differ from traditional production methods, developed to replace them."}},{"p":"AMT encompasses techniques such as:"},{"ul":["Computer Aided Design and Manufacturing (CAD/CAM);","Flexible Manufacturing System (FMS);","Total Quality Management (TQM);","Material Resources Planning (MRP);","Just-In-Time (JIT); and others."]},{"p":"AMT techniques promise effectiveness and a healthy ability to compete — producing high-quality goods at low cost, thereby maximising customer satisfaction. In short, AMT aids effective competition in a technologically driven world."}]},{"n":"14.7","t":"Lean accounting","b":[{"def":{"t":"Lean accounting","d":"a methodology that integrates accounting practices with the principles of lean manufacturing and lean thinking — emphasising the elimination of waste, improvement of efficiency, and enhancement of customer value. Unlike traditional accounting, which may not fully support lean principles, lean accounting aims to simplify financial reporting and provide relevant performance metrics that aid decision-making."}},{"h4":"Key components of lean accounting"},{"ol":["**Simplified financial reporting** — making financial statements easier to understand and relevant to specific business operations, by streamlining information flow and accounting procedures.","**Value stream costing** — instead of traditional product costing methods, lean accounting focuses on value streams, encompassing all actions required to bring a product from concept to customer.","**Lean culture** — a culture of continuous improvement within the accounting department itself, encouraging teamwork and effective communication to raise overall productivity.","**Lean tools** — various techniques used to reduce waste and improve efficiency within the accounting function, aligning financial management with the company's wider lean strategies."]},{"h4":"Benefits of lean accounting"},{"ul":["**Enhanced decision-making** — clearer, more relevant financial information supports better decisions;","**Increased efficiency** — streamlined accounting operations, reducing unnecessary steps and focusing on value-added activities;","**Alignment with business goals** — financial practices stay closely tied to the organisation's overall goals, promoting a unified approach to operational excellence."]}]},{"n":"14.8","t":"Throughput accounting","b":[{"p":"Businesses use throughput accounting as a mechanism to plan production, with the purpose of **maximising profit**. It applies specifically when there is a **bottleneck** (a constraint) limiting production of multiple products."},{"def":{"t":"Bottleneck","d":"the material, labour, machine hours, or any other factor that limits production capacity."}},{"p":"Throughput accounting is applicable only when both of the following hold:"},{"ol":["the business has a certain bottleneck; and","the business produces more than one product, and these products use the bottleneck resource in different proportions."]},{"h4":"Working it through, step by step"},{"steps":["Identify the bottleneck resource — this requires a detailed analysis of the production facility; the scarce resource can be labour hours, machine hours, raw material, or any other factor.","Calculate the contribution per unit of each product — deduct all variable costs from revenue.","Divide contribution per unit by the amount of scarce resource each product uses — this gives the **throughput value** per unit of scarce resource.","Rank the throughput values from highest to lowest — the highest-ranked product uses the least scarce resource to generate the most contribution, so priority in the production plan goes to it first, subject always to that product's own maximum demand."]},{"key":"This is structurally the **same limiting-factor ranking logic** used in marginal costing decision-making (Chapter 9) — contribution per unit of scarce resource, ranked highest to lowest. Throughput accounting formalises that same idea into its own dedicated ratio and terminology."},{"h4":"The throughput accounting ratio"},{"fbox":{"h":"Throughput accounting ratio","rows":[{"lb":"Throughput value","tex":"\\dfrac{\\text{Contribution}}{\\text{Scarce resource unit}}"},{"lb":"Fixed cost per unit","tex":"\\dfrac{\\text{Total fixed cost}}{\\text{Total production}}"},{"lb":"Throughput Accounting Ratio","tex":"\\dfrac{\\text{Throughput value}}{\\text{Fixed cost per unit}}"}]}},{"note":"If the ratio computes to **more than 1**, the throughput value is able to cover fixed cost. If it is **less than 1**, throughput value does not cover the business's fixed expenses — a signal that making the product, at least on current terms, may not be viable."},{"h4":"Throughput accounting and the theory of constraints"},{"p":"The **theory of constraints** describes the situation where limiting factors (the same bottlenecks/scarce resources described above) halt or restrict production, and the production plan must be managed in line with those constraints. Throughput accounting supplies the calculation method used to plan production effectively within this framework."},{"h4":"Throughput accounting vs. cost accounting"},{"p":"Throughput accounting is narrowly focused on maximising utilisation of scarce resources and achieving higher profitability. Cost accounting is the broader discipline, covering the operational aspects of overall cost, price, production, contribution, cost behaviour, cost analysis, process accounting and manufacturing."},{"h4":"Advantages and disadvantages"},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Advantages","Disadvantages"],"rows":[["Helps formulate the best possible production plan, maximising utilisation of scarce resources and profit.","Can be difficult to understand the concept of throughput accounting."],["Helps closely understand contribution, production procedures, and fixed/variable cost behaviour.","Not easy to compile small details like variable and fixed production elements."],["Can be applied quickly — flexible in application and greatly helps decision making.","Only applicable when there are bottleneck resources and multiple products."],["Rational — considers product demand while making the production plan.","A short-term approach to profitability that does not consider long-term objectives that might be achieved by a different product mix than throughput suggests."],["—","Application becomes more complicated where there are multiple bottlenecks."]]}}]},{"n":"14.9","t":"Data analytics, predictive analytics, and cost transparency","b":[{"def":{"t":"Data analytics","d":"the process of examining raw data to draw meaningful insights that can inform decision-making and drive business strategies — collecting, transforming and organising data to uncover patterns, trends and insights, drawing on statistics, mathematics and computer science."}},{"h4":"The four types of data analytics"},{"table":{"align":"lll","head":["Type","Question it answers","What it does"],"rows":[["**Descriptive**","\"What happened?\"","Summarises past data to identify trends and patterns, using techniques like data aggregation and mining."],["**Diagnostic**","\"Why did it happen?\"","Investigates anomalies and identifies the causes behind certain trends or events."],["**Predictive**","\"What is likely to happen in the future?\"","Uses historical data to forecast future outcomes."],["**Prescriptive**","\"What should we do?\"","Recommends actions based on data analysis to achieve desired outcomes."]]}},{"key":"These four types build on each other in a natural sequence — descriptive establishes *what* happened, diagnostic explains *why*, predictive projects forward to what is *likely* to happen, and prescriptive closes the loop by recommending *what to do about it*."},{"p":"Data analytics is essential for optimising performance, improving decision-making and gaining a competitive edge — letting businesses better understand customers, enhance operational efficiency, and identify new growth opportunities, driven by data rather than intuition or guesswork alone."},{"h4":"Cost transparency"},{"def":{"t":"Cost transparency","d":"the practice of disclosing the costs associated with products, services or activities to relevant stakeholders — customers, suppliers, employees and investors — making costs visible and understandable to enhance trust and decision-making."}},{"p":"Why cost transparency matters:"},{"ol":["**Informed decision-making** — stakeholders can make better choices with access to clear, accurate cost information (customers can compare prices and value; suppliers can negotiate on transparent cost structures).","**Trust and accountability** — openly sharing cost information builds trust with customers and partners, and fosters accountability, since stakeholders can hold each other responsible for actions and results.","**Enhanced efficiency** — organisations can identify areas for cost reduction and optimise resource allocation, improving financial performance."]},{"p":"Benefits by stakeholder group:"},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Stakeholder","Benefit"],"rows":[["Customers","Detailed cost breakdowns enhance satisfaction and loyalty — customers are more confident purchasing when they understand what they are paying for."],["Suppliers","Can align operations with customer expectations and negotiate fairer contracts, enabling collaborative efforts to reduce cost and improve quality."],["Companies","Improved market competitiveness — research shows consumers are more likely to purchase products when they understand the costs involved in production."]]}},{"p":"Challenges in achieving cost transparency: complex financial systems (disparate systems that make clear cost data hard to obtain), data quality issues (poor data quality and lack of methodological rigour), and resistance to change (internal resistance to new transparency practices, especially where they require significant process changes)."}]},{"n":"14.10","t":"Sustainability cost accounting and sustainable cost management","b":[{"p":"Sustainable Cost Accounting (SCA) has attracted increasing interest from both business practitioners and policymakers, driven by growing awareness of the need to include environmental perspectives in financial reporting. Traditional accounting systems essentially relate to financial success and, in many cases, do not allow consideration of the environmental or social costs that arise from a corporate operation."},{"def":{"t":"Sustainable Cost Accounting (SCA)","d":"accounting that ensures an enterprise accounts for the financial impact of its ecological footprint — carbon emissions, resource utilisation, ecosystem destruction — taking a more holistic approach that considers **externalities**: costs or advantages not well reflected in the market price of a commodity."}},{"h4":"Why sustainable cost management matters"},{"p":"Cost management directly impacts profitability and long-term sustainability. Sustainable cost management aims to strike a balance between financial efficiency and responsible resource usage, ensuring long-term viability for both the business and the planet — reducing carbon footprint, minimising waste, and conserving natural resources (e.g. energy-efficient technologies, recycling/reuse, optimised transportation), which often also delivers direct cost savings. It also strengthens reputation and brand image with an increasingly sustainability-conscious customer base."},{"h4":"Key principles of sustainable cost management"},{"ol":["**Identify and prioritise cost-saving opportunities** — through a thorough analysis of operations, focusing effort where the greatest potential for cost savings aligns with sustainability goals.","**Implement sustainable procurement practices** — considering the environmental and social impact of procurement decisions, not just their direct cost.","**Foster a culture of cost-consciousness and sustainability** — through employee education and engagement programmes.","**Continuously monitor and measure cost-saving initiatives** — via key performance indicators (KPIs) tied to both cost reduction and sustainability.","**Collaborate with stakeholders and share best practices** — with suppliers, customers and industry peers, to collectively drive industry-wide improvement."]},{"h4":"Benefits of sustainable cost management"},{"ul":["**Increased profitability** — eliminating wasteful practices reduces costs directly;","**Enhanced brand reputation** — attracts environmentally and socially conscious consumers;","**Reduced risk and increased resilience** — diversified supply chains reduce dependence on limited resources, cushioning disruption;","**Compliance with regulations** — proactively integrating sustainability avoids penalties and legal issues tied to non-compliance;","**Employee engagement and retention** — purposeful, values-aligned work attracts and retains talent."]},{"h4":"Challenges in sustainable cost management, and how they are overcome"},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Challenge","How it is typically overcome"],"rows":[["Resistance to change","Communicate the benefits effectively (cost savings, efficiency, environmental impact); involve employees in decision-making; provide training."],["Limited budget and resources","Start with low-cost/no-cost initiatives that deliver immediate benefit, then gradually reallocate the savings into larger initiatives."],["Lack of data and metrics","Invest in data collection/management systems to generate reliable insight into energy consumption, waste, and other sustainability metrics."],["Supplier collaboration","Establish clear sustainability criteria for suppliers; maintain regular communication; share success stories to motivate adoption."],["Regulatory compliance","Stay current with changing regulation; engage with regulatory bodies and industry associations; use sustainability certifications to demonstrate commitment."]]}},{"h4":"Tools and technologies for sustainable cost management"},{"ol":["**Cost tracking software** — monitors and analyses expenses in real time, identifying overspending or waste.","**Energy management systems** — smart meters and building automation systems that monitor and control energy consumption.","**Life Cycle Assessment (LCA) tools** — assess environmental impact across a product's entire life cycle (raw material extraction, manufacturing, transport, use, disposal).","**Supply chain management software** — gives visibility across the whole supply chain, optimising logistics and reducing waste.","**Data analytics and business intelligence tools** — surface operational inefficiencies and cost drivers from large datasets.","**Green building design tools** — simulate and optimise the environmental performance of buildings at the design stage.","**Cost benchmarking tools** — compare a business's costs and performance against industry peers."]},{"h4":"The role of leadership"},{"p":"Effective leadership is central to driving sustainable cost management — setting the tone for the whole organisation. Key leadership actions:"},{"ol":["**Championing a culture of sustainability** — integrating it into company values and mission.","**Setting clear goals and targets** — providing a roadmap the organisation can follow and be held accountable to.","**Empowering employees** — fostering a collaborative environment that taps the workforce's collective knowledge and creativity.","**Investing in training and education** — equipping employees with the tools to identify cost-saving opportunities and implement sustainable practices.","**Monitoring and reporting progress** — robust mechanisms that track progress, identify areas for improvement, and foster accountability through transparency."]}]},{"n":"14.11","t":"Environmental cost accounting and carbon footprint accounting","b":[{"def":{"t":"Environmental accounting","d":"organisations using conventional accounting and finance principles to factor environmental costs into their business results — helping them manage and understand the trade-offs between conventional environmental and economic goals, and making them more accountable and transparent."}},{"p":"Businesses typically carry out this accounting through three methods: **managerial**, **financial**, and **national income** accounting. Implementation is best served by senior management support and cross-functional teams (spanning sales, finance, procurement and manufacturing) to administer the procedure."},{"h4":"Objectives of environmental accounting"},{"ul":["Fostering stakeholder participation and engagement in environmental decision-making;","Encouraging accountability and transparency in managing environmental effects and natural-resource use;","Offering a detailed view of economic activities' true benefits and costs, incorporating social and environmental considerations;","Supporting the switch to a green economy and promoting sustainable development; and","Integrating environmental benefits and costs into decision-making procedures and national accounts."]},{"h4":"Types of environmental accounting"},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Type","Focus"],"rows":[["**Environmental Management Accounting**","Resource management and internal decision-making — identifying, analysing and measuring environmental costs and benefits, and feeding the data into management systems to enhance environmental performance."],["**Sustainability Accounting**","Measuring and reporting an organisation's social, environmental and economic performance (energy/resource indicators, social impact assessments, greenhouse gas emissions), offering a holistic view of sustainable development and promoting stakeholder engagement."],["**Full Cost Accounting**","Computing a product or service's overall social, environmental and economic costs, including hidden charges normally excluded from financial accounting — enabling a more detailed assessment of true production/consumption cost."],["**Environmental National Accounting**","A national-level process, focusing on externality costs, environmental costs and more."],["**Environmental Financial Accounting**","Focuses specifically on reporting environmental liability costs alongside other environmental costs."]]}},{"h4":"Advantages and disadvantages"},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Advantages","Disadvantages"],"rows":[["Encourages innovation and enhances resource efficiency.","Non-recognition of the expenditure a company incurs to protect the environment."],["Discloses details of natural-resource utilisation — the cost of resources used and the income generated by using them.","Inconsistent treatment of natural capital: the national accounts system treats gradual depletion of a physical asset (equipment, a plant) as depreciation, yet treats exhaustion of natural capital (e.g. a forest) as income."],["Helps gauge overall contributions made by companies toward their social responsibilities.","No clear, universally agreed definition of \"environmental costs,\" so companies are inconsistent in reporting and monitoring them."],["Offers environmental protection by helping organisations become more environmentally friendly.","—"],["Improves customer loyalty and enhances corporate reputation.","—"],["Helps organisations follow government-set environmental regulations.","—"]]}},{"h4":"Conventional accounting vs. environmental accounting"},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Conventional Accounting","Environmental Accounting"],"rows":[["Deals with the economic unit's physical/financial aspects.","Deals with the relationship between environmental and financial aspects."],["Objective: to save money and time against serious business mistakes.","Objective: to determine the favourable and unfavourable environmental effects of an organisation's actions."],["Output: management accounting reports and financial statements.","Output: environmental reports."]]}},{"h4":"Elements and characteristics of environmental accounting"},{"p":"Elements: environmental management accounting; life cycle assessment; environmental costs and benefits; carbon footprinting; environmental performance indicators; and environmental auditing."},{"p":"Basic characteristics: identification of costs; comprehensive data collection; performance tracking and benchmarking; stakeholder engagement; meeting environmental regulations; risk evaluation; merger with financial accounting; and identification of benefits."},{"p":"Environmental accounting has both **internal** and **external** functions: internal systems accumulate ecological information (expressed in physical units) for management's own use; external systems accumulate and disclose information for outside stakeholders with an interest in environmental issues."},{"h4":"Carbon footprint accounting"},{"def":{"t":"Carbon footprint","d":"the collective greenhouse gas emissions caused by enterprises, organisations, activities, products, or individuals — used as a measure of the extent of their global warming impact."}},{"p":"Nearly three-quarters of greenhouse gas emissions derive from energy consumption, and the manufacturing industry is a core focus for achieving \"dual-carbon\" (carbon peak and carbon neutrality) goals. The main carbon-footprint accounting methods include:"},{"ul":["Input-Output Analysis (IOA);","Life Cycle Assessment (LCA);","the IPCC method; and","the Kaya carbon emission identity equation."]},{"p":"All the data feeding these methods is collectively called **carbon data**, characterised by uncertainty, dynamics, and coupling — properties that significantly affect the reliability of carbon footprint accounting results."},{"note":"The Life Cycle Assessment (LCA) method analyses simple scenarios under fixed assumptions well, but is less reliable for the complex, dynamic processes of real production — a manufacturing process is also disturbed by things like equipment failure, delayed or incorrect material delivery, and urgent orders, all of which change a product's carbon footprint **dynamically**, not just statically, over its life cycle."},{"p":"Since carbon data of different sources and types are coupled and cannot be used directly for product carbon-footprint accounting, **allocation** is required. The ISO (International Organization for Standardization) provides three methods for this inventory-data allocation, applied in order of preference:"},{"ol":["Wherever possible, use subdivision or system-expansion methods to avoid allocation issues entirely;","If allocation issues cannot be avoided, use methods based on the physical (chemical or biological) relationships between inventory data; and","If neither of the above is feasible, use allocation approaches based on other methods, such as economic value."]},{"key":"Allocation is widely regarded, in the research literature this section draws on, as one of the most debated issues in Life Cycle Assessment — it is genuinely difficult to identify a single \"correct\" allocation method, and reducing the uncertainty it introduces (alongside the broader challenge of scarce, costly-to-obtain carbon data) remains an active area of research."}]},{"n":"14.12","t":"Cloud-based solutions and cost accounting: automation and integration","b":[{"def":{"t":"Cloud-based solutions","d":"services and resources accessed over the internet rather than hosted on local servers or personal computers — allowing users to store, manage and process data remotely, with significant advantages in accessibility and efficiency."}},{"h4":"The three categories of cloud solutions"},{"table":{"align":"lll","head":["Category","What it provides","Examples"],"rows":[["**Infrastructure as a Service (IaaS)**","Virtualised computing resources over the internet — servers, storage and networking rented without investing in physical hardware.","Google Compute Engine, Microsoft Azure"],["**Platform as a Service (PaaS)**","A platform for developers to build, deploy and manage applications without dealing with the underlying infrastructure, streamlining development and enhancing productivity.","—"],["**Software as a Service (SaaS)**","Software applications delivered over the internet, eliminating the need for local installation and maintenance.","Google Workspace, Salesforce"]]}},{"h4":"Benefits of cloud-based solutions"},{"ul":["**Cost efficiency** — organisations avoid the cost of purchasing/maintaining hardware, paying instead for the services used, often on a subscription basis;","**Scalability** — resources can be scaled up or down easily based on demand, so businesses only pay for what they need;","**Flexibility and accessibility** — services can be accessed from anywhere with an internet connection, facilitating remote work and collaboration;","**Enhanced security** — many providers offer robust security measures, including data encryption and regular backups, that can be more effective than traditional on-premises solutions."]},{"h4":"Deployment models"},{"ul":["**Public cloud** — services offered over the public internet and shared among multiple users, cost-effective and scalable;","**Private cloud** — dedicated to a single organisation, providing greater control and security, often necessary for businesses with strict compliance requirements;","**Hybrid cloud** — combines public and private clouds, keeping sensitive data private while leveraging public-cloud scalability for less-critical operations."]}]},{"n":"14.13","t":"Blockchain technology and cost accounting: transparency, security and smart contracts","b":[{"p":"Distributed ledger technology — blockchain — has challenged current ways of doing business, including a significant shift in the role of the cost accountant. Because cost accounting functions are closely connected to an organisation's transactions (translated into monetary values), they are affected by any new technology that changes how those transactions are conducted. If cost accountants do not understand the change, they cannot understand the controls embedded in the technology or trace financial transactions recorded on the blockchain — meaning they cannot correctly perform most of their work."},{"key":"The **basics** of cost accounting are not changed by blockchain adoption — but blockchain adds new **risks to consider and controls to test**, so cost accountants must still learn it and build it into their functions."},{"p":"Two assertions cost accountants should give assurance on under blockchain: the **completeness** of recording all transactions, and the **existence** of the costs of recorded transactions. Completeness is achieved when all transactions occurring during the period are presented in the accounts — an assertion that can be duly verified. For example, an entity's cryptocurrency use can be verified by reviewing its transaction history on the blockchain, and existence can be verified by tracing a transaction hash to identify the receiving party and confirming the asset resides where described. Because this data is independently verified across the network rather than supplied by one party alone, it can be considered more trustworthy than traditional single-party data."},{"h4":"Smart contracts"},{"def":{"t":"Smart contract","d":"code reflecting the terms and conditions of a contract between an entity and a supplier, which executes itself without any human intervention."}},{"p":"Smart contracts work by following simple \"if/when...then...\" statements written into code on a blockchain — when predetermined conditions are met, a network of computers executes the actions specified (e.g. automatically releasing funds to a party once a service is confirmed complete). For operations, a cost accountant may review the flow of procedures by tracing internal transactions on the blockchain during a period, and may test for governance issues by verifying who can create, verify, and review transactions."},{"h4":"Key characteristics of smart contracts"},{"ol":["**Self-executing** — automatically enforce and execute the terms of the agreement without human intervention;","**Immutable** — once deployed on the blockchain, cannot be altered, so the terms remain unchanged;","**Distributed** — all parties on the network have access to the contract, enhancing transparency and trust;","**Deterministic** — the outcome is consistent and predictable, executing the same way every time under the same conditions."]},{"p":"Smart contracts are commonly associated with cryptocurrencies, particularly Ethereum (which introduced a robust framework for building complex contracts using a Turing-complete programming language), and are fundamental to decentralised finance (DeFi) applications, non-fungible tokens (NFTs), and other blockchain-based solutions."},{"h4":"How blockchain changes accounting risk"},{"p":"Traditional accounting practices can allow payments to be made to fake suppliers or fraudulent bank accounts — for example, a fraudster posing as a supplier and sending an invoice with their own account number, or hacking a supplier's email system to redirect invoices and bank details. Blockchain removes much of this risk because all payments are verified by the supplier and recorded in a block with a transaction hash for later reference; both the supplier and the organisation can see the full history of their transactions on the network, giving them the ability to identify fraudulent activity."},{"p":"Blockchain also changes accounting around the **delivery of goods and services**: customers can now confirm receipt directly by verifying the delivery transaction on the blockchain, and contract execution with suppliers can be completed automatically via smart contracts embedded in the blockchain's code."},{"warn":"Blockchain is like any new technology an organisation adopts: it **necessitates** development of the cost accountant's skill set to understand, manage and assess new types of transactions. Entities implementing blockchain should train accountants to initiate, verify and trace transactions using it — failing to do so prevents the cost accountant from recording and reviewing transactions once the new technology becomes the organisation's sole means of recording and verifying financial transactions, and prevents them testing embedded blockchain controls, affecting their competency."}]},{"n":"14.14","t":"Chapter summary","b":[{"p":"This chapter surveyed thirteen developments that have reshaped cost accounting beyond the traditional techniques of earlier chapters — grouped here by the underlying idea they share."},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Cluster","Development(s)"],"rows":[["**Inventory philosophy**","JIT (demand-pull, zero inventory) and Kanban (a step further — a demand signal spreading through the supply chain, ideally e-Kanban)."],["**Costing to match**","Backflush accounting — costing that follows JIT's near-zero inventory by dropping detailed WIP/stores tracking and attaching cost straight to output."],["**Design-stage cost thinking**","Life cycle costing (recovering *all* cradle-to-grave cost over the product's full expected sales) and target costing (working backward from market price to the cost the design must achieve)."],["**Production technology**","Advanced Manufacturing Technology (AMT) — CAD/CAM, FMS, TQM, MRP, JIT and others, replacing criticised traditional methods."],["**Accounting philosophy for lean/constrained operations**","Lean accounting (waste elimination applied to the accounting function itself, via value-stream costing) and throughput accounting (ranking products by contribution per unit of bottleneck resource, via the throughput accounting ratio)."],["**Data and transparency**","Data analytics (descriptive → diagnostic → predictive → prescriptive) and cost transparency (disclosing costs to build stakeholder trust)."],["**Sustainability and environment**","Sustainability cost accounting/management, environmental accounting (management/sustainability/full-cost/national/financial variants), and carbon footprint accounting (IOA, LCA, IPCC, Kaya methods, and the ISO allocation hierarchy)."],["**Technology infrastructure**","Cloud-based solutions (IaaS/PaaS/SaaS, public/private/hybrid) and blockchain (completeness/existence assurance, smart contracts, fraud-risk reduction)."]]}}]},{"n":"14.15","t":"Study text questions — multiple-choice and short-answer","b":[{"p":"The study text closes the chapter with ten questions — five multiple-choice and five short-answer — reproduced below in full with the official answers. The chapter has **no examination-type questions**; it ends here."},{"eg":{"tag":"Study text","t":"The ten questions, with the official answers","open":true,"q":[{"ol":["In relation to Just-in-Time (JIT) systems, what does **\"demand-pull\"** mean? (A) Each process in production is linked to the other; (B) The system is based purely on demand and supply; (C) The production chain is activated real-time by demand; (D) Goods are produced before demand; (E) Production deadlines are strictly monitored.","Kanban systems are a step ahead of JIT because they ensure that ……………… (A) Intermediate inventory held in the supply chain is better managed; (B) Materials suppliers are kept on their toes at all times; (C) There are no opening or closing inventory; (D) Much factory space is saved; (E) There are no production wastages.","In backflush costing, ………………………… (A) Every accounting entry is flushed out of the system; (B) There is no need for detailed tracking of material movement through stores and production; (C) There is a separate account for work in progress; (D) Financial records are not necessary; (E) The cost ledger is fully automated.","Which of the following costs is **NOT** part of life cycle costing? (A) Product promotion costs; (B) Manufacturing costs; (C) Research and development costs; (D) Product development costs; (E) Public relations costs.","Which of the following is the main objective of Advanced Manufacturing Technology (AMT)? (A) Production of high quality goods at low cost, thereby maximising customer satisfaction; (B) Large scale production of durable goods; (C) Uninterrupted production; (D) Reduction of cost of labour; (E) Industry cooperation.","A system which seeks to eliminate the need to keep inventories by producing or procuring inventory at the time they are required is known as ………………………...","A feature of Kanban systems which eliminates common manual entry errors and provides quick and precise information is called ………………………..","A system which seeks to reduce or eliminate detailed accounting entries by starting from the finished goods and working backwards is known as …………………………","The total cost of production from the research and development stage through to the end of the product can be recovered over the estimated number of units it expects to sell through a costing system known as ……………………………………..","A product cost estimate derived by subtracting a desired profit margin from a competitive market price is called ………………….."]}],"a":[{"ol":["**C — the production chain is activated in real time by demand.** \"Demand-pull\" means nothing is made until a customer order (or the next process's requirement) pulls it through; the opposite is the traditional \"push\" system, where goods are made to a forecast and pushed into inventory.","**A — intermediate inventory held in the supply chain is better managed.** Kanban is the signalling mechanism that makes the pull actually work between workstations, controlling the work-in-progress between them.","**B — there is no need for detailed tracking of material movement** through stores and production. Backflush accounting eliminates the conventional sequence of entries by working **backwards** from finished output.","**E — public relations costs.** Life cycle costing covers every cost attributable to the product over its whole life — research and development, design, manufacturing, marketing and promotion, distribution, and decommissioning. General public relations is a corporate cost not attributable to a specific product.","**A — production of high quality goods at low cost, thereby maximising customer satisfaction.** Reduction of labour cost (D) is a consequence of AMT, not its objective.","**Just-in-Time (JIT) systems.**","**Electronic Kanban (e-kanban).**","**Backflush accounting (backflush costing).**","**Life cycle costing.**","**Target costing.**"]}]}}]}],"formulas":[{"lb":"Target Cost","tex":"\\text{Competitive Market Price} - \\text{Desired Profit Margin}"},{"lb":"Throughput value","tex":"\\dfrac{\\text{Contribution}}{\\text{Scarce resource unit}}"},{"lb":"Fixed cost per unit (throughput)","tex":"\\dfrac{\\text{Total fixed cost}}{\\text{Total production}}"},{"lb":"Throughput Accounting Ratio","tex":"\\dfrac{\\text{Throughput value}}{\\text{Fixed cost per unit}}"}],"focus":"This chapter is conceptual, not computational — expect definition-matching and short-answer questions rather than numeric problems (the throughput accounting ratio is the one formula genuinely worth being able to compute). Focus on being able to name each development from a one-line description of what it does, and on the handful of genuinely confusable pairs: JIT vs Kanban (Kanban is JIT taken one step further, as an electronic/visual demand signal), backflush accounting vs traditional process costing (backflush drops WIP tracking entirely), life cycle costing vs target costing (life cycle costing recovers cost across a product's full life; target costing works backward from price to a cost target at the design stage), and lean accounting vs throughput accounting (lean is about eliminating waste generally; throughput is specifically about ranking products against a bottleneck resource).","errors":["Confusing JIT purchasing with JIT production — purchasing is about supplier delivery arrangements; production is about the demand-pull trigger inside the factory itself.","Describing Kanban as simply \"another name for JIT\" rather than a variant that specifically adds a demand-signal mechanism spreading through the supply chain.","Thinking backflush accounting means no accounting entries at all — it reduces and reorders entries (working backward from finished goods), it does not eliminate them.","Mixing up life cycle costing (recovering total cost across a product's full sales life) with target costing (a design-stage price-minus-margin cost target) — they address different questions even though both are \"beyond factory-gate\" costing ideas.","Treating the throughput accounting ratio's denominator as total fixed cost rather than fixed cost *per unit* — the ratio only makes sense once fixed cost has been divided by total production.","Assuming blockchain changes the fundamentals of cost accounting itself — the chapter is explicit that the basics are unaffected; what changes is the risk profile and the controls that must be tested."],"quiz":{"mcq":[{"q":"In relation to Just-in-Time (JIT) systems, what does \"demand-pull\" mean?","o":["Each process in production is linked to the other","The system is based purely on demand and supply","The production chain is activated real-time by demand","Goods are produced before demand","Production deadlines are strictly monitored"],"a":2,"w":"Demand-pull means the production chain is activated in real time by actual customer demand, rather than goods being made ahead of time and held in stock.","src":"Chapter 14.1","sec":"14.1"},{"q":"Kanban systems are considered a step ahead of JIT because they ensure that...","o":["Intermediate inventory held in the supply chain is better managed","Materials suppliers are kept on their toes at all times","There is no opening or closing inventory","Much factory space is saved","There are no production wastages"],"a":0,"w":"Kanban is a variant of JIT, one step ahead of it, specifically because it acts as a demand signal that spreads through the supply chain, ensuring intermediate inventory held there is better managed.","src":"Chapter 14.2","sec":"14.2"},{"q":"In backflush costing,...","o":["Every accounting entry is flushed out of the system","There is no need for detailed tracking of material movement through stores and production","There is a separate account for work in progress","Financial records are not necessary","The cost ledger is fully automated"],"a":1,"w":"Backflush costing starts from finished goods and works backward, attributing cost between cost of sales and finished goods/materials inventory, with no separate account for WIP or detailed tracking of material movement through stores.","src":"Chapter 14.3","sec":"14.3"},{"q":"Which of the following costs is NOT part of life cycle costing?","o":["Product promotion costs","Manufacturing costs","Research and development costs","Product development costs","Public relations costs"],"a":4,"w":"Life cycle costing captures research and development, plant and equipment, manufacturing, product development and promotion costs — public relations costs are not among the categories the chapter lists.","src":"Chapter 14.4","sec":"14.4"},{"q":"Which of the following is the main objective of Advanced Manufacturing Technology (AMT)?","o":["Production of high quality goods at low cost, maximising customer satisfaction","Large scale production of durable goods","Uninterrupted production","Reduction of cost of labour","Industry cooperation"],"a":0,"w":"AMT techniques promise effectiveness and healthy competitiveness — producing high-quality goods at low cost and thereby maximising customer satisfaction.","src":"Chapter 14.6","sec":"14.6"},{"q":"A product cost estimate derived by subtracting a desired profit margin from a competitive market price is called","o":["Life cycle cost","Standard cost","Target cost","Throughput cost","Full cost"],"a":2,"w":"This is CIMA's own definition of target cost: Competitive Market Price − Desired Profit Margin.","src":"Chapter 14.5","sec":"14.5"},{"q":"A throughput accounting ratio of less than 1 means that","o":["Throughput value is able to cover fixed cost","Throughput value does not cover the business's fixed expenses","The bottleneck has been fully eliminated","The product should always be dropped immediately","Contribution per unit is negative"],"a":1,"w":"A ratio above 1 means throughput value covers fixed cost; a ratio below 1 means it does not — a signal the product may not be viable as currently made and priced.","src":"Chapter 14.8","sec":"14.8"},{"q":"Which type of data analytics answers the question \"Why did it happen?\"","o":["Descriptive analytics","Diagnostic analytics","Predictive analytics","Prescriptive analytics","Comparative analytics"],"a":1,"w":"Diagnostic analytics investigates anomalies and identifies the causes behind trends or events — the \"why\" question, distinct from descriptive (\"what happened\") and predictive (\"what is likely to happen\").","src":"Chapter 14.9","sec":"14.9"},{"q":"Under blockchain-based transaction processing, the risk of payments being made to fake suppliers is reduced mainly because","o":["Smart contracts are illegal to alter, so fraud is impossible","All payments are verified by the supplier and recorded with a transaction hash both parties can see","Blockchain eliminates the need for suppliers entirely","Cost accountants no longer need to review transactions","Cryptocurrency cannot be used in fraud"],"a":1,"w":"Because payments are verified by the supplier and recorded in a block with a transaction hash, and both supplier and organisation can see the full transaction history, fraudulent activity becomes far easier to identify than under traditional single-party invoicing.","src":"Chapter 14.13","sec":"14.13"}],"theory":[{"q":"Distinguish between Just-in-Time (JIT) purchasing and Just-in-Time (JIT) production.","marks":4,"a":[{"p":"**JIT purchasing** is an arrangement with reliable suppliers for regular, frequent deliveries of inventory exactly as and when required — eliminating the need to tie down capital in inventory, saving factory space, and cutting the paperwork of large, long-term orders."},{"p":"**JIT production** applies the same demand-pull principle inside the factory: production is triggered by a confirmed customer order, and depends on two assurances — prompt delivery by suppliers at the exact time required, and 100% quality with zero rejects."}],"src":"Chapter 14.1","sec":"14.1"},{"q":"Explain the concept of the \"target cost\" and how it differs from traditional cost-plus pricing.","marks":5,"a":[{"p":"A target cost (per CIMA) is a product cost estimate derived by subtracting a desired profit margin from a competitive market price — i.e. Target Cost = Competitive Market Price − Desired Profit Margin."},{"p":"This is the reverse of traditional cost-plus pricing. Cost-plus pricing starts from the cost of making the product and adds a margin to arrive at a selling price. Target costing starts from the price the market will actually bear, deducts the required profit margin, and works backward to find the cost the product **must** be designed and made to hit — placing heavy emphasis on the design stage, since design is considered to consume the major share of a product's costs before it ever reaches manufacturing."}],"src":"Chapter 14.5","sec":"14.5"},{"q":"What conditions must hold before throughput accounting can be applied, and what is the throughput accounting ratio used to assess?","marks":5,"a":[{"p":"Throughput accounting applies only when a business has a genuine bottleneck (a scarce resource limiting production capacity — e.g. labour hours, machine hours or raw material), and the business produces more than one product, where those products use the bottleneck resource in different proportions."},{"p":"The throughput accounting ratio = throughput value ÷ fixed cost per unit, where throughput value = contribution ÷ scarce resource unit, and fixed cost per unit = total fixed cost ÷ total production. A ratio above 1 means the throughput value covers fixed cost; a ratio below 1 means it does not, signalling the product may not be viable as currently made."}],"src":"Chapter 14.8","sec":"14.8"}]}},{"n":15,"t":"Cost Reduction and Control","brief":"The final chapter closes the loop on everything a cost accountant does: cost control (keeping actual cost in line with a target) is not enough on its own, especially in a recession, so this chapter covers cost reduction and the dedicated toolkit built for it — value analysis, value engineering, Organisation and Methods, work study, and cost audit — plus a modern coda on managing cost in cloud computing environments.","outcomes":["Explain the terms \"cost reduction\" and \"cost control\"","Differentiate between cost reduction and cost control","Explain value analysis and value engineering","Explain Organisation and Methods (O&M)","Explain work study and differentiate between method study and work measurement","Explain cost audit"],"secs":[{"n":"15.1","t":"Introduction","b":[{"p":"Changes in the business environment have compelled organisations to adopt methods that help keep costs to the barest minimum, so they remain competitive and profitable. The important consideration is that new technology both lowers cost and improves quality."}]},{"n":"15.2","t":"Cost reduction","b":[{"p":"Periods of economic recession are usually characterised by a reduction in demand for products, and cost control alone is not sufficient to improve demand in such periods. What a firm needs, to remain in business and compete favourably at such times, is **cost reduction**."},{"def":{"t":"Cost reduction programme","d":"the real and permanent savings in unit operation cost, achieved by reducing cost without impairing consumer satisfaction."}},{"h4":"Two forms of a cost reduction programme"},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Form","How it works"],"rows":[["**Crash programme**","Not planned — reactive measures such as expenditure cutting, staff retrenchment, and capital expenditure deferment."],["**Planned programme**","Operations are deliberately planned and monitored to ensure compliance with the cost reduction goal."]]}}]},{"n":"15.2.1","t":"Comparison between cost control and cost reduction","b":[{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Cost control","Cost reduction"],"rows":[["Ensures actual costs incurred conform with a **target already set** (using techniques such as standard costing, budgetary control, and capital expenditure control).","Concerned with a **permanent reduction** in cost, without impairing the quality of products or the efficiency of operations."]]}},{"key":"The major reason cost reduction exists as its own discipline: **cost control alone cannot guarantee a firm's survival** in a period of economic recession — it can only keep spending in line with a target that may itself now be unaffordable, not find genuinely new, permanent savings."}]},{"n":"15.2.2","t":"Where to apply cost reduction","b":[{"p":"As a general rule, when a new product is to be designed, a firm should introduce cost reduction at the **product design stage** — design is also then carried through into the production process stage. At the design stage, it is important to determine the appropriate facilities to acquire for production. What cost reduction targets:"},{"ol":["the use of cheaper materials that will not impair product specification;","reduction in ordering and holding costs;","reduction in re-order period or lead time;","control of overtime work;","adoption of merit rating for promotion;","adequate utilisation of production capacity; and","elimination of non-value time."]}]},{"n":"15.3","t":"Value analysis","b":[{"def":{"t":"Value analysis","d":"a systematic, interdisciplinary examination of factors affecting the cost of a product or service, in order to devise means of achieving its specific purpose most economically, at the required standard of quality or reliability."}},{"p":"Value analysis is an approach to improving the value of a product or process by understanding its constituent components and their associated costs, then seeking improvements to those components — either by reducing their cost, or by increasing the value of the functions they deliver."},{"h4":"Three key concepts of value analysis"},{"ol":["**Value** — the ratio between a function's contribution to customer satisfaction and the cost of that function;","**Function** — the effect produced by a product, or one of its elements, in order to satisfy customer needs; and","**Need** — something that is necessary or desired by the customer."]},{"p":"The object analysed can be an existing product/process or a new one. It is usually accomplished by a team, following a structured work plan, with the main purpose of improving every component in a product."},{"h4":"The value analysis process"},{"steps":["Orientation/preparation","Information","Analysis","Innovation/creativity","Evaluation","Implementation and monitoring"]},{"note":"Applying value analysis needs only basic techniques such as matrices, Pareto charts, PERT and Gantt diagrams — most of the process is structured thinking, not specialised technical tooling."},{"p":"The producer measures value analysis as the relationship between product worth and product cost:"},{"tex":"\\text{Value} = \\dfrac{\\text{Product worth}}{\\text{Price paid}}"},{"key":"The higher this ratio, the higher the customer's satisfaction — value analysis is ultimately about maximising *worth received per unit of price paid*, not simply minimising price."},{"h4":"Advantages of value analysis"},{"ul":["A high customer orientation, focusing on the aspects of a product/service that better satisfy customer needs;","Cost reduction by eliminating functions that supply no specific advantage toward satisfying customer requirements;","New ideas from the creativity/innovation phase, which may bring radical changes and competitive advantages the market will notice; and","A new systematic mentality carried forward into the design of future products, or to systematically improve existing ones."]},{"h4":"Rules for a successful value analysis exercise"},{"ul":["Avoid making generalisations and superficial statements — be precise at every moment;","Collect, determine and examine all costs involved — only when one is genuinely cost-conscious is it possible to determine the value of the thing being assessed; and","Make use of information from the best possible sources."]}]},{"n":"15.4","t":"Value engineering","b":[{"p":"Value engineering is an organised approach to providing the necessary functions of a product or service at the **lowest cost**. From its inception, the concept was seen as a cost-validation exercise that did not affect product quality."},{"warn":"That original framing — \"does not affect quality\" — is now seen as a shortcoming of the definition itself, since it left no room for genuinely *enhancing* value, not just holding it constant. The terminology has since been broadened accordingly (see below), and the chapter presents both formulations deliberately: the historical starting point, and the fuller definition that replaced it."},{"def":{"t":"Value engineering (current definition)","d":"an organised approach to the identification and elimination of unnecessary cost — formally, a redesign of an activity, product or service so that value to the customer is enhanced while costs are reduced (or, at least, increased by less than the resulting price increase)."}},{"def":{"t":"Unnecessary cost","d":"a cost which does not provide use, life, quality, appearance, or customer features, and does not add to customer satisfaction."}},{"h4":"Tasks involved in value engineering practice"},{"ol":["Preparing and administering maintenance programmes;","Forecasting expenditure flows;","Advising on cost limits and preparing budgets;","Advising on cash flow forecasting;","Advising on life cycle costing;","Cost analysis;","Cost-benefit analysis;","Estimating;","Evaluating alternative designs;","Investment appraisal; and","Measuring and describing construction work, but only in terms of cost planning."]},{"key":"Value **analysis** and value **engineering** are close relatives, not synonyms: analysis is the *examination* — understanding components and costs to find where improvement is possible; engineering is the *redesign* that acts on those findings to eliminate unnecessary cost while protecting or enhancing value."}]},{"n":"15.5","t":"Organisation and Methods (O&M)","b":[{"p":"Organisation and Methods originated with the pioneers of scientific management — Taylor and Gilbreth — whose work influenced the early approaches to, and eventual establishment of, the function."},{"def":{"t":"Organisation and Methods (O&M)","d":"the systematic examination of activities in order to improve the effective use of human and other material resources."}},{"p":"O&M is a specialist function whose primary objective is improving an organisation's efficiency and control — an essential function that should be part of the make-up of any organisation. O&M and its associated techniques form the basis for:"},{"ol":["Business Process Re-engineering; and","Business Process Improvement."]},{"p":"Because line management typically lacks the time or specialist skill to provide this service themselves, O&M functions as a **consultative service** to management — staff and management working daily on a process may not be able to think far beyond it, and so miss obvious improvement opportunities that a fresh outlook, from O&M principles, is able to spot."},{"note":"Until recently, O&M departments existed as a clearly identifiable unit within organisations. In more recent times the function has commonly been renamed **Project Management** or **Business Improvement**, and in many organisations today it has been subsumed into other functions — most often IT."},{"h4":"The O&M approach — six steps"},{"steps":["Select the area/process that requires attention;","Record the current situation;","Examine and analyse the existing situation;","Develop, design and evaluate alternative solutions, and recommend improvement opportunities;","Implement the chosen solution; and","Monitor and maintain the implemented solution."]},{"key":"O&M is indispensable precisely because it provides a basis for the approach to **almost any project**, regardless of the specific process being examined."}]},{"n":"15.6","t":"Work study","b":[{"def":{"t":"Work study","d":"a system of increasing or maximising the productivity of an operating unit by reorganising the work of that unit."}},{"p":"Work study is sub-divided into two major methods:"},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Method","Definition"],"rows":[["**Method study**","The recording and critical examination of existing methods of doing work, and comparing them with proposed methods, with a view to arriving at easier methods that would be more effective and cheaper in the long run."],["**Work measurement**","Seeks to measure the time required for a qualified worker to complete a specific assignment, at a specified level of performance."]]}},{"key":"§15.6's own distinction, stated plainly: **method study asks \"what is the best way to do this work?\"**, while **work measurement asks \"how long should the best way take?\"** — the two are complementary halves of the same overall work study effort, not competing techniques."}]},{"n":"15.7","t":"Cost audit","b":[{"p":"All organisations should strive to ensure their cost structure correctly reflects operational performance — achievable only where cost audit is carried out as a **continuous process**. Cost audit assists immensely in the process of cost reduction."},{"def":{"t":"Cost Audit (CIMA)","d":"verification of cost records and accounts, and a check on adherence to the prescribed cost accounting procedures and their continuous relevance."}},{"p":"Cost audit is a continuous process of auditing current operations, comparing current audit results with previous audit findings."},{"h4":"The two forms of cost audit"},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Form","What it ensures"],"rows":[["**Efficiency (Performance) Audit**","That the organisation's plans have been carried out efficiently; that costs incurred are not overstated; and that the organisation's resources are properly utilised."],["**Propriety Audit**","Investigates management plans with respect to financing — its core aim is to ensure management takes right decisions, and establishes proper control and monitoring activities."]]}}]},{"n":"15.8","t":"Differences between cost audit and financial audit","b":[{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Financial Audit","Cost Audit"],"rows":[["May be statutory (compulsory for all PLCs) and, in such instances, must be carried out by an **external** auditor.","Non-statutory, and can be carried out by persons appointed (internally or externally) by management."],["Based on specific past records — a \"post-mortem\" check.","Based on current and continuous operations."],["Tries to verify **financial** records.","Verifies **cost accounting** records."]]}}]},{"n":"15.9","t":"Cost audit programme","b":[{"p":"A reliable cost audit programme must be designed for the entire organisation, and kept updated as the organisation's activities change. It should cover all three elements of cost and every other relevant area. For example, the audit programme for **material transactions** may check:"},{"ul":["procedures adopted in selecting suppliers;","reliability of material classification and codification, so that expenditure can be easily traced;","whether materials are purchased economically;","the control established in the store, and the security established for store items;","the estimate for normal loss, and that abnormal gain receives proper accounting treatment;","methods adopted for issuing materials from stores to the factory; and","whether there is a quality control unit for testing purchased materials and finished goods produced."]}]},{"n":"15.10","t":"Cost control and cloud systems","b":[{"p":"As desirable as moving computing infrastructure to the cloud is, staying within budget can be a serious challenge — the variables include the amount of storage used, computing demand, and the application running on each cloud instance. It is important to know all cloud costs and service requirements, so a good balance is struck between money spent on cloud infrastructure and the company's actual needs."},{"def":{"t":"Cloud cost management","d":"the process organisations use to keep cloud costs under control — whether cloud requirements are left to a single vendor or shared across multiple vendors, controlling costs is difficult, and an organisation can easily lose track of exactly what services it is consuming and whether it is overpaying for them."}},{"p":"Cloud cost management **tools** help companies control spending by monitoring cloud use and demand — alerting IT managers about resources that are underutilised, or that have become unexpectedly expensive."},{"h4":"Factors that contribute to cloud costs"},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Factor","How it drives cost"],"rows":[["**Resource usage**","The more computing power, storage and bandwidth each cloud instance uses, the higher the bill — a company that does not monitor resources may be surprised by unexpectedly high charges."],["**Instance types**","Virtual machines (VMs — software emulating a full operating-system instance) and containers (prefabricated software packages running a specific application, several of which can run on one VM) are priced differently; containers are usually cheaper, though usage fees can erode that gap."],["**Storage costs**","Both the amount of data stored online and the storage type affect the bill, alongside separate costs for moving data into and out of storage — a data-intensive business that frequently uploads/downloads can incur substantial charges."],["**Autoscaling**","Automatic scaling of resources up or down on demand can be expensive, but lets a company handle traffic spikes (e.g. a product going viral) that might otherwise cripple its servers when performance is needed most."],["**Geographic location**","Different data-centre locations carry different costs; hosting data far from where it is used can sometimes be cheaper (lower-cost resources/utilities/infrastructure, or tax incentives), but that saving must be weighed against increased network latency, slower response times, and security/regulatory concerns."],["**Support**","Contracted or ad hoc support carries an additional cost — support backed by a service level agreement (SLA), e.g. 99.9% uptime with 24/7 phone support, can be expensive, but not every cloud instance actually needs that level of guarantee."]]}},{"h4":"Advantages of cloud cost management"},{"ul":["**Cost cutting** — tracking cloud spend lets a business eliminate inefficiencies and optimise spending, finding (and ideally preventing) waste such as duplicative services or resources that have outlived their usefulness;","**Cost allocation** — lets a company charge cloud costs back to the departments actually using the services, giving those departments an incentive to manage their own spending;","**Budgetary control** — helps companies understand what they spend on cloud services and better set and adhere to a budget, e.g. via chargebacks and showbacks (accounting for resource consumption without necessarily levying the actual charge);","**Forecasting** — knowing past cloud spend provides a basis for budgeting future corporate initiatives;","**Visibility** — understanding where and how data and other resources are used, eliminating duplicative/conflicting databases and securing corporate and customer information;","**Rightsizing** — detecting an instance created with inappropriate resources (e.g. more memory/processing cores than the application needs) and suggesting optimisation to save money."]},{"h4":"Cloud cost management strategies"},{"ol":["**Remove unnecessary instances** — there is no reason to pay for an idle instance, since the meter generally runs whether it is used or not; identify and turn off what is not needed.","**Set organisational rules** — internal processes governing who may contract for cloud services, under what circumstances, and within what spending limits, keep departments from spinning up unauthorised or wasteful instances.","**Implement autoscaling** — rather than provisioning for maximum load at all times, allow instances to efficiently accommodate demand surges and dips; autoscaling may be expensive, but is less costly than paying for more services than are necessary day in and day out.","**Schedule demand** — not all instances need to run 24/7; shutting down or \"sleeping\" instances at night or on weekends, when no one is likely to use them, can save money without affecting service."]}]},{"n":"15.11","t":"Chapter summary","b":[{"p":"This chapter, and the whole Cost Accounting study text, close on the theme that cost management is not a single technique but a **layered discipline**: cost control keeps actual cost aligned with a pre-set target; cost reduction goes further, finding real, permanent savings — especially necessary in recession, when a target itself may no longer be affordable."},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Tool","What it does"],"rows":[["**Value analysis**","Examines a product/process's components and costs to find where cost can be cut, or value increased, without harming what the customer actually needs."],["**Value engineering**","Acts on those findings — redesigning to eliminate unnecessary cost while enhancing (or at least protecting) customer value."],["**Organisation and Methods (O&M)**","A consultative, systematic examination of activities to improve the effective use of human and material resources — the basis for Business Process Re-engineering/Improvement."],["**Work study**","Method study (finding the *best way* to do work) plus work measurement (finding *how long* the best way should take)."],["**Cost audit**","Continuous verification of cost records and procedures — efficiency/performance audit plus propriety audit — distinct from financial audit in being non-statutory, continuous rather than a post-mortem, and focused on cost (not financial) records."],["**Cloud cost management**","The newest addition to the toolkit — applying the same underlying discipline (monitor, control, allocate, forecast) to a specifically cloud-computing cost structure."]]}}]},{"n":"15.12","t":"Study text questions — multiple-choice and short-answer","b":[{"p":"The study text closes the chapter with eight questions — three multiple-choice and five short-answer — reproduced below in full with the official answers. The chapter has **no examination-type questions**; it ends here. (Cost reduction is nonetheless examined: see Chapter 12's examination-type question Q11, ICAG May 1999, which is worked in §12.20.)"},{"eg":{"tag":"Study text","t":"The eight questions, with the official answers","open":true,"q":[{"ol":["Which of the following best differentiates cost reduction and cost control? (A) Cost reduction and cost control are the same; (B) Cost reduction aims to slash costs whilst cost control aims to increase costs; (C) Cost reduction is a scientific method of controlling costs whilst cost control is arbitrary; (D) Cost control seeks to maximise the benefits of every amount spent whilst cost reduction seeks to generally reduce the cost of products and services; (E) Cost control is used in respect of products whilst cost reduction is concerned only with services.","Which of the following best describes **Cost Audit**? (A) It is the audit of the cost of materials; (B) It is the rechecking and confirmation of suppliers' prices; (C) It is the process of ascertaining whether every aspect of the business is managed in the most cost-effective way; (D) It is the independent checking of material costs from different sources; (E) It is the complete building up of the cost of manufacturing.","Which of the following best describes **Organisation and Methods**? (A) The total overhaul of an organisation and its methods of operation in order to improve profitability; (B) The systematic examination of activities in order to improve the effective use of human and other natural resources; (C) A complete review of production processes with a view to improvement; (D) An independent analysis of structures and policies of an organisation to ensure harmonisation; (E) A periodic review of the effectiveness of production machinery to ensure their optimum output at all times.","An organised approach to the identification and elimination of unnecessary costs of running a business is called …………………………..","An approach towards improving the value of a product or process by understanding its constituent components and the associated costs is known as …………………….","A system of increasing or maximising the productivity of an operating unit by reorganising the work of that unit is referred to as ……………….","The recording and critical examination of existing methods of doing work, with a view to coming up with better and more effective methods, is called ………………………..","The measurement of the time required for a qualified worker to complete a specific assignment is known as ……………………………."]}],"a":[{"ol":["**D — cost control seeks to maximise the benefits of every amount spent whilst cost reduction seeks generally to reduce the cost of products and services.** Cost control keeps costs within a predetermined standard; cost reduction lowers the standard itself (§15.2.1).","**C — the process of ascertaining whether every aspect of the business is managed in the most cost-effective way.** Per CIMA, cost audit is the verification of cost records and accounts and a check on adherence to the prescribed cost accounting procedures and their continued relevance (§15.7).","**B — the systematic examination of activities in order to improve the effective use of human and other natural resources.** O&M is the discipline applied to administrative and clerical work, as work study is to production work (§15.5).","**Cost reduction.**","**Value analysis.**","**Organisation and Methods (O&M).**","**Method study.**","**Work measurement.**"]},{"note":"The study text's own answer key gives **4 = Value engineering** and **5 = Value analysis**. Answer 5 is right, but answer 4 is not: an **organised approach to the identification and elimination of unnecessary costs of running a business** is the definition of **cost reduction** itself (§15.2), and in any case value engineering is applied at the **design** stage of a product rather than to \"the costs of running a business\". Question 5's wording — improving the value of an **existing** product or process by examining its components and their costs — is value analysis, which the key correctly gives. Answers 6, 7 and 8 in the key (work study, method study, work measurement) are shifted by one against the questions; they have been realigned above.","lbl":"An error in the study text's answer key"}]}}]}],"formulas":[{"lb":"Value (value analysis)","tex":"\\dfrac{\\text{Product worth}}{\\text{Price paid}}"}],"focus":"Like Chapter 14, this chapter is conceptual rather than computational — expect definition-matching and short-answer questions. The examiner's favourite distinction is cost control vs. cost reduction (target-conformity vs. permanent savings) — know it cold, since it is also the conceptual thread tying this entire chapter, and this entire study text, together. The second most-tested pair is value analysis (examining/understanding) vs. value engineering (redesigning/acting), and the third is method study vs. work measurement (best way vs. how long the best way takes).","errors":["Treating cost control and cost reduction as synonyms — cost control conforms spending to a pre-set target; cost reduction finds permanent savings, which is a fundamentally different and broader goal.","Describing value engineering as \"the same thing as value analysis\" — analysis examines and diagnoses; engineering redesigns and acts on what analysis finds.","Confusing method study with work measurement — method study finds the best way to do a task; work measurement times how long the best way should take. They are the two halves of work study, not alternatives to each other.","Assuming cost audit is statutory like financial audit — cost audit is explicitly non-statutory, and can be carried out by persons appointed internally or externally by management, not necessarily an independent external auditor.","Overlooking that cost reduction is best applied at the **design stage** of a new product, not only during the production process once the design is already fixed."],"quiz":{"mcq":[{"q":"Which of the following best differentiates cost reduction and cost control?","o":["Cost reduction and cost control are the same","Cost reduction aims to slash costs whilst cost control aims to increase costs","Cost reduction is a scientific method of controlling costs whilst cost control is arbitrary","Cost control seeks to maximize the benefits of every amount spent whilst cost reduction seeks to generally reduce cost of products and services","Cost control is used in respect of products whilst cost reduction is concerned only with services"],"a":3,"w":"Cost control ensures actual costs conform to a set target — maximising the benefit of every amount already budgeted to be spent — while cost reduction seeks a real, permanent reduction in the cost of products and services themselves.","src":"Chapter 15.2","sec":"15.2"},{"q":"Which of the following best describes Cost Audit?","o":["It is the audit of the cost of materials","It is the rechecking and confirmation of suppliers' prices","It is the process of ascertaining whether every aspect of the business is managed in the most cost-effective way","It is the independent checking of material costs from different sources","It is the complete building up of cost of manufacturing"],"a":2,"w":"CIMA defines cost audit as verification of cost records and accounts and a check on adherence to prescribed cost accounting procedures — in substance, an ongoing check that every aspect of the business is managed in the most cost-effective way.","src":"Chapter 15.7","sec":"15.7"},{"q":"Which of the following best describes Organisation and Methods?","o":["The total overhaul of an organisation and its methods of operation in order to improve profitability","The systematic examination of activities in order to improve the effective use of human and other material resources","A complete review of production processes with a view to improvement","An independent analysis of structures and policies of an organisation to ensure harmonisation","A periodic review of the effectiveness of production machinery to ensure their optimum output at all times"],"a":1,"w":"This is the chapter's own definition of O&M — the systematic examination of activities to improve the effective use of human and other material resources.","src":"Chapter 15.5","sec":"15.5"},{"q":"An organised approach to the identification and elimination of unnecessary costs of running a business is called","o":["Value analysis","Cost audit","Value engineering","Work study","Cost reduction"],"a":2,"w":"Value engineering is defined as an organised approach to the identification and elimination of unnecessary cost.","src":"Chapter 15.4","sec":"15.4"},{"q":"An approach towards improving the value of a product or process by understanding its constituent components and the associated costs is known as","o":["Value engineering","Cost reduction","Value analysis","Work measurement","Method study"],"a":2,"w":"Value analysis is the systematic, interdisciplinary examination of factors affecting cost by understanding a product/process's constituent components and their associated costs.","src":"Chapter 15.3","sec":"15.3"},{"q":"The recording and critical examination of existing methods of doing work, with a view to coming up with better and more effective methods, is called","o":["Work study","Work measurement","Method study","Value engineering","O&M"],"a":2,"w":"Method study is defined precisely as the recording and critical examination of existing methods, compared against proposed methods, to find an easier, more effective and cheaper method.","src":"Chapter 15.6","sec":"15.6"},{"q":"Which of the following is a genuine difference between financial audit and cost audit?","o":["Financial audit is non-statutory while cost audit is always statutory","Financial audit is based on current, continuous operations while cost audit is a post-mortem check","Financial audit may be statutory for PLCs while cost audit is non-statutory","Cost audit must always be carried out by an external auditor","There is no genuine difference — the two terms describe the same process"],"a":2,"w":"Financial audit may be statutory (compulsory for PLCs, requiring an external auditor), while cost audit is non-statutory and can be carried out by persons appointed internally or externally by management.","src":"Chapter 15.8","sec":"15.8"},{"q":"A \"crash programme\" form of cost reduction is best described as","o":["A carefully planned and monitored set of operations","An unplanned response, such as expenditure cutting, staff retrenchment or capital expenditure deferment","A statutory audit procedure","A form of value engineering only","A method used exclusively in cloud cost management"],"a":1,"w":"A crash programme is explicitly the *unplanned* form of cost reduction — reactive measures such as expenditure cutting, staff retrenchment and capital expenditure deferment, as opposed to a planned programme where operations are deliberately planned and monitored.","src":"Chapter 15.2","sec":"15.2"}],"theory":[{"q":"Distinguish between cost control and cost reduction, and explain why cost reduction is needed even where cost control is already in place.","marks":6,"a":[{"p":"**Cost control** ensures that actual costs incurred conform with a target that has already been set, using techniques such as standard costing, budgetary control and capital expenditure control."},{"p":"**Cost reduction** is concerned with achieving a **permanent** reduction in cost, without impairing the quality of products or the efficiency of operations."},{"p":"Cost reduction is needed alongside cost control because cost control alone cannot guarantee a firm's survival in a period of economic recession: it only keeps spending aligned with an existing target, but does nothing if that target itself has become unaffordable as demand falls. Cost reduction finds genuinely new, permanent savings instead."}],"src":"Chapter 15.2","sec":"15.2"},{"q":"Distinguish between value analysis and value engineering, and outline the steps followed in the value analysis process.","marks":7,"a":[{"p":"**Value analysis** is a systematic, interdisciplinary *examination* of the factors affecting a product or service's cost — understanding its components and their associated costs to find where cost can be reduced, or value increased, without harming customer satisfaction."},{"p":"**Value engineering** is the organised approach that *acts* on those findings — redesigning an activity, product or service so that value to the customer is enhanced while costs are reduced (or increased by less than the resulting price increase). In short, analysis diagnoses; engineering redesigns."},{"p":"The value analysis process has six steps: orientation/preparation; information; analysis; innovation/creativity; evaluation; and implementation and monitoring."}],"src":"Chapter 15.3-15.4","sec":"15.3"}]}}]});
